// Philodyssey: beginner-friendly profiles of every Compass position and named view.
window.ATLAS_PROFILES = {"options":{"a1|thing":{"summary":"Beauty is a real feature of objects, so “this is beautiful” is true or false of the thing, whatever anyone feels.","idea":"Aesthetic realism says beauty belongs to things much as shape or mass does. When you call a sunset beautiful, you describe the sunset, and you can be right or wrong about it. The view denies that beauty is just pleasure, a cultural habit, or an outburst of feeling. Its appeal is that it matches how we talk and learn: we say people “miss” the beauty of a piece, we train our ears to “find” it, and we feel corrected when a teacher shows us what was there all along. A child bored by a Bach fugue who later loves it seems to have discovered something, not merely changed mood.","example":"G. E. Moore’s two worlds: imagine one world exceedingly beautiful and another the ugliest pile of filth, and suppose no one will ever see either. Moore held it is still rational to think the beautiful world better. If so, beauty does not depend on anyone experiencing it.","history":"Plato and Aquinas treated beauty as a real feature of things, rooted in reality itself. Thomas Reid’s Essays on the Intellectual Powers of Man (1785) argued, against Hume, that beauty is a real excellence in objects. G. E. Moore’s Principia Ethica (1903) treated beauty as objectively valuable. Today Nick Zangwill’s The Metaphysics of Beauty (2001) is the leading defence.","misread":"Realism does not say everyone sees beauty or agrees about it; it says there is a fact to get right, which many people may miss.","variants":["Reductive realism: beauty is identical to some describable feature, such as proportion.","Non-reductive realism: beauty is real but cannot be reduced to any other feature."],"reading":[{"title":"Beauty (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauty/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Judgment (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/"}]},"a1|us":{"summary":"Beauty depends on how people respond: to call something beautiful is to say it pleases, or would please, human beings.","idea":"On this view beauty is real but relational, like sweetness or funniness. A joke really is funny, yet “funny” means apt to amuse people like us; a sunset really is beautiful because of how it affects minds like ours. The view denies that beauty would exist in a universe without perceivers, but it also denies that beauty is a private whim, since human responses are widely shared and can be studied. It is attractive because it keeps beauty tied to pleasure, where our experience of beauty starts, while still letting us say some things really are beautiful and others are not.","example":"Locke’s distinction between primary and secondary qualities is the standard model. Shape and size belong to an object; warmth and taste are powers it has to cause sensations in us. Response theorists treat beauty as a secondary quality: a cherry blossom has it because of the delight it reliably produces in normal human viewers.","history":"Francis Hutcheson’s Inquiry into the Original of Our Ideas of Beauty and Virtue (1725) proposed an inner sense of beauty. Hume’s “Of the Standard of Taste” (1757) placed beauty in the mind that contemplates things, yet built a standard on shared human nature. Kant’s Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790) also grounds beauty in feeling. Later response-dependence theories, developed by Mark Johnston and others, refine the idea.","misread":"Response-based does not mean “whatever I like is beautiful”; most versions appeal to normal or ideal responders, not to one person’s mood.","variants":["Dispositional view: beauty is a power to please suitable observers.","Ideal-observer view: beauty is what well-placed judges would enjoy.","Sentimentalism: beauty is grounded in human feeling rather than reason."],"reading":[{"title":"Hume’s Aesthetics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume-aesthetics/"},{"title":"18th Century British Aesthetics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetics-18th-british/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Judgment (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/"}]},"a1|neither":{"summary":"Aesthetic judgments do not describe anything; they express feelings or attitudes, so they are not literally true or false.","idea":"Non-cognitivism says “That sunset is beautiful” works more like a cheer than a report. It does not describe the sunset, or even the speaker; it voices delight and perhaps invites others to share it. It denies that there is any property of beauty, in things or in us, for the sentence to be about. People find it attractive because it is economical: it needs no mysterious beauty property, and it explains why aesthetic words are so bound up with emotion. Think of applause at a concert: clapping is not true or false, yet it clearly says something about how you received the music.","example":"Ayer’s verification test is the classic case. Ask what observation could show “this sonata is beautiful” to be true or false. Pitch, tempo and length can be measured, but no measurement settles beauty. Ayer concluded the sentence has no factual content: it expresses approval, the way a tone of voice can.","history":"A. J. Ayer’s Language, Truth and Logic (1936) argued that aesthetic words, like ethical ones, express feelings and state no facts. Charles Stevenson’s Ethics and Language (1944) developed the idea that such words express attitudes and aim to influence others. Later expressivists, especially Simon Blackburn and Allan Gibbard, built more sophisticated versions, mostly for ethics, which others then applied to taste.","misread":"Non-cognitivists do not say aesthetic talk is meaningless or insincere; they say its meaning lies in expressing attitudes, not in describing facts.","variants":["Emotivism: aesthetic words express and arouse feelings.","Quasi-realism: expressive judgments that still earn talk of truth and error.","Norm-expressivism: judgments express acceptance of standards for feeling."],"reading":[{"title":"Aesthetic Judgment (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}]},"a1b|harmony":{"summary":"Things are beautiful when their parts are ordered, proportioned and unified, so that variety comes together into one harmonious whole.","idea":"The classical theory locates beauty in structure: the relations among parts. A face, a temple or a melody is beautiful when its elements fit together in the right proportions, with nothing missing and nothing excessive. It denies that beauty comes from colour, material or personal association alone. Its appeal is that it gives beauty something like a recipe you can study: architects, composers and designers really do use ratios, symmetry, and repetition with variation. A well-designed poster, where text, image and empty space balance each other, shows the idea at a glance.","example":"Pythagoras and the blacksmith’s hammers: hearing hammers ring in harmony, Pythagoras is said to have found that consonant intervals match simple ratios, 2:1 for the octave and 3:2 for the fifth. The sculptor Polykleitos applied the same thought to the body, building his Canon statue on exact proportions between its parts.","history":"The Pythagoreans (6th to 5th century BCE) tied beauty to number. Aristotle’s Metaphysics named order, symmetry and definiteness as the chief forms of beauty. Vitruvius’s On Architecture (1st century BCE) applied proportion to buildings. Thomas Aquinas listed wholeness, proportion and clarity as conditions of beauty. Hutcheson’s “uniformity amidst variety” (1725) carried the idea into modern times.","misread":"The theory does not demand rigid symmetry or one golden ratio; most classical thinkers prized unity in variety, which rules out mere monotony.","variants":["Mathematical proportion: beauty follows fixed numerical ratios.","Unity in variety: many different parts organised into one whole (Hutcheson).","Thomist account: wholeness, proportion and clarity together (Aquinas)."],"reading":[{"title":"Beauty (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauty/"},{"title":"Aesthetics, Ancient (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ancient-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Medieval Theories of Aesthetics (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/medieval-theories-of-aesthetics/"}]},"a1b|higher":{"summary":"Beautiful things are beautiful because they share in or reflect a perfect, eternal Beauty that exists beyond the physical world.","idea":"Platonism says every beautiful face, song or act is a partial copy of one source: Beauty itself, which never changes, fades or depends on anyone’s opinion. The beauty we see is real, but borrowed. The view denies that beauty is just pleasure or just arrangement. Its appeal is that it explains why beauty feels like a summons: a piece of music can make you want to become better, or seem to show something larger than itself. Many people feel that a starry night hints at something beyond the stars; Platonists take that hint seriously.","example":"In the Hippias Major, Socrates asks what the beautiful is. Hippias answers “a beautiful girl”, then “gold”. Socrates replies that a girl looks plain beside a goddess, and a fig-wood spoon suits soup better than a gold one. Each example is beautiful only in some respect, so what makes them all beautiful must be something else.","history":"Plato’s Symposium and Phaedrus (4th century BCE) set out the theory of a Form of Beauty. Plotinus’s essay “On Beauty” (Enneads I.6, 3rd century CE) argued that things are beautiful by sharing in form, and that the soul recognises beauty as its kin. Through Augustine and Pseudo-Dionysius the idea shaped Christian thought about God as Beauty, and Marsilio Ficino revived it in Renaissance Florence.","misread":"Platonists do not despise earthly beauty; they treat it as a real first step on the way to what it reflects.","variants":["Neoplatonism: beauty as the radiance of form flowing from the One (Plotinus).","Theological aesthetics: beauty as a name or reflection of God."],"reading":[{"title":"Plato’s Aesthetics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/plato-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Plotinus (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/plotinus/"},{"title":"Beauty (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauty/"}]},"a1b|fit":{"summary":"Things are beautiful when they look well made for what they are or do, so fitness for function is a source of beauty.","idea":"Functional beauty says that a thing looking well suited to its job can itself be beautiful. A hawk’s wing or a balanced kitchen knife strikes us partly because it seems to do its work with nothing wasted. The view denies that beauty must be useless or purely decorative. Its appeal is that it explains why understanding a thing changes how it looks: once you learn what a strange tool is for, its odd shape can suddenly seem elegant. Even a plain wooden chair can be beautiful because every part so plainly serves sitting.","example":"“Form follows function”, the architect Louis Sullivan’s 1896 slogan, became the creed of modernist design. A Shaker chair, with no ornament and every part serving a use, is the standard illustration: it looks right precisely because it looks exactly fit for what it does.","history":"Xenophon’s Memorabilia (4th century BCE) reports Socrates linking beauty to usefulness. Hume’s Treatise (1739 to 1740) noted that convenience and utility make up much of the beauty of houses and tools. Kant distinguished free beauty from adherent beauty, which is judged by what a thing is meant to be. Glenn Parsons and Allen Carlson’s Functional Beauty (2008) gave the modern defence.","misread":"The view does not say that whatever works is beautiful; it says looking fit for function is one important way things become beautiful.","variants":["Adherent beauty (Kant): beauty judged relative to the concept of what a thing should be.","Modernist functionalism: ornament should give way to form that shows use."],"reading":[{"title":"Beauty (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauty/"},{"title":"Aesthetics of the Everyday (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetics-of-everyday/"}]},"a1b|simple":{"summary":"Beauty is a real quality of things, but no rule about shape, order or usefulness defines it; we learn to perceive it directly.","idea":"This view agrees with realism that beauty is really there, but denies that it can be reduced to proportion, function, or any checklist. Beauty depends on a thing’s other features, yet no formula says which features produce it. So taste is a skill of perception, trained by looking and listening, not by applying rules. Its appeal is that it fits how critics actually work: they can show what makes this painting succeed without claiming the same move works everywhere. A melody can be lovely because of one surprising note that would ruin a different melody.","example":"Sibley described how critics really teach taste. They cannot prove a vase is graceful from its measurements, so they point, compare it with clumsy vases, use metaphors, and draw attention to a line until you see it. That is how you learn to see colours too: by being shown, not by being given definitions.","history":"Thomas Reid (1785) held that beauty is a real excellence perceived by a natural power of taste. The modern version comes from Frank Sibley’s “Aesthetic Concepts” (1959), which argued that aesthetic qualities depend on other features without following rules. In 1983 Philip Pettit and John McDowell compared aesthetic properties to colours. Mary Mothersill’s Beauty Restored (1984) argued there are no laws of taste.","misread":"“No recipe” does not mean no reasons: critics can still explain why a work is beautiful by pointing to its features, without claiming a general law.","variants":["Particularism about taste: reasons for beauty work only case by case.","Perceptual realism: aesthetic qualities are perceived, like colours, by trained observers."],"reading":[{"title":"Aesthetic Judgment (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/"},{"title":"Beauty (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauty/"}]},"a1b|transient":{"summary":"Beauty lies in imperfection, age and impermanence: worn, simple, incomplete or passing things move us most deeply.","idea":"Wabi-sabi finds beauty in what classical theory calls flaws: asymmetry, roughness, patina, and the marks of time. A handmade bowl with an uneven glaze, moss on a stone lantern, or blossoms already falling can be more beautiful than a flawless new object. It denies that perfection, completeness or permanence is the standard of beauty. Its appeal is that it fits a widely felt truth: what is fragile and fleeting can be precious because it will not last. A favourite pair of jeans, faded and patched, can feel more beautiful than a brand-new pair.","example":"Kintsugi is the standard illustration: broken pottery is mended with lacquer mixed with gold, so the repair becomes a visible, celebrated part of the bowl’s history. A well-known story has the tea master Sen no Rikyū sweeping a garden path perfectly clean, then shaking a tree so a few leaves fall back onto it.","history":"Yoshida Kenkō’s Essays in Idleness (c. 1330) praised the unfinished and the fading. Murata Jukō and Sen no Rikyū (1522 to 1591) made wabi, rustic simplicity, central to the tea ceremony. Matsuo Bashō (1644 to 1694) gave sabi, the beauty of age and solitude, its place in haiku. Motoori Norinaga (1730 to 1801) named mono no aware.","misread":"Wabi-sabi is not a love of mess or neglect; it prizes natural ageing and honest simplicity, often maintained with great discipline and care.","variants":["Wabi: quiet, rustic simplicity, linked to austerity and solitude.","Sabi: the beauty of age, patina and loneliness.","Mono no aware: the gentle sorrow of things passing."],"reading":[{"title":"Japanese Aesthetics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/japanese-aesthetics/"}]},"a1a|better":{"summary":"Some people judge taste better than others, and the standard of taste is the joint verdict of experienced, attentive, unbiased judges.","idea":"The ideal-critic theory grants that beauty rests on feeling, yet denies that all feelings are equally sound. Like wine tasting or medical diagnosis, taste can be trained, and some people are simply better at it. The standard is what qualified judges, with practice, wide comparison, sharp perception and no prejudice, agree on. It denies the slogan that there is no accounting for taste. Its appeal is that it treats taste like expertise elsewhere. Most of us trust a film critic who has seen thousands of films over a friend who has seen five.","example":"Hume retells a story from Don Quixote. Two of Sancho’s kinsmen taste a cask of wine. One detects a slight taste of iron, the other of leather. They are mocked, until the cask is emptied and an old key on a leather strap is found at the bottom. Delicate taste detects what is really there.","history":"David Hume’s “Of the Standard of Taste” (1757) is the founding text, naming the qualities of a true judge. Earlier, Shaftesbury and Joseph Addison had linked taste to cultivation. In the Analects, Confucius judges one ancient music both perfectly beautiful and perfectly good, and another beautiful but not perfectly good, as a cultivated connoisseur. Jerrold Levinson and others still debate Hume’s standard.","misread":"It does not mean deferring to famous critics: real critics often fall short of the ideal, and the standard is what ideal judges would agree on.","variants":["Humean standard: the joint verdict of true judges.","Test of time: works that keep pleasing across ages and nations show real merit."],"reading":[{"title":"Hume’s Aesthetics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Taste (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetic-taste/"}]},"a1a|kant":{"summary":"A judgment of beauty rests on feeling, yet rightly demands everyone’s agreement, because it springs from mental capacities all humans share.","idea":"Kant’s view sits between subjectivism and realism. A judgment of beauty is not based on concepts or rules: you cannot prove a rose is beautiful. It rests on a pleasure you feel. Yet unlike enjoying a flavour, this pleasure is disinterested, free of desire or personal advantage, and it comes from capacities every human mind has. So you speak with a “universal voice” and expect agreement. The view denies that beauty is a measurable property and denies that it is mere private liking. Compare “I like mango ice cream” with “this symphony is beautiful”: only the second claims to speak for others.","example":"Kant’s contrast between the agreeable and the beautiful: someone who says Canary wine is agreeable happily adds “to me”, and no one quarrels. But someone who calls a building or poem beautiful and adds “to me” sounds absurd. Kant also used a flower, judged beautiful without any thought of what botanists say it is for.","history":"Immanuel Kant set out the view in the Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790), building on British writers on taste such as Hutcheson and Hume. It shaped Friedrich Schiller’s Letters on the Aesthetic Education of Man (1795) and much later German aesthetics. Twentieth-century formalists, and Hannah Arendt in her lectures on Kant’s political philosophy, revived his idea of a shared sense.","misread":"Kant does not claim everyone will in fact agree; he claims that judging something beautiful involves demanding, as if by right, that they should.","variants":["Free beauty: beauty judged without any concept of what the thing should be.","Adherent beauty: beauty judged relative to a thing’s purpose.","The sublime: pleasure in what overwhelms the senses, like storms or mountains."],"reading":[{"title":"Kant’s Aesthetics and Teleology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Immanuel Kant: Aesthetics (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/kantaest/"}]},"a1a|relative":{"summary":"Standards of taste are real but belong to traditions; judgments can be better or worse within a culture, never across all cultures.","idea":"Relativism says taste has genuine standards, but they are local. A gamelan musician, an opera singer and a rapper each work within practices that set what counts as skilful or beautiful, and inside those practices experts truly know more. What relativism denies is a neutral standpoint from which to rank the traditions themselves. Its appeal is respect: it avoids treating one culture’s tastes as everyone’s measure, while also avoiding “anything goes”. Consider food: a sushi chef and a pastry chef can each be judged excellent or poor, but asking which cuisine is simply better seems confused.","example":"Hume’s own concession is the classic case. He allowed that some differences in taste are blameless: a young person warms to passionate, amorous poetry, an older reader to wise reflection, and readers prefer the manners of their own age and country. Neither preference is a mistake, and no standard can decide between them.","history":"Ancient sceptics such as Sextus Empiricus collected conflicting customs and tastes. Hume’s “Of the Standard of Taste” (1757) allowed blameless differences due to age and culture. In the 20th century, anthropology of art and Barbara Herrnstein Smith’s Contingencies of Value (1988) gave relativism new support. Philosophers of language such as John MacFarlane now apply relativist semantics to taste words.","misread":"Relativism is not the view that no taste is better; inside a tradition, it allows real expertise and real mistakes.","variants":["Cultural relativism: standards are set by cultures or traditions.","Assessment relativism: whether a taste claim is true depends on the assessor’s standard (MacFarlane)."],"reading":[{"title":"Relativism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/relativism/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Taste (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetic-taste/"}]},"a1a|none":{"summary":"No taste is better than another; calling something beautiful only reports or reflects one’s own liking.","idea":"Subjectivism says judgments of beauty are about the judge. “This painting is beautiful” amounts to “I like this painting”, and since each person is the authority on their own likes, no one can be wrong. It denies any standard, natural, cultural or ideal, that could make one person’s taste better. Its appeal is modesty and fairness: it refuses to let experts, classes or cultures impose their preferences as truth, and it fits the fact that arguing about taste rarely changes minds. If you love a song your friends call cheesy, subjectivism says you have nothing to defend.","example":"The proverb de gustibus non est disputandum, “there is no disputing about taste”, is the slogan. A clear case: two friends leave a film, one moved to tears, the other bored. Subjectivism says each has reported their experience truly, and there is no further question of who saw the film rightly.","history":"Protagoras’s saying that “man is the measure of all things” is often read this way. Hume’s essay (1757) stated the subjectivist view clearly, as common opinion, before arguing against it. George Santayana’s The Sense of Beauty (1896) defined beauty in terms of pleasure. The view remains common in everyday thought, though few specialists in aesthetics defend it.","misread":"Subjectivism differs from non-cognitivism: subjectivists think “beautiful” truly or falsely describes the speaker’s feelings; non-cognitivists think it describes nothing.","variants":["Simple subjectivism: “beautiful” means “pleasing to me”.","Speaker relativism: a taste claim is true relative to the speaker’s own standard."],"reading":[{"title":"Aesthetic Taste (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetic-taste/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Judgment (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/"}]},"a1c|quasi":{"summary":"Aesthetic judgments express attitudes rather than facts, yet some responses are genuinely better: better informed, more attentive and more defensible.","idea":"Quasi-realism starts from non-cognitivism: calling a painting beautiful voices an attitude, not a description of a property. But it argues that we can still earn the right to call a judgment correct or mistaken, because attitudes can be criticised. A view formed after careful attention, with relevant knowledge and without bias, beats a snap reaction. So the view denies that beauty is out there in the world, yet also denies that all reactions are equal. Your first dismissal of jazz, before you knew how to hear improvisation, can rightly be called a mistake you later corrected.","example":"A useful test: picture your verdict on a poem, then picture a version of yourself who has read more poetry, paid closer attention and dropped a grudge against the poet. If that improved self would judge differently, your current verdict can be faulted, even though no fact about the poem is being described.","history":"Simon Blackburn developed quasi-realism for ethics in Spreading the Word (1984) and Essays in Quasi-Realism (1993), often comparing moral and aesthetic attitudes. Allan Gibbard’s Wise Choices, Apt Feelings (1990) offered a related norm-expressivism. Writers in aesthetics, including Cain Todd, have adapted the approach to taste. Hume’s ideal critic is often seen as an ancestor.","misread":"Quasi-realists are not secret realists: when they call a judgment true or mistaken, they take themselves to be endorsing or criticising attitudes, not reporting facts.","variants":["Quasi-realism (Blackburn): earning talk of truth and error for attitudes.","Norm-expressivism (Gibbard): judgments express acceptance of norms for feelings."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Judgment (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/"}]},"a1c|emotiv":{"summary":"Aesthetic judgments simply express feelings, so no response to beauty is better than another, only different.","idea":"Emotivism holds that “beautiful” is a word for voicing and sharing feeling, like “hooray”. Because feelings are not true or false, one person’s thrill at an opera and another’s boredom cannot be ranked as better or worse; they are just different reactions. It denies that aesthetic talk tracks facts and denies that any response is more correct. Disputes about art, on this view, are clashes of attitude, sometimes mixed with factual disputes about the work. Its appeal is clarity: it explains why taste arguments often stall, and it strips snobbery out of appreciation.","example":"Stevenson’s distinction between disagreement in belief and disagreement in attitude is the classic tool. Two people may agree on every fact about a pop song, its chords, lyrics and sales, and still disagree, because one likes it and one does not. That leftover disagreement is in attitude, and evidence cannot settle it.","history":"C. K. Ogden and I. A. Richards’s The Meaning of Meaning (1923) treated “beautiful” as an emotive use of language. A. J. Ayer’s Language, Truth and Logic (1936) held that aesthetic terms express feeling, so criticism can only point out facts about a work in the hope of stirring a feeling. Charles Stevenson’s Ethics and Language (1944) developed emotivism in detail.","misread":"Emotivists can still love art passionately; they deny only that their passion states a fact others are mistaken to lack.","variants":["Radical emotivism (Ayer): aesthetic words merely express feeling.","Persuasive emotivism (Stevenson): they also aim to change others’ attitudes."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Judgment (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/"}]},"a4|repr":{"summary":"Something is art because it imitates or represents things: people, scenes, actions or the world.","idea":"The mimetic theory says representing is what art does. A painting shows a harbour, a play shows a murder, a statue shows a god. It denies that decoration or pure pattern is the heart of art. Its appeal is historical fit: for most of human history, in most cultures, the arts people prized were mostly depictions, and we still praise art for being lifelike or true to its subject. Even a child’s drawing of her family is art in this sense: it holds a picture of something real.","example":"Pliny’s story of Zeuxis and Parrhasius is the classic. Zeuxis painted grapes so real that birds flew down to peck them. Parrhasius then invited Zeuxis to draw back the curtain over his own picture, but the curtain was the painting. Zeuxis admitted defeat: he had fooled birds, Parrhasius had fooled a painter.","history":"Plato’s Republic, Book X (4th century BCE), treated painting and poetry as imitation, likening art to holding up a mirror. Aristotle’s Poetics made mimesis central to tragedy. Charles Batteux’s The Fine Arts Reduced to a Single Principle (1746) defined the fine arts as imitation of beautiful nature. Xie He’s six principles of Chinese painting (6th century CE) included likeness to the object, though ranked below spirit resonance.","misread":"Mimesis need not mean copying appearances exactly; Aristotle thought tragedy imitates actions and kinds of people, and can show what might happen.","variants":["Imitation theory: art copies nature or appearances.","Representation theory: art depicts or stands for a subject, lifelike or not."],"reading":[{"title":"The Definition of Art (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-definition/"},{"title":"Aesthetics, Ancient (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ancient-aesthetics/"}]},"a4|express":{"summary":"Something is art because it expresses feeling: making it is how its maker clarifies and conveys an emotion.","idea":"Expression theory says art’s core is emotion given form. The artist does not copy the world but works out an inner state, often one they could not name beforehand, and embodies it in sound, paint or words so others can grasp it. It denies that skilful imitation or entertainment is enough for art. Its appeal is that it explains why art feels personal and why we value sincerity in it. A teenager who writes a song after a breakup is doing what this theory says all art does: discovering what the feeling is by giving it shape.","example":"Collingwood’s contrast between art and craft is the classic case. An advertiser or propagandist plans in advance which feelings to arouse in an audience, like an engineer working to a blueprint: that is craft. An artist starts with a vague, troubling feeling and only learns what it is by expressing it, as a poet finds out what she feels by writing.","history":"Romantic poets, such as Wordsworth in his 1800 Preface to Lyrical Ballads, tied poetry to powerful feeling. Leo Tolstoy’s What Is Art? (1897) defined art as passing on felt emotion. Benedetto Croce’s Aesthetic (1902) identified art with intuition and expression. R. G. Collingwood’s The Principles of Art (1938) gave the most developed version.","misread":"The theory does not mean art is self-indulgent venting; Collingwood held that merely venting or arousing feelings is exactly what art is not.","variants":["Transmission theory (Tolstoy): art infects others with the artist’s feeling.","Clarification theory (Collingwood): art is working out an unclear emotion.","Intuition-expression (Croce): art is the inner expression itself."],"reading":[{"title":"Collingwood’s Aesthetics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/collingwood-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Art and Emotion (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/art-and-emotion/"}]},"a4|aesthetic":{"summary":"Something is art because it was made to be experienced for its beauty or form, to reward perceptual and imaginative attention.","idea":"The aesthetic definition says art is marked by its purpose: it is made to give aesthetic experience, to be looked at, listened to or read for its own sake, not to be used. It denies that representation, emotion or social status is what makes art art. Its appeal is that it captures something shared by paintings, poems, dances and music across many cultures: they invite us to attend to how things look, sound or feel. A carved wooden spoon made only to stir soup is a tool; carved the same way to be admired on a shelf, it is closer to art.","example":"A kitchen chair and a sculpture of a chair can both be well shaped, but only one is made mainly to be contemplated. Defenders say this intention is why a cave painting, a Bach suite and a Song dynasty landscape scroll all count as art without needing a gallery.","history":"Kant’s Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790) linked fine art to free, disinterested enjoyment. Clive Bell’s Art (1914) and other formalists treated aesthetic response as the mark of art. Monroe Beardsley’s “An Aesthetic Definition of Art” (1983) gave the classic modern statement. Nick Zangwill’s Aesthetic Creation (2007) defends a related view.","misread":"The view does not require art to be pretty; aesthetic qualities include the harsh, tense, sombre and grotesque, not just the beautiful.","variants":["Intentional version (Beardsley): made with the intention of affording aesthetic experience.","Functional version: art is whatever serves an aesthetic function."],"reading":[{"title":"The Definition of Art (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-definition/"},{"title":"The Definition of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/definition-of-art/"},{"title":"Beardsley’s Aesthetics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beardsley-aesthetics/"}]},"a4|artworld":{"summary":"Something is art because of its place in the art world: its relation to artists, institutions, art history and theory.","idea":"These theories say no visible feature makes something art. What matters is a relation: how the object is presented, understood, or connected to earlier art. A urinal or a pile of bricks can be art in a gallery and junk in a skip. The views deny that beauty, skill or expression is required. Their appeal is that they handle twentieth-century art, where many works deliberately look like ordinary things. Even the idea that “it’s art if an artist says so” fits here, since it makes art depend on who presents it, not on how it looks.","example":"Duchamp’s Fountain (1917), a mass-produced urinal signed “R. Mutt” and submitted to an exhibition in New York, is the classic test. Nothing about its look changed when it became art. Its status came from being put forward within the practices of artists and exhibitions, and from the debate it started.","history":"Marcel Duchamp’s readymades, from 1913 on, set the problem. Arthur Danto’s essay “The Artworld” (1964) argued that seeing something as art requires an atmosphere of theory and history. George Dickie’s Art and the Aesthetic (1974) built the institutional theory. Jerrold Levinson’s “Defining Art Historically” (1979) offered the historical alternative.","misread":"These theories do not say gallery owners decide on a whim; most require a real practice with reasons, roles and history behind it.","variants":["Institutional theory (Dickie): art status comes from presentation within the art world.","Artworld theory (Danto): art embodies meanings made possible by art history and theory.","Historical definition (Levinson): art is intended to be regarded as earlier art was."],"reading":[{"title":"The Definition of Art (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-definition/"},{"title":"The Definition of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/definition-of-art/"}]},"a4|cluster":{"summary":"No single feature makes something art; anything with enough of the typical marks, like beauty, expression, skill and meaning, counts.","idea":"The cluster account lists features that count toward something being art: beauty, expressing emotion, intellectual challenge, complex form, saying something, originality, skill, belonging to an art form, being made to be art. None is required, and none is enough alone, but an object with enough of them is art. It rejects the search for one essence while still giving guidance. Its appeal is flexibility. Think of how doctors diagnose some conditions: a patient need not show every symptom, but enough of them together settles the case.","example":"Hard cases test it. A Japanese tea ceremony has beauty, skill, a tradition and expressive meaning, but produces no object for display; a cluster account can count it as art. A glorious sunset has beauty but none of the human-made features, so it does not qualify.","history":"The idea of cluster concepts draws on Wittgenstein’s family resemblance and John Searle’s “Proper Names” (1958). Berys Gaut’s “‘Art’ as a Cluster Concept” (2000) applied it to art with a list of ten criteria. Denis Dutton’s The Art Instinct (2009) offered a similar list of features found in art across human cultures.","misread":"A cluster account is not “anything goes”; it gives definite criteria, and only denies that any one of them is necessary.","variants":["Gaut’s cluster account: ten criteria, none necessary, enough jointly sufficient.","Dutton’s cluster criteria: features of art found across human cultures.","Disjunctive definitions: art is whatever meets one of several listed conditions."],"reading":[{"title":"The Definition of Art (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-definition/"},{"title":"The Definition of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/definition-of-art/"}]},"a4|open":{"summary":"Art cannot be defined by any set of features, because “art” is an open concept that artists keep stretching with new kinds of work.","idea":"Anti-essentialism says the search for a definition of art is a mistake. Artworks resemble each other in overlapping ways, but no feature is shared by all of them, and new works keep breaking old patterns. Deciding whether a new case counts is a fresh judgment, often a debate, not the application of a rule. It denies that definitions by imitation, expression or form can succeed. Its appeal is that it fits modern art history, where movement after movement, from Impressionism to video art, was first called “not art” by someone.","example":"Wittgenstein’s games in Philosophical Investigations are the model. Board games, ball games, card games and patience share no single feature: not winning, not competition, not skill. They form a family of resemblances. Morris Weitz argued art is the same, so a puzzling new work is settled by decision, not by checking a definition.","history":"Ludwig Wittgenstein’s Philosophical Investigations (1953) introduced family resemblance. Morris Weitz’s “The Role of Theory in Aesthetics” (1956) applied it to art, arguing that “art” is open. Paul Ziff (1953) and W. B. Gallie (1956) made related arguments. Maurice Mandelbaum’s reply (1965) led to relational theories, but anti-essentialism remains a live view.","misread":"Anti-essentialists do not say we cannot tell art from non-art; we often can, just not by applying a fixed definition.","variants":["Open concept view (Weitz): new cases call for decisions, not definitions.","Family resemblance: overlapping similarities with no common core.","Essentially contested concept (Gallie): “art” is permanently disputed."],"reading":[{"title":"The Definition of Art (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-definition/"},{"title":"Wittgenstein’s Aesthetics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wittgenstein-aesthetics/"}]},"a4a|status":{"summary":"An artwork is an artifact presented to an art-world public, and that presentation within the art world gives it the status of art.","idea":"The institutional theory explains art status socially. Just as two people become married through a ceremony, not through any change in their bodies, an object becomes art by being put forward within an established practice of artists, curators, critics and audiences. It denies that art needs any special look, feeling or quality. Its appeal is that it explains readymades and conceptual art easily, and it treats art as what it plainly is in modern life: a social institution. A rock placed on a plinth at an art fair can be art; the same rock in a garden is not.","example":"Dickie discussed paintings by Betsy, a chimpanzee at the Baltimore Zoo. Shown at a natural history museum as animal behaviour, they are not art. Hung in an art museum by someone acting for the art world, he argued, they could be. The paint is the same; only the presentation differs.","history":"Arthur Danto’s “The Artworld” (1964) inspired it, though Danto rejected the institutional reading. George Dickie set it out in Aesthetics: An Introduction (1971) and Art and the Aesthetic (1974), then revised it in The Art Circle (1984), dropping the idea of conferring status. Richard Wollheim and Monroe Beardsley were among its critics.","misread":"Dickie’s later version needs no official body or committee; anyone working within the practice, even a lone artist, can present something as art.","variants":["Early institutional theory (1974): status conferred on behalf of the art world.","Art circle theory (1984): art defined by a network of roles and practices."],"reading":[{"title":"The Definition of Art (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-definition/"},{"title":"The Definition of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/definition-of-art/"}]},"a4a|meaning":{"summary":"An artwork is about something and embodies that meaning, which only an art-historical and theoretical context makes possible.","idea":"Danto’s theory says artworks are embodied meanings. A work has a subject, takes a point of view on it, and presents it through its physical form, and art history and theory are what let an object carry such meaning. It denies that art is defined by looks or by institutional say-so. Its appeal is that it explains why two identical objects can differ as art: one has something to say and one does not. A canvas deliberately slashed by Lucio Fontana is about the picture surface; a canvas torn in shipping is about nothing.","example":"Danto’s red squares open The Transfiguration of the Commonplace. Imagine identical red canvases: one titled after the Israelites crossing the Red Sea, one called Kierkegaard’s Mood, one a still life of a red tablecloth, one a primed canvas never painted. They look the same, yet they are different works, and one is not art at all.","history":"Arthur Danto (1924 to 2013) raised the problem in “The Artworld” (1964), prompted by Andy Warhol’s Brillo Box. He developed the theory in The Transfiguration of the Commonplace (1981), After the End of Art (1997) and What Art Is (2013). He also argued that art history reached an “end” once art turned into a kind of philosophy.","misread":"Danto did not say art is whatever theorists declare; theory makes certain meanings possible, but the work itself must embody them.","variants":["Embodied meaning: artworks are about something and embody it.","End of art thesis: after Warhol, art need not look any special way."],"reading":[{"title":"The Definition of Art (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-definition/"},{"title":"The Definition of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/definition-of-art/"}]},"a4a|history":{"summary":"Something is art if its maker seriously intends it to be regarded in some way that earlier artworks were correctly regarded.","idea":"The historical definition ties art to art’s past. A new object becomes art when its maker intends people to treat it as they have treated earlier art: to contemplate its form, find its meaning, or feel what it expresses. It denies that an institution must confer status, and denies that a work must have any particular look. Its appeal is that it explains how art changes yet stays one practice: each new work reaches back to old ones. A teenager who builds a strange object in her room, meaning it to be looked at as sculptures are, has made art even if no gallery ever sees it.","example":"A standard test case: a recluse arranges stones in her yard, intending them to be looked at the way people look at sculpture, and never shows anyone. On the historical view she has made art. A similar pile left by a landslide, or stacked only for storage, is not art, since no one intends it to be regarded as art has been.","history":"Jerrold Levinson proposed the definition in “Defining Art Historically” (1979) and refined it in “Refining Art Historically” (1989). Noël Carroll’s narrative approach, which links new works to old through stories of artistic development, and Robert Stecker’s historical functionalism in Artworks (1997) are related historical accounts.","misread":"The maker need not know art history in detail; she need only intend the object to be regarded in a way art has actually been regarded.","variants":["Intentional-historical definition (Levinson): art is tied to past art by its maker’s intention.","Narrative account (Carroll): new art is identified by stories linking it to past art.","Historical functionalism (Stecker): art fulfils functions that art forms have had."],"reading":[{"title":"The Definition of Art (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-definition/"},{"title":"The Definition of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/definition-of-art/"}]},"a9|yes":{"summary":"Whether something is art depends on the object and what it offers, so an AI image can be art even with no artist.","idea":"The product view says art status lives in the thing, not in its backstory. If an image is striking, well composed and rewards attention, then it is art, whether a person, a machine or an accident produced it. It denies that a human maker with intentions is required. Its appeal is that we judge art by meeting it: you can be moved by a song on the radio before you know who wrote it. If an unlabelled AI image hangs beside human paintings and visitors admire it just as much, the product view says they are right to.","example":"In 2018 Christie’s sold Portrait of Edmond de Belamy, an image generated by an algorithm and printed on canvas by the French collective Obvious, for $432,500. It was signed with part of a mathematical formula instead of a name. Buyers treated it as art though no one painted the face; the product view says the sale recognised what the object offers.","history":"The view draws on aesthetic and formalist definitions of art, such as Clive Bell’s Art (1914) and Monroe Beardsley’s work, which focus on what a work offers to experience. It was debated around Harold Cohen’s AARON program from the 1970s and became urgent with image generators such as DALL·E (2021) and Midjourney (2022). Experimental philosophers have since surveyed public intuitions.","misread":"The product view does not claim the AI is an artist or has intentions; it says the image can be art without anyone being its artist.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Philosophy of Digital Art (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/digital-art/"},{"title":"The Definition of Art (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-definition/"}]},"a9|tool":{"summary":"AI is a tool like a camera; the person who prompts, selects and presents the image is its artist.","idea":"The tool view says image generators extend human creativity rather than replace it. The person chooses the subject, writes and rewrites prompts, picks among outputs, edits and presents the result, so the work is theirs, as a photograph belongs to the photographer, not the camera. It denies both that the AI is an author and that the image lacks one. Its appeal is precedent: new tools, from oil paint to synthesisers to Photoshop, were accused of doing the artist’s work, yet artists absorbed them. A DJ who builds a track from samples is still credited as its artist.","example":"In 2022 Jason Allen won first prize for digital art at the Colorado State Fair with Théâtre D’opéra Spatial, made with Midjourney; he reported many hours of prompting and editing. The tool view counts him the artist, much as the US Supreme Court in 1884 counted Napoleon Sarony the author of his photograph of Oscar Wilde.","history":"The 19th-century debate over photography is the model. Baudelaire attacked photography in 1859, but in Burrow-Giles Lithographic Co. v. Sarony (1884) the US Supreme Court held that a photographer can be an author through creative choices. Artists such as Harold Cohen and Refik Anadol treat software as part of their practice, and Aaron Hertzmann’s “Can Computers Create Art?” (2018) defends the tool view.","misread":"The tool view need not say every prompter is an artist; as with photography, a quick snapshot involves little authorship and careful, sustained work much more.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Creativity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/creativity/"},{"title":"Philosophy of Digital Art (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/digital-art/"}]},"a9|ai":{"summary":"An AI that makes creative choices no human made can itself be the artist of the image it produces.","idea":"This view says creativity is a matter of what a system does, not what it is made of. If an AI produces something new, surprising and valuable, settling composition, colour and style that its user never specified, then it is acting as an artist. The one-line prompt is like a commission: the person who orders a portrait is not its painter. It denies that only humans, or only conscious beings, can be authors. Its appeal is consistency: we would not deny authorship to an alien painter just because its brain was built differently from ours.","example":"The patron analogy: a Renaissance duke who ordered “a Madonna with saints” did not paint the altarpiece; the painter did. Defenders of AI authorship argue that a one-line prompt is the same kind of commission, so the system that turned it into a finished image is the closer candidate for the title of artist.","history":"Ada Lovelace (1843) argued that the Analytical Engine could originate nothing. Alan Turing’s “Computing Machinery and Intelligence” (1950) answered this “Lady Lovelace’s objection”. Margaret Boden’s The Creative Mind (1990) argued computers can be creative. Simon Colton’s program The Painting Fool and Stephen Thaler’s attempts to register works made by his system carried the debate into practice and the courts.","misread":"AI authorship need not claim the AI is conscious or feels anything; some defenders think creative choice, not inner experience, is what authorship requires.","variants":["Boden’s three kinds of creativity: combinational, exploratory and transformational."],"reading":[{"title":"Creativity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/creativity/"},{"title":"Artificial Intelligence (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/art-inte/"}]},"a9|no":{"summary":"Art needs a maker who means or feels something; an AI image from a brief prompt has no such maker, so it is not art.","idea":"The intention requirement says art is communication between minds: someone makes something to say, show or express something. The AI system has no thoughts or feelings to communicate, and a one-line prompt contributes too little to make the human its author. So the image, however striking, is like frost patterns on a window: worth looking at, but not a work of art. It denies that looking like art makes something art. Its appeal is that it explains why we care who made things: a love letter loses its point if you learn a machine wrote it.","example":"Steven Knapp and Walter Benn Michaels’s wave poem is the classic case. Imagine waves on a beach leaving marks that spell out a stanza of Wordsworth. Is it a poem? Most say no: with no writer, the marks only look like words. Critics of AI art argue a generated image is in the same position.","history":"Intention-based accounts of art run from Collingwood’s The Principles of Art (1938) to Levinson’s historical definition (1979) and Paisley Livingston’s Art and Intention (2005). Sean Dorrance Kelly’s essay in MIT Technology Review (2019) and Ted Chiang’s “Why A.I. Isn’t Going to Make Art” in The New Yorker (2024) applied the idea to AI.","misread":"The view does not deny that AI can help make art; a person who works heavily with the tool, making many real choices, may still be an artist.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Creativity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/creativity/"},{"title":"The Definition of Art (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-definition/"}]},"a5|actual":{"summary":"A work means what its author actually intended, provided the words or images can bear that meaning.","idea":"Actual intentionalism treats a poem like an utterance: someone is saying something, and understanding it means grasping what she meant. In its common moderate form, her intention decides between readings the text allows, but cannot make words mean what they cannot mean. It denies that published words float free of their maker. Its appeal is that it matches ordinary conversation: if a friend’s text message is ambiguous, you ask what they meant, and their answer settles it. Here the diary resolves an ambiguity the poem already allowed.","example":"Humpty Dumpty in Lewis Carroll’s Through the Looking-Glass says that “glory” means “a nice knock-down argument”. He meant it, but the word cannot carry that meaning. Moderate intentionalists use this case to mark their limit: intention picks among possible meanings; it cannot create impossible ones.","history":"E. D. Hirsch’s Validity in Interpretation (1967) defended the author’s meaning as the only stable standard. Steven Knapp and Walter Benn Michaels’s “Against Theory” (1982) argued that meaning just is intention. Noël Carroll, Paisley Livingston and Robert Stecker developed moderate versions. In early China, Mencius urged readers to use their understanding to trace the poet’s intent.","misread":"Actual intentionalists do not think authors can make a work mean anything; most say intentions count only when the text supports them.","variants":["Extreme actual intentionalism: the work means whatever the author intended.","Moderate actual intentionalism: intention counts when the work can bear it."],"reading":[{"title":"Art and Interpretation (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/art-and-interpretation/"}]},"a5|hypo":{"summary":"A work means what an ideal, well-informed audience would best hypothesise the author intended, using public evidence only.","idea":"Hypothetical intentionalism keeps the idea that a work is someone’s utterance but moves the standard from the author’s private mind to the best public reading of it. An ideal reader knows the text, the language, the genre, and the author’s public life and times, and forms the best hypothesis about what was meant. Private documents like a secret diary do not count. It denies both that actual intention rules and that intention is irrelevant. Its appeal is that literature is published for an audience, and an audience can only work with what was offered.","example":"Levinson leans on a distinction from philosophy of language: what a speaker meant versus what her utterance means in context. If a speaker at a public event privately intends an irony no listener could detect, her speech still says what it plainly says. A published poem is a public utterance in this sense, so a hidden diary cannot change it.","history":"William Tolhurst’s “On What a Text Is and How It Means” (1979) introduced the view. Jerrold Levinson developed it in “Intention and Interpretation in Literature” (1996). Alexander Nehamas’s idea of a “postulated author” (1981) and Gregory Currie’s “fictional author” are related ideas. Noël Carroll has been its leading critic.","misread":"It is not a guess we would drop on learning the author’s real intention; the best public hypothesis is itself the standard of meaning.","variants":["Utterance-meaning version (Levinson): the best hypothesis of an informed audience.","Postulated author (Nehamas): meaning attributed to an author implied by the work."],"reading":[{"title":"Art and Interpretation (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/art-and-interpretation/"}]},"a5|text":{"summary":"A work’s meaning lies in its words and the conventions of language, not in what the author intended, publicly or privately.","idea":"Anti-intentionalism says that once a poem is published, its meaning is fixed by its language: grammar, word meanings, literary conventions, and the evidence inside the text. Evidence about the author’s mind, from diaries, interviews or letters, is irrelevant unless it shows up in the work. It denies that authors have special authority over what their works mean. Its appeal is that it keeps interpretation public and checkable: anyone can examine the words, but no one can inspect a dead poet’s mind. If you mistype a word in an essay, your reader rightly reads what you wrote.","example":"Wimsatt and Beardsley discussed T. S. Eliot’s notes to The Waste Land, which explain the poem’s sources. Should the notes guide reading? If they work as part of the poem, they are evidence like any other text; if they only report what Eliot had in mind, they cannot fix what the poem means.","history":"W. K. Wimsatt and Monroe Beardsley’s “The Intentional Fallacy” (1946) named the view. It was central to the New Criticism of the 1930s to 1950s, with I. A. Richards, Cleanth Brooks and others practising close reading. Beardsley’s Aesthetics (1958) and The Possibility of Criticism (1970) defended it philosophically.","misread":"Anti-intentionalists do not say meaning is whatever readers like; they hold that a text’s language yields determinate, often correct readings.","variants":["New Critical formalism: meaning found by close reading of the text alone.","Conventionalism: meaning set by public rules of language and genre."],"reading":[{"title":"Art and Interpretation (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/art-and-interpretation/"},{"title":"Beardsley’s Aesthetics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beardsley-aesthetics/"}]},"a5|reader":{"summary":"Readers create meaning; a work supports many valid interpretations and no single one, not even the author’s, is the correct one.","idea":"Reader-response theory says meaning happens when a reader meets a text, shaped by that reader’s experience, culture and expectations. The text guides but does not dictate. So the lost-love reading and the grandmother reading are both genuine meanings of the poem, and the diary adds a reading rather than ending debate. It denies that interpretation is the discovery of one hidden correct meaning. Its appeal is that it fits how art actually lives: Shakespeare means one thing to Elizabethan audiences, another to a classroom in Lagos, another to you. A song from your first heartbreak means that to you.","example":"Stanley Fish’s classroom experiment is the classic. A list of linguists’ names was left on the board from one class. Fish told his next class, of poetry students, that it was a religious poem. They interpreted it richly, finding symbols and structure. Fish concluded that interpretive communities, not texts alone, produce meaning.","history":"Hans-Georg Gadamer’s Truth and Method (1960) saw understanding as a fusion of horizons. Roland Barthes’s “The Death of the Author” (1967) and Michel Foucault’s “What Is an Author?” (1969) challenged authorial authority. Wolfgang Iser and Hans Robert Jauss developed reception theory in Germany, and Stanley Fish’s Is There a Text in This Class? (1980) argued for interpretive communities.","misread":"Most reader-response theorists do not say every reading is as good as any other; Fish held that interpretive communities constrain what counts as a reading.","variants":["Death of the author (Barthes): a text’s unity lies in its reader, not its writer.","Reception theory (Iser, Jauss): readers fill gaps against a horizon of expectations.","Interpretive communities (Fish): shared practices produce meaning."],"reading":[{"title":"Art and Interpretation (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/art-and-interpretation/"}]},"a7|same":{"summary":"A painting’s aesthetic value depends only on how it looks; discovering it is a forgery changes its price and story, not its quality.","idea":"Aesthetic empiricism says the value of a work as a picture lies in what can be seen in it. If experts admired its light, composition and colour, those features are still there after the fraud is exposed. What changes is its market value, its historical interest, and our feelings about deception. It denies that facts about origin can change aesthetic value unless they show up in perception. Its appeal is that it resists snobbery: if a meal tasted wonderful, learning the cook was unknown should not make it taste worse.","example":"Van Meegeren’s The Supper at Emmaus (1937) is the standard case. The leading Vermeer scholar Abraham Bredius hailed it as a supreme Vermeer, and the Boijmans Museum in Rotterdam bought it. After van Meegeren confessed in 1945, its standing collapsed, though not a single brushstroke had changed.","history":"Monroe Beardsley’s Aesthetics (1958) held that criticism should judge only what is in the work. Alfred Lessing’s “What Is Wrong with a Forgery?” (1965) argued that the fault of forgery is not aesthetic, though forgeries lack originality. Clive Bell’s formalism supports the view. Denis Dutton’s collection The Forger’s Art (1983) gathered the debate.","misread":"Empiricists do not approve of forgery; they say its wrong is moral and historical, not a change in how good the picture is to look at.","variants":["Strict empiricism: only perceptible features matter to aesthetic value.","Moderate formalism (Zangwill): many, though not all, aesthetic features are purely perceptual."],"reading":[{"title":"Aesthetic Formalism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetic-formalism/"},{"title":"Beardsley’s Aesthetics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beardsley-aesthetics/"}]},"a7|learn":{"summary":"Knowing a painting is a fake gives reason to look again and trains the eye to see real differences, so the fake can prove worse.","idea":"Goodman’s view stays with perception but argues that perception is not fixed. Knowing that two pictures differ, even when you cannot yet see how, changes how you should look, and over time it changes what you can see. So a forgery that once looked identical may not be visually equal after all; we just lacked the trained eye. It denies that how a painting looks to you now fixes its aesthetic properties. Its appeal is realism about learning: a beginner cannot tell two wines apart, but after being told they differ, and tasting again, often can.","example":"Goodman’s own thought experiment: Rembrandt’s Lucretia hangs beside a perfect copy, and you cannot tell them apart. Is there any aesthetic difference for you? Goodman said yes: knowing which is which shapes how you look now, and tells you that future looking, with a trained eye, may reveal differences.","history":"Nelson Goodman set out the argument in “Art and Authenticity”, chapter three of Languages of Art (1968). There he also distinguished autographic arts like painting, which can be forged, from allographic arts like music, where any correct performance is genuine. The argument drew wide debate, much of it collected in Denis Dutton’s The Forger’s Art (1983).","misread":"Goodman does not say the fake is worse simply because it is fake; knowing it is one helps us discover visible differences that make it worse.","variants":["Autographic versus allographic arts: only arts like painting, not music, can be forged."],"reading":[{"title":"Goodman’s Aesthetics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/goodman-aesthetics/"}]},"a7|achieve":{"summary":"A painting is also an achievement; a forgery that imitates an invented style is a smaller feat, so it is worse as art.","idea":"The achievement view says we judge artworks partly as things someone did. Vermeer solved problems of light and space that no one had solved; a forger working centuries later solved an easier problem, copying a known solution. So even if both canvases look the same, they differ as achievements, and that counts in their artistic value. It denies that art is only about the surface. Its appeal is that we judge other performances this way: a climber who first finds a route achieves more than one who later follows the chalk marks.","example":"A student copies Beethoven’s Fifth Symphony note for note and enters it in a composition contest. Every bar is excellent, yet no judge would credit the student with Beethoven’s achievement. Achievement theorists say a forgery in an invented style is a subtler version of the same thing: brilliant execution, borrowed invention.","history":"Kant’s account of genius (1790), as the talent that gives the rule to art, stressed originality. Denis Dutton’s “Artistic Crimes” (1979) argued that artworks are performances to be judged as achievements. Gregory Currie’s An Ontology of Art (1989) and David Davies’s Art as Performance (2004) treat works as actions rather than mere objects.","misread":"The view need not say forgeries are ugly; it says that, as achievements, they are lesser, which lowers their overall value as art.","variants":["Performance view (Dutton): art judged as human performance.","Action-type hypothesis (Currie): works are types of creative action.","Art as performance (Davies): works are the generative acts of artists."],"reading":[{"title":"The Value of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/value-of-art/"},{"title":"Creativity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/creativity/"}]},"a7|context":{"summary":"What a work is, and what qualities it has, depends on who made it, when and why; a fake is a different work.","idea":"Contextualism holds that artworks are not just physical objects but objects in a historical setting. A painting’s boldness, originality or wit depends on what came before and around it. The same brushstrokes are a daring innovation in 1660 and an echo in 1937. So the forgery is not the same work a real Vermeer would be, and has different artistic properties even if it looks identical. It denies that perceptible features fix what a work is. Its appeal shows in everyday speech: “I’m fine” means different things after a funeral and after a nap.","example":"Borges’s story “Pierre Menard, Author of the Quixote” (1939) is the classic case. Menard, a 20th-century French writer, composes chapters word for word identical to Cervantes’s novel. The narrator claims Menard’s text is richer and stranger, since the same words mean something else from a modern writer. Danto used the story to show context makes the work.","history":"Kendall Walton’s “Categories of Art” (1970) showed that a work’s perceived qualities depend on the category it belongs to. Jerrold Levinson’s “What a Musical Work Is” (1980) tied works to their composer and time. Arthur Danto’s The Transfiguration of the Commonplace (1981) used indiscernible pairs. Gregory Currie’s An Ontology of Art (1989) is related.","misread":"Contextualists do not say looks are unimportant; they say how a work looks takes its meaning and value from the context in which it was made.","variants":["Categorial contextualism (Walton): qualities depend on the work’s correct category.","Ontological contextualism (Levinson): works are structures as indicated by a maker at a time."],"reading":[{"title":"Aesthetic Formalism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetic-formalism/"},{"title":"The Value of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/value-of-art/"}]},"a2|onlyform":{"summary":"A work’s greatness as art lies only in its form: how its lines, colours, sounds or words are arranged.","idea":"Formalism says subject matter, message and moral lessons are irrelevant to artistic greatness. What makes a painting great is the arrangement of shapes and colours; what makes music great is the pattern of sounds in time. A great still life is not great because apples are interesting. It denies that representing or teaching adds artistic value. Its appeal is that it explains why the same subject can be painted brilliantly or badly, and why Cézanne’s few apples on a table can outrank a vast, crowded battle scene. Think of a stunning abstract rug: nothing depicted, everything arranged.","example":"Clive Bell’s treatment of William Powell Frith’s Paddington Station (1862) is the classic. Frith’s crowded railway scene is full of stories and social detail, but Bell argued it lacks significant form, so it is not a work of art at all, however interesting as a document of its time.","history":"Kant’s idea of free beauty (1790) is a root. Eduard Hanslick’s On the Musically Beautiful (1854) argued that music’s content is form. Clive Bell’s Art (1914) and Roger Fry’s Vision and Design (1920) defended significant form. Clement Greenberg championed formal values in modernist painting. Nick Zangwill defends a moderate version today.","misread":"Formalists do not deny that art often has subjects; they deny that the subject is what makes it great as art.","variants":["Extreme formalism: only form matters to artistic value.","Moderate formalism (Zangwill): some works’ value is purely formal, others’ not.","Musical formalism (Hanslick): music’s content is form moving in sound."],"reading":[{"title":"Aesthetic Formalism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetic-formalism/"}]},"a2|exper":{"summary":"A work is great to the extent that it offers a rich, rewarding experience to someone who attends to it with understanding.","idea":"This view places artistic value in the experience a work makes possible. The better the work, the more it rewards attention: more to notice, more unity among its parts, more intensity, more to return to. It denies that a work’s value lies in its usefulness, its message taken alone, or its price. Its appeal is that it explains why value is tied to engaging with art rather than reading about it. A plot summary of a great novel is worth very little; the experience of reading the novel is where the value lies.","example":"Beardsley’s three general canons of criticism are the standard tools. Judging a symphony, a critic asks: Is it unified, holding together as one experience? Is it intense, with strong qualities like tension or joy? Is it complex, with much to follow? The more of all three, the greater its aesthetic value.","history":"Kant and the British theorists of taste prepared the ground. John Dewey’s Art as Experience (1934) placed art’s value in heightened experience. Monroe Beardsley’s Aesthetics (1958) gave the canons of unity, intensity and complexity. Malcolm Budd’s Values of Art (1995) located artistic value in the experience a work offers.","misread":"The view does not measure value by how much people enjoy a work; it concerns the experience it offers to someone who properly understands it.","variants":["Beardsley’s instrumentalism: value is the capacity to produce aesthetic experience.","Budd’s view: value is the intrinsic value of the experience a work offers."],"reading":[{"title":"Aesthetic Experience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-experience/"},{"title":"The Value of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/value-of-art/"}]},"a2|feeling":{"summary":"A work is great when it expresses a feeling truly and clearly, so that audiences come to know and share it.","idea":"On this view the measure of art is emotional truth. A great work captures a feeling precisely, sincerely and fully, so that you understand what it is to feel that way, sometimes better than your own life taught you. It denies that technical brilliance or beautiful form is enough. Its appeal is that it explains why some simple works move us deeply while some polished works leave us cold. A blues song can say more about grief than a long essay, because it lets you feel grief’s shape from the inside.","example":"Susanne Langer’s account of music is a classic illustration. Music does not express the composer’s mood the way a cry does, she argued; its patterns of tension, release, motion and rest share the shape of emotional life. A great funeral march does not report grief but gives grief its form in sound.","history":"Leo Tolstoy’s What Is Art? (1897) judged art by the sincerity, clarity and individuality of the feeling it passes on. Benedetto Croce and R. G. Collingwood tied art’s value to successful expression. Susanne Langer’s Feeling and Form (1953) treated art as forms of feeling. Jenefer Robinson’s Deeper than Reason (2005) is a recent defence.","misread":"Expressivism need not mean the artist must feel what the work expresses; many versions locate the feeling in the work, not in the artist’s biography.","variants":["Tolstoy’s test: sincerity, clarity and individuality of transmitted feeling.","Langer’s symbolism: art presents forms of feeling."],"reading":[{"title":"Art and Emotion (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/art-and-emotion/"},{"title":"Collingwood’s Aesthetics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/collingwood-aesthetics/"}]},"a2|insight":{"summary":"A work is great largely because of what it reveals: true and important understanding of life, people or the world.","idea":"Cognitivism says art is a way of knowing. A great novel can show what jealousy does to a marriage, a painting can teach you to see light differently, and a tragedy can reveal the limits of human control. This understanding is part of what makes the work great as art, not a side benefit. It denies that art is mere pleasure or pure form. Its appeal is that it explains why we take art seriously and why people say a book changed how they see things. After a war memoir, you may understand fear as no statistic could teach you.","example":"Oscar Wilde’s claim in “The Decay of Lying” (1891) is a playful classic: Londoners did not really see their fogs until painters and poets showed them the fogs’ mysterious beauty. Art, on this picture, does not just copy the world but teaches us to perceive it.","history":"Aristotle’s Poetics (4th century BCE) held that poetry is more philosophical than history, since it shows the universal. Hegel’s lectures on aesthetics (1820s) saw art as a way of grasping truth. Nelson Goodman’s Languages of Art (1968) treated aesthetics as part of epistemology. Iris Murdoch, Martha Nussbaum and Berys Gaut are key modern defenders.","misread":"Cognitivism does not reduce art to lessons or slogans; the knowledge can be of how things feel, look or matter, not only statable facts.","variants":["Propositional cognitivism: art conveys true claims.","Non-propositional cognitivism: art gives perception, know-how or acquaintance.","Moral cognitivism: art refines moral understanding."],"reading":[{"title":"Art and Epistemology (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/art-and-epistemology/"},{"title":"The Value of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/value-of-art/"}]},"a2|skill":{"summary":"A work is great because of the skill, daring and originality it took to make: greatness as artistic achievement.","idea":"This view judges artworks partly by what their makers did. Overcoming technical difficulty, solving artistic problems in a new way, and taking risks that pay off all count toward greatness. It denies that a work’s value can be read off its surface alone. Its appeal is that it fits widespread practice: across cultures, people admire virtuosity, and art history celebrates firsts, such as the invention of linear perspective. We admire a jazz solo more once we know it was invented on the spot rather than written out and memorised.","example":"Vasari’s story of Giotto’s O is the classic. Asked by a papal envoy for a drawing to prove his talent, Giotto is said to have drawn a perfect circle freehand with one turn of his arm. The envoy felt mocked, but the Pope understood what such control meant. Skill itself was the masterpiece.","history":"Giorgio Vasari’s Lives of the Artists (1550) praised artists for invention and difficulty overcome. Kant’s account of genius (1790) prized originality. Denis Dutton’s “Artistic Crimes” (1979) and The Art Instinct (2009) defended achievement as central to art. David Davies’s Art as Performance (2004) is a related philosophical view.","misread":"The view is not only about technical polish; originality and daring count too, so a simple-looking work can be a great achievement.","variants":["Performance view (Dutton): artworks as displays of skill and achievement.","Originality view: being first to solve an artistic problem adds value."],"reading":[{"title":"The Value of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/value-of-art/"},{"title":"Creativity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/creativity/"}]},"a2|moral":{"summary":"Great art is great because of what it does for people: shaping character, binding communities and moving people toward justice.","idea":"This view measures art by its effects on persons and societies. Art can teach virtue, train emotions, bring people together in shared feeling, or expose injustice, and the greatest works do these things powerfully and well. It denies that art is a sealed world of form, separate from life. Its appeal is that art clearly has social power: anthems, hymns and protest songs all shape people. Think of how a single song, sung together in a stadium, can make thousands of strangers feel like one community.","example":"The Confucian debate with Mozi is the classic case. Mozi condemned elaborate court music as a waste of the people’s resources. Xunzi replied in his “Discourse on Music” that music, by giving form to feeling, makes people peaceable and unites rulers and subjects, so the right music is necessary to a good society.","history":"Confucius’s Analects and Xunzi’s essay on music (3rd century BCE) tied music to moral cultivation. Plato’s Republic set rules for poetry and music. Friedrich Schiller’s Letters on the Aesthetic Education of Man (1795) saw art as preparing people for freedom. Tolstoy’s What Is Art? (1897) prized art that unites people. John Dewey and Marxist critics stressed art’s social roles.","misread":"The view need not mean art should preach; many defenders think art does moral work best through feeling and form, not explicit lessons.","variants":["Confucian view: music and ritual cultivate character and social harmony.","Tolstoyan view: the best art unites all people in shared feeling.","Socialist realism: art should portray society in its revolutionary development."],"reading":[{"title":"Xunzi (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/xunzi/"},{"title":"Ethical Criticism of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ethical-criticism-of-art/"}]},"a2|plural":{"summary":"Works of art are great for many different reasons; no single feature makes all great art great.","idea":"Pluralism says artistic value comes in many kinds: formal beauty, emotional power, insight, skill, originality, moral vision, wit. Different works excel in different ways, and a theory that picks one value will misjudge many masterpieces. It denies the monist claim that one thing underlies all artistic value. Its appeal is that it fits the variety of art we love. A great comedy and a great requiem are both great, but not for the same reasons; judging the requiem by how funny it is, or the comedy by its solemnity, would be absurd.","example":"Film critics praising Hitchcock’s Vertigo show pluralism at work. They cite its formal design, its insight into obsession, its craft in colour and music, its emotional pull and its influence. No single one of these explains its standing; together they do, and other great films stand on quite different combinations.","history":"Pluralism draws on value pluralism in ethics, associated with Isaiah Berlin, who argued that genuine values are many and can conflict. In aesthetics, Berys Gaut and Robert Stecker, in Artworks (1997), hold that artistic value has many sources. Pluralists often argue against monists such as Malcolm Budd, whose Values of Art (1995) seeks a unifying core.","misread":"Pluralism does not mean all judgments are equal; it says there are many standards of greatness, each of which can be met well or badly.","variants":["Value pluralism: many irreducible kinds of artistic value.","Category-relative pluralism: each art form or genre has its own values."],"reading":[{"title":"The Value of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/value-of-art/"}]},"a6|perception":{"summary":"Aesthetic experience is, at heart, close and attentive perception: noticing a work’s details, structure and qualities as they unfold.","idea":"The perceptual view says aesthetic experience is perception at its most engaged. Listening with full attention means hearing the melody’s shape, the instruments answering each other, the build and release of tension. No special emotion or trance is needed; the experience is defined by what it attends to and how carefully. It denies that pleasure or a mystical state is the core. Its appeal is that it is concrete and teachable: a music teacher can help you hear more, and your experience deepens as you notice more. A birdwatcher absorbed in a bird’s flight shows the same structure.","example":"Learning to hear a fugue is the classic illustration. At first you hear a pleasant blur. Then someone points out the theme, and you start to follow it entering in one voice after another, overlapping, turned upside down. The experience changes completely, though the sound does not. That change, perceptualists say, is what aesthetic experience is.","history":"Frank Sibley’s “Aesthetic Concepts” (1959) treated aesthetic qualities as things perceived with taste. Noël Carroll’s “Aesthetic Experience Revisited” (2002) defended a content-oriented account: attending to a work’s form and expressive qualities. Bence Nanay’s Aesthetics as Philosophy of Perception (2016) argued that it involves a distinctive way of distributing attention.","misread":"The view does not make aesthetic experience cold or technical; perceiving expressive qualities, like a melody’s sadness, is part of what it attends to.","variants":["Content-oriented account (Carroll): attending to form and expressive properties.","Distributed attention (Nanay): attention focused on one object but spread across many of its features."],"reading":[{"title":"Aesthetic Experience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-experience/"},{"title":"The Concept of the Aesthetic (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-concept/"}]},"a6|pleasure":{"summary":"The heart of aesthetic experience is pleasure: enjoying what you perceive, for its own sake.","idea":"The hedonic view says what makes an experience aesthetic is the enjoyment it involves: delight in a melody, a view or a poem, valued for itself and not for what it brings. Pleasure explains why we seek out art and why we linger with it. It denies that attention alone or a special mental state is the core; without enjoyment, the experience would lose its point. Its appeal is simplicity and honesty: most people go to concerts because they love the music. You put a favourite song on repeat because you like it, and that liking is the whole story.","example":"Kant’s contrast between two pleasures is the classic test. A warm bath or a tasty meal pleases the senses and feeds desire; a beautiful melody pleases in mere contemplation, with no wish to own or use it. Hedonists debate whether aesthetic pleasure is a special kind, as Kant thought, or ordinary enjoyment aimed at art.","history":"Francis Hutcheson (1725) and David Hume (1757) put pleasure at the centre of taste, and Kant’s Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790) made a special pleasure the basis of judging beauty. George Santayana’s The Sense of Beauty (1896) defined beauty in terms of pleasure. Mohan Matthen and others defend hedonic views today. In Sanskrit, rasa literally means taste or savour.","misread":"Hedonists do not say only cheerful art counts; tragedies and sad songs can give deep pleasure through how they are made and understood.","variants":["Disinterested pleasure (Kant): pleasure in contemplation, free of desire.","Pleasure of engagement (Matthen): pleasure that keeps you attending."],"reading":[{"title":"Aesthetic Experience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-experience/"},{"title":"The Concept of the Aesthetic (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-concept/"}]},"a6|unique":{"summary":"Aesthetic experience is a special state of its own kind, in which self and time fall away in absorbed contemplation.","idea":"This view says aesthetic experience differs in kind from ordinary seeing, thinking or enjoying. In it, you are absorbed in the object, released from your own desires and worries, and lose track of time. It denies that the experience can be broken down into perception plus pleasure. Its appeal is that many people recognise it from their own lives: the moment in a concert when you forget where you are, or standing before a painting until you are surprised the gallery is closing. Thinkers in very different cultures have described it independently.","example":"Edward Bullough’s fog at sea is the classic case. For sailors, a dense fog means danger, anxiety and delay. But if you set your practical concerns aside, you can experience it differently: the milky veil over the water, the strange stillness, the muffled sounds. Bullough called this shift psychical distance.","history":"Kant’s idea of disinterested pleasure (1790) prepared the way. Arthur Schopenhauer’s The World as Will and Representation (1818) described will-less contemplation. Edward Bullough’s “Psychical Distance” (1912) and Jerome Stolnitz’s aesthetic attitude theory developed it. In India, Bharata’s Nāṭyaśāstra and Abhinavagupta (10th to 11th century) theorised rasa as a distinct kind of savouring.","misread":"The view does not require mystical trances; many defenders describe ordinary attention transformed, familiar to anyone deeply absorbed in music.","variants":["Will-less contemplation (Schopenhauer): knowing freed from desire.","Psychical distance (Bullough): setting practical concerns aside.","Rasa (Abhinavagupta): savouring an emotion freed from personal stake."],"reading":[{"title":"Schopenhauer’s Aesthetics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/schopenhauer-aesthetics/"},{"title":"The Aesthetic Attitude (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetic-attitude/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Experience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-experience/"}]},"a6|none":{"summary":"There is no special aesthetic kind of experience or attitude; there is only ordinary attention and enjoyment, directed at art.","idea":"Skepticism about aesthetic experience says the idea of a special aesthetic state is a myth. When you are absorbed in music, you are paying close attention and enjoying it, much as you might when absorbed in a chess game. Calling it “disinterested” or “contemplative” adds nothing; distraction is simply inattention. It denies that art should be defined or valued by a unique experience. Its appeal is economy: it explains everything the special-state theories explain using ordinary psychology, and it avoids making art seem mystical.","example":"Dickie’s jealous husband is the classic case. A man watching Othello keeps thinking about his own wife’s faithfulness. Attitude theorists say he lacks distance. Dickie replied that he is simply not paying attention to the play. No special attitude is missing, only ordinary attention.","history":"I. A. Richards’s Principles of Literary Criticism (1924) denied that aesthetic experience is a special kind of experience. George Dickie’s “The Myth of the Aesthetic Attitude” (1964) and “Beardsley’s Phantom Aesthetic Experience” (1965) attacked attitude and experience theories. Pierre Bourdieu and other sociologists treated the “pure gaze” as a learned social habit.","misread":"Skeptics do not deny that music can absorb us intensely; they deny that this absorption is a distinct, specifically aesthetic kind of mental state.","variants":["Attitude skepticism (Dickie): no special aesthetic attitude.","Experience skepticism: no distinctive aesthetic experience either."],"reading":[{"title":"The Aesthetic Attitude (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetic-attitude/"},{"title":"The Concept of the Aesthetic (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-concept/"}]},"a8|equal":{"summary":"The best popular songs can be as great as the best symphonies; brevity, simplicity and mass appeal do not make art lesser.","idea":"This view says greatness is not reserved for long, complex, elite forms. A three-minute song can achieve perfect unity of melody, words, rhythm and performance, and can express feeling with a precision no symphony attempts. It denies that difficulty, length or prestige measure artistic value. Its appeal is fairness and experience: people’s lives are shaped by popular songs, and many have rewarded decades of listening. Shakespeare wrote popular entertainment in his day, and the Beatles are now studied in universities.","example":"Bob Dylan’s 2016 Nobel Prize in Literature is a frequently cited case. The Swedish Academy honoured him for creating new poetic expressions within the great American song tradition. Defenders of popular art saw this as recognition that songs can stand with novels and poems as great art.","history":"Gilbert Seldes’s The Seven Lively Arts (1924) praised jazz, comics and film as serious arts. Richard Shusterman’s Pragmatist Aesthetics (1992) defended rap as art. Theodore Gracyk’s Rhythm and Noise (1996) analysed rock recordings as works. Noël Carroll’s A Philosophy of Mass Art (1998) argued that mass art is real art.","misread":"The view does not say all pop is great; it says the best pop can equal the best of any form.","variants":["Mass art theory (Carroll): mass art as a distinct, legitimate kind of art.","Pragmatist defence (Shusterman): popular arts judged by lived, embodied experience."],"reading":[{"title":"The Aesthetics of Popular Music (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetics-of-popular-music/"}]},"a8|higher":{"summary":"Pop songs can be wonderful, but the greatest works in deep, demanding traditions achieve more and are greater art.","idea":"The hierarchy view grants that pop songs can be excellent of their kind, but holds that some forms and traditions allow greater achievement. A symphony can develop ideas over an hour, build vast structures, and reward a lifetime of study; a three-minute song has less room. So the greatest symphony is a greater work than the greatest pop song. It denies that all forms have equal ceilings. Its appeal is that it explains why some works keep yielding more after hundreds of hearings. Compare a perfect short story with War and Peace: both can be flawless, but one does more.","example":"Clement Greenberg’s “Avant-Garde and Kitsch” (1939) contrasted a Picasso with a battle painting by Ilya Repin. An untutored viewer, he imagined, prefers the Repin because it delivers its effects ready-made. Picasso demands work from the viewer, and Greenberg located higher value in that demand.","history":"Kant (1790) and Hegel’s lectures on aesthetics (1820s) ranked the arts. Matthew Arnold’s Culture and Anarchy (1869) championed “the best which has been thought and said”. Clement Greenberg (1939) and Dwight Macdonald’s “Masscult and Midcult” (1960) attacked mass culture. Roger Scruton’s The Aesthetics of Music (1997) defended the classical tradition.","misread":"The view does not say pop is worthless; it says pop’s best achievements are smaller in scope than the greatest works of demanding traditions.","variants":["Hierarchy of forms: some forms permit greater works than others.","Tradition-based view (Scruton): depth comes from long, complex traditions."],"reading":[{"title":"The Aesthetics of Popular Music (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetics-of-popular-music/"},{"title":"The Value of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/value-of-art/"}]},"a8|incomp":{"summary":"Pop songs and symphonies are great in different kinds of ways, so asking which is greater has no clear answer.","idea":"This view says greatness is always greatness of a kind. A great pop song excels at hooks, vocal character, recorded sound and concise expression; a great symphony excels at large-scale structure and development. There is no common scale on which to rank them. It denies both that pop is lesser and that it is equal, since “equal” would also need a common measure. Its appeal is that it avoids snobbery and reverse snobbery at once. Asking whether the best novelist beats the best painter feels confused for the same reason.","example":"Joseph Raz’s small-improvement test is the standard tool. Suppose a great symphony and a great pop song seem neither better than the other. Now imagine the song slightly improved. If it still does not beat the symphony, the two were never equal on one scale; they are incommensurable.","history":"Joseph Raz’s The Morality of Freedom (1986) gave a classic account of incommensurable values. Ruth Chang’s edited Incommensurability, Incomparability, and Practical Reason (1997) and her idea of parity refined the debate. In aesthetics, Bruce Baugh’s “Prolegomena to Any Aesthetics of Rock Music” (1993) and Theodore Gracyk argued that rock needs its own standards.","misread":"Incommensurability is not relativism; within a kind there can be clear better and worse, and some versions allow rough comparisons across kinds.","variants":["Incommensurability (Raz): no common measure of value.","Parity (Chang): comparable without being better, worse or equal.","Genre-relative standards: each musical tradition judged by its own norms."],"reading":[{"title":"Incommensurable Values (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/value-incommensurable/"},{"title":"The Aesthetics of Popular Music (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetics-of-popular-music/"}]},"a8|industry":{"summary":"Mass-produced music is standardised to sell, soothing listeners rather than challenging them, so it cannot match serious art.","idea":"The culture industry critique says the problem with pop is not that it is popular, but how it is made: by an industry aiming at profit. Songs are assembled from interchangeable parts so that hits can be produced reliably, and listeners are trained to expect the familiar. The result keeps people comfortable and passive, distracted from the conditions of their lives. It denies that market success measures artistic value. Its appeal is that it explains real features of commercial culture, like formula chord progressions and endless sequels. A playlist engineered to keep you streaming is the modern image.","example":"Adorno’s idea of pseudo-individualisation is the classic illustration. A pop song’s chorus could be swapped into another song without loss, he argued, yet small gimmicks, like a vocal quirk or a novel sound, make each song seem unique. Listeners feel they are choosing freely among products that are really much the same.","history":"Max Horkheimer and Theodor Adorno’s Dialectic of Enlightenment (written 1944, published 1947) introduced “the culture industry”. Adorno’s “On Popular Music” (1941, with George Simpson) applied it to songs. Herbert Marcuse’s One-Dimensional Man (1964) extended Frankfurt School themes. Walter Benjamin’s “The Work of Art in the Age of Mechanical Reproduction” (1935) took a more hopeful view of mass media.","misread":"The critique is not just snobbery about simple music; Adorno also attacked how classical music was packaged and consumed as a commodity.","variants":["Standardisation thesis: mass products are built from interchangeable parts.","Pseudo-individualisation: trivial differences hide underlying sameness."],"reading":[{"title":"Theodor W. Adorno (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/adorno/"},{"title":"Aesthetics in Critical Theory (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetics-critical-theory/"}]},"a8|social":{"summary":"The ranking of high art above popular art reflects class and social power, not artistic quality.","idea":"This view says the distinction between high and low culture is a social tool. Groups with education and wealth learn to appreciate demanding art, and their taste becomes a badge of distinction, marking them as cultured and others as vulgar. The hierarchy tracks who listens, not how good the music is. It denies that the symphony’s prestige reflects intrinsic superiority. Its appeal is that it explains striking facts: taste correlates closely with schooling and class, and low forms often rise once elites adopt them. Jazz moved from dance halls to concert halls.","example":"Lawrence Levine’s Highbrow/Lowbrow (1988) is a classic case study. In 19th-century America, Shakespeare was popular entertainment, performed with songs and farces for rowdy mixed audiences. By around 1900 he had become high culture, watched in reverent silence by educated elites. The plays had not changed; their social meaning had.","history":"Thorstein Veblen’s The Theory of the Leisure Class (1899) described taste as display. Pierre Bourdieu’s Distinction (1979, English 1984), based on 1960s surveys of French taste, introduced “cultural capital”. Herbert Gans’s Popular Culture and High Culture (1974) and Lawrence Levine (1988) developed the critique. Richard Peterson’s work on cultural omnivores from 1992 revised it.","misread":"The critique does not by itself prove no music is better; it shows social forces shape rankings, which is separate from whether the rankings are right.","variants":["Cultural capital (Bourdieu): taste as a resource that marks social position.","Taste cultures (Gans): parallel cultures, each with its own standards.","Cultural omnivore thesis (Peterson): elites now mark status by breadth of taste."],"reading":[{"title":"Aesthetic Taste (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetic-taste/"},{"title":"Beauty (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauty/"}]},"a3|autonomy":{"summary":"Artistic and moral value are separate; a moral flaw in a work does not make it worse as art.","idea":"Autonomism holds that judging a work as art and judging it morally are two different assessments. A novel can be brilliantly written and morally repugnant; a well-meaning poem can be clumsy. Moral praise or blame of a work is legitimate, but it is not artistic praise or blame. It denies that ethical flaws lower artistic value. Its appeal is that it keeps criticism focused and resists censorship: we can admire the craft of works whose views we reject. You can admit that a political speech you hate was brilliantly delivered.","example":"The 1949 Bollingen Prize went to Ezra Pound’s The Pisan Cantos, though Pound was confined to a psychiatric hospital after broadcasting fascist propaganda, and the poems contain antisemitic passages. The judges defended the award on purely poetic grounds. The public storm that followed is a classic autonomist test case.","history":"Théophile Gautier’s preface to Mademoiselle de Maupin (1835) launched “art for art’s sake”. Oscar Wilde’s preface to The Picture of Dorian Gray (1891) and the Aesthetic movement popularised it. Clive Bell’s formalism supported it. James Anderson and Jeffrey Dean’s “Moderate Autonomism” (1998) gave a careful modern version.","misread":"Autonomists do not say art cannot be morally criticised; they say moral criticism is a separate judgment from artistic evaluation.","variants":["Radical autonomism: moral evaluation of art is out of place.","Moderate autonomism (Anderson and Dean): moral criticism is fine but separate from artistic value."],"reading":[{"title":"Ethical Criticism of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ethical-criticism-of-art/"}]},"a3|moderate":{"summary":"A moral flaw sometimes makes a work worse as art, namely when it stops the work achieving the response it aims for.","idea":"Moderate moralism says some moral flaws are artistic flaws, but not all. A work that asks for a response, like laughter, pity or admiration, depends on its audience being able to give it. If the work’s moral outlook is so flawed that decent viewers cannot respond as asked, the work fails on its own terms. It denies that every moral flaw matters artistically, and denies that none do. Its appeal is moderation: it explains real cases without turning critics into moral judges of everything. Picture a sitcom character meant to be lovable who is plainly cruel.","example":"Carroll discussed Bret Easton Ellis’s American Psycho (1991), meant as a satire of greedy 1980s yuppie culture. Its long, graphic violence against women, he argued, so repels many readers that they cannot hold the satirical stance the book needs. The moral flaw becomes an artistic one: the satire does not work.","history":"David Hume’s “Of the Standard of Taste” (1757) said that when a work presents vice without proper blame, readers cannot enter into its sentiments, and this is a real blemish. Noël Carroll’s “Moderate Moralism” (1996) gave the modern version. Mary Devereaux’s work on Leni Riefenstahl’s Triumph of the Will explored related questions.","misread":"Moderate moralism is not about whether a work depicts immoral acts; showing evil is fine, and what matters is the response the work invites.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Ethical Criticism of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ethical-criticism-of-art/"},{"title":"Hume’s Aesthetics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume-aesthetics/"}]},"a3|ethicism":{"summary":"Any moral flaw in the attitude a work invites always counts against it as art, though other merits may outweigh it.","idea":"Ethicism says that whenever a work asks us to take up a morally bad attitude, such as enjoying cruelty or admiring injustice, that is an artistic defect, and whenever it invites a good attitude, that is an artistic merit. These count but can be outweighed by other strengths. It denies the autonomist claim that ethics is irrelevant, and denies the moralist claim that ethics settles everything. Its appeal is that it treats moral reactions as part of real engagement with art, while still allowing great but flawed masterpieces. A thrilling film that cheers on bullying is thrilling, yet also flawed as art.","example":"D. W. Griffith’s The Birth of a Nation (1915) is the classic test case. Its innovations in editing and spectacle made it a landmark of film history, yet it glorifies the Ku Klux Klan and invites viewers to cheer its violence. Ethicists say that invited attitude is an artistic flaw, outweighed in places but never erased.","history":"Berys Gaut’s “The Ethical Criticism of Art” (1998) and Art, Emotion and Ethics (2007) set out ethicism and the merited-response argument. Wayne Booth’s The Company We Keep (1988) had treated reading as keeping company with books whose values matter. Ethicism is a pro tanto principle, modelled on similar principles in ethics.","misread":"Ethicism does not judge a work by its characters; a novel may portray terrible people while inviting a morally sound attitude toward them.","variants":["Merited-response argument (Gaut): a work fails insofar as the response it prescribes is undeserved."],"reading":[{"title":"Ethical Criticism of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ethical-criticism-of-art/"}]},"a3|immoral2":{"summary":"A work’s moral flaw can sometimes make it better as art, especially when it draws us into sympathy with what is wrong.","idea":"Immoralism says some moral defects are artistic virtues. A work that makes you root for a criminal, or enjoy what you know is wrong, can give understanding that morally sound works cannot: how evil feels attractive from the inside, how easily your sympathies can be steered. The point is not merely depicting evil, which all agree art may do, but inviting approval of it. It denies the ethicist claim that moral flaws always count against art. Its appeal is that it fits our fascination with antiheroes, from Milton’s Satan to TV crime bosses we cheer despite ourselves.","example":"A. W. Eaton’s rough heroes are the classic case. In The Sopranos, Tony Soprano is a murderer, yet the show makes us like him and want him to succeed. Eaton argued that this deliberately cultivated, uncomfortable sympathy is a hard artistic achievement, so the work’s moral defect is part of its artistic merit.","history":"Romantic readers of Milton, such as William Blake, admired the pull of Satan in Paradise Lost. In analytic philosophy, Daniel Jacobson’s “In Praise of Immoral Art” (1997), Matthew Kieran’s “Forbidden Knowledge” (2003) and A. W. Eaton’s “Robust Immoralism” (2012) gave careful versions of the view.","misread":"Immoralists do not say evil works are great because they are evil; they say certain moral flaws can serve real artistic achievements.","variants":["Cognitive immoralism (Kieran): immoral perspectives can yield understanding.","Robust immoralism (Eaton): a moral defect can itself be an aesthetic merit."],"reading":[{"title":"Ethical Criticism of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ethical-criticism-of-art/"}]},"a3|moralism":{"summary":"Art’s purpose is moral, so a work that corrupts or teaches wrongly is bad art, whatever its other merits.","idea":"Radical moralism says moral value decides artistic value. Because art shapes character and society so powerfully, its success must be measured by whether it makes people better; skill used to corrupt is skill misused, and the work is a failure. It denies that beauty or form can redeem a morally harmful work. Its appeal is that it takes art’s power seriously: if music and stories truly shape who we become, how could their moral effect be beside the point? Parents who keep violent games from young children act on a version of this reasoning.","example":"Plato’s banishing of the poets in the Republic is the classic case. A poet able to imitate anything, Plato says, should be honoured, anointed with myrrh and crowned, then sent away to another city. The ideal city keeps only hymns to the gods and praise of good people, because stories shape young souls.","history":"Plato’s Republic (4th century BCE) and the Confucian tradition, notably Xunzi’s essay on music, judged art by its effect on character. Leo Tolstoy’s What Is Art? (1897) condemned much celebrated art as bad, including most of his own fiction. Soviet socialist realism and some religious traditions have also treated art as answerable to moral ends.","misread":"Moralism is not just the view that art can be morally judged, which nearly everyone accepts; it claims moral value settles artistic value.","variants":["Radical moralism: moral value determines artistic value.","Didacticism: art’s purpose is to teach moral lessons."],"reading":[{"title":"Plato’s Aesthetics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/plato-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Ethical Criticism of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ethical-criticism-of-art/"}]},"e1|req":{"summary":"You must pull the lever: saving five at the cost of one is not just allowed but required.","idea":"This view says that when you can prevent a much greater harm at a smaller cost, doing nothing is itself a wrong choice. It denies that standing back keeps your hands clean: refusing to act is still a decision with victims. People find it attractive because it treats every life as counting equally and refuses to let squeamishness decide who dies. Compare a lifeguard who could save five swimmers by swimming left instead of right toward one: few would say either direction is fine.","example":"The trolley case itself, sometimes called the Bystander case: you stand by a switch, not on the train. Holders of this view argue that the bystander who refuses to switch has, in effect, chosen four extra deaths in exchange for not having to act.","history":"Philippa Foot introduced the runaway trolley in “The Problem of Abortion and the Doctrine of the Double Effect” (1967). Judith Jarvis Thomson named “the trolley problem” in 1976 and developed the bystander version in 1985. Consequentialists from Jeremy Bentham to Peter Singer imply that pulling is required, and Joshua Greene’s psychological studies from 2001 onward found that most people at least approve of pulling.","misread":"Saying pulling is required does not mean the one person’s death is good; it means it is the lesser of two terrible outcomes.","variants":["Act consequentialist version: pull because it produces the best outcome, full stop.","Duty of rescue version: pull because you have a strong duty to save lives when the cost is small to you."],"reading":[{"title":"Doing vs. Allowing Harm (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/doing-allowing/"},{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"}]},"e1|allow":{"summary":"Pulling the lever is morally allowed, but so is refusing; neither choice is wrong.","idea":"This view treats pulling as permissible rather than obligatory. It denies that you must kill someone, even to save more, while also denying that saving the five is forbidden. Its appeal is that it respects two intuitions at once: five deaths are worse than one, yet nobody can be forced to become the one who kills an innocent. Think of a stranger who could donate a kidney to save a life: it would be admirable, but most people think he may say no.","example":"Thomson’s Bystander case is the standard test. Her famous contrast was with the Transplant case, where a surgeon could kill one patient to give organs to five. The permissibility view tries to explain why the bystander may redirect the trolley while the surgeon may not kill.","history":"Judith Jarvis Thomson defended the permission to switch in “The Trolley Problem” (Yale Law Journal, 1985). Frances Kamm developed detailed principles of permissible harm in Intricate Ethics (2007). The view fits common deontology, which recognises a zone of options between the required and the forbidden. Thomson herself later argued in “Turning the Trolley” (2008) that the bystander may not switch after all.","misread":"Permissible does not mean the choice doesn’t matter; it means morality leaves this particular hard choice to you.","variants":["Doctrine of double effect: you may cause the one death as a foreseen side effect, not as your aim.","Doing and allowing: killing is worse than letting die, so you are not required to kill."],"reading":[{"title":"Doctrine of Double Effect (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/double-effect/"},{"title":"Doing vs. Allowing Harm (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/doing-allowing/"}]},"e1|wrong":{"summary":"You must not pull: turning the trolley makes you kill an innocent person, and that is wrong even to save five.","idea":"This view holds that there is a deep difference between killing someone and failing to save someone. By pulling, you become the cause of the one person’s death; by not pulling, the five die from a danger you did not create. It denies that lives can simply be added up and traded. People find it attractive because it protects each person from being sacrificed for others. In everyday life, we do not let a doctor take one patient’s organs, however many might be saved.","example":"The Transplant case: a surgeon could kill one healthy visitor and use his organs to save five patients. Almost everyone says no. Defenders of not pulling argue that the bystander at the switch is in the same position and should also refrain.","history":"Judith Jarvis Thomson, who first defended the permission to switch, reversed herself in “Turning the Trolley” (Philosophy and Public Affairs, 2008), arguing that the bystander may not make the one pay a cost he would not pay himself. Absolutist strands of deontology, from Kant’s Groundwork (1785) to Elizabeth Anscombe’s “Modern Moral Philosophy” (1958), also resist killing the innocent as a means to good results.","misread":"Not pulling is not indifference to the five; it is a claim that you lack the right to kill one to save them.","variants":["Absolutism: killing an innocent is never allowed, whatever the stakes.","Thomson’s 2008 view: you may sacrifice yourself, but you may not impose that sacrifice on someone else."],"reading":[{"title":"Doing vs. Allowing Harm (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/doing-allowing/"},{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"}]},"e1|nofact":{"summary":"There is no moral fact about the lever: neither pulling nor refusing is objectively right or wrong.","idea":"This is moral skepticism applied to the trolley case. It denies that any choice here is truly correct, because it doubts there are objective moral facts at all, or that we could ever know them. It does not deny that people have strong feelings about the case; it says those feelings are not tracking a fact. Its appeal is honesty: centuries of argument have produced no agreement. Compare arguing about whether chocolate is better than vanilla: strong views, but nothing to be right about.","example":"J. L. Mackie’s argument from queerness: if objective moral facts existed, they would be utterly unlike anything else, facts that by themselves tell you what to do. Mackie thought such strange things are not part of the world, so moral claims about the trolley are all false.","history":"Ancient Pyrrhonian skeptics, recorded by Sextus Empiricus (c. 2nd century CE), suspended judgment about good and bad. In modern times J. L. Mackie’s Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977) defended the error theory, and Walter Sinnott-Armstrong’s Moral Skepticisms (2006) surveyed doubts about moral knowledge. Richard Joyce’s The Myth of Morality (2001) extended Mackie’s case.","misread":"A moral skeptic can still care deeply and choose to pull; the claim is only that no objective fact makes that choice correct.","variants":["Error theory: moral claims aim at facts but are all false.","Epistemological skepticism: there may be moral facts, but we cannot know them.","Pyrrhonism: suspend judgment on whether any moral claim is true."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}]},"e1a|yes":{"summary":"Strict impartiality: five lives outweigh one, even when the one is your own child.","idea":"This view says morality looks at the situation from no one’s point of view in particular. Each person counts as one and no more than one, so your child’s life cannot outweigh four extra deaths. It denies that personal ties change what is right, though it may admit they make the right act harder. Its appeal is fairness: the five also have parents who love them. Imagine a judge deciding a case involving her own son; we expect her to rule as she would for a stranger.","example":"William Godwin’s fire case: you can save either the philosopher Fénelon, who will benefit thousands, or his chambermaid, who is your mother. Godwin argued you should save Fénelon, since “my mother” has no special moral weight.","history":"William Godwin’s Enquiry Concerning Political Justice (1793) gave the classic impartialist answer in the fire case. The Chinese philosopher Mozi (5th century BCE) taught jian ai, impartial care for all, and was criticised by Mencius for it. Utilitarians such as Henry Sidgwick, in The Methods of Ethics (1874), and Peter Singer defend strong impartiality at the level of what is right.","misread":"Impartialists need not think loving your child is bad; many hold that a world where parents favour children usually goes better.","variants":["Utilitarian impartiality: everyone’s wellbeing counts equally in the total.","Mohist universal care: love others’ families as you love your own."],"reading":[{"title":"Impartiality (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/impartiality/"}]},"e1a|may":{"summary":"You are permitted to save your child instead, though pulling would still be the better act.","idea":"This view grants an agent-relative permission: you may give your own child extra weight, even though impartially the five matter more. It denies both that you must sacrifice your child and that you must favour her. The appeal is that it captures two thoughts: pulling would be heroic, yet no one could blame a parent who could not do it. Compare spending money on your own child’s birthday when it could have helped strangers more: many think this is allowed, not required.","example":"Bernard Williams’s drowning wife case: a man can save only one of two drowning people, one of them his wife. Williams said that if he stops to check whether morality permits saving his wife, he has had “one thought too many.”","history":"Samuel Scheffler’s The Rejection of Consequentialism (1982) defended an agent-centred prerogative to give extra weight to one’s own concerns. Thomas Nagel’s The View from Nowhere (1986) analysed agent-relative reasons. Bernard Williams’s “Persons, Character and Morality” (1976) introduced the drowning wife case. The idea also fits the common notion of supererogation: acts that are good but beyond duty.","misread":"A permission is not a duty: this view says you may favour your child, not that favouring her is the better act.","variants":["Agent-centred prerogative: you may weigh your own projects and loved ones more heavily than impartial value.","Supererogation: pulling would be praiseworthy but goes beyond what duty demands."],"reading":[{"title":"Reasons for Action: Agent-Neutral vs. Agent-Relative (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reasons-agent/"},{"title":"Supererogation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/supererogation/"}]},"e1a|protect":{"summary":"Partiality: you have a real duty to protect your own child, so you must not pull.","idea":"This view holds that special relationships create special duties. A parent owes her child protection in a way she does not owe strangers, so pulling would betray an obligation, not merely forgo a permission. It denies that morality is fundamentally impartial. People find it attractive because a parent who calmly sacrificed their child for strangers seems to have failed at something important. Similarly, we think you owe your friend a visit in hospital in a way you do not owe every patient there.","example":"Confucian ethics offers a classic case in the Analects (13.18): when told of a son who testified against his father for stealing a sheep, Confucius said that in his community fathers and sons cover for each other. Loyalty within the family comes first.","history":"Confucius (551 to 479 BCE) and Mencius (4th century BCE) taught graded love, with duties strongest toward family. Bernard Williams (1976, 1981) argued that deep personal attachments are part of what gives life meaning. Modern defenders of special obligations include Samuel Scheffler, Boundaries and Allegiances (2001), and care ethicists such as Nel Noddings in Caring (1984).","misread":"Partiality does not say strangers don’t matter; it says those close to you generate additional, stronger claims.","variants":["Special obligations: relationships themselves create duties.","Confucian graded love: care radiates outward from family.","Care ethics: morality starts from concrete caring relationships."],"reading":[{"title":"Impartiality (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/impartiality/"},{"title":"Care Ethics (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/care-ethics/"}]},"e2|req":{"summary":"You must push the stranger: one death to prevent five is required, whether by lever or by hand.","idea":"This view says the footbridge is morally no different from the lever. What matters is how many die, not whether you touch anyone or use someone’s body. It denies that the strong feeling against pushing reflects a real moral difference. Its attraction is consistency: if you accept pulling the lever because five outweigh one, it seems arbitrary to change your mind because the method is more hands-on. Compare: a pilot steering a crashing plane away from a city is not excused for choosing the city because steering felt uncomfortable.","example":"The Footbridge case, introduced by Thomson (1985): a large man stands on a bridge, and only his body can stop the trolley. Joshua Greene argued that our refusal to push comes from a fast emotional alarm, not a sound principle.","history":"Judith Jarvis Thomson set out the footbridge variant in “The Trolley Problem” (1985). Act consequentialists such as J. J. C. Smart, in Utilitarianism: For and Against (1973), and Peter Singer, in “Ethics and Intuitions” (2005), argued that such intuitions should not override counting lives. Joshua Greene’s fMRI studies (2001) and Moral Tribes (2013) gave a psychological debunking of the intuition against pushing.","misread":"Defenders do not say pushing feels fine; they say the feeling, though understandable, does not track a moral difference.","variants":["Act utilitarian version: push because it maximises wellbeing.","Debunking version: push because the intuition against it is an unreliable evolved reaction."],"reading":[{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"},{"title":"Experimental Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/experimental-moral/"}]},"e2|allow":{"summary":"Pushing is allowed but not required: you may sacrifice the stranger to save five, but you may also refrain.","idea":"This view grants that saving five can justify killing one, even by hand, but denies that anyone is obliged to do something so terrible. It leaves room for an agent who simply cannot bring himself to push. Its appeal is that it accepts the logic of numbers while respecting the moral weight of personally killing. Compare a soldier who could end a siege by an act of brutal violence: some think he may do it, few think he is required to.","example":"The Footbridge case again, read through the idea of options. Just as you may give your kidney but need not, the view says you may push but need not, because the cost to your own integrity is very great.","history":"This combination is less common in the literature than the others. It draws on Samuel Scheffler’s agent-centred prerogative, in The Rejection of Consequentialism (1982), which permits agents not to do what is best overall, and on Bernard Williams’s integrity objection in Utilitarianism: For and Against (1973), which stressed what certain acts cost the person who does them.","misread":"Permission to push does not mean pushing is trivial; it means the wrongness of not pushing is excused by the enormous personal cost.","variants":["Prerogative view: maximising is best, but you may decline at great personal cost.","Integrity view: no one can be required to commit an act that would shatter their moral identity."],"reading":[{"title":"Reasons for Action: Agent-Neutral vs. Agent-Relative (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reasons-agent/"},{"title":"Doing vs. Allowing Harm (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/doing-allowing/"}]},"e2|wrong":{"summary":"You must not push: killing someone by using his body to stop the trolley is wrong even to save five.","idea":"This view holds that some ways of treating a person are off-limits whatever the gain. Pushing the man treats him as an object to be used, or makes you the direct agent of his death. It denies that five lives automatically outweigh one when the one must be killed this way. The view matches the judgment of most people asked about the case. Similarly, we would condemn someone who threw a passer-by in front of a runaway car to shield a crowd.","example":"Footbridge is the defining case. Its partner is Transplant: just as a surgeon may not harvest a healthy patient’s organs to save five, the bystander may not throw a man off a bridge. Many see the two as the same wrong.","history":"Thomson (1985) used Footbridge to show that most people reject pushing. Kant’s Groundwork (1785) grounds the ban in never treating humanity merely as a means. Warren Quinn’s “Actions, Intentions, and Consequences” (1989) and the doctrine of double effect, traced to Aquinas’s Summa Theologiae, explain the wrong as intending harm. Large surveys, such as Bago and colleagues (2022) across 45 countries, found majorities reject pushing.","misread":"Refusing to push is not valuing one life over five; it is denying that you may kill him to save them.","variants":["Means principle: you may not use a person as a tool.","Doctrine of double effect: you may not intend an innocent’s death.","Personal force view: direct violence against someone is especially wrong."],"reading":[{"title":"Doctrine of Double Effect (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/double-effect/"},{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"}]},"e2|nofact":{"summary":"There is no moral fact about pushing: neither choice is objectively right or wrong.","idea":"This is moral skepticism applied to the footbridge. It denies that the strong reactions people have, against pushing or for saving five, are tracking any objective truth. Its appeal is partly the difficulty of the case: philosophers have tried for decades to find a principle that explains why pushing differs from pulling and have not agreed. The skeptic suggests there is nothing to find. Compare a dream where two choices feel momentous but nothing real hangs on them.","example":"Mackie’s argument from relativity: moral codes vary greatly across cultures and eras, and this is better explained by different ways of life than by some cultures seeing moral facts more clearly. The disagreement over Footbridge, he would say, fits the same pattern.","history":"J. L. Mackie’s Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977) gave the arguments from relativity and queerness. Gilbert Harman’s The Nature of Morality (1977) argued that moral facts play no role in explaining our observations. Richard Joyce (2001, 2006) and Sharon Street (2006) offered evolutionary debunking arguments against objective moral knowledge.","misread":"Skepticism here is not a refusal to think about the case; it is a considered claim about whether any answer can be true.","variants":["Error theory: all claims that pushing is right or wrong are false.","Evolutionary debunking: our moral intuitions were shaped for survival, not truth."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}]},"e2a|means":{"summary":"The means principle: pushing uses the man as a tool to stop the train, while the lever does not need the victim at all.","idea":"This view says it is wrong to treat a person merely as a means: to use their body or life as an instrument for your goal without their consent. On the side track, the one person dies as a side effect; if he jumped clear, your plan would work even better. On the bridge, his body is the plan. It denies that harm is all that matters; how the harm figures in your plan matters too. Compare borrowing a stranger’s car without asking versus accidentally scratching it while parking.","example":"Thomson’s Loop case tests the view: the side track loops back to the five, so the train is stopped only because it hits the one man. His body is now needed, yet many still think pulling is allowed. Defenders have refined the principle in response.","history":"Immanuel Kant’s Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785) states the Formula of Humanity: treat humanity never merely as a means. The doctrine of double effect, traced to Thomas Aquinas’s Summa Theologiae (13th century), separates intended harm from foreseen harm. Warren Quinn (1989) sharpened it, and Derek Parfit discussed the Means Principle at length in On What Matters (2011).","misread":"The principle forbids using people merely as means; we use each other as means all the time, like hiring a plumber, with consent.","variants":["Doctrine of double effect: intending harm is worse than foreseeing it.","Kantian Formula of Humanity: respect persons as ends in themselves.","Doctrine of triple effect (Kamm): acting because of an effect differs from acting in order to bring it about."],"reading":[{"title":"Doctrine of Double Effect (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/double-effect/"},{"title":"Kant’s Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-moral/"}]},"e2a|redirect":{"summary":"The redirection principle: the lever deflects an existing threat, while pushing introduces a new victim into danger.","idea":"This view locates the difference in how the harm comes about. Pulling the lever turns a threat that already exists away from the many and toward the few. Pushing creates a new harmful chain by grabbing someone who was safe. It denies that intention alone explains the cases. People find it attractive because it matches the idea that redistributing an unavoidable harm is different from starting one. Compare steering a skidding car toward an empty-looking verge versus shoving a pedestrian into its path.","example":"Frances Kamm’s Lazy Susan case: the five sit on a turntable, and turning it moves them away from the trolley but pushes a bystander into its path. Kamm argued the case shows that the way harm arises from the good you do, not just intention, matters.","history":"Judith Jarvis Thomson’s “The Trolley Problem” (1985) proposed that the bystander may redirect a threat when it falls on fewer people by means that do not themselves violate rights. Frances Kamm developed the Principle of Permissible Harm in Morality, Mortality (1996) and Intricate Ethics (2007), holding that harm may come from a greater good but not be a means to it.","misread":"The view does not allow any redirection; you may move the threat only onto fewer people and not by violating rights in the act itself.","variants":["Thomson’s distributive exemption: redirecting a threat onto fewer can be permissible.","Kamm’s Principle of Permissible Harm: harm may be caused by the good, not as a means to it."],"reading":[{"title":"Doing vs. Allowing Harm (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/doing-allowing/"},{"title":"Doctrine of Double Effect (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/double-effect/"}]},"e2a|force":{"summary":"Personal force: the difference is that pushing harms someone by direct bodily violence, while the lever is impersonal.","idea":"This view says our judgments respond to whether harm is done up close with our own muscles. Physically shoving someone triggers a strong sense of wrongdoing that pulling a switch does not. Some who hold this think the feeling marks a genuine moral difference; others think it is just psychology and should be discounted. Its appeal is that it fits how people actually react. Compare how much harder it is to strike someone than to sign an order that harms them.","example":"Variants of Footbridge where you drop the man through a trapdoor by pressing a button. Studies found people judge this much less harshly than pushing him by hand, even though he is used as a means in both cases.","history":"Joshua Greene and colleagues’ fMRI study in Science (2001) linked “personal” dilemmas to emotional brain responses. Greene and colleagues’ “Pushing Moral Buttons” in Cognition (2009) found personal force interacts with intention. Greene’s Moral Tribes (2013) treated this sensitivity as an unreliable alarm. A 45 country replication led by Bence Bago (2022) found the personal force effect across cultures.","misread":"Noticing that force affects our judgments is a psychological finding; whether force really makes pushing worse is a separate moral question.","variants":["Debunking reading: the reaction to force is an evolved bias we should correct.","Vindicating reading: direct violence expresses a worse attitude toward the victim."],"reading":[{"title":"Experimental Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/experimental-moral/"}]},"e2a|twolevel":{"summary":"Rule-based reasoning: pushing might work once, but a general practice allowing it would make everyone less safe.","idea":"This view explains the difference by looking at rules rather than single acts. A society where people may throw strangers off bridges when they calculate it would save more would be a frightening place, and people would often get the calculation wrong. So a good rule forbids pushing, while redirecting dangers can be allowed. It denies that the right answer comes from judging the one case in isolation. Compare traffic laws: running a red light on an empty road harms no one this time, but the rule saves lives overall.","example":"The Transplant case is the classic illustration: if surgeons could kill patients for organs whenever numbers favoured it, no one would trust hospitals. A rule forbidding it produces far better results, which is why rule and two-level consequentialists condemn pushing too.","history":"John Stuart Mill’s Utilitarianism (1861) defended secondary rules as guides. R. M. Hare’s Moral Thinking (1981) set out two levels: intuitive rules for daily life, critical calculation for rare cases. Richard Brandt and Brad Hooker, in Ideal Code, Real World (2000), developed rule consequentialism. Joshua Greene’s Moral Tribes (2013) gave a related defence of trusting such rules.","misread":"This is not a pure intuition about pushing; it is a claim about which general policies produce the best results.","variants":["Two-level consequentialism (Hare): follow good rules in practice, judge by results in theory.","Rule consequentialism (Hooker): the right act follows the optimal rule even when breaking it would do better."],"reading":[{"title":"Rule Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism-rule/"},{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"}]},"e3|equal":{"summary":"Equal consideration: equal pain matters equally, whether it is felt by a pig or a human.","idea":"This view says what makes pain bad is how it feels, not who feels it. If a pig and a human truly suffer the same amount, the two sufferings deserve equal weight. It denies that species membership by itself is morally relevant, calling that bias speciesism, comparable to racism or sexism. It does not claim pigs and humans are equal in every way, or that their lives are equally valuable. Compare two people with equally painful burns: we would not treat one as mattering less because of their nationality.","example":"The argument from marginal cases: some humans, such as newborns or people with severe cognitive disabilities, have mental abilities no greater than some animals. If we would not inflict equal pain on them, consistency suggests we should not inflict it on animals either.","history":"Jeremy Bentham, in An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789), argued that the question is whether animals can suffer. Peter Singer’s Animal Liberation (1975) made equal consideration of interests famous. Earlier, the Jain principle of ahimsa, non-harm to all living beings, and some Buddhist teachings extended moral concern to animals in ancient India.","misread":"Equal consideration is not equal treatment: pigs don’t need votes, but their equal pain counts equally.","variants":["Utilitarian version (Singer): count all suffering equally in the total.","Rights version (Regan): animals that are subjects of a life have equal inherent value."],"reading":[{"title":"The Moral Status of Animals (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-animal/"},{"title":"The Grounds of Moral Status (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/grounds-moral-status/"}]},"e3|less":{"summary":"The hierarchical view: animal pain matters morally, but less than the same pain felt by a human.","idea":"This view grants that animals have real moral standing: hurting a pig for no reason wrongs the pig. But it holds that beings with richer mental lives, such as self-awareness, long-term plans or moral agency, have a higher moral status, so their interests weigh more. It denies both speciesist dismissal and strict equality. Its appeal is that it matches common practice, where we care about animal welfare yet put humans first. Compare a vet who treats pets with care but would save a child before a dog.","example":"The lifeboat case: a lifeboat can carry only four of five passengers, four humans and a dog. Tom Regan, though an animal rights defender, argued the dog should go overboard because death harms the humans more. Hierarchical views generalise this kind of ranking.","history":"Robert Nozick, in Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), discussed “utilitarianism for animals, Kantianism for people.” Shelly Kagan’s How to Count Animals, More or Less (2019) defends a detailed hierarchical theory of moral status. Many religious traditions, including Christian and Confucian thought, place humans above animals while condemning cruelty.","misread":"Holding that animal pain counts less does not mean it counts for nothing; it can still outweigh minor human interests.","variants":["Degrees of moral status (Kagan): status rises with psychological capacities.","Unequal consideration: equal interests weighed by a discount for the animal."],"reading":[{"title":"The Grounds of Moral Status (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/grounds-moral-status/"},{"title":"The Moral Status of Animals (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-animal/"}]},"e3|none":{"summary":"Indirect duties: animals lack moral standing of their own; cruelty is wrong only for its effects on humans.","idea":"This view says only beings with reason or moral agency are owed anything directly. We should not torture animals, but because cruelty damages our character and makes us likelier to mistreat people, or because animals belong to someone. It denies that a pig’s pain gives anyone a reason in its own right. Its appeal is that it ties moral standing to the capacity to take part in morality. Compare damaging a beautiful painting: wrong, but not wronging the painting.","example":"Kant’s dog example: a man who shoots his loyal old dog because it can no longer serve him does not wrong the dog, Kant said, but damages his own humanity, which he owes it to himself and others to cultivate.","history":"Thomas Aquinas’s Summa contra Gentiles argued that cruelty to animals is wrong because it leads to cruelty toward humans. Immanuel Kant’s Lectures on Ethics (1780s) gave the indirect duty view its classic form. René Descartes had earlier treated animals as machines. Peter Carruthers’s The Animals Issue (1992) defended a contractualist version.","misread":"The view does not permit cruelty; it condemns it, but locates the wrong in what it does to people and character.","variants":["Kantian indirect duty: cruelty corrupts our humanity.","Contractualist version (Carruthers): only parties to the moral contract have direct standing."],"reading":[{"title":"The Moral Status of Animals (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-animal/"},{"title":"Kant’s Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-moral/"}]},"e3|nofact":{"summary":"There is no moral fact about how much a pig’s pain matters compared with a human’s.","idea":"This is moral skepticism applied to animals. The view denies there is any objective answer to how much animal suffering should count, because it doubts objective moral facts exist at all. Our attitudes to animals, on this view, reflect culture, habit and sympathy rather than discovery. Its appeal is that attitudes to animals vary enormously between societies and eras. Compare debates over which foods are disgusting: strong reactions, wide variation, nothing to be right about.","example":"The variety of practices toward the same animal: pigs are forbidden food in some religions, a staple in others, and pets for some families. The skeptic argues that no fact settles which attitude is correct.","history":"J. L. Mackie’s Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977) defended moral error theory across all moral claims. Richard Joyce’s The Evolution of Morality (2006) argued our moral sense evolved for cooperation within groups, not to track truths. Ancient Pyrrhonists, recorded by Sextus Empiricus, suspended judgment on all such questions.","misread":"A skeptic can still choose to be vegetarian; the claim is only that no objective fact requires or forbids it.","variants":["Error theory: all claims about animals’ moral standing are false.","Relativism (a neighbouring view): the answer depends on the culture."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}]},"e6|strict":{"summary":"A strict duty of beneficence: you must give until giving more would cost you something comparably important.","idea":"This view says that if you can prevent something very bad without sacrificing anything of comparable moral importance, you must do it. A holiday is not comparable to a life, so you must give the money. It denies that distance, or the presence of other people who could help, weakens your duty. Its appeal is that it follows from a principle almost everyone accepts in a simple case. The result is demanding: most of what affluent people spend on luxuries becomes, on this view, wrongly spent.","example":"Peter Singer’s drowning child: you walk past a shallow pond where a child is drowning. Wading in will ruin your expensive shoes. Clearly you must save the child. Singer argues that a child dying of poverty far away is morally no different.","history":"Peter Singer’s “Famine, Affluence, and Morality” (1972) gave the classic argument. Peter Unger’s Living High and Letting Die (1996) extended it with many variant cases. Singer’s The Life You Can Save (2009) brought it to a wide audience and inspired the effective altruism movement, linked to Toby Ord and William MacAskill. Earlier, Mozi taught impartial concern for all.","misread":"Singer offered a weaker version too: give if you can prevent something bad without sacrificing anything morally significant.","variants":["Strong principle: give until marginal utility, sacrificing nothing comparably important.","Moderate principle: give when you can prevent great harm without sacrificing anything morally significant.","Effective altruism: give where your money does the most good."],"reading":[{"title":"The Principle of Beneficence in Applied Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/principle-beneficence/"}]},"e6|imperfect":{"summary":"An imperfect duty: you must help others in need, but you choose how much, when and whom.","idea":"This view says beneficence is a real duty, but a wide one. You may not adopt a policy of never helping anyone, yet no particular act of giving is required at a particular moment. You choose your causes and your timing. It denies both that charity is optional and that you must give until it hurts. Its appeal is that it respects both other people’s needs and your freedom to shape your own life. Compare a duty to stay in touch with friends: real, but you pick when to call.","example":"Kant’s fourth example in the Groundwork: a prosperous man who sees others struggling and thinks, “what concern is it of mine?” Kant argues he could not will a world where no one helps others, since he himself might one day need help.","history":"Immanuel Kant distinguished perfect from imperfect duties in the Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785) and developed duties of virtue, including beneficence, in The Metaphysics of Morals (1797). Barbara Herman and Thomas Hill have elaborated Kantian beneficence. Christian and Islamic teachings on charity, such as Islamic sadaqah alongside obligatory zakat, show related structures.","misread":"An imperfect duty is not a weak or optional one: you really must make others’ welfare one of your ends.","variants":["Kantian imperfect duty: adopt others’ happiness as an end, with latitude in action.","Fair share view (Liam Murphy): give your fair share of what would solve the problem if everyone did theirs."],"reading":[{"title":"Kant’s Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-moral/"},{"title":"The Principle of Beneficence in Applied Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/principle-beneficence/"}]},"e6|negative":{"summary":"Negative duties only: you must not harm others, but you have no strict duty to help them.","idea":"This view says what you owe everyone is restraint: no killing, stealing, deceiving or coercing. Helping is good and admirable but not owed, unless you have made a promise or caused the problem. It denies that strangers have a claim on your money. Its appeal is that it gives clear limits to morality and protects your right to run your own life. Compare: everyone agrees you must not push someone into a river, but disagree about how far you must go to pull strangers out.","example":"The violinist case from Thomson’s “A Defense of Abortion” (1971): you wake up connected to a famous violinist who needs your kidneys for nine months. Thomson argued it would be kind to stay, but you are not obliged to keep someone alive with your body.","history":"Robert Nozick’s Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974) defended a libertarian view where rights are side constraints against interference and no one is owed positive aid. Jan Narveson’s The Libertarian Idea (1988) developed similar themes. Many legal systems, including English common law, traditionally imposed no general duty to rescue strangers.","misread":"The view does not say helping is bad or pointless; it says helping is charity, not something others can demand.","variants":["Libertarianism: only duties not to interfere with others’ rights.","Common law view: no duty to rescue unless you caused the danger or have a special role."],"reading":[{"title":"Doing vs. Allowing Harm (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/doing-allowing/"},{"title":"Supererogation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/supererogation/"}]},"e6|scalar":{"summary":"The scalar view: giving more is better and giving less is worse, but there is no fixed line of what is required.","idea":"This view says morality is fundamentally about better and worse, not right and wrong. Giving the holiday money is better than keeping it; giving half is better than nothing. But the theory does not draw a sharp line where obligation begins. It denies that “must” picks out a deep moral fact. Its appeal is that it avoids both an extremely demanding duty and an arbitrary cutoff. Compare grading essays: there are better and worse essays without a natural line between passing and failing.","example":"The spectrum of donations: imagine giving 1 percent, 2 percent, up to 50 percent of your income. The scalar consequentialist says each step up is better, and asks why there should be a precise percentage where it suddenly becomes required.","history":"Michael Slote raised scalar ideas in Common-sense Morality and Consequentialism (1985). Alastair Norcross developed scalar consequentialism in papers and in Morality by Degrees (2020). Some readings of John Stuart Mill’s Utilitarianism (1861), which says actions are right “in proportion” as they promote happiness, have been cited as early support.","misread":"Scalar theorists don’t say anything goes; they say how good your choice is varies continuously rather than flipping at a line.","variants":["Scalar consequentialism (Norcross): rank acts by goodness of results, with no deontic thresholds."],"reading":[{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"},{"title":"Supererogation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/supererogation/"}]},"e6|nofact":{"summary":"There is no moral fact about whether you must give: neither giving nor keeping the money is objectively right.","idea":"This is moral skepticism applied to charity. The view denies that any objective fact makes keeping the holiday money wrong, or makes giving it required. You may still want to give out of compassion, but that is a preference, not a discovery. Its appeal lies in the deep, unresolved disagreement over how much we owe strangers. Compare whether you should support a sports team out of loyalty to your city: people feel strongly, but there is nothing to be right about.","example":"Mackie’s argument from queerness: an objective moral requirement to give would have to be a fact that by itself tells you what to do, regardless of what you want. Mackie thought nothing in the world could be like that.","history":"J. L. Mackie’s Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977) set out moral error theory. Gilbert Harman’s The Nature of Morality (1977) questioned whether moral facts explain anything. Richard Joyce’s The Myth of Morality (2001) developed the error theory further and proposed fictionalism as a response.","misread":"The skeptic is not necessarily selfish; many skeptics give generously, just without believing it is objectively required.","variants":["Error theory: all claims that you must give are false.","Moral fictionalism: keep talking about duties as a useful fiction."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}]},"e4|results":{"summary":"Consequentialism: an action is right or wrong only because of the good or bad results it brings about.","idea":"Consequentialism says the rightness of an act depends entirely on its outcomes: who is helped, who is harmed, and how much. Rules, motives and character matter only because of the results they tend to produce. It denies that any act, such as lying, is wrong in itself. Its appeal is simplicity and concern for what actually happens to people. Compare choosing a medical treatment: you pick the one with the best outcomes, not the one that follows tradition.","example":"Bernard Williams’s Jim and the Indians: Jim can shoot one villager, and the captain will spare nineteen others; refuse, and all twenty die. Consequentialists say Jim should shoot. Williams used the case to probe what this leaves out.","history":"The Chinese philosopher Mozi (5th century BCE) judged policies by their benefit to the people. Jeremy Bentham’s An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789) and John Stuart Mill’s Utilitarianism (1861) founded classical utilitarianism. Elizabeth Anscombe coined the word “consequentialism” in “Modern Moral Philosophy” (1958). Modern defenders include Peter Singer and Shelly Kagan.","misread":"Consequentialists need not calculate every decision; most think following good rules of thumb usually brings the best results.","variants":["Utilitarianism: the best result is the most total wellbeing.","Rule consequentialism: follow rules whose general acceptance has the best results.","Prioritarianism: benefits to the worse off count more."],"reading":[{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"},{"title":"The History of Utilitarianism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/utilitarianism-history/"}]},"e4|duties":{"summary":"Deontology: an action is right if it respects certain duties or rules, even when breaking them would turn out better.","idea":"Deontology says some acts are required or forbidden in themselves, not only because of their effects. You must keep promises, tell the truth and not kill the innocent, even when breaking these duties would produce better outcomes. It denies that good results justify any means. Its appeal is that it protects individuals from being sacrificed for the greater good and explains our sense that some things are simply not done. Compare a promise to a dying friend: you keep it even if no one would ever know.","example":"Kant’s murderer at the door: a would-be killer asks where your friend is hiding. Kant notoriously argued you still may not lie. Most deontologists disagree with him on this case, and debate whether duties can ever be outweighed.","history":"Immanuel Kant’s Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785) gave the categorical imperative. W. D. Ross’s The Right and the Good (1930) proposed several prima facie duties. Robert Nozick (1974) described rights as side constraints. Frances Kamm and Thomas Nagel defended agent-relative constraints. The Hebrew and Christian commandments express an older rule-based ethics.","misread":"Deontologists can care about consequences; they deny only that consequences are the whole story.","variants":["Kantian ethics: duties derived from the categorical imperative.","Rossian pluralism: several prima facie duties weighed by judgment.","Contractualism: duties are rules no one could reasonably reject."],"reading":[{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"},{"title":"Kant’s Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-moral/"}]},"e4|virtue":{"summary":"Virtue ethics: an action is right if it is what a person of good character, kind, honest and brave, would do.","idea":"Virtue ethics puts character at the centre. The basic question is not “which rule applies?” but “what kind of person should I be?” Virtues such as courage, honesty, generosity and practical wisdom are stable traits that help us live well. It denies that morality can be reduced to a formula. Its appeal is that it fits how we actually learn ethics: by admiring good people and practising good habits. Compare learning to play music: rules help, but mastery comes from trained skill and judgment.","example":"Aristotle’s doctrine of the mean: courage lies between cowardice and recklessness, and generosity between stinginess and extravagance. The virtuous person sees, in each situation, what the right amount is, which no rule can fully specify.","history":"Aristotle’s Nicomachean Ethics (4th century BCE) is the founding Western text; Confucius’s Analects (compiled after 479 BCE) centres on ren, humaneness. The Stoics made virtue the only good. Elizabeth Anscombe’s “Modern Moral Philosophy” (1958) sparked a revival, continued by Alasdair MacIntyre’s After Virtue (1981), Rosalind Hursthouse’s On Virtue Ethics (1999) and Philippa Foot’s Natural Goodness (2001).","misread":"Virtue ethics can guide action: it tells you to do what an honest, kind, wise person would do in your situation.","variants":["Eudaimonist virtue ethics: virtues are traits needed for a flourishing life.","Sentimentalist virtue ethics (Hume, Slote): virtues are traits we admire.","Confucian role ethics: virtues fit our roles and relationships."],"reading":[{"title":"Virtue Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-virtue/"},{"title":"Aristotle’s Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-ethics/"}]},"e4|care":{"summary":"Ethics of care: right action means responding well to the real relationships you are in and the care they call for.","idea":"This view starts ethics from concrete relationships, like parent and child, friends, or neighbours, rather than from abstract rules for everyone. Being moral means paying attention to particular people, understanding their needs and responding with care. It denies that the impartial, rule-following perspective is the whole of morality. Its appeal is that it takes seriously work and attachments that much of traditional ethics overlooked. Compare a nurse who follows every procedure but never notices a frightened patient: something moral is missing.","example":"Heinz’s dilemma, from Lawrence Kohlberg: should Heinz steal a drug to save his dying wife? Carol Gilligan noticed that a girl, Amy, looked for ways to keep the relationships intact, like talking to the druggist, rather than ranking rights. Gilligan argued this voice was undervalued.","history":"Carol Gilligan’s In a Different Voice (1982) challenged Kohlberg’s theory of moral development. Nel Noddings’s Caring (1984), Joan Tronto’s Moral Boundaries (1993), Eva Feder Kittay’s Love’s Labor (1999) and Virginia Held’s The Ethics of Care (2006) built care ethics into a theory. Scholars have compared it with Confucian emphasis on family relationships.","misread":"Care ethics is not only about women or the family; its defenders apply it to politics, health care and global justice.","variants":["Noddings’s care ethics: morality rooted in the caring relation between carer and cared-for.","Political care ethics (Tronto, Held): care as a value for society and policy."],"reading":[{"title":"Care Ethics (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/care-ethics/"},{"title":"Feminist Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/feminism-ethics/"}]},"e4|plural":{"summary":"Pluralism: several basic moral factors, such as results, duties and character, all count, and none always wins.","idea":"Pluralism holds that morality has more than one foundation. Doing good, keeping promises, avoiding harm, being fair and showing gratitude are all genuine reasons, and in a given case they can pull against each other. You must weigh them by judgment. It denies that one master principle explains everything. Its appeal is realism: real decisions often feel like balancing several things that matter. Compare a doctor weighing a patient’s wishes, their health and fairness to other patients.","example":"Ross’s promise case: you have promised to meet a friend, but on the way you can help at an accident. Both duties are real; here helping wins, but the broken promise still leaves something to regret and make up for.","history":"W. D. Ross’s The Right and the Good (1930) listed prima facie duties: fidelity, reparation, gratitude, justice, beneficence, self-improvement and non-maleficence. Isaiah Berlin defended value pluralism in political thought, notably in “Two Concepts of Liberty” (1958). Robert Audi’s The Good in the Right (2004) updated Ross’s view.","misread":"Pluralism is not relativism: it says there are several real moral factors, not that anything goes.","variants":["Rossian pluralism: prima facie duties weighed case by case.","Ranked pluralism: some values always outrank others.","Moral particularism (Dancy): reasons change force from case to case."],"reading":[{"title":"William David Ross (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/william-david-ross/"},{"title":"Value Pluralism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/value-pluralism/"}]},"e4|exist":{"summary":"Existentialism: no moral rules are given in advance; you create values through your choices and are responsible for them.","idea":"Existentialism holds that humans have no fixed nature or predetermined purpose: “existence precedes essence.” There is no rulebook written by God, nature or reason to tell you what to do. You make yourself through your choices and must own them rather than hiding behind excuses, which Sartre called bad faith. It denies that morality is discovered rather than chosen. Its appeal is its emphasis on freedom and responsibility. Compare someone who blames “the system” for every choice versus someone who owns what they do.","example":"Sartre’s student: during the Second World War, a young man must choose between staying to care for his mother and joining the Free French. Sartre said no moral theory could decide for him: “You are free, therefore choose.”","history":"Søren Kierkegaard (1813 to 1855) and Friedrich Nietzsche (1844 to 1900) are seen as precursors. Jean-Paul Sartre’s Being and Nothingness (1943) and the lecture “Existentialism Is a Humanism” (1945) made the view famous. Simone de Beauvoir’s The Ethics of Ambiguity (1947) developed an existentialist ethics in which willing your own freedom requires willing others’.","misread":"Existentialism isn’t “do whatever you like”; it demands that you choose seriously and accept full responsibility for what you choose.","variants":["Sartrean authenticity: choose freely and own the choice.","Beauvoir’s ethics of ambiguity: true freedom requires others’ freedom.","Nietzschean value creation: create higher, life-affirming values."],"reading":[{"title":"Existentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/existentialism/"},{"title":"Jean-Paul Sartre (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sartre/"}]},"e4|nothing":{"summary":"Moral skepticism: nothing makes an action really right or wrong, because there are no objective moral facts.","idea":"This view denies that any action is objectively right or wrong. Moral judgments may express feelings, social conventions or useful habits, but they do not describe real features of the world. The most influential version, error theory, says ordinary moral talk aims at objective truth but always misses, because the facts it needs do not exist. Its appeal is that it fits a scientific picture of the world and explains persistent moral disagreement. Compare belief in witches: once common, now seen as mistaken.","example":"Mackie’s argument from queerness: objective values would be “intrinsically action-guiding,” facts that by themselves demand you act, and we would need a special faculty to detect them. Mackie concluded that it is simpler to say there are no such facts.","history":"Ancient Pyrrhonists, as reported by Sextus Empiricus, suspended moral judgment. J. L. Mackie’s Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977) launched modern error theory. Richard Joyce’s The Myth of Morality (2001) and Jonas Olson’s Moral Error Theory (2014) defended it further. Nietzsche’s critique of morality is sometimes read in a related way.","misread":"Moral skeptics are not necessarily cruel or nihilistic in practice; most still live by values, just not ones they think are objective.","variants":["Error theory: moral claims are all false.","Moral fictionalism: keep moral talk as a useful pretence.","Moral abolitionism: drop moral talk altogether."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}]},"e4a|everyone":{"summary":"Utilitarianism: the right act brings about the most good for everyone, with each person’s wellbeing counted equally.","idea":"Utilitarianism is the best-known form of consequentialism. It says to add up the wellbeing produced by each option, counting everyone, yourself and strangers alike, and choose the option with the most. It denies that your own happiness or your family’s gets extra weight. Its appeal is its fairness and its focus on reducing suffering. Compare a government health budget: it should fund treatments that do the most total good, not those that help officials’ friends.","example":"The utility monster, from Robert Nozick (1974): a creature gets vastly more pleasure from resources than anyone else. Simple utilitarianism seems to say everything should go to it. Utilitarians reply in various ways, and the case is a classic test of aggregation.","history":"Jeremy Bentham’s An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789), John Stuart Mill’s Utilitarianism (1861) and Henry Sidgwick’s The Methods of Ethics (1874) founded the tradition. Mozi’s impartial concern in ancient China is an earlier relative. Modern utilitarians include R. M. Hare, Peter Singer, and Katarzyna de Lazari-Radek and Singer in The Point of View of the Universe (2014).","misread":"Counting everyone equally does not mean treating everyone the same; equal weight can mean giving more to those who can benefit most.","variants":["Total utilitarianism: maximise the total sum of wellbeing.","Average utilitarianism: maximise average wellbeing.","Negative utilitarianism: focus on minimising suffering."],"reading":[{"title":"The History of Utilitarianism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/utilitarianism-history/"},{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"}]},"e4a|prior":{"summary":"Prioritarianism: everyone’s good counts, but benefits matter more the worse off the person receiving them is.","idea":"Prioritarianism says a benefit counts for more when it goes to someone badly off. Giving a meal to a starving person adds more moral value than giving the same meal to someone well fed, even if the pleasure were equal. It denies that what matters is equality itself: it is not about the gap between people, but about how badly off each person is. Its appeal is that it captures concern for the worst off without levelling down. Compare triage: help the most desperate first.","example":"The levelling down objection: making the rich poorer, with no one gaining, would reduce inequality. Strict egalitarians seem to see something good in that. Prioritarians do not, which Parfit used to motivate priority over equality.","history":"Derek Parfit’s Lindley Lecture “Equality or Priority?” (1991) introduced the priority view by name and set out the levelling down objection. Thomas Nagel’s “Equality” (1977) and John Rawls’s difference principle in A Theory of Justice (1971) are related ideas. Later defenders include Richard Arneson and Nils Holtug.","misread":"Prioritarianism isn’t egalitarianism: it gives extra weight to the badly off, not to closing gaps between people.","variants":["Weighted prioritarianism: benefits weighted by a concave function of wellbeing.","Sufficientarianism (a neighbouring view): what matters is everyone having enough."],"reading":[{"title":"Egalitarianism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egalitarianism/"},{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"}]},"e4a|close":{"summary":"Self-referential altruism: everyone’s good counts, but people closer to you, family and friends, count for more.","idea":"This view is a form of consequentialism that weights results by your relationship to the people affected. You should promote good outcomes for everyone, but your own children, friends and community carry more weight than strangers. It denies strict impartiality while keeping concern for all. Its appeal is that it matches how most people live and fits the importance of personal bonds. Compare how a mayor cares for every citizen but would still rush first to her own child’s school in an emergency.","example":"Concentric circles of concern, a picture found in the Stoic Hierocles: yourself at the centre, then family, relatives, fellow citizens and all humanity. Hierocles urged pulling the outer circles inward; self-referential altruism accepts that inner circles weigh more.","history":"C. D. Broad named the view “self-referential altruism” in discussing common-sense morality. The Stoic Hierocles (2nd century CE) described circles of concern. Confucius and Mencius taught graded love, care strongest toward family. Contemporary discussion appears in work on partiality by Samuel Scheffler and in Douglas Portmore’s Commonsense Consequentialism (2011).","misread":"The view isn’t selfishness: it gives strangers real weight, just less than those you are close to.","variants":["Confucian graded love: care radiates outward from family.","Agent-relative consequentialism: the goodness of outcomes is ranked from the agent’s point of view."],"reading":[{"title":"Impartiality (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/impartiality/"},{"title":"Reasons for Action: Agent-Neutral vs. Agent-Relative (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reasons-agent/"}]},"e4a|me":{"summary":"Ethical egoism: the right act is whatever best serves your own long-term interest.","idea":"Ethical egoism says each person ought to do what is best for themselves in the long run. It is a theory of what you should do, not a claim that people always act selfishly (that is psychological egoism). It denies that you have basic duties to promote others’ good, though helping them is often in your interest. Its appeal is that it makes morality’s authority easy to explain: of course you have reason to do what benefits you. Compare an investor who keeps promises because a good reputation pays.","example":"The Ring of Gyges, from Plato’s Republic: a shepherd finds a ring that makes him invisible and uses it to seize power. Glaucon challenges Socrates to show that justice pays even when you could get away with anything. Egoists must answer the same challenge.","history":"Plato’s Republic (c. 375 BCE) poses the question through Glaucon. Epicurus (341 to 270 BCE) grounded ethics in one’s own pleasure. Thomas Hobbes’s Leviathan (1651) built morality from self-interest. Henry Sidgwick’s The Methods of Ethics (1874) treated egoism as a serious method. Ayn Rand’s The Virtue of Selfishness (1964) defended rational egoism.","misread":"Ethical egoism does not recommend being a jerk: egoists often argue that honesty, cooperation and friendship serve your interest best.","variants":["Rational egoism (Rand): pursue flourishing as a rational being.","Hedonistic egoism (Epicurus): pursue your own lasting pleasure.","Enlightened self-interest: cooperation usually pays."],"reading":[{"title":"Egoism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egoism/"},{"title":"Egoism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/egoism/"}]},"e4a|both":{"summary":"Dualism of practical reason: reason supports both doing what’s best overall and doing what’s best for yourself, with no way to settle conflicts.","idea":"This view says practical reason speaks with two voices. From the point of view of the universe, your good counts no more than anyone’s, so you should do what is best for all. But it is also rational to care especially about your own life. When they conflict, neither has clear authority. It denies that ethics can be fully unified. Its appeal is honesty about a real tension. Compare a soldier ordered on a mission that saves others but costs his own life: both pulls seem reasonable.","example":"Sidgwick’s case of self-sacrifice: where duty to the general good demands giving up your own happiness, and no afterlife or social reward makes it pay. Sidgwick thought only a moral order guaranteeing that virtue and happiness coincide could resolve this, and he could not prove one.","history":"Henry Sidgwick’s The Methods of Ethics (1874), especially its concluding chapter, called the conflict between rational egoism and utilitarianism the “dualism of the practical reason.” Derek Parfit discussed it in Reasons and Persons (1984) and On What Matters (2011). Katarzyna de Lazari-Radek and Peter Singer’s The Point of View of the Universe (2014) argued it can be resolved in favour of impartiality.","misread":"Sidgwick did not mean ethics is pointless; he meant reason seems unable to rank its two strongest principles when they collide.","variants":["Sidgwick’s original dualism: egoism and utilitarianism both rationally authoritative.","Parfit’s wide value-based view: both impartial and personal reasons can be sufficient."],"reading":[{"title":"Henry Sidgwick (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sidgwick/"}]},"e4b|act":{"summary":"Act consequentialism: the right act is the one with the best results, and you should aim at it directly, case by case.","idea":"This view applies the test of best results to each individual act. Rules are at most handy summaries; if breaking one would truly do more good in this case, breaking it is right. It denies that following a rule has value in itself. Its appeal is directness: if outcomes are what matter, why ever choose a worse one? Compare a chess player who evaluates each position on its own merits instead of always obeying opening principles.","example":"Williams’s Jim and the Indians: by shooting one villager Jim saves nineteen. The act consequentialist says shoot. Bernard Williams argued this ignores Jim’s integrity: his own projects and the fact that he would be the one killing.","history":"Jeremy Bentham (1789) is often read as an act utilitarian. J. J. C. Smart’s “An Outline of a System of Utilitarian Ethics,” printed in Utilitarianism: For and Against (1973) with Bernard Williams’s critique, is the classic statement. Peter Singer, in Practical Ethics (1979), and Shelly Kagan, in The Limits of Morality (1989), defended act-focused views.","misread":"Act consequentialists distinguish what makes acts right from how to decide; many still recommend rules of thumb for everyday decisions.","variants":["Act utilitarianism: maximise wellbeing in each act.","Expected value version: maximise expected rather than actual results."],"reading":[{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"}]},"e4b|twolevel":{"summary":"Two-level consequentialism: best results make acts right, but you usually reach them by following trusted rules and habits.","idea":"This view separates the standard of rightness from the method of deciding. The right act is still the one with the best results. But humans are biased, short of time and bad at predicting, so you will do better by following well-tested rules and calculating only in unusual or conflicting cases. It denies that consequentialists must calculate constantly. Its appeal is that it keeps the theory but avoids its scariest practical results. Compare an experienced pilot who flies by checklists and reasons from first principles only in an emergency.","example":"Hare’s archangel and prole: the archangel has perfect knowledge and can always calculate; the prole cannot and must rely on rules. Hare said real humans are partly both, and should mostly think like proles.","history":"Henry Sidgwick’s The Methods of Ethics (1874) argued common-sense rules usually serve utility. R. M. Hare’s Moral Thinking (1981) developed explicit intuitive and critical levels. Peter Railton’s “Alienation, Consequentialism, and the Demands of Morality” (1984) called the related view “sophisticated consequentialism.” Joshua Greene’s Moral Tribes (2013) gave a psychological case for it.","misread":"This is not rule consequentialism: the right act is still the best act, even when following the rule would miss it.","variants":["Hare’s two-level utilitarianism: intuitive rules plus critical thinking.","Sophisticated consequentialism (Railton): have dispositions that produce good results, not a calculating mindset."],"reading":[{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"},{"title":"Henry Sidgwick (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sidgwick/"}]},"e4b|rule":{"summary":"Rule consequentialism: the right act follows the rules whose general acceptance would produce the best results.","idea":"This view moves the test of results from acts to rules. Ask which set of rules would have the best consequences if nearly everyone accepted them; the right act is what those rules require, even when breaking one would do better this time. It denies that a single act’s outcome decides its rightness. Its appeal is that it explains why promise-breaking or killing the innocent is wrong while still grounding morality in wellbeing. Compare a tax system: it works only if people pay even when their own payment makes no visible difference.","example":"The question “what if everybody did that?”: one person walking on the grass does no harm, but if everyone did, the lawn would be ruined. Rule consequentialism builds this familiar test into a theory.","history":"George Berkeley’s Passive Obedience (1712) is sometimes cited as an early version. Richard Brandt developed rule utilitarianism in the mid 20th century, and David Lyons’s Forms and Limits of Utilitarianism (1965) argued it collapses into act utilitarianism. Brad Hooker’s Ideal Code, Real World (2000) is the leading modern defence.","misread":"Rule consequentialism isn’t rule worship: it can include rules like “prevent disaster,” and its rules are chosen for their results.","variants":["Ideal code rule consequentialism (Hooker): rules best if accepted by the overwhelming majority.","Actual rule consequentialism: follow society’s existing rules when they work well."],"reading":[{"title":"Rule Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism-rule/"}]},"e4r|none":{"summary":"Pure consequentialism: no act is off-limits in principle; if it truly gives the best results, it is right.","idea":"This view accepts no constraints or exceptions beyond producing the best outcome. Killing, lying or betrayal are wrong only when, and because, they make things worse. It denies both moral constraints (acts forbidden even when beneficial) and personal options (permission to do less than the best). Its appeal is consistency: if outcomes are what ultimately matter, a constraint that leads to more deaths seems hard to justify. Compare a firefighter who must decide which building to save based purely on how many people are inside.","example":"The paradox of deontology, as Samuel Scheffler and Robert Nozick discussed it: if killing is so bad, how can it be wrong to kill one to prevent five other killings? Pure consequentialists say it cannot be.","history":"J. J. C. Smart’s contribution to Utilitarianism: For and Against (1973) embraced this pure form. Shelly Kagan’s The Limits of Morality (1989) argued that ordinary morality’s constraints and options both lack a good defence. Peter Singer’s work generally follows this line.","misread":"Pure consequentialists usually still advise against lying or killing in practice, since these almost never truly give the best results.","variants":["Maximising act consequentialism: always do the very best act.","Kagan’s extremism: no constraints and no options."],"reading":[{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"}]},"e4r|constrained":{"summary":"Constrained consequentialism: aim at the best results, but some acts, like torture or killing the innocent, stay off-limits.","idea":"This view takes good outcomes as the main goal of morality while recognising limits on how you may pursue them. You should generally promote the best results, but never by certain means, such as killing or torturing innocents. It denies that outcomes justify absolutely anything. Its appeal is that it keeps most of consequentialism’s concern for results while matching the widely held belief that some acts are simply wrong. Compare a company that seeks profit within strict legal and ethical lines it refuses to cross.","example":"Nozick’s utilitarianism of rights: should you violate one person’s rights to prevent several other rights violations? Nozick argued that rights work as side constraints, not goals, so you may not, and constrained views agree.","history":"Robert Nozick’s Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974) distinguished side constraints from goals. Amartya Sen’s “Rights and Agency” (1982) proposed a goal rights system mixing outcomes with rights. Later work on consequentializing, by Douglas Portmore and Jamie Dreier, asked whether constraints can be built into an agent-relative ranking of outcomes.","misread":"This is not full deontology: within the constraints, the view still says to produce the best results.","variants":["Side constraints (Nozick): rights limit how goals may be pursued.","Agent-relative consequentialism: constraints built into how each agent ranks outcomes.","Threshold view: constraints hold except to avert catastrophe."],"reading":[{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"},{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"}]},"e4r|prereq":{"summary":"An agent-centred prerogative: you may sometimes put your own life and projects first, even when that isn’t best overall.","idea":"This view keeps best results as the main standard but allows a personal exception. You are permitted to weigh your own interests and projects somewhat more heavily than an impartial count would, so you need not give up your career or savings whenever a stranger would gain slightly more. It denies that morality may demand everything. Its appeal is that it answers the charge that consequentialism is too demanding. Compare being allowed to spend a weekend on your own hobby rather than volunteering every spare hour.","example":"The demandingness objection: if pure consequentialism is true, nearly every evening out, gift or holiday is wrong, since the money could save lives. A prerogative gives a principled limit to this demand.","history":"Samuel Scheffler’s The Rejection of Consequentialism (1982) introduced the agent-centred prerogative, arguing it reflects the independence of the personal point of view. Bernard Williams’s integrity objection (1973) motivated it. Shelly Kagan’s The Limits of Morality (1989) criticised it, and Douglas Portmore’s Commonsense Consequentialism (2011) defended a related view.","misread":"A prerogative is an option, not a duty to be selfish: you may still choose the impartially best act.","variants":["Scheffler’s hybrid view: consequentialism plus a prerogative but no constraints.","Satisficing consequentialism (Slote): doing well enough, not the best, can be right."],"reading":[{"title":"Reasons for Action: Agent-Neutral vs. Agent-Relative (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reasons-agent/"},{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"}]},"e4c|hedon":{"summary":"Hedonism about wellbeing: pleasure is the only thing good for you and pain the only thing bad, so felt experience is all.","idea":"Hedonism says a life goes well for you exactly to the extent it contains pleasant rather than painful experience. Achievements, knowledge and friendship matter only because of how they feel. It denies that anything can make your life better if it makes no difference to your experience. Its appeal is that pleasure and pain seem obviously good and bad, and it explains why it is odd to say someone was harmed by something they never noticed. Compare a delicious meal: its value lies in the enjoyment.","example":"The experience machine itself, from Robert Nozick’s Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974): neuroscientists could give you any experiences you like. Hedonists must say plugging in would be no loss, and some, like Roger Crisp, bite this bullet.","history":"Aristippus and the Cyrenaics (4th century BCE) prized immediate pleasure; Epicurus (341 to 270 BCE) prized calm, lasting pleasure. Jeremy Bentham (1789) made hedonism the basis of utilitarianism; John Stuart Mill (1861) added higher and lower pleasures. Roger Crisp’s Reasons and the Good (2006) and Ben Bramble defend modern hedonism.","misread":"Hedonism isn’t about wild partying; most hedonists, from Epicurus to Mill, stress deep, lasting pleasures like friendship and learning.","variants":["Quantitative hedonism (Bentham): pleasures differ only in amount.","Qualitative hedonism (Mill): some pleasures are higher in kind.","Attitudinal hedonism (Feldman): pleasure as taking pleasure in states of affairs."],"reading":[{"title":"Hedonism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hedonism/"},{"title":"Well-Being (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/well-being/"}]},"e4c|pref":{"summary":"Preference satisfaction: your life goes well when your desires are actually fulfilled, not just when you feel they are.","idea":"This view says what is good for you is getting what you want, really getting it. If you want your novel to be good, it is not enough to believe it is good; it must be. It denies that feeling is everything, which is why the experience machine falls short. Its appeal is that it respects each person’s own values instead of imposing a list. Compare a parent who wants their child to be happy: learning years later that it was all faked would show that the wish was not fulfilled.","example":"The deceived businessman, discussed by Thomas Nagel: a man dies happy, believing his family loves him and his partners respect him, when in fact they mocked and betrayed him. Desire theorists say his life went worse, though he never knew.","history":"Desire views became central in 20th century economics and philosophy. R. M. Hare and Peter Singer, in Practical Ethics (1979), defended preference utilitarianism. John Harsanyi used preferences in welfare economics. Richard Brandt’s A Theory of the Good and the Right (1979) and Peter Railton refined it into informed desire theories.","misread":"Desire theories needn’t count every passing whim; many count only informed or rational desires.","variants":["Informed desire theory: what you would want if fully informed.","Global desire theory (Parfit): preferences about your life as a whole.","Preference utilitarianism: maximise preference satisfaction."],"reading":[{"title":"Well-Being (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/well-being/"}]},"e4c|objlist":{"summary":"Objective list theory: some things, like friendship, knowledge and achievement, are good for you whether or not you want them.","idea":"This view says wellbeing consists in having certain goods, typically friendship, knowledge, achievement, health and autonomy. They benefit you even if you do not desire them or feel pleasure from them. It denies that your attitudes alone make something good for you. Its appeal is that it explains why a life spent counting blades of grass seems to go badly even if the person enjoys it. Compare a teenager who doesn’t want an education: many think learning still benefits them.","example":"John Rawls’s grass counter: a brilliant person whose only desire is to count the blades of grass on lawns. If he succeeds and enjoys it, desire and hedonist theories say he is doing well. Objective list theorists say something is missing.","history":"Derek Parfit named “objective list theories” in Appendix I of Reasons and Persons (1984). Aristotle’s Nicomachean Ethics is often seen as an ancestor. John Finnis’s Natural Law and Natural Rights (1980) listed basic goods; Martha Nussbaum’s capabilities approach lists central capabilities. Rawls’s grass counter appears in A Theory of Justice (1971).","misread":"Objective list theorists can value pleasure too; it is usually on the list, just not the only item.","variants":["Perfectionism: wellbeing is developing your human nature.","Natural law basic goods (Finnis).","Capabilities approach (Nussbaum, Sen)."],"reading":[{"title":"Well-Being (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/well-being/"}]},"e4c|nonwel":{"summary":"Ideal (non-welfarist) utilitarianism: maximise goodness, where beauty and knowledge can be good in themselves, beyond wellbeing.","idea":"This view says the outcomes that matter are not only about how lives go for people. Some things, such as beauty, knowledge or virtue, are intrinsically good even if no one benefits from them. The right act brings about the most of all such goods. It denies welfarism, the claim that only wellbeing matters. Its appeal is that it explains why destroying an unseen masterpiece or a pristine ecosystem seems to make the world worse. Compare protecting a remote coral reef that almost no one will ever visit.","example":"Moore’s two worlds: imagine one world exceedingly beautiful and another a heap of filth, neither ever seen by anyone. Moore argued it would still be better for the beautiful one to exist, so beauty is good in itself.","history":"G. E. Moore’s Principia Ethica (1903) argued that intrinsic goods include beauty and personal affection and that right acts maximise good. Hastings Rashdall named “ideal utilitarianism” in The Theory of Good and Evil (1907). Discussion continues in work on intrinsic value and in environmental ethics.","misread":"Ideal utilitarians do not ignore wellbeing; pleasure and happiness are among the goods, just not the only ones.","variants":["Moore’s ideal utilitarianism: maximise intrinsic good, chiefly beauty and personal affection.","Rashdall’s ideal utilitarianism: virtue and knowledge alongside pleasure."],"reading":[{"title":"Moore’s Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moore-moral/"},{"title":"Intrinsic vs. Extrinsic Value (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/value-intrinsic-extrinsic/"}]},"e4d|pleasure":{"summary":"Epicurean egoism: your own good is lasting pleasure and freedom from pain, wisely managed.","idea":"This view combines egoism with hedonism. Your aim should be your own pleasure, but understood as calm contentment: freedom from bodily pain and mental disturbance. It denies that intense indulgence is the best route, since it usually brings more pain later. Simple living, friendship and freedom from fear are the keys. Its appeal is its practical wisdom about what actually makes people happy. Compare choosing a quiet evening with friends over a party that leaves you exhausted and broke.","example":"Epicurus’s classification of desires: natural and necessary ones (food, shelter) should be met; natural but unnecessary ones (fancy food) can be enjoyed if easy; empty ones (fame, wealth) should be dropped, since chasing them brings anxiety.","history":"Epicurus (341 to 270 BCE) taught in his Garden in Athens; his Letter to Menoeceus summarises the ethics, and Lucretius’s On the Nature of Things (1st century BCE) carried it to Rome. The goals were ataraxia, tranquillity of mind, and aponia, absence of bodily pain. Pierre Gassendi revived Epicureanism in the 17th century.","misread":"“Epicurean” today suggests fine food, but Epicurus taught a simple, frugal life aimed at tranquillity.","variants":["Classical Epicureanism: ataraxia and aponia as the highest pleasures.","Cyrenaic hedonism (contrast): immediate bodily pleasures."],"reading":[{"title":"Epicurus (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epicurus/"},{"title":"Hedonism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hedonism/"}]},"e4d|desire":{"summary":"Desire-based egoism: your good is getting whatever you actually want, whatever that is.","idea":"This view says your own good is the satisfaction of your desires, with no outside standard for which desires are worthy. If what you want is to collect stamps, getting stamps is good for you. The egoist then says you should pursue this good. It denies that there is a single right way to live for everyone. Its appeal is respect for individual differences and a minimal, scientific-sounding account of value. Compare economics, where people are modelled as pursuing their own preferences, whatever they are.","example":"Hobbes’s definition of good: whatever someone desires, they call good, and whatever they hate, they call evil. There is nothing simply good in itself, apart from people’s wants, so each person’s good is set by their own desires.","history":"Thomas Hobbes’s Leviathan (1651, chapter 6) defined good as the object of desire. Rational choice theory in economics, from the 20th century onward, treats rationality as maximising one’s own preferences. David Gauthier’s Morals by Agreement (1986) used this preference-based picture to derive morality from self-interest.","misread":"Desire-based egoism doesn’t assume all desires are selfish; you may want others to thrive, and satisfying that counts too.","variants":["Hobbesian egoism: good as the object of appetite.","Rational choice egoism: maximise your considered preferences."],"reading":[{"title":"Egoism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egoism/"},{"title":"Hobbes’s Moral and Political Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hobbes-moral/"}]},"e4d|flourish":{"summary":"Rational egoism: your good is your flourishing as a rational, independent person living by your own judgment and achievements.","idea":"This view says your own good is not mere pleasure or desire satisfaction but the life proper to a rational being: productive work, independent thought, earned self-esteem. Each person should pursue this flourishing as their highest moral purpose. It denies that sacrificing yourself for others is a virtue, and also denies that taking from others serves your real interest. Its appeal is its celebration of self-reliance and achievement. Compare an architect who builds what she judges best rather than what others demand.","example":"Howard Roark in Ayn Rand’s novel The Fountainhead (1943): an architect who refuses to compromise his designs for public taste or money. Rand presented him as the ideal of a rational egoist.","history":"Ayn Rand’s novels The Fountainhead (1943) and Atlas Shrugged (1957), and her essay collection The Virtue of Selfishness (1964), set out her philosophy, Objectivism. Tara Smith’s Ayn Rand’s Normative Ethics (2006) gave an academic reconstruction. The view draws on Aristotle’s idea of flourishing, though Aristotle was not an egoist in Rand’s sense.","misread":"Rand’s egoism opposes exploiting others; she held that rational self-interest requires honesty, trade and respect for rights.","variants":["Objectivism (Rand): reason, purpose and self-esteem as cardinal values."],"reading":[{"title":"Ayn Rand (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ayn-rand/"},{"title":"Egoism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egoism/"}]},"e4d|longrun":{"summary":"Enlightened self-interest: your good is your long-run interest, which usually means cooperating with and helping others.","idea":"This view says you should pursue your own interest, but intelligently. Over time, honesty, fairness and helping others tend to pay off through trust, reputation and reciprocity. It denies that self-interest means grabbing short-term gains. Its appeal is that it reconciles self-interest with ordinary decency. Compare a shopkeeper who gives honest change even to children, because a reputation for honesty brings customers back for years.","example":"The repeated prisoner’s dilemma: in Robert Axelrod’s computer tournaments, the simple strategy tit-for-tat, cooperate first then copy the other player, beat more aggressive strategies. Cooperation proved to be in each player’s long-run interest.","history":"Thomas Hobbes’s Leviathan (1651) argued that rational self-interest leads to peace and agreement. Alexis de Tocqueville’s Democracy in America (1835 to 1840) praised Americans’ “self-interest rightly understood.” Robert Axelrod’s The Evolution of Cooperation (1984) gave game-theoretic support. David Gauthier’s Morals by Agreement (1986) derived cooperative morality from self-interest.","misread":"It doesn’t claim helping always pays; it claims that, as a general policy, cooperation tends to serve you best.","variants":["Tocqueville’s self-interest rightly understood.","Hobbesian contractarianism: morality as rational agreement among self-interested people."],"reading":[{"title":"Egoism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egoism/"},{"title":"Contractarianism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/contractarianism/"}]},"e4e|deont":{"summary":"Deontology grounded in reason: duties come from morality itself, and reason can see that some acts are simply wrong.","idea":"This view holds that moral duties are not created by God, agreement or results; they are truths that reason can grasp, much as it grasps truths of mathematics. Lying or killing the innocent is wrong in itself. It denies that duties need any further foundation. Its appeal is that it explains why moral rules feel binding regardless of anyone’s wishes. Compare knowing that two plus two is four: you don’t need a vote or a command to see it.","example":"Kant’s universal law test: could you will that everyone follow your maxim? A false promise to borrow money fails, because if everyone made false promises, no one would believe promises and the scheme would collapse.","history":"Immanuel Kant’s Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785) and Critique of Practical Reason (1788) grounded duty in pure practical reason. H. A. Prichard’s “Does Moral Philosophy Rest on a Mistake?” (1912) and W. D. Ross’s The Right and the Good (1930) held that we directly know our duties. Christine Korsgaard and Onora O’Neill developed Kantian ethics further.","misread":"Grounding duties in reason doesn’t mean everyone agrees on them; it means their authority doesn’t depend on anyone’s say-so.","variants":["Kantian rationalism: duties follow from the categorical imperative.","Intuitionism (Ross, Prichard): basic duties are self-evident.","Kantian constructivism (Korsgaard): duties built from the standpoint of rational agency."],"reading":[{"title":"Kant’s Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-moral/"},{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"}]},"e4e|contract":{"summary":"Contractualism: an act is wrong if it is ruled out by principles no one affected could reasonably reject.","idea":"This view grounds duties in justifiability to others. An act is right if it is allowed by principles that everyone affected could accept; wrong if someone could reasonably object. It denies that morality is about maximising good or obeying commands. Its appeal is that it treats each person as an equal whose point of view must be answered. Compare a group setting house rules: a fair rule is one you could defend to each roommate, including the one it burdens most.","example":"Scanlon’s World Cup case: a technician is trapped and suffering electric shocks during a broadcast watched by millions; rescuing him means interrupting the match. Scanlon argued you must rescue him, since his complaint outweighs each viewer’s, however many viewers there are.","history":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau’s The Social Contract (1762) and Kant’s ideas are ancestors. John Rawls’s A Theory of Justice (1971) used the veil of ignorance to choose fair principles. T. M. Scanlon’s “Contractualism and Utilitarianism” (1982) and What We Owe to Each Other (1998) gave the main statement. Contractarianism, from Hobbes to David Gauthier, is a self-interest-based cousin.","misread":"The contract is hypothetical: no actual agreement is needed, only principles no one could reasonably reject.","variants":["Scanlonian contractualism: principles no one could reasonably reject.","Rawlsian contractualism: principles chosen behind a veil of ignorance.","Contractarianism (Hobbes, Gauthier): rules that serve each party’s interest."],"reading":[{"title":"Contractualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/contractualism/"},{"title":"Contractarianism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/contractarianism/"}]},"e4e|divine":{"summary":"Theistic ethics: moral duties come from God, through divine commands or the moral order God built into human nature.","idea":"This view grounds morality in God. On divine command theory, an act is obligatory because God commands it. On natural law theory, God created human nature with built-in purposes, and morality is what helps us fulfil them, knowable by reason. It denies that morality floats free of any source. Its appeal is that it explains why moral duties have such authority and objectivity. Compare how a law gets its force from a legitimate lawgiver, though the analogy has limits.","example":"The Euthyphro dilemma, from Plato’s Euthyphro: is something good because the gods love it, or do they love it because it is good? If the first, morality seems arbitrary; if the second, goodness seems independent of God. Theistic ethicists offer several answers.","history":"Plato’s Euthyphro (c. 399 to 395 BCE) posed the dilemma. Thomas Aquinas’s Summa Theologiae (1265 to 1274) set out natural law. William of Ockham (c. 1287 to 1347) defended voluntarism. In Islamic thought, Ash‘arites such as al-Ghazali held that right is what God commands, against Mu‘tazilite rationalism. Robert Adams’s Finite and Infinite Goods (1999) defends a modified divine command theory.","misread":"Theistic ethics doesn’t imply nonbelievers can’t be moral; natural law theorists hold moral truths are knowable by reason alone.","variants":["Divine command theory: right because God commands it.","Natural law theory: right because it fits the nature God created.","Modified divine command theory (Adams): commands of a loving God."],"reading":[{"title":"Divine Command Theory (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/divine-command-theory/"},{"title":"The Natural Law Tradition in Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/natural-law-ethics/"},{"title":"Theological Voluntarism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/voluntarism-theological/"}]},"e4k|doing":{"summary":"Actively causing harm is morally worse than failing to prevent the same harm, even when the result is identical.","idea":"This view says the line that matters most is between what you do and what you merely let happen. Your duty not to harm people is strict; your duty to help them is real but weaker and easier to outweigh. That is why you may not push someone into a river, yet you are not a murderer every time you fail to rescue a stranger. Defenders like that it explains everyday intuitions and protects each person's life from being spent for others, while leaving room for a life of your own.","example":"Foot's trolley case: a driver whose brakes fail may steer away from five toward one, since either way he is driving into someone. But a surgeon may not cut up one healthy patient to give organs to five, since that would be killing rather than failing to save.","history":"The distinction is old in law and religion: most legal systems punish killing far more than failing to rescue. Philippa Foot made it central to modern ethics in “The Problem of Abortion and the Doctrine of Double Effect” (1967). Warren Quinn defended it in a 1989 paper, and Frances Kamm developed detailed versions in Intricate Ethics (2007).","misread":"It does not say letting die is fine; it says killing is worse, so allowing harm can still be seriously wrong.","variants":["Strong version: allowing harm is permitted unless helping is cheap.","Moderate version: allowing harm is wrong but less wrong than doing it."],"reading":[{"title":"Doing vs. Allowing Harm (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/doing-allowing/"},{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"}]},"e4k|intend":{"summary":"Harm you aim at, as a goal or a means, is worse than the same harm you foresee as a side effect.","idea":"This is the doctrine of double effect. One act can have two effects, a good one you aim at and a bad one you only foresee. The view says you may sometimes accept bad side effects for a serious enough good, but you may never make harm your goal or your means. A doctor who gives morphine to ease pain, knowing it may shorten life, differs from one who gives it in order to kill. Defenders think intention shows what your will is set on, and that this matters morally.","example":"Strategic bomber and terror bomber: both expect the same number of civilians to die. The strategic bomber targets a weapons factory and foresees civilian deaths. The terror bomber kills civilians to break the enemy's morale. Many judge the first permissible in a just war and the second murder.","history":"Thomas Aquinas is usually credited with the idea, in his discussion of killing in self defence in the Summa Theologiae (II-II, question 64, article 7). It became a staple of Catholic moral theology and just war theory. Elizabeth Anscombe defended it in the twentieth century, and it shapes debates in medical ethics about pain relief and end of life care.","misread":"It does not allow any side effect at all: the harm must also be proportionate to the good and not avoidable by a better route.","variants":["Traditional four conditions: good act, good intention, harm not the means, proportionate reason.","Means principle reading: what matters is that the victim is used, not your mental state."],"reading":[{"title":"Doctrine of Double Effect (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/double-effect/"},{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"}]},"e4k|means":{"summary":"What makes an act wrong is treating a person merely as an instrument for your goals, not as someone who matters in themselves.","idea":"This view puts respect for persons at the centre. Using others is normal: you use a baker to get bread. What is wrong is using someone merely as a means, in a way they could not possibly agree to, ignoring that they have goals of their own. Killing one patient for organs treats him as a box of spare parts. Giving a scarce drug to five instead of one does not use the one at all. Defenders like that this explains why some harms feel like violations rather than mere losses.","example":"The organ transplant case: five patients will die without organs, and one healthy visitor has a perfect match. Killing him would save five, yet nearly everyone thinks it is wrong, because his body would be used as a resource for others without his consent.","history":"The idea comes from Immanuel Kant's Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785), whose Formula of Humanity says to treat humanity always as an end and never merely as a means. Robert Nozick built on it in Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), and Kamm and Korsgaard have developed modern versions.","misread":"It does not forbid using people at all; it forbids using them merely as means, without respect for their own will.","variants":["Consent version: using someone is wrong when they could not rationally agree to how they are treated.","Kamm's principle of permissible harm: harm may come from a greater good, not be the means to it."],"reading":[{"title":"Kant's Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-moral/"},{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"}]},"e4l|victim":{"summary":"The constraint against killing exists because of the victim: each person has a right not to be sacrificed, whatever others need.","idea":"This view grounds the ban on killing one to save five in the dignity and rights of the person who would be killed. Each of us is a separate person, not a part of some larger sum, so the needs of five do not entitle anyone to take one life. The focus is outward, on what the victim is owed. Defenders like that it explains why the wrong is a wrong done to someone, and that it treats people as having a kind of moral inviolability.","example":"Nozick's point about separateness of persons: there is no social entity that gains when one person is sacrificed for others. There are only individual people, and using one of them for others' benefit does not make up for what he loses.","history":"Kant's idea of dignity lies in the background. Robert Nozick, in Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), called rights “side constraints” on what anyone may do. Frances Kamm, in Morality, Mortality (1996) and Intricate Ethics (2007), argued that constraints express each person's high status as inviolable.","misread":"It is not that the five matter less; it is that no one may be treated as a resource, including them.","variants":["Rights-based constraint: the victim's right sets the limit.","Inviolability account: constraints express the moral status of every person."],"reading":[{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"},{"title":"Kant's Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-moral/"}]},"e4l|agent":{"summary":"The constraint exists because of you: each person is responsible first for their own actions, and killing would make you a killer.","idea":"This view grounds the ban in the agent's own responsibility. Morality gives each of us a special concern with what we ourselves do, not just with how the world turns out. If you kill the one, the killing is your act, even if others would otherwise kill five. Philosophers call this an agent-relative constraint: it tells you not to kill, rather than telling you to minimise killings overall. Defenders say this matches how we feel responsibility: a doctor's first job is not to harm her own patient.","example":"Williams's case of Jim and the Indians: a captain will shoot twenty villagers unless Jim, a visitor, shoots one of them himself. Williams argued that what Jim would be doing, and his integrity as the one doing it, makes a real moral difference.","history":"Samuel Scheffler named “agent-centred restrictions” in The Rejection of Consequentialism (1982). Thomas Nagel, in The View from Nowhere (1986), distinguished agent-relative from agent-neutral reasons. Bernard Williams's critique of utilitarianism in Utilitarianism: For and Against (1973) is a classic source. The idea is now central to debates about whether deontology is rational.","misread":"It is not selfish concern for clean hands: the constraint is about your action toward the victim, not your reputation or feelings.","variants":["Agent-relative reasons: each person's reasons concern their own acts.","Integrity objection: Williams's view that morality must leave room for an agent's own projects."],"reading":[{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"},{"title":"Doing vs. Allowing Harm (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/doing-allowing/"}]},"e4f|absolute":{"summary":"Some kinds of act, such as murdering the innocent or lying, are always wrong, whatever good would come from doing them.","idea":"Absolutism says certain moral rules have no exceptions. No amount of benefit, even saving many lives, could make torturing an innocent child or framing an innocent man acceptable. Defenders say this is exactly what makes these rules serious: a rule that can be broken for a good enough reason will be broken whenever someone thinks the reason is good enough. Absolutes also protect people from being calculated away. Critics point to disasters, but absolutists answer that some deeds are beyond what anyone may do.","example":"The murderer at the door: a killer asks where your friend is hiding. Kant famously argued in a 1797 essay that you still may not lie to him, though you may stay silent. Many absolutists accept other bans but reject Kant's view on lying.","history":"Kant's Groundwork (1785) and his essay “On a Supposed Right to Lie from Philanthropy” (1797) are classic sources. Catholic moral teaching holds some acts “intrinsically evil”, and Elizabeth Anscombe defended absolute prohibitions in “Modern Moral Philosophy” (1958). Some readings of Jain and Buddhist precepts against killing are also strict.","misread":"Absolutism need not mean many rules: most absolutists list only a few, such as not killing or torturing the innocent.","variants":["Kantian absolutism: duties of right come from reason and admit no exceptions.","Catholic intrinsic evil: some acts are wrong by their very object."],"reading":[{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"},{"title":"Kant's Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-moral/"}]},"e4f|threshold":{"summary":"Duties hold firm against ordinary gains, but can be overridden when the stakes reach a catastrophic level.","idea":"Threshold deontology tries to keep what is right in both duty and outcomes. Below a certain line, you may not kill one to save five, or even fifty. But if the alternative is a nuclear attack on a city, the duty finally gives way. Defenders find this the most honest reaction to extreme cases: almost no one thinks you should let a million die rather than tell one lie. Critics ask where exactly the threshold falls and why it should be there.","example":"The ticking bomb: a terrorist has hidden a bomb that will kill thousands, and only he knows where. Threshold deontologists typically say torture is forbidden for ordinary gains but might be permitted here, if the stakes truly are catastrophic and certain.","history":"Michael S. Moore defended threshold deontology in “Torture and the Balance of Evils” (1989). Thomas Nagel, in “War and Massacre” (1972), suggested that absolute bans might give way in extreme cases. The view is now widely discussed in the Stanford entry on deontological ethics.","misread":"It is not just consequentialism with extra steps: below the threshold, numbers really do not decide the case.","variants":["Fixed threshold: one level of catastrophe for all duties.","Sliding threshold: the bar rises with how serious the duty is."],"reading":[{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"}]},"e4f|mixed":{"summary":"Duties differ in strength: some, like not torturing innocents, are absolute, while others, like keeping promises, can be outweighed.","idea":"This view rejects a one size fits all answer. Breaking a promise to lunch so you can save a drowning child is plainly right, yet torturing a baby to save many still seems forbidden. So some duties are defeasible, giving way to weightier reasons, while a small core admit no exceptions. Defenders say this matches the shape of ordinary moral judgment better than either pure absolutism or one universal threshold, since duties really do seem to come in different strengths.","example":"Promise versus rescue: you promised to meet a friend, but on the way you can save someone from a fire. You should break the promise. Yet no rescue could justify torturing an innocent child for information, the mixed view says.","history":"W. D. Ross, in The Right and the Good (1930), treated most duties as prima facie, able to be outweighed. The UN Convention against Torture (1984) states that no exceptional circumstances whatever can justify torture. The mixed combination is a common position in contemporary deontology rather than one named school.","misread":"It is not inconsistent: it simply denies that every duty has the same weight.","variants":["Ross-plus-absolutes: prima facie duties with a few exceptionless constraints.","Rights hierarchy: some rights can be overridden, others are non-derogable."],"reading":[{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"},{"title":"William David Ross (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/william-david-ross/"}]},"e4g|mutual":{"summary":"Moral rules are a bargain between self-interested people: each gains more by accepting them than by living without them.","idea":"Contractarianism says morality is a deal. Without rules, life would be insecure for everyone, so rational people agree to restrain themselves on condition that others do too. Rules count as moral because they are mutually advantageous. The view denies that morality needs anything mysterious, like objective values or God: it needs only people who want to do well and see that cooperation pays. Defenders like that it explains why morality binds even someone who cares only about himself, and that it is realistic about human motives.","example":"Hobbes's state of nature: without a government or shared rules, life would be a war of all against all, “solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short”. Everyone does better by agreeing to keep the peace.","history":"Thomas Hobbes, Leviathan (1651), is the founding text. Glaucon states an early version in Plato's Republic, book II. In modern ethics, David Gauthier's Morals by Agreement (1986) and Jan Narveson's The Libertarian Idea (1988) are leading defences. In game theory terms, morality is often modelled as a solution to the prisoner's dilemma, and contractarians draw on this work.","misread":"It does not say people should be selfish; it says morality can be justified even to people who are.","variants":["Hobbesian contractarianism: rules for mutual security.","Gauthier's constrained maximization: be the kind of person who keeps fair deals."],"reading":[{"title":"Contractarianism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/contractarianism/"}]},"e4g|fair":{"summary":"Moral rules are those that no one could reasonably reject, treating each person as an equal whose point of view counts.","idea":"Contractualism also uses the idea of agreement, but the point is fairness rather than advantage. A rule is right if it can be justified to each person affected, including the weakest, so that none could reasonably reject it. The view denies that bargaining power should decide morality. Defenders like that it explains why wronging someone is something done to them: you act on a rule you cannot justify to them. It also puts mutual respect, not calculation, at the heart of ethics.","example":"Rawls's veil of ignorance: choose the rules for society without knowing whether you will be rich or poor, strong or weak. Rules chosen behind the veil are fair because no one can tilt them toward themselves.","history":"Rousseau's The Social Contract (1762) and Kant are forerunners. John Rawls's A Theory of Justice (1971) revived the approach for politics, and T. M. Scanlon's What We Owe to Each Other (1998) developed it as a theory of right and wrong.","misread":"It is not about real agreements anyone signed; the contract is hypothetical, a test of whether rules are justifiable.","variants":["Rawlsian contractualism: principles chosen behind a veil of ignorance.","Scanlonian contractualism: principles no one could reasonably reject."],"reading":[{"title":"Contractualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/contractualism/"}]},"e4s|indirect":{"summary":"Animals and infants are protected because people who can agree care about them, not because they are parties to the agreement.","idea":"This view accepts that only those who can make or understand agreements are directly covered by the rules. Animals and newborns cannot, so their protection comes through others: we agree not to harm what other people love and value, and cruelty damages our own character. Defenders say this keeps the theory clear and still forbids most cruelty in practice. It denies that every creature that can suffer has the same moral standing as a reasoning adult.","example":"Kant's dog: Kant said that a man who shoots his loyal old dog because it can no longer serve him damages his own humanity, which he ought to cultivate. The wrong lies in the man's character, not a duty owed to the dog.","history":"Kant's Lectures on Ethics (1780s) argued that duties to animals are indirect duties to humanity. Peter Carruthers's The Animals Issue (1992) defended an indirect contractualist view of animals. Hobbesian contractarians like Gauthier accept a similar consequence. The view remains a standard reference point in debates about whether contract theories can protect beings who cannot bargain.","misread":"It does not permit cruelty: indirect protection can still forbid nearly all of it, just for different reasons.","variants":["Character version: cruelty corrupts the agent.","Sentiment version: others care about animals and infants, so harming them wrongs those people."],"reading":[{"title":"The Moral Status of Animals (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-animal/"},{"title":"Contractualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/contractualism/"}]},"e4s|trustee":{"summary":"Those who cannot speak for themselves are still covered, because the rules must be justifiable to someone representing their interests.","idea":"This view widens the circle of agreement. A rule is unjust if it could not be justified to a trustee speaking on behalf of a baby, a severely disabled person or an animal. They need not bargain or reason themselves; it is enough that they have a good that can go well or badly. Defenders say this keeps the core contractualist idea, justifiability to each, while answering the worry that the weakest are left out. It denies that being able to argue is what earns moral standing.","example":"Behind the veil: Mark Rowlands proposed that, in Rawls's original position, parties should not know whether they will turn out to be rational, so for all they know they could be another animal. Then rules permitting cruelty to animals would be rejected.","history":"Scanlon, in What We Owe to Each Other (1998), discussed trustees who could object on behalf of beings unable to speak for themselves. Mark Rowlands extended Rawls's framework in Animals Like Us (2002), and others have built on these suggestions.","misread":"It does not claim animals actually agree; trustees represent their interests in a hypothetical justification.","variants":["Scanlonian trusteeship: a trustee can reject principles on their behalf.","Rowlands's expanded veil: parties do not know whether they will be rational."],"reading":[{"title":"Contractualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/contractualism/"},{"title":"The Moral Status of Animals (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-animal/"}]},"e4s|gap":{"summary":"Agreement explains what we owe each other as reasoners, but duties to animals and others outside the contract come from a separate source.","idea":"This view accepts the limit honestly. Agreement-based ethics is a theory of one part of morality, the duties of fairness among people who can reason together. It need not cover everything. Duties to animals might come from the badness of suffering, or from virtues like compassion. Defenders like that this avoids stretching the contract until it means nothing, and it lets them combine contractualism with other moral sources. Critics worry this concedes that agreement is not the foundation of ethics after all.","example":"Rawls's admission: in A Theory of Justice he said that justice as fairness does not cover how we should treat animals, though cruelty to them is still wrong. Our duties there come from elsewhere, such as compassion.","history":"John Rawls, A Theory of Justice (1971), section 77, set animals outside justice while condemning cruelty. Scanlon, in What We Owe to Each Other (1998), presented contractualism as an account of one narrower domain of morality. Martha Nussbaum's Frontiers of Justice (2006) pressed this gap as a key objection.","misread":"It does not say we owe animals nothing; it says the contract is not where those duties come from.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Contractualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/contractualism/"},{"title":"The Moral Status of Animals (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-animal/"}]},"e4h|command":{"summary":"An act is right simply because God commands it; God's will creates morality and could in principle have willed differently.","idea":"Voluntarism says moral facts depend on God's will. Murder is wrong because God forbids it, not because of some standard God must follow. This preserves God's complete sovereignty: nothing, not even goodness, stands above God. Defenders also say it gives morality a clear authority and explains why moral duties feel like commands from someone. The cost is the first horn of the Euthyphro dilemma: if God had commanded cruelty, cruelty would have been right, a conclusion bold voluntarists accept.","example":"Euthyphro's dilemma: in Plato's dialogue, Socrates asks whether the gods love what is holy because it is holy, or whether it is holy because the gods love it. Voluntarists take the second horn for God and rightness.","history":"William of Ockham (14th century) is the classic Christian voluntarist, and Duns Scotus held a partial version. In Islam, the Ash'arite school held that good and evil are fixed by God's command. Philip Quinn's Divine Commands and Moral Requirements (1978) is a modern defence.","misread":"It does not say God is arbitrary in practice; it says God's commands are not answerable to an outside standard.","variants":["Strong voluntarism: all moral truths depend on God's will.","Partial voluntarism: some duties depend on God's will, others on God's nature or reason."],"reading":[{"title":"Theological Voluntarism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/voluntarism-theological/"},{"title":"Divine Command Theory (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/divine-command-theory/"}]},"e4h|right":{"summary":"God commands what is right because it is right; goodness has its own standard, which God knows perfectly and reveals.","idea":"This view takes the other horn of the Euthyphro dilemma. Right and wrong are rooted in the nature of things, especially human nature and what makes human life flourish, and God commands accordingly. Reason can discover much of morality without revelation. Defenders like that this makes God's commands non-arbitrary and lets believers and non-believers share moral reasoning. Critics answer that it seems to make God unnecessary for morality, though natural law theorists reply that God is the creator of the natures that ground it.","example":"Theft in natural law: stealing is wrong because human beings flourish through secure cooperation and property for their needs. God forbids theft because it is harmful, and anyone reflecting carefully could see why.","history":"Thomas Aquinas set out natural law in the Summa Theologiae (1265 to 1274). Earlier, the Stoics and Cicero spoke of a law of nature. In Islam, the Mu'tazilite school held that reason can know good and evil independently of revelation. John Finnis's Natural Law and Natural Rights (1980) is a leading modern version.","misread":"Natural law does not mean whatever is natural is good; it means morality follows from what fulfils human nature.","variants":["Thomistic natural law: morality grounded in human nature and known by reason.","New natural law: Grisez and Finnis's theory of basic human goods."],"reading":[{"title":"The Natural Law Tradition in Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/natural-law-ethics/"},{"title":"Religion and Morality in Western Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-morality/"}]},"e4h|nature":{"summary":"God's commands create our duties, but they always flow from God's perfectly good nature, so they could never be cruel.","idea":"This view tries to escape the Euthyphro dilemma. Goodness is identical with, or grounded in, God's own character, so it does not stand above God. But God's commands are not arbitrary either, because a perfectly good being would never command cruelty. Obligation comes from God's commands, while goodness comes from God's nature. Defenders like that it keeps both God's authority and the reliability of morality. Critics ask whether the dilemma simply returns: is God's nature good because it is God's, or because it meets a standard?","example":"Could God command cruelty for its own sake? On this view, no: not because a rule outside God forbids it, but because commanding it would contradict God's loving nature, much as a perfectly honest person could not tell a casual lie.","history":"Robert Adams proposed it in “A Modified Divine Command Theory of Ethical Wrongness” (1973) and developed it in Finite and Infinite Goods (1999). William Alston's “Some Suggestions for Divine Command Theorists” (1990) defended a similar view. Aquinas's idea that God is goodness itself is a forerunner.","misread":"It does not say goodness exists independently of God; it identifies the standard with God.","variants":["Adams's view: the good is God; obligation is what God commands.","Divine nature theory: moral facts grounded wholly in God's character."],"reading":[{"title":"Divine Command Theory (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/divine-command-theory/"},{"title":"Theological Voluntarism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/voluntarism-theological/"}]},"e4t|yes":{"summary":"If God truly commanded the sacrifice, obeying would be right, because God's command outranks every human moral judgment.","idea":"This view holds that faith can require going beyond ordinary ethics. If God is the source of all goodness, then a real command from God settles what is right, even when it horrifies us. Our moral sense is limited and fallible; God's knowledge is complete. Defenders see Abraham's willingness as the model of trust. Some, like Aquinas, argue God as author of life may rightly take it, so obedience is not murder. The view does not say anyone should act on such commands today.","example":"The binding of Isaac (the Akedah): in Genesis 22, God tells Abraham to offer his son Isaac as a sacrifice on Mount Moriah. Abraham sets out, binds Isaac, and raises the knife before an angel stops him.","history":"Kierkegaard's Fear and Trembling (1843), written under the name Johannes de silentio, explored a “teleological suspension of the ethical”. Aquinas argued in the Summa Theologiae that God, as lord of life and death, may command a death without injustice. Ockham's voluntarism also supports the view.","misread":"Kierkegaard did not simply endorse obedience: his narrator says he cannot understand Abraham and treats the case as a terrifying paradox.","variants":["Kierkegaardian faith: the individual stands in a relation to God above the ethical.","Thomist view: God's authority over life makes the command just."],"reading":[{"title":"Søren Kierkegaard (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kierkegaard/"},{"title":"Theological Voluntarism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/voluntarism-theological/"}]},"e4t|no":{"summary":"A good God would never command cruelty like this, so any such command must be a mistake or a false voice.","idea":"This view uses moral knowledge to judge claims of revelation. We are more certain that killing an innocent child is wrong than we could ever be that a particular voice comes from God. So the right response to such a command is to doubt its source. Defenders say this protects believers from fanaticism and keeps faith tied to goodness. It denies that faith requires giving up moral judgment, and fits with views on which God's commands flow from God's nature.","example":"Kant's reply to Abraham: Kant suggested Abraham should have answered the voice that it was quite certain he should not kill his good son, but not certain that the voice really came from God.","history":"Kant made this argument in The Conflict of the Faculties (1798). Robert Adams, in Finite and Infinite Goods (1999), argued that a command to do something abhorrent should not be accepted as from God. Many Jewish and Christian thinkers read the story as rejecting child sacrifice.","misread":"It does not deny that God commands anything; it denies that a command to do evil could be authentic.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Religion and Morality in Western Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-morality/"},{"title":"Divine Command Theory (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/divine-command-theory/"}]},"e4t|test":{"summary":"The command was a test of Abraham's trust, never meant to be carried out, so it shows no real demand for sacrifice.","idea":"This view reads the story as being about faith rather than about killing. God knew the outcome and intended to stop the sacrifice all along; what was tested was whether Abraham trusted God completely. The story then teaches that God does not want human sacrifice, which was common in the ancient world. Defenders point to the text itself, which opens by saying God tested Abraham, and ends with a ram replacing Isaac. The view avoids saying God ever wanted an innocent death.","example":"The ram in the thicket: at the last moment an angel stops Abraham, and he sacrifices a ram caught in a bush instead. Many readers see the ending, not the command, as the point.","history":"Genesis 22:1 itself describes the episode as a test. Jewish tradition calls it the Akedah and often reads it as one of Abraham's ten trials. Maimonides discusses it as a trial in The Guide for the Perplexed (12th century), and many modern scholars read it as a rejection of child sacrifice.","misread":"It does not make the story easy: critics ask whether a good God would test someone in such a terrifying way.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Søren Kierkegaard (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kierkegaard/"},{"title":"Religion and Morality in Western Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-morality/"}]},"e4i|special":{"summary":"Close relationships like family and friendship are valuable in themselves and create duties to those people that we do not owe strangers.","idea":"This view says partiality is not a bias to be excused but part of morality. Being someone's parent, friend or spouse gives you special obligations: to care for them, stand by them, put them first in many choices. These duties come from the relationship itself, not from a calculation that favouring them makes the world better. Defenders like that it fits what loving people actually do, and say a morality that forbade special concern would make deep relationships impossible.","example":"Williams's “one thought too many”: a man can save his wife or a stranger from drowning. If he first checks that impartial morality permits saving his wife, he has had one thought too many. The motivating thought should simply be that it is his wife.","history":"Bernard Williams made the point in “Persons, Character and Morality” (1976). Samuel Scheffler developed accounts of special obligations in Boundaries and Allegiances (2001). The idea is ancient: Aristotle's Nicomachean Ethics gives friendship a central place in the good life. Confucian thought, which gives family duties a central place, offers a non-Western parallel.","misread":"It does not say strangers count for nothing; it says we owe more to those we are bound to.","variants":["Relationship-based view: the relationship itself grounds duties.","Projects view: commitments to particular people are part of a meaningful life."],"reading":[{"title":"Impartiality (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/impartiality/"}]},"e4i|graded":{"summary":"Love begins with family and extends outward in widening circles, so caring well for your own is the root of caring for all.","idea":"This Confucian view says love is naturally graded: strongest for parents and family, then neighbours, then the wider world. Rather than seeing this as a flaw, it treats family love as the training ground for all goodness. Someone who cannot love their own parents cannot really love strangers. Defenders like that it is realistic about human nature while still aiming at universal concern. It denies that we should care for everyone equally, as if a stranger's father were the same to us as our own.","example":"Mencius's debate with Yi Zhi: a Mohist said love should be impartial, with differences only in how it is applied. Mencius replied that no one truly loves a neighbour's child as much as his own brother's, and that love has one root, in family.","history":"The Analects of Confucius (5th century BCE) calls filial piety the root of humaneness (ren). Mencius (4th century BCE) defended graded love against the Mohists, who taught impartial care (jian ai). Neo-Confucians like Zhu Xi (12th century) continued the tradition.","misread":"Graded love is not indifference to strangers: the circles are meant to keep widening.","variants":["Mencian sprouts: compassion is natural and must be extended.","Neo-Confucian unity: all things form one body, cared for in graded ways."],"reading":[{"title":"Confucius (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confucius/"},{"title":"Mencius (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mencius/"},{"title":"Mohism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mohism/"}]},"e4i|care":{"summary":"Morality begins in concrete relationships of caring for particular people; abstract rules about everyone are secondary or misleading.","idea":"Care ethics says the basic moral experience is responding to the needs of someone in front of you, like a parent with a child. Morality is about attentiveness, responsiveness and maintaining relationships, not applying impartial principles. The view criticises traditional theories for picturing people as independent strangers. In reality we all begin dependent, and much of life is giving and receiving care. Defenders like that it takes seriously work and virtues long dismissed as private or as “women's work”.","example":"Heinz's dilemma: should Heinz steal a drug he cannot afford to save his dying wife? Gilligan noted that one girl, Amy, looked for ways to keep everyone in relationship, like talking to the druggist, instead of ranking rights.","history":"Carol Gilligan's In a Different Voice (1982) and Nel Noddings's Caring (1984) began the tradition. Joan Tronto's Moral Boundaries (1993) and Virginia Held's The Ethics of Care (2006) developed it further and applied it to politics. Some scholars see parallels with Confucian ethics.","misread":"Care ethics is not only for women: Gilligan described a different voice, not a female one, and later theorists apply it to everyone.","variants":["Noddings's relational care: caring as a relationship between carer and cared-for.","Political care ethics: Tronto and Held apply care to institutions."],"reading":[{"title":"Care Ethics (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/care-ethics/"},{"title":"Feminist Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/feminism-ethics/"}]},"e4w|little":{"summary":"Real care needs a relationship, or the chance of one, so our duties to faraway strangers are limited.","idea":"This view says caring is not a general feeling toward humanity but a relationship where the cared-for can be seen and can respond. Since we cannot be in such relationships with millions of distant strangers, our obligations to them are weak. Caring about distant suffering is good, but it is not caring for them. Defenders like that it protects real relationships from being swamped by impossible demands. Critics reply that it makes moral concern depend on the accident of geography.","example":"Noddings's starving children: Noddings argued that we are not obliged to care for the starving children of Africa in the same way we care for those we can meet, because there is no chance of completing the caring relation with them.","history":"Nel Noddings, Caring: A Feminine Approach to Ethics and Moral Education (1984), drew the line between “caring for” and “caring about”, and limited obligation by the possibility of relationship. Later care ethicists, including Noddings herself in Starting at Home (2002), revisited the question.","misread":"It does not say distant suffering is unimportant; it says it does not create the same obligations as near relationships.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Care Ethics (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/care-ethics/"}]},"e4w|institutions":{"summary":"We owe distant strangers care, but it is properly delivered through just institutions, laws and politics rather than personal bonds.","idea":"This view extends care ethics beyond private life. Caring values, like attentiveness and responsibility, should shape how societies and even global institutions work. Since no one can personally care for millions, the right channel is political: fair trade rules, aid systems, healthcare and migration policy. Defenders like that it combines care with justice instead of setting them against each other, and that it takes seriously how distant suffering is shaped by structures, not just bad luck.","example":"Global care chains: many nannies and nurses leave their own children behind in poor countries to care for children in rich ones. Care theorists argue that responding to this requires changing policies on migration, wages and childcare, not only private kindness.","history":"Joan Tronto's Moral Boundaries (1993) argued care is a political concept. Fiona Robinson's Globalizing Care (1999) applied care ethics to international relations. Virginia Held's The Ethics of Care (2006) argued that care and justice must be integrated. These writers argue that care and justice need each other, rather than being rival moral outlooks.","misread":"","variants":["Political care ethics: care as a value for public institutions.","Critical care ethics: attention to global structures of inequality."],"reading":[{"title":"Care Ethics (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/care-ethics/"},{"title":"Feminist Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/feminism-ethics/"}]},"e4w|direct":{"summary":"Caring about suffering anywhere is part of being a caring person, so we have direct obligations to strangers far away.","idea":"This view holds that a truly caring person cannot be indifferent to distant pain just because it is distant. Empathy may be weaker for strangers, but concern for humanity as a whole is part of a mature caring character. So care ethics does generate direct duties of aid, though perhaps weaker than duties to those near us. Defenders like that it keeps care's emotional core while blocking the objection that care ethics excuses ignoring distant suffering.","example":"The drowning child: Singer asked whether you should ruin your shoes to save a child drowning in a pond. If so, why not give the same amount to save a child far away? Care theorists who accept direct duties feel the force of this.","history":"Michael Slote, in Morals from Motives (2001) and The Ethics of Care and Empathy (2007), argued that balanced care includes humanitarian concern for distant people. Peter Singer's “Famine, Affluence, and Morality” (1972) supplies the classic challenge. Cosmopolitan traditions, from the Stoics on, share the idea.","misread":"It need not mean caring equally: most holders say near ties still weigh more, but distance never cancels concern.","variants":["Slote's balanced caring: intimate and humanitarian concern combined.","Cosmopolitan care: concern for all human beings as such."],"reading":[{"title":"Care Ethics (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/care-ethics/"},{"title":"Cosmopolitanism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/cosmopolitanism/"}]},"e4j|eudaim":{"summary":"A trait is a virtue because it helps its owner live a flourishing, fully good human life.","idea":"Eudaimonism ties virtue to eudaimonia, flourishing or living well. Human beings, like other living things, have a characteristic way of thriving, and the virtues are the traits that make that possible. Courage, honesty and justice are not just useful to others; they are part of what it is to live well as a human. Defenders like that this connects morality and happiness: being good is not a sacrifice but the core of a good life, rightly understood.","example":"Plants and people: Foot compared virtues to a plant's deep roots or a wolf's hunting with its pack. Just as we judge a tree defective if its roots are weak, we judge a person defective if they lack courage or honesty.","history":"Aristotle's Nicomachean Ethics (4th century BCE) is the classic source; Plato and the Stoics were also eudaimonists. The view revived after Elizabeth Anscombe's “Modern Moral Philosophy” (1958), with Philippa Foot's Natural Goodness (2001) and Rosalind Hursthouse's On Virtue Ethics (1999).","misread":"Flourishing is not pleasure or feeling happy; it means living a complete, excellent human life, which a contented scoundrel lacks.","variants":["Aristotelian eudaimonism: virtue as the core of flourishing.","Neo-Aristotelian naturalism: Foot and Hursthouse ground virtue in human nature."],"reading":[{"title":"Virtue Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-virtue/"},{"title":"Aristotle's Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-ethics/"}]},"e4j|sentiment":{"summary":"A trait is a virtue because it is the kind of character we naturally approve of and admire when we view it calmly.","idea":"Moral sentimentalism grounds virtue in human feelings. When we look at someone's character from a general point of view, not just our own interest, we feel approval for kindness, generosity and fairness, and disapproval for cruelty. Those feelings mark the virtues. The view denies that reason alone could show what is good; feelings like sympathy do the work. Defenders like that it explains why moral judgment moves us and why virtue is admired across cultures.","example":"Admiring an ancient hero: we feel admiration for a just ruler from centuries ago who can never benefit us. Hume used such cases to show that moral approval comes from sympathy with the people affected, not self-interest.","history":"Francis Hutcheson's An Inquiry into the Original of Our Ideas of Beauty and Virtue (1725) and David Hume's Treatise of Human Nature (1739 to 1740) and Enquiry concerning the Principles of Morals (1751) are the classics. Adam Smith's Theory of Moral Sentiments (1759) followed. Michael Slote has revived the view.","misread":"It does not mean whatever you happen to feel is right; approval must come from a calm, general point of view.","variants":["Humean sentimentalism: virtues are traits useful or agreeable to self or others.","Slote's agent-based sentimentalism: empathy as the basis of virtue."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Sentimentalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-sentimentalism/"},{"title":"Hume's Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume-moral/"}]},"e4j|conseq":{"summary":"A trait is a virtue because it reliably produces good results for others and the world.","idea":"Virtue consequentialism defines virtues by their effects. Kindness, honesty and courage are virtues because people who have them make lives go better. This lets the view explain why some traits count as virtues even when they involve no special wisdom, such as modesty that comes from not noticing your own talents. It denies that virtues must benefit their owner. Defenders like that it keeps the focus on character while explaining why character matters at all.","example":"Driver's modest person: Julia Driver noted that modesty seems to involve underestimating your own worth. That is a kind of ignorance, yet modesty is still a virtue, because modest people tend to make social life go better.","history":"Julia Driver's Uneasy Virtue (2001) is the leading defence. Hume's view that virtues are traits useful to others is a forerunner. Some consequentialists, including Henry Sidgwick in The Methods of Ethics (1874), discussed virtues in these terms. Thomas Hurka's Virtue, Vice, and Value (2001) offers a related account within a consequentialist framework.","misread":"It does not tell you to calculate outcomes before acting; it judges traits by their effects over time.","variants":["Driver's objective consequentialism: virtues produce good consequences.","Hurka's view: virtues are fitting attitudes toward goods and evils."],"reading":[{"title":"Virtue Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-virtue/"},{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"}]},"e4j|role":{"summary":"A trait is a virtue because it makes you good in your roles and relationships, as a child, parent, friend, teacher or ruler.","idea":"Confucian role ethics says there is no abstract self apart from our relationships. You are always someone's child, sibling, friend or student, and moral life consists in living these roles well. Virtues are excellences of relationship, like filial devotion, loyalty and humaneness. The view denies that morality starts from isolated individuals and universal rules. Defenders like that it fits how people actually experience moral life, through concrete ties and duties rather than abstract principles.","example":"Confucius on governing: asked about government, Confucius answered, roughly, “Let the ruler be a ruler, the minister a minister, the father a father, the son a son” (Analects 12.11). Order comes when each fulfils their role well.","history":"The Analects of Confucius (5th century BCE) and Mencius lay the foundation, with the five relationships later stressed in Confucian teaching. Roger Ames's Confucian Role Ethics (2011) and Henry Rosemont Jr. developed the modern label and argued it differs from Western virtue ethics.","misread":"Roles are not rigid job descriptions: Confucians stress judgment, sincerity and mutual care within them.","variants":["Ames and Rosemont role ethics: persons as constituted by relationships.","Confucian virtue ethics: reading Confucius as close to Aristotle."],"reading":[{"title":"Confucius (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confucius/"},{"title":"Chinese Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-chinese/"}]},"e4m|stoic":{"summary":"Virtue alone is enough for a good life; health, wealth and freedom are not truly good or bad in themselves.","idea":"Stoicism says the only true good is virtue, and the only true evil is vice. Everything else, health, money, reputation, even life, is “indifferent”: some things are preferable, but none can make your life good or bad. Since virtue is entirely up to you, no one can take your happiness away. Defenders find this a powerful source of freedom and calm, a way to stay steady in illness, loss or prison. It denies that luck can damage what truly matters.","example":"Epictetus's dichotomy of control: some things are up to us, our judgments and choices, and some are not, our bodies, property and reputation. Peace comes from caring only about what is up to us.","history":"Zeno of Citium founded Stoicism in Athens around 300 BCE. Later Romans include Seneca, Epictetus (whose Discourses and Handbook were recorded by his student Arrian) and the emperor Marcus Aurelius, author of the Meditations. Modern philosophers like Lawrence Becker have defended it.","misread":"Stoics do not say feel nothing: they aim to free themselves from destructive passions while keeping good feelings like joy.","variants":["Preferred indifferents: health and wealth are worth choosing but not good.","Modern Stoicism: practical techniques drawn from ancient texts."],"reading":[{"title":"Stoicism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/stoicism/"},{"title":"Epictetus (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epictetus/"}]},"e4m|aristotle":{"summary":"Virtue is the core of a good life, but flourishing also needs some external goods like health, friends and fortune.","idea":"The Aristotelian view says virtue is the main ingredient of living well, and a virtuous person will act well whatever happens. But a fully good life also requires some external goods: enough health and resources to act, friends, and freedom from crushing misfortune. A good person tortured or bereaved of everyone has not lost their goodness, but their life is not fully happy. Defenders find this more realistic than Stoicism, honouring both character and the vulnerability of human lives.","example":"Priam of Troy: Aristotle noted that King Priam lived well for years, then lost his sons and his city in old age. No one, he said, would call such a man happy at the end, though he did not become bad.","history":"Aristotle's Nicomachean Ethics, books I and X (4th century BCE), sets out the view, including the remark that no one calls a person broken on the wheel happy (book VII). Martha Nussbaum's The Fragility of Goodness (1986) is a modern exploration.","misread":"Aristotle does not say luck makes you good; it affects whether your life flourishes, not your character.","variants":["Aristotelian eudaimonism: virtue as dominant but not sufficient for happiness.","Moral luck debates: how much fortune affects a life's goodness."],"reading":[{"title":"Aristotle's Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-ethics/"},{"title":"Virtue Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-virtue/"}]},"e4n|unity":{"summary":"A person who truly has one virtue has them all, because each requires practical wisdom, and wisdom brings the rest.","idea":"The unity of virtue thesis says the virtues are connected. Real courage is not mere boldness; it is facing danger for the right reasons in the right way, which needs practical wisdom. But wisdom is the ability to see what matters in every situation, so it cannot exist alongside cruelty or injustice. A “brave” bully has a counterfeit virtue. Defenders like that this explains why we hesitate to call a gangster courageous, and why virtue seems to be a single excellence of character.","example":"Plato's Protagoras: Socrates argues against Protagoras that courage, justice, wisdom and the other virtues are not separate parts like the parts of a face, but somehow one, since each turns out to depend on knowledge.","history":"Plato's Protagoras (4th century BCE) discusses the thesis. Aristotle, in Nicomachean Ethics book VI, argued that full virtue requires practical wisdom and so the virtues come together. Aquinas defended the connection of the virtues, and John McDowell's “Virtue and Reason” (1979) is a modern statement.","misread":"It does not deny that people have partial good traits; it says these are natural virtues, not full virtues.","variants":["Strong unity: all virtues are one thing, knowledge of good.","Reciprocity: the virtues are distinct but require each other."],"reading":[{"title":"Plato's Ethics: An Overview (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/plato-ethics/"},{"title":"Aristotle's Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-ethics/"}]},"e4n|disunity":{"summary":"The virtues are separate traits, so a person can genuinely have some, like courage, while lacking others, like kindness.","idea":"This view takes ordinary observation at face value. We all know people who are brave but unkind, or honest but timid. On this view, those are real virtues, just incomplete sets. Each virtue concerns a different area of life, fear, money, anger, truth, and skill in one area need not bring skill in others. Defenders like that it fits psychology and avoids saying almost no one has any virtue. Critics answer that without wisdom, these traits can be misused.","example":"The brave Nazi soldier: a soldier risks his life repeatedly for comrades while serving an evil cause. Disunity theorists say he can have genuine courage despite serious vices; unity theorists say his courage is defective.","history":"Neera Badhwar's “The Limited Unity of Virtue” (1996) argued against full unity. Psychological studies of character, discussed by John Doris in Lack of Character (2002), have also put pressure on strong unity. Defenders of unity reply that such traits are only natural virtues, not full virtues, an answer that goes back to Aristotle.","misread":"It does not say virtues never support each other; it denies that having one requires having all.","variants":["Limited unity: virtues are unified within areas of life, not across all of them.","Situationist challenge: traits may be narrower than thought."],"reading":[{"title":"Virtue Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-virtue/"}]},"e4o|agent":{"summary":"In a hard case, the right act is whatever a fully virtuous person would characteristically do in your circumstances.","idea":"The qualified-agent view gives virtue ethics a test for right action. Instead of a rule list, you ask: what would an honest, kind, just and wise person do here? Defenders say the virtue terms themselves give plenty of guidance: “do what is honest, do not do what is cruel”. This answers the complaint that virtue ethics cannot guide action. It also lets virtue ethics compare directly with consequentialism and deontology, which each have their own definition of right action.","example":"The exam cheat: you could pass an important exam by copying from a friend. Ask what a virtuous person would do: she would not cheat, since cheating is dishonest and unfair. The answer is clear without consulting a principle.","history":"Rosalind Hursthouse stated the view in “Virtue Theory and Abortion” (1991) and On Virtue Ethics (1999), defining right action as what a virtuous agent would characteristically do in the circumstances. Aristotle's remark that the good person is the standard is a forerunner.","misread":"It does not mean imitating a famous hero; it means asking what the virtues require in your actual situation.","variants":["V-rules: rules drawn from virtue and vice terms, like “do what is honest”.","Hursthouse's account of right action."],"reading":[{"title":"Virtue Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-virtue/"}]},"e4o|wisdom":{"summary":"No formula decides hard cases; a wise person perceives what matters in the particular situation, and that perception cannot be reduced to rules.","idea":"This view says the core of virtue is practical wisdom, the ability to see a situation rightly. Hard cases are hard because they have many relevant features, and which ones matter depends on the details. No rule could anticipate them all. A wise person sees the situation clearly and responds well, as an expert doctor diagnoses without a checklist. Defenders think this is honest about moral life, and that demanding a formula misunderstands ethics.","example":"The expert doctor: an experienced doctor sees that a patient is seriously ill before any test confirms it, from small cues she cannot fully list. Practical wisdom works similarly, noticing what matters in a situation.","history":"Aristotle described phronesis (practical wisdom) in Nicomachean Ethics book VI, saying ethics admits only as much precision as its subject allows. John McDowell's “Virtue and Reason” (1979) and Iris Murdoch's The Sovereignty of Good (1970) developed the perceptual model. Hans-Georg Gadamer also drew on phronesis in his account of interpretation.","misread":"It is not mysticism: wisdom is learned by experience and its judgments can be explained afterwards, even if not by a rule.","variants":["McDowell's perceptual model: virtue as sensitivity to reasons.","Particularism: no general rules capture what matters."],"reading":[{"title":"Aristotle's Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-ethics/"},{"title":"Moral Particularism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-particularism/"}]},"e4o|ritual":{"summary":"In a hard case, rely on the roles, rituals and admired examples that have trained your judgment over time.","idea":"This view says judgment does not come from nowhere: it is shaped by practices. Rituals, manners and stories of admirable people teach us how to feel and act, until right responses become natural. In a hard case, you draw on these forms and examples instead of inventing a solution from first principles. Defenders like that it explains how people actually learn ethics, from parents, teachers and traditions, and that it values inherited wisdom.","example":"Learning grief: Confucian funeral rites prescribe mourning periods and gestures. Xunzi argued that such rituals give shape to raw emotion, teaching people to grieve deeply but not destructively, and refining character over time.","history":"The Analects of Confucius and Xunzi (3rd century BCE) stress li, ritual propriety, as central to moral training. Linda Zagzebski's Exemplarist Moral Theory (2017) builds ethics on admiring exemplars. Contemporary scholars of Chinese philosophy have revived the topic. Xunzi held that human nature needs shaping, and that ritual is the main tool.","misread":"Ritual here is not empty ceremony: it is meant to shape sincere feeling and good judgment.","variants":["Confucian li: ritual propriety training character.","Exemplarism: moral concepts anchored by admirable people."],"reading":[{"title":"Confucius (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confucius/"},{"title":"Xunzi (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/xunzi/"}]},"e4p|ross":{"summary":"We have several basic duties, and when they conflict we must weigh them case by case, with no fixed ranking.","idea":"Rossian pluralism says there are several genuine duties: keeping promises, not harming, helping, gratitude, justice and others. Each is a prima facie duty, a real reason that holds unless outweighed. When duties conflict, you use judgment to see which is weightier in this case. There is no master principle and no permanent ranking. Defenders like that it matches ordinary moral thinking: we really do recognise many moral reasons, and we really do weigh them.","example":"Promise versus accident: you promised to meet a friend, but pass a car crash where you could help. The duty to keep promises and the duty to help both apply, and here helping clearly outweighs, though in another case it might not.","history":"W. D. Ross set out the view in The Right and the Good (1930), listing duties of fidelity, reparation, gratitude, justice, beneficence, self-improvement and non-maleficence. Robert Audi's The Good in the Right (2004) is a leading modern version. Ross held that we know these duties by intuition, as we know mathematical axioms.","misread":"Prima facie does not mean only apparently; a prima facie duty is a real reason that can be outweighed.","variants":["Ross's prima facie duties.","Audi's Kantian intuitionism: Ross's duties systematised by Kant's humanity formula."],"reading":[{"title":"William David Ross (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/william-david-ross/"},{"title":"Intuitionism in Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/intuitionism-ethics/"}]},"e4p|ranked":{"summary":"Some values always outrank others, so conflicts are settled by a fixed order, such as justice before welfare.","idea":"Ranked pluralism accepts several basic values but orders them. A higher value must be fully satisfied before a lower one counts, or at least always wins in a direct clash. For instance, rights might always beat welfare, or not harming always beat helping. Defenders like that it gives clear guidance and stops important values from being traded for small gains in others. Critics say real cases are too varied for any fixed order to work everywhere.","example":"Rawls's priority of liberty: Rawls held that basic liberties, like freedom of speech, may not be sacrificed for greater economic gains. Liberty has lexical priority, coming first like A before B in a dictionary.","history":"John Rawls's A Theory of Justice (1971) gave his principles a lexical ordering. Many deontologists rank negative duties, not harming, above positive ones, helping. Ross himself thought non-maleficence usually more stringent than beneficence, though he did not fix a strict ranking.","misread":"It does not deny that lower values matter; they count fully when higher ones are not at stake.","variants":["Lexical priority: higher values come strictly first.","Weighted priority: higher values count more but can be outweighed in extremes."],"reading":[{"title":"Value Pluralism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/value-pluralism/"},{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"}]},"e4p|particular":{"summary":"No general moral principles hold; whether a feature counts for or against an act depends entirely on the situation.","idea":"Particularism says moral reasons are context-dependent. That an act gives pleasure usually counts in its favour, but not if the pleasure is a sadist's. That you promised usually counts in favour, but not if the promise was forced. So no principle, not even “pleasure is good”, holds in all cases. Moral wisdom is skill in seeing what matters here. Defenders like that it explains why every proposed rule seems to have exceptions, and why moral experts reason case by case.","example":"The joke at the funeral: that a joke is funny usually makes telling it better. At a funeral, its being funny might make telling it worse. The same feature can switch from a reason for to a reason against.","history":"Jonathan Dancy's Moral Reasons (1993) and Ethics Without Principles (2004) are the main defences. John McDowell and Margaret Little have supported related views. Aristotle's emphasis on practical wisdom over rules is often cited as a forerunner. The debate between particularists and generalists has become a major topic in contemporary moral theory.","misread":"Particularism does not say anything goes; it says what is right depends on all the features of the case.","variants":["Holism about reasons: reasons can change polarity across contexts.","Moderate particularism: principles may exist but are not needed for moral thought."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Particularism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-particularism/"}]},"e4u|intuition":{"summary":"Basic moral truths, like that promises matter, are self-evident to a careful, mature mind on reflection.","idea":"Ethical intuitionism says we can know some moral truths directly, not by deriving them from something else. When you consider carefully whether gratuitous cruelty is wrong, you can just see that it is, as you see that two plus two is four. This does not mean first impressions are always right: intuitions must be considered and mature. Defenders like that it explains how moral knowledge is possible without making it depend on science or feeling, and say all reasoning has to start somewhere.","example":"Gratuitous torture: consider whether it would be wrong to set a cat on fire just for fun. You do not need an argument; reflection alone shows it is wrong, and that knowledge is more certain than any premise you could offer.","history":"Henry Sidgwick's The Methods of Ethics (1874), G. E. Moore's Principia Ethica (1903), H. A. Prichard and W. D. Ross's The Right and the Good (1930) are classic intuitionist works. Robert Audi and Michael Huemer, in Ethical Intuitionism (2005), revived it.","misread":"Intuition here is not a gut feeling; it is a considered judgment after careful reflection.","variants":["Rossian intuitionism: self-evident prima facie duties.","Phenomenal conservatism: Huemer's view that seemings justify belief."],"reading":[{"title":"Intuitionism in Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/intuitionism-ethics/"}]},"e4u|equilibrium":{"summary":"We justify moral beliefs by testing principles against judgments about cases and revising each until they cohere.","idea":"Reflective equilibrium is a method, not a list of values. You start with your considered judgments about particular cases and your candidate principles. Where a principle gives a monstrous verdict, you revise the principle; where a strong principle shows a judgment to be prejudice, you revise the judgment. You go back and forth until they fit together. Defenders like that it uses all the evidence we have and treats no belief as untouchable. It is the most widely used method in moral philosophy.","example":"Slavery and principles: a society might begin with the judgment that slavery is acceptable. When it adopts principles of equal human dignity that explain many other judgments, the old judgment no longer fits and has to be revised.","history":"Nelson Goodman described the method for logic in Fact, Fiction, and Forecast (1955). John Rawls named and used it in A Theory of Justice (1971). Norman Daniels's “Wide Reflective Equilibrium” (1979) extended it to include background theories. Today it is probably the most widely used method in normative ethics.","misread":"It is not conservative by nature: strong principles can overturn even deeply held judgments.","variants":["Narrow equilibrium: principles and case judgments only.","Wide equilibrium: adds background theories of human nature and society."],"reading":[{"title":"Reflective Equilibrium (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reflective-equilibrium/"}]},"e4u|experience":{"summary":"We learn what counts morally through experience and upbringing, which train us to perceive situations correctly.","idea":"This view treats moral knowledge like expertise. A good upbringing teaches you to notice cruelty, unfairness and need, and to feel the right way about them. Over time, you see what matters in a situation, the way a trained musician hears what is wrong with a chord. It denies that moral knowledge comes mainly from pure reason or abstract theory. Defenders like that it explains why moral sensitivity varies with experience, and why people learn ethics best through practice.","example":"Murdoch's mother-in-law: a woman thinks her son's wife is vulgar. Without saying anything, she works at looking again, justly and lovingly, and comes to see her as refreshingly simple. Her moral vision has changed through attention.","history":"Aristotle, Nicomachean Ethics book II, said we become just by doing just acts, through habituation from youth. Mencius spoke of moral sprouts that must be cultivated. Iris Murdoch's The Sovereignty of Good (1970) and John McDowell's work defended moral perception.","misread":"Upbringing is not the final word: trained perception can be refined, and bad training corrected, through further experience.","variants":["Moral perception: we can perceive moral properties.","Habituation: Aristotle's view that virtue comes by practice."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Epistemology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-epistemology/"},{"title":"Aristotle's Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-ethics/"}]},"e4q|authentic":{"summary":"With no values handed to us, what matters is choosing freely, owning the choice, and not hiding behind excuses.","idea":"Existentialist authenticity says human beings have no fixed essence or built-in purpose; we make ourselves through our choices. Since no God or human nature dictates values, the measure of a choice is how it is made: lucidly, freely, and taking full responsibility. Sartre added that in choosing for yourself you implicitly propose an image of how anyone should live. Defenders find this liberating: it puts each person in charge of their life and denies them comforting excuses.","example":"Sartre's student: during the war, a young man must choose between joining the Free French to fight and staying with his lonely mother. No theory decides for him. Sartre told him: you are free, choose, that is, invent.","history":"Jean-Paul Sartre's lecture “Existentialism Is a Humanism” (1945, published 1946) and Being and Nothingness (1943) are the main sources. Kierkegaard and Heidegger, in Being and Time (1927), developed related ideas of authentic selfhood. Simone de Beauvoir, Albert Camus and others developed existentialist ethics in the same postwar period in France.","misread":"Authenticity is not doing whatever you like; it means facing your freedom and responsibility honestly.","variants":["Sartrean authenticity: radical freedom and responsibility.","Heideggerian authenticity: owning one's existence in the face of death."],"reading":[{"title":"Authenticity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/authenticity/"},{"title":"Jean-Paul Sartre (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sartre/"},{"title":"Existentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/existentialism/"}]},"e4q|beauvoir":{"summary":"A choice is better when it respects and promotes the freedom of others too, because willing your own freedom means willing theirs.","idea":"Beauvoir's ethics of ambiguity says human freedom is always shared. We depend on others to give our projects meaning, so to truly want my freedom I must also want a world where others are free. This gives existentialism a real standard: oppressing others contradicts the freedom you claim for yourself. Defenders like that it keeps existentialist freedom without collapsing into “anything goes”, and grounds an ethics of solidarity and resistance to oppression.","example":"The oppressor's contradiction: a slave owner claims freedom for himself while denying it to others. Beauvoir argued his freedom is inauthentic, since it rests on treating free beings as things.","history":"Simone de Beauvoir's The Ethics of Ambiguity (1947) set out the view, and The Second Sex (1949) applied it to the oppression of women. Her ideas influenced later existentialist and feminist ethics, and Sartre's later work moved closer to them.","misread":"Beauvoir does not reject existentialism: she builds an objective standard from within it.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Simone de Beauvoir (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauvoir/"},{"title":"Existentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/existentialism/"}]},"e4q|nietzsche":{"summary":"Some created values are higher than others: those that are braver, more creative and more life-affirming.","idea":"Nietzsche thought traditional morality had lost its foundation, but he did not conclude that all choices are equal. Some ways of valuing express strength, creativity and love of life; others express weakness, resentment and fear of life. The task is to create values that affirm life, even its suffering. Defenders find this a demanding standard of excellence that does not depend on God or social approval. It denies that equality of choice follows from the absence of given values.","example":"The eternal recurrence: imagine a demon tells you that you must live your life again, every moment, forever. Would you curse him or welcome the news? Nietzsche used this test to ask whether you truly affirm your life.","history":"Friedrich Nietzsche's The Gay Science (1882), Thus Spoke Zarathustra (1883 to 1885), Beyond Good and Evil (1886) and On the Genealogy of Morality (1887) develop these ideas. He influenced later existentialists, though he wrote before the movement had a name.","misread":"Nietzsche's higher values are not about dominating others physically; he valued self-overcoming and creativity.","variants":["Self-overcoming: becoming more than one has been.","Master and slave morality: Nietzsche's genealogy of values."],"reading":[{"title":"Nietzsche's Moral and Political Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nietzsche-moral-political/"},{"title":"Existentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/existentialism/"}]},"e4v|badfaith":{"summary":"Pretending you are not free, as if circumstances or roles forced your choices, is bad faith, the central moral failure.","idea":"For Sartre, we are always free to choose, even in hard situations. Bad faith is self-deception about this freedom: telling yourself “I had no choice” when you did. It is comforting, because it shifts responsibility onto your boss, your nature or society. But it is a lie to yourself. Defenders see the concept as a sharp tool for honesty: it challenges people who hide behind orders, roles or excuses.","example":"Sartre's waiter: a café waiter moves with exaggerated precision, playing at being a waiter as if that were all he is. Sartre saw this as bad faith, treating himself as a thing with a fixed role rather than a free person.","history":"Jean-Paul Sartre developed the idea in Being and Nothingness (1943), which includes the waiter and other examples. It shaped later discussions of responsibility, including debates about people who claim they were only following orders. Sartre also discussed a woman on a date who leaves her hand in a man's hand while pretending not to notice, another case of bad faith.","misread":"Bad faith is not ordinary lying; it is lying to yourself about your own freedom.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Jean-Paul Sartre (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sartre/"},{"title":"Existentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/existentialism/"}]},"e4v|situated":{"summary":"Real pressures can limit freedom, especially for the oppressed, so not every claim of having no choice is bad faith.","idea":"This view keeps the idea that we are free but adds that freedom is always situated: shaped by our bodies, history, society and power. A worker who may lose her job, or a woman in a society with few options, faces real limits. Blaming the oppressed for bad faith can add insult to injury. Defenders like that it keeps responsibility while being realistic about circumstances, and directs criticism at the structures that limit people.","example":"Beauvoir's slave and child: Beauvoir compared people raised in oppression with no knowledge of other ways of life, such as some slaves and women in the past, to children. Their acceptance of their position is not bad faith in the same way as a powerful person's excuses.","history":"Simone de Beauvoir's The Ethics of Ambiguity (1947) and The Second Sex (1949) stress situated freedom. Maurice Merleau-Ponty's Phenomenology of Perception (1945) also emphasised the embodied, situated self. Frantz Fanon's Black Skin, White Masks (1952) applied similar ideas to colonial racism.","misread":"It does not deny freedom; it says freedom comes in degrees depending on one's situation.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Simone de Beauvoir (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauvoir/"},{"title":"Existentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/existentialism/"}]},"e4v|nofail":{"summary":"There is no objective standard for calling a choice a failure; judging it so is itself just another chosen value.","idea":"This view takes the absence of given values all the way. If no values are built into the world, then even “authenticity” or “honesty with oneself” is just one value among others, chosen rather than discovered. So calling someone's choice a failure only expresses the critic's own values. Defenders think this is the consistent conclusion from existentialist starting points, and that critics of bad faith quietly smuggle in an objective standard they officially deny.","example":"Stirner's spooks: Max Stirner argued that ideals like humanity, morality and even freedom are “spooks”, abstractions people let rule them. The egoist owns his choices and recognises no higher standard that could judge them.","history":"Max Stirner's The Ego and Its Own (1844) rejected all fixed ideals. Critics of Sartre, from the 1940s on, argued that his authenticity was an unjustified value. Later debates on moral anti-realism discuss similar points about whether any standard is non-arbitrary.","misread":"It does not mean nothing matters to the person; it means their values cannot be shown to be objectively better.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Existentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/existentialism/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}]},"e5|yes":{"summary":"Moral claims state objective facts: some things really are right or wrong regardless of anyone's beliefs or feelings.","idea":"Moral realism says “torturing a child for fun is wrong” is true in the same basic way as “water boils at 100°C”: it describes how things are, and would be true even if everyone disagreed. Moral disagreement is then like scientific disagreement: some people are mistaken. Realists like that this fits how we talk and argue about morality, and that it lets us say some societies made real moral progress, as when they abolished slavery.","example":"Moral progress: most people think abolishing slavery was a real improvement, not just a change of fashion. Realists argue this only makes sense if slavery was really wrong, even when most people approved.","history":"Plato's theory of the Form of the Good is an ancient form of realism. G. E. Moore's Principia Ethica (1903) defended it, and modern realists include Richard Boyd, David Brink, Russ Shafer-Landau, Derek Parfit and David Enoch. In surveys of professional philosophers, such as the PhilPapers survey of 2009, moral realism was the most popular answer.","misread":"Realism does not mean moral truths are easy to know or that the realist is always right; disagreement is expected.","variants":["Naturalist realism: moral facts are natural facts.","Non-naturalist realism: moral facts are a distinct kind.","Theistic realism: moral facts grounded in God."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-realism/"},{"title":"Metaethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaethics/"}]},"e5|construct":{"summary":"Moral truths are not facts out in the world; they are what correct practical reasoning would produce or endorse.","idea":"Constructivism takes a middle path. It agrees with realists that moral claims can be true or false, and that some views are mistaken. But it denies that their truth comes from a separate moral reality. Instead, moral truths are constructed by an idealised procedure of reasoning, like choosing rules from a fair position. Defenders like that this explains both the objectivity and the practical, action-guiding nature of morality without strange metaphysics.","example":"Rawls's original position: imagine rational people choosing principles behind a veil of ignorance. Whatever principles they would choose count as just. Justice is not discovered in nature; it is the output of a fair procedure.","history":"Kant's moral philosophy is the main inspiration. John Rawls's “Kantian Constructivism in Moral Theory” (1980) named the view. Christine Korsgaard's The Sources of Normativity (1996) and Sharon Street's work developed Kantian and Humean versions. Thomas Scanlon's contractualism is sometimes classed as a form of constructivism about wrongness.","misread":"Constructivism is not relativism: most versions say the procedure gives the same answer for everyone.","variants":["Kantian constructivism: binding on all rational agents.","Humean constructivism: depends on agents' starting values."],"reading":[{"title":"Constructivism in Metaethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/constructivism-metaethics/"}]},"e5|no":{"summary":"Moral claims do not report objective facts; something else, like feeling, error or cultural agreement, explains what they are.","idea":"Moral anti-realism denies that there are objective moral facts. Anti-realists differ on what moral talk is: some say it expresses feelings, some that it describes cultural standards, some that it is systematically false. What they share is the view that morality is not part of the world's furniture independent of minds. Defenders like that it fits a scientific picture of the world and explains why moral disagreement persists. Many anti-realists remain deeply committed to their values.","example":"Mackie's argument from relativity: moral codes vary widely across societies and times. Mackie thought the best explanation is that people's morals reflect their ways of life, not that they perceive objective moral facts with different accuracy.","history":"Ancient Greek sophists like Protagoras held relativist views. David Hume's Treatise (1739 to 1740) inspired later forms. Twentieth century versions include A. J. Ayer's emotivism (1936), J. L. Mackie's error theory (1977) and Simon Blackburn's quasi-realism. Friedrich Nietzsche's critiques of morality are often read as anti-realist too.","misread":"Anti-realists need not be nihilists or uncaring; most remain strongly committed to their moral views.","variants":["Error theory: moral claims are all false.","Non-cognitivism: moral claims express attitudes.","Relativism: moral claims are true relative to standards."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"},{"title":"Metaethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaethics/"}]},"e5a|relativism":{"summary":"Moral claims are true or false only relative to a culture's or person's standards, not absolutely.","idea":"Moral relativism says there is no single moral truth for everyone. Instead, “slavery is wrong” is true relative to some moral frameworks and false relative to others. The view is often motivated by deep and lasting disagreement between cultures, and by a wish to avoid arrogance about one's own values. Defenders say it explains moral diversity without assuming that everyone except us is mistaken. Critics ask whether it can make sense of moral reformers.","example":"Herodotus's funeral customs: the Greek historian told how King Darius asked Greeks if they would eat their dead fathers, and Callatians if they would burn theirs. Each was horrified. Herodotus concluded that custom is king.","history":"Herodotus's Histories (5th century BCE) gives the classic illustration. Anthropologists like Ruth Benedict, in Patterns of Culture (1934), promoted cultural relativism. Philosophers Gilbert Harman, in “Moral Relativism Defended” (1975), and David Wong, in Moral Relativity (1984), defended careful versions. Debates continue over whether relativism can make sense of moral disagreement.","misread":"Relativism does not follow automatically from disagreement, and it does not by itself require tolerance.","variants":["Agent relativism: rightness depends on the agent's culture.","Appraiser relativism: depends on the judge's standards."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Relativism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-relativism/"},{"title":"Moral Relativism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/moral-re/"}]},"e5a|error":{"summary":"Moral claims try to state objective facts, but there are no such facts, so all positive moral claims are false.","idea":"Error theory agrees with realists about what moral claims mean: they claim objective, binding truths. But it says there are no such truths. So moral claims are systematically mistaken, like claims about witches. The main argument is that moral facts would have to be metaphysically strange: facts that give reasons to everyone regardless of their desires. Defenders like that this takes moral language seriously while keeping a scientific view of the world.","example":"Mackie's queerness argument: an objective moral fact would have to be something that, once you knew it, gave you reason to act whatever you wanted. Mackie argued nothing in the world could be like that.","history":"J. L. Mackie's Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977) is the classic statement. Richard Joyce's The Myth of Morality (2001) and Jonas Olson's Moral Error Theory (2014) developed it. Earlier, Axel Hägerström in Sweden held related views. Mackie also argued from the wide variation of moral codes across cultures.","misread":"Error theory does not say people should behave badly; it is a view about the status of moral claims.","variants":["Fictionalism: keep moral talk as useful pretence.","Abolitionism: give up moral talk.","Conservationism: keep believing moral claims."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}]},"e5a|noncog":{"summary":"Moral claims are not statements of fact at all; they express attitudes like approval or disapproval, so are neither true nor false.","idea":"Non-cognitivism says moral sentences have a different job from factual ones. Saying “torture is wrong” does not describe the world; it expresses disapproval, or recommends against torture. This explains why moral judgments motivate us, since they are attitudes, and why no scientific instrument could detect wrongness. Defenders like that it fits a naturalistic picture without saying all morality is mistaken. Critics raise the Frege-Geach problem about how moral words work inside arguments.","example":"The Frege-Geach problem: “If lying is wrong, getting your brother to lie is wrong” uses “lying is wrong” without expressing disapproval. Critics ask how non-cognitivists can explain the logic of such sentences.","history":"A. J. Ayer's Language, Truth and Logic (1936) introduced emotivism, and Charles Stevenson's Ethics and Language (1944) developed it. R. M. Hare's prescriptivism, Simon Blackburn's quasi-realism and Allan Gibbard's norm-expressivism followed. Hume's sentimentalism is a forerunner. Its rivals call these views cognitivist, since they treat moral judgments as beliefs.","misread":"Non-cognitivists do not say morality is unimportant or arbitrary; they explain what moral judgments are.","variants":["Emotivism: moral claims express emotions.","Prescriptivism: moral claims are universal commands.","Quasi-realism: earns the right to talk of moral truth."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Cognitivism vs. Non-Cognitivism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-cognitivism/"},{"title":"Non-Cognitivism in Ethics (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/non-cogn/"}]},"e5b|natural":{"summary":"Moral facts are natural facts, like facts about health or wellbeing, that science could in principle investigate.","idea":"Naturalist realism holds that there are objective moral facts and that they are part of the natural world. Goodness might be identical with what promotes human flourishing, just as health is a natural property of bodies. We learn moral facts as we learn other facts, through observation and theory. Defenders like that this makes moral knowledge unmysterious and fits morality into a scientific worldview. Critics press Moore's open question argument.","example":"Water and H2O: we discovered that water is H2O through science, not by analysing the word “water”. Naturalists argue that goodness might likewise turn out to be some natural property, even if the word does not mean that.","history":"Aristotle's ethics grounded virtue in human nature. Modern naturalist realists include the Cornell realists Richard Boyd, Nicholas Sturgeon and David Brink (Moral Realism and the Foundations of Ethics, 1989), Peter Railton (“Moral Realism”, 1986) and Philippa Foot. Sam Harris's The Moral Landscape (2010) gave a popular version.","misread":"Naturalism does not mean whatever is natural is good; it means moral properties are part of the natural world.","variants":["Cornell realism: moral properties as natural kinds.","Analytic naturalism: moral terms defined by natural terms."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Naturalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/naturalism-moral/"},{"title":"Moral Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-realism/"}]},"e5b|nonnatural":{"summary":"Moral facts are real and objective but not physical or natural; they are a distinct kind known by reason, like truths of logic.","idea":"Non-naturalism says moral facts exist independently of us, but they are not reducible to physical facts. Just as mathematical truths are not made of atoms but are still objective, moral truths stand on their own. We know them through reason and intuition. Defenders like that this explains why moral facts seem so different from scientific facts, and avoids the problems they see in reducing goodness to anything natural.","example":"Moore's open question: for any natural property, like “what maximises pleasure”, we can sensibly ask, “but is it good?” Moore thought this showed goodness could not simply be that natural property.","history":"G. E. Moore's Principia Ethica (1903) is the founding work. W. D. Ross and other intuitionists held related views. Modern defenders include Russ Shafer-Landau (Moral Realism: A Defence, 2003), Derek Parfit (On What Matters, 2011) and David Enoch (Taking Morality Seriously, 2011).","misread":"Non-natural does not mean supernatural; these facts are abstract, like those of logic or mathematics.","variants":["Robust non-naturalism: moral facts are metaphysically weighty.","Quietist non-naturalism: moral truths need no metaphysical backing."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Non-Naturalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-non-naturalism/"},{"title":"Moral Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-realism/"}]},"e5b|theistic":{"summary":"Moral facts are objective because they are grounded in God's nature or will.","idea":"Theistic realism says morality is objective because it rests on God, a perfect and necessary being. Goodness may be identical with God's nature, or duties may come from God's commands. Defenders like that this explains why moral facts have authority over everyone, and why goodness and reality are connected. Many theists argue that without God objective morality would be hard to explain, which becomes the moral argument for God's existence.","example":"The moral argument: if objective moral values exist, they need a foundation. Theistic realists argue that a perfectly good God is the best explanation of why some things are really right or wrong.","history":"Aquinas's Summa Theologiae grounded morality in God's eternal law. Robert Adams's Finite and Infinite Goods (1999) identified goodness with God. William Lane Craig and others have defended the moral argument for God's existence. Natural law theorists, from Aquinas to modern writers, also see moral order as part of God's creation.","misread":"It does not say only believers can know right from wrong; it is about what grounds morality, not who knows it.","variants":["Divine nature theory: goodness grounded in God's character.","Divine command theory: duties grounded in God's commands."],"reading":[{"title":"Religion and Morality in Western Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-morality/"},{"title":"Divine Command Theory (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/divine-command-theory/"}]},"e5f|kantian":{"summary":"Every rational agent, just by reasoning about what to do, is committed to the same basic moral principles.","idea":"Kantian constructivism says morality comes from the structure of rational agency itself. Anyone who acts for reasons must value their own humanity as a source of reasons, and consistency then requires valuing everyone's humanity. So moral principles bind all rational beings, whatever their desires. Defenders like that this gives morality universal authority without needing objective moral facts in the world.","example":"Korsgaard's argument from reasons: whenever you act for a reason, you treat your own choices as mattering. If you think about why they matter, you must see that the same goes for every other person's choices.","history":"Kant's Groundwork (1785) and Critique of Practical Reason (1788) are the inspiration. John Rawls's “Kantian Constructivism in Moral Theory” (1980) and Christine Korsgaard's The Sources of Normativity (1996) and Self-Constitution (2009) are leading statements. Onora O'Neill developed another version. Rawls's later work applied constructivism to political justice.","misread":"It does not say everyone already agrees; it says everyone who reasons correctly is committed to the same principles.","variants":["Korsgaard's constitutivism: morality follows from what agency is.","Rawlsian constructivism: principles chosen under fair conditions."],"reading":[{"title":"Constructivism in Metaethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/constructivism-metaethics/"},{"title":"Kant's Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-moral/"}]},"e5f|humean":{"summary":"Moral truths are constructed from the values an agent already holds, so a consistent monster may have no reason to be kind.","idea":"Humean constructivism agrees that moral truths come from reasoning, not from facts in the world. But it denies that reason alone fixes them. What you have reason to do depends on your starting values and desires, corrected for consistency and information. Most human beings share enough values that morality is largely common, but in principle a perfectly coherent person with strange values could have different reasons. Defenders like that it is honest about where moral force comes from.","example":"Gibbard's ideally coherent Caligula: imagine a Roman emperor whose cruel values fit together perfectly, with no inconsistency or ignorance. Humean constructivists accept that reasoning alone may not show he has reason to stop.","history":"David Hume's Treatise (1739 to 1740) argued that reason is the slave of the passions. Bernard Williams's “Internal and External Reasons” and Sharon Street's papers, including “Constructivism about Reasons” (2008), are key modern works. Street argues that the Humean view best fits an evolutionary account of how we came to have values.","misread":"It does not say morality is just personal preference; reasons are corrected by consistency and full information.","variants":["Internalism about reasons: reasons depend on an agent's motivations.","Street's Humean constructivism."],"reading":[{"title":"Constructivism in Metaethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/constructivism-metaethics/"},{"title":"Reasons for Action: Internal vs. External (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reasons-internal-external/"}]},"e5c|universal":{"summary":"Slavery was wrong even in a society that approved of it, because moral truths do not depend on cultural approval.","idea":"This answer rejects relativism at the decisive test. If slavery was wrong only for us, then abolitionists in slaveholding societies were mistaken by their own society's standards. Most people think they were right. Universalists hold that some moral standards apply to every society, regardless of what its members believe. This can be held by realists, constructivists or even universalist relativists who think some shared human standards exist.","example":"The reformer's dilemma: if right means what my culture approves, then reformers like abolitionists were by definition wrong when they challenged their society. Universalists take this as a reason to reject cultural relativism.","history":"Critics of relativism, such as James Rachels in The Elements of Moral Philosophy (1986), press the reformer objection. Human rights traditions, from the Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948), assume universal standards. Many classical thinkers, including the Stoics, held that moral law applies to all people.","misread":"Universalism does not mean every custom is open to judgment; many differences are not moral at all.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Relativism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-relativism/"},{"title":"Moral Relativism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/moral-re/"}]},"e5c|cultural":{"summary":"What is right depends on the culture of the person acting, so slavery was not wrong for people whose culture approved of it.","idea":"Agent relativism says an action's rightness depends on the moral standards of the agent's own culture. People in a slaveholding society who followed its norms did not act wrongly, by the standards that applied to them. Defenders say this explains why we hesitate to blame ancient people as harshly as we would a modern slaveholder. Critics reply that it makes moral reform incoherent and leaves victims, whose voices were not counted, without a standard to appeal to.","example":"Harman's cannibals: Harman argued that it would be odd to say cannibals who have no reason in their own framework to refrain from eating people “ought not” to do it, in the inner sense of ought.","history":"Gilbert Harman's “Moral Relativism Defended” (1975) defended a form of agent relativism based on implicit agreements. Earlier, the sociologist William Graham Sumner, in Folkways (1906), held that mores can make anything right. Anthropologists such as Melville Herskovits also defended cultural relativism in the twentieth century.","misread":"It does not mean individuals can choose their own morality; it ties rightness to a shared culture.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Relativism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-relativism/"}]},"e5c|standards":{"summary":"Whether slavery was wrong depends on who is judging: wrong by our standards, right by theirs, and neither judgment is mistaken.","idea":"Appraiser relativism says the truth of a moral judgment depends on the standards of the person making the judgment. When we say slavery was wrong, we speak truly by our standards; when slaveholders said it was right, they spoke truly by theirs. Defenders like that it lets each culture use its own moral language without claiming the others are in error. Critics ask how genuine disagreement is possible, if both sides are right.","example":"Is the fish tasty? If I say a dish is tasty and you say it is not, we may both be right, judged by our own tastes. Appraiser relativists think moral claims may work in a similar way.","history":"The distinction between agent and appraiser relativism is discussed in Chris Gowans's Stanford entry on moral relativism. John MacFarlane's work on assessment sensitivity offers a formal framework, and David Wong's Natural Moralities (2006) defends a pluralistic relativism. It is often compared with relativism about taste or humour.","misread":"It does not mean anything goes; within a set of standards, judgments can still be mistaken.","variants":["Assessment relativism: truth depends on the context of assessment.","Pluralistic relativism: more than one true morality, within limits."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Relativism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-relativism/"}]},"e5d|fiction":{"summary":"Since moral claims are false, we should keep using moral language as a useful fiction, without really believing it.","idea":"Fictionalism says that even if error theory is true, giving up moral talk would be costly. Moral thinking helps us resist temptation and cooperate. So we should keep speaking morally, in a pretend way, as people talk about characters in novels. Defenders like that it keeps morality's practical benefits without self-deception. Critics ask whether pretend morality would keep its motivating force, and whether people can really pretend this way.","example":"Sherlock Holmes: we can say “Holmes lives at 221B Baker Street” without believing there is such a person. Fictionalists suggest moral claims might work similarly: useful and meaningful, but not literally believed.","history":"Richard Joyce's The Myth of Morality (2001) defended revolutionary moral fictionalism. Mark Kalderon's Moral Fictionalism (2005) offered a hermeneutic version, saying ordinary moral talk may already be a kind of non-literal practice. Earlier, Hans Vaihinger's The Philosophy of 'As If' (1911) argued that many useful ideas are fictions.","misread":"Fictionalism does not recommend lying to others; it recommends using moral language without literal belief.","variants":["Revolutionary fictionalism: we should start treating morality as fiction.","Hermeneutic fictionalism: morality already works like a fiction."],"reading":[{"title":"Fictionalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fictionalism/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}]},"e5d|abolish":{"summary":"If nothing is truly right or wrong, we should stop using moral talk altogether and reason in other terms.","idea":"Abolitionism takes error theory to its practical conclusion. Moral language, on this view, does more harm than good: it fuels self-righteousness, punishment and endless conflict, while claiming an authority it lacks. We could do better by talking openly about our desires, interests and compassion. Defenders think this would be more honest and more peaceful. Critics doubt that society could function without the coordinating power of moral language.","example":"Garner's alternative: instead of saying “eating meat is wrong”, an abolitionist might say “I care about animals and don't want them to suffer”. The concern stays, but the claim to objective authority goes.","history":"Richard Garner's Beyond Morality (1994) and Ian Hinckfuss's The Moral Society (1987) argued for abolishing morality. Joel Marks, in Ethics without Morals (2013), defended a similar “amoralism”. The view is discussed in Stanford's entry on moral anti-realism. Abolitionists usually keep compassion and care as motives for action.","misread":"Abolitionism does not mean becoming cruel; it means pursuing care and cooperation without moral language.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}]},"e5d|conserve":{"summary":"Even though moral claims are false, we should keep sincerely believing them, because the belief does more good than harm.","idea":"Conservationism says the best response to error theory is to carry on as before, believing moral claims in everyday life. We can hold the philosophical view in the study and the moral beliefs elsewhere. Defenders say only genuine belief gives morality its full motivating and coordinating power. Critics question whether one can choose to keep believing what one thinks is false, and whether this is a form of intellectual dishonesty.","example":"Sunrise talk: people keep saying “the sun rises” after learning that the earth turns. Conservationists suggest moral belief could survive learning error theory, used in daily life but set aside in theoretical moments.","history":"Jonas Olson's Moral Error Theory (2014) defended moral conservationism. Terence Cuneo and Sean Christy and others have discussed the options for what error theorists should do with their moral thinking. Olson presents it as an alternative to fictionalism and abolitionism, the two older responses to error theory.","misread":"It does not ask people to believe anything new; it says error theorists may keep the moral beliefs they already have.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}]},"e5e|quasi":{"summary":"Moral claims express attitudes, but some attitudes are better informed, more consistent and more defensible, so we can talk of moral truth.","idea":"Quasi-realism starts from expressivism: moral claims express attitudes, not beliefs about facts. But it argues that this does not make all attitudes equal. Some are based on ignorance, inconsistency or bias; others survive scrutiny. And expressivists can earn the right to say “torture is really wrong” and even “it would be wrong whatever anyone thought”, since saying this itself expresses a firm attitude. Defenders like that it keeps everything realists want to say without their metaphysics.","example":"Mind-independence: a quasi-realist can say, “Even if everyone approved of torture, it would still be wrong”. This expresses disapproval of torture regardless of who approves, not a claim about a moral fact.","history":"Simon Blackburn developed quasi-realism in Spreading the Word (1984), Essays in Quasi-Realism (1993) and Ruling Passions (1998). Allan Gibbard’s Wise Choices, Apt Feelings (1990) and Thinking How to Live (2003) offered a related norm-expressivism. Blackburn’s name for the view reflects its aim of earning realist talk without realist metaphysics.","misread":"Quasi-realism is not secretly realism: it explains moral truth talk without positing moral facts.","variants":["Blackburn's quasi-realism.","Gibbard's norm-expressivism: moral claims express acceptance of norms."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Cognitivism vs. Non-Cognitivism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-cognitivism/"},{"title":"Non-Cognitivism in Ethics (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/non-cogn/"}]},"e5e|emotiv":{"summary":"Moral claims just express feelings, so no moral attitude is better than another, as no taste is better than another.","idea":"Emotivism says moral claims express emotions and try to influence others' emotions. “Stealing is wrong” is like saying “stealing, boo!” in a disapproving tone. Since feelings are not true or false, moral disputes cannot be settled by facts alone, except where they rest on factual disagreements. Defenders like that this view is simple and fits a scientific view of the world. Critics say it makes moral argument seem like a shouting match.","example":"Boo and hooray: Ayer suggested that “You acted wrongly in stealing that money” adds nothing factual to “You stole that money”. It just expresses disapproval, as if saying it in a shocked tone.","history":"A. J. Ayer's Language, Truth and Logic (1936) launched the view. Charles Stevenson's Ethics and Language (1944) developed a more detailed account of emotive meaning. It drew on the logical positivists' view that only verifiable claims are factual. Stevenson stressed that moral words have emotive meaning as well as descriptive meaning.","misread":"Emotivists did not think morality is trivial; they thought moral argument works by changing attitudes, often through facts.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Non-Cognitivism in Ethics (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/non-cogn/"},{"title":"Moral Cognitivism vs. Non-Cognitivism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-cognitivism/"}]},"k9|yes":{"summary":"There really is a God, or there are gods: a being or beings worthy of worship who made or govern the world.","idea":"Theism, in the broad sense used here, is the claim that at least one divine being exists. It denies that the physical world is all there is, and it denies that the universe is ultimately indifferent to us. People find it attractive because it offers an explanation for why anything exists, a ground for meaning and morality, and a fit with experiences of prayer or awe. Think of someone who looks at a newborn and feels not just luck but gratitude to someone. Theism says that feeling is aimed at someone real. It is compatible with doubt: believing is not the same as being certain.","example":"The classic illustration is the “watch on the heath”: William Paley imagined finding a watch while walking and asked whether anyone would think it simply happened. Theists of many kinds argue that the world, like the watch, points beyond itself, though many base belief on experience or revelation rather than this argument.","history":"Belief in gods is found in nearly every recorded culture. Philosophical monotheism was developed by the Hebrew prophets, by Greek thinkers such as Plato and Aristotle, and later by Augustine (354 to 430), Ibn Sina (980 to 1037), Maimonides (1138 to 1204), Aquinas (1225 to 1274) and Ramanuja (traditionally 1017 to 1137). Today Alvin Plantinga and Richard Swinburne are leading defenders.","misread":"Theism is not one religion: Jews, Christians, Muslims, Sikhs, devotional Hindus, deists and polytheists are all theists in this broad sense.","variants":["Monotheism: exactly one God.","Polytheism: many gods.","Pantheism and panentheism: God identical with, or including, the world."],"reading":[{"title":"God and Other Ultimates (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/god-ultimates/"},{"title":"God, Western Concepts of (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/god-west/"}]},"k9|ultimate":{"summary":"Something sacred and ultimate is real or reachable, but it is not a personal being who thinks and chooses.","idea":"This is the stance of non-theistic religion. It agrees with theists that the physical world is not the whole story: there is a deepest reality, truth or freedom that gives life its point. But it denies that this ultimate is a person with plans, moods or commands. A personal God, on this view, would be too small, one being among others. Compare the difference between a ruler who makes laws and the laws of mathematics themselves: the ultimate is more like the second. Many find this attractive because it keeps the sacred without the puzzles of a God who chooses to allow suffering.","example":"Shankara’s rope and snake: at dusk you see a snake, then light reveals a rope. The fear was real, but its object was a misreading. Advaita Vedanta says the world of separate things is like the snake, and Brahman is the rope. Buddhists use different images, such as a flame going out when its fuel is gone.","history":"Non-theistic paths include early Buddhism (from the Buddha, around the 5th century BCE), Jainism (Mahavira, roughly the same era), philosophical Daoism (the Daodejing and the Zhuangzi, around the 4th century BCE) and Advaita Vedanta (Shankara, around the 8th century CE). In the West, Plotinus (c. 204 to 270) and the theologian Paul Tillich (1886 to 1965), who called God “being itself”, come close.","misread":"Non-theistic does not mean atheist in the everyday sense: many such traditions honour gods or devas, but treat them as mortal beings, not as the ultimate.","variants":["Non-dual Absolute: Brahman or the One, identical with your deepest self.","Way or pattern: the Dao.","Liberation without creator: Buddhist nirvana, Jain moksha."],"reading":[{"title":"God and Other Ultimates (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/god-ultimates/"},{"title":"Mysticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mysticism/"}]},"k9|no":{"summary":"There is no God and there are no gods; the world is not made or ruled by any divine being.","idea":"Atheism, as chosen here, is the positive belief that no God exists, not just the absence of a belief. It denies any supreme being worthy of worship, though an atheist can still find life meaningful, morality binding, and nature awe-inspiring. Atheists argue that natural causes explain the world, that arguments for God fail, and that suffering or divine silence count against God. Think of a detective who checks every lead for a suspected culprit and finds nothing: at some point “there was no culprit” becomes the reasonable verdict. Like theism, atheism does not require certainty.","example":"Laplace’s reply: when Napoleon asked why his book on the solar system never mentioned God, Pierre-Simon Laplace is reported to have said he had no need of that hypothesis. The story, whether exact or not, captures the atheist idea that good explanations simply stop needing God.","history":"Doubt about the gods appears early: the Indian Cārvāka school rejected gods and an afterlife, and Greek thinkers like Epicurus (341 to 270 BCE) put the gods out of human affairs. Open atheism grew from d’Holbach’s System of Nature (1770). Modern defenders include Bertrand Russell, J. L. Mackie (The Miracle of Theism, 1982), and Graham Oppy.","misread":"Atheism is not the claim to have proved there is no God; most atheists hold it as a reasonable belief on the evidence, as theists hold theirs.","variants":["Positive (strong) atheism: believing there is no God.","Negative (weak) atheism: simply lacking belief in God.","Local atheism: denying a particular conception of God."],"reading":[{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"},{"title":"Atheism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/atheism/"}]},"k9|agnostic":{"summary":"Neither believing nor disbelieving in God, because the reasons seem balanced or the question lies beyond what we can know.","idea":"Agnosticism is a settled position, not a shrug. The agnostic has looked at the question and judges that neither “God exists” nor “God does not exist” deserves belief. It denies that the theist or atheist has made their case. Some agnostics think the evidence is evenly balanced; others think human minds simply cannot reach the answer. The appeal is intellectual honesty: believe only as strongly as the evidence warrants. Compare a juror who, after hearing the whole trial, honestly cannot say whether the defendant did it. Saying “I don’t know” then is not laziness but accuracy.","example":"Russell’s two audiences: Bertrand Russell said that to philosophers he would call himself an agnostic, since he could not strictly disprove God, but to ordinary people an atheist, since he thought God about as likely as the Olympian gods. The case shows how much hangs on what “agnostic” means.","history":"T. H. Huxley coined “agnostic” in 1869 for his rule of not claiming knowledge one lacks. Much earlier, Protagoras (5th century BCE) said he could not know whether the gods exist, and the Rig Veda’s Creation Hymn (10.129) ends by wondering whether even the highest overseer knows how the world began. Anthony Kenny and Paul Draper are recent defenders.","misread":"Agnosticism is not the same as being undecided: it is a considered judgment that belief either way is unwarranted, not a view you haven’t got around to forming.","variants":["Weak or evidential agnosticism: the evidence happens to be balanced.","Strong agnosticism: the question cannot be known by humans at all."],"reading":[{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"},{"title":"Atheism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/atheism/"}]},"k9|nomeaning":{"summary":"Talk of God fails to make a genuine factual claim, so it is neither true nor false and cannot be believed or disbelieved.","idea":"Theological noncognitivism says the sentence “God exists” does not state a fact. Either the word “God” is too unclear to pick anything out, or nothing we could ever observe would bear on it, so the sentence says nothing about the world. It therefore rejects theism, atheism and agnosticism alike, since all three treat the sentence as meaningful. Compare “the universe doubled in size overnight, along with every ruler and every measuring device.” If no test could ever tell, did anything happen? The noncognitivist asks the same of God.","example":"Flew’s invisible gardener: two explorers find a clearing that might be tended. One insists there is a gardener; they set up fences, dogs and electric wires, but nothing ever shows up. The believer says the gardener is invisible, silent and leaves no trace. What, asks Flew, is left of the claim?","history":"The view grew from the verification principle of the Vienna Circle. Rudolf Carnap’s “The Elimination of Metaphysics” (1932) and A. J. Ayer’s Language, Truth and Logic (1936) argued that talk of a transcendent God is literally meaningless. Antony Flew’s “Theology and Falsification” (1950) gave the gardener parable. John Hick replied with “eschatological verification”: experience after death could confirm it.","misread":"It is not a sneaky form of atheism: Ayer stressed that if “God exists” is meaningless, then so is “God does not exist.”","variants":["Verificationist noncognitivism: unverifiable claims are meaningless.","Ignosticism: the term “God” must be defined before the question can be asked."],"reading":[{"title":"Religious Language (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-language/"},{"title":"Logical Empiricism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logical-empiricism/"}]},"k9a|theism":{"summary":"One personal God, all-knowing, all-powerful and good, who knows the whole future, including our free choices.","idea":"Classical personal theism holds that there is exactly one God who is a person in the sense of knowing, willing and loving, and who is perfect. Perfection includes complete knowledge, so God knows not only the past and present but every future free act. It denies that God is ignorant, limited or still learning. Its appeal is that it gives a God fully worthy of trust: one who cannot be surprised and whose promises cannot fail. Think of a parent who already knows how the story turns out but still lets the child choose each step.","example":"Boethius’s watcher on the hill: someone standing high above a road sees all travellers at once, while each traveller sees only the stretch ahead. God, outside time, sees all of history at a glance, so knowing what you will do is like seeing it, not causing it.","history":"The view is shared by Jewish, Christian and Islamic philosophy: Philo, Augustine, Boethius’s Consolation of Philosophy (c. 524), Ibn Sina, al-Ghazali, Maimonides’ Guide of the Perplexed (c. 1190) and Aquinas’s Summa Theologiae. In India Ramanuja and Madhva defended a personal supreme God, Vishnu, and Guru Nanak (1469 to 1539) founded Sikhism on devotion to one God.","misread":"It does not deny free will: most classical theists hold that God’s foreknowledge, unlike fate, leaves our choices genuinely ours.","variants":["Classical theism: God is simple, timeless and unchanging (Aquinas).","Molinism: God knows what every person would freely do in any situation (Luis de Molina).","Neo-classical theism: God is personal and omniscient but inside time."],"reading":[{"title":"Divine Foreknowledge and Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/free-will-foreknowledge/"},{"title":"Omniscience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/omniscience/"},{"title":"Monotheism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/monotheism/"}]},"k9a|open":{"summary":"God is personal and loving but does not know in advance what free creatures will choose, so God responds and adapts.","idea":"Open theism keeps a personal, powerful, loving God but denies that the future is already settled, even for God. Future free choices do not yet exist to be known, so God knows them as possibilities, not certainties. God therefore takes real risks, changes plans in response to prayer, and enters into give-and-take relationships. The appeal is a God who genuinely interacts rather than watching a finished film. Compare a chess master playing a beginner: the master cannot predict every move but can answer any of them and still win.","example":"The changed mind of God: in the Bible, God announces Nineveh’s destruction, the people repent, and God relents (Jonah 3). Classical theists read this as a figure of speech. Open theists read it literally: God really responded to something God had not fixed beforehand.","history":"Elements appear in the medieval Jewish philosopher Gersonides (1288 to 1344), who held that God knows future choices only as possibilities, and among 16th and 17th century Socinians. The modern movement began with Richard Rice’s The Openness of God (1980) and the 1994 collection of the same title by Clark Pinnock, Rice, John Sanders, William Hasker and David Basinger.","misread":"Open theists do not think God is weak or ignorant of facts; they hold that there are no facts yet about future free choices to know.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Open Theism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/o-theism/"},{"title":"Divine Foreknowledge and Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/free-will-foreknowledge/"},{"title":"Divine Providence (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/providence-divine/"}]},"k9a|panentheism":{"summary":"The world exists within God, like a body within a self, yet God is greater than the whole world.","idea":"Panentheism means “all in God.” It sits between classical theism, which keeps God wholly separate from the world, and pantheism, which identifies God with it. On this view the world is real and is part of God’s life, so what happens in the world, including suffering, affects God. But God is more than the world: God has a mind and purposes that go beyond it. The appeal is a God who is intimately present, not a distant monarch. Think of how a sharp pain in your foot is happening to you, yet you are more than your foot.","example":"Ramanuja’s body analogy: the soul controls and sustains its body, and the body exists for the soul, yet the soul is not reduced to it. Ramanuja held that the universe of souls and matter is the body of Brahman, the personal God Vishnu, in exactly this sense.","history":"Ramanuja (traditionally 1017 to 1137) taught Viśiṣṭādvaita, “qualified non-dualism.” The word panentheism was coined by the German philosopher Karl Krause in the early 19th century. Alfred North Whitehead’s Process and Reality (1929) and Charles Hartshorne’s Man’s Vision of God (1941) built process theology on it. Some read Hegel this way.","misread":"Panentheism is not pantheism: the extra “en” matters, because God exceeds the world rather than simply being it.","variants":["Process theism: God and the world change together (Whitehead, Hartshorne).","Viśiṣṭādvaita: the world is God’s body (Ramanuja)."],"reading":[{"title":"Panentheism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/panentheism/"},{"title":"Process Theism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/process-theism/"},{"title":"Ramanuja (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ramanuja/"}]},"k9a|pantheism":{"summary":"God and the universe are one and the same: nature as a whole is divine, with nothing beyond it.","idea":"Pantheism identifies God with everything that exists. It denies a creator standing outside the world and usually denies that God has a personality who plans and judges. What it affirms is that the whole of reality is a unity worthy of reverence. The appeal is reverence without the supernatural: a scientist marvelling at the laws of physics can be engaged in something like worship. Picture standing under a clear night sky and feeling that you are part of something vast and sacred; the pantheist says that whole is all God is.","example":"Spinoza’s “God or Nature”: in his Ethics Spinoza treated God and Nature as one infinite substance. In 1929 Einstein, asked whether he believed in God, said his God was Spinoza’s, shown in the lawful harmony of the world, not a God who concerns himself with human fates and actions.","history":"Ancient Stoics treated the cosmos as a living divine whole. Baruch Spinoza’s Ethics (1677) is the classic statement. John Toland coined “pantheist” in 1705. The German Romantics and some American Transcendentalists took it up. Some readings of Advaita and of Daoism are close to it, though both traditions resist the label.","misread":"Pantheists do not usually worship trees or rocks one by one; they revere the unity of the whole.","variants":["Spinozist pantheism: one substance with infinite attributes.","Naturalistic pantheism: reverence for nature without anything supernatural."],"reading":[{"title":"Pantheism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pantheism/"},{"title":"Baruch Spinoza (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spinoza/"}]},"k9a|deism":{"summary":"A God created the world and its laws but does not intervene: no miracles, revelations or answered prayers.","idea":"Deism accepts a creator, usually on the strength of reason, but rejects any later divine interference. God designed the universe so well that it needs no adjustment. It denies revealed scriptures, miracles and special providence, and holds that natural reason is enough for religion and ethics. Its appeal is that it keeps a cause for the universe while fitting a world that runs on regular laws. The usual picture is a watchmaker: once a good watch is wound, the maker does not need to keep opening the back to move the hands.","example":"The cosmic watchmaker: a perfectly crafted clock that runs without its maker’s help reflects better on the maker than one that needs constant repairs. Deists argued that a world governed by fixed laws is evidence of a greater God than one that needs miracles.","history":"Lord Herbert of Cherbury’s De Veritate (1624) is often called the start of English deism. John Toland, Matthew Tindal’s Christianity as Old as the Creation (1730) and Anthony Collins followed. Voltaire championed it in France, and Thomas Paine’s The Age of Reason (1794 to 1807) spread it in America, where Thomas Jefferson held related views.","misread":"Deists are not secret atheists: most sincerely believed in a creator and often in an afterlife; they rejected revelation, not God.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"American Enlightenment Thought (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/american-enlightenment-thought/"},{"title":"Anthony Collins (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/collins/"},{"title":"God, Western Concepts of (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/god-west/"}]},"k9a|poly":{"summary":"There are many gods, each with their own character and domain, rather than one supreme God.","idea":"Polytheism holds that the divine is plural: many distinct gods, goddesses or spirits, often linked to places, forces of nature or aspects of life. It denies that all divinity is gathered into a single being. Many polytheists see the gods as real persons who can be honoured with offerings and festivals. The appeal is that the world itself looks plural and conflicting, and a family of gods fits it better than a single will. Compare a city whose life is shaped by many people, each with a role, rather than one ruler.","example":"Shinto kami: a Shinto shrine may honour the kami of a mountain, a waterfall or an ancestor. A visitor can ask one for help with exams and another for a safe birth, without thinking one god must rule the rest.","history":"Ancient Greek religion (Homer, Hesiod’s Theogony), Norse, Egyptian and Yoruba traditions are polytheistic, as is Shinto, recorded in the Kojiki (712). The Neoplatonist Proclus (412 to 485) gave a philosophical defence of the gods. David Hume’s Natural History of Religion (1757) argued polytheism came first historically. Revived forms include Hellenic and Norse paganism.","misread":"Many polytheisms also have a high god or a single source behind the gods, so the line between polytheism and monotheism is not always sharp.","variants":["Hard polytheism: the gods are fully distinct persons.","Soft polytheism: many gods are faces of one divine reality."],"reading":[{"title":"Monotheism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/monotheism/"},{"title":"God and Other Ultimates (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/god-ultimates/"}]},"k9f|absolute":{"summary":"One impersonal reality, beyond all qualities, underlies everything, and your deepest self is identical with it.","idea":"This view holds that there is a single ultimate reality with no parts, no personality and no features that words can capture. The world of many separate things depends on it, or is an appearance of it, and the true self is not separate from it. It denies that the ultimate is a person, and denies that the self is finally one thing among others. The appeal is unity: the sense in deep meditation or love that boundaries fall away. Think of waves on the ocean: each seems separate, yet each is water through and through.","example":"“You are that”: in the Chandogya Upanishad, the sage Uddalaka has his son dissolve salt in water. The salt cannot be seen, yet every sip tastes of it. So too, he teaches, the subtle essence pervades everything, and “you are that.” Advaita reads this as the identity of self and Brahman.","history":"The Upanishads (from around 800 BCE) speak of Brahman and Atman. Gaudapada and then Shankara (c. 8th century CE), in commentaries on the Brahma Sutras, Upanishads and Gita, founded Advaita Vedanta. In Greece Plotinus’s Enneads (c. 270) described the One beyond being. Meister Eckhart (c. 1260 to 1328) spoke of a Godhead beyond God.","misread":"Advaita does not say the world is a pure hallucination; it is real at the everyday level, but not ultimately real.","variants":["Advaita Vedanta: Brahman alone is real, the world is appearance (Shankara).","Neoplatonism: all things flow from the One (Plotinus)."],"reading":[{"title":"Śaṅkara (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/shankara/"},{"title":"Vedanta, Advaita (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/advaita-vedanta/"},{"title":"Plotinus (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/plotinus/"}]},"k9f|dao":{"summary":"The ultimate is the Dao, a nameless source and pattern that everything follows, without plan or will.","idea":"Daoism takes the ultimate to be the Way: the spontaneous pattern by which things arise, flourish and return. It has no purposes and gives no commands; it simply works, “doing nothing, yet nothing is left undone.” It denies that the cosmos is run by a ruler, and warns that fixed names and rigid rules distort it. The appeal is a way of living that goes with the grain of things. Think of water: soft, yielding, taking the lowest place, yet over time it carves stone. The sage learns to act like that.","example":"Cook Ding and the ox: in the Zhuangzi, a cook carves an ox so skilfully that his blade stays sharp for years. He no longer sees the whole ox but follows its natural joints. Effortless action, wu wei, means living by the Dao in the same way.","history":"The Daodejing, traditionally credited to Laozi and probably compiled by the 4th century BCE, and the Zhuangzi (4th to 3rd century BCE) are the founding texts. Wang Bi (226 to 249) wrote an influential commentary. Religious Daoism, from the Celestial Masters of the 2nd century CE, added gods, rituals and practices for long life.","misread":"Daoism is not passivity: wu wei means action without forcing, the ease of a master craftsperson, not doing nothing at all.","variants":["Philosophical Daoism: Laozi and Zhuangzi.","Religious Daoism: deities, ritual and immortality practices."],"reading":[{"title":"Daoism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/daoism/"},{"title":"Laozi (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/laozi/"},{"title":"Zhuangzi (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/zhuangzi/"}]},"k9f|nirvana":{"summary":"There is no creator and no lasting self, yet liberation from craving, suffering and rebirth is real and attainable.","idea":"Buddhist non-theism holds that suffering comes from craving and from clinging to a self that does not really exist. When craving ends, suffering ends: that ending is nirvana. It denies a creator God and denies an eternal soul. Gods may exist, but they are mortal beings inside the cycle of rebirth, needing liberation too. The appeal is a path that depends on practice and insight, not on a being’s favour. Think of an ember that glows only while fed: remove the fuel and it goes out. Nirvana literally means “blowing out.”","example":"The poisoned arrow: a man shot with an arrow refuses treatment until he knows who shot it, from what family, with what bow. He will die first. The Buddha used this to explain why he set aside questions about the cosmos: the urgent task is ending suffering.","history":"Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha (around the 5th century BCE), taught the Four Noble Truths and no-self (anattā). Nāgārjuna (c. 2nd century CE) developed the Madhyamaka philosophy of emptiness. Vasubandhu and Dharmakīrti argued explicitly against a creator. Theravada, Mahayana and Vajrayana traditions differ over how to describe nirvana.","misread":"Nirvana is not simply annihilation; the Buddha declined to say the liberated person exists or does not exist after death.","variants":["Theravada: liberation of the individual arhat.","Mahayana: the bodhisattva path for all beings.","Madhyamaka: nirvana and samsara both empty of fixed nature."],"reading":[{"title":"The Buddha (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/buddha/"},{"title":"Ethics in Indian Buddhism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-indian-buddhism/"}]},"k9f|moksha":{"summary":"There is no creator, but each soul is eternal and can free itself from karma and rebirth through its own effort.","idea":"Jainism holds that the universe is beginningless and uncreated. It contains countless eternal souls (jīva), each naturally all-knowing and blissful but weighed down by karma, which Jains picture as a fine matter that sticks to the soul through harmful action. Through non-violence, truthfulness and austerity a soul sheds its karma and rises to liberation (moksha). It denies a creator; liberated souls are honoured as perfect, not as world-makers. The appeal is radical self-reliance: no one saves you but you. Think of a gourd coated in mud that sinks, then floats once the mud washes off.","example":"Jinasena’s challenge to a creator: in his Mahāpurāṇa (9th century), the Jain teacher Jinasena asked where a creator was before creation, what materials he used, and why a perfect being would want anything. If the world needs a maker, he argued, so would the maker.","history":"Mahavira (traditionally 599 to 527 BCE, though many date him later) is the 24th tīrthaṅkara of the present age. Umāsvāti’s Tattvārtha Sūtra (dated between the 2nd and 5th centuries CE) is accepted by both Digambara and Śvetāmbara Jains. Jain thinkers also developed anekāntavāda, the doctrine that reality has many sides.","misread":"Jains are not atheists in the sense of rejecting the sacred: they worship the tīrthaṅkaras as perfected souls and role models.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Jaina Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/jaina-philosophy/"},{"title":"Jain Philosophy (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/jain/"}]},"k9f|mystery":{"summary":"Something sacred is real, but no words, concepts or single tradition can fully capture it.","idea":"This mystical stance affirms a sacred reality while holding that all descriptions fall short. It denies that any creed has the final word and denies that the sacred is a mere feeling. Knowledge of it comes, if at all, by direct experience or by stripping away inadequate ideas. Its appeal is humility combined with openness: you can take religious experience seriously without signing up to one set of doctrines. Think of trying to describe the taste of a mango to someone who has never had one: words help, but they are not the thing.","example":"“Not this, not this”: in the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad, the sage Yajnavalkya describes the self only by denial: it is not this, not that, ungraspable. Mystics in many traditions follow the same path, approaching the sacred by setting aside every description as too small, until only silent awareness is left.","history":"Apophatic (negative) theology runs from Pseudo-Dionysius (c. 500) through The Cloud of Unknowing (14th century) and Meister Eckhart. Sufis such as Rumi (1207 to 1273) and Ibn Arabi wrote of union beyond words. William James’s Varieties of Religious Experience (1902) listed ineffability as the first mark of mystical states. Aldous Huxley’s The Perennial Philosophy (1945) popularised a common core.","misread":"Mysticism need not mean vague spirituality; many mystics belonged to strict traditions and practised rigorous disciplines.","variants":["Perennialism: all mystics touch the same reality (Huxley, Stace).","Constructivism: each experience is shaped by its tradition (Steven Katz)."],"reading":[{"title":"Mysticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mysticism/"},{"title":"Religious Experience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-experience/"}]},"k9d|arguments":{"summary":"Belief in God or the sacred is justified by careful reasoning, such as the argument that the universe needs a cause.","idea":"This is natural theology: the project of showing by reason alone, without appeal to scripture, that God exists. It rejects the idea that faith must be blind. Arguments may start from the existence of anything at all, from order, from the idea of a perfect being, or from consciousness and morality. The appeal is that reason is shared by everyone, so the case can be put to anyone. Compare how a detective reasons from clues to the existence of an unseen culprit, rather than waiting for a confession.","example":"Aquinas’s Five Ways: in the Summa Theologiae, Aquinas argued from change, causation, contingency, degrees of perfection and goal-directed behaviour to a first source of all things. His contingency argument asks why anything exists that could have failed to exist.","history":"Plato’s Laws Book X and Aristotle’s unmoved mover began the tradition. Al-Kindi and al-Ghazali developed the kalām argument from a beginning; Ibn Sina argued from necessary existence. The Nyāya philosopher Udayana (10th to 11th century) gave a famous Indian proof of God. Aquinas, Leibniz, William Paley and, today, Richard Swinburne and William Lane Craig carry it on.","misread":"Natural theologians rarely claim strict proof; many, like Swinburne, argue that the evidence together makes God more probable than not.","variants":["Deductive natural theology: proofs with certain conclusions (Aquinas).","Cumulative case: arguments that together raise probability (Swinburne)."],"reading":[{"title":"Natural Theology (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/theo-nat/"},{"title":"Cosmological Argument (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/cosmological-argument/"},{"title":"The Epistemology of Religion (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-epistemology/"}]},"k9d|moral":{"summary":"Belief rests on morality: real right and wrong make sense only if there is a God or a sacred order behind them.","idea":"Here belief is grounded in the moral life. We feel bound by duties, sense that some acts are wrong whatever anyone thinks, and hope that goodness and happiness will finally meet. Defenders argue that a universe of mere matter could not produce real obligations, so morality points to a source beyond us. It denies that ethics is just preference, evolution or social agreement. The appeal is that our firmest moral convictions become evidence. Compare the sense that a promise binds you even when no one would ever find out you broke it.","example":"Kant’s highest good: Kant argued that morality commands us to pursue a world where virtue and happiness match. Since nature alone does not guarantee this, practical reason must postulate a God who can bring it about, even though theoretical reason cannot prove God exists.","history":"Immanuel Kant’s Critique of Practical Reason (1788) made God a postulate of morality. John Henry Newman argued from conscience in the 19th century. C. S. Lewis popularised the argument in Mere Christianity (1952). Robert Adams (Finite and Infinite Goods, 1999) and C. Stephen Evans defend it today. In India, karma and dharma give a similar moral order without a personal God.","misread":"It is not the claim that atheists cannot be good; it is the claim that goodness itself needs God to exist.","variants":["Kantian moral argument: God as postulate of practical reason.","Divine command theory: obligation is constituted by God’s commands."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Arguments for the Existence of God (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-arguments-god/"},{"title":"Theological Voluntarism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/voluntarism-theological/"}]},"k9d|experience":{"summary":"Belief rests on direct experience of God or the sacred, one’s own or that of trusted others, in daily life, prayer or meditation.","idea":"This view grounds belief in encounter rather than inference. Just as you believe a tree is in front of you because you see it, a person may believe God is present because they experience that presence. It denies that belief must wait for an argument. Experiences range from quiet assurance in prayer to overwhelming mystical states and the awakening reported in meditation. The appeal is directness: most of what we know rests on experience, so why exclude this kind? Compare trusting that a friend loves you because of how they treat you, not because of a proof.","example":"Teresa of Ávila’s certainty: in The Interior Castle (1577), Teresa described prayer in which God’s presence was so evident that afterward she could not doubt it. Philosophers ask whether such conviction is like perceiving a tree, or only like a powerful feeling.","history":"Al-Ghazali’s Deliverance from Error (12th century) describes finding certainty through Sufi practice after doubting reason. Friedrich Schleiermacher (1768 to 1834), William James (1902), and Rudolf Otto’s The Idea of the Holy (1917) put experience at religion’s centre. William Alston’s Perceiving God (1991) and Richard Swinburne developed its epistemology. Hindu and Buddhist traditions value direct realisation.","misread":"","variants":["Perceptual model: experiencing God is like perceiving an object (Alston).","Testimonial: trusting others’ reports of experience (Swinburne)."],"reading":[{"title":"Religious Experience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-experience/"},{"title":"The Epistemology of Religion (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-epistemology/"}]},"k9d|reveal":{"summary":"Belief rests on revelation: a scripture or tradition that one has good reason to trust as coming from God or an enlightened teacher.","idea":"This view grounds belief in testimony from the divine or from a teacher who saw the truth. Much of what anyone knows comes from trusted testimony, such as textbooks or experts, and believers argue that a reliable scripture or tradition is a source of knowledge too. It denies that unaided reason is the only route, though most believers think there are reasons to trust the source. The appeal is that it explains how ordinary people, not only philosophers, can know about God. Compare trusting a doctor’s diagnosis without being able to check it yourself.","example":"Augustine’s “believe in order to understand”: Augustine held that faith in revealed truth comes first and then lets the mind grasp what it could not alone. Anselm later called theology “faith seeking understanding.”","history":"Judaism grounds belief in the Torah given at Sinai, Christianity in the Bible and Church, Islam in the Qur’an as God’s speech, Sikhism in the Guru Granth Sahib. Indian Mīmāṃsā thinkers held the Vedas to be authorless and eternal. Augustine, Aquinas, al-Ghazali and Maimonides discussed how revelation and reason relate; John Locke’s Essay (1689) set rules for trusting revelation.","misread":"Belief from revelation is not necessarily blind: most traditions offer reasons, such as miracles, fulfilled prophecy or a text’s quality, for trusting the source.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Faith (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/faith/"},{"title":"The Epistemology of Religion (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-epistemology/"},{"title":"Arabic and Islamic Philosophy of Religion (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/arabic-islamic-religion/"}]},"k9d|basic":{"summary":"Belief in God can be rational without argument, as a basic belief arising from experience, like trusting memory or other minds.","idea":"Reformed epistemology says some beliefs are rightly held without being inferred from others. You do not argue your way to believing you had breakfast or that other people have minds; you simply find yourself believing, and that is reasonable. Belief in God, formed when you see a starry sky or feel guilt or gratitude, can be like that. It rejects evidentialism, the demand that every belief be backed by argument. The appeal is that it fits how most believers actually believe. Of course basic beliefs can still be overturned by strong counter-evidence.","example":"The Great Pumpkin objection: critics asked whether anyone could then call belief in the Great Pumpkin basic. Plantinga answered that basic beliefs must arise in the right conditions from a reliable faculty, and there is no faculty for sensing pumpkins.","history":"John Calvin’s Institutes (1536, final edition 1559) spoke of a sense of the divine in everyone. Thomas Reid (1764) defended trusting our faculties without proof. Alvin Plantinga (God and Other Minds, 1967; “Reason and Belief in God”, 1983; Warranted Christian Belief, 2000), Nicholas Wolterstorff and William Alston developed it.","misread":"It does not say belief in God is immune to argument; basic beliefs can be defeated, and Plantinga spends much effort answering objections.","variants":["Warrant view: belief produced by properly working faculties aimed at truth (Plantinga)."],"reading":[{"title":"Reformed Epistemology (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ref-epis/"},{"title":"The Epistemology of Religion (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-epistemology/"}]},"k9d|fideism":{"summary":"Belief rests on faith alone, which neither needs nor seeks the support of reason.","idea":"Fideism holds that religious faith is its own ground. It denies that faith should be measured by the standards of argument and evidence; some fideists think reason is simply not equipped for these questions, others that faith deliberately goes beyond it. The appeal is that it frees faith from the ups and downs of debate and treats it as a personal commitment, the way love is. Think of someone who stays loyal to a friend accused of something, not because the evidence is clear, but because loyalty is part of who they are.","example":"Abraham on Mount Moriah: in Fear and Trembling, Kierkegaard dwells on Abraham, told to sacrifice Isaac. No ethical reasoning could justify it, yet Abraham goes in faith. Kierkegaard calls faith a passion that holds on where understanding stops.","history":"Tertullian (c. 160 to 220) contrasted Athens and Jerusalem. Michel de Montaigne and Pierre Bayle combined scepticism with faith. Blaise Pascal’s Pensées (1670) held that the heart has its reasons. Søren Kierkegaard (Fear and Trembling, 1843; Concluding Unscientific Postscript, 1846) is the classic fideist. Karl Barth rejected natural theology in the 20th century.","misread":"Most fideists do not say faith is irrational; they say reason is the wrong tool for judging it.","variants":["Sceptical fideism: reason cannot settle anything, so faith fills the gap (Montaigne).","Existential fideism: faith as passionate commitment (Kierkegaard)."],"reading":[{"title":"Fideism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fideism/"},{"title":"Søren Kierkegaard (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kierkegaard/"}]},"k9b|evidence":{"summary":"The amount of seemingly pointless suffering in the world is strong evidence that no all-good, all-powerful God exists.","idea":"This is atheism from evil. A God who is perfectly good would want to prevent needless suffering, and an all-powerful God could. Yet the world contains vast suffering that seems to serve no purpose, from childhood cancers to animals dying in agony over millions of years. The atheist concludes that such a God almost certainly does not exist. It does not need to show God is impossible, only that the evidence points away. Compare a person who says they love their dog yet watches it suffer when they could easily help: we would doubt the love or the power.","example":"Rowe’s fawn: in a distant forest, lightning starts a fire. A fawn is trapped, horribly burned, and lies in agony for days before dying, unseen. William Rowe asked what good could possibly require this, and argued that cases like it make God unlikely.","history":"Epicurus (as reported by Lactantius) and David Hume’s Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion (1779) pressed the problem. William Rowe’s “The Problem of Evil and Some Varieties of Atheism” (1979) gave the modern evidential version, and Paul Draper (1989) argued that suffering fits indifference better than God. Buddhist thinkers also used suffering against a creator.","misread":"This argument targets an all-good, all-powerful God; it does not touch a limited God or a non-personal ultimate.","variants":["Evidential argument: pointless evil makes God improbable (Rowe).","Argument from indifference: suffering fits an indifferent universe best (Draper)."],"reading":[{"title":"The Evidential Problem of Evil (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/evil-evi/"},{"title":"The Problem of Evil (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/evil/"}]},"k9b|hidden":{"summary":"A perfectly loving God would make sure open seekers could find God, yet many sincere seekers never do, so there is no such God.","idea":"The hiddenness argument does not start from suffering but from absence. A perfectly loving God would want a relationship with everyone, and a relationship requires believing the other exists. So such a God would make belief available to anyone not resisting. Yet there are people who sincerely search, pray and stay open, and never find any sign. The atheist concludes there is no perfectly loving God. Compare a parent who loves their child but never once lets the child know they exist: we would doubt that parent is loving, or there.","example":"The sincere seeker: imagine someone raised religious who loses belief, spends years seeking God through prayer, scripture and study, wants to believe, and still finds nothing. Schellenberg argues that a loving God would not leave such a person in the dark.","history":"Pascal noted God’s hiddenness in the 17th century, and Kierkegaard reflected on it. J. L. Schellenberg’s Divine Hiddenness and Human Reason (1993) turned it into a formal atheist argument, and The Hiddenness Argument (2015) restated it. Theodore Drange gave a related argument from nonbelief. Michael Rea and others have replied.","misread":"The argument is not that God would be obvious to everyone; it is that God would be findable by anyone genuinely open.","variants":["Schellenberg’s argument: nonresistant nonbelief is incompatible with perfect love.","Argument from nonbelief: God would want everyone to believe (Drange)."],"reading":[{"title":"Hiddenness of God (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/divine-hiddenness/"},{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"}]},"k9b|default":{"summary":"Without good evidence for God, and with natural explanations available, the reasonable default is not to believe.","idea":"This view puts the burden of proof on the believer. Extraordinary claims need support, and none of the arguments for God succeeds, while science explains the world without appeal to God. So the rational stance is disbelief, as with any unsupported claim. It denies that atheism needs a disproof. The appeal is consistency: you do not believe in fairies, ghosts or Zeus without evidence, so why make an exception? Compare a rumour that a classmate is a secret millionaire: with no evidence, you don’t need to disprove it to doubt it.","example":"The dragon in the garage: Carl Sagan imagined a friend claiming an invisible, silent, heatless, floating dragon lives in his garage. Every test you propose, the friend explains away. Sagan asked what difference there is between such a dragon and no dragon at all, and why anyone should believe in it.","history":"Bertrand Russell (“Why I Am Not a Christian”, 1927; “Is There a God?”, 1952) is the classic source. Antony Flew’s “The Presumption of Atheism” (1972) argued that atheism is the default. Michael Scriven (1966) and later Graham Oppy, who compares theism and naturalism as rival worldviews, developed it.","misread":"The view does not say atheism needs no reasons; it says the lack of good reasons for God, plus naturalism’s success, is itself a reason.","variants":["Presumption of atheism: the burden of proof lies on theists (Flew).","Comparative naturalism: naturalism explains everything theism does more simply (Oppy)."],"reading":[{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"},{"title":"Naturalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/naturalism/"}]},"k9b|debunk":{"summary":"Belief in God is best explained by human psychology, upbringing and culture rather than by God really existing.","idea":"Debunking arguments do not attack the content of belief directly. They explain why people believe and show that the explanation does not involve God. If belief comes from a tendency to see agents everywhere, from wishful thinking, or from the religion one happened to be raised in, then people would hold it even in a godless world, so holding it shows nothing. The appeal is that it accounts for why belief is so widespread yet so varied. Compare a belief you discover was planted by an advertiser: knowing its cause makes you trust it less.","example":"Rustling in the grass: our ancestors who mistook wind for a predator lived; those who mistook a predator for wind died. Cognitive scientists suggest this left us quick to detect hidden agents, so belief in invisible beings may be a side effect rather than a perception of one.","history":"Xenophanes (6th century BCE) noted that people picture gods like themselves. Hume’s Natural History of Religion (1757), Feuerbach’s The Essence of Christianity (1841), Marx and Freud’s The Future of an Illusion (1927) gave debunking explanations. Cognitive science of religion, for example Pascal Boyer’s Religion Explained (2001) and Dennett’s Breaking the Spell (2006), continues it.","misread":"A causal explanation of belief does not automatically prove it false; that would be the genetic fallacy. The argument claims it removes the belief’s support.","variants":["Projection theories: God as projected human ideals (Feuerbach, Freud).","Cognitive science: belief as byproduct of agency detection."],"reading":[{"title":"Evolutionary Approaches to Religion (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-evolutionary/"},{"title":"Hume, David: Religion (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/hume-rel/"}]},"k9b|diversity":{"summary":"The world’s religions contradict one another, and none has a better claim than the rest, so none should be believed.","idea":"This argument starts from disagreement. Thousands of religions make rival claims about God, salvation and the afterlife, and their followers are equally sincere and often equally intelligent. Most people hold the religion of the place where they were born. Since no tradition stands out on neutral grounds, the atheist concludes that the likeliest explanation is that none is right. It denies that any faith can claim special access. Compare a jury hearing ten witnesses who each give a different story: the sensible verdict may be that none of them saw clearly.","example":"Born in a different place: a Christian in Texas, a Muslim in Cairo and a Hindu in Varanasi might each have held another’s faith had they been born elsewhere. Critics ask whether a belief so tied to birthplace can be reliable.","history":"Xenophanes mocked rival pictures of the gods. Hume’s Enquiry (1748) argued that rival religions’ miracles cancel each other out. John Stuart Mill noted the accident of birth in On Liberty (1859). Contemporary discussions include Philip Kitcher and the epistemology of disagreement. The issue also drives religious pluralism, which draws an opposite conclusion.","misread":"The argument does not just count religions; it claims none has a neutral advantage, so equal confidence in each is unwarranted.","variants":["Contingency argument: religious belief tracks birthplace, not truth.","Disagreement argument: equally qualified peers disagree, so suspend or reject."],"reading":[{"title":"Religious Diversity (Pluralism) (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-pluralism/"},{"title":"Disagreement, Religious (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/rel-disa/"}]},"k9b|incoherent":{"summary":"The concept of God is incoherent: some of the attributes God is said to have contradict each other, so no such being can exist.","idea":"This is a conceptual argument. God is said to be all-powerful, all-knowing, perfectly good, unchanging, timeless and free. But, say critics, these cannot all fit together: a being that never changes cannot know that it is now Tuesday; a perfectly good being cannot do evil, so is not all-powerful. If a concept contradicts itself, nothing answers to it, as there are no square circles. It targets classical theism specifically. The appeal is that it settles the question by logic rather than evidence.","example":"The knowledge of fear: Michael Martin argued that an all-knowing God would know everything, including what fear and despair feel like from the inside. But a perfect being who can never be in danger could never feel fear. So either God lacks some knowledge, or God is not perfect.","history":"Medieval theologians discussed these puzzles in depth, as did Averroes. Norman Kretzmann’s “Omniscience and Immutability” (1966) argued an unchanging God cannot know what time it is. Anthony Kenny’s The God of the Philosophers (1979) argued omniscience is incoherent, and Michael Martin and Ricki Monnier edited The Impossibility of God (2003).","misread":"The argument usually shows only that one conception of God is incoherent; theists can revise the attributes, as open theists do.","variants":["Single-attribute arguments: omnipotence or omniscience is itself contradictory.","Multiple-attribute arguments: two attributes clash (immutability and omniscience)."],"reading":[{"title":"Immutability (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/immutability/"},{"title":"Omniscience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/omniscience/"},{"title":"Divine Simplicity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/divine-simplicity/"}]},"k9b|nocreator":{"summary":"An unchanging, eternal creator could not cause a world in which things arise one after another, so there is no creator God.","idea":"This is the classic Buddhist argument against Īśvara, a creator Lord. Effects arise when their causes are complete. If God is eternal and unchanging, God’s causal power is always complete, so everything should arise at once and forever, not in sequence. If instead God waits on further conditions, then those conditions explain the timing, and God adds nothing. Either way an eternal creator explains nothing. It denies the creator, not every kind of sacred reality. The appeal is that it uses only ideas of causation everyone accepts. Compare a switch that is always on: it cannot explain why a light flickers on and off.","example":"The seed and the sprout: a seed grows only when soil, water and warmth come together. Buddhist logicians argued that everything arises this way, from changing conditions, and that adding an unchanging creator to the list adds nothing, since it is equally present whether the sprout appears or not.","history":"Vasubandhu’s Abhidharmakośabhāṣya (4th to 5th century) argued that if God were the sole cause, all things would arise together. Dharmakīrti’s Pramāṇavārttika (7th century) refined the argument against the Nyāya proof of God. Śāntarakṣita continued it. Nyāya thinkers such as Udayana (10th to 11th century) replied in defence of God.","misread":"Buddhists using this argument still accept rebirth, karma and nirvana; they reject a creator, not the sacred.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Vasubandhu (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/vasubandhu/"},{"title":"Dharmakīrti (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dharmakiirti/"}]},"k9m|balanced":{"summary":"There are strong arguments on both sides of the God question, and they roughly cancel each other out.","idea":"Evidential agnosticism comes from weighing the case. The agnostic grants real force to arguments for God, such as fine-tuning or the existence of anything at all, and real force to arguments against, such as pointless suffering and hiddenness. When honestly weighed, neither side wins. It denies both that the case for God is overwhelming and that atheism is obviously right. The appeal is intellectual fairness: it takes the best thinkers on both sides seriously. Compare a sports referee reviewing footage from two angles that show opposite things: the honest call is that it cannot be called.","example":"Draper’s balance: the philosopher Paul Draper has argued that once you put fine-tuning and consciousness on one side and suffering and natural evolution on the other, the scales come out roughly even, so suspending judgment is the rational result.","history":"Thomas Huxley’s agnosticism (1869 onward) was partly evidential. Anthony Kenny defended agnosticism in The Unknown God (2004) and What I Believe (2006). Paul Draper has written the recent standard account and defends evidential agnosticism. Robin Le Poidevin’s Agnosticism (2010) also discusses it.","misread":"Balanced agnosticism does not mean thinking God is fifty percent likely in some precise sense; it means neither belief nor disbelief is warranted.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"},{"title":"Atheism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/atheism/"}]},"k9m|beyond":{"summary":"Whether God exists is beyond what human minds and evidence can ever reach, so no one can know.","idea":"Strong agnosticism is not just a report on current evidence. It claims the question is in principle beyond human knowledge: our concepts are drawn from experience of the world, and God, if real, lies outside it. So neither believers nor atheists can know. It denies that any future discovery will settle the matter. The appeal is humility about the limits of the mind. Compare asking a fish to determine what lies above the water: its senses and concepts are built for a different world, and some questions may be like that for us.","example":"Kant’s limits of reason: in the Critique of Pure Reason (1781), Kant argued that reason trying to prove or disprove God goes beyond possible experience and falls into contradiction. He still believed in God on practical grounds, but held that theoretical knowledge cannot reach that far.","history":"Protagoras (5th century BCE) said the obscurity of the subject and the brevity of life prevent knowledge of the gods. The Rig Veda’s Creation Hymn (10.129) ends in uncertainty, and the Buddha set aside certain metaphysical questions as unanswerable. Kant (1781) and Huxley (from 1869) developed the idea in modern form.","misread":"Strong agnosticism is a positive claim, that the question is unknowable, and so needs its own defence.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"},{"title":"Atheism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/atheism/"}]},"k9m|weak":{"summary":"The arguments both for and against God that one has met are all poor, so the right response is to withhold judgment.","idea":"This is agnosticism from the weakness of the case rather than its strength. The agnostic finds the proofs for God unconvincing and the disproofs equally unconvincing. With no good reason either way, they withhold belief, as one would on any unsettled question. It denies that either side has done its job. The appeal is a straightforward epistemic rule: believe only on good grounds. Compare being asked whether the number of grains of sand on a particular beach is even or odd: you have no reason for either answer, so you don’t pick one.","example":"Huxley’s principle: Thomas Huxley described agnosticism as a method, not a creed: follow reason as far as it goes and do not claim conclusions are certain when they are not demonstrated. Applied to God, the weak case on both sides calls for withholding belief.","history":"Ancient Pyrrhonian sceptics such as Sextus Empiricus (2nd to 3rd century CE) practised suspending judgment when arguments balance or fail. Thomas Huxley gave agnosticism its name in 1869 and described it as a method rather than a creed. Anthony Kenny and Robin Le Poidevin (Agnosticism, 2010) discuss versions today, and the Buddha’s refusal to answer some metaphysical questions is a distant parallel.","misread":"Withholding judgment is compatible with continuing to inquire; it describes where the evidence leaves you now.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"}]},"k9e|bet":{"summary":"When reason cannot settle whether God exists, it is rational to bet on God, since the possible gain is infinite and the cost small.","idea":"Pascal’s wager is an argument about choice, not about evidence. You must live either as if God exists or as if not. Set out as a table of four outcomes, an infinite reward multiplied by any chance above zero swamps every finite cost, so the expected value of believing beats not believing. The appeal is practical: it shows how belief can be wise even without proof. Compare buying insurance against a rare disaster.","example":"Act as if you believe: Pascal admitted that you cannot simply choose to believe. His advice was to live as believers do, attending services and practising, so belief may follow, much as we come to feel what we rehearse.","history":"Blaise Pascal (1623 to 1662) set out the wager in a fragment of the Pensées, published after his death in 1670. Diderot raised the “many gods” objection in 1746. Recent defenders include Jeff Jordan, and Alan Hájek has studied its decision theory.","misread":"The wager is not an argument that God exists; it is an argument that believing is the prudent choice given uncertainty.","variants":["Infinite-reward wager: Pascal’s original.","Finite wagers: versions without infinite payoffs (Jordan)."],"reading":[{"title":"Pascal’s Wager (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pascal-wager/"},{"title":"Pascal’s Wager about God (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/pasc-wag/"}]},"k9e|will":{"summary":"When evidence cannot settle a question that matters for how you live, it is reasonable to let your hopes and needs decide.","idea":"This is William James’s “will to believe.” Some choices are live (both options are real for you), forced (you cannot stay neutral) and momentous (much depends on them). For such choices, when evidence is not decisive, refusing to believe is itself a bet, and you may let your passions choose. Unlike Pascal’s wager, this is not calculation of payoffs but permission to commit. The appeal is that it respects both evidence and the needs of a lived life. Compare deciding to trust a new friend before you have proof: trust is often what lets the friendship prove itself.","example":"The mountain climber: James imagined a climber who must leap a gap. If he believes he can, his confidence may give him the strength; if he doubts, he may fail. Some truths, James argued, depend on our believing them first.","history":"William James delivered “The Will to Believe” in 1896, replying to W. K. Clifford’s “The Ethics of Belief” (1877). James developed related ideas in The Varieties of Religious Experience (1902) and Pragmatism (1907). Pascal’s wager is the main predecessor. Richard Gale and Jeff Jordan have analysed it since.","misread":"James did not say you may believe anything you like; the option must be live, forced and momentous, and the evidence undecisive.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"William James (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/james/"},{"title":"The Ethics of Belief (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-belief/"}]},"k9e|nobet":{"summary":"Betting on God is a mistake: belief cannot be chosen for a payoff, and there are too many possible gods to know which to back.","idea":"This position rejects the wager. First, belief does not respond to rewards: you cannot believe your hair is purple because someone pays you. Second, the wager assumes only two options, but there are countless possible gods, some of whom might punish the wrong kind of believer. Third, belief adopted as insurance may not be the faith a God would value. The appeal is that it takes both belief and religion seriously. Compare being offered a million dollars to believe that two plus two is five: the offer changes nothing about what you can sincerely believe.","example":"The imam’s wager: Denis Diderot pointed out that an imam could reason just like Pascal and conclude you should become a Muslim. If the wager works for every faith, it cannot tell you which one to adopt.","history":"Denis Diderot’s Philosophical Thoughts (1746) gave the many-gods objection. Voltaire criticised the wager too. Bernard Williams’s “Deciding to Believe” (1970) argued that belief cannot be willed. Michael Martin proposed an “atheist’s wager” (1990): a good God might reward honest unbelief. Alan Hájek has examined the wager’s mathematics.","misread":"Critics need not deny that belief is important; they reject treating it as a gamble.","variants":["Many-gods objection: rival gods make the bet unclear.","Doxastic involuntarism: belief cannot be chosen at will."],"reading":[{"title":"Pascal’s Wager (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pascal-wager/"},{"title":"Pascal’s Wager about God (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/pasc-wag/"}]},"k9k|logical":{"summary":"Suffering is logically incompatible with an all-good, all-powerful God, so such a God cannot exist.","idea":"The logical problem of evil says there is a contradiction, not just tension. An all-good being would eliminate evil as far as it could, and an all-powerful being has no limits on what it can do. So if such a God existed, there would be no evil. There is evil, so there is no such God. It denies that any reason could justify God in allowing suffering. The appeal is decisiveness: if successful, it does not depend on probabilities. Compare a claim that a room is both completely empty and contains a chair.","example":"Epicurus’s riddle: as later reported by Lactantius, Epicurus asked whether God is willing but unable to stop evil, or able but unwilling, or neither, or both. If both, where does evil come from? The riddle remains the shortest statement of the problem.","history":"The argument is credited to Epicurus and was restated by Hume’s Philo in the Dialogues (1779). J. L. Mackie’s “Evil and Omnipotence” (1955) is the classic modern version. Alvin Plantinga’s free will defence (God, Freedom, and Evil, 1974) persuaded many philosophers that the strict version fails.","misread":"It is not the claim that evil makes God unlikely; it is the stronger claim that the two cannot coexist at all.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Logical Problem of Evil (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/evil-log/"},{"title":"The Problem of Evil (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/evil/"}]},"k9k|evidential":{"summary":"Suffering does not strictly rule God out, but its amount and pointlessness make God unlikely.","idea":"The evidential problem admits that God might have reasons for allowing some evil. But it argues that the world contains so much suffering, apparently serving no purpose, that the existence of an all-good, all-powerful God becomes improbable. It denies that we must find a strict contradiction before evil counts. The appeal is realism: this is how we reason about everything else, from evidence to likely conclusions. Compare a company that claims to care for its workers but keeps sending them into danger with no explanation: no proof of bad faith, but strong evidence.","example":"Sue’s case: William Rowe added a human example to the fawn, a five-year-old girl who was assaulted and killed. He argued that no good we know of justifies such evils, and that this makes it likely no good justifies them.","history":"William Rowe’s 1979 article founded the modern debate. Paul Draper’s “Pain and Pleasure” (1989) argued that the pattern of suffering fits an indifferent universe better than theism. Hume’s Dialogues (1779) anticipated both. Defenders of theism reply with skeptical theism and with theodicies.","misread":"The evidential argument can grant that God is possible; its conclusion is about likelihood.","variants":["Rowe’s argument: gratuitous evil probably exists.","Draper’s argument: suffering is better explained by indifference."],"reading":[{"title":"The Evidential Problem of Evil (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/evil-evi/"},{"title":"The Problem of Evil (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/evil/"}]},"k9k|outweigh":{"summary":"Suffering is genuine evidence against God, but the reasons for God are stronger and win overall.","idea":"This position concedes the force of the problem of evil. Suffering really does make God less likely than it would otherwise be. But other evidence, such as the existence of the universe, its fine-tuning, consciousness, morality or religious experience, counts for God more heavily. So, all things considered, belief is still reasonable. It denies both that evil is irrelevant and that it is decisive. The appeal is honesty: it treats all the evidence, not just the convenient part. Compare a doctor who notes one worrying symptom but judges, on all the tests together, that the patient is healthy.","example":"Rousseau’s reply to Voltaire: after the Lisbon earthquake, Rousseau wrote to Voltaire (1756) granting that the disaster was a real evil, partly worsened by crowding people into tall city houses, yet holding that his wider reasons for believing in a good providence still outweighed it.","history":"Richard Swinburne’s The Existence of God (1979) and Providence and the Problem of Evil (1998) take this cumulative approach. Peter van Inwagen and others discuss evil as evidence. Daniel Howard-Snyder’s edited volume The Evidential Argument from Evil (1996) gathered the debate.","misread":"","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"The Problem of Evil (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/evil/"},{"title":"The Evidential Problem of Evil (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/evil-evi/"}]},"k9k|noforce":{"summary":"Once the answers are understood, suffering does not count against God: there are good reasons why a good God would allow it.","idea":"This view holds that the problem of evil has been answered. Free will explains moral evil: a world of free creatures is better than a world of puppets, and freedom means the possibility of wrongdoing. Other theodicies explain natural evil as the cost of a stable, law-governed world, or as part of growth toward goodness. It denies that suffering is evidence against God at all once these goods are seen. The appeal is that it takes evil seriously and gives reasons rather than avoiding it. Compare a parent who lets a child ride a bike, knowing a scrape is possible.","example":"The free will defence: Plantinga argued that God might not be able to create free beings who never go wrong, because whether they go wrong is up to them. If so, there is no contradiction in a good God permitting evil.","history":"Augustine (354 to 430) explained evil as misused freedom and as a lack of good. Irenaeus (2nd century) saw the world as a place of growth. Leibniz’s Theodicy (1710) argued this is the best possible world. Plantinga’s God, Freedom, and Evil (1974) and John Hick’s Evil and the God of Love (1966) are the modern classics.","misread":"A defence and a theodicy differ: a defence shows God possibly has reasons, a theodicy claims to give God’s actual reasons.","variants":["Free will defence: freedom requires the possibility of evil (Plantinga).","Best possible world: this world is optimal overall (Leibniz)."],"reading":[{"title":"Theodicies (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/theodicies/"},{"title":"Logical Problem of Evil (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/evil-log/"}]},"k9k|limited":{"summary":"Suffering counts against an all-powerful, all-good God, but not against a God of limited power or a non-personal sacred reality.","idea":"This view accepts the problem of evil as decisive against the classical God, but not against every form of the divine. A God who is good but not all-powerful may be doing everything possible against evil, as process theology holds. A non-personal ultimate, such as the Dao or Brahman, does not choose, so cannot be blamed. The appeal is that it keeps a sacred reality while taking suffering at full weight. Compare a doctor who cannot cure every disease: we do not doubt their goodness because some patients die.","example":"Mill’s limited God: John Stuart Mill argued that the order of nature shows some intelligence at work, but its cruelty shows the designer’s power is limited. Better a good God who struggles against evil than an omnipotent one who permits it.","history":"John Stuart Mill’s Three Essays on Religion (1874) defended a limited God. Edgar Brightman developed a “finite God” in the 1930s. Process theists Whitehead, Hartshorne and David Ray Griffin (God, Power, and Evil, 1976) argued God persuades rather than coerces. Harold Kushner’s When Bad Things Happen to Good People (1981) brought it to many readers.","misread":"Limiting God’s power is not the same as atheism; it is a revision of the concept of God.","variants":["Process theodicy: God lures but cannot force (Griffin).","Finite theism: God is good but limited (Mill, Brightman)."],"reading":[{"title":"Process Theism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/process-theism/"},{"title":"The Problem of Evil (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/evil/"}]},"k9c|freewill":{"summary":"God allows evil because a world with genuinely free creatures is better than one without, even though they sometimes choose evil.","idea":"The free will response says moral freedom is a great good: it makes love, responsibility and real goodness possible. But freedom to do good is also freedom to do evil. Even God cannot make people freely choose only good, since that would not be freedom. So evil is the price of a world with real agents. It denies that God causes the evil people do. The appeal is that it fits the moral importance we place on choice. Compare a teacher who could make students give right answers by hypnosis, but whose students would then learn nothing.","example":"Plantinga’s transworld depravity: Plantinga suggested that every possible free person God could create might go wrong at least once if free. If so, God could not create a world of free beings with no evil, so evil is no evidence against God.","history":"Augustine’s On Free Choice of the Will (c. 388 to 395) blamed evil on misused freedom. Medieval Jewish, Christian and Islamic Mu‘tazilite thinkers held similar views. Alvin Plantinga’s God, Freedom, and Evil (1974) is the classic modern statement. Richard Swinburne extended it to natural evil.","misread":"The defence covers evil people do; natural disasters need a further story, such as stable natural laws.","variants":["Free will defence: shows evil is consistent with God (Plantinga).","Free will theodicy: claims freedom is God’s actual reason (Swinburne)."],"reading":[{"title":"Theodicies (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/theodicies/"},{"title":"Logical Problem of Evil (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/evil-log/"}]},"k9c|soulmaking":{"summary":"Suffering makes possible great goods worth its cost, such as courage, compassion and moral growth.","idea":"The greater-good response says some goods need suffering to exist. There can be no courage without danger, no compassion without pain, no forgiveness without wrongs. A world without any hardship would produce comfortable but shallow beings. On Hick’s soul-making version, the world is a place where souls grow toward goodness, not a paradise for finished creatures. It denies that a good God’s aim is simply to maximise comfort. The appeal is that it matches how people often grow through hardship. Compare an athlete who becomes strong only through painful training.","example":"The vale of soul-making: borrowing a phrase from John Keats, Hick argued the world is designed for character building. Like a gym rather than a hotel, it is built to make people, not to please them.","history":"Irenaeus of Lyon (2nd century) saw humanity as created immature and growing toward God. Leibniz’s Theodicy (1710) argued goods outweigh evils in the best world. John Hick’s Evil and the God of Love (1966) developed the soul-making theodicy. Richard Swinburne’s Providence and the Problem of Evil (1998) also argues from greater goods.","misread":"The view does not say each individual suffering improves its victim; it says the world as a whole makes growth possible.","variants":["Soul-making theodicy: the world builds character (Hick).","Best possible world: all evils serve the whole (Leibniz)."],"reading":[{"title":"Theodicies (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/theodicies/"},{"title":"The Problem of Evil (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/evil/"}]},"k9c|skeptical":{"summary":"God may have reasons for allowing suffering that humans are simply not in a position to see.","idea":"Skeptical theism says we should not expect to understand God’s reasons. Our grasp of what goods exist, how they connect and what they require is tiny compared with an infinite mind. So the fact that we cannot see a reason for some suffering is no evidence there is none. It denies the key step of the evidential argument, the move from “no reason we can see” to “no reason at all.” The appeal is humility. Compare a beginner watching a chess grandmaster sacrifice her queen: the move looks pointless, and only twenty moves later does its purpose appear.","example":"Wykstra’s CORNEA: if you look around a garage and see no dog, that is evidence there is no dog. If you see no flea, that is not evidence, since fleas are too small. Stephen Wykstra argued God’s reasons are more like fleas than dogs.","history":"The Book of Job, where God answers Job only by pointing to the vastness of creation, is an ancient source. Stephen Wykstra’s 1984 reply to Rowe introduced the CORNEA principle. Michael Bergmann, Peter van Inwagen and Daniel Howard-Snyder developed skeptical theism further.","misread":"It does not claim God has no reasons; it claims we cannot infer the absence of reasons from failing to see them.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Skeptical Theism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skeptical-theism/"},{"title":"The Evidential Problem of Evil (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/evil-evi/"}]},"k9c|karma":{"summary":"Suffering is the result of each being’s own past actions, in this or earlier lives, so it is not unjust.","idea":"The karma response says no suffering is undeserved. Every action has consequences that return to the one who acted, if not in this life then in a later one. A child born into hardship is reaping the fruit of past deeds. So the world is morally fair, even if it looks unfair from a single lifetime. It denies that suffering is random or that God is to blame. The appeal is a complete moral order. Compare a field where each crop grows from the seeds sown there: the farmer reaps what was planted, not what someone else planted.","example":"The rain and the seeds: Shankara, commenting on the Brahma Sutras (2.1.34), compared God to rain. Rain falls equally, but each seed grows according to its own nature. So God gives the fruits of karma without partiality; the differences come from beings’ own deeds.","history":"Karma is central in the Upanishads, Buddhism and Jainism. Buddhists and Jains need no God to administer it; Nyāya and Vedānta theists said God oversees it. Shankara gave the rain analogy. Max Weber called the doctrine an especially consistent theodicy. Arthur Herman’s The Problem of Evil and Indian Thought (1976) examined it critically.","misread":"Karma does not mean you should blame sufferers or refuse help; Buddhist, Jain and Hindu ethics all stress compassion.","variants":["Theistic karma: God dispenses the fruits (Shankara, Nyāya).","Non-theistic karma: an impersonal law (Buddhism, Jainism)."],"reading":[{"title":"Ethics in Indian Buddhism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-indian-buddhism/"},{"title":"Jaina Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/jaina-philosophy/"},{"title":"Śaṅkara (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/shankara/"}]},"k9c|redeem":{"summary":"God will heal or outweigh every suffering, in this life or the next, so no one’s suffering is finally wasted.","idea":"This response focuses not on why God allows evil but on what God will do about it. Even the worst horrors, which seem to ruin a person’s life, will be overcome: God will bring each sufferer into a good so great that they will see their life as worth living, even with the horror. It denies that suffering has the last word. The appeal is that it takes the victims’ point of view seriously. Compare a long illness followed by recovery so complete and joyful that the person later sees the illness as part of a good life story.","example":"Horrendous evils: Marilyn McCord Adams pointed to cases like a parent who accidentally runs over their own child. No general good justifies this. Only a God who makes each victim’s life a great good to them, she argued, can answer such evils.","history":"Julian of Norwich’s Revelations of Divine Love (late 14th century) hoped that all would be well. Ivan Karamazov’s refusal in Dostoevsky’s The Brothers Karamazov (1880) is the classic protest it answers. Marilyn McCord Adams’s Horrendous Evils and the Goodness of God (1999) is the main modern statement. Eleonore Stump’s Wandering in Darkness (2010) is related.","misread":"The view does not say suffering is good; it says God can defeat it, making the whole life good despite it.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"The Problem of Evil (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/evil/"},{"title":"Heaven and Hell in Christian Thought (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/heaven-hell/"}]},"k9c|trust":{"summary":"One has no answer to why God allows suffering, but continues to trust God anyway, as Job did.","idea":"This response gives no theory. It admits the problem of evil is real and unsolved, yet holds that faith does not depend on solving it. Trust in God rests on relationship, revelation or experience, and that trust can survive not understanding. It denies that faith must explain everything to be reasonable. The appeal is honesty about the limits of explanation. Compare trusting a close friend who does something you cannot understand: because of all you know of them, you keep trusting while you wait.","example":"Job’s story: in the Hebrew Bible, Job loses his children, wealth and health. His friends offer explanations; Job rejects them and demands an answer from God. God speaks from a whirlwind about creation, never explaining, and Job is satisfied. The friends, not Job, are rebuked.","history":"The Book of Job (dated variously from the 6th to 4th century BCE) is the source. Jewish responses after the Holocaust, such as those of Elie Wiesel, include protest within faith. Kierkegaard’s writings on Job, and Eleonore Stump’s Wandering in Darkness (2010), are philosophical reflections. Many Islamic traditions emphasise patient trust (sabr).","misread":"This is not a refusal to think; many who hold it have thought hard and concluded that no theory fully works.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"The Problem of Evil (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/evil/"},{"title":"Faith (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/faith/"}]},"k9c|notmoral":{"summary":"God is not a moral agent with duties like ours, so asking why God did not stop suffering misunderstands what God is.","idea":"This response denies a hidden assumption of the problem of evil: that God is like a very powerful, very good person who ought to prevent harm. On this view God is the source of all being, not one agent among others, and has no obligations to creatures. Whatever God does is just because God owns and creates all things. It denies that human moral standards apply to God in the same way. The appeal is that it takes God’s transcendence seriously. Compare asking whether an author wronged a character by writing a sad chapter: the author’s relation to the story is not that of a fellow character.","example":"The three brothers: al-Ash‘ari is said to have asked about three brothers, one who died a believing adult, one a sinner, and one who died a child. Why did God take the child before he could earn more reward? The story challenges any claim that God must act by human standards.","history":"The Ash‘arite school of Islamic theology, founded by al-Ash‘ari (874 to 936) and developed by al-Ghazali, held that God’s acts define justice. Aquinas held that God is not a member of a moral community. Brian Davies’s The Reality of God and the Problem of Evil (2006) defends this Thomist view.","misread":"It does not deny God’s goodness; it says God is good in a way different from a dutiful person.","variants":["Ash‘arite voluntarism: God’s will defines justice.","Thomist view: God is good but not a moral agent (Davies)."],"reading":[{"title":"Arabic and Islamic Philosophy of Religion (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/arabic-islamic-religion/"},{"title":"Theological Voluntarism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/voluntarism-theological/"},{"title":"al-Ghazali (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/al-ghazali/"}]},"k9c|none":{"summary":"None of the proposed responses solves the problem of suffering for an all-good, all-powerful God.","idea":"This is the verdict that the problem of evil stands unanswered. Free will does not explain natural disasters; growth-based answers struggle with misery that destroys a person instead of strengthening them; skeptical theism threatens our ability to make any moral judgments. Each answer covers some cases but not others. It denies that the theist has an adequate reply, though it does not by itself say what follows: some conclude atheism, others a limited God, others faith without answers. The appeal is refusing to accept explanations that seem too easy. Compare a set of keys, none of which opens the lock.","example":"Ivan Karamazov returns his ticket: in Dostoevsky’s novel, Ivan describes children tortured by adults. Even if all harmony followed, he says, he would refuse a ticket to a world bought at that price. No theodicy, he insists, could make it right.","history":"Hume’s Philo argued that no account fits the world we see. J. L. Mackie (1955, 1982) and William Rowe (1979) rejected the main theodicies. D. Z. Phillips, a believer, argued in The Problem of Evil and the Problem of God (2004) that theodicies themselves are morally troubling. Some Jewish post-Holocaust thinkers reject theodicy too.","misread":"Holding that no theodicy works does not settle whether God exists; believers and non-believers both hold it.","variants":["Anti-theodicy: theodicies are morally objectionable (Phillips)."],"reading":[{"title":"The Problem of Evil (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/evil/"},{"title":"Humean Arguments from Evil against Theism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/humeevil/"}]},"k9l|perceive":{"summary":"Religious experiences are evidence, like ordinary perception: trust them unless there is a good reason not to.","idea":"The principle of credulity says appearances are innocent until proven guilty: how things seem to you is a reason to believe they are that way, unless something specific tells against it. That is how we treat all perception. Applied consistently, it means experiences of God count as evidence, just as seeing a tree does. It denies that religious experiences should be singled out for suspicion. The appeal is consistency. Compare seeing a friend across the street: you believe it is them unless you have reason to think you are mistaken, such as knowing they are abroad.","example":"Alston’s doxastic practices: William Alston argued that we cannot prove ordinary sense perception is reliable without relying on perception itself. Christian mystical practice is in the same boat, so it is equally reasonable to trust it.","history":"Richard Swinburne’s The Existence of God (1979) introduced the principle of credulity and of testimony. William Alston’s Perceiving God (1991) developed the perceptual model. Caroline Franks Davis and Jerome Gellman have defended related views. Al-Ghazali’s account of mystical “tasting” is an earlier parallel.","misread":"Credulity does not mean believing every experience; strong defeaters, like drugs or contradictions, override it.","variants":["Principle of credulity: seemings justify belief (Swinburne).","Doxastic practice approach: belief-forming practices are innocent until proven guilty (Alston)."],"reading":[{"title":"Religious Experience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-experience/"},{"title":"The Epistemology of Religion (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-epistemology/"}]},"k9l|personal":{"summary":"Religious experience can justify belief for the person who has it, but gives others no reason to believe.","idea":"This view grants that a vivid encounter with the sacred can make it reasonable for the experiencer to believe. They know what they underwent. But for others, a report of such an experience is just one more claim, competing with reports of opposite experiences and with natural explanations. So experience is not a public argument. It denies both that experiences are worthless and that they should convince everyone. The appeal is balance. Compare someone who sees a rare bird alone in the woods: they may be confident, but others reasonably want more.","example":"James’s verdict on mysticism: in The Varieties of Religious Experience, William James concluded that mystical states, when well developed, have the right to be authoritative for those who have them, but impose no duty on outsiders to accept their revelations uncritically.","history":"William James’s Varieties (1902) is the classic source. C. D. Broad discussed the evidential value of mystical experience. William Rowe’s critique of the principle of credulity (1982) and Richard Gale’s The Nature and Existence of God (1991) argued that experiences fail as public evidence.","misread":"","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Religious Experience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-experience/"},{"title":"William James (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/james/"}]},"k9l|natural":{"summary":"Religious experiences are fully explained by brain processes, psychology and culture, and point to nothing beyond us.","idea":"The naturalistic explanation holds that we already know enough about the brain and mind to account for religious experiences without anything supernatural. Meditation changes brain activity; expectations shape what people feel; cultures teach people how to interpret strong emotions. Since the experiences would happen anyway, they give no evidence for God. It denies that the experiences are perceptions of a sacred reality. The appeal is that it fits our wider scientific picture. Compare a dream of a lost relative: vivid and meaningful, but explained by memory and emotion.","example":"The sensed presence: neuroscientists have reported that stimulating certain brain regions, or sensory deprivation, can create a feeling that someone is nearby. Some see this as showing how religious presence could arise naturally; others note results have been hard to replicate.","history":"Ludwig Feuerbach and Sigmund Freud (The Future of an Illusion, 1927) gave psychological explanations. Michael Persinger’s experiments in the 1980s and 1990s and Andrew Newberg’s brain imaging of meditators studied the neuroscience. Wayne Proudfoot’s Religious Experience (1985) argued that experiences are shaped by prior beliefs.","misread":"Finding a brain process behind an experience does not by itself show nothing is perceived; seeing a tree also involves brain processes.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Religious Experience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-experience/"},{"title":"Evolutionary Approaches to Religion (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-evolutionary/"}]},"k9l|conflict":{"summary":"Religious experiences contradict each other across traditions, so they cannot support one religion over another.","idea":"Christians experience the Trinity, Hindus experience Krishna, Buddhists experience no-self, Muslims experience the one God without partners. If experiences were reliable perceptions, they would not tell such different stories. So they cannot be used to favour one tradition. It does not necessarily deny that something real is experienced, but denies that experience settles which religion is right. The appeal is that it takes all experiencers equally seriously. Compare witnesses to a crime who give flatly opposite descriptions: their testimony cannot convict one suspect.","example":"The blind men and the elephant: each touches a different part and describes the elephant as a rope, a wall or a pillar. Critics say religious experiences are like this, but note that we only know the elephant story because someone could see the whole animal.","history":"Hume (1748) argued that rival religions’ evidence cancels out. C. D. Broad and Michael Martin discussed conflicting experiences. Steven Katz’s “Language, Epistemology, and Mysticism” (1978) argued that experiences are shaped by traditions. John Hick drew a pluralist conclusion, while William Alston replied that conflicts weaken but do not destroy justification.","misread":"","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Religious Experience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-experience/"},{"title":"Religious Diversity (Pluralism) (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-pluralism/"}]},"k9l|feeling":{"summary":"Religious experiences are real and valuable as feelings, but they do not reveal anything that actually exists beyond them.","idea":"This view treats religious experiences as genuine and important, like experiences of beauty or love, but not as perceptions of an external object. A sense of awe, dependence or unity can transform a life without being a glimpse of God. It denies both that the experiences are evidence and that they are worthless. The appeal is that it honours the experiences while staying cautious about what they show. Compare being moved to tears by music: the experience is real and valuable, but it does not mean the music has a hidden mind.","example":"Feeling of absolute dependence: Schleiermacher held that religion rests on a feeling of utter dependence. Later readers took two paths: some saw this feeling as awareness of God, others as a valuable human emotion with no object beyond itself.","history":"Friedrich Schleiermacher (On Religion, 1799; The Christian Faith, 1821 to 1822) placed religion in feeling. William James’s Varieties (1902) gave experience central importance. Ludwig Feuerbach treated religious feeling as human self-awareness. Wayne Proudfoot (1985) and religious naturalists today value experience without supernatural claims.","misread":"Calling an experience a feeling is not dismissing it; feelings can shape values and lives profoundly.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Religious Experience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-experience/"},{"title":"Friedrich Schleiermacher (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/schleiermacher/"}]},"k9g|design":{"summary":"The physical constants are fine-tuned because an intelligent designer set them so that life could exist.","idea":"The design explanation says the values of physical constants are not an accident. If life-permitting values are extremely rare among possible ones, it is far more likely we would find them if a designer chose them. It denies that chance or necessity is a better explanation. The appeal is that this is how we reason in ordinary cases: improbable, purposeful arrangements suggest a mind. Compare finding a lottery won by the same person ten times in a row: you suspect someone arranged it.","example":"The firing squad: John Leslie imagined surviving a firing squad of fifty trained marksmen who all miss. You should not shrug and say “if they had hit, I wouldn’t be here to wonder.” You should suspect the miss was arranged.","history":"Fine-tuning was noticed by physicists such as Fred Hoyle, whose 1950s prediction about carbon later led him to speak of a “superintellect,” and Brandon Carter (1974). John Leslie’s Universes (1989), Richard Swinburne and Robin Collins developed the theistic argument. Martin Rees’s Just Six Numbers (1999) presented the physics.","misread":"Fine-tuning arguments do not need biology to be designed; they focus on the laws and constants of physics.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Fine-Tuning (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fine-tuning/"},{"title":"Teleological Arguments for God’s Existence (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/teleological-arguments/"},{"title":"Design Arguments for the Existence of God (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/design-arguments-for-existence-of-god/"}]},"k9g|multiverse":{"summary":"There are many universes with different constants, so it is unsurprising that we find ourselves in one where life is possible.","idea":"The multiverse explanation says our universe is one of an enormous number, each with different physical settings. Most are lifeless, but with enough universes some will permit life, and only in those can observers exist to wonder. So we should expect to find fine-tuning. It denies that a designer is needed. The appeal is that some physical theories, such as eternal inflation and string theory, seem to predict many universes. Compare a lottery with billions of tickets: someone winning is no surprise, even if each ticket was unlikely.","example":"The anthropic principle: Brandon Carter pointed out that we can only observe a universe compatible with our existence. Combined with many universes, this selection effect explains why our observations show fine-tuning, just as fish can only find themselves in water.","history":"Brandon Carter stated the anthropic principle in 1974. Inflationary cosmology from Alan Guth and Andrei Linde, and the string landscape described in Leonard Susskind’s The Cosmic Landscape (2005), suggested many universes. Martin Rees defends the view; Ian Hacking (1987) and Roger White (2000) raised the “inverse gambler’s fallacy” objection.","misread":"The multiverse is not just made up to avoid God; it is suggested by some physical theories, though it remains unconfirmed.","variants":["Inflationary multiverse: bubble universes from eternal inflation.","String landscape: many vacuum states of string theory."],"reading":[{"title":"Fine-Tuning (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fine-tuning/"}]},"k9g|necessary":{"summary":"A deeper theory of physics will show that the constants could not have been otherwise.","idea":"This explanation says fine-tuning is only apparent. The constants seem as if they could have had other values because we do not yet have the deepest theory. When we do, it will show they had to be exactly what they are, as the value of pi must be what it is. It denies that the values were ever chosen or left to chance. The appeal is that physics has often shown apparently arbitrary facts to follow from deeper principles. Compare the orbits of planets, once thought arbitrary, later explained by Newton’s law of gravity.","example":"Einstein’s question: Einstein is reported to have said that what really interested him was whether God had any choice in creating the world. A theory with no free choices would explain the constants without either design or chance.","history":"Albert Einstein hoped for a unified theory with no free parameters. Steven Weinberg’s Dreams of a Final Theory (1992) discussed this hope. Some string theorists sought a unique solution, though the theory now seems to allow many. Philosophers note that even necessary laws would leave the question of why those laws.","misread":"Necessity does not remove all mystery; it moves the question to why the deeper theory is the one that holds.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Fine-Tuning (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fine-tuning/"},{"title":"Principle of Sufficient Reason (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sufficient-reason/"}]},"k9g|brute":{"summary":"The values of the constants are simply a brute fact: not everything has an explanation.","idea":"The brute-fact view says that at some point explanation stops. The constants are what they are, and there may be no deeper reason. It denies the principle that everything must have an explanation. The appeal is honesty: every view must have some starting point, and explaining the constants by God or a multiverse just moves the question. Compare asking why your birthday is the date it is: there may be causes, but no deep reason why it had to be that date and not another.","example":"Hume’s twenty particles: in the Dialogues, Hume’s character Cleanthes says that if you explain the cause of each of twenty particles of matter, it would be unreasonable to then ask for the cause of the twenty as a whole. Explain each link, and nothing further about the whole is owed.","history":"David Hume questioned whether every fact needs a cause (Dialogues, 1779). Bertrand Russell took this line against Frederick Copleston in their 1948 BBC radio debate. Contemporary philosophers like Graham Oppy argue that theism and naturalism both need brute facts. Philosophers debating the principle of sufficient reason continue the discussion.","misread":"Accepting brute facts is not giving up on science; it is accepting that science ends somewhere.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Principle of Sufficient Reason (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sufficient-reason/"},{"title":"Fine-Tuning (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fine-tuning/"}]},"k9g|nofine":{"summary":"The universe is not really fine-tuned: we do not know how probable other constants were, or whether life needs exactly these.","idea":"This view challenges the premise. To say our constants are improbable, we need a way to assign probabilities to possible values, but there is no clear way to do that over infinite ranges. And claims that life needs these exact values assume life must be like ours, based on carbon and chemistry. Other settings might permit other kinds of complexity. It denies there is anything to explain. The appeal is caution about large claims from limited knowledge. Compare saying it is incredibly lucky you were born exactly as you are: lucky compared to what?","example":"The normalizability problem: Timothy McGrew, Lydia McGrew and Eric Vestrup argued that if possible constants range over infinite values with equal chance, the probabilities cannot add up to one, so no meaningful probability of fine-tuning can be calculated.","history":"Timothy McGrew, Lydia McGrew and Eric Vestrup’s 2001 paper “Probabilities and the Fine-Tuning Argument” raised the normalizability problem. Victor Stenger’s The Fallacy of Fine-Tuning (2011) argued that life is possible across many settings. Elliott Sober has questioned the probabilistic reasoning involved.","misread":"","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Fine-Tuning (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fine-tuning/"}]},"k9h|cosmo":{"summary":"The strongest argument for God is that something must explain why anything exists at all: a first cause or necessary being.","idea":"The cosmological argument starts from the bare fact that something exists. Everything we see depends on something else, but the chain cannot explain itself. So, the argument goes, there must be a first cause or a being that exists necessarily, which explains everything else. It denies that the universe can be its own explanation. The appeal is the simplicity of its starting point: no one doubts something exists. Compare a row of falling dominoes: however long the row, something must have started it.","example":"The kalām argument: whatever begins to exist has a cause; the universe began to exist; so it has a cause. Medieval Islamic theologians developed this, and William Lane Craig revived it, pointing to the Big Bang as evidence the universe began.","history":"Aristotle argued for an unmoved mover. Al-Kindi and al-Ghazali gave the kalām version; Ibn Sina argued from necessary existence. Aquinas’s Five Ways (13th century) and Leibniz’s principle of sufficient reason (Monadology, 1714) extended it. The Nyāya philosopher Udayana gave an Indian version. William Lane Craig and Alexander Pruss defend it today.","misread":"It does not claim God caused everything at a moment in time; Aquinas’s version works even for an eternal universe.","variants":["Kalām argument: from the beginning of the universe.","Contingency argument: from the existence of dependent things (Leibniz).","Thomist argument: from ongoing causal dependence (Aquinas)."],"reading":[{"title":"Cosmological Argument (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/cosmological-argument/"},{"title":"Principle of Sufficient Reason (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sufficient-reason/"}]},"k9h|design":{"summary":"The strongest argument is that nature’s order, from living things to physical laws, points to a designer.","idea":"The design argument starts from order. Living things, the laws of physics and the fine-tuning of constants seem arranged to achieve results, as human artefacts are. Since in human life order like that comes from minds, the best explanation of nature’s order is a mind. It denies that blind chance and natural processes are enough. The appeal is that it starts from something we can all see: the beauty and regularity of the world. Compare finding a message written in the sand: you assume a person wrote it.","example":"Cicero’s orrery: in On the Nature of the Gods, the Stoic speaker says that if someone carried Posidonius’s model of the heavens to Scythia or Britain, no one there would doubt it was made by reason. The real heavens, he argues, show far greater skill.","history":"Ancient Stoics and Cicero’s On the Nature of the Gods (45 BCE) used design arguments. Aquinas’s Fifth Way argued from goal-directed behaviour. William Paley’s Natural Theology (1802) is the classic. David Hume’s Dialogues (1779) and Charles Darwin’s theory of evolution challenged the biological version, leading modern defenders like Robin Collins to focus on physics.","misread":"Modern design arguments mostly focus on physics, not biology, accepting that evolution explains much biological design.","variants":["Biological design: from organisms (Paley).","Fine-tuning: from physical constants (Collins)."],"reading":[{"title":"Teleological Arguments for God’s Existence (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/teleological-arguments/"},{"title":"Design Arguments for the Existence of God (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/design-arguments-for-existence-of-god/"}]},"k9h|onto":{"summary":"The strongest argument is that God’s existence follows from the very idea of a greatest possible being.","idea":"The ontological argument tries to prove God from the concept alone. God is defined as a being than which nothing greater can be conceived. A being that existed in reality would be greater than one that existed only in thought. So if God existed only in thought, we could think of something greater, which contradicts the definition. Therefore God exists. It denies that existence can be left out of perfection. The appeal is that, if valid, it is pure reason. Compare knowing that a triangle has three sides just by understanding the idea.","example":"Gaunilo’s island: the monk Gaunilo imagined an island than which none greater can be conceived, and asked whether Anselm’s logic would prove it exists too. Anselm replied that the argument only works for a necessary being, not for things like islands.","history":"Anselm of Canterbury’s Proslogion (1077 to 1078) introduced it. Descartes gave a version in his Meditations (1641). Immanuel Kant’s Critique of Pure Reason (1781) argued that existence is not a property. Alvin Plantinga’s The Nature of Necessity (1974) and Kurt Gödel gave modal versions.","misread":"The argument is not saying that thinking makes things exist; it claims the concept of a necessary perfect being implies its reality.","variants":["Anselm’s version: from the greatest conceivable being.","Modal version: if God is possible, God is necessary (Plantinga)."],"reading":[{"title":"Ontological Arguments (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ontological-arguments/"},{"title":"Anselm: Ontological Argument (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/anselm-ontological-argument/"}]},"k9h|moral":{"summary":"The strongest argument is that objective right and wrong need a source beyond us, and God best provides it.","idea":"The moral argument says objective moral facts, things that are right or wrong whatever anyone thinks, are hard to explain in a purely physical universe. Where do obligations come from? Why should we care? God, as perfectly good and the source of everything, explains both the existence of moral facts and why we can know them. It denies that evolution or social agreement can make morality truly binding. The appeal is that it takes our deepest moral convictions as evidence. Compare the sense that torturing innocents for fun is wrong, whoever says otherwise.","example":"The Euthyphro dilemma: in Plato’s Euthyphro, Socrates asks whether the gods love what is good because it is good, or whether it is good because they love it. Defenders of the moral argument often say goodness is God’s own nature, not arbitrary will.","history":"Immanuel Kant (1788) made God a postulate of morality. John Henry Newman argued from conscience. C. S. Lewis’s Mere Christianity (1952) popularised it. Robert Adams’s Finite and Infinite Goods (1999), William Lane Craig and C. Stephen Evans defend it today. Plato’s Euthyphro raised the main objection.","misread":"It does not say atheists cannot be moral; it says morality itself needs a foundation God provides.","variants":["Moral ontology: God as ground of moral facts.","Moral epistemology: God explains moral knowledge."],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Arguments for the Existence of God (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-arguments-god/"},{"title":"Theological Voluntarism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/voluntarism-theological/"}]},"k9h|experience":{"summary":"The strongest argument is that countless people across cultures report experiencing God directly.","idea":"The argument from religious experience says that the widespread human experience of the divine is evidence that it exists. If millions of people across history and cultures report seeing a mountain, we believe it exists; why not the same for God? It denies that all these experiences are simply mistakes. The appeal is the sheer number and seriousness of the reports. Compare a new kind of bird reported by many independent observers: their combined testimony counts as evidence, even if you have not seen it yourself.","example":"Swinburne’s principle of testimony: Swinburne argued that we should usually believe what others tell us they experienced, absent reasons to doubt. If many people sincerely report experiences of God, we have evidence that God exists.","history":"Teresa of Ávila, al-Ghazali, Ramakrishna and many others reported such experiences. William James’s Varieties (1902) studied them. Richard Swinburne (1979) and William Alston (1991) developed the argument. Caroline Franks Davis’s The Evidential Force of Religious Experience (1989) is another key work.","misread":"","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Religious Experience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-experience/"}]},"k9h|pragmatic":{"summary":"The strongest case is practical: believing in God is the wiser choice, or makes for a better life, whether or not it can be proved.","idea":"Pragmatic arguments do not try to prove God exists. They argue that believing is wise or beneficial. Pascal argued that the possible reward of belief is so great that it is rational to bet on God. William James argued that when evidence is unclear and the choice matters, we may follow our hopes. Others point to the benefits of belief for meaning, community or moral strength. It denies that evidence alone should govern every belief. The appeal is practicality. Compare choosing optimism: it may not be proven correct, but it can make life better.","example":"Pascal’s wager: if God exists and you believe, you gain infinitely. If God does not exist and you believe, you lose little. So, Pascal argued, it is rational to bet on God even without proof.","history":"Blaise Pascal’s Pensées (1670) gave the wager. William James’s “The Will to Believe” (1896) argued for belief where evidence is undecisive. Kant’s moral argument (1788) has a practical side: God is postulated because morality needs it, not proved. Jeff Jordan’s Pascal’s Wager: Pragmatic Arguments and Belief in God (2006) defends such arguments today.","misread":"Pragmatic arguments aim at the wisdom of believing, not at proving God exists.","variants":["Pascal’s wager: a decision-theoretic bet.","Will to believe: permission to believe in forced, momentous choices (James)."],"reading":[{"title":"Pascal’s Wager (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pascal-wager/"},{"title":"William James (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/james/"}]},"k9h|none":{"summary":"No argument for God’s existence has real force.","idea":"This view holds that all the traditional arguments fail. The cosmological argument assumes everything needs a cause; the design argument is undermined by evolution and the multiverse; the ontological argument confuses concepts with reality; the moral argument assumes morality needs God; experience is explained naturally. It denies that the case for God gives any real support. The appeal is rigour: arguments must earn belief. Compare a set of proofs offered for a mathematical theorem, each with a gap: none establishes the result.","example":"Mackie’s survey: in The Miracle of Theism, J. L. Mackie went through every major argument for God and argued each fails, leaving theism unsupported. The title is ironic: belief persisting without good arguments is the “miracle.”","history":"David Hume’s Dialogues (1779) and Immanuel Kant’s Critique of Pure Reason (1781) criticised the classic arguments. J. L. Mackie’s The Miracle of Theism (1982) and Graham Oppy’s Arguing about Gods (2006) are modern surveys concluding none succeeds. Earlier, Buddhist and Cārvāka thinkers in India rejected the Nyāya proofs of a creator.","misread":"Holding that no argument works does not by itself mean God does not exist; some believers hold it too.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"},{"title":"Natural Theology (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/theo-nat/"}]},"k9i|never":{"summary":"It is never reasonable to believe anything important without sufficient evidence.","idea":"Evidentialism holds that a belief is justified only if it fits the evidence. If you lack sufficient evidence, you should suspend judgment. It denies that faith, hope or usefulness can justify belief. The appeal is intellectual integrity: beliefs affect actions, and actions affect others, so we have a duty to believe carefully. Compare a scientist who refuses to announce a result before the data supports it, however much she wants it to be true.","example":"Clifford’s shipowner: W. K. Clifford imagined a shipowner who sends an old, unsafe ship to sea, having talked himself into believing it is safe. It sinks. Clifford argued he is guilty even though his belief was sincere, because he had no right to believe on such evidence.","history":"W. K. Clifford’s “The Ethics of Belief” (1877) is the classic statement. John Locke (Essay Concerning Human Understanding, 1689) and David Hume (Enquiry, 1748) proportioned belief to evidence. Richard Feldman and Earl Conee defend evidentialism today. Bertrand Russell applied it to religion.","misread":"Evidentialism does not require certainty; it requires belief to match the strength of the evidence.","variants":["Clifford’s evidentialism: a moral duty to believe on evidence.","Epistemic evidentialism: justification is fit with evidence (Feldman, Conee)."],"reading":[{"title":"The Ethics of Belief (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-belief/"},{"title":"Faith and Reason (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/faith-re/"}]},"k9i|basic":{"summary":"Some beliefs, possibly including religious ones, are reasonable without argument, like trusting your memory or senses.","idea":"Proper basicality says that not every belief needs to be inferred from evidence. Some beliefs, such as that you had breakfast or that other people have minds, are justified directly by experience. Religious beliefs can be like this: someone might find themselves believing God is present when they feel guilt or gratitude, without reasoning to it. It denies that evidentialism is correct for all beliefs. The appeal is that it fits how we actually believe most things. Compare remembering where you put your keys: you do not prove it, you just remember.","example":"Plantinga’s basic beliefs: Plantinga argued that belief in God can be properly basic, like belief in other minds. Just as you cannot prove other people have minds, but are reasonable to believe they do, belief in God can be reasonable without proof.","history":"Thomas Reid’s Inquiry into the Human Mind (1764) defended trusting common-sense beliefs that cannot be proved. Alvin Plantinga (God and Other Minds, 1967; Warranted Christian Belief, 2000), Nicholas Wolterstorff and William Alston developed Reformed epistemology. John Calvin’s idea of a sense of the divine is a source.","misread":"It does not say any belief can be basic; it must arise from reliable faculties in the right conditions.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Reformed Epistemology (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ref-epis/"},{"title":"The Epistemology of Religion (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-epistemology/"}]},"k9i|will":{"summary":"When a question matters for how you live and evidence cannot settle it, you may let your hopes and passions decide.","idea":"The will to believe says that some choices are forced on us: we must either believe or not, and refusing to believe is itself a choice. When such a choice is live (both options are real possibilities), forced and momentous (much depends on it), and evidence cannot decide, we are allowed to believe what we hope is true. It denies that we must always suspend judgment. The appeal is that it respects both evidence and our needs. Compare deciding to trust someone you love before you can prove they deserve it.","example":"The forced choice: James argued that refusing to believe in God is not neutral; it is betting against God. If God exists and wants our trust, the evidentialist who waits forever may miss the very relationship that would provide evidence.","history":"William James’s “The Will to Believe” (1896), first given as a lecture to student clubs at Yale and Brown, replied directly to W. K. Clifford’s “The Ethics of Belief” (1877). James developed related ideas in Pragmatism (1907). Pascal’s wager is a predecessor. Richard Gale and Jeff Jordan have analysed it since.","misread":"James did not mean you may believe anything you want; the choice must be live, forced and momentous, with evidence undecisive.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"William James (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/james/"},{"title":"The Ethics of Belief (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-belief/"}]},"k9i|above":{"summary":"Faith goes beyond reason without contradicting it: reason can show faith is reasonable, but some truths of faith exceed what reason can prove.","idea":"This view holds that faith and reason are allies. Reason can establish some things about God, such as that God exists, and show that faith is not irrational. But some truths, such as the Trinity or the details of revelation, go beyond what reason could discover alone. These are not against reason, but above it. It denies both that faith contradicts reason and that reason can prove everything. The appeal is harmony. Compare trusting an expert’s claim you cannot check yourself: your trust is reasonable, but the claim itself goes beyond what you could prove.","example":"Aquinas’s preambles of faith: Aquinas held that reason can prove God exists and is one. These are the preambles of faith. But truths like the Trinity can only be known by revelation, though reason can show they are not contradictory.","history":"Thomas Aquinas’s Summa contra Gentiles (1259 to 1265) set out the view. Maimonides and Averroes (Decisive Treatise, c. 1179) held related views in Judaism and Islam. The First Vatican Council (1870) affirmed it for Catholicism. John Locke distinguished things above reason from things contrary to reason.","misread":"","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Faith and Reason (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/faith-re/"},{"title":"Faith (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/faith/"}]},"k9i|beyond":{"summary":"Faith does not answer to evidence or reason: it is a commitment that reason has no business judging.","idea":"Strong fideism holds that faith is its own authority. Reason is not the right tool for religious truths, and trying to support or criticise faith with arguments misunderstands it. It denies that evidence is relevant to faith. The appeal is that it protects faith from the ups and downs of argument and treats it as a personal commitment. Compare deep love: you do not reason your way to it, and arguments against it do not touch it.","example":"Kierkegaard’s leap: Kierkegaard held that faith requires a leap beyond reason. Christianity’s central claim, that God became human, is an absolute paradox to reason, and faith embraces it with passion, not proof.","history":"Tertullian (c. 160 to 220) contrasted faith and philosophy. Søren Kierkegaard (Fear and Trembling, 1843; Concluding Unscientific Postscript, 1846) is the classic fideist. Ludwig Wittgenstein’s followers, sometimes called Wittgensteinian fideists, held that religious language has its own rules. Kai Nielsen coined that label critically in 1967.","misread":"Strong fideists do not usually say faith is irrational; they say reason is the wrong judge.","variants":["Kierkegaardian fideism: faith as passionate leap.","Wittgensteinian fideism: religion as a self-contained form of life."],"reading":[{"title":"Fideism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fideism/"},{"title":"Søren Kierkegaard (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kierkegaard/"}]},"k9i|trust":{"summary":"Religious faith does not require believing its claims are true: one can trust, hope and live by a religion without such belief.","idea":"Non-doxastic faith separates faith from belief. You can have faith that something is so while not being sure enough to believe it: you can hope, trust and commit your life. It denies that faith must involve believing doctrines. The appeal is that it allows doubters to participate fully in religious life. Compare a person who commits to a marriage with hope and trust, without being certain it will last forever.","example":"Faith without belief: Louis Pojman argued that someone can hope God exists and live by that hope, without believing it. Daniel Howard-Snyder argues that faith requires a positive attitude toward a proposition but not belief.","history":"Louis Pojman’s “Faith without Belief?” (1986), Robert Audi, J. L. Schellenberg (Prolegomena to a Philosophy of Religion, 2005) and Daniel Howard-Snyder developed the view in recent decades. Buddhist saddhā, trust in the Buddha’s path before one has verified it in practice, is a parallel, and some liberal Jewish and Christian thinkers describe their faith in similar terms.","misread":"Non-doxastic faith is not faith without commitment; it often involves deep practical dedication.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Faith (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/faith/"}]},"k9j|exclusive":{"summary":"One religion is true; where others contradict it they are mistaken, and it alone is the way to salvation or liberation.","idea":"Exclusivism holds that one religion has the truth, and others are wrong where they contradict it. It also usually holds that this religion is the only path to salvation or liberation. It denies that all religions are equally true. The appeal is that it takes religious claims seriously as claims about reality: if they contradict, they cannot all be right. Compare two scientific theories that predict opposite results: at most one can be correct.","example":"Plantinga’s defence: Plantinga argued that an exclusivist who believes her religion is true, after carefully considering others, is not being arrogant or irrational. Everyone, including the pluralist, holds views others reject.","history":"Many traditional forms of Christianity, Islam and other religions hold exclusivist views, and some Hindu schools, such as Madhva’s Dvaita, held that only devotees of Vishnu reach liberation. Karl Barth (1886 to 1968) and Hendrik Kraemer defended it in Christianity. Alvin Plantinga’s “A Defense of Religious Exclusivism” (1995) answered the charge of arrogance.","misread":"Exclusivism about truth does not require intolerance; one can believe others are wrong while respecting them.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Religious Diversity (Pluralism) (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-pluralism/"},{"title":"Religious Diversity, Theories of (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/reli-div/"}]},"k9j|inclusive":{"summary":"One religion is the truest, but others contain real truth, and their followers can reach salvation or liberation through it.","idea":"Inclusivism holds that one religion has the fullest truth, but others share in it partially. Followers of other religions can be saved or liberated, but through the truth or grace that the fullest religion best expresses. It denies both that other religions are entirely wrong and that all are equal. The appeal is that it combines commitment with generosity. Compare a map that is the most complete, while other maps capture real parts of the same terrain.","example":"Anonymous Christians: Karl Rahner proposed that a sincere Buddhist or Hindu who lives by love and conscience may be saved by Christ’s grace without knowing it. Such people are, he said, anonymous Christians. Critics asked how a Buddhist would feel being called an anonymous Christian.","history":"Karl Rahner (20th century) and the Second Vatican Council’s Nostra Aetate (1965) expressed Christian inclusivism. Hindu thinkers such as Swami Vivekananda saw other religions as partial paths to Vedantic truth. The Bhagavad Gita has Krishna say that people approach him in many ways.","misread":"Inclusivism is not pluralism: it still holds that one religion is truest.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Religious Diversity (Pluralism) (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-pluralism/"},{"title":"Religious Diversity, Theories of (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/reli-div/"}]},"k9j|plural":{"summary":"The great religions are different human responses to the same ultimate reality.","idea":"Pluralism holds that the major religions are equally valid responses to one ultimate reality, which no single tradition captures fully. Their different claims reflect different cultural lenses, not one being right and others wrong. It denies that any one religion has the final truth. The appeal is that it explains why sincere and good people are found in all traditions. Compare different languages describing the same sunrise: each captures something, none says it all.","example":"The blind men and the elephant: each touches a different part and describes the elephant differently. Pluralists say religions are like this, each grasping a part of one reality. Critics ask who sees the whole elephant.","history":"The Rig Veda (1.164.46) says truth is one, though the wise call it by many names. Ramakrishna (1836 to 1886), Mahatma Gandhi and the Mughal emperor Akbar held pluralist views. John Hick’s An Interpretation of Religion (1989) is the classic philosophical defence, using Kant’s distinction between things as they are and as they appear.","misread":"Pluralism does not say all religions are identical; it says they are equally valid responses to one reality.","variants":["Hick’s pluralism: religions as responses to “the Real.”"],"reading":[{"title":"Religious Diversity (Pluralism) (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-pluralism/"},{"title":"Pluralism, Religious (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/rel-plur/"},{"title":"Hick, John (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/hick/"}]},"k9j|suspend":{"summary":"Sincere, well-informed people disagree about religion, so no one should be confident their religion is right.","idea":"This view holds that religious disagreement itself should make us less confident. When equally intelligent, informed and sincere people disagree, each should recognise that they might be the one in error. So no one should hold their religious views with strong confidence. It denies that one can reasonably be sure. The appeal is humility. Compare two expert doctors who disagree on a diagnosis: neither should be overconfident.","example":"Peer disagreement: Richard Feldman asked how two equally informed and intelligent people can both be reasonable when they disagree. He argued that both should suspend judgment, since neither has reason to prefer their own view.","history":"Richard Feldman’s “Reasonable Religious Disagreements” (2007) and the broader epistemology of disagreement (David Christensen, Thomas Kelly) shaped this view. Ancient Pyrrhonian sceptics, as reported by Sextus Empiricus, suspended judgment whenever arguments balanced, and the Jain doctrine of many-sidedness counsels a related caution about any single view.","misread":"Suspending judgment is not saying all religions are equally true; it is saying we cannot tell which, if any, is.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Disagreement, Religious (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/rel-disa/"},{"title":"Religious Diversity (Pluralism) (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-pluralism/"}]},"k9j|allfalse":{"summary":"Religions’ claims about gods and the supernatural are mistaken, whatever wisdom they may contain.","idea":"Naturalism about religion holds that there are no gods or supernatural realities, so religious claims about them are false. This does not mean religions are worthless: they may contain moral wisdom, art and community. It denies that religion reveals truths about a supernatural reality. The appeal is consistency with a scientific worldview. Compare ancient myths about thunder gods: we can appreciate the stories while knowing thunder has natural causes.","example":"Religion as a natural phenomenon: Dennett argued that religion should be studied scientifically, like any other human behaviour, to understand how it arose and why it persists, without assuming its claims are true.","history":"Ancient thinkers like the Indian Cārvākas and the Greek atomists and Epicureans doubted the supernatural. David Hume (Natural History of Religion, 1757), Ludwig Feuerbach and Sigmund Freud explained religion naturally. Daniel Dennett’s Breaking the Spell (2006) continues this tradition, and religious naturalists keep the practices without the supernatural.","misread":"Naturalists need not despise religion; many value its cultural and moral contributions.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Naturalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/naturalism/"},{"title":"Evolutionary Approaches to Religion (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-evolutionary/"}]},"k9j|practice":{"summary":"Religions are ways of living, not rival theories about the world, so they do not really compete.","idea":"Religious non-realism holds that religious language is not trying to describe a supernatural reality. It expresses commitments, values, ways of seeing the world and forms of life. So religions do not contradict each other the way rival scientific theories do. It denies that religions make factual claims about gods. The appeal is that it explains how religion can be meaningful without supernatural claims. Compare different styles of music: they are not competing theories, but different ways of expressing life.","example":"Wittgenstein on the Last Judgment: Wittgenstein said that a believer in the Last Judgment and a non-believer are not contradicting each other like two people disagreeing about a fact. The believer has a picture that guides their life.","history":"Ludwig Wittgenstein’s Lectures on Religious Belief (given 1938, published 1966) inspired the view. R. B. Braithwaite (1955) read religious assertions as commitments to a moral policy, supported by stories. D. Z. Phillips and Don Cupitt (Taking Leave of God, 1980) developed it. George Lindbeck’s The Nature of Doctrine (1984) is related.","misread":"Non-realists are not saying religion is meaningless; they say its meaning lies in practice, not in describing supernatural facts.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Religious Language (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-language/"},{"title":"Ludwig Wittgenstein (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wittgenstein/"}]},"l1|yes":{"summary":"A human life can have real meaning, whether that meaning comes from God, from what is truly valuable, or from what we care about.","idea":"This is the default view of most philosophers working on the topic. It claims that some lives, or some parts of lives, have a point: they matter in a way that goes beyond being pleasant. It denies nihilism, the view that no life could ever have meaning. Defenders disagree sharply about where meaning comes from, which is why the next question splits them. What unites them is a simple observation: we already judge some lives as more meaningful than others. Most people think a life spent raising children or curing disease has more point than one spent collecting bottle caps, and that judgement seems to track something.","example":"Compare two people who both feel content. One spent decades building a school in her village; the other spent the same years watching the same three films on repeat. Most people instantly see a difference in meaning, even if both were happy. The view says that intuition is about something real.","history":"Questions about the point of life run through Ecclesiastes, the Buddha and Confucius. The modern analytic debate took shape in the twentieth century with Kurt Baier's lecture The Meaning of Life (1957) and Robert Nozick's Philosophical Explanations (1981), and was mapped by Thaddeus Metz in Meaning in Life (2013), which sorts answers into supernaturalist and naturalist (subjective, objective, hybrid) families.","misread":"Saying life can have meaning is not saying every life has meaning, or that meaning must be one grand cosmic purpose; most defenders talk of meaning in life, in degrees.","variants":["Supernaturalism: meaning comes from God or a spiritual realm","Naturalism: meaning is available in a purely physical world","Hybrid views: meaning needs both caring and worth"],"reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}]},"l1|no":{"summary":"Nihilism about meaning: no human life has a real point, and nothing we could do or make would give it one.","idea":"Nihilism about meaning says the search comes up empty. People feel that some lives matter more than others, but on this view that feeling tracks nothing real. It denies both that meaning is handed to us and that we can make it, since what we make is just more stuff that will vanish or that only seems important because we care. Its appeal is honesty: it refuses comforting stories. Think of a sandcastle built with great effort: it may be fun to build, but the tide does not care. Nihilism about meaning is a claim about value and significance, not a recommendation about how anyone should feel or act.","example":"Tolstoy, in A Confession, describes asking of each achievement, “And then what?” His fame, his books, his estates: he could find nothing that death would not undo. The nihilist accepts that the question has no good answer, though Tolstoy himself went on to find one in faith.","history":"The word was popularised by Ivan Turgenev's novel Fathers and Sons (1862). Friedrich Nietzsche (1844 to 1900) diagnosed nihilism as the crisis that follows the loss of belief in God, though he hoped to overcome it. In analytic philosophy, the clash between our seriousness and our smallness was examined in Thomas Nagel's essay The Absurd (1971).","misread":"Nihilism about meaning is a philosophical thesis about value, not a mood of despair; many nihilists live cheerful, caring lives and see no contradiction in that.","variants":["Atheist nihilism: only God could supply meaning, and there is none","Cosmic insignificance: we are too small and brief to matter","Value nihilism: nothing is objectively valuable at all"],"reading":[{"title":"Nihilism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/nihilism/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"}]},"l1|confused":{"summary":"The question “what is the meaning of life?” rests on a mistake, so it has no answer to find or to miss.","idea":"This position does not say life is meaningless. It says the question is badly formed. Words have meanings and tools have purposes because people use them for something. A life is not a sentence or a hammer, so asking for its meaning is like asking what colour the number seven is. The view denies that there is a hidden fact waiting to be discovered. Its attraction is relief: if the question is confused, failing to answer it is no failure. Many people notice the question bothers them most when life is going badly and fades when they are absorbed in work or friends.","example":"Wittgenstein's remark at the end of the Tractatus is the classic case: people who have found the sense of life after long doubt often cannot say what it consists in. The problem does not get solved; it stops arising, the way a puzzle about a word goes away once you see how the word is used.","history":"Ludwig Wittgenstein's Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus (1921) said the solution to the problem of life is seen in its vanishing. Logical positivists such as A. J. Ayer, in The Claims of Philosophy (1947), argued that asking for life's purpose wrongly assumes life was designed. Zen teachers have long redirected such questions toward ordinary practice rather than theory.","misread":"This is not nihilism: it does not say lives lack meaning, only that “the meaning of life” is the wrong shape of question to ask.","variants":["Positivist dismissal: the question is literally meaningless","Wittgensteinian quietism: the question dissolves through living, not theory"],"reading":[{"title":"Ludwig Wittgenstein (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wittgenstein/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Early Continental and Analytic Perspectives (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ear/"}]},"l1a|god":{"summary":"Supernaturalism: meaning comes from God, through a purpose God gives, a relationship with God, or a life that continues after death.","idea":"Supernaturalism holds that what makes a life meaningful depends on a spiritual reality. Versions differ: some say meaning is fulfilling the purpose God assigned you, others that it is knowing and loving God, others that only an eternal life can matter forever. It denies that a purely physical world, where everything ends, could fully supply meaning. Its appeal is that it seems to answer the deepest worry: that whatever we do will be erased. Think of a letter written to someone who will keep it forever, compared with one that will be burned unread. Supernaturalists think God makes our lives like the first letter.","example":"Tolstoy's crisis in A Confession is the standard case: a famous, wealthy man finds nothing in his life that his death will not destroy, and resolves his crisis only by turning to faith. Supernaturalists see his story as showing what only God can provide.","history":"Augustine's Confessions (c. 397 to 400) describes the heart as restless until it rests in God. Thomas Aquinas (1225 to 1274) placed human fulfilment in the vision of God. Similar ideas appear in Islamic thought, for example in al-Ghazali. In recent analytic philosophy, William Lane Craig has defended it, and Thaddeus Metz has mapped it alongside its critics.","misread":"Supernaturalists need not say atheists' lives are worthless; many grant real value to love and work, and only claim the deepest meaning needs God.","variants":["Purpose theory: meaning is fulfilling God's purpose for you","Soul-centred view: meaning requires an immortal soul","Moderate supernaturalism: God adds a deeper layer to earthly meaning"],"reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}]},"l1a|worth":{"summary":"Objectivism: a life is meaningful when it engages with things that are really worthwhile, like love, helping others, truth and beauty.","idea":"Objectivism says meaning depends on what you actually do, not on how you feel about it. Some activities are worth doing whether or not anyone wants them: caring for others, discovering truths, creating beauty, standing up for justice. It denies that feelings or choices alone settle the matter. Its appeal is that it matches how we judge lives from outside. We call a nurse's working life meaningful even on her grumpy days. The view also fits traditions where meaning lies above all in relationships: ubuntu teaches that a person becomes a person through other persons, and Confucian thought centres a good life on family and community roles.","example":"Consider a scientist who spends her career quietly developing a vaccine, finds the lab work dull, and never sees the people it saves. Objectivists say her life has great meaning through what it accomplished, whatever she felt on any given Tuesday.","history":"Aristotle (384 to 322 BCE) tied the best life to exercising human excellences. Confucius (551 to 479 BCE) located the good life in humane relationships. In recent philosophy, Peter Singer's How Are We to Live? (1993) links meaning to working for causes larger than oneself, and Thaddeus Metz's Meaning in Life (2013) defends an objective theory focused on the good, the true and the beautiful.","misread":"Objectivism does not say feelings are irrelevant to a good life; it says they are not what makes the life meaningful, which is a narrower claim.","variants":["Consequentialist objectivism: meaning lies in making the world better","Perfectionism: meaning lies in developing human excellences","Relational views: meaning lies in good relationships, as in ubuntu and Confucian thought"],"reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}]},"l1a|hybrid":{"summary":"The hybrid view: meaning arises when you truly love something that truly deserves to be loved, so both caring and worth are needed.","idea":"The hybrid view, best known from Susan Wolf, says meaning arises when subjective attraction meets objective attractiveness. You must be gripped by what you do, and what you do must be worth being gripped by. It denies the pure subjectivist claim that any passion counts, and the pure objectivist claim that good works count without any engagement. Its appeal is that it explains two judgements at once: we hesitate to call a life spent passionately collecting rubber bands meaningful, and we also feel something is missing in someone doing valuable work they are completely alienated from. A devoted teacher who loves her pupils is the paradigm case.","example":"Wolf contrasts the happy grass counter (fulfilled, but by something pointless) with the alienated worker doing valuable work he cannot care about. Each lacks one ingredient. The full case is someone like a gardener who loves tending a community garden that feeds her neighbours.","history":"Susan Wolf developed the view in her essay Happiness and Meaning (1997) and the Tanner Lectures published as Meaning in Life and Why It Matters (2010). Earlier hints appear in Aristotle's link between virtue and taking pleasure in virtuous acts. Thaddeus Metz and others have since debated whether Wolf's two ingredients are both strictly necessary.","misread":"The hybrid view does not ask you to love whatever is valuable; it asks that what you love be worth loving, leaving room for many different meaningful lives.","variants":["Wolf's fitting fulfilment: loving what merits love","Fitting-attitude views: meaning is appropriate emotional engagement with value"],"reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}]},"l1a|subjective":{"summary":"Subjectivism: a life gets its meaning from whatever its owner cares about, chooses or commits to; meaning is made, not found.","idea":"Subjectivism says meaning comes from inside: from your passions, choices and commitments. If you care deeply about something and pursue it, your life has meaning, whatever others think of your project. It denies that there is an independent list of meaningful activities that everyone must match. Its appeal is respect for individuals: it lets a stamp collector, a monk and a footballer each find meaning in their own way, without a philosopher ranking them. It also fits a secular picture of the world in which value depends on minds. Think of a grandmother who devotes herself to her rose garden: the garden matters because it matters to her.","example":"Richard Taylor retold the myth of Sisyphus: imagine the gods gave Sisyphus a strong desire to roll stones. Then his endless task would be exactly what he wanted, and his life would have meaning for him. Taylor used the case to argue that meaning comes from within.","history":"Richard Taylor's Good and Evil (1970) contains the classic Sisyphus argument. Jean-Paul Sartre's lecture Existentialism Is a Humanism (1945, published 1946) holds that we create our own essence through choices. Harry Frankfurt's The Importance of What We Care About (1988) grounds much of what matters to us in caring and love.","misread":"Subjectivism does not mean anything goes in ethics; it is a view about meaning, and many subjectivists still hold firm moral standards.","variants":["Desire-based subjectivism: meaning is getting what you deeply want","Existentialist subjectivism: meaning is created through free choice and commitment","Care-based views: meaning comes from what you love (Frankfurt)"],"reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"Existentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/existentialism/"}]},"l1b|yes":{"summary":"Consistent subjectivism: if counting grass truly fulfils him, then his life is meaningful, because fulfilment is what meaning is.","idea":"This answer bites the bullet. If meaning comes from what you care about, then the grass counter, who cares intensely, has a meaningful life. It denies that outsiders can overrule his own experience by declaring his project trivial. Defenders point out that many of our own projects look odd from outside: solving crossword puzzles, breeding pigeons, memorising digits of pi. Who decides which passions are serious? The view also warns against snobbery about other people's lives. A man who finds joy in restoring old radios no one will use is, on this view, living just as meaningfully as a celebrated novelist.","example":"The grass counter comes from John Rawls's A Theory of Justice (1971), where a man spends his days counting blades of grass in parks and lawns. Rawls said such a person's good would be grass counting. Taylor's rewired Sisyphus makes the same point with a mythological case.","history":"Rawls introduced the grass counter in A Theory of Justice (1971) while discussing rational plans of life. Richard Taylor's Good and Evil (1970) argued similarly with Sisyphus. Wolf later used the grass counter against subjectivism, and subjectivists replied that her intuition reflects our tastes, not a deep fact.","misread":"Accepting the grass counter's life as meaningful is not praising grass counting; it is saying meaning belongs to the person, not to the activity viewed from outside.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}]},"l1b|no":{"summary":"The grass counter's fulfilment is real, but something is missing: meaning also requires a project that deserves the devotion.","idea":"This answer pulls away from pure subjectivism toward the hybrid view. Fulfilment matters, but fulfilment in a pointless task leaves a gap. The grass counter may be happy, and his happiness counts, yet his life lacks the kind of point that love, friendship, discovery or creation would give it. The view denies that feeling fulfilled is enough on its own. Its appeal is that it explains why we pity, a little, someone devoted to a trivial pursuit, even if they are content. A person who spends years loving a con artist who is using them may feel fulfilled, but we sense their devotion is misplaced.","example":"Susan Wolf turns Rawls's grass counter into a test: if he is fulfilled yet we still hesitate to call his life meaningful, then fulfilment alone is not meaning. The same goes for someone devoted to a goldfish that has long since died, but who doesn't know it.","history":"Susan Wolf made this argument in Meaning in Life and Why It Matters (2010), using the grass counter from John Rawls's A Theory of Justice (1971). Others, including Thaddeus Metz, accept the intuition and build objective conditions into meaning; subjectivists reply that the intuition may only express our preferences.","misread":"Saying his life lacks meaning is not saying he is foolish or that his happiness is fake; it is a claim about one dimension of a life.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}]},"l1e|yes":{"summary":"Objectivism: the doctor's life is meaningful because of the lives she saves, however little she feels about her work.","idea":"This answer holds that meaning lives in what you do and its effects, not in your inner attitude. The doctor saves thousands of people: that is among the most valuable things a person can do, and it gives her life deep meaning whether or not she enjoys it. It denies that boredom or alienation can cancel that. Its appeal is that it keeps meaning tied to the real world rather than to moods. Many people do hard, worthwhile jobs without passion, such as cleaning hospitals or caring for a sick parent out of duty, and it seems unfair to call their lives less meaningful because of how they feel.","example":"Picture a reluctant firefighter who dislikes the job but keeps doing it because the town needs him, and pulls dozens of people from burning buildings over his career. Objectivists say his life is plainly meaningful, and his feelings about it do not change that.","history":"Peter Singer's How Are We to Live? (1993) and Practical Ethics link a meaningful life to making the world better. Thaddeus Metz's Meaning in Life (2013) argues that meaning depends on positively orienting reason toward fundamental conditions of human life. Both draw on a long consequentialist and perfectionist tradition going back to Aristotle and Mill.","misread":"Objectivists can still say her life would go better for her if she cared; they distinguish meaning from personal happiness.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}]},"l1e|no":{"summary":"The hybrid view: the doctor's work is valuable, but her life is not fully meaningful unless she also cares about it.","idea":"This answer says her achievements are real and important, but meaning in her life needs her to be engaged with them. The doctor is like a channel through which good flows, but she is not living the good in her own life. It denies that results alone make a life meaningful. Its attraction is that it captures the sense of alienation: someone who feels nothing about their work often says their life feels empty, and the hybrid view takes that report seriously. Think of a gifted musician who plays beautifully but hates every note: the music is valuable, yet something in her life is missing.","example":"Susan Wolf's alienated worker is the standard case: someone doing valuable work, like the doctor here, who cannot bring himself to care. Wolf's point is that he lacks one of meaning's two ingredients, just as the fulfilled grass counter lacks the other.","history":"Susan Wolf argued for this in Meaning in Life and Why It Matters (2010). Aristotle's Nicomachean Ethics held that a truly virtuous person takes pleasure in acting well, not just in the results. Bernard Williams's work on integrity and ground projects also stresses that a life must be lived from the inside.","misread":"This does not say the doctor did nothing good; it says the good she did is not enough, alone, to make her own life fully meaningful.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}]},"l1f|no":{"summary":"Extreme supernaturalism: without God and eternity, everything we do would be wiped out, and no life would have any meaning.","idea":"This view says God is not just one source of meaning but the only one. If there were no God and no life after death, everything would end in the same nothing, and so nothing would ultimately matter. It denies that love, achievement or kindness could give meaning on their own, because death and the end of the universe erase them. Its appeal is its logic: if what matters must matter forever, only an eternal reality can supply it. Think of building a beautiful house knowing it will be bulldozed tomorrow with no trace kept anywhere: the effort seems to lose its point.","example":"William Lane Craig's essay The Absurdity of Life without God gives the modern version: if every person and the universe itself end in death, then every life, however heroic, ends in the same place. Tolstoy's question, whether anything in his life would survive his death, frames the case.","history":"Tolstoy's A Confession (written 1879 to 1880) states the worry vividly. Augustine (354 to 430) taught that human hearts find rest only in God. Blaise Pascal's Pensées (published 1670) depicts humans as lost without God. William Lane Craig's essay The Absurdity of Life without God, in Reasonable Faith, is the best-known recent defence.","misread":"Extreme supernaturalists are not saying nonbelievers lead worse moral lives; they claim only that, if atheism were true, no life could ultimately matter.","variants":["Eternity-based version: only what lasts forever can matter","Purpose-based version: only a divine plan can give a life a point"],"reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}]},"l1f|less":{"summary":"Moderate supernaturalism: love, work and goodness give real meaning even without God, but God and eternity add a deeper meaning.","idea":"This view keeps God central without making God the only source. In a godless world, helping your neighbour, raising children or discovering truths would still matter, but the meaning available would be more limited. With God, these acts gain a further significance: they connect to an eternal purpose and do not simply vanish. It denies that atheism would make every life pointless. Its appeal is that it fits common experience: believers and nonbelievers alike recognise meaningful lives around them. Think of a song: it is beautiful even if only heard once, but more fully appreciated if it is recorded and loved forever.","example":"Imagine two equally devoted nurses, one religious and one not. The moderate supernaturalist says both lives have real meaning; the believer holds that, if God exists, the nurse's care also forms part of an eternal story, which gives it a significance it would otherwise lack.","history":"Thaddeus Metz's survey of the meaning of life (Stanford Encyclopedia) introduced the label and showed how many religious thinkers fit it. Many religious philosophers fit it, holding that earthly goods are real but that God's existence would give them further significance. Richard Swinburne's theism, which stresses the value of free human choices in a created world, is often read this way.","misread":"Moderate supernaturalism is not a half-hearted faith; it is a precise claim that God increases meaning rather than being its only source.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}]},"l1g|god":{"summary":"Atheist nihilism: only God or an afterlife could give life a point, and since neither exists, no life has meaning.","idea":"Atheist nihilism agrees with extreme supernaturalists on one step and disagrees on the other. Both say that without God life would be meaningless; the atheist nihilist adds that there is no God. So it combines a religious standard for meaning with an atheist picture of the world. Its appeal is consistency: if meaning requires an eternal purpose and you do not believe in one, the conclusion seems to follow. It denies that humanist sources like love or achievement can substitute. Think of someone who loses their faith and feels that the meaning went with it, not just a belief.","example":"Ivan Karamazov in Dostoevsky's The Brothers Karamazov (1880) explores what follows if there is no God and no immortality, and the novel shows the moral and existential vertigo he feels. He became the classic literary image of this position.","history":"Nietzsche (1844 to 1900) argued that the death of God would bring nihilism unless new values were created. Dostoevsky dramatised the worry in his novels. In the twentieth century, Camus in The Myth of Sisyphus (1942) began from a godless world and asked what follows; many religious apologists use the atheist nihilist's reasoning to argue for faith.","misread":"Atheist nihilists need not think atheism leads to despair; they hold that it rules out ultimate meaning, which is a narrower claim.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Nihilism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/nihilism/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"}]},"l1g|cosmic":{"summary":"Cosmic insignificance: humans are too small and too brief for anything we do to make a real difference to the universe.","idea":"This view starts from scientific facts. The universe is around 13.8 billion years old and enormous. Each of us lives a few decades on a small planet; humanity itself will end, and the stars will burn out. From the universe's point of view, nothing we do makes any difference. It denies that the human perspective, in which our lives seem important, is the final word. Its appeal is that it seems simply to follow from taking science seriously. Think of an ant colony destroyed by a passing shoe: busy, organised, and then gone, with nothing in the universe noticing.","example":"Bertrand Russell's essay A Free Man's Worship (1903) imagines the whole temple of human achievement buried under the debris of a dying solar system. Thomas Nagel's essay The Absurd (1971) discusses the “view from nowhere” in which our lives seem insignificant.","history":"Ecclesiastes asks what lasting gain humans have from their toil. Russell's A Free Man's Worship (1903) and Nagel's The Absurd (1971) are modern statements. Guy Kahane's essay Our Cosmic Insignificance (2014) argued in reply that size and duration may not be what makes something significant.","misread":"Cosmic insignificance does not show our lives matter to no one; it says they make no difference on a cosmic scale, which is a different question.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"Nihilism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/nihilism/"}]},"l1g|novalue":{"summary":"Value nihilism: nothing is really valuable; we project value onto a valueless world, so nothing could make a life worthwhile.","idea":"Value nihilism goes deeper than doubts about meaning. It says nothing at all is really good or bad: no pleasure, no friendship, no achievement. We feel that things matter, and we project that feeling onto the world, the way we see faces in clouds. It denies that our sense of value tracks any fact. Its appeal is scientific economy: physics describes particles and forces, and value seems nowhere among them. If value is a projection, meaning built on value is too. Think of how a souvenir seems precious to its owner and like junk to a stranger: the value lies in the viewer.","example":"J. L. Mackie's argument from queerness asks what objective values would be: strange entities with a built-in “to be pursued” attached, unlike anything else science finds. If they do not exist, every value claim is false.","history":"David Hume (1711 to 1776) described the mind as gilding and staining the world with colours from its own sentiment. J. L. Mackie's Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977) defended moral error theory. Richard Joyce's The Myth of Morality (2001) extended the case. Value nihilism about meaning applies their reasoning to significance.","misread":"Value nihilism does not forbid caring about things; it says caring is all there is, and no further fact makes the caring correct.","variants":["Error theory: value claims are all false","Projectivism: value is a projection of feeling onto the world"],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"},{"title":"Nihilism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/nihilism/"}]},"l1c|revolt":{"summary":"Revolt: face the absurd honestly and live fully anyway, without false hope and without giving in.","idea":"This is Albert Camus's answer. The absurd is the clash between our hunger for meaning and a silent universe that offers none. One response is escape: inventing hope in a hidden purpose. Camus rejects that as dishonest. Another is to give up on life: Camus rejects that too, insisting that the absurd must be kept alive, not ended. What remains is revolt: living with full awareness, freedom and passion, while refusing both false hope and resignation. Its appeal is a kind of heroic honesty. Think of a climber who knows the summit is pointless and climbs with total commitment anyway.","example":"Camus closes The Myth of Sisyphus with the image of Sisyphus, condemned to push his rock uphill forever. As he walks back down, Camus says, we must imagine him happy: his lucidity and his scorn for his fate make the task his own.","history":"Albert Camus (1913 to 1960) set out the view in The Myth of Sisyphus (1942) and his novel The Stranger (1942), then developed it into collective solidarity in The Plague (1947) and The Rebel (1951). Though often grouped with existentialism, Camus rejected the label.","misread":"Revolt is not anger or rebellion against society; it is a stance of lucid, wholehearted living that refuses both false hope and giving up on life.","variants":["Individual revolt: lucid, passionate living (The Myth of Sisyphus)","Solidarity: revolt shared with others against suffering (The Plague, The Rebel)"],"reading":[{"title":"Albert Camus (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/camus/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Early Continental and Analytic Perspectives (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ear/"}]},"l1c|irony":{"summary":"Ironic acceptance: keep caring about your life, but see the absurdity of taking it so seriously, and meet it with a smile.","idea":"Thomas Nagel argued that life is absurd because we take our projects with total seriousness, yet we can always step back and see them as arbitrary from outside. The absurd comes from this double vision, not from the universe being too big. Since we cannot stop being serious or stop stepping back, the absurd cannot be escaped. But it does not call for heroic defiance or despair. Nagel recommended irony: carry on, notice the gap, and smile. Its appeal is proportion. Think of someone frantically ironing a shirt before an interview who suddenly sees the comedy of it, laughs, and keeps ironing.","example":"One of Nagel's own examples: as you are being knighted, your trousers fall down. The absurd is the gap between pretension and reality. Human life, he said, is like that from a detached view, and the fitting response is closer to amusement than tragedy.","history":"Thomas Nagel (born 1937) published The Absurd in The Journal of Philosophy (1971), reprinted in Mortal Questions (1979). He developed the idea of stepping back in The View from Nowhere (1986). His essay is partly a reply to Camus, whom he thought too dramatic.","misread":"Irony here does not mean sarcasm or not caring; it means caring fully while seeing that the caring has no cosmic backing.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Early Continental and Analytic Perspectives (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ear/"}]},"l1c|pessim":{"summary":"Pessimism: life holds more striving and pain than good, so want less, expect little, and be gentle with others who suffer.","idea":"Pessimism says life on balance holds more suffering than satisfaction. Desire is endless: we strive, we briefly get what we want, then become bored or start striving again. The response is not despair but resignation: quieting desire, finding moments of rest in art and contemplation, and treating others with compassion because they suffer as we do. It denies that progress or effort will ever deliver lasting happiness. Its appeal is honesty about pain and a surprising gentleness. Think of how a new phone thrills for a week and then becomes normal: pessimists see this pattern everywhere and recommend wanting less.","example":"Schopenhauer's pendulum: life swings between pain, when we lack what we want, and boredom, when we have it. Brief moments of relief come in aesthetic experience, such as being absorbed in music, when desire falls silent for a while.","history":"Arthur Schopenhauer (1788 to 1860) set out the view in The World as Will and Representation (1818, expanded 1844), influenced by Buddhist and Hindu texts. He held that compassion is the basis of ethics. Philipp Mainländer and Eduard von Hartmann extended it in nineteenth century Germany. David Benatar's The Human Predicament (2017) is a recent defence.","misread":"Pessimism is a view about the balance of good and bad, not advice to give up; Schopenhauer argued that ending one's life does not escape the problem he described.","variants":["Schopenhauerian pessimism: the will to live produces endless striving","Buddhist-inflected pessimism: suffering comes from craving"],"reading":[{"title":"Arthur Schopenhauer (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/schopenhauer/"},{"title":"Nihilism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/nihilism/"}]},"l1c|nothing":{"summary":"Thoroughgoing nihilism: without meaning there is no “should” either, so no way of living is better than any other.","idea":"This position follows nihilism all the way. If nothing has value, then nothing can tell us how to live. Camus's revolt, Nagel's irony and Schopenhauer's compassion all quietly assume something is better than something else. The thoroughgoing nihilist denies even that. Every answer to “how should I live?” fails, including “live defiantly”, because there is no should. Its appeal is consistency: it refuses to smuggle values back in. Note what this does and does not imply: it gives no reason for any choice, including drastic ones. People simply go on living by habit, care and preference, which needs no justification to continue.","example":"Think of a chess player told the game has no rules. He cannot win or lose, but he can still move the pieces, and enjoy it. The thoroughgoing nihilist sees ordinary life like that: no move is correct, yet the game goes on.","history":"Nietzsche distinguished passive nihilism (exhaustion) from active nihilism (clearing away old values), and hoped to move beyond both. More recently, Ray Brassier's Nihil Unbound (2007) defends a radical nihilism, while many critics argue it is self-defeating because it treats consistency as worth having.","misread":"Thoroughgoing nihilism does not recommend anything, including despair or harm; it holds that no recommendation has backing, which leaves ordinary caring untouched.","variants":["Passive nihilism: loss of value felt as exhaustion (Nietzsche)","Active nihilism: clearing away false values (Nietzsche)"],"reading":[{"title":"Nihilism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/nihilism/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}]},"l3|same":{"summary":"Hedonism about well-being: only how life feels from the inside matters to how well it goes, so the deceived man lost nothing.","idea":"Hedonism about well-being says what makes your life go well for you is pleasure, and what makes it go badly is pain. The deceived man had exactly the same experiences as a man whose wife and friends were sincere. So, for him, his life went just as well. It denies that facts you never discover can affect your well-being. Its appeal is simplicity and a clear link to experience: how could something make your life worse if it never touches your consciousness? Think of a surprise party ruined behind the scenes but enjoyed perfectly by the guest: from her point of view, nothing went wrong.","example":"Robert Nozick's experience machine is the famous test: would you plug into a machine that gives you any experiences you like, while you float in a tank? Hedonists must say life in the machine is as good for you as real life.","history":"Epicurus (341 to 270 BCE) held that pleasure is the goal of life, understood as freedom from pain and anxiety. Jeremy Bentham's Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789) and John Stuart Mill's Utilitarianism (1863) built ethical theories on it. Fred Feldman's Pleasure and the Good Life (2004) is a modern defence.","misread":"Hedonism about well-being is not a recommendation to party; Epicurus sought simple pleasures and calm, and hedonism only says what makes a life go well.","variants":["Quantitative hedonism: only amount of pleasure counts (Bentham)","Qualitative hedonism: higher pleasures count more (Mill)","Attitudinal hedonism: well-being is taking pleasure in things (Feldman)"],"reading":[{"title":"Hedonism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hedonism/"},{"title":"Well-Being (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/well-being/"}]},"l3|worse":{"summary":"Anti-experientialism: some things affect how well your life goes even if you never experience them, like being betrayed unknowingly.","idea":"This position says your well-being depends partly on how things really are, not only on how they feel. The deceived man wanted to be loved and respected, not just to feel loved and respected. Since that wish failed, his life went worse for him, even though he never found out. It denies the experience requirement: the claim that only what you experience can affect you. Its appeal is that most people, asked whether they would prefer the deceived life or the real one, choose the real one without hesitation. Think of how you would feel learning that a friend you trusted for years had secretly despised you.","example":"Shelly Kagan's deceived businessman: he dies believing his family loved him and his colleagues respected him, but all of it was a sham. Kagan argued that his life went worse for him, a case closely modelled on Thomas Nagel's betrayed man.","history":"Thomas Nagel's essay Death (1970) argued that a man betrayed behind his back is harmed though he never knows. Robert Nozick's Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974) offered the experience machine. Shelly Kagan's essay Me and My Life (1994) gave the deceived businessman. Desire theories and objective list theories both reject experientialism.","misread":"This view does not say ignorance is never bliss; it says ignorance can protect your feelings without protecting how well your life is really going.","variants":["Desire-satisfaction theories: getting what you really want","Objective list theories: some goods benefit anyone","Hybrid theories: goods that are also wanted"],"reading":[{"title":"Well-Being (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/well-being/"},{"title":"Death (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"}]},"l3a|desire":{"summary":"Preference satisfaction: your life goes well to the extent you get what you really want, informed and on reflection.","idea":"On this view, your well-being consists in getting what you want, ideally the things you would still want if you knew all the facts. The TV watcher, fully informed, wants exactly the life he has, so his life is going as well as it could. It denies that philosophers can tell people what is good for them against their own informed desires. Its appeal is respect: each person is the authority on their own good. Think of a friend who chooses a quiet life in a small town over a glamorous career: it is her life to judge.","example":"The grass counter (from Rawls) shows the view's commitment: if someone, fully informed, wants only to count grass, then grass counting is his good. Critics use such cases against the view; defenders accept them as the price of respecting persons.","history":"Desire theories dominate welfare economics, where well-being is measured by preference satisfaction. Henry Sidgwick, in The Methods of Ethics (1874), considered informed desire. Richard Brandt and Peter Railton developed informed or ideal desire versions in the 1970s and 1980s. Derek Parfit's Reasons and Persons (1984) classified it as one of three main theories.","misread":"The view does not say whatever you want right now is good for you; most versions count only desires you would keep if fully informed.","variants":["Actual desire theory: what you want now","Informed desire theory: what you would want if fully informed","Global desire theory: desires about your life as a whole"],"reading":[{"title":"Well-Being (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/well-being/"}]},"l3a|objlist":{"summary":"Objective list theory: some things, like friendship, knowledge and achievement, are good for anyone, whether or not they want them.","idea":"This view says there is a list of goods that make any human life better: friendship, knowledge, achievement, health, pleasure, perhaps also beauty and virtue. They benefit you even if you do not want them. The TV watcher is missing out, whatever he prefers. It denies that well-being is just pleasure or just getting what you want. Its appeal is that it explains our concern for people who seem content with very narrow lives, and it matches what most cultures recognise as goods. Think of a teenager who says he wants no friends: we suspect his life would go better with some, even if he cannot see it now.","example":"Martha Nussbaum's list of central capabilities (life, health, emotions, practical reason, affiliation, play and others) is a well-known version. The capabilities approach she shares with Amartya Sen inspired the United Nations' Human Development Index, which measures lives by more than income.","history":"Aristotle's account of the good life is an ancestor. Derek Parfit coined the label objective list theories in Reasons and Persons (1984). John Finnis's Natural Law and Natural Rights (1980) listed basic goods. Martha Nussbaum and Amartya Sen developed the capabilities approach from the 1980s onward.","misread":"Objective list theorists are not saying the TV watcher should be forced into friendships; they make a claim about his good, not about what others may do.","variants":["Perfectionism: well-being is developing human nature","Capabilities approach: well-being as real freedoms to function (Nussbaum, Sen)","Natural law basic goods (Finnis)"],"reading":[{"title":"Well-Being (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/well-being/"}]},"l3a|hybrid":{"summary":"Hybrid theory of well-being: good things benefit you only when they are both objectively good and genuinely wanted or enjoyed.","idea":"Hybrid theories combine the other two. Friendship and knowledge are really valuable, but they only make your life go better if you also want or enjoy them. The TV watcher would benefit from friendship only if he came to care. It denies both that wanting alone is enough (or the grass counter does as well as anyone) and that goods imposed on someone unwilling benefit him. Its appeal is that it explains both kinds of case at once. Think of learning a language: it enriches the life of a student who loves it, but drilled into an unwilling student it may do little for him.","example":"Parfit's suggestion: what is good for us is neither just pleasure nor just knowledge, but taking pleasure in knowledge, loving people who are worth loving, and enjoying true beauty. The objective and subjective parts fit together like lock and key.","history":"Derek Parfit suggested the idea in an appendix to Reasons and Persons (1984). Later philosophers, including Shelly Kagan and Christopher Woodard, have developed or discussed hybrid accounts. Susan Wolf's view of meaning has a similar shape, joining a subjective and an objective condition in one account.","misread":"Hybrid theorists do not say that only enjoyed things matter; they say benefit requires both the good thing and a fitting attitude toward it.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Well-Being (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/well-being/"}]},"l2|yes":{"summary":"Death can be bad for the person who dies, at least sometimes, even if it is painless and they never experience it.","idea":"Most contemporary philosophers hold that death can harm the person who dies, typically by depriving them of the good life they would otherwise have had. It denies the Epicurean claim that because the dead feel nothing, nothing can be bad for them. Its appeal is that it matches the common reaction to an early death: we feel sorry for the young person, not only for their family. Think of a young musician killed in an accident just before her first concert tour: it seems she lost something, even if she never knew it. The view does not require any afterlife.","example":"Thomas Nagel's argument by analogy: a man betrayed behind his back is harmed though he never knows it. If harm need not be felt, death too can be bad without being experienced. Being deprived of good years is a harm of this kind.","history":"Thomas Nagel's essay Death (1970) is the classic modern statement. Fred Feldman's Confrontations with the Reaper (1992) and Ben Bradley's Well-Being and Death (2009) defended the deprivation account. Jeff McMahan's The Ethics of Killing (2002) refined it with time-relative interests.","misread":"Saying death is bad for the one who dies does not mean they suffer after death; the harm is the loss of what life would have held.","variants":["Deprivation view: death takes good years","Life-plan view: death cuts off plans","Mixed view: both count"],"reading":[{"title":"Death (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"}]},"l2|no":{"summary":"Death is never bad for the person who dies, not even someone young and healthy, though it may be bad for others.","idea":"This position holds that, whatever death is, it is not a harm to the one who dies. Different defenders give different reasons: there is no one left to harm, death is natural, there is no lasting self to lose, or life continues after death. What they share is the claim that fearing death as a harm to oneself is a mistake. It denies that the dead are worse off. Its traditional appeal is consolation: philosophers from Epicurus to Zhuangzi offered it to free the living from fear. It does not deny that grief is real, or that a person's death can be terrible for those who love them.","example":"Epicurus's argument is the classic: a harm needs a subject who is harmed. While you exist, death is absent; when death is present, you do not exist. So death never meets you. Many find the argument hard to refute and hard to believe.","history":"Epicurus (341 to 270 BCE), in his Letter to Menoeceus, and Lucretius (c. 99 to c. 55 BCE), in On the Nature of Things, gave the classic case. Zhuangzi (fourth century BCE) offered a Daoist version. Buddhist teaching on no-self and many religious traditions give other routes. Stephen Rosenbaum defended Epicurus in the 1980s.","misread":"This view does not make life cheap: Epicureans held that life and its pleasures are good to have, only that being dead is not an evil to the dead.","variants":["Epicurean view: no one is left to harm","Natural acceptance: death is part of nature","No-self view: there is no lasting self to lose"],"reading":[{"title":"Death (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"},{"title":"Epicurus (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epicurus/"}]},"l2a|depriv":{"summary":"The deprivation view: death is bad for you whenever it takes away good life you would otherwise have had, at any age.","idea":"On this view, death is bad because of what it takes: the good experiences, relationships and projects you would have enjoyed had you lived. The badness is comparative: your life with the death compared with your life without it. It applies at any age: if a 95-year-old would have enjoyed three more quiet years, losing them is a loss, though smaller than a child's. It denies that death harms only those with unfinished plans. Its appeal is clarity and fit with how we compare lives. Think of a holiday cut short by a storm: the shorter holiday was worse than the full one would have been.","example":"Compare two deaths: a child who would have lived 80 more good years, and an old woman who would have lived two more. The deprivation view says both lost something, and it explains why the child's death seems far worse: much more was lost.","history":"Thomas Nagel's Death (1970) argued the core idea. Fred Feldman's Confrontations with the Reaper (1992) gave a precise version. Ben Bradley's Well-Being and Death (2009) defended it at length. Jeff McMahan's time-relative interest account in The Ethics of Killing (2002) modified it to explain why infant deaths may be less bad than they seem.","misread":"The deprivation view does not imply death is bad for everyone; if someone's future would be dominated by suffering, it says death would not deprive them of good.","variants":["Life-comparative account (Feldman, Bradley)","Time-relative interest account (McMahan)"],"reading":[{"title":"Death (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"}]},"l2a|early":{"summary":"The life-plan view: death is bad when it cuts off a life before its central plans, projects and hopes are fulfilled.","idea":"This view ties the badness of death to the shape of a life. A person who has done what they set out to do has a complete life; death then takes nothing that really matters to them. Death is bad when it interrupts: a student who dies before graduating, a parent before seeing the children grow. It denies that losing a few more pleasant years is, by itself, a harm. Its appeal is that it matches the sense that the death of a 95-year-old who has done everything is sad but not tragic. Think of a novel left half finished compared with one completed: only the first is cut short.","example":"Bernard Williams's categorical desires: some desires give us reasons to go on living, like finishing a life's work or raising one's children. Death is bad when it frustrates them. A person who no longer has any such desires, like Elina Makropulos at 342, may not be harmed by dying.","history":"Bernard Williams developed the idea in The Makropulos Case (1973), reprinted in Problems of the Self. Aristotle's Nicomachean Ethics spoke of happiness as requiring a complete life. Recent defenders of narrative or life-shape views of well-being, such as J. David Velleman, have pressed similar themes.","misread":"The life-plan view does not say old people's lives are worth less; it says that the harm of death depends on what it cuts short.","variants":["Categorical desire view (Williams)","Narrative view: death harms by ruining a life's story"],"reading":[{"title":"Death (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"}]},"l2a|both":{"summary":"Mixed view: any loss of good life is bad, but death is especially bad when it cuts a life short before its plans are fulfilled.","idea":"This view combines the two answers. Death harms you whenever it takes good time away, as the deprivation view says. But a death that interrupts a life's central projects is worse, because it also damages the shape of the whole life. It denies that only quantity of lost time matters, and that only unfinished plans matter. Its appeal is that it captures both judgements: the 95-year-old lost something, and the 25-year-old lost more, not just in years but in what the years were for. Think of a race: stopping a runner near the finish line loses less time, but cuts off the point.","example":"Jeff McMahan's case of a young adult and a newborn: the young adult has plans and psychological ties to his future, so his death seems worse than the infant's, though the infant loses more years. Mixed views use such cases to show that both factors count.","history":"Jeff McMahan's The Ethics of Killing (2002) argued that death's badness depends both on the goods lost and on how connected the person is to their future. Thomas Nagel's and Bernard Williams's insights are often combined in this way in recent work on death.","misread":"","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Death (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"}]},"l2b|epicur":{"summary":"The Epicurean view: once you are dead, you no longer exist, so nothing, including death, can be bad for you.","idea":"Epicurus argued that good and bad consist in sensation, and death is the end of sensation. While you live, death is not present; once it is present, you are not. So death never touches you. The view denies that being dead is a state you suffer. Its aim was practical: to free people from fear of death so they could enjoy the life they have. Its appeal is its tight logic: harm seems to need someone harmed. Think of being afraid of a party you will not attend: you cannot be bored there, because you will not be there.","example":"Epicurus, Letter to Menoeceus: when we exist death is not, and when death is, we are not. It is often summed up as death is nothing to us. It remains a live puzzle in philosophy because the argument is simple and its conclusion so surprising.","history":"Epicurus (341 to 270 BCE) founded the Garden in Athens and wrote the Letter to Menoeceus, preserved by Diogenes Laertius. Lucretius's On the Nature of Things (first century BCE) gave a poetic defence. Stephen Rosenbaum's How to Be Dead and Not Care (1986) revived it in analytic philosophy.","misread":"Epicurus did not say dying is painless or that life is not worth having; he said being dead is not bad for the dead, so fearing it is pointless.","variants":["Existence requirement: nothing is bad for someone who does not exist","Experience requirement: only what is felt can harm"],"reading":[{"title":"Epicurus (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epicurus/"},{"title":"Death (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"},{"title":"Epicurus (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/epicur/"}]},"l2b|natural":{"summary":"Natural acceptance: death belongs to the natural order of things, like autumn after summer, and what is natural is no evil.","idea":"This view sees death as part of how nature works. Everything that comes into being passes away; to resent death is to resent the order that also gave you life. It denies that death is a disaster or an enemy. In Daoism, life and death are transformations of the same flowing process; for the Stoics, nature is rational, and its course should be accepted calmly. Its appeal is peace of mind and a sense of belonging to something larger. Think of leaves falling in autumn: we do not call this a tragedy, but part of the cycle that brings spring again.","example":"When Zhuangzi's wife died, his friend Huizi found him drumming on a bowl and singing. Zhuangzi explained that he had grieved, but then saw that she had come from formlessness and returned to it, like the passing of the seasons.","history":"The Zhuangzi (fourth to third centuries BCE), chapter 18, tells the story of his wife's death. The Stoics, including Seneca (c. 4 BCE to 65 CE), Epictetus (c. 50 to c. 135) and Marcus Aurelius (121 to 180), taught that death is a natural process to be accepted. Marcus's Meditations returns to the theme repeatedly.","misread":"Natural acceptance does not mean grief is wrong; Zhuangzi admitted grieving, and the Stoics allowed tears, only not resentment of nature itself.","variants":["Daoist transformation (Zhuangzi)","Stoic acceptance of nature (Marcus Aurelius, Epictetus)"],"reading":[{"title":"Zhuangzi (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/zhuangzi/"},{"title":"Stoicism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/stoicism/"}]},"l2b|noself":{"summary":"The no-self view: there is no lasting self for death to destroy, only a changing stream of experiences.","idea":"This view says death seems terrible because we imagine a single, enduring self who will be lost. But on examination there is no such self: only body, feelings, perceptions, mental habits and consciousness, each constantly changing. The Buddha taught this as anatta, no-self. If there was never a permanent you, death does not take one away. Its appeal is that it removes the core of the fear. It denies the common belief in a fixed inner owner of experiences. Think of a river: it seems to be one thing, but its water is always new, and its “end” at the sea destroys no single object.","example":"The chariot simile from The Questions of King Milinda: the monk Nagasena asks the king what the chariot is: the wheels, the axle, the pole? None of these alone is the chariot. “Chariot” is a convenient name for parts, just as a person is a name for changing processes.","history":"The Buddha (c. fifth century BCE) taught anatta in early discourses such as the Discourse on the Characteristic of Not-Self. The Questions of King Milinda (around the first century BCE) gives the chariot simile. Derek Parfit's Reasons and Persons (1984) argued that personal identity is not what matters, and reported that this made him less troubled by death.","misread":"No-self does not mean nobody exists or nothing matters; it means persons are processes, not fixed things, and compassion for those processes still matters greatly.","variants":["Buddhist anatta","Parfit's reductionism: identity is not what matters"],"reading":[{"title":"Buddha (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/buddha/"},{"title":"Mind in Indian Buddhist Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mind-indian-buddhism/"}]},"l2b|after":{"summary":"The afterlife view: death is not bad for the person because it is not the end; they continue to exist after it.","idea":"Many religious traditions hold that death is a passage, not an ending. The soul may survive, the person may be raised again, or they may be reborn. On this view, death cannot deprive you of all future goods, because your existence continues. It denies the starting premise of both Epicureans and deprivation theorists: that death is annihilation. Its appeal is plain consolation, and for believers, reasoned faith. Whether death is good or bad then depends on what comes after it. Think of moving to another country: hard to leave home, but not the end of you.","example":"Plato's Phaedo describes Socrates' last day: calmly discussing arguments for the immortality of the soul before drinking the hemlock. He tells his friends that a philosopher should not fear death, since the soul will survive the body.","history":"Plato's Phaedo (fourth century BCE) gives the classic Greek arguments for the soul's survival. Christianity, Islam and Judaism teach resurrection; Hindu, Buddhist and Jain traditions teach rebirth. Many African traditions, described by John Mbiti in African Religions and Philosophy (1969), hold that the dead remain present as spirits.","misread":"Belief in an afterlife does not automatically make death good; most traditions hold that what comes after depends on how one has lived.","variants":["Immortal soul: the soul survives the body","Resurrection: body and soul raised again","Rebirth: a new life shaped by karma"],"reading":[{"title":"Heaven and Hell in Christian Thought (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/heaven-hell/"},{"title":"Ancient Theories of Soul (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ancient-soul/"}]},"l2c|asym":{"summary":"Asymmetry: time before birth was not bad for you, but death is, because it takes away a future you would otherwise have had.","idea":"This view accepts Lucretius's premise that prenatal nonexistence did not harm us, but denies his conclusion. Death and prenatal nonexistence differ: death deprives you of a future life you would have had, while you could not have been born much earlier and still be you, or so many argue. Also, we care about the future in a way we do not care about the past. Its appeal is that it keeps both intuitions: no regret for not having existed earlier, but real loss in dying early. Think of being sad that a concert ends early, but not sad that it started at seven rather than five.","example":"Derek Parfit's hospital case: you wake up and do not know whether you had a painful operation yesterday or will have a shorter one later today. Most people hope it is in the past. Our bias toward the future helps explain why death seems worse than prenatal nonexistence.","history":"Thomas Nagel's Death (1970) suggested that you could not have been born much earlier. Frederik Kaufman developed this in the 1990s. Anthony Brueckner and John Martin Fischer's Why Is Death Bad? (1986) appealed to our asymmetric concern for future goods. Parfit's hospital case appears in Reasons and Persons (1984).","misread":"The asymmetry view does not say the past is unimportant; it says our attitudes to past and future goods rightly differ.","variants":["Identity version: you could not have been born much earlier (Nagel, Kaufman)","Attitude version: we rightly care more about future goods (Brueckner and Fischer)"],"reading":[{"title":"Death (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"},{"title":"Lucretius (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lucretius/"}]},"l2c|sym":{"summary":"The symmetry argument: prenatal nonexistence was not bad for you, and death is its mirror image, so death is not bad either.","idea":"Lucretius argued that the time after death is like the time before birth. You did not exist for billions of years before you were born, and that did not trouble you or harm you. Death simply returns you to the same condition. If one is not bad, neither is the other. It denies that the direction of time makes any difference to harm. Its appeal is its neat logic and its consolation: it uses an attitude we already have to cure a fear we wish to lose. Think of a long book you finished: you do not mourn the pages before the first chapter.","example":"Lucretius's mirror: nature holds up the time before our birth as a mirror of the time after our death. Look into it, he says, and see nothing frightening, only a deep sleep without dreams.","history":"Lucretius (c. 99 to c. 55 BCE) gave the argument in Book III of On the Nature of Things, defending Epicurus. Modern discussion began with Thomas Nagel's Death (1970) and continues in work by Frederik Kaufman, John Martin Fischer and others, who mostly resist the conclusion.","misread":"The symmetry argument does not deny that dying can be painful or that loved ones suffer; it targets only the fear of being dead.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Lucretius (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lucretius/"},{"title":"Death (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"}]},"l2c|bothbad":{"summary":"Both are losses: if you could have been born earlier, missing those years was bad for you, just as death is.","idea":"This view accepts the symmetry, but runs it the other way: if death is bad because it deprives you of good years, then being born later than you could have been also deprived you of good years. Both are losses. It denies that the asymmetry between past and future matters to harm. Its appeal is consistency: the deprivation account seems to treat lost years the same wherever they fall. Most people do not feel this loss, but defenders say our feelings may simply be biased toward the future. Think of discovering you could have joined a great school a year earlier: you lost a good year.","example":"Imagine you learn you were conceived through IVF, and the embryo was frozen for ten years before implantation. Had it been implanted sooner, you would have lived ten more years. Some philosophers think you were deprived of those years, as you will be by death.","history":"Fred Feldman, in Confrontations with the Reaper (1992), accepted that late birth can be bad for a person in the same way as early death. Ben Bradley's Well-Being and Death (2009) discusses the case. The view draws its structure from Lucretius's original mirror argument.","misread":"This view does not say you should grieve your birth date; it says that, on reflection, lost years are lost wherever they fall.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Death (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"},{"title":"Lucretius (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lucretius/"}]},"l5|end":{"summary":"Annihilation: when you die, you simply stop existing; there is no soul, afterlife or rebirth.","idea":"This view holds that a person is a living physical being, or depends wholly on one. When the brain stops working, the mind stops too, and there is no further experience. It denies that there is a soul that could survive or a second life to come. Its appeal is that it follows the evidence of neuroscience: damage to the brain alters memory, personality and consciousness, which suggests the mind depends on it. Many who hold it find it calming rather than bleak. Think of a candle flame: when the candle burns out, the flame does not go somewhere else; it stops.","example":"Hume, dying in 1776, was visited by James Boswell, who was astonished to find him cheerful and untroubled at the thought of annihilation. Hume compared his nonexistence after death to his nonexistence before birth.","history":"Epicurus and Lucretius held that the soul is made of atoms that scatter at death. The Charvaka (Lokayata) school in India denied any afterlife. David Hume's essay Of the Immortality of the Soul (published 1777) argued against survival. Bertrand Russell's What I Believe (1925) stated that he expected to rot when he died.","misread":"Annihilation is a view about what happens, not a view that life is meaningless; most who hold it think this life is all the more worth living.","variants":["Materialism: the mind is the brain","Epicurean atomism: the soul's atoms disperse at death"],"reading":[{"title":"Death (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"},{"title":"Dualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/"}]},"l5|soul":{"summary":"The immortal soul: a nonphysical soul, the real you, survives the death of the body and lives on without it.","idea":"This view holds that a person is, or has, a soul distinct from the body. The body dies, but the soul continues, conscious and personal. It denies that the mind is just the brain. Its appeal includes religious teaching, reports of near-death experiences, and philosophical arguments that consciousness cannot be fully explained physically. In many African traditions, as John Mbiti described them, the dead live on as the “living-dead”, close to their families while remembered by name. Think of a pilot leaving a plane after landing: the plane is grounded, but the pilot walks away.","example":"Descartes's argument from doubt: I can doubt that I have a body, but not that I am thinking. So I am a thinking thing distinct from my body, and could exist without it. Critics reply that being able to imagine this does not show it is possible.","history":"Plato's Phaedo (fourth century BCE) gave arguments for the soul's immortality. René Descartes's Meditations on First Philosophy (1641) defended mind and body as distinct substances. Ibn Sina (Avicenna, 980 to 1037) offered the flying man argument. John Mbiti's African Religions and Philosophy (1969) describes the living-dead.","misread":"Belief in an immortal soul is not the same as belief in resurrection; Christianity, Islam and Judaism traditionally teach that the body is raised too.","variants":["Platonic dualism: the soul is the true self","Cartesian dualism: mind and body are distinct substances","African ancestor traditions: the living-dead"],"reading":[{"title":"Dualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/"},{"title":"Ancient Theories of Soul (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ancient-soul/"}]},"l5|resur":{"summary":"Resurrection: after death you will be raised again, body and soul, at the end of time.","idea":"This view holds that God will raise the dead, giving each person a renewed body, at the end of history. It is the traditional teaching of Christianity, Islam and much of Judaism. Many believers hold that the soul survives in between, others that the person sleeps until then. It denies both annihilation and the idea that a disembodied soul is the final state: a human being is a union of body and soul, and full life requires both. Its appeal combines faith with the conviction that bodies matter. Think of a house restored after a fire, rebuilt and made new, not just its memory kept.","example":"Peter van Inwagen's puzzle about resurrection: if a body has decayed and its atoms are scattered, how could a raised body be the same person? He suggested God might preserve the body or its core in some way, showing the problem is about identity, not power.","history":"Paul's First Letter to the Corinthians (chapter 15) and the Qur'an teach bodily resurrection. Maimonides (1138 to 1204) listed resurrection among his Thirteen Principles. Thomas Aquinas held that the soul is the form of the body and awaits reunion. Peter van Inwagen's The Possibility of Resurrection (1978) is a modern classic.","misread":"Resurrection is not the same as the soul simply living on; it insists that the whole person, including a body, is restored.","variants":["Soul-and-body view: the soul survives until resurrection (Aquinas)","Soul-sleep or materialist view: the person is recreated"],"reading":[{"title":"Heaven and Hell in Christian Thought (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/heaven-hell/"}]},"l5|rebirth":{"summary":"Rebirth: after death you are reborn into another life, again and again, until you are freed from the cycle.","idea":"This view, central to Hindu, Buddhist and Jain traditions, holds that death leads to a new birth, shaped by karma, the consequences of one's past actions. The cycle, samsara, continues until liberation: moksha in Hinduism and Jainism, nirvana in Buddhism. It denies that this life is the only one. Traditions differ on what passes from life to life: an eternal self (atman) in most Hindu schools, a karmic soul (jiva) in Jainism, or a causal stream without a fixed self in Buddhism. Its appeal includes moral fairness: good and bad deeds bear fruit. Think of a flame passed from candle to candle.","example":"The Bhagavad Gita compares rebirth to changing clothes: as a person casts off worn-out garments and puts on new ones, so the self casts off worn-out bodies and takes on new ones.","history":"The early Upanishads (from around the seventh century BCE onward) contain early teachings on rebirth and karma. The Bhagavad Gita, composed in the last centuries BCE, develops them. The Buddha (c. fifth century BCE) taught rebirth without a lasting self. Mahavira (around the sixth or fifth century BCE) shaped Jain teaching.","misread":"Rebirth is usually seen in these traditions as something to escape, not a reward; the goal is liberation from the cycle.","variants":["Hindu reincarnation of the atman","Buddhist rebirth without a self","Jain transmigration of the jiva"],"reading":[{"title":"Hindu Philosophy (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/hindu-ph/"},{"title":"Buddha (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/buddha/"}]},"l5|merge":{"summary":"Return to the whole: the separate self dissolves back into the ultimate reality it was always part of.","idea":"This view, most fully developed in Advaita Vedanta, holds that the individual self (atman) is identical with ultimate reality (Brahman). The sense of being a separate person is a kind of ignorance. When liberation comes, after rebirths end, there is no individual left over, only the one reality that always was. It denies that the self is a separate thing with its own fate. Its appeal is a profound sense of unity and freedom from fear. Think of a wave on the ocean: when it subsides, nothing is lost; the water simply is the ocean again, as it always was.","example":"In the Chandogya Upanishad, Uddalaka asks his son Shvetaketu to dissolve salt in water. The salt cannot be seen, but every sip tastes of it. So the ultimate reality pervades everything, and the teaching ends: you are that.","history":"The Upanishads contain the teaching, notably the Chandogya Upanishad's “tat tvam asi”. Shankara (traditionally eighth century CE) systematised Advaita Vedanta. Similar ideas appear in some forms of Western mysticism and in Spinoza's single substance, though with differences. The Advaita teacher Ramana Maharshi (1879 to 1950) brought it to many modern readers.","misread":"Return to the whole is not simple annihilation; Advaita holds that what remains is pure being and consciousness, not nothing.","variants":["Advaita Vedanta: atman is Brahman","Mystical union traditions"],"reading":[{"title":"Advaita Vedanta (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/advaita-vedanta/"},{"title":"Hindu Philosophy (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/hindu-ph/"}]},"l5|unsure":{"summary":"Agnosticism about the afterlife: no one can know what happens at death, and the evidence does not favour either side.","idea":"This view holds that the question of life after death lies beyond what we can know. Arguments for survival and against it both fall short; experience gives no clear answer. It denies both confident belief and confident disbelief. Its appeal is intellectual humility: it refuses to claim more than the evidence supports. In Confucian thought, it also turns attention back to how we live now. Think of trying to describe a country no traveller has returned from: you may have hopes and fears, but no map.","example":"Confucius, asked about death by his student Zilu, replied that until we understand life, we cannot understand death. The Analects use such answers to turn questions about the unknown back toward ethical life here and now.","history":"Confucius (551 to 479 BCE) set aside questions about death in Analects book 11. Thomas Henry Huxley coined the word agnostic in 1869. Protagoras (fifth century BCE) said he could not know whether the gods exist. Many modern scientists and philosophers adopt agnosticism specifically about survival.","misread":"Agnosticism is not indecision or not caring; it is a considered judgement that the evidence does not settle the question.","variants":["Confucian focus on this life","Evidential agnosticism: the evidence is balanced"],"reading":[{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"},{"title":"Confucius (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confucius/"}]},"l7|yes":{"summary":"Immortality is desirable: an endless healthy life would always hold more to learn, love and do.","idea":"This view holds that living forever, healthy and with those you love, would be good. There would always be new things to discover, new skills, new friendships, and many pleasures can be enjoyed again and again without growing stale. It denies Bernard Williams's claim that endless life must become tedious. Its appeal is that most people, asked whether they would like one more good year, say yes, and it is hard to see where the series should stop. Think of a favourite meal or piece of music: you do not tire of it forever just because you have had it before.","example":"John Martin Fischer's repeatable pleasures: a good meal, sex, music or a walk in the woods can be enjoyed again after a break. Unlike a puzzle solved once, they do not run out. An immortal life could be woven around such goods.","history":"John Martin Fischer's essay Why Immortality Is Not So Bad (1994) and his book Death, Immortality, and Meaning in Life (2020) are leading defences. Many religious traditions describe an endless blessed life as the highest good. Transhumanists such as Nick Bostrom argue that radical life extension would be good.","misread":"Defenders do not claim immortality would be good in any form; they mean healthy, free and chosen, unlike the endlessly ageing struldbruggs in Gulliver's Travels.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Death (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"}]},"l7|longer":{"summary":"Longer, not endless: a much longer life of centuries would be good, but living forever would not be.","idea":"This view splits the difference. Our lives are too short: most people die with much still to learn, love and do. Centuries more would be welcome. But forever is different: eventually even a rich life might run out of new things that matter, or lose shape. It denies both that immortality is ideal and that our current lifespan is the right length. Its appeal is that it takes both the tedium worry and the deprivation of early death seriously. Think of a long holiday: two weeks may be too short, two months wonderful, but a holiday that never ends stops being a holiday.","example":"Elina Makropulos in Čapek's play is a case for this view: her first centuries were full and eager; only after more than three hundred years did life become empty. A few extra centuries, on this view, would have been a gift.","history":"Bernard Williams's The Makropulos Case (1973) allowed that death can come too soon even if immortality is undesirable. Shelly Kagan's Death (2012) argues that immortality would not be good, while allowing that many of us die too early. Life extension research gives the question practical bite.","misread":"This view does not set a fixed ideal lifespan; it says only that the best life is longer than ours and shorter than forever.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Death (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"}]},"l7|bored":{"summary":"The tedium of immortality: an endless life would eventually run out of things that matter to you and become empty.","idea":"Bernard Williams argued that a person is held together by categorical desires: deep commitments that give reasons to go on living. Over an endless life, either these desires would be satisfied and exhausted, leaving boredom, or your character would change so much that the future person would not really be you. Either way, immortality would not be good for you. It denies that more life is always better. Its appeal is psychological realism. Think of someone who loved chess as a child but, after a thousand years of playing, finds it as dull as brushing teeth.","example":"The Makropulos case: in Karel Čapek's play The Makropulos Affair (1922), Elina Makropulos has lived 342 years. Everything has become cold and pointless to her, and she refuses the elixir that would extend her life.","history":"Bernard Williams's The Makropulos Case: Reflections on the Tedium of Immortality (1973) is the source. Simone de Beauvoir's novel All Men Are Mortal (1946) portrays an immortal man whose life loses all meaning. Jorge Luis Borges's story The Immortal (1947) explores similar themes.","misread":"Williams did not argue that death is good at any age; he thought death can be a great loss while immortality would still be undesirable.","variants":["Boredom horn: categorical desires are exhausted","Identity horn: one changes until one is no longer oneself"],"reading":[{"title":"Death (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"}]},"l7|precious":{"summary":"Mortality gives value: without an end, nothing would be urgent or precious, because there would always be time.","idea":"This view says limits are part of what makes things matter. Because time is scarce, we must choose, and choices carry weight. Courage requires risk; generosity requires giving up what we could have kept. Without an end, nothing would be urgent, and many human values might lose their point. It denies that removing death would simply add more good. Its appeal is that it explains why moments feel precious: a last conversation, a final summer before leaving home. Think of a sunset you know you will see only once compared with one repeated every hour forever.","example":"In the Odyssey, the goddess Calypso offers Odysseus immortality and eternal youth if he stays with her. He refuses, choosing to return to his mortal wife Penelope and his home. The story is often read as a choice of a mortal life's meaning over endless life.","history":"Homer's Odyssey offers the ancient image. Martha Nussbaum's essay Mortal Immortals (1989) argued that many human virtues presuppose mortality. Samuel Scheffler's Death and the Afterlife (2013) argued that our values depend on our lives being temporally bounded. Heidegger linked authenticity to finitude.","misread":"This view does not say death is good or that people should welcome it early; it says finitude shapes value, and long lives can still be precious.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Death (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"},{"title":"Martin Heidegger (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/heidegger/"}]},"l8|good":{"summary":"Existence can benefit: if a person's life goes well overall, being born was good for them.","idea":"This view holds that bringing someone into a good life benefits them. Their life contains joys, love and achievement, and these are good for the person who has them. It denies that the absence of a person before birth makes benefit impossible: we do not need a prior “someone” to compare with, only the good life itself. Its appeal is that it matches ordinary gratitude: many people are glad they exist and thank their parents for life. Think of a guest at a wonderful party: it was good for him to be invited, even though, had he not been, he would not have missed it.","example":"Imagine a person who, at 80, reflects that her life was full of love and meaning, despite some hard times. It seems natural for her to say existence was good for her. Defenders say her judgement is not a confusion but a straightforward truth.","history":"Derek Parfit's Reasons and Persons (1984) discussed whether causing someone to exist can benefit them. Nils Holtug and Melinda Roberts have defended the possibility of benefit through existence. The view is central to replies to David Benatar and to debates on the nonidentity problem.","misread":"Saying existence can benefit does not mean people have a duty to have children; the view is about benefit, not obligation.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"The Nonidentity Problem (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nonidentity-problem/"},{"title":"Parenthood and Procreation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/parenthood/"}]},"l8|neutral":{"summary":"Procreative neutrality: being born is neither good nor bad for the child, since before existing there was no one to compare.","idea":"This view says benefit and harm need comparison: better or worse than what? Before conception there was no one at all, so being born cannot make someone better off than they were. A person may have a good life, but being brought into it is neither a benefit nor a harm to them. It denies both that existence is a gift and that it is a harm. Its appeal is logical tidiness and a refusal of both cheerful and gloomy extremes. Think of asking whether being born made you taller than you would otherwise have been: there was no “otherwise” for you.","example":"Jan Narveson's slogan, from his work on population ethics: we are in favour of making people happy, but neutral about making happy people. Adding a happy person to the world, on this view, is not a favour to that person.","history":"Jan Narveson defended the neutrality intuition in articles from the late 1960s and early 1970s, including Moral Problems of Population (1973). John Broome and other population ethicists have analysed it. Derek Parfit's Reasons and Persons (1984) discusses its strengths and difficulties.","misread":"Neutrality about birth does not mean the child's later life does not matter; it says only that being created is not a benefit or harm compared with not existing.","variants":["Person-affecting view: only effects on existing or future people count"],"reading":[{"title":"The Nonidentity Problem (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nonidentity-problem/"},{"title":"Parenthood and Procreation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/parenthood/"}]},"l8|pessim":{"summary":"Pessimism about life: most lives contain more suffering than good, so starting one is usually a bad bet for the child.","idea":"This view looks at the facts of human life: illness, grief, frustration, ageing and death. It argues that people systematically overrate how well their lives go, thanks to optimism bias and forgetting pain. Taking these into account, most lives may contain more bad than good. If so, starting a life is usually not good for the one who lives it. It denies that ordinary lives are usually net positive. Its appeal is its refusal of comforting illusion. It is a judgement about starting lives, not about whether lives already underway are worth continuing, which Benatar and others insist is a separate and different question.","example":"Benatar's Pollyanna principle: psychologists find people remember good experiences more than bad ones and rate their lives above average. Benatar argues that this bias makes our self-assessments unreliable guides to how good lives really are.","history":"The chorus in Sophocles' Oedipus at Colonus (401 BCE) says not to be born is best. Arthur Schopenhauer's The World as Will and Representation (1818, expanded 1844) argued that suffering outweighs satisfaction. David Benatar's Better Never to Have Been (2006) argued that people overestimate the quality of their lives.","misread":"Pessimism about starting lives is not a claim that existing people's lives are not worth living on; Benatar explicitly separates the two questions.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Anti-Natalism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/anti-natalism/"},{"title":"Arthur Schopenhauer (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/schopenhauer/"}]},"l8|harm":{"summary":"Anti-natalism: being born is always a harm, however good the life, because its pains are real while the unborn miss nothing.","idea":"David Benatar's anti-natalism rests on an asymmetry: the absence of pain is good even if no one enjoys it, but the absence of pleasure is not bad unless someone is deprived. So never existing has only advantages, while existing has pains as well as pleasures. Every birth therefore includes harm. The view concerns starting lives, not continuing them. Benatar insists that a life not worth starting can still be well worth continuing: those who already exist have interests in going on living, and nothing in his argument tells anyone to give up their life. It denies that a good life makes being born a benefit.","example":"Benatar's asymmetry: if a child who would suffer terribly is never born, that is good; if a happy child is never born, that is not bad, since no one misses out. Benatar argues that this lopsidedness, which many people already accept, implies that coming into existence is always a harm.","history":"David Benatar's Better Never to Have Been (2006) is the main source; The Human Predicament (2017) adds his views on meaning and death, defending the line between starting and continuing a life. Earlier, Arthur Schopenhauer and Peter Wessel Zapffe, in The Last Messiah (1933), expressed related ideas. Critics include Elizabeth Harman and David DeGrazia.","misread":"Anti-natalism is about whether to start new lives, not whether existing lives are worth living; Benatar holds that continuing a life can be well worthwhile.","variants":["Asymmetry argument (Benatar)","Misanthropic argument: humans cause great harm (Benatar's later work)","Pessimist argument: lives are worse than people think"],"reading":[{"title":"Anti-Natalism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/anti-natalism/"},{"title":"Parenthood and Procreation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/parenthood/"}]},"l6|remember":{"summary":"Memento mori: keep death in mind every day, so you stop wasting time on what does not matter and grow ready to accept it.","idea":"Memento mori, Latin for “remember you must die”, holds that regularly reflecting on death improves life. It cuts through trivial worries, sharpens priorities, and over time reduces fear, because what is familiar is less frightening. It denies that ignoring death is the path to a good life. Its appeal is practical: people facing serious illness often report that their priorities became clearer. The point is not gloom but focus and gratitude. Think of a friend leaving for a long journey: knowing it is your last evening together, you put your phone away and really talk.","example":"Seneca's letters to Lucilius advise living each day as though it might be the last, so that nothing is postponed and nothing is left undone. Marcus Aurelius's Meditations returns again and again to his own death to keep his attention on what matters.","history":"Plato's Phaedo presents philosophy as practice for dying. The Stoics Seneca (c. 4 BCE to 65 CE) and Marcus Aurelius (121 to 180) made reflection on mortality a daily exercise. Montaigne's essay That to Philosophise Is to Learn to Die (1580) took up the theme. Buddhist practice includes meditation on death (maranasati).","misread":"Memento mori is not morbid brooding or a wish for death; it is a discipline for living with more attention and less fear.","variants":["Stoic daily reflection","Buddhist maranasati: mindfulness of death"],"reading":[{"title":"Stoicism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/stoicism/"},{"title":"Seneca (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/seneca/"}]},"l6|calm":{"summary":"Epicurean calm: understand that death is nothing to you, and fear of it will fall away, freeing you to enjoy life.","idea":"The Epicurean strategy is to reason your way out of fear. Once you see that being dead cannot harm you, since there will be no you to harm, the dread loses its grip. Epicurus thought fear of death and of the gods were the chief sources of human anxiety, and that philosophy could cure them. It denies that fear of death is natural and unavoidable. Its appeal is that it promises freedom without requiring belief in an afterlife. Think of a child afraid of the dark who, once shown the room is empty, can finally sleep.","example":"The Epicurean four-part cure (tetrapharmakos), preserved by Philodemus: God is nothing to fear, death is nothing to worry about, the good is easy to get, and the terrible is easy to endure.","history":"Epicurus (341 to 270 BCE) taught this in the Letter to Menoeceus and the Principal Doctrines. Lucretius (first century BCE) turned it into poetry in On the Nature of Things. Philodemus of Gadara (first century BCE) preserved the four-part cure. Martha Nussbaum's The Therapy of Desire (1994) analyses it.","misread":"Epicurean calm does not mean not caring about one's life; Epicurus valued life's pleasures greatly and thought fear spoiled them.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Epicurus (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epicurus/"},{"title":"Epicurus (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/epicur/"}]},"l6|accept":{"summary":"Acceptance and non-attachment: everything, including you, is always changing, and fear of death comes from clinging to what must pass.","idea":"This view, found in Buddhism and in Daoism, says suffering about death comes from grasping: wanting to hold on to a self, a body and a life that are by nature impermanent. Seeing change clearly loosens the grip, and with it the fear. It denies that the problem is death itself; the problem is attachment. Its appeal is that it offers a path, through meditation and practice, rather than an argument. Think of holding sand: the tighter you squeeze, the faster it runs out; open your hand and you can hold it calmly for as long as it stays.","example":"The Buddhist story of Kisa Gotami: grieving her dead son, she asks the Buddha for medicine. He sends her to fetch mustard seeds from a house where no one has died. Finding none, she understands that death touches every family and lets go.","history":"The Buddha (c. fifth century BCE) taught impermanence and non-attachment. The Zhuangzi (fourth to third centuries BCE) presents life and death as transformations. The Japanese Zen master Dogen (1200 to 1253), in the Shobogenzo fascicle Birth and Death, taught that birth and death are themselves the life of the Buddha.","misread":"Non-attachment is not coldness or not loving others; Buddhist teachers pair it with compassion and say it makes love less possessive.","variants":["Buddhist non-attachment","Daoist acceptance of transformation","Zen: birth and death as they are (Dogen)"],"reading":[{"title":"Buddha (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/buddha/"},{"title":"Zhuangzi (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/zhuangzi/"}]},"l6|own":{"summary":"Being-toward-death: facing your own death squarely frees you from living as everyone else does, so you can make your life your own.","idea":"Martin Heidegger argued that most of the time we live in the mode of “the They” (das Man): doing what one does, thinking what one thinks. We also treat death as something that happens to people in general. Facing the fact that my death is certain, could come at any time and is mine alone, shakes me out of conformity. It shows my life as a whole and as mine to shape. It denies that the right response is to distract ourselves. Its appeal is its link to authenticity. Think of someone who, after a close call, quits a career chosen to please others.","example":"Tolstoy's The Death of Ivan Ilyich (1886): a judge who lived a conventional, comfortable life realises, while dying, that he never truly lived it as his own. Heidegger cited the story in Being and Time as an illustration of evasion of death.","history":"Martin Heidegger's Being and Time (1927), Division Two, develops being-toward-death and authenticity. Søren Kierkegaard (1813 to 1855) earlier stressed facing one's own death as an individual. Jean-Paul Sartre in Being and Nothingness (1943) disagreed, arguing death is absurd and cannot give life meaning.","misread":"Being-toward-death does not mean thinking about death constantly or wishing for it; it means owning your life in light of its finitude.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Martin Heidegger (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/heidegger/"},{"title":"Existentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/existentialism/"}]},"l6|life":{"summary":"Focus on life: live well and let death take care of itself, since wisdom lies in attending to life, not death.","idea":"This view holds that dwelling on death, whether in fear or in meditation, distracts from what matters: living well now. A good life is the best preparation for its end, and brooding adds nothing. It denies that facing death is the key to wisdom. Its appeal is simplicity and health: attention goes to relationships, work and the present. Think of a gardener who tends her plants daily without worrying about the frost that will come eventually: the garden flourishes because her attention is on it.","example":"Spinoza's Ethics: the free person thinks of nothing less than of death, and their wisdom is a meditation on life. Confucius similarly turned questions about death back to how we should live.","history":"Baruch Spinoza's Ethics (1677), Part IV, Proposition 67, states the view. Confucius (551 to 479 BCE) set aside questions about death in Analects book 11. Epicurus, too, urged attention to the pleasures of the present life rather than dread of its end. Many modern secular writers adopt a similar stance.","misread":"Focusing on life does not mean denying death or refusing to plan for it; it means not letting death set the agenda.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Baruch Spinoza (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spinoza/"},{"title":"Confucius (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confucius/"}]},"l6|hope":{"summary":"Hope beyond death: how you live now shapes what comes after death, so live in preparation for it.","idea":"This view, common to many religions, holds that this life is not the whole story. What we do now has consequences beyond death: judgement, reward, purification, or the karma that shapes rebirth. Living well is partly preparation for what comes next. It denies that death is a final full stop. Its appeal is that it gives present choices an ultimate significance and offers hope for justice and reunion with loved ones. Think of a student studying for an exam: the effort makes sense because of what follows.","example":"In the ancient Egyptian Book of the Dead, the heart of the dead person is weighed against the feather of Maat, truth and justice. A heart heavy with wrongdoing fails; a light one passes into the afterlife. Many later traditions share this picture of life as preparation.","history":"The Egyptian Book of the Dead dates from the New Kingdom (from about 1550 BCE). Plato's Phaedo ends with a myth of judgement after death. Christianity, Islam and Judaism teach judgement; Hindu, Buddhist and Jain traditions teach karma shaping future lives. Augustine and Aquinas developed Christian accounts.","misread":"Hope beyond death does not mean neglecting this life; most traditions hold that how you love and act now is exactly what matters.","variants":["Judgement traditions (Christianity, Islam, Judaism)","Karma traditions (Hinduism, Buddhism, Jainism)"],"reading":[{"title":"Heaven and Hell in Christian Thought (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/heaven-hell/"},{"title":"Hindu Philosophy (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/hindu-ph/"}]},"l6|resist":{"summary":"Resistance to death: death is an enemy, and we should work, through medicine and science, to defeat it.","idea":"This view refuses to make peace with death. Death destroys persons, their relationships and everything they might have become; to call that natural or acceptable is to excuse a great evil. We should fight it, as we fight disease. Today this often means supporting research into ageing and life extension. It denies that acceptance is wisdom. Its appeal is moral urgency: we already fight cancer and heart disease; why stop at ageing itself? Think of how people once accepted smallpox as part of life until a vaccine ended it.","example":"Nick Bostrom's The Fable of the Dragon-Tyrant (2005): a dragon demands thousands of people each day, and the kingdom grows used to it, even calling it natural, until people realise it can be killed. The dragon stands for ageing.","history":"The Epic of Gilgamesh (from around 2100 BCE in early versions) tells of a king's quest for immortality. Daoist alchemists sought physical immortality. Modern transhumanists, including Nick Bostrom, whose Fable of the Dragon-Tyrant appeared in the Journal of Medical Ethics in 2005, argue for defeating ageing.","misread":"Resisting death is not denying it or fearing it more; it treats death as a problem to solve rather than a fate to accept.","variants":["Transhumanism: technology to overcome ageing","Religious defiance: death as the last enemy"],"reading":[{"title":"Death (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"}]},"m1|yes":{"summary":"The mind is entirely a matter of physical processes in the brain and body; once those are fixed, everything mental is fixed too.","idea":"Physicalism says everything that exists is physical or fixed by the physical, minds included. Thinking, feeling and deciding are things a brain does, just as pumping is something a heart does. It denies that there is any soul or extra mental ingredient. Its appeal is simplicity and a strong track record: science keeps explaining things once thought mysterious, like life and heredity, in physical terms. Everyday case: a strong coffee makes you alert and a concussion can wipe out memories, which is just what you would expect if the mind is the working brain.","example":"The supervenience test: imagine a second world identical to ours in every physical detail, down to the last particle. If physicalism is true, it must also be identical in every mental detail, so your twin there could not feel even slightly differently from you. No difference in mind without a difference in matter.","history":"Ancient atomists such as Democritus (5th century BCE) and the Indian Cārvāka school already held that mind arises from matter. Thomas Hobbes defended materialism in Leviathan (1651). The modern form grew in the 20th century with Herbert Feigl, J.J.C. Smart’s “Sensations and Brain Processes” (1959) and David Lewis. In recent surveys of professional philosophers it is the most popular view of the mind.","misread":"Physicalism does not say feelings are unreal or unimportant; it says they are real and are physical processes.","variants":["Reductive physicalism: each kind of mental state is identical to a kind of physical state","Non-reductive physicalism: mental states depend on physical states without being identical to any one kind of them"],"reading":[{"title":"Physicalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/physicalism/"}]},"m1|no":{"summary":"At least some features of the mind, especially how experiences feel, are not fully physical and cannot be fully explained by brain science.","idea":"Non-physicalists say a complete physical description of the world would leave something out: the felt quality of experience, often called “qualia” or “what it is like”. It denies that physics and neuroscience, however advanced, could capture everything about minds. The family includes dualists, panpsychists, idealists and neutral monists, who disagree about what the missing ingredient is. The appeal starts from a simple thought: you could know every fact about how a brain processes red light and still not know what seeing red is like, just as reading a headache’s neural signals is not the same as having the headache.","example":"Frank Jackson’s “Mary’s room”: Mary is a brilliant scientist who knows every physical fact about colour vision but has lived her whole life in a black and white room. When she walks out and sees a ripe tomato, does she learn something new? If so, the physical facts were not all the facts.","history":"Plato’s Phaedo argued that the soul is distinct from the body, and René Descartes made the modern case in his Meditations (1641). In the 20th century Thomas Nagel’s “What Is It Like to Be a Bat?” (1974), Frank Jackson’s “Epiphenomenal Qualia” (1982) and David Chalmers’ The Conscious Mind (1996) revived it. Jackson later changed his mind and became a physicalist.","misread":"Rejecting physicalism does not mean rejecting science; most non-physicalists accept all of neuroscience and argue only that it is incomplete.","variants":["Dualism: mind and matter are two different basic kinds of thing or property","Panpsychism: experience is a basic feature of all matter","Idealism: reality is fundamentally mental"],"reading":[{"title":"Dualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/"},{"title":"Qualia: The Knowledge Argument (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/qualia-knowledge/"}]},"m1|unclear":{"summary":"The mind-body question has no clear answer because nobody can define “physical” in a way that makes physicalism both meaningful and plausible.","idea":"This view holds that the debate between physicalists and their rivals rests on a word nobody can pin down. If “physical” means whatever current physics describes, physicalism is probably false, since physics is incomplete and keeps changing. If it means whatever an ideal future physics would describe, the claim becomes nearly empty: whatever minds turn out to be, that physics would include it. So there is no sharp yes-or-no fact to settle. Its appeal is honesty about vague terms. Compare arguing whether a hot dog is a sandwich: once you know everything about it, the fight is only about the word.","example":"Hempel’s dilemma, named after Carl Hempel: physicalism defined by today’s physics is likely false, and physicalism defined by future physics is trivial or unknowable. Noam Chomsky made a related point: since Newton accepted forces acting at a distance, we have had no fixed idea of “body” to contrast the mind with.","history":"Carl Hempel posed the dilemma in papers from 1969 and 1980. Noam Chomsky argued in New Horizons in the Study of Language and Mind (2000) that without a clear concept of body the mind-body problem cannot even be stated. Bas van Fraassen, in The Empirical Stance (2002), treated materialism as a stance rather than a factual claim. Wittgenstein-inspired quietists treat many philosophical problems as confusions of language.","misread":"This quietism does not say the mind is mysterious; it says the question as posed lacks clear content, so neither side has said something definite.","variants":["Hempel’s dilemma: neither current nor future physics gives a usable definition of “physical”","Chomsky’s view: there is no settled concept of body, so no well-posed mind-body problem","Materialism as a stance (van Fraassen): an attitude toward inquiry, not a factual claim"],"reading":[{"title":"Physicalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/physicalism/"}]},"m1a|identity":{"summary":"Each type of mental state, like pain, is literally identical to a type of brain process, a discovery like lightning turning out to be electricity.","idea":"The identity theory says mental states are brain states, not merely caused by or correlated with them. Pain is a certain pattern of neural activity in the way that water is H2O. It denies that there are two things to be linked; there is one thing described in two vocabularies, everyday and scientific. Its appeal: it explains why mind and brain change together so closely, and it avoids the puzzle of how something non-physical could move a body. Everyday case: when a painkiller blocks certain nerve signals and the pain goes away, the identity theorist says nothing extra disappeared.","example":"“Pain is C-fibre firing” became the stock philosophers’ slogan for this view. It was only a placeholder for whatever the true neural story turns out to be, chosen to show the form of the claim: one kind of mental state, one kind of brain process, discovered to be the same thing.","history":"U.T. Place’s “Is Consciousness a Brain Process?” (1956), Herbert Feigl’s “The ‘Mental’ and the ‘Physical’” (1958) and J.J.C. Smart’s “Sensations and Brain Processes” (1959) founded the view; Place and Smart worked at Adelaide, so it was nicknamed “Australian materialism”. D.M. Armstrong’s A Materialist Theory of the Mind (1968) and David Lewis developed it.","misread":"Identity theorists never claimed pain science was finished; “C-fibre firing” was a placeholder for whatever brain process neuroscience actually finds.","variants":["Type identity: each kind of mental state is one kind of brain state","Token identity: each particular mental event is some physical event, though the kinds may not line up"],"reading":[{"title":"The Mind/Brain Identity Theory (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mind-identity/"}]},"m1a|function":{"summary":"A mental state is defined by its job: what causes it, what it causes, and how it links with other states, whatever its material.","idea":"Functionalism says what makes something a pain is the role it plays, not the material it is made of. Pain is the state that damage causes and that makes you want it to stop, protect the hurt part and say “ouch”. It denies that minds require neurons in particular; silicon or some alien chemistry could, in principle, have the same states if organised the right way. Its appeal: it lets an octopus, whose nervous system is very different from ours, feel pain too. Everyday case: a mousetrap can be built from wood, plastic or wire; what makes it a mousetrap is what it does.","example":"Hilary Putnam’s “multiple realizability”: humans, octopuses and perhaps Martians may all feel pain with very different hardware, so pain cannot be one specific brain state. The famous challenge is Ned Block’s “China brain”: if everyone in China simulated one neuron by radio, would the nation as a whole feel pain?","history":"Hilary Putnam proposed machine functionalism in 1960s papers, especially “Psychological Predicates” (1967). Jerry Fodor, Sydney Shoemaker and David Lewis developed other versions. Ned Block’s “Troubles with Functionalism” (1978) pressed the main objections. Putnam himself later rejected the view in Representation and Reality (1988). Functionalism became the default framework of cognitive science.","misread":"Functionalism is not behaviourism: the defining roles include inner states causing other inner states, not just outward behaviour.","variants":["Machine functionalism: mental states are states of an abstract machine table (Putnam)","Analytic functionalism: the roles are fixed by everyday talk about the mind (Lewis)","Psychofunctionalism: the roles are fixed by scientific psychology (Fodor)"],"reading":[{"title":"Functionalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/functionalism/"}]},"m1a|folk":{"summary":"Everyday talk of beliefs and desires is a flawed theory that mature brain science will replace rather than explain.","idea":"Eliminative materialism says “folk psychology”, our ordinary explanations in terms of beliefs, desires and intentions, is a theory like any other, and probably a false one. If so, there may be no beliefs or desires as we picture them, just as there turned out to be no phlogiston and no witches. It denies that neuroscience will find a neat brain counterpart for each belief. Its appeal: folk psychology has barely changed in thousands of years and explains little about sleep, memory or mental illness. Everyday case: people once blamed gloomy moods on “black bile”; perhaps “belief” will go the same way.","example":"The fate of phlogiston: chemists once explained burning by a substance released in fire. Lavoisier’s oxygen theory did not identify phlogiston with anything; it simply dropped it. Paul Churchland predicts the same for “belief” and “desire” once neuroscience offers better categories.","history":"Early versions came from Paul Feyerabend and Richard Rorty in the 1960s; Rorty’s “Mind-Body Identity, Privacy, and Categories” (1965) imagined people who talk only of brain states. Paul Churchland’s “Eliminative Materialism and the Propositional Attitudes” (1981) gave the classic statement, and Patricia Churchland’s Neurophilosophy (1986) extended it. Stephen Stich’s From Folk Psychology to Cognitive Science (1983) raised related doubts.","misread":"Eliminativists do not deny that people act, think or feel in some sense; they deny that the folk categories correctly describe what is going on inside.","variants":["Propositional attitude eliminativism: there are no beliefs or desires as folk psychology pictures them (the Churchlands)","Qualia eliminativism: there are no qualia (Dennett, “Quining Qualia”, 1988)"],"reading":[{"title":"Eliminative Materialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/materialism-eliminative/"}]},"m1a|elim":{"summary":"Consciousness seems to have private inner qualities beyond brain processing, but that appearance is an illusion produced by the brain’s model of itself.","idea":"Illusionism accepts that we have experiences in a functional sense: we notice, react and report. What it denies is “phenomenal consciousness”, special inner qualities over and above all that processing. The brain represents its own states in a simplified way, and this makes it seem there is an inner glow. The illusionist’s task then becomes the “illusion problem”: explaining why it seems that way. Its appeal: it keeps physicalism and turns the hard problem into a scientific one. Everyday case: in a magic trick, what needs explaining is why the coin seems to vanish, not where it went.","example":"Change blindness: people shown two nearly identical pictures, flickering back and forth, often fail to spot large differences, such as a missing building. This suggests our visual experience is much less rich than it seems. Illusionists use such findings to argue that our confident beliefs about inner experience are unreliable.","history":"Daniel Dennett’s “Quining Qualia” (1988) and Consciousness Explained (1991) laid the groundwork; Dennett called consciousness a “user illusion”. Keith Frankish named and defended “illusionism” in a 2016 special issue of the Journal of Consciousness Studies. Nicholas Humphrey and Michael Graziano, with his attention schema theory, defend related views.","misread":"Illusionism does not say pain does not hurt or that nobody feels anything; it denies only that hurting involves extra inner qualities beyond what the brain does.","variants":["Strong illusionism: phenomenal consciousness does not exist at all (Frankish)","Attention schema theory: the illusion comes from the brain’s simplified model of its own attention (Graziano)"],"reading":[{"title":"Consciousness (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consciousness/"},{"title":"The Hard Problem of Consciousness (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/hard-problem-of-conciousness/"}]},"m1a|mystery":{"summary":"Consciousness is a natural feature of the brain, but human minds may be built in a way that prevents us from ever understanding how.","idea":"Mysterianism says there is a natural, non-magical link between brain and experience, but our concepts may be the wrong shape to grasp it. Colin McGinn calls this “cognitive closure”: every kind of mind has limits, and the brain-experience link may lie beyond ours. It denies both that dualism is needed and that ordinary neuroscience will dissolve the puzzle. Its appeal: it explains why centuries of effort have made so little progress on consciousness without giving up naturalism. Everyday case: a dog can watch you read every day and never grasp what reading is.","example":"McGinn’s two windows: introspection shows us experiences but never neurons, and perception shows us neurons but never experiences. Neither faculty reveals the property that links them, so we may have no way to form the concept that would join the two pictures.","history":"Colin McGinn’s “Can We Solve the Mind-Body Problem?” (1989) launched the view, later expanded in The Mysterious Flame (1999). Owen Flanagan coined the name “new mysterianism” in The Science of the Mind (1991) to criticise it. Noam Chomsky distinguished problems we can solve from mysteries beyond our reach, and Thomas Nagel has voiced related doubts.","misread":"Mysterianism is not dualism and does not call consciousness supernatural; it says the answer is natural but beyond what human minds can grasp.","variants":["Transcendental naturalism (McGinn): the link is natural but cognitively closed to us","Temporary mysterianism: we cannot solve it now but might with new concepts (Nagel’s hope)"],"reading":[{"title":"The Hard Problem of Consciousness (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/hard-problem-of-conciousness/"},{"title":"Consciousness (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consciousness/"}]},"m1b|propdual":{"summary":"There is only one kind of thing, the physical body, but brains have special non-physical properties: the felt qualities of experience.","idea":"Property dualism agrees there is no soul: you are a physical organism. But it says some properties of your brain, such as the hurtfulness of pain or the redness of red as you see it, are not physical properties and cannot be derived from them. These properties arise from the brain, perhaps through basic laws of nature linking physical organisation to experience. Its appeal: it takes consciousness seriously without positing ghostly substances. Everyday case: a song is just vibrations in the air, yet how it sounds to you seems like a further fact that acoustics alone does not capture.","example":"David Chalmers’ “hard problem”: science can explain every function of the brain, such as discriminating colours, focusing attention and reporting states. But even after all of that, it seems open why any of this processing is accompanied by experience at all. Property dualists say that extra fact needs extra properties.","history":"British emergentists such as C.D. Broad, in The Mind and Its Place in Nature (1925), held that new mental properties emerge from complex matter. Thomas Nagel and Frank Jackson’s “Epiphenomenal Qualia” (1982) helped revive the view. David Chalmers’ The Conscious Mind (1996), proposing “naturalistic dualism” with psychophysical laws, is the leading modern statement.","misread":"Property dualism does not posit a soul or a ghost; there is one thing, the brain, with two kinds of properties.","variants":["Emergent property dualism: experience emerges from complex brains (Broad)","Naturalistic dualism: basic psychophysical laws link physical organisation to experience (Chalmers)"],"reading":[{"title":"Dualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/"},{"title":"Consciousness (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consciousness/"}]},"m1b|soul":{"summary":"The mind is a non-physical thing, distinct from the body, which thinks and feels and could in principle exist without the body.","idea":"Substance dualism says there are two basic kinds of thing: physical bodies and non-physical minds or souls. You are, or essentially include, a mind that interacts with your body but is not made of it. It denies that thinking could ever be fully explained by matter. Its appeal: it fits how many people experience themselves, as a single “I” looking out through a body, and makes life after death intelligible. Everyday case: you can easily imagine waking up in a different body, which suggests you are not simply this body.","example":"Avicenna’s “floating man”: imagine a person created in mid-air, eyes covered, limbs apart, touching nothing and sensing nothing. Avicenna argued he would still be aware that he exists, though aware of no body. So the self he is aware of is not the body. Descartes later argued similarly from his ability to doubt the body but not himself.","history":"Plato’s Phaedo argues the soul is separable and immortal. Avicenna (Ibn Sina, 980 to 1037) offered the floating man, and René Descartes gave the classic modern version in his Meditations on First Philosophy (1641). The Indian Nyāya and Sāṃkhya schools also held that selves are distinct from matter. Modern defenders include Richard Swinburne and E.J. Lowe.","misread":"Substance dualists need not deny that the brain matters; most accept that the mind depends heavily on the brain during life.","variants":["Cartesian dualism: mind and body are distinct substances that interact (Descartes)","Emergent substance dualism: the soul arises from the brain but is a new thing (William Hasker)"],"reading":[{"title":"Dualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/"}]},"m1b|form":{"summary":"The soul is the form of the living body, the organisation that makes it a living, thinking human, not a separate thing lodged inside.","idea":"Hylomorphism, from Greek words for “matter” and “form”, says every living thing is matter organised in a certain way, and the soul is that organisation: the form of a body that has life. It denies the picture of a ghost inside a machine, but also denies that a human is just matter, since the form is real and not reducible to the parts. Its appeal: it treats a human as one unified being. Everyday case: a house is bricks plus the arrangement that makes it a house; take away the arrangement and you have a pile of bricks, not a house.","example":"Aristotle’s eye analogy in On the Soul: if an eye were an animal, sight would be its soul. An eye that cannot see is an eye in name only, like an eye in a painting or carved in stone. Likewise, a dead body is no longer a human body in the full sense.","history":"Aristotle (384 to 322 BCE) set out the view in De Anima (On the Soul). Averroes wrote major commentaries on it. Thomas Aquinas (about 1225 to 1274) adapted it in the Summa Theologiae, arguing that the intellect works without a bodily organ, so the human soul can survive death, though incomplete without its body. Today philosophers such as David Oderberg defend it.","misread":"Hylomorphism is not substance dualism with new words: the soul is not a second thing, though Aquinas held the human soul can exist after death.","variants":["Aristotelian hylomorphism: the soul is the form of a living body and, on most readings, does not survive it","Thomistic hylomorphism: the human soul is a form that can subsist after death (Aquinas)"],"reading":[{"title":"Aristotle’s Psychology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-psychology/"},{"title":"Form vs. Matter (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/form-matter/"}]},"m1b|panpsych":{"summary":"Some form of experience is a basic and widespread feature of matter, and complex minds like ours are built out of it.","idea":"Panpsychism says consciousness did not suddenly appear when brains evolved; very simple forms of experience exist at the most basic level of reality, perhaps in electrons or quarks. Brains organise these into the rich experience we have. It denies that experience can arise from wholly non-experiential stuff. Its appeal: it avoids the puzzle of how unfeeling matter could ever produce feeling. Physics tells us what matter does, not what it is in itself; panpsychists suggest it is, at bottom, experiential. Everyday case: a mosaic’s picture appears from many tiny tiles, and the colour was in the tiles all along.","example":"The “combination problem”, first posed by William James in The Principles of Psychology (1890): take twelve people and give each one word of a sentence. However closely they stand together, nobody thereby thinks the whole sentence. How, then, can many tiny experiences add up to one unified mind? Every panpsychist theory must answer this.","history":"Thales is reported by Aristotle to have said that all things are full of gods, and Spinoza and Leibniz held views often read as panpsychist. William James and Alfred North Whitehead developed related ideas. Bertrand Russell’s The Analysis of Matter (1927) inspired the modern revival, led by Galen Strawson’s “Realistic Monism” (2006), Philip Goff’s Galileo’s Error (2019) and David Chalmers.","misread":"Panpsychists do not claim rocks or chairs think or feel; only basic particles, and certain organised wholes like brains, have experience.","variants":["Constitutive panpsychism: our minds are built from micro-experiences","Cosmopsychism: the universe as a whole is conscious and individual minds derive from it","Russellian monism: physics describes structure, and experience is matter’s hidden nature"],"reading":[{"title":"Panpsychism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/panpsychism/"}]},"m1b|idealism":{"summary":"Reality is fundamentally mental: physical objects exist only as experiences or ideas in minds, not as independent stuff.","idea":"Idealism reverses the physicalist picture. Instead of minds being made of matter, matter depends on minds. Tables, stars and brains are patterns in experience; to exist is to be perceived or to be part of some mind. It denies that there is mind-independent material substance behind our experiences. Its appeal: all we ever directly meet is experience, so positing something entirely different behind it seems unnecessary. Everyday case: in a dream, the rooms and furniture seem solid, yet they exist only in experience. Idealists say the waking world is similar, though shared and stable.","example":"The tree in the quad: if to be is to be perceived, does a tree vanish when nobody looks at it? Berkeley answered that God always perceives everything, so the world stays put. A famous limerick by Ronald Knox turns exactly this reply into a joke.","history":"Indian Yogācāra Buddhists such as Vasubandhu (around the 4th to 5th century CE) argued that the experienced world is consciousness only. Advaita Vedānta treats the world as appearance in pure consciousness. George Berkeley defended immaterialism in A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge (1710). Absolute idealists like Hegel and F.H. Bradley followed in the 19th century.","misread":"Idealists do not say the world is imaginary or can be changed by wishing; it is as stable as ever, just mental in nature.","variants":["Subjective idealism: things exist as perceptions in minds, sustained by God (Berkeley)","Absolute idealism: reality is one all-encompassing mind or spirit (Hegel, Bradley)","Yogācāra: only consciousness exists, without external objects (Vasubandhu)"],"reading":[{"title":"Idealism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/idealism/"},{"title":"George Berkeley (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/berkeley/"}]},"m1b|neutral":{"summary":"Mind and matter are two ways of organising one underlying kind of reality that is, in itself, neither mental nor physical.","idea":"Neutral monism says there is one basic kind of stuff (“monism”), but it is neither mental nor physical (“neutral”). Arrange the basic elements one way and you get what we call a mind; arrange them another way and you get what we call a body. It denies both that matter is fundamental, as physicalists say, and that mind is, as idealists say. Its appeal: it closes the gap between mind and matter at the root without favouring either side. Everyday case: the same photos can be sorted into an album about a person or an album about a place.","example":"William James’s room: your experience of a room can be counted as part of your mental history, among your memories and thoughts, and also as part of the physical history of the house. One and the same experience belongs to both series, just as a point can lie on two intersecting lines.","history":"Baruch Spinoza’s Ethics (1677) held that mind and body are two attributes of one substance, a close relative. Ernst Mach, William James in “Does ‘Consciousness’ Exist?” (1904) and Bertrand Russell in The Analysis of Mind (1921) developed neutral monism proper. Today Russellian monism, which shades into panpsychism, carries the idea forward.","misread":"Neutral monism is not physicalism in disguise; the basic stuff is not physical as such, and both mind and matter are built from it.","variants":["Radical empiricism (James): “pure experience” is the neutral stuff","Russell’s neutral monism: events that physics describes only by their structure","Dual-aspect monism: one reality with mental and physical aspects (Spinoza, on some readings)"],"reading":[{"title":"Neutral Monism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/neutral-monism/"}]},"m1c|interact":{"summary":"Mental states, though non-physical, cause physical events in the body, and bodily events cause mental states in turn.","idea":"Interactionism says causation runs both ways between mind and body: a burn causes pain, and pain helps cause you to pull your hand back. It denies that the non-physical mind is a passive spectator. Its appeal is that it fits common sense: you eat because you are hungry and speak because you want to say something. If feelings did nothing, it would be odd that evolution produced them and odd that we can talk about them. Everyday case: you decide to raise your hand in class, and up it goes.","example":"Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia’s challenge to Descartes (1643): how can a soul with no size or location push a body, when pushing seems to require contact? Descartes’ replies did not satisfy her, and her question remains the central objection. A modern version appeals to the “causal closure” of physics: every physical event already has a sufficient physical cause.","history":"Descartes held that mind and body interact, suggesting the pineal gland as the point of contact in The Passions of the Soul (1649). Elisabeth’s letters pressed the problem. In the 20th century Karl Popper and the neuroscientist John Eccles defended interactionism in The Self and Its Brain (1977). Richard Swinburne is a leading recent defender.","misread":"Interactionists need not say the mind breaks physical laws; many argue it acts where physics leaves outcomes open, or that conservation laws do not forbid it.","variants":["Cartesian interactionism: soul and body meet and act on each other in the brain","Quantum interactionism: the mind acts at points of quantum indeterminacy (Eccles)"],"reading":[{"title":"Dualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/"},{"title":"Mental Causation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mental-causation/"}]},"m1c|epiphen":{"summary":"Mental states are caused by brain events but cause nothing themselves; the brain does all the causal work.","idea":"Epiphenomenalism says experiences are by-products of brain activity: real, but causally idle. Physical causes are enough for every movement of your body, and feelings ride along on top. It denies that pain, in its felt quality, ever causes anything physical. Its appeal: it respects the scientific view that the physical world is causally closed while still admitting non-physical experience. Everyday case: a car’s speedometer needle shows how fast you are going, but it does not move the car.","example":"Thomas Huxley’s steam whistle: consciousness is to the body as the whistle is to a locomotive, accompanying its work without influencing its machinery. Critics reply with the “paradox of self-knowledge”: if experiences cause nothing, they could not cause us to talk or write about them, including in books defending epiphenomenalism.","history":"Thomas Henry Huxley’s 1874 lecture “On the Hypothesis that Animals are Automata, and its History” gave the classic statement, and Shadworth Hodgson held a similar view. William James attacked it in The Principles of Psychology (1890) on evolutionary grounds. Frank Jackson’s “Epiphenomenal Qualia” (1982) revived it for the felt qualities of experience.","misread":"Epiphenomenalists do not say experiences are unreal; they are fully real effects, just not causes of physical events.","variants":["Qualia epiphenomenalism: only felt qualities are idle, not beliefs and desires (Jackson, 1982)"],"reading":[{"title":"Epiphenomenalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epiphenomenalism/"}]},"m1c|parallel":{"summary":"Mind and body never act on each other; their events correspond perfectly because they were arranged to match, by God or by their nature.","idea":"Parallelism says mental and physical events run in exact step without either causing the other. When you will to raise your arm, your arm rises, but not because of your will. It denies any interaction across the mind-body divide, which avoids the puzzle of how a soul could push matter. Its appeal: it keeps mind and body fully real and leaves physics undisturbed. Everyday case: a film’s soundtrack matches the picture exactly, yet the sound does not move the actors’ lips.","example":"Leibniz’s two clocks: two clocks could agree in three ways. A physical link could connect them; a craftsman could keep adjusting them; or they could be made so perfectly that they agree on their own. The first is interaction, the second occasionalism, and the third Leibniz’s own “pre-established harmony”.","history":"Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz set out pre-established harmony in his “New System of Nature” (1695) and the Monadology (1714). Occasionalists held instead that God brings about each match: al-Ghazali, in The Incoherence of the Philosophers (about 1095), denied natural causation generally, and Nicolas Malebranche applied occasionalism to mind and body in The Search After Truth (1674 to 1675).","misread":"Parallelism does not deny that mind and body are correlated; it denies only that the correlation involves one causing the other.","variants":["Pre-established harmony: God set mind and body to agree from the start (Leibniz)","Occasionalism: God causes each matching event at each moment (al-Ghazali, Malebranche)","Spinozist parallelism: mind and body correspond as two attributes of one substance"],"reading":[{"title":"Leibniz’s Philosophy of Mind (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/leibniz-mind/"},{"title":"Occasionalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/occasionalism/"}]},"m1d|incoherent":{"summary":"A physical duplicate of you that feels nothing is not even coherently conceivable, because having those physical processes just is feeling.","idea":"This view says the zombie story falls apart on careful thought. If a being has all your brain processes, including those that make you notice pain, believe you are in pain and say so, then it is in pain; nothing is left to subtract. It denies that we can genuinely conceive of a zombie, as opposed to merely thinking we can. Its appeal: it removes one of the main arguments against physicalism at the root. Everyday case: picture an engine with every part moving exactly as in a running engine, yet somehow not running; “running” is not an extra ingredient.","example":"Dennett’s “zimboes”: a zombie able to monitor its own inner states would believe and insist that it is conscious, just as you do. Dennett argues that once you spell out everything such a creature does, the claim that it lacks experience loses its content, so people who think they imagine zombies are imagining something less.","history":"Gilbert Ryle’s The Concept of Mind (1949) prepared the ground by attacking the “ghost in the machine”. Daniel Dennett, in Consciousness Explained (1991) and “The Unimagined Preposterousness of Zombies” (1995), argued that zombies are not really conceivable. Analytic functionalists such as David Lewis held that mental terms mean “whatever plays this role”, which makes zombies contradictory.","misread":"The claim is not that our imagination fails; we can tell the story in words, but on this view the story contradicts itself once its terms are understood.","variants":["Analytic functionalism: mental terms are defined by roles, so zombies are contradictory","Type-A materialism: Chalmers’ label for denying any deep explanatory gap"],"reading":[{"title":"Zombies (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/zombies/"}]},"m1d|imaginable":{"summary":"Zombies can be imagined, but imaginability does not prove possibility; experience is necessarily physical, as water is necessarily H2O.","idea":"This position, often called type-B physicalism, admits a gap in our concepts: nothing in the idea of neurons firing logically implies the idea of pain, so we can picture one without the other. But it denies that this shows a gap in reality. Pain is a brain state, though we discover this rather than deduce it. Its appeal: it accepts the strong intuition behind zombies while keeping physicalism. Everyday case: someone who doesn’t know Superman is Clark Kent can imagine Superman flying while Clark sits at his desk, but that cannot happen, since they are the same person.","example":"The “phenomenal concept strategy”: we think about our experiences using special first-person concepts that work quite differently from scientific concepts. Because the two kinds of concept are independent, we can imagine one applying without the other, even though both pick out the same brain state. The gap is in our thinking, not in the world.","history":"Saul Kripke’s Naming and Necessity (1980) showed that some necessary truths, like “water is H2O”, are known only by discovery, though Kripke himself used this against the identity theory. Joseph Levine named the “explanatory gap” in 1983. Brian Loar’s “Phenomenal States” (1990), Christopher Hill, Brian McLaughlin and David Papineau developed type-B replies.","misread":"This view does not say zombies are unimaginable; it grants we can imagine them and denies that this reveals what is genuinely possible.","variants":["Phenomenal concept strategy: the gap comes from special first-person concepts (Loar, Papineau)","Type-B physicalism: Chalmers’ label for mind-brain identity known by discovery"],"reading":[{"title":"Zombies (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/zombies/"},{"title":"Physicalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/physicalism/"}]},"m1d|possible":{"summary":"A being physically identical to you but with no experience is genuinely possible, so consciousness is something beyond the physical.","idea":"This view holds that zombies are not only imaginable but metaphysically possible: a universe could have all our physics and no feeling. If so, the physical facts do not settle the facts about experience, and physicalism is false. It denies that physical structure and function, however complete, guarantee consciousness, though in our world laws of nature may still always pair the two. Its appeal: when we think clearly about particles and their arrangements, nothing in that description seems to require that it feels like something. Everyday case: nothing in a robot’s wiring diagram seems to require that it hurts when it says “ouch”.","example":"David Chalmers’ zombie argument: (1) a zombie world is conceivable; (2) what is conceivable in the relevant ideal sense is possible; (3) so a zombie world is possible; (4) so consciousness is not physical. Most of the debate targets the second step.","history":"Keith Campbell’s Body and Mind (1970) described an “imitation man”, and Robert Kirk introduced philosophical zombies in “Zombies v. Materialists” (1974), though Kirk later rejected the argument. David Chalmers made it famous in The Conscious Mind (1996). Descartes had argued similarly that because he could clearly conceive mind without body, they are distinct.","misread":"Believing zombies are possible is not believing zombies exist; defenders usually think the laws of our world rule them out.","variants":["Property dualism: experience is an extra natural property (Chalmers)","Russellian monism: a zombie world would lack matter’s intrinsic, experiential nature"],"reading":[{"title":"Zombies (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/zombies/"},{"title":"Dualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/"}]},"m6|program":{"summary":"Having a mind is running the right kind of information processing, so a suitably programmed computer would genuinely understand and could genuinely feel.","idea":"Computational functionalism says minds are what certain computations do; neurons are one way of running them, and chips could be another. If a machine ran the same program at the right level of detail, it would have the same thoughts and experiences. It denies that biology is special. Its appeal: it explains how meaning and reasoning fit into a physical world and matches the success of cognitive science. Everyday case: a chess game is the same game whether played with wooden pieces, on a screen or by post.","example":"The neuron replacement thought experiment: your neurons are replaced one by one with chips that do exactly the same job, and your behaviour never changes. Did your experiences fade out along the way? David Chalmers argues that slowly fading or suddenly vanishing experience is implausible, so the finished silicon brain is conscious too.","history":"Alan Turing’s “Computing Machinery and Intelligence” (1950) proposed judging machine intelligence by conversation. Hilary Putnam’s machine functionalism and Jerry Fodor’s The Language of Thought (1975) made the computational theory of mind central. John Searle named the view “strong AI” in “Minds, Brains, and Programs” (1980) in order to attack it. David Chalmers defends a version for consciousness.","misread":"The view does not say today’s chatbots are conscious; it says the right program would suffice, leaving open which programs those are.","variants":["Computational theory of mind: thinking is computation over mental symbols (Fodor)","Strong AI: Searle’s name for the claim that the right program is a mind"],"reading":[{"title":"The Computational Theory of Mind (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/computational-mind/"},{"title":"The Chinese Room Argument (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/chinese-room/"}]},"m6|split":{"summary":"Machines could genuinely think, understand and reason, but there would be nothing it is like to be them: intelligence without consciousness.","idea":"This view separates two features we usually find together in humans. Understanding, believing and reasoning are matters of information processing that machines can have. Felt experience is different and may depend on something computation alone does not supply. It denies that real understanding requires consciousness. Its appeal: it lets us credit capable AI systems with real cognitive achievements while staying cautious about their inner lives. Everyday case: much of your own thinking, like parsing a sentence or catching a ball, happens without awareness; a machine might do all its thinking that way.","example":"Blindsight: some patients with damage to the visual cortex say they see nothing in part of their visual field, yet they point to objects there far better than chance. They seem to process visual information without experiencing it. A thinking machine might be like this throughout.","history":"The split draws on Ned Block’s distinction between “access consciousness” and “phenomenal consciousness” in “On a Confusion about a Function of Consciousness” (1995). Lawrence Weiskrantz’s blindsight studies from the 1970s gave a human analogue. David Chalmers’ “Could a Large Language Model Be Conscious?” (2023) treats understanding and consciousness in AI as separate questions.","misread":"This view does not say machines merely simulate thinking; it says they genuinely think, and only felt experience is missing.","variants":["Access without phenomenal consciousness: information available for reasoning but not felt (from Block’s distinction)"],"reading":[{"title":"Consciousness (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consciousness/"},{"title":"Artificial Intelligence (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/artificial-intelligence/"}]},"m6|stuff":{"summary":"Minds are caused by specific biological powers of brains, so running a program alone cannot produce understanding or consciousness.","idea":"Biological naturalism says consciousness is a natural biological phenomenon, like digestion, caused by the specific physical and chemical workings of brains. A program is just syntax, rules for shuffling symbols, and syntax alone never adds up to meaning or experience. It denies that the right program is enough, but not that an artificial machine could have a mind: it would need the brain’s causal powers, not just its pattern. Its appeal: it is naturalist without treating the mind as software. Everyday case: a computer simulation of a rainstorm leaves nobody wet.","example":"Searle’s answer to the systems reply: let the man in the Chinese room memorise the whole rulebook and do every step in his head. Now he is the whole system, yet he still understands no Chinese. So, Searle concludes, running the program is not enough for understanding.","history":"John Searle introduced the Chinese room in “Minds, Brains, and Programs” (1980) and defended biological naturalism in Intentionality (1983) and The Rediscovery of the Mind (1992). Christof Koch, drawing on Giulio Tononi’s integrated information theory, has argued in The Feeling of Life Itself (2019) that ordinary digital computers would not be conscious however they behaved.","misread":"Biological naturalism does not say only living creatures can have minds; Searle allows an artificial machine could, if it duplicated the brain’s causal powers.","variants":["Integrated information theory: consciousness depends on a system’s physical causal structure, not its program (Tononi, Koch)"],"reading":[{"title":"The Chinese Room Argument (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/chinese-room/"}]},"m6|never":{"summary":"No built machine could ever truly think or feel, because minds require something artifacts cannot have, such as life or a soul.","idea":"This view holds that a real mind needs a feature no constructed device could possess. For some it is a soul. For others it is life itself: a mind belongs to an organism that keeps itself going and has its own needs, while a machine only serves purposes we give it. It denies that more speed, data or clever design could ever cross the line. Its appeal: it explains the strong sense that imitation is not the real thing. Everyday case: a perfect wax figure of your friend, however lifelike, is not your friend.","example":"Descartes’ language test in the Discourse on the Method (1637): a machine might be built to utter words, even in response to being touched, but it could never arrange words to answer meaningfully whatever is said to it, as even the dullest human can. For Descartes this showed that reason belongs to a soul, not a mechanism.","history":"Descartes argued in the Discourse on the Method (1637) that machines lack reason. J.R. Lucas’s “Minds, Machines and Gödel” (1961) and Roger Penrose’s The Emperor’s New Mind (1989) argued from Gödel’s incompleteness theorem that human understanding is not computation. Hubert Dreyfus’s What Computers Can’t Do (1972) stressed embodied skill. Many religious traditions hold that minds require souls.","misread":"The view is not just that current AI falls short; it says no future machine, however advanced, could have a genuine mind.","variants":["Soul-based view: only beings with souls can think or feel","Life-based view: mind requires a living, self-maintaining organism","Gödelian view: human understanding is not computable (Lucas, Penrose)"],"reading":[{"title":"Artificial Intelligence (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/artificial-intelligence/"},{"title":"The Chinese Room Argument (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/chinese-room/"}]},"m6|unknown":{"summary":"Whatever a machine does, we have no reliable test for whether it genuinely understands or feels, so the question may be unanswerable for us.","idea":"This agnostic view says the problem is about evidence. We judge that other humans have minds by analogy: they are built like us and act like us. A machine may act like us but is built very differently, and rival theories of consciousness give different verdicts about it. It denies that behaviour, however impressive, settles the matter, and also that we can rule machine minds out. Its appeal: humility in the face of deep disagreement. Everyday case: a friend’s tears convince you she is sad, but an actor’s tears on stage convince you of nothing.","example":"The Turing test: Alan Turing proposed that if a machine’s conversation cannot be told apart from a human’s, we should count it as thinking. Agnostics reply that passing shows only skill at producing human-like answers; it cannot reveal whether understanding or feeling lies behind the words.","history":"The problem of other minds is old; John Stuart Mill gave the argument from analogy in An Examination of Sir William Hamilton’s Philosophy (1865). Alan Turing’s “Computing Machinery and Intelligence” (1950) proposed a behavioural test partly to sidestep it. Recently Eric Schwitzgebel and Jonathan Birch, in The Edge of Sentience (2024), have argued we may build AI whose moral status is genuinely uncertain.","misread":"Agnosticism is not the claim that machines lack minds; it says we cannot know either way, which itself raises moral questions.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Other Minds (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/other-minds/"},{"title":"Artificial Intelligence (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/artificial-intelligence/"}]},"m2|yes":{"summary":"There is a single, real self that persists through time and is the subject of all your thoughts, feelings and actions.","idea":"This view holds that “I” refers to something real: one subject who has all your experiences and who existed ten years ago and still exists now. Versions disagree about what the self is, a soul, an organism, a life story or pure awareness, but agree it is not a fiction. It denies that the self is merely a convenient label for a series of mental events. Its appeal: experience seems to belong to someone, and promises, guilt and plans all assume the same person persists. Everyday case: you can feel embarrassed now about something you did as a child, because you did it.","example":"Descartes’ cogito: even while doubting everything else, he could not doubt that he, the one doing the doubting, existed. Defenders of the self argue that every thought implies a thinker. Critics, starting with Georg Lichtenberg, replied that it shows only that thinking is going on, not that there is a lasting someone doing it.","history":"The early Upaniṣads teach a true self, ātman. Plato and later Descartes identified the self with a thinking soul. John Locke’s Essay Concerning Human Understanding (second edition, 1694) tied personal identity to consciousness and memory. Thomas Reid defended a simple, persisting self against Hume in the 18th century. Modern defenders include Richard Swinburne, Eric Olson and Dan Zahavi.","misread":"Believing in a self does not require believing in a soul; many defenders identify the self with the living human being.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"},{"title":"Self-Consciousness (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/self-consciousness/"}]},"m2|no":{"summary":"There is no single, persisting thing that has all your experiences; there are only experiences and processes, connected in various ways.","idea":"The no-self view says that when you look for the one thing that owns all your thoughts, you find only more thoughts, feelings and perceptions. What we call a self is a pattern, a bundle or a construction, not an extra entity. It denies there is a hidden owner behind experiences, while usually accepting that persons exist in a looser, everyday sense. Its appeal: it fits both introspection and neuroscience, which has found no single “self centre” in the brain. Everyday case: a river is real and has a name, though its water keeps changing and there is no river-thing beyond the flow.","example":"Split-brain cases: when the bundle of fibres joining the brain’s two halves is cut to treat severe epilepsy, each half can sometimes respond to different information, as if two streams of awareness were running. Derek Parfit argued that such cases show the unity of a mind is not the work of a single underlying self.","history":"The Buddha (around the 5th century BCE, on many scholars’ dating) taught anattā, not-self. David Hume’s A Treatise of Human Nature (1739) described the self as a bundle of perceptions. In recent philosophy Derek Parfit’s Reasons and Persons (1984), Daniel Dennett’s “The Self as a Center of Narrative Gravity” (1992) and Thomas Metzinger’s Being No One (2003) defend related views.","misread":"Denying a self does not mean denying that you exist or can be responsible; it denies only one extra thing behind your experiences.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Mind in Indian Buddhist Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mind-indian-buddhism/"},{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"}]},"m2a|simple":{"summary":"The self is a simple, indivisible subject without parts, the single thing that thinks, feels and chooses in you.","idea":"The simple view holds that you are not built from parts the way your body is. You are one undivided subject, so you either exist entirely or not at all; there cannot be half of you. It denies that the self is a collection of brain parts or mental states. Its appeal: experience seems unified, with sights, sounds and thoughts all present to one point of view, and it is hard to see how separate parts could produce that unity. Everyday case: it seems you cannot be 60 percent the person who wakes up tomorrow; either it will be you or it won’t.","example":"Descartes’ divisibility argument in the sixth Meditation: a body can be divided, but a mind cannot. If you lose a foot or an arm, nothing is taken away from your mind; it is still one whole thinker. So the mind is simple, and therefore different from the body.","history":"Plato’s Phaedo argued the soul is not composite and so cannot decay. Descartes (1641), Leibniz, who called minds simple substances or monads, and Thomas Reid in his Essays on the Intellectual Powers of Man (1785) defended simplicity. Kant attacked arguments for it as “paralogisms” in the Critique of Pure Reason (1781). Richard Swinburne and E.J. Lowe defend versions today.","misread":"The simple view does not deny that brains have parts; it says you, the subject, are not identical to any collection of those parts.","variants":["Cartesian soul: a simple thinking substance","Simple view of personal identity: identity over time is a further fact, not analysable in other terms (Swinburne)"],"reading":[{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"},{"title":"Dualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/"}]},"m2a|person":{"summary":"You are a living human being, a body and brain functioning together, with no further inner self hidden inside.","idea":"This view says the self is not mysterious: you are the human organism, or the person that the organism makes up. Thinking and feeling are things this whole being does. It denies the need for a soul or an inner observer, but unlike no-self views it insists the self is real and persists. Its appeal: it matches how we actually pick each other out, by bodies, faces and voices, and it fits biology. Everyday case: when you point at yourself in a photo of your fifth birthday, you point at a smaller version of this same living body.","example":"Eric Olson’s “thinking animal” argument: there is a human animal sitting in your chair, and that animal can think. If you were something other than the animal, there would be two thinkers in your chair. The simplest answer is that you are the animal.","history":"Aristotle treated humans as rational animals. P.F. Strawson’s Individuals (1959) argued that “person” is a basic concept for beings with both mental and physical features. Bernard Williams, Peter van Inwagen in Material Beings (1990) and Eric Olson in The Human Animal (1997) defended bodily or animal views. Lynne Rudder Baker’s Persons and Bodies (2000) held that persons are constituted by animals, not identical to them.","misread":"Saying you are a human being does not mean memory and personality don’t matter; it means they are features of you, not what you are.","variants":["Animalism: you are a human organism (Olson)","Constitution view: you are a person constituted by, but not identical to, an organism (Baker)","Strawsonian persons: basic beings with both mental and physical features"],"reading":[{"title":"Animalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/animalism/"},{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"}]},"m2a|story":{"summary":"The self is real, but it is constituted by the ongoing story that links your past, present choices and future plans into one life.","idea":"The narrative view says a self is created and sustained by the way you understand your life as a story: where you came from, what you care about and where you are going. Experiences become part of you when they are woven into that story. It denies that the self is a ready-made object like a soul, but also denies that it is unreal. Its appeal: it explains how people can change deeply while remaining themselves, and why losing one’s story through severe amnesia feels like losing oneself. Everyday case: someone who says “I’m not who I was in high school” is revising the story, not reporting a new body.","example":"Galen Strawson’s challenge in “Against Narrativity” (2004): some people, he argues, live “episodically”, without experiencing their lives as a story, yet live well and ethically. Narrative theorists must say whether such people have selves. Marya Schechtman replies that the narrative need not be told aloud or be literary; it can be implicit in how a life is organised.","history":"Alasdair MacIntyre’s After Virtue (1981) and Charles Taylor’s Sources of the Self (1989) argued that lives have narrative unity. Paul Ricoeur’s Oneself as Another (1990) distinguished narrative identity from mere sameness. Marya Schechtman’s The Constitution of Selves (1996) gave the main analytic version. Daniel Dennett’s “center of narrative gravity” is a deflationary cousin.","misread":"A narrative self is not fake because it is a story; the story is how the self is organised, as a constitution makes a nation real.","variants":["Narrative self-constitution (Schechtman): personhood comes from a self-understood life story","Narrative identity (Ricoeur): identity as selfhood through a life, distinct from sameness of a thing"],"reading":[{"title":"Personal Identity and Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-ethics/"}]},"m2a|witness":{"summary":"Your true self is pure, unchanging awareness, the silent witness in which body, thoughts and personality appear, not any of those things.","idea":"The witness view says that everything you can observe, including your body, emotions and even your thoughts, is an object for awareness, and so not the self. The real self is the subject that can never become an object: changeless consciousness that only witnesses. It denies that the self is the body, the mind or the personality. In Advaita Vedānta this awareness, ātman, is identical with brahman, the one reality. Its appeal: thoughts and moods come and go, but awareness of them seems constant. Everyday case: a cinema screen shows fires and floods, but the screen is never burned or soaked.","example":"The rope and the snake: at dusk you mistake a coiled rope for a snake and feel fear. The snake was never there; only the rope was. Advaita teachers use this to show how the world and the individual ego are superimposed on pure awareness, which remains untouched throughout.","history":"The Upaniṣads, especially the Bṛhadāraṇyaka and Chāndogya, teach ātman as the inner seer that cannot be seen. Śaṅkara (traditionally dated to the 8th century CE) systematised Advaita Vedānta in his commentaries. Sāṃkhya, in Īśvarakṛṣṇa’s Sāṃkhyakārikā, holds that pure consciousness (puruṣa) witnesses matter, with one puruṣa for each being. Ramana Maharshi taught a modern form.","misread":"The witness is not a little person watching inside your head; it is awareness itself, with no shape, location or personality.","variants":["Advaita Vedānta: one witness, identical with brahman (Śaṅkara)","Sāṃkhya and Yoga: many witnessing selves (puruṣas), distinct from matter"],"reading":[{"title":"Advaita Vedanta (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/adv-veda/"}]},"m2b|bundle":{"summary":"A self is nothing more than a collection of perceptions, thoughts, feelings and memories linked by relations like memory, resemblance and causation.","idea":"Bundle theory says that when you look inward you find particular experiences, a pang of hunger, a memory, a tune, but never a further thing that has them. The self just is the bundle, held together by relations such as memory, resemblance and cause and effect. It denies there is an owner or substance behind experiences. Its appeal is empirical: it accepts only what can be found. Everyday case: a football club survives changes of players, coaches and stadium; there is no extra club essence beyond its connected history.","example":"Hume’s introspection test in the Treatise: whenever he looked most closely into what he called himself, he always stumbled on some particular perception, of heat or cold, love or hatred, and never caught himself without one. In the Appendix he admitted he could not explain what binds the bundle together.","history":"Buddhist Abhidharma texts analysed persons into five aggregates (skandhas) long before Hume. David Hume set out the view in A Treatise of Human Nature, Book 1 (1739). Ernst Mach and William James held related views. Derek Parfit’s Reasons and Persons (1984) revived it, arguing that a person’s existence just consists in physical and psychological connections.","misread":"Bundle theorists do not say the self is a random heap; the bundle is tightly organised by memory and causation, which is why lives hang together.","variants":["Humean bundle: perceptions linked by resemblance and causation","Parfitian reductionism: a person’s existence consists in psychological and physical continuity"],"reading":[{"title":"David Hume (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume/"},{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"}]},"m2b|conv":{"summary":"“Self” is a convenient label for a changing stream of bodily and mental processes; taking it as a lasting entity leads to clinging and suffering.","idea":"The Buddhist teaching of anattā analyses a person into five changing groups, the skandhas: body, feeling, perception, mental formations and consciousness. None is a permanent self, and there is no self apart from them. “I” is a conventional name, useful in daily life but not ultimately real. It denies a permanent soul while still accepting rebirth and responsibility as chains of cause and effect. What sets it apart is the practical point: grasping at a self fuels craving and suffering, and seeing through it is part of liberation. Everyday case: an insult stings less once you stop treating “my image” as something to defend.","example":"The chariot in the Milindapañha: the monk Nāgasena asks King Milinda whether the chariot is the axle, the wheels or the frame. It is none of them, nor something apart from them; “chariot” is a name used for the parts in combination. The same, he says, holds for the name “Nāgasena”.","history":"The Buddha taught not-self in the early discourses, such as the Discourse on the Characteristic of Not-Self (Anattalakkhaṇa Sutta). Vasubandhu’s Abhidharmakośabhāṣya (around the 4th to 5th century CE) argued against the Pudgalavāda school, which accepted a quasi-real person. Nāgārjuna’s Madhyamaka treated even the aggregates as empty. Mark Siderits’s Personal Identity and Buddhist Philosophy (2003) connects the view with Parfit.","misread":"Not-self does not mean you don’t exist or nothing matters; Buddhists accept conventional persons and responsibility, denying only an unchanging essence.","variants":["Abhidharma reductionism: persons reduce to momentary events (dharmas), which are ultimately real","Madhyamaka: even the parts lack intrinsic existence (Nāgārjuna)","Pudgalavāda: a dissenting school holding that the person is neither identical to nor separate from the aggregates"],"reading":[{"title":"Mind in Indian Buddhist Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mind-indian-buddhism/"}]},"m2b|noself":{"summary":"The brain constructs a model of itself as a self, and because we cannot see the model as a model, we experience being a self.","idea":"Self-model theory says the brain builds an internal representation of the whole organism, its body, feelings and point of view, to guide behaviour. The model is “transparent”: we look through it rather than at it, so we take its content for reality and feel there is someone inside. It denies that any self exists beyond this representation. Its appeal: it explains why the sense of self is so convincing and why brain injury, drugs or meditation can alter it. Everyday case: deep in a good video game you stop seeing pixels and feel you are the character.","example":"The rubber hand illusion: a person’s real hand is hidden while a rubber hand in view is stroked in time with it. Many people soon feel the rubber hand is their own. Full-body versions using virtual reality show that even the felt location of “me” can be shifted, as self-model theory predicts.","history":"Thomas Metzinger developed the view in Being No One (2003) and The Ego Tunnel (2009). Matthew Botvinick and Jonathan Cohen reported the rubber hand illusion in 1998, and Olaf Blanke and Metzinger studied full-body illusions. Anil Seth’s Being You (2021) treats the self as the brain’s best guess about the body, and Dennett’s narrative view is a related deflationary idea.","misread":"The view does not say nothing is there; the organism and its brain are real, and only the self as a thing is a construction.","variants":["Self-model theory of subjectivity (Metzinger): the self is the content of a transparent brain model","Predictive processing views: the self as the brain’s best guess about the body (Seth)"],"reading":[{"title":"Self-Consciousness (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/self-consciousness/"}]},"m4|you":{"summary":"You survive teleportation because what makes you the same person over time is continuity of memory, personality and other psychological connections.","idea":"Psychological continuity theory says personal identity consists in overlapping chains of memories, intentions, beliefs and character traits, caused in the right way. If the person on Mars has your mind, continuous with yours, that person is you, whatever happened to the original atoms. It denies that sameness of body or soul is what makes you you. Its appeal: in imagined body swaps and brain transplants, most people’s intuitions follow the mind. Everyday case: many of your cells are replaced over the years, yet you remain you because your memories and character carry on.","example":"Locke’s prince and cobbler: if the soul of a prince, carrying his memories, entered the body of a cobbler, everyone would see the cobbler, but the person would be the prince. Locke concluded that personal identity follows consciousness, not the body or even the soul.","history":"John Locke added the chapter “Of Identity and Diversity” to the second edition of An Essay Concerning Human Understanding (1694). Thomas Reid’s “brave officer” objection showed that a simple memory account breaks down. Sydney Shoemaker, John Perry and Derek Parfit developed continuity theories in the 1970s and 1980s to answer it.","misread":"The view does not require remembering everything; overlapping chains suffice, like a rope whose fibres never run its full length.","variants":["Memory theory: identity follows memory (Locke)","Psychological continuity: overlapping chains of psychological connections (Shoemaker, Parfit)"],"reading":[{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"},{"title":"Personal Identity (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/person-i/"}]},"m4|someone":{"summary":"Teleportation kills you and makes a copy; personal identity needs the same body, brain or soul, not just the same memories.","idea":"This view says a perfect copy, however exact, is a new person: being similar is not being the same. Something must carry over, such as your living body, your brain or your soul. It denies that psychological similarity, even with an unbroken causal link, makes the copy you. Its appeal: if the machine did not destroy the original, there would plainly be two people, and the one on Mars would not be you; destroying the original seems to add nothing that could make it you. Everyday case: a forgery identical to the Mona Lisa is still not the Mona Lisa.","example":"Parfit’s branch-line case in Reasons and Persons: the teleporter malfunctions, leaving you intact on Earth while a replica appears on Mars. You can talk to your replica by video. Most people feel they are the one on Earth, which suggests the replica would never have been you in the normal case either.","history":"Joseph Butler and Thomas Reid argued against Locke in the 18th century that identity is not a matter of memory. Bernard Williams’s “The Self and Its Future” (1970) turned intuitions toward the body. Animalists such as Eric Olson and Peter van Inwagen, and soul theorists such as Richard Swinburne, all deny that teleportation is survival.","misread":"The view does not deny that the copy is a full person with real memories; it denies only that the copy is you.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"}]},"m4|nofact":{"summary":"Once all physical and psychological facts are known, there is no further fact about whether the person on Mars is you.","idea":"Reductionism about personal identity says a person’s existence just consists in physical and psychological facts: bodies, brains, memories and the causal links between them. In the teleporter case we know all of these. Whether to call the person on Mars “you” is like deciding whether a ship rebuilt plank by plank is the same ship. It denies that identity is a deep further fact. Its appeal: it dissolves the puzzle cases and frees us from fear of losing a hidden “real self”. Everyday case: whether a band that changed every member is still “the same band” depends on how we use the words.","example":"Parfit’s physical spectrum: imagine a range of cases in which 1 percent, 2 percent, and so on up to 100 percent of your cells are replaced by exact copies. At one end it is clearly you, at the other a replica. There seems to be no sharp point where you stop existing, which suggests no deep fact is at stake.","history":"Derek Parfit set out the view in “Personal Identity” (1971) and Reasons and Persons (1984), comparing it to the Buddha’s teaching. David Hume had already called questions of personal identity grammatical rather than philosophical difficulties. Mark Johnston and others have since challenged whether reductionism supports Parfit’s further conclusions.","misread":"Reductionists do not say you never existed; they say your existence consists in more basic facts, and some identity questions have no answer.","variants":["Parfitian reductionism: identity consists in psychological and physical continuity, and is not what matters","Conventionalism: some identity facts depend on our practices or decisions"],"reading":[{"title":"Personal Identity and Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-ethics/"},{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"}]},"m4a|both":{"summary":"In a branching case you survive as both copies: one person can come to occupy two places and live two lives.","idea":"This answer accepts psychological continuity and follows it consistently. Each copy is continuous with you, so each counts as you, and after the split you are in two places at once. It denies that survival must be one-to-one going forward. Its appeal: neither copy has a better claim, and it seems strange that a double success should count as death. Some defenders say there were really two people sharing one body all along, which keeps the logic of identity intact. Everyday case: a road that forks continues along both branches.","example":"David Lewis’s “Survival and Identity” (1976): before a split, two persons already overlapped, like two roads sharing a stretch before they part. Each later copy is the continuation of one of them. So no single person becomes two; two persons simply stop coinciding.","history":"John Perry’s “Can the Self Divide?” (1972) explored answers to branching cases. David Lewis offered the multiple occupancy view in “Survival and Identity” (1976), part of his four-dimensional picture of persons as extended through time. Derek Parfit discussed the “both” answer in Reasons and Persons (1984) but rejected it as an account of identity.","misread":"On Lewis’s version, one person does not magically gain two bodies; two people shared one body until the split.","variants":["Multiple occupancy: two persons coincided before the split (Lewis)","Scattered person: one person who now has two bodies"],"reading":[{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"}]},"m4a|neither":{"summary":"A person cannot become two, and neither copy has a better claim, so after a branching duplication the original person no longer exists.","idea":"The non-branching view adds a condition to psychological continuity: you survive only if the continuity is one-to-one. Identity is transitive (if A is B and B is C, then A is C), and the two copies are clearly not each other, so they cannot both be you. Since neither has a better claim, neither is you. It denies that identity can branch. Its appeal: it respects the strict logic of identity. Everyday case: if a club splits and two new clubs each claim to be the original, a fair judge might rule that neither is.","example":"Fission by brain division: each half of your brain is transplanted into a different body, and both resulting people have your memories and character. Either would have counted as you had the other half died. Non-branching theorists say that, with both alive, you did not survive.","history":"David Wiggins discussed division in Identity and Spatio-Temporal Continuity (1967). Sydney Shoemaker and Robert Nozick, with his “closest continuer” theory in Philosophical Explanations (1981), built uniqueness into identity. Derek Parfit included a non-branching clause in his definition of identity but argued that identity is not what matters.","misread":"The view does not say the copies are not real people; it says both are new people, each with your memories.","variants":["Closest continuer theory: you are whoever best continues you, if there is a unique best (Nozick)","Non-branching continuity: you survive only if continuity runs one-to-one (Shoemaker, Parfit)"],"reading":[{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"}]},"m4a|nomatter":{"summary":"There is no fact about which copy is you, but that does not matter, since what we care about in survival continues in both.","idea":"This view, Derek Parfit’s, separates identity from what matters. Identity is all-or-nothing and one-to-one, so in a split you are neither copy. But what you really care about, that your memories, plans, projects and loves carry on, is fully present in both. Splitting is therefore about as good as ordinary survival. It denies that identity itself is what matters to us. Its appeal: it removes the absurdity of treating double success as death. Everyday case: a beloved band splits into two that both keep playing your favourite songs; whether either is “the” band hardly matters.","example":"Parfit’s “My Division” in Reasons and Persons: imagining his brain divided between two bodies, he concluded the outcome would not be like death. He reported finding the view liberating: he became less concerned about his own death and more concerned about other people’s lives.","history":"Derek Parfit argued in “Personal Identity” (1971) and Reasons and Persons (1984) that identity is not what matters. Mark Johnston, in “Human Concerns without Superlative Selves” (1997), and Peter Unger, in Identity, Consciousness and Value (1990), objected that our concern for the future is tied to identity. Parfit linked his view to the Buddha’s teaching on not-self.","misread":"Parfit does not say the future is unimportant; he says what matters is psychological continuity, which can hold even when identity fails.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Personal Identity and Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-ethics/"}]},"m4b|body":{"summary":"You are a human animal, so you persist as long as this living organism persists, even through total loss of memory.","idea":"Animalism says each of us is identical to a biological organism of the species Homo sapiens. Your persistence is biological: you exist as long as the organism’s life continues. It denies that memory or personality decides who you are; you could lose them all, or never have had them, as a foetus, and still be you. Its appeal: it avoids having two thinkers in your chair, the animal and the person, and it fits biology. Everyday case: a grandparent with advanced dementia is, in plain fact, the same human being who once raised you.","example":"The foetus problem: you were once a foetus with no psychology at all. Psychological theories must say you did not exist then and the foetus was something else. Animalists say simply: you were that foetus, because you are the same organism.","history":"Aristotle treated humans as rational animals. In modern debates Paul Snowdon, Eric Olson in The Human Animal: Personal Identity without Psychology (1997) and Peter van Inwagen in Material Beings (1990) defended the view, with roots in David Wiggins and in Bernard Williams’s case for a bodily criterion.","misread":"Animalism is not a claim about what is valuable; it says what you are, leaving open what matters morally about your life.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Animalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/animalism/"}]},"m4b|brain":{"summary":"You go where your brain goes: personal identity follows the brain, the organ that carries your mind, not the rest of the body.","idea":"The brain view says you are, or are tracked by, your brain or the part of it that supports consciousness. If your brain were transplanted into another body, you would wake up there, while your old body would be an empty shell. It denies that the rest of the body is essential, and also that a mere copy of your mind is you; it must be the same physical organ. Its appeal: it combines the intuition that the mind matters with the demand for physical continuity. Everyday case: people with artificial limbs or transplanted hearts remain themselves; the brain seems the one part you cannot swap.","example":"Sydney Shoemaker’s “Brownson” case in Self-Knowledge and Self-Identity (1963): Brown’s brain is put into Robinson’s body. The resulting person, Brownson, has Brown’s memories and character. Most people judge that Brownson is Brown, which seems to favour the brain over the rest of the body.","history":"Shoemaker’s Brownson case (1963) made the brain transplant a standard thought experiment. Thomas Nagel suggested in The View from Nowhere (1986) that you may be your brain. Jeff McMahan’s The Ethics of Killing (2002) held that we are embodied minds, located in the parts of the brain that generate consciousness.","misread":"The brain view is not psychological continuity: a perfect copy of your brain built from new matter would still not be you.","variants":["Embodied mind account: you are the parts of the brain that support consciousness (McMahan)","Brain identity: you literally are your brain (suggested by Nagel)"],"reading":[{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"}]},"m4b|soul":{"summary":"Personal identity consists in having the same immaterial soul, which no machine could scan or copy, so a replica is never you.","idea":"The soul view says what makes you the same person over time is a non-physical soul. Bodies change and memories fade, but the soul remains, and wherever it goes, you go. It denies that physical or psychological continuity is enough, or even required. A teleporter can copy atoms and memories but not a soul, so the person on Mars is someone new. Its appeal: identity seems all-or-nothing and definite, which fits a simple soul better than continuity, which comes in degrees. Everyday case: asking “Will it be me who feels that pain?” seems to have a definite answer.","example":"Richard Swinburne’s hemisphere case: your two brain hemispheres are placed in two new bodies. One person might be you, or the other, or neither. All the physical and mental facts could be the same whichever is true, so, Swinburne argues, what settles it must be something further: the soul.","history":"Plato’s Phaedo, Augustine and Descartes identified the person with the soul. Joseph Butler, in “Of Personal Identity” (1736), and Thomas Reid held that identity is simple and cannot be defined in other terms. Richard Swinburne defends the view in Personal Identity (with Sydney Shoemaker, 1984) and Mind, Brain, and Free Will (2013).","misread":"The soul view does not make memory irrelevant; memory is good evidence of identity, just not what identity consists in.","variants":["Simple view: identity is a further fact, not reducible to body or mind (Swinburne)"],"reading":[{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"},{"title":"Dualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/"}]},"m3|libert":{"summary":"We have free will, which requires that choices are not fixed by prior causes: at the moment of choice, more than one future is open.","idea":"Libertarianism (not the political view) holds two things: free will is incompatible with determinism, and we have free will, so determinism is false for us. When you chose tea, you could have chosen coffee with the past and the laws exactly as they were. It denies that being caused by your own desires is enough. Its appeal: it matches how deciding feels, as if the future is genuinely up to you, and secures a strong sense of deserving praise and blame. Everyday case: torn between keeping a promise and going to a party, it seems either choice is truly available.","example":"The Consequence Argument: if determinism is true, your acts follow from the laws of nature and events before you were born. Neither of those is up to you, so neither are your acts. Libertarians accept this reasoning and conclude, since we are free, that determinism is false.","history":"Epicurus (341 to 270 BCE) posited a random swerve of atoms partly to make room for freedom. Thomas Reid defended the active power of agents in his Essays on the Active Powers of Man (1788). Peter van Inwagen’s An Essay on Free Will (1983) made the Consequence Argument central, and Robert Kane’s The Significance of Free Will (1996) developed a leading account.","misread":"Libertarians do not claim choices are random; they claim choices are undetermined yet still controlled by the agent.","variants":["Agent causation: the agent, not an event, causes the choice","Event-causal libertarianism: reasons cause the choice without determining it","Non-causal libertarianism: free choices are uncaused but done for reasons"],"reading":[{"title":"Incompatibilist (Nondeterministic) Theories of Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-theories/"},{"title":"Arguments for Incompatibilism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-arguments/"}]},"m3|compat":{"summary":"Free will is compatible with determinism: a choice is free when it flows from your own reasons and values without coercion, even if predictable.","idea":"Compatibilism says the freedom that matters for responsibility is not freedom from causes but freedom from the wrong kind of causes, such as force, threats, addiction or manipulation. If you choose tea because you like tea and nothing compels you, you choose freely, even if a perfect replay would always come out the same. It denies that determinism, if true, would show nobody is responsible. Its appeal: it keeps responsibility in a scientific world and matches ordinary practice, which never checks physics before praising or blaming. Everyday case: a friend who always helps because she is kind is no less praiseworthy for being reliable.","example":"Hume’s definition in An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748): liberty is a power of acting or not acting according to the determinations of the will. A prisoner in chains lacks it; anyone not in chains has it, even if their will has causes. Hume thought the long dispute over freedom was largely a confusion about words.","history":"The Stoics, especially Chrysippus (3rd century BCE), argued that fate and responsibility are compatible. Thomas Hobbes, John Locke and David Hume developed classical compatibilism in the 17th and 18th centuries. In the 20th century Harry Frankfurt, P.F. Strawson, Daniel Dennett and John Martin Fischer offered new versions. It is the most common view among professional philosophers in recent surveys.","misread":"Compatibilists do not claim determinism is true; they claim free will would survive if it were.","variants":["Classical compatibilism: freedom is acting on your will without constraint","Hierarchical views: freedom is acting on desires you endorse (Frankfurt)","Semicompatibilism: responsibility is compatible with determinism even if the ability to do otherwise is not (Fischer)"],"reading":[{"title":"Compatibilism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/compatibilism/"}]},"m3|unsure":{"summary":"Free will requires that choices be undetermined, but we do not know whether they are, so whether we have free will is an open question.","idea":"Agnostic incompatibilism accepts half of the libertarian view: free will requires that the replay could come out differently. But it holds that whether our decisions really are undetermined is an empirical question about the brain that no one can yet answer. It denies both the confident yes of libertarians and the confident no of skeptics. Its appeal: it respects the incompatibilist intuition while being honest about the limits of physics and neuroscience. Everyday case: you can be sure a coin toss is fair only if nothing fixes the result, while admitting you cannot tell whether something does.","example":"Mark Balaguer’s “torn decisions”: cases where you are torn between options, such as two job offers, and choose without a decisive reason. Whether such choices are undetermined depends on whether the relevant brain events are, which, Balaguer argues, is a scientific question still open.","history":"Mark Balaguer defended this stance in Free Will as an Open Scientific Problem (2010). Peter van Inwagen, an incompatibilist who believes we are free, called free will a mystery in “Free Will Remains a Mystery” (2000). Debates about quantum indeterminacy in the brain, going back to the physicist Arthur Compton in the 1930s, keep the empirical question alive.","misread":"The agnostic is not undecided about everything; they are confident about what free will needs and unsure only whether the world supplies it.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Arguments for Incompatibilism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-arguments/"},{"title":"Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freewill/"}]},"m3|no":{"summary":"Nobody has the kind of free will that would make them truly deserve praise or blame, whether or not determinism is true.","idea":"Free will skepticism holds that the control needed for basic desert, deserving blame or praise just because of what you did, is something no one has. Determinism would rule it out, and indeterminism would only add chance, not control. It denies that retribution is ever justified, but not that people make choices, deliberate or can be held to account in forward-looking ways. Its appeal: it fits the scientific picture of humans as part of nature and points toward less punitive responses to crime. Everyday case: we stop blaming someone for an outburst once we learn a brain tumour caused it; skeptics say every act has causes we did not choose.","example":"The case of Charles Whitman, who in 1966 killed his wife, his mother and more than a dozen others in Austin, Texas. An autopsy found a tumour near his amygdala, though its role is disputed. Skeptics ask whether there is a principled line between his case and ordinary wrongdoers, whose brains and histories we simply understand less well.","history":"Spinoza’s Ethics (1677) held that people think themselves free only because they are ignorant of the causes of their actions. Baron d’Holbach argued for hard determinism in The System of Nature (1770). Contemporary skeptics include Derk Pereboom in Living Without Free Will (2001), Galen Strawson, Gregg Caruso and the neuroscientist Robert Sapolsky in Determined (2023).","misread":"Skeptics do not say anything goes or that criminals should be released; they reject deserved punishment, not protection, prevention or moral improvement.","variants":["Hard determinism: determinism is true and rules out free will","Hard incompatibilism: free will is ruled out whether or not determinism is true (Pereboom)","Impossibilism: the needed kind of free will is impossible in principle (Galen Strawson)"],"reading":[{"title":"Skepticism About Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral-responsibility/"}]},"m3b|agent":{"summary":"A free choice is caused by you, the agent, as a basic cause, not by earlier events in your brain or mind.","idea":"Agent causation says that in free action the cause is a person rather than an event like a desire or a brain state. You yourself start the causal chain, without being caused to start it. It denies that free choices are simply the latest link in a chain of events, whether deterministic or chancy. Its appeal: it seems to capture exactly what we mean by “I did it”: not that something happened in me, but that I made it happen. Everyday case: asked why your arm went up, “because I raised it” seems a complete answer.","example":"Chisholm’s staff, borrowed from Aristotle’s Physics: a staff moves a stone, a hand moves the staff, and a man moves the hand. Roderick Chisholm argued that in free action the chain ends with the man himself, who acts as a “prime mover unmoved”: something not caused by any earlier event.","history":"Thomas Reid’s Essays on the Active Powers of Man (1788) defended the active power of agents. Roderick Chisholm’s lecture “Human Freedom and the Self” (1964) revived it. Timothy O’Connor’s Persons and Causes (2000) and Randolph Clarke’s Libertarian Accounts of Free Will (2003) developed modern versions, and Richard Swinburne holds a related view.","misread":"Agent causation is not uncaused randomness: the choice has a cause, namely you, and that is what is supposed to supply control.","variants":["Emergent agent causation: the agent’s power emerges from the brain’s complexity (O’Connor)","Integrated agent causation: agent and reasons jointly cause the action (Clarke)"],"reading":[{"title":"Incompatibilist (Nondeterministic) Theories of Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-theories/"}]},"m3b|event":{"summary":"Free choices are caused by your reasons and efforts, but not deterministically, so the outcome stays open until you choose and is still yours.","idea":"Event-causal libertarianism keeps the ordinary picture of action, with beliefs, desires and efforts causing choices, but holds that in key decisions those causes do not fix the outcome. Some indeterminism in the brain leaves more than one result possible, and whichever happens is caused by your own reasons and effort. It denies that freedom needs a special kind of agent causation. Its appeal: it fits science better than agent causation while keeping genuine alternatives. Everyday case: you struggle between studying and going out; whichever way you go, it was your effort that won.","example":"Robert Kane’s businesswoman: on her way to an important meeting, she sees an assault in an alley. She is torn between helping and keeping her appointment, and makes efforts toward both. On Kane’s view, whichever effort succeeds, the choice is hers: a “self-forming action” that shapes her future character.","history":"Robert Kane developed the view in The Significance of Free Will (1996) and in Four Views on Free Will (2007, with Fischer, Pereboom and Vargas). Daniel Dennett, in a 1978 essay, and Alfred Mele sketched “two-stage” models in which chance supplies options and the agent selects among them. Laura Ekstrom and Mark Balaguer offer other versions.","misread":"The view does not let chance make the choice; indeterminism keeps options open, while your reasons and efforts do the causing.","variants":["Self-forming actions (Kane): undetermined choices that shape character","Two-stage models: chance offers options, then reasons decide (Dennett, Mele)"],"reading":[{"title":"Incompatibilist (Nondeterministic) Theories of Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-theories/"}]},"m3b|noncausal":{"summary":"Free choices need no cause at all; a choice is free when it is uncaused yet done for reasons, which explain it without causing it.","idea":"Non-causal libertarianism says a free choice is a basic mental act that is not caused, either by prior events or by the agent as a cause. What makes it yours is its own character as an act. It is explained by your reasons, but reason-explanations give purposes rather than pushes: “I chose tea in order to stay calm” tells what the choice was for. It denies that control requires causation. Its appeal: it avoids both determinism and the puzzling idea of a person as a cause. Everyday case: asking “what caused you to choose?” can seem odd, while “why?” seems the right question.","example":"Carl Ginet’s “actish” feel: Ginet argues that simple mental acts, like deciding, have a distinctive quality of seeming directly done by you. On his account, a mental act with this quality, explained by your intention to do it for some purpose, is free even though nothing causes it.","history":"Carl Ginet’s On Action (1990) is the leading statement. Hugh McCann, in The Works of Agency (1998), and Stewart Goetz have defended related views, Goetz stressing that reasons explain by purpose rather than by cause. Critics such as Randolph Clarke argue that without causation the agent’s control goes missing.","misread":"Uncaused does not mean unexplained or random; the choice is explained by its purpose, the agent’s reasons.","variants":["Ginet’s account: free acts are uncaused mental acts with an intrinsic “actish” quality","Teleological libertarianism: reasons explain choices by purpose, not cause (Goetz)"],"reading":[{"title":"Incompatibilist (Nondeterministic) Theories of Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-theories/"}]},"m3c|classic":{"summary":"To act freely is to do what you want without external constraint: no chains, threats or physical force stopping or pushing you.","idea":"Classical compatibilism defines freedom as the ability to act on your will. If you want tea and nothing stops you, you act freely; if someone holds a gun to your head, you do not. What caused your desire does not matter. It denies that freedom requires anything metaphysically deep, like an uncaused will. Its appeal: it is simple, captures the cases in which we excuse people (force, threats, restraint) and fits a scientific world. Everyday case: you are free to leave a dull lecture if the door is open and nobody blocks your way.","example":"John Locke’s locked room in the Essay (1690): a man is carried asleep into a room and locked in. He wakes to find good company and stays willingly, not knowing the door is locked. He stays voluntarily, Locke says, but he is not free to leave. The case later inspired Frankfurt-style cases.","history":"Thomas Hobbes, in Leviathan (1651) and his debate with Bishop Bramhall, defined liberty as the absence of external impediments. John Locke and David Hume followed. In the 20th century G.E. Moore’s Ethics (1912), Moritz Schlick and A.J. Ayer restated the view, analysing “could have done otherwise” as “would have, if I had chosen”.","misread":"Classical compatibilists do not count every desire-driven act as free; compulsion and threats are their standard exceptions.","variants":["Conditional analysis: “could have done otherwise” means “would have, if you had chosen to” (Moore)"],"reading":[{"title":"Compatibilism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/compatibilism/"}]},"m3c|hier":{"summary":"You act freely when the desire moving you is one you endorse at a higher level: a desire you want to have and act on.","idea":"The hierarchical view, from Harry Frankfurt, says persons have first-order desires (to smoke, to study) and second-order desires about which first-order desires they want to win. You act freely when the desire that moves you is the one you want to move you. It denies that simply doing what you want is enough, since in a sense an addict does what he wants. Its appeal: it explains why compulsions and addictions undermine freedom even with no outside force. Everyday case: you want to scroll your phone but want to want to read; when reading wins, you act as you truly wish.","example":"Frankfurt’s two addicts in “Freedom of the Will and the Concept of a Person” (1971): the unwilling addict hates his craving but is overcome by it; the willing addict embraces his addiction. Both take the drug, but only the willing addict acts of his own free will, on Frankfurt’s view.","history":"Harry Frankfurt introduced the view in “Freedom of the Will and the Concept of a Person” (1971). Gerald Dworkin developed a similar account of autonomy. Gary Watson’s “Free Agency” (1975) replaced higher desires with values, and Michael Bratman later appealed to self-governing policies. The view shapes modern theories of personal autonomy.","misread":"Endorsement need not mean conscious reflection before each act; what matters is whether your motive fits your settled higher-order attitudes.","variants":["Valuational view: freedom is acting on what you value (Watson)","Planning theory: endorsement through self-governing policies (Bratman)"],"reading":[{"title":"Compatibilism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/compatibilism/"},{"title":"Personal Autonomy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/personal-autonomy/"}]},"m3c|reasons":{"summary":"A choice is free when it comes from a decision-making process that would recognise and respond to good reasons to do otherwise.","idea":"Reasons-responsiveness says responsibility depends on whether your choice comes from a process, your own practical reasoning, that is sensitive to reasons. If you had learned the tea was poisoned, you would have chosen differently. It denies that responsibility requires undetermined alternatives; it requires the right kind of sensitivity, which could exist even in a deterministic world. Its appeal: it explains why we excuse young children, people in the grip of severe delusions and the hypnotised: their choices would not track reasons. Everyday case: a friend who changes plans when told the road is closed shows she is acting on reasons.","example":"Fischer and Ravizza’s “guidance control”: they contrast regulative control, the power to do otherwise, with guidance control, acting from your own reasons-responsive process. Frankfurt cases, they argue, show that guidance control alone is enough for responsibility.","history":"John Martin Fischer and Mark Ravizza set out the view in Responsibility and Control (1998). Susan Wolf’s Freedom Within Reason (1990) argued that freedom is the ability to act in accordance with the True and the Good. Dana Nelkin, Michael McKenna and Carolina Sartorio have developed related accounts.","misread":"The view does not require responding to every possible reason; a moderate, sensible pattern of responsiveness is enough.","variants":["Guidance control (Fischer and Ravizza): owning a moderately reasons-responsive mechanism","Reason view (Wolf): freedom is the ability to act well for good reasons"],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-responsibility/"},{"title":"Compatibilism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/compatibilism/"}]},"m3f|determ":{"summary":"Determinism is true and rules out free will: every choice follows from causes reaching back before your birth, so none is truly up to you.","idea":"Hard determinism combines two claims: every event, including every choice, is fixed by earlier causes and the laws of nature, and such causation rules out free will. You chose tea because of your desires, which came from your brain, genes and experiences, which came from earlier causes, and so on. It denies that being caused by your own desires is enough for responsibility. Its appeal: it takes the picture of a lawful universe seriously and refuses to soften its implications. Everyday case: a ball rolling downhill does not choose its path; we are vastly more complex, but not different in kind.","example":"Laplace’s demon: an intellect that knew the positions and forces of every particle at one moment could calculate the whole future, including every human choice. If such a being is possible in principle, your choices were settled before you were born.","history":"Spinoza’s Ethics (1677) taught that nothing in nature is contingent. Baron d’Holbach’s The System of Nature (1770) argued that humans are wholly determined. Pierre-Simon Laplace described his all-knowing intellect in A Philosophical Essay on Probabilities (1814). In the 20th century the lawyer Clarence Darrow and the psychologist B.F. Skinner drew practical conclusions.","misread":"Hard determinism is not fatalism: your choices still make a difference to what happens; they are just caused themselves.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Causal Determinism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/determinism-causal/"},{"title":"Skepticism About Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral-responsibility/"}]},"m3f|luck":{"summary":"Free will fails with or without determinism: either causes beyond you or mere chance settle your choice, and neither gives you control.","idea":"Hard incompatibilism holds that free will is ruled out either way. If determinism is true, your choices are the product of factors beyond your control. If the brain is indeterministic, an undetermined choice is partly a matter of chance, and chance is not control. It denies that libertarianism rescues responsibility, and unlike hard determinism it does not depend on whether determinism is true. Its appeal: it seems to hold whatever physics finds. Everyday case: you would not be responsible for a random twitch of your hand, and adding randomness to the brain seems to add twitches, not freedom.","example":"The rollback argument, posed by Peter van Inwagen: if God rewound time to just before an undetermined choice a thousand times, the agent might choose one way about half the time and the other way the rest. Then the outcome on any single run looks like a matter of luck.","history":"Derk Pereboom named and defended hard incompatibilism in Living Without Free Will (2001) and Free Will, Agency, and Meaning in Life (2014). His four-case argument claims that a manipulated agent is no less free than an ordinary determined one. Peter van Inwagen’s rollback appeared in “Free Will Remains a Mystery” (2000), though van Inwagen himself remains a libertarian.","misread":"Hard incompatibilists do not deny that people make choices for reasons; they deny that this gives the control needed for basic desert.","variants":["Four-case argument: manipulation cases show determinism undermines responsibility (Pereboom)"],"reading":[{"title":"Skepticism About Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral-responsibility/"},{"title":"Arguments for Incompatibilism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-arguments/"}]},"m3f|selfmade":{"summary":"True responsibility would require being the cause of yourself, which is impossible, so no one is ultimately responsible for anything they do.","idea":"The Basic Argument says you act as you do because of how you are. To be truly responsible for your act, you would need to be responsible for how you are. But to have made yourself that way, you would have had to act from some earlier way you were, for which you would also need to be responsible, and so on without end. It denies that anyone can meet this condition, whether determinism is true or not. Its appeal: its simplicity. Everyday case: someone praised for hard work did not choose the temperament or upbringing that made them hardworking.","example":"Galen Strawson’s heaven and hell test: true moral responsibility would be the kind that could make it fair to send someone to eternal torment or eternal bliss for what they did. Strawson argues nobody could ever deserve that, since nobody is the cause of themselves, so nobody has that kind of responsibility.","history":"Galen Strawson set out the Basic Argument in Freedom and Belief (1986) and “The Impossibility of Moral Responsibility” (1994). Friedrich Nietzsche, in Beyond Good and Evil (1886), mocked the wish to be causa sui, the cause of oneself. Neil Levy’s Hard Luck (2011) reaches a similar conclusion from the pervasiveness of luck.","misread":"Strawson does not deny that people choose or deliberate; he denies only ultimate responsibility, the kind that could make deep desert fair.","variants":["Impossibilism: the needed kind of free will is impossible in principle (Galen Strawson)"],"reading":[{"title":"Skepticism About Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral-responsibility/"}]},"m3f|science":{"summary":"Neuroscience shows decisions are prepared by unconscious brain activity before we feel we choose, so conscious will does not cause our actions.","idea":"Scientific skepticism argues from experiments rather than abstract argument. Brain recordings show activity related to a movement beginning before people report deciding to move. If the brain has started the action before you consciously choose, your feeling of willing it seems to come too late to cause it. It denies that the conscious sense of choosing is what makes things happen. Its appeal: it claims experimental backing for what philosophers argue in the abstract. Everyday case: you sometimes find your hand reaching for a snack before you feel you have decided to have one.","example":"Benjamin Libet’s 1983 experiment: people flexed their wrist whenever they felt like it and noted, on a fast clock, when they first felt the urge. A brain signal called the readiness potential began roughly 350 milliseconds before the reported urge. Later brain-scan studies by John-Dylan Haynes’s team found patterns predicting simple choices seconds earlier, with modest accuracy.","history":"Benjamin Libet and colleagues published their timing study in the journal Brain in 1983. Daniel Wegner’s The Illusion of Conscious Will (2002) argued that the feeling of willing is an after-the-fact interpretation. Critics such as Alfred Mele, in Effective Intentions (2009), and Aaron Schurger, whose 2012 model treats the readiness potential as build-up of random neural noise, dispute what the findings show.","misread":"Libet himself did not conclude that free will is an illusion; he suggested we might still consciously veto an action after the urge appears.","variants":["Will as illusion: the feeling of willing is an inference, not a cause (Wegner)"],"reading":[{"title":"Skepticism About Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral-responsibility/"},{"title":"Free Will (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/freewill/"}]},"m3d|quarantine":{"summary":"Without deserved blame, we may still restrain dangerous people, but only as much as protection requires, as we quarantine carriers of disease.","idea":"The quarantine model, from Derk Pereboom, says that even if no one deserves punishment, society may still protect itself. We quarantine people with dangerous diseases though they are not to blame, and by the same right we may detain dangerous offenders. But the analogy limits what is allowed: only the least harmful measures, decent conditions and a focus on rehabilitation. It denies that suffering is ever deserved for its own sake. Its appeal: it keeps public safety without retribution. Everyday case: a school keeps a contagious student home kindly, and only for as long as necessary.","example":"Norway’s prison system is often cited in these debates: comfortable conditions, a strong focus on education and rehabilitation, and low reoffending rates. Defenders of the quarantine model say it shows that a system aimed at safety and reintegration, rather than deserved suffering, can work in practice.","history":"Derk Pereboom proposed the model in Living Without Free Will (2001), drawing on Ferdinand Schoeman’s work on preventive detention. Gregg Caruso developed it into a “public health-quarantine model” in Rejecting Retributivism (2021), stressing prevention by tackling social causes of crime such as poverty and poor education.","misread":"The model does not license locking people up indefinitely; the quarantine analogy restricts detention to what genuine safety requires.","variants":["Public health-quarantine model (Caruso): prevention and social causes first"],"reading":[{"title":"Skepticism About Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral-responsibility/"}]},"m3d|answer":{"summary":"Without deserved blame, we can still hold people to account by asking them to explain themselves, repair harm and reform, all aimed at the future.","idea":"Forward-looking blame keeps much of our practice of responsibility but changes its justification. We still call wrongdoers to account, express disapproval and expect apology and change, because doing so protects others, repairs relationships and helps people improve. It denies that anyone deserves to suffer simply for what they did. Its appeal: it keeps moral conversation and accountability alive without retribution. Everyday case: a coach tells a player who skipped practice that it let the team down, not to make him suffer, but so it won’t happen again.","example":"Pereboom’s “answerability”: when someone acts badly, we can ask why they did it and invite them to judge their own action. This serves three forward-looking aims: protecting potential victims, restoring damaged relationships and helping the wrongdoer’s moral growth. None of them needs backward-looking desert.","history":"J.J.C. Smart’s “Free Will, Praise and Blame” (1961) argued that blame is justified by its effects. Derk Pereboom’s Free Will, Agency, and Meaning in Life (2014) developed a forward-looking answerability account. Manuel Vargas’s Building Better Beings (2013) defends a revisionist, forward-looking justification of responsibility practices, and restorative justice movements share many of the same aims.","misread":"Forward-looking blame is not mere manipulation; it addresses people as reasoners who can understand criticism and change.","variants":["Answerability (Pereboom): calling people to account without desert","Agency cultivation model (Vargas): responsibility practices justified by building better agents"],"reading":[{"title":"Skepticism About Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral-responsibility/"},{"title":"Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-responsibility/"}]},"m3d|abolish":{"summary":"We should give up blame and responsibility talk altogether, treating wrongdoing as a problem to solve rather than a fault to hold against anyone.","idea":"Responsibility abolitionism says the very concepts of moral responsibility and blame do more harm than good and should be dropped, not just reformed. Blame, even forward-looking blame, breeds anger, revenge and self-righteousness, and focuses on individuals instead of causes. We should instead ask what produced the harm and how to prevent it. It denies that holding people responsible is needed for a decent society. Its appeal: it promises more compassionate and effective responses to harm. Everyday case: when a machine injures a factory worker, a good investigation looks for causes and fixes, not someone to shame.","example":"Blame-free incident reporting in aviation: pilots and crews report their own mistakes without fear of punishment, and investigators fix the systems that made the errors likely. Abolitionists point to the safety gains of such systems as a model of what replacing blame with causal analysis can achieve.","history":"B.F. Skinner’s Beyond Freedom and Dignity (1971) urged replacing praise and blame with better design of environments. Bruce Waller’s Against Moral Responsibility (2011) and The Stubborn System of Moral Responsibility (2015) make the philosophical case for abolition. Spinoza had earlier held that understanding the causes of human action weakens hatred.","misread":"Abolitionists do not ignore harm or refuse to restrain dangerous people; they reject the blaming frame, not protection or prevention.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Skepticism About Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral-responsibility/"}]},"m3d|illusion":{"summary":"Nobody truly deserves blame, but belief in free will and desert is socially valuable, so it is better that most people keep believing it.","idea":"Saul Smilansky’s illusionism holds that, at the deepest level, no one has the free will that would ground true desert, yet our belief that we do plays a vital role. It supports respect for persons, self-esteem and a sense of justice. If everyone fully accepted that no one deserves anything, these goods might erode. So the illusion should largely stay in place, and the truth should be handled with care. It denies that knowing the truth is always better. Its appeal: realism about human psychology. Everyday case: a coach lets a team believe effort alone decides games, because the belief makes them try harder.","example":"Kathleen Vohs and Jonathan Schooler’s 2008 study: participants who read a passage arguing that free will is an illusion cheated more on a later task than those who read a neutral passage. Illusionists cite such findings, though later attempts to replicate them have had mixed results.","history":"Saul Smilansky set out the view in Free Will and Illusion (2000). It echoes older arguments for useful beliefs, such as the “noble lie” in Plato’s Republic. Free will skeptics such as Derk Pereboom and Gregg Caruso reply that life without the illusion would be better, not worse, and that honesty is safer than managed belief.","misread":"Smilansky does not urge lying to everyone; he holds that the illusion is already widespread and that destroying it carelessly would cause harm.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Skepticism About Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral-responsibility/"}]},"m3e|yes":{"summary":"Responsibility depends on how an action actually came about, from the agent’s own reasons, not on whether the agent could have done otherwise.","idea":"The source view says what matters for responsibility is the actual source of your action. In the hidden device case you chose Ben for your own reasons and the device did nothing, so you are as responsible as ever, even though you could not have done otherwise. It denies the “principle of alternative possibilities”, that responsibility requires being able to do otherwise. Its appeal: unused safeguards don’t seem to change who did what. Everyday case: a lifeguard who would have stopped you swimming too far doesn’t make you less responsible for turning back on your own.","example":"The quiz case is a Frankfurt case, after Harry Frankfurt’s “Alternate Possibilities and Moral Responsibility” (1969). In his version, Black secretly ensures Jones will do what Black wants, but Jones does it on his own and Black never steps in. Frankfurt concluded that Jones is responsible though he could not have done otherwise.","history":"Harry Frankfurt’s 1969 paper launched the debate, with Locke’s locked room as a forerunner. John Martin Fischer built semicompatibilism on it. Source incompatibilists such as Derk Pereboom and Eleonore Stump accept Frankfurt’s conclusion while holding that responsibility still requires that the action not be determined by outside causes.","misread":"Accepting the source view does not settle the free will debate: both compatibilists and some incompatibilists hold it.","variants":["Source compatibilism: the right actual sequence is compatible with determinism (Fischer)","Source incompatibilism: being the true source requires indeterminism (Pereboom, Stump)"],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Responsibility and the Principle of Alternative Possibilities (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/alternative-possibilities/"},{"title":"Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-responsibility/"}]},"m3e|no":{"summary":"Being responsible requires having had a genuine alternative; if you could not have done otherwise, you are not fully responsible for what you did.","idea":"The leeway view upholds the principle of alternative possibilities: you are morally responsible for what you did only if you could have done otherwise. In the device case you could never have ended up voting for Ana, so your responsibility is reduced. It denies that the actual sequence of events alone settles responsibility. Its appeal: blame seems unfair if there was nothing else you could have done, and the principle that “ought implies can” points the same way. Everyday case: we don’t blame someone for breaking a promise to meet us while they were in hospital.","example":"The “flicker of freedom” strategy, so named by its critic John Martin Fischer: even in a Frankfurt case, some tiny alternative remains, such as starting to lean toward Ana, which would trigger the device. Defenders argue that this flicker is enough to ground responsibility; critics say it is too thin to matter.","history":"Aristotle linked voluntary action to what is “up to us”. Peter van Inwagen’s “Ability and Responsibility” (1978), Carl Ginet, David Widerker and Robert Kane defended leeway views against Frankfurt. Margery Naylor (1984) and Michael Otsuka’s “Incompatibilism and the Avoidability of Blame” (1998) argued for versions of the principle.","misread":"Leeway theorists need not reject determinism; some compatibilists accept the principle and read “could have done otherwise” conditionally.","variants":["Flicker of freedom: tiny remaining alternatives ground responsibility","Avoidability of blame (Otsuka): you are blameworthy only if you could have avoided blame"],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Responsibility and the Principle of Alternative Possibilities (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/alternative-possibilities/"}]},"m3e|dilemma":{"summary":"Frankfurt-style cases fail: either your choice was determined before you made it, or the device could not know in time which way you were going.","idea":"The dilemma defence targets the hidden device’s need for a warning sign. Either the sign guarantees what you will choose, in which case your choice was determined and the case assumes what incompatibilists deny, or it does not, in which case you could still have chosen Ana and had an alternative after all. Either way, the case fails to show responsibility without alternatives. Its appeal: it exposes a hidden assumption in a famous argument. Everyday case: a mind reader who claims to know your pick in advance either has a cue that settles it or is guessing.","example":"The blush: in many versions of the case, the device watches for a prior sign, such as the person blushing, that reliably comes just before a decision for Ana. The dilemma asks whether the blush guarantees that decision. If it does, the outcome was settled before the choice; if it does not, the decision for Ana remained possible.","history":"Robert Kane raised the objection in Free Will and Values (1985). David Widerker’s “Libertarianism and Frankfurt’s Attack on the Principle of Alternative Possibilities” (1995) and Carl Ginet’s “In Defense of the Principle of Alternative Possibilities” (1996) developed it. Later Frankfurt-style cases tried to avoid any prior sign, and the debate continues.","misread":"The defence does not claim Frankfurt cases are impossible to describe; it says they either beg the question or leave an alternative open.","variants":["Kane-Widerker objection: the prior sign either determines the choice or leaves it open"],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Responsibility and the Principle of Alternative Possibilities (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/alternative-possibilities/"}]},"m5|make":{"summary":"Though you did not choose your starting traits, you can be responsible for who you become through the choices you make with them.","idea":"Self-making views say responsibility for character is built over time. You start with genes and an upbringing you did not choose, but your choices, especially in hard moments, shape your character, and later actions flow from that shaped self. It denies that unchosen origins cancel all responsibility for character. Its appeal: it fits the experience of moral growth and the idea that adults are held to higher standards than children. Everyday case: two siblings with the same upbringing make different choices as teenagers and grow into very different adults.","example":"Aristotle’s sick man in the Nicomachean Ethics, Book III: someone who became ill by ignoring his doctors cannot now become healthy just by wishing, yet he is responsible for his illness, because it came from his choices. Likewise, Aristotle argued, an unjust person is responsible for having become unjust through repeated unjust acts.","history":"Aristotle argued in the Nicomachean Ethics (Book III) that we are partly responsible for our characters, since they come from repeated actions. Robert Kane’s “self-forming actions”, in The Significance of Free Will (1996), and Jean-Paul Sartre’s lecture Existentialism Is a Humanism (published 1946) both make self-creation central, though in very different ways.","misread":"Self-making views do not say you made yourself from nothing; they say responsibility grows from earlier choices, starting small.","variants":["Aristotelian habituation: character formed by repeated chosen acts","Self-forming actions (Kane): undetermined choices that shape character","Existentialist self-creation (Sartre): we define ourselves through our acts"],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-responsibility/"}]},"m5|own":{"summary":"How your values came about doesn’t matter for responsibility; what matters is that they are genuinely yours now, endorsed and part of your character.","idea":"Ownership views hold that responsibility depends on your present psychological structure, not your history. If your values are ones you identify with, reflect on and act from, they are yours, whatever genes or upbringing produced them. It denies that you must have created yourself to be responsible. Its appeal: no one could meet a self-creation standard, and in daily life we judge people by who they are now. Everyday case: we still admire a kind person even though loving parents helped make her kind.","example":"Alfred Mele’s Ann and Beth: Ann became generous through her own development; Beth was secretly reprogrammed by neuroscientists to have exactly Ann’s values. A purely present-focused ownership view must say Beth is as responsible as Ann, which many find hard to accept, so some theorists add a history condition.","history":"Harry Frankfurt, in “Three Concepts of Free Action” (1975), held that responsibility depends on present identification, not on how one came to be that way. Gary Watson’s valuational view, Angela Smith’s “rational relations” view and T.M. Scanlon’s work are related. Alfred Mele argued in Autonomous Agents (1995) that history matters too.","misread":"Ownership views do not ignore manipulation entirely; many add conditions to exclude values implanted by brainwashing.","variants":["Structural views: only present psychology counts (Frankfurt)","Historical views: values must not have come about by manipulation (Mele)","Attributability views: actions reflect the agent’s own evaluative stance (Watson, Smith)"],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-responsibility/"},{"title":"Personal Autonomy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/personal-autonomy/"}]},"m5|practice":{"summary":"Responsibility is grounded in our natural human practice of resentment, gratitude, indignation and forgiveness, which no theory about causes could overturn.","idea":"The reactive attitudes view, from P.F. Strawson, says responsibility is not a metaphysical fact waiting for science to confirm or refute. It is rooted in our emotional responses to each other’s good or ill will: resentment when someone wrongs us, gratitude when they help. These attitudes are part of human relationships, and their justification lies inside that framework. It denies that the truth of determinism could give us reason to abandon them wholesale. Its appeal: it moves the debate from physics to human life. Everyday case: if a friend cancels on you to help a stranger, your resentment fades; if she cancels out of spite, it grows.","example":"Strawson’s “objective attitude”: sometimes we view a person as something to be managed, handled or treated, as with a very young child or someone in the grip of severe mental illness. Strawson argued we could not take this stance toward everyone all the time and remain recognisably human, and no general thesis like determinism could make us.","history":"P.F. Strawson’s British Academy lecture “Freedom and Resentment” (1962) founded the approach. Gary Watson’s “Responsibility and the Limits of Evil” (1987), R. Jay Wallace’s Responsibility and the Moral Sentiments (1994) and Michael McKenna’s Conversation and Responsibility (2012) developed it. Pamela Hieronymi defended Strawson’s argument in Freedom, Resentment, and the Metaphysics of Morals (2020).","misread":"Strawson does not say responsibility is just a feeling; our practices have internal standards for when resentment is fitting or excused.","variants":["Strawsonian compatibilism: responsibility grounded in the reactive attitudes","Conversational theory: blame as a move in a moral conversation (McKenna)"],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-responsibility/"}]},"m5|noult":{"summary":"Since no one creates themselves, no one can ultimately deserve credit or blame for who they are or for what flows from it.","idea":"This view says ultimate responsibility, the kind that would make you deserve praise or blame in the deepest sense, requires being the ultimate source of yourself. Your character comes from genes and surroundings you did not choose, and any effort to change it comes from a character you also did not choose. It denies that anyone meets this standard. Its appeal: it seems to follow from plain facts about our origins, and it encourages humility and compassion. Everyday case: a successful person can see that their discipline, health and opportunities all trace back to good fortune.","example":"Thomas Nagel’s “constitutive luck”, from “Moral Luck” (1979): someone born with a greedy or cowardly temperament did not choose it, yet we blame them for what flows from it. Nagel showed how far luck reaches into what we hold people responsible for, and this view follows that thought to its end.","history":"Galen Strawson’s Basic Argument (1994) is the most cited version. Thomas Nagel’s “Moral Luck” (1979) exposed constitutive luck. Robert Kane named the standard “ultimate responsibility” in The Significance of Free Will (1996), though Kane thinks self-forming actions can meet it. Neil Levy’s Hard Luck (2011) argues that nobody does.","misread":"Denying ultimate responsibility is compatible with holding people answerable; it rules out only deep desert, not every kind of responsibility.","variants":["Basic Argument (Galen Strawson): no one is the cause of themselves","Luck pincer (Levy): choices are always subject to present luck or constitutive luck"],"reading":[{"title":"Skepticism About Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral-responsibility/"}]},"m5|nothing":{"summary":"Praise and blame are justified by their good effects in shaping behaviour and character, not by any backward-looking fact about desert.","idea":"Forward-looking responsibility treats praise and blame as tools. They are fair when they help people learn, improve and act better, and when they protect others. It denies that responsibility needs a deeper metaphysical fact such as self-creation. Unlike outright skepticism, it keeps responsibility but rebuilds it on future benefits. Its appeal: it makes sense of how we treat children and of everyday practices like coaching and feedback. Everyday case: a teacher praises a shy student’s first question in class, not because it was heroic, but to encourage more.","example":"Moritz Schlick’s answer in Problems of Ethics (1930): asking who is responsible for an act is asking where praise or punishment must be applied to influence future behaviour. A person is responsible, on this view, when blame can reach and change their motives.","history":"Moritz Schlick’s Problems of Ethics (1930) and J.J.C. Smart’s “Free Will, Praise and Blame” (1961) gave classic consequentialist accounts. Earlier, utilitarians such as Jeremy Bentham justified punishment by its effects on future conduct. Manuel Vargas’s Building Better Beings (2013) defends a modern version focused on cultivating agents who respond to moral reasons.","misread":"The view is not that blame is mere training, as with a pet; most versions address people as reasoners who can understand why they are criticised.","variants":["Consequentialist responsibility (Schlick, Smart): praise and blame justified by effects","Agency cultivation (Vargas): practices justified by building better agents"],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-responsibility/"}]},"k4|yes":{"summary":"You really do know ordinary facts about the world, such as that you have hands, even though a perfect dream is conceivable.","idea":"Anti-skepticism says the skeptic’s conclusion is false: you know you have hands, that there is a table in front of you, that you had breakfast. It denies that knowing requires first ruling out every wild alternative, such as dreaming or being in a simulation. Its appeal is simple: almost everyone, including skeptics outside the seminar room, acts as if they know these things. If you pack an umbrella because you saw rain through the window, you treat that as knowledge, not as a lucky guess. The hard part, which the follow-up question asks, is explaining how you know.","example":"G. E. Moore’s “Proof of an External World”: he raised one hand, then the other, and said here are two hands, so external things exist. Philosophers still argue over whether this is a brilliant reply that puts common sense first or a refusal to engage with the skeptic’s question.","history":"Resistance to skepticism is as old as skepticism. Aristotle (4th century BCE) held that demanding proof of everything leads to endless regress. Thomas Reid’s Inquiry into the Human Mind (1764) defended trust in the senses against Hume. G. E. Moore’s papers of 1925 and 1939 made common sense a starting point, and most twentieth-century epistemologists, including reliabilists and contextualists, side with the anti-skeptic in some form.","misread":"Anti-skepticism is not the claim that you can prove you are awake; most anti-skeptics accept that you cannot, and say knowledge does not need such proof.","variants":["Moorean anti-skepticism: we are more certain of ordinary facts than of any skeptical premise.","Externalist anti-skepticism: reliable senses give knowledge without our proving their reliability.","Contextualism: we know by everyday standards, though not by the skeptic’s raised standards."],"reading":[{"title":"Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism/"},{"title":"George Edward Moore (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moore/"}]},"k4|no":{"summary":"Since you cannot rule out that you are dreaming or deceived, you do not know even basic facts about the external world.","idea":"Radical skepticism about the external world says that knowledge requires being able to rule out alternatives you cannot distinguish from reality. A perfect dream, an evil demon or a computer simulation would feel exactly the same, so you cannot rule them out, so you do not know you have hands. It denies that confidence or habit amounts to knowledge. People find it compelling because each step looks hard to deny: if you cannot tell whether a convincing banknote is forged, you do not know it is genuine, and the skeptic only applies that rule everywhere.","example":"The brain in a vat: a scientist removes your brain, keeps it alive in a vat and feeds it electrical signals that perfectly mimic a normal life. Everything you see and feel would be just as it is now, so nothing in your experience can tell you that this is not your situation.","history":"Zhuangzi’s butterfly dream (4th century BCE) raised the worry in China. René Descartes’ Meditations (1641) gave it its modern form with the dream argument and the evil demon, though Descartes himself meant to answer it. Barry Stroud’s The Significance of Philosophical Scepticism (1984) and Peter Unger’s Ignorance (1975) argued the skeptical case is stronger than it looks. The brain-in-a-vat version became standard after Hilary Putnam discussed it in 1981.","misread":"Skeptics usually do not claim the world is fake; they claim only that we cannot know it is real.","variants":["Cartesian skepticism: we cannot rule out dreams or deceivers, so we lack knowledge of the external world.","Closure-based skepticism: if you knew you had hands, you could know you are not a handless brain in a vat, which you cannot."],"reading":[{"title":"Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism/"},{"title":"The Brain in a Vat Argument (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/brainvat/"}]},"k4|pointless":{"summary":"A question that no possible observation could settle has no real content, so “am I really dreaming forever?” says nothing.","idea":"Verificationism is a theory of meaning: a factual statement means something only if some possible experience could count for or against it. A “dream” that could never be detected, even in principle, makes no difference to any experience, so the skeptic’s hypothesis is not false but empty. It denies that there can be facts beyond all possible evidence. Its attraction is that it dissolves endless arguments at a stroke. If two friends argue whether everything in the universe doubled in size overnight, including rulers, a verificationist says there is nothing for them to disagree about.","example":"The invisible gardener: two explorers find a clearing with flowers. One insists a gardener tends it. They set guards, fences and dogs, and nothing is ever detected. The believer says the gardener is invisible, silent and intangible. The other asks how this differs from no gardener at all.","history":"The Vienna Circle of the 1920s and 1930s, led by Moritz Schlick with Rudolf Carnap, made verifiability the test of meaning. A. J. Ayer’s Language, Truth and Logic (1936) spread the idea in English. Antony Flew adapted the gardener parable from John Wisdom in 1950. Charles Peirce’s pragmatic maxim (1878) was an earlier cousin. By the 1960s most philosophers had abandoned strict verificationism, partly because the principle seems unverifiable itself.","misread":"Verificationism is not Karl Popper’s falsifiability test: Popper’s criterion separates science from non-science, while verificationism claims unverifiable sentences are literally meaningless.","variants":["Strong verificationism: a statement is meaningful only if experience could conclusively establish it.","Weak verificationism (Ayer): experience need only make the statement more or less probable."],"reading":[{"title":"Logical Empiricism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logical-empiricism/"},{"title":"Vienna Circle (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/vienna-circle/"}]},"k4a|fallib":{"summary":"Knowledge needs reliable belief-forming senses, not proof; you can know things without being able to show a skeptic that you do.","idea":"This view combines two ideas. Fallibilism says knowledge does not require certainty: you can know something even though you could in principle be wrong. Externalism says what turns true belief into knowledge can be a fact you have no inner access to, such as your eyesight actually being reliable. So if your senses really do track the world, you know you have hands, whether or not you can rule out the dream. It denies that the knower must be able to answer the skeptic. A dog knows where its bowl is without any argument at all.","example":"The chicken sexer: trained workers sort newborn chicks by sex with very high accuracy, yet often cannot say how they do it and have no theory of their own reliability. Many people still say they know which chick is which. Externalists say ordinary perception works the same way.","history":"Charles Peirce coined “fallibilism” in the late nineteenth century. Externalism took shape in the 1960s and 1970s: D. M. Armstrong’s Belief, Truth and Knowledge (1973) compared a knower to a reliable thermometer, and Alvin Goldman’s “What Is Justified Belief?” (1979) launched process reliabilism. Later externalists include Ernest Sosa and John Greco. Some scholars read the Indian Nyāya school, which treats knowledge as true cognition produced by a reliable source (pramāṇa), in a similar way.","misread":"Externalists do not say you should ignore evidence; they say the reliability that grounds knowledge need not be something you can prove from the inside.","variants":["Process reliabilism (Goldman): a belief is justified if produced by a reliable process.","Proper functionalism (Plantinga): knowledge comes from faculties working as designed in the right environment."],"reading":[{"title":"Reliabilist Epistemology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reliabilism/"},{"title":"Fallibilism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/fallibil/"}]},"k4a|common":{"summary":"You can be more certain that you have hands than of any philosophical premise used to argue that you don’t.","idea":"The common-sense response turns the skeptic’s argument upside down. The skeptic reasons: I can’t rule out dreaming, so I don’t know I have hands. The common-sense philosopher reasons: I do know I have hands, so one of the skeptic’s premises must be false. Which way you run the argument depends on what you are most sure of, and the claim is that ordinary certainties win. It denies that philosophy can overturn basic convictions. Compare a clever proof that motion is impossible: you keep walking and conclude the proof has a flaw, even before you find it.","example":"Zeno’s paradoxes: Zeno argued that a runner can never finish a race, since she must first cover half the distance, then half the remainder, forever. Few people concluded that nobody ever arrives anywhere. Common-sense philosophers treat skeptical arguments the same way: as puzzles to diagnose, not reasons to give up knowledge.","history":"Thomas Reid, in An Inquiry into the Human Mind (1764) and Essays on the Intellectual Powers of Man (1785), founded the Scottish common-sense school against Hume. G. E. Moore revived the approach in “A Defence of Common Sense” (1925) and “Proof of an External World” (1939). In recent decades William Lycan, Thomas Kelly and others have defended “Moorean facts” as fixed points that philosophy must respect.","misread":"Common-sense philosophy is not “whatever most people believe is true”; it singles out a small set of basic certainties, not popular opinions.","variants":["Reidian common sense: first principles of belief are built into human nature and need no proof.","Mooreanism: when an argument clashes with a Moorean fact, reject a premise rather than the fact."],"reading":[{"title":"George Edward Moore (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moore/"},{"title":"Thomas Reid (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reid/"}]},"k4a|hinge":{"summary":"Some certainties, like “I have hands,” are not conclusions we reached but the fixed background that all reasoning and doubt rely on.","idea":"Hinge epistemology says that some beliefs work like the hinges of a door: they must stay put for the door of inquiry to turn. “I have hands,” “the Earth has existed for a long time,” “my name is what I think it is” are not hypotheses supported by evidence; they are what makes weighing evidence possible. The view denies that every belief needs grounds, and that doubting a hinge is a sensible move. Think of checking a calculation: you can recheck the sums, but you cannot sensibly doubt that the digits on the page stay the same while you look away.","example":"Moore’s hand revisited: Wittgenstein agreed with Moore that “I have hands” is not in doubt, but thought Moore was wrong to say he knew it. A doubt about one’s own hands, he suggested, would not be a careful doubt but a sign that the person no longer grasps what doubting is.","history":"Ludwig Wittgenstein wrote the notes collected as On Certainty in 1949 to 1951, in response to Moore; they were published in 1969. Crispin Wright’s “entitlement” view (2004), Duncan Pritchard’s Epistemic Angst (2015), Annalisa Coliva’s Extended Rationality (2015) and Danièle Moyal-Sharrock’s work developed rival readings, and the label “hinge epistemology” spread in the 2010s.","misread":"Hinge epistemology does not say hinges are arbitrary or chosen; they are held fast by our practices, not adopted like rules of a game.","variants":["Entitlement view (Wright): we are rationally entitled to accept hinges without evidence.","Non-epistemic view (Moyal-Sharrock, Pritchard): hinges are not beliefs or knowledge at all, but ways of acting."],"reading":[{"title":"Certainty (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/certainty/"},{"title":"Ludwig Wittgenstein (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wittgenstein/"}]},"k4a|pragma":{"summary":"Knowledge serves action; a doubt that would change nothing you do is not a genuine doubt and needs no answer.","idea":"The pragmatic response treats belief as a habit of action and doubt as a real hesitation that blocks action. Inquiry starts only when genuine doubt arises, for example when a plan fails or the evidence surprises you. The dream hypothesis creates no such hesitation: nobody stops eating because they might be dreaming. So the pragmatist denies that philosophy must answer every logically possible doubt before we can claim knowledge. Real doubt needs a real reason. A mechanic doubts the battery because the car won’t start, not because batteries could, in theory, all be illusions.","example":"Peirce’s doubt-belief theory: in “The Fixation of Belief” he described doubt as an uneasy state we struggle to escape and belief as a calm habit that guides action. Descartes’ method of doubting everything, he argued, was a paper doubt that no one can actually feel.","history":"Charles Peirce attacked Cartesian doubt in “Some Consequences of Four Incapacities” (1868) and “The Fixation of Belief” (1877). William James and John Dewey, especially in Dewey’s The Quest for Certainty (1929), developed the view that knowledge is a tool of living rather than a mirror of reality. Later pragmatists such as Richard Rorty and Cheryl Misak continued the line.","misread":"Pragmatists are not saying “believe whatever is useful”; they say doubt and inquiry need real triggers, and beliefs still answer to experience.","variants":["Peircean pragmatism: real doubt arises from surprise and drives inquiry toward settled belief.","Deweyan instrumentalism: knowing is a way of solving problems in experience."],"reading":[{"title":"Pragmatism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pragmatism/"},{"title":"Charles Sanders Peirce (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/peirce/"}]},"k4a|abduct":{"summary":"A real external world is the best explanation of your orderly experiences, so you are justified in believing it over dream hypotheses.","idea":"Inference to the best explanation reasons the way detectives and scientists do: when several hypotheses fit the evidence, believe the one that explains it best, by being simpler, more unified and less ad hoc. The real-world hypothesis explains why experiences fit together, why objects reappear where you left them, and why other people report the same things. A dream or simulation can fit the evidence only by copying all that structure and adding a hidden layer. It denies that equal fit with the evidence means equal reason to believe. You conclude a mouse lives in your kitchen from the nibbled bread, not that crumbs appear by chance.","example":"Russell’s cat: in The Problems of Philosophy, Russell noted that if a cat appears in one corner of the room and later in another, the simplest explanation is that it moved through the space between. If it were only sense-data, its getting hungry while unseen would be inexplicable.","history":"Charles Peirce described “abduction,” reasoning to an explanatory hypothesis, in the late nineteenth century. Bertrand Russell’s The Problems of Philosophy (1912) used simplicity to defend belief in an external world. Gilbert Harman named “inference to the best explanation” in 1965. Jonathan Vogel’s “Cartesian Skepticism and Inference to the Best Explanation” (1990) is the modern statement of this anti-skeptical strategy.","misread":"The view does not claim to prove there is a world; it claims belief in one is more reasonable than the rivals, which is weaker but enough.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Abduction (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/abduction/"}]},"k4a|proof":{"summary":"A sound philosophical argument can actually rule out the skeptical scenario, so the dream or deceiver worry is defeated rather than ignored.","idea":"This position accepts the skeptic’s challenge on its own terms: if knowledge needs the dream ruled out, then rule it out by argument. Different philosophers offer different proofs: from God’s goodness, from the nature of meaning, or from the conditions of self-awareness. What they share is the claim that some necessary truth makes global deception impossible or self-defeating. It denies that the skeptic’s scenario is even coherent once examined. Compare someone claiming “I am lying right now”: careful reasoning, not new observation, shows something has gone wrong.","example":"Putnam’s brain in a vat: words refer to what causes their use. A vat-brain’s word “vat” would be caused by electrical signals, not real vats, so when it thinks “I am a brain in a vat” it says something false. So the thought “I am a brain in a vat” can never be truly thought.","history":"René Descartes’ Meditations (1641) argued that a perfect God would not let us be systematically deceived. Immanuel Kant added the “Refutation of Idealism” to the second edition of the Critique of Pure Reason (1787). Hilary Putnam’s Reason, Truth and History (1981) gave the semantic argument. Later work by Anthony Brueckner and Crispin Wright tested how much Putnam’s argument actually proves.","misread":"Putnam’s argument, even if sound, does not show you are not in a vat by observing anything; it is about what your words can mean.","variants":["Theological proof (Descartes): a non-deceiving God guarantees clear and distinct perceptions.","Semantic externalist proof (Putnam): the skeptic’s hypothesis cannot be truly thought.","Transcendental argument (Kant): self-awareness in time requires something permanent outside me."],"reading":[{"title":"Content Externalism and Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-content-externalism/"},{"title":"Descartes’ Epistemology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/descartes-epistemology/"}]},"k4a|context":{"summary":"Whether you “know” depends on the standards in play in a conversation; everyday standards are met, the skeptic’s raised standards are not.","idea":"Contextualism says the word “know” is like “tall” or “flat”: its standard shifts with context. A road can be flat for driving but not flat for a marble. In ordinary talk, “I know I have hands” is true. When someone raises the dream possibility, the standards rise, and “I don’t know I have hands” becomes true in that conversation. Both claims are right in their own contexts, so the skeptic and the ordinary person are not really contradicting each other. It denies that skeptical arguments show everyday knowledge claims were false all along.","example":"The bank case: on Friday you drive past your bank and say “I know it’s open on Saturday, I was there two Saturdays ago.” Normally that seems fine. But if a large cheque bounces unless deposited by Saturday, and your partner notes banks change hours, saying “I know” now seems wrong.","history":"Stewart Cohen’s “How to Be a Fallibilist” (1988), Keith DeRose’s “Contextualism and Knowledge Attributions” (1992) and “Solving the Skeptical Problem” (1995), and David Lewis’s “Elusive Knowledge” (1996) built the view. Rivals that also explain the bank case include subject-sensitive invariantism (Jason Stanley, John Hawthorne) and John MacFarlane’s relativism.","misread":"Contextualism is not relativism about truth; once the context is fixed, a knowledge claim is simply true or false.","variants":["Relevant alternatives (Lewis): knowing means ruling out all possibilities not properly ignored in the context.","Sensitivity-based contextualism (DeRose): context sets how far from actuality your belief must track the truth."],"reading":[{"title":"Epistemic Contextualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/contextualism-epistemology/"},{"title":"Contextualism in Epistemology (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/contextu/"}]},"k4a|struct":{"summary":"If we live in a simulation, tables, hands and cities are still real; they are digital objects rather than ultimately made of particles.","idea":"Simulation realism accepts that you might be in a simulation and denies that this would make your world fake. Your hands would exist, be solid in the only sense solidity ever had, and cause what they seem to cause. They would just be built from computational processes. On this view, the simulation hypothesis is a hypothesis about what lies underneath physics, like the claim that atoms are made of quarks. It denies that “simulated” means “illusory.” Learning that a song you love is stored as ones and zeros does not make it not a song.","example":"The Matrix (1999 film): Neo learns his world is a computer simulation. Chalmers argues that the people inside the Matrix are mostly right: when they say “there is a chair here,” there is one, made of bits, so they are not deceived about ordinary things, only about what those things are ultimately made of.","history":"Nick Bostrom’s “Are You Living in a Computer Simulation?” (2003) argued the scenario deserves real probability. David Chalmers replied in “The Matrix as Metaphysics” (2003) that it would not undermine most of our knowledge, and expanded the case in Reality+ (2022). The idea echoes physicist John Wheeler’s “it from bit” proposal (1989) that physical reality may rest on information.","misread":"Simulation realism is not the claim that we are in a simulation; it is the claim that even if we were, ordinary objects would still be real.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Content Externalism and Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-content-externalism/"},{"title":"Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism/"}]},"k4a|ideal":{"summary":"Physical objects are made of experiences or ideas, so there is no hidden world behind appearances for a dream to fake.","idea":"Idealism removes the gap the skeptic exploits. The skeptic worries that experience might not match an outside world. The idealist says there is no outside world of mind-independent matter to match: a hand just is a stable, shared pattern of seeing and feeling. So knowing your hand is knowing your experiences, which are directly present. Idealists deny that matter exists independently of minds, not that tables are real. Rainbows are a helpful comparison: a rainbow is real and lawful, yet it exists only as a pattern of light seen from a point of view.","example":"The tree in the quad: if no one is looking at a tree, does it still exist? Berkeley’s answer was yes, because God always perceives it. A famous limerick by Ronald Knox makes the point: the tree continues to be, since it is observed by God.","history":"George Berkeley’s Principles of Human Knowledge (1710) and Three Dialogues (1713) argued that to be is to be perceived. Vasubandhu’s Twenty Verses (4th or 5th century CE) defended the Yogācāra Buddhist view that the world is “consciousness only.” Later forms include the absolute idealism of Hegel and F. H. Bradley. Kant’s transcendental idealism (1781) is related but distinct, since it keeps unknowable things in themselves.","misread":"Idealists do not say the world is imaginary or that you can change it by wishing; experience is orderly and shared, and you cannot walk through walls.","variants":["Subjective idealism (Berkeley): only minds and their ideas exist, with God sustaining order.","Yogācāra (Vasubandhu): what appear as outer objects are only manifestations of consciousness.","Absolute idealism (Hegel, Bradley): reality is a single all-inclusive mind or experience."],"reading":[{"title":"Idealism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/idealism/"},{"title":"George Berkeley (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/berkeley/"},{"title":"Vasubandhu (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/vasubandhu/"}]},"k4b|pyrrho":{"summary":"Suspend judgment about how things really are, live by appearances and custom, and tranquillity will follow.","idea":"Pyrrhonism is skepticism as a way of life, not a doctrine. For any claim about hidden reality, the Pyrrhonist finds equally strong reasons on the other side, and so suspends judgment. Crucially, the Pyrrhonist does not even assert that knowledge is impossible, since that would be taking a side. Daily life goes on by appearances: honey seems sweet, so you eat it, without claiming honey is sweet in itself. The payoff is ataraxia, calm, because you stop being anxious about which theory is true. Think of someone who stops fighting over politics and finds they sleep better.","example":"The painter Apelles: Sextus tells of a painter who could not depict a horse’s foam and, in frustration, threw his sponge at the picture. The mark it left looked exactly like foam. Pyrrhonists likewise found calm came not from finding the truth, but from giving up the hunt.","history":"Pyrrho of Elis (c. 360 to 270 BCE), who travelled to India with Alexander the Great, inspired the movement but wrote nothing. Aenesidemus revived it in the first century BCE, and Sextus Empiricus’s Outlines of Pyrrhonism (c. 2nd century CE) is our main source. Its rediscovery in the sixteenth century shaped Michel de Montaigne’s Essays (1580).","misread":"Pyrrhonists do not claim “nothing can be known”; that claim is a dogma too, and they suspend judgment on it as well.","variants":["Rustic Pyrrhonism: suspend judgment on all beliefs whatsoever.","Urbane Pyrrhonism: suspend judgment only on theoretical claims about hidden natures, keeping everyday beliefs."],"reading":[{"title":"Ancient Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-ancient/"},{"title":"Sextus Empiricus (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sextus-empiricus/"},{"title":"Pyrrho (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/pyrrho/"}]},"k4b|academic":{"summary":"Nothing can be known with certainty, but some impressions are more persuasive than others, and we may rely on them in degrees.","idea":"Academic skepticism holds that we lack knowledge, understood as certainty, but denies that this leaves us with no guidance. We can grade impressions as more or less plausible, check them against each other, and act on the best. It differs from Pyrrhonism by being willing to say that knowledge is unavailable and by keeping probable belief. Many find it the reasonable middle: it takes skeptical arguments seriously without paralysis. A weather forecast that says “80 per cent chance of rain” is a good model: no certainty, but a clear basis for taking an umbrella.","example":"Carneades’ coiled rope: in a dark room you see what might be a snake. If little is at stake, the first impression is enough. If more is at stake, you check it from several angles, then poke it with a stick. The more important the decision, the more tests the impression should pass.","history":"Arcesilaus (c. 316 to 241 BCE) turned Plato’s Academy toward skepticism, appealing to Socrates’ profession of ignorance. Carneades (214 to 129 BCE) developed the idea of the persuasive impression. Cicero’s Academica (45 BCE) preserved the debates. David Hume described his own view as a “mitigated” or academic skepticism in the Enquiry concerning Human Understanding (1748).","misread":"Academic skeptics are not committed to believing nothing; they deny certainty and knowledge, but allow graded, revisable belief.","variants":["Arcesilaus: withhold assent, yet act on what is “reasonable.”","Carneades: rank impressions by how persuasive, consistent and tested they are."],"reading":[{"title":"Ancient Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-ancient/"},{"title":"Carneades (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/carneades/"}]},"k4b|natural":{"summary":"Skeptical arguments may be unanswerable, but human nature forces us to believe anyway, so the doubts cannot govern life.","idea":"Natural belief, often called Humean naturalism, grants that reason cannot refute the skeptic. But belief in the external world, in causes and in the future is not produced by reasoning in the first place. It comes from instinct and habit, which are too strong for argument to dislodge. So the right attitude is a relaxed, mitigated skepticism: modest about what reason proves, but unworried. It denies that we must justify these beliefs before holding them. You can find an argument for the unreality of time baffling and still set an alarm for tomorrow.","example":"Hume’s backgammon: at the end of Book I of the Treatise, after reasoning himself into deep doubt, Hume reports that nature cures him. He dines, plays backgammon and talks with friends, and after a few hours his skeptical conclusions seem cold and strained.","history":"David Hume’s A Treatise of Human Nature (1739 to 1740) and Enquiry concerning Human Understanding (1748) are the classic sources. Thomas Reid, Hume’s critic, also grounded belief in human constitution. Norman Kemp Smith’s The Philosophy of David Hume (1941) emphasised this naturalist reading, and P. F. Strawson’s Skepticism and Naturalism (1985) argued that such beliefs are not open to serious doubt.","misread":"The view does not say skepticism is refuted; it says skepticism is true as a conclusion of reason but powerless as a way of life.","variants":["Humean naturalism: instinct and custom fix basic beliefs that reason cannot support.","Strawsonian naturalism: some beliefs are framework commitments that demands for justification simply bypass."],"reading":[{"title":"David Hume (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume/"},{"title":"Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism/"}]},"k11|jtb":{"summary":"Knowledge is justified true belief: believe it, have good reason for it, and be right. Nothing else is needed.","idea":"The justified-true-belief analysis lists three conditions: you believe the claim, the claim is true, and your belief is well supported. Defending it in the stopped-clock case means biting the bullet: your reason was good, your belief was true, so you knew, and the sense that you didn’t is an untrustworthy reaction. Defenders deny that philosophers’ intuitions about odd cases should outweigh a clean, general theory. They note that in daily life we credit people with knowledge on just these grounds: a student who reasons well from reliable notes and gets the answer right is said to know it.","example":"Gettier’s ten coins: Smith has strong evidence that Jones will get the job and that Jones has ten coins in his pocket, so he believes “the man who gets the job has ten coins.” In fact Smith gets the job, and Smith also happens to have ten coins. Justified, true, yet lucky.","history":"Plato’s Theaetetus and Meno (4th century BCE) discuss knowledge as true belief with an account. Edmund Gettier’s three-page “Is Justified True Belief Knowledge?” (1963) offered counterexamples that most philosophers accepted. Some experimental philosophers, starting with Weinberg, Nichols and Stich (2001), questioned whether Gettier reactions are universal, though later large cross-cultural studies found them widespread. A minority defend the analysis, or a simpler one, against the cases.","misread":"Gettier’s cases do not show justification is unnecessary for knowledge; they aim to show it is not sufficient when combined with truth.","variants":["Classical JTB: knowledge is exactly justified true belief, so Gettier intuitions are mistaken.","Knowledge as true belief (Sartwell): justification is evidence of knowledge but not part of it."],"reading":[{"title":"The Analysis of Knowledge (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/knowledge-analysis/"},{"title":"Gettier Problems (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/gettier/"}]},"k11|track":{"summary":"To know, your belief must be connected to the truth: you wouldn’t believe it if it were false, or couldn’t easily be wrong.","idea":"Modal theories of knowledge say knowledge requires a non-accidental link between belief and fact, spelled out in terms of what would happen in nearby situations. Sensitivity asks: if it were false, would you still believe it? Safety asks: in situations close to this one, would your belief still be true? Reliabilism asks whether the belief came from a process that usually produces truths. The stopped clock fails all three tests. These views deny that good reasons alone ensure knowledge. A thermometer stuck at 20 degrees on a 20-degree day isn’t telling you the temperature.","example":"Fake barn county: Henry drives through a region dotted with barn facades, flat fronts that look like barns from the road. He happens to look at the one real barn and believes “that’s a barn.” He is right and his eyes work fine, but he could too easily have been fooled.","history":"Fred Dretske’s “Conclusive Reasons” (1971) and Robert Nozick’s Philosophical Explanations (1981) developed the sensitivity condition. Alvin Goldman’s “Discrimination and Perceptual Knowledge” (1976) presented the barn case, credited to Carl Ginet, and Goldman’s 1979 paper founded process reliabilism. Ernest Sosa (1999), Timothy Williamson and Duncan Pritchard developed the safety condition.","misread":"Sensitivity does not require that you could never be wrong; only that, in the closest situations where the claim is false, you would not believe it.","variants":["Sensitivity (Nozick, Dretske): if p were false, you would not believe p.","Safety (Sosa, Pritchard, Williamson): in nearby situations, your belief would not be false.","Process reliabilism (Goldman): your belief came from a truth-conducive process."],"reading":[{"title":"The Analysis of Knowledge (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/knowledge-analysis/"},{"title":"Reliabilist Epistemology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reliabilism/"}]},"k11|defeat":{"summary":"Knowledge is justified true belief whose justification doesn’t depend on any false assumption, or can’t be undermined by a hidden truth.","idea":"This repair keeps the classical analysis and adds a fourth condition. In the clock case, your reasoning secretly passed through a falsehood: “the clock is working.” Remove beliefs that rest on false steps and the counterexample disappears. A more refined version, defeasibility theory, says knowledge requires that there be no true fact which, if you learned it, would destroy your justification. It denies that knowledge requires a new kind of ingredient beyond good reasons. If a friend tells you the shop is open, but has mixed it up with another shop that happens to keep the same hours, you were right only by accident.","example":"Tom Grabit: you see Tom, whom you know well, steal a library book. Unknown to you, his mother has claimed Tom was far away and his identical twin was in the library. That misleading claim seems to undermine your knowledge, though in fact she is lying and has no twin.","history":"Michael Clark’s “Knowledge and Grounds” (1963) proposed the no-false-lemma condition months after Gettier. Keith Lehrer and Thomas Paxson’s “Knowledge: Undefeated Justified True Belief” (1969) and Peter Klein’s work in the 1970s developed defeasibility theory, using cases like Tom Grabit to sort misleading defeaters from genuine ones.","misread":"","variants":["No false lemmas (Clark): knowledge must not be inferred from any false belief.","Defeasibility (Lehrer and Paxson, Klein): no true fact would defeat the justification if added."],"reading":[{"title":"The Analysis of Knowledge (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/knowledge-analysis/"},{"title":"Gettier Problems (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/gettier/"}]},"k11|virtue":{"summary":"Knowledge is true belief that is a success of skill: you are right because of your intellectual abilities, not by luck.","idea":"Virtue epistemology moves the focus from beliefs to believers. Like a skilled performance in sport or music, knowing is an achievement: true belief that comes about through the exercise of cognitive abilities, such as good eyesight, careful reasoning or open-mindedness. In the clock case your reading skill did not explain your getting it right; the coincidence did. The view denies that knowledge is just a matter of a belief’s properties. A tennis player’s ace is credited to her if her serve made it land, not if the ball hit the net cord and dribbled over.","example":"The stranger in Chicago: a visitor asks a passer-by the way to a landmark and is told correctly. He seems to gain knowledge, yet the credit belongs mostly to the passer-by. Jennifer Lackey used this case to test how much of knowledge must be “to your credit.”","history":"Ernest Sosa introduced virtue epistemology in “The Raft and the Pyramid” (1980) and developed it in A Virtue Epistemology (2007). Linda Zagzebski’s Virtues of the Mind (1996) drew on Aristotle’s ethics, and Lorraine Code’s Epistemic Responsibility (1987) stressed traits of character. John Greco’s Achieving Knowledge (2010) defends knowledge as credit-worthy success. Lackey’s critique appeared in 2007.","misread":"Virtue here does not mean moral goodness; it means intellectual excellence, which can be a faculty like vision as well as a trait like curiosity.","variants":["Virtue reliabilism (Sosa, Greco): virtues are reliable faculties such as perception and memory.","Virtue responsibilism (Zagzebski, Code): virtues are character traits such as open-mindedness and care."],"reading":[{"title":"Virtue Epistemology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epistemology-virtue/"}]},"k11|infall":{"summary":"You know only when your grounds make error impossible; reasons that could in principle mislead you never yield knowledge.","idea":"Infallibilism says that if your evidence is compatible with your being wrong, you do not know. The stopped clock shows the danger of fallible grounds: any non-guaranteeing reason can come apart from the truth, and then luck decides. Infallibilists deny that probability, however high, adds up to knowledge. The view has a clean logic, explains why Gettier cases arise, and matches how “know” sounds in some sentences: “I know it, but I might be wrong” sounds contradictory. Its cost is that knowledge may become rare, unless evidence itself is redefined to include facts about the world.","example":"The lottery: you hold one ticket in a fair lottery of a million. Before the draw, it is overwhelmingly likely you will lose, yet it seems wrong to say you know you’ll lose. Infallibilists explain why: your evidence, the odds, leaves losing merely probable, not guaranteed.","history":"Plato’s Republic separates knowledge, which cannot err, from opinion. René Descartes’ Meditations (1641) sought foundations immune to all doubt. Peter Unger’s Ignorance (1975) argued that knowledge requires certainty, so we know almost nothing. Recent defenders such as Julien Dutant argue that infallibilism need not lead to skepticism, especially if your evidence can include what you see to be the case.","misread":"Infallibilism is not the claim that people never make mistakes; it is a claim about what counts as knowing when they don’t.","variants":["Skeptical infallibilism (Unger): knowledge needs certainty, so we know little or nothing.","Non-skeptical infallibilism: evidence includes worldly facts, so ordinary knowledge can still be infallible."],"reading":[{"title":"Certainty (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/certainty/"},{"title":"Fallibilism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/fallibil/"}]},"k11|basic":{"summary":"Knowledge cannot be analysed into belief plus further conditions; it is a basic mental state, used to explain belief and evidence.","idea":"Knowledge-first epistemology reverses the traditional order. Instead of building knowledge from belief, truth and justification, it treats knowing as the fundamental notion and explains the others through it: belief is aiming at knowledge, and your evidence is what you know. The failure of decades of attempted analyses is taken as a sign there is nothing to find. It denies that knowledge is a combination of an inner state plus a lucky outer match. Seeing is a good comparison: you can’t build “seeing that the cup is broken” from “seeming to see” plus extra conditions, yet you know what it is.","example":"Children’s words: studies of language development suggest children use and understand “know” earlier than “think.” Jennifer Nagel cites such evidence to argue that knowledge is a basic category in how humans read minds, not a complicated construction out of belief.","history":"Timothy Williamson’s Knowledge and Its Limits (2000) launched the programme, arguing that knowledge is the most general factive mental state and that one’s evidence equals one’s knowledge. Jennifer Nagel’s “Knowledge as a Mental State” (2013) linked it to psychology. Critics and developers include Jessica Brown, Alexander Bird and Clayton Littlejohn, who extended it to theories of justification.","misread":"Knowledge-first does not say knowledge is indefinable mystery; it can be described and theorised, just not broken into simpler ingredients.","variants":["E = K (Williamson): your evidence is exactly what you know.","Knowledge norm of assertion: you should assert only what you know."],"reading":[{"title":"The Analysis of Knowledge (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/knowledge-analysis/"},{"title":"Knowledge-First Theories of Justification (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/knowledge-first-theories-of-justification/"}]},"k3|rational":{"summary":"Pure reasoning can give knowledge of real features of the world, not merely of definitions, without relying on observation.","idea":"Rationalism says some substantive truths are knowable a priori, by reason alone. Geometry, logic, basic metaphysics and perhaps some ethics are examples: you grasp why they must hold, rather than collecting cases. Classical rationalists added that some ideas are innate, present before experience. Rationalism denies that every fact about the world must be learned through the senses. Its attraction is the necessity of mathematics: you didn’t learn that every triangle’s angles add to 180 degrees by measuring triangles, and no measurement could teach you that it holds for all of them.","example":"Plato’s slave boy: in the Meno, Socrates draws squares in the sand and asks an uneducated boy questions, without telling him any answers. The boy works out how to double a square’s area. Plato concluded the knowledge was already in him, waiting to be recollected.","history":"Plato’s Meno and Phaedo (4th century BCE) argued for knowledge by recollection. In the seventeenth century René Descartes, Baruch Spinoza and G. W. Leibniz built systems on reason; Leibniz’s New Essays (written 1704) defended innate ideas against Locke. In recent times Laurence BonJour’s In Defense of Pure Reason (1998) and George Bealer defended a priori intuition.","misread":"Rationalists do not say experience is useless; most hold it is essential for many truths, but not for all.","variants":["Innate ideas: some concepts or truths are part of our mind’s original equipment.","Intuition and deduction: reason can directly see some truths and derive others from them."],"reading":[{"title":"Rationalism vs. Empiricism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rationalism-empiricism/"},{"title":"A Priori Justification and Knowledge (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/apriori/"}]},"k3|empiric":{"summary":"All knowledge of the world comes from experience; reason only rearranges what the senses provide, or analyses our own definitions.","idea":"Empiricism says the mind starts without built-in knowledge of the world, and that every substantive fact is learned through the senses. Truths that seem knowable by thought alone are either about meanings, like “all bachelors are unmarried,” or are really general facts learned from experience. It denies innate knowledge and a priori insight into reality. Empiricism is attractive because it fits science, which tests ideas against observation, and because it is modest: you can’t sit in an armchair and deduce the boiling point of water or what pineapple tastes like.","example":"Molyneux’s problem: a man born blind learns to tell a cube from a sphere by touch. If he suddenly gained sight, could he tell which is which just by looking? Locke answered no: the link between how things look and feel must be learned from experience.","history":"John Locke’s Essay concerning Human Understanding (1689) compared the newborn mind to blank paper. George Berkeley and David Hume extended the view, and John Stuart Mill’s A System of Logic (1843) treated even arithmetic as empirical. The logical empiricists of the 1920s and 1930s made maths true by meaning, and W. V. O. Quine’s “Two Dogmas of Empiricism” (1951) blurred that line. In India the Cārvāka school accepted perception as the only reliable source of knowledge.","misread":"Empiricists need not deny that the mind has inborn abilities; they deny inborn knowledge or ideas about the world.","variants":["Classical empiricism (Locke, Hume): all ideas come from sense experience or reflection.","Logical empiricism (Carnap): a priori truths are true by meaning and say nothing about the world.","Radical empiricism (Mill, Quine): even logic and maths answer ultimately to experience."],"reading":[{"title":"Rationalism vs. Empiricism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rationalism-empiricism/"},{"title":"Logical Empiricism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logical-empiricism/"}]},"k3|kant":{"summary":"The mind supplies built-in forms, like space, time and causality, that structure experience, so some truths are both informative and knowable before experience.","idea":"Kant’s view splits the difference between rationalism and empiricism. Experience provides content; the mind provides the framework that makes experience possible at all. Because the framework is ours, we can know a priori that every experience will be in space and time and governed by cause and effect. Kant called these truths synthetic a priori: informative, yet not learned by observation. The price is that we know things only as they appear to minds like ours, not as they are in themselves. Think of a camera’s format: every photo will be rectangular, and you know that before taking one.","example":"Copernican revolution in philosophy: Copernicus explained the sky’s motion by moving the observer. Kant proposed the same flip: instead of assuming our knowledge must conform to objects, suppose objects as we experience them must conform to our ways of knowing. Then a priori knowledge of them is no mystery.","history":"Immanuel Kant’s Critique of Pure Reason (1781, revised 1787) set out the view, prompted by Hume’s doubts about causation. Arthur Schopenhauer’s The World as Will and Representation (1818) adapted it. The Neo-Kantian movements in Germany, around Hermann Cohen and others, dominated late nineteenth-century philosophy. P. F. Strawson’s The Bounds of Sense (1966) revived interest in English. Konrad Lorenz later suggested evolution explains our built-in categories.","misread":"Kant does not say the world is only in your head; he called himself an empirical realist: tables really are in space, as experienced.","variants":["Transcendental idealism: space, time and categories belong to how we experience, not to things in themselves.","Evolutionary Kantianism (Lorenz): our built-in categories are adaptations shaped by natural selection."],"reading":[{"title":"Kant’s Transcendental Idealism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-transcendental-idealism/"},{"title":"Rationalism vs. Empiricism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rationalism-empiricism/"}]},"k13|corresp":{"summary":"A statement is true when it matches how the world is: truth is a relation between what we say and the facts.","idea":"The correspondence theory says truth depends on reality: “the cat is on the mat” is true because of an arrangement of a cat and a mat, and false otherwise. Truth is not a matter of what we believe, agree on, or find useful. Most people find this obvious, which is part of its appeal. It explains why we can be wrong together, and why discovering a truth feels like finding something rather than deciding something. A map is the everyday model: it is accurate when its roads line up with the real roads, whatever the mapmaker hoped.","example":"Truthmakers: what makes “there are penguins” true? Penguins. What makes “Caesar crossed the Rubicon” true? A past event. Truthmaker theory asks, for every truth, what in the world it depends on, and finds puzzles in negative truths like “there are no unicorns.”","history":"Aristotle’s Metaphysics (Book Gamma) defined truth as saying of what is that it is. Medieval thinkers such as Thomas Aquinas spoke of the adequation of intellect and thing. Bertrand Russell and the early Wittgenstein (Tractatus, 1921) made facts the truthmakers of propositions. J. L. Austin defended a version in 1950, and D. M. Armstrong developed truthmaker theory in Truth and Truthmakers (2004).","misread":"Correspondence is a view about what truth is, not about how we find out; a correspondence theorist can grant that checking always uses other beliefs.","variants":["Fact-based correspondence (Russell, early Wittgenstein): true propositions mirror the structure of facts.","Truthmaker theory (Armstrong): every truth is made true by something that exists."],"reading":[{"title":"The Correspondence Theory of Truth (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/truth-correspondence/"},{"title":"Truth (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/truth/"}]},"k13|cohere":{"summary":"A belief is true when it fits into a complete, consistent and mutually supporting system of beliefs, not by matching something outside.","idea":"The coherence theory says truth consists in membership of an ideal, comprehensive system of beliefs that hang together. Its defenders argue we can never step outside our beliefs to compare them with bare reality; every check uses more beliefs. So truth must be defined within the web. It denies that truth is a relation to mind-independent facts. Think of a jigsaw: you decide a piece belongs by how it fits its neighbours and the emerging picture, not by comparing it with the finished image, which you don’t have.","example":"Neurath’s boat: we are like sailors who must rebuild their ship on the open sea, plank by plank, never able to bring it into dry dock and start from scratch. Each belief is tested and replaced while relying on the rest of the structure.","history":"Elements appear in Spinoza and Hegel. British idealists F. H. Bradley and H. H. Joachim (The Nature of Truth, 1906) and later Brand Blanshard (The Nature of Thought, 1939) defended coherence as the nature of truth. Otto Neurath gave his boat image in 1932 while arguing with fellow logical empiricists. Nicholas Rescher’s The Coherence Theory of Truth (1973) is a later statement.","misread":"The coherence theory of truth differs from coherentism about justification: many think coherence is how we justify beliefs without thinking it is what truth is.","variants":["Idealist coherence (Bradley, Blanshard): truth is coherence within an all-encompassing system of thought.","Neurath’s coherence: statements are compared only with other statements, never with bare facts."],"reading":[{"title":"The Coherence Theory of Truth (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/truth-coherence/"}]},"k13|pragma":{"summary":"True beliefs are those that would survive all further inquiry and keep working in practice, rather than mirroring a hidden reality.","idea":"The pragmatist theory ties truth to inquiry and practice. A belief is true if it would hold up under unlimited investigation, prove reliable in action and fit future experience. It denies that truth is a static copy relation that we could never check, while keeping a sharp line between true belief and mere opinion: what you happen to like believing is not what would survive endless testing. Its appeal is that it explains why truth matters: true beliefs are the ones you can build on. A bridge design is “right” if it carries traffic and passes every test you could throw at it.","example":"James’s cow path: lost in the woods and starving, you spot a cow path and believe there is a farmhouse at its end. Following it, you find one. For James, the belief’s truth shows up in its leading you successfully to the house, through the course of experience.","history":"Charles Peirce’s “How to Make Our Ideas Clear” (1878) identified truth with the opinion that inquiry is fated to converge on. William James’s Pragmatism (1907) linked truth to what works in experience, and John Dewey favoured “warranted assertibility” in his Logic (1938). Hilary Putnam’s Reason, Truth and History (1981) proposed truth as idealised rational acceptability, and Cheryl Misak has revived Peirce’s version.","misread":"Pragmatists do not say a belief is true just because believing it makes you happy; it must keep working under testing and further experience.","variants":["Peircean: truth is what inquiry would agree on in the long run.","Jamesian: truth is what is verified and works in the course of experience.","Putnam’s internal realism: truth is idealised rational acceptability."],"reading":[{"title":"The Pragmatic Theory of Truth (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/truth-pragmatic/"}]},"k13|deflate":{"summary":"Saying a statement is true adds nothing beyond the statement; “true” is a useful logical device, not a substantial property needing a theory.","idea":"Deflationism says the whole content of truth is captured by instances like: “snow is white” is true if and only if snow is white. There is no deeper nature of truth to discover, any more than there is a deep nature of the word “and.” But “true” is still useful, because it lets us endorse claims we can’t list, as in “everything in the contract is true.” Deflationists deny that truth explains anything substantial, such as why beliefs succeed. Agreeing with a friend by saying “that’s true” works just like repeating their sentence.","example":"Blind endorsement: you trust your doctor and say “whatever she told me is true,” though you don’t remember her exact words. Without the word “true” you could only list every sentence she said. Deflationists say generalising like this is the whole job of the truth predicate.","history":"Gottlob Frege hinted at it in “The Thought” (1918), and Frank Ramsey’s “Facts and Propositions” (1927) gave the redundancy theory. Alfred Tarski’s work on truth (1933) supplied the famous schema, though his own view is debated. W. V. O. Quine’s Philosophy of Logic (1970) defended disquotation, and Paul Horwich’s Truth (1990) and Hartry Field developed modern minimalism.","misread":"Deflationists do not deny that statements are true; they deny that truth is a substantial property with a hidden nature.","variants":["Redundancy theory (Ramsey): “it is true that p” just means p.","Disquotationalism (Quine, Field): “‘p’ is true” is a way of reusing the sentence p.","Minimalism (Horwich): truth is fully described by all instances of the equivalence schema."],"reading":[{"title":"Deflationism About Truth (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/truth-deflationary/"}]},"k13|plural":{"summary":"Truth comes in more than one kind: different subject matters are true in different ways, such as by correspondence or by proof.","idea":"Truth pluralism says no single theory fits every area of discourse. Correspondence seems right for “Paris is in France,” but strained for “7 + 5 = 12” or “cruelty is wrong,” where there may be no physical facts to match. Pluralists propose that truth in those areas consists in something else, such as provability or idealised agreement. What unites them is a shared role: truth is what assertion aims at and what valid reasoning preserves. It denies that any one monistic theory is complete. Compare “winning”: winning at chess and winning a race are different achievements that play the same role.","example":"The mixed inference problem: “Torture is wrong, and if torture is wrong it should be banned; so it should be banned.” If the premises are true in different ways, in what sense is the conclusion true, and what does valid reasoning preserve? Pluralists must answer this.","history":"Crispin Wright’s Truth and Objectivity (1992) proposed that truth varies by discourse, with “superassertibility” for some areas. Michael Lynch’s Truth as One and Many (2009) developed a functionalist version in which a single role is played by different properties. Nikolaj Jang Lee Linding Pedersen and others extended the debate. Christine Tappolet’s 2000 paper on mixed inferences set a central challenge.","misread":"Pluralism is not relativism: it does not say truth varies by person or culture, but by subject matter.","variants":["Alethic functionalism (Lynch): one truth role, realised by different properties in different domains.","Wright’s platitude-based pluralism: any property satisfying truth’s core platitudes counts as truth for a domain."],"reading":[{"title":"Pluralist Theories of Truth (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/truth-pluralist/"}]},"k1|true":{"summary":"Our best scientific theories are approximately true, including what they say about unobservable things like electrons and black holes.","idea":"Scientific realism says mature, successful science gives us a roughly accurate picture of the world, beyond what we can see. Electrons, genes and dark matter are taken to exist much as theories describe. Realists deny that the observable and unobservable deserve different attitudes: if microscopes and particle detectors justify beliefs, they justify beliefs about tiny things. The view’s pull lies in success: theories predict new phenomena, guide technologies and agree with each other. Your phone works because transistors behave as quantum theory says, which would be a strange coincidence if electrons were fictions.","example":"Perrin and the atom: around 1908 Jean Perrin measured Avogadro’s number from Brownian motion, and it matched values found by quite different methods. The agreement helped persuade even doubters such as Wilhelm Ostwald that atoms exist, which realists see as a model of reasonable belief in unobservables.","history":"J. J. C. Smart (1963) and Hilary Putnam (“Mathematics, Matter and Method,” 1975) argued that realism alone keeps science’s success from being a miracle. Richard Boyd developed the case in the 1970s and 1980s. Larry Laudan’s “A Confutation of Convergent Realism” (1981) raised the history of failed theories. Stathis Psillos’s Scientific Realism (1999) is a leading defence.","misread":"Realists do not say current theories are final or certainly true; they say they are approximately true and improving.","variants":["No-miracles realism (Putnam, Boyd): realism is the best explanation of science’s success.","Convergent realism: successive theories get closer to the truth over time."],"reading":[{"title":"Scientific Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/scientific-realism/"}]},"k1|selective":{"summary":"Believe the parts of theories that do the real predictive work and survive revolutions, such as equations or manipulable entities, not every story.","idea":"Selective realism takes the history of discarded theories seriously while keeping realism. When old theories were replaced, their successful parts were often kept: equations, structures or causal entities, while their wider pictures were dropped. So the rational bet is to believe those parts of today’s theories. It denies both that everything in successful theories is true and that nothing is. Compare an old explorer’s map: the coastline was right, while the sea monsters in the margins were not, and you can tell which features the voyages actually relied on.","example":"Fresnel’s ether: in the 1820s Augustin Fresnel modelled light as waves in an elastic ether. The ether was abandoned, but his equations for how light reflects and refracts survived into Maxwell’s electromagnetic theory. John Worrall argued that Fresnel had captured the structure of light, though not its nature.","history":"Henri Poincaré’s Science and Hypothesis (1902) noted that relations survive theory change. John Worrall’s “Structural Realism: The Best of Both Worlds?” (1989) revived the idea. Ian Hacking’s Representing and Intervening (1983) and Nancy Cartwright defended entity realism. Philip Kitcher (1993) and Stathis Psillos (1999) argued for trusting only the “working posits.” James Ladyman and Steven French developed ontic structural realism.","misread":"","variants":["Structural realism (Worrall; Ladyman and French): believe the mathematical structure, not the nature behind it.","Entity realism (Hacking, Cartwright): believe in entities we can use as tools to intervene.","Working-posit realism (Kitcher, Psillos): believe the parts actually responsible for successful predictions."],"reading":[{"title":"Structural Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/structural-realism/"},{"title":"Scientific Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/scientific-realism/"}]},"k1|tools":{"summary":"Science reliably gets the observable world right, but we need not believe its claims about hidden, unobservable reality are true.","idea":"Scientific anti-realism grants that science is a triumph at prediction and control, and denies that this success shows theories truly describe unobservable reality. Many different theories could fit the same data, and the history of science is full of successful but false theories. So it is reasonable to trust what theories say about observable outcomes and stay neutral, or deflationary, about the rest. Anti-realists deny that empirical success is a reliable sign of truth about hidden things. A good tax model predicts revenue well without anyone thinking it describes taxpayers’ thoughts.","example":"Ptolemy’s epicycles: for over a thousand years, astronomers predicted planetary positions using circles on circles around a central Earth. The model worked well enough for calendars and navigation, yet its picture of the heavens was false. Anti-realists ask why today’s successes should be different.","history":"Andreas Osiander’s preface to Copernicus (1543) treated astronomy as calculation. Ernst Mach and Pierre Duhem (The Aim and Structure of Physical Theory, 1906) resisted reading physics literally. Bas van Fraassen’s The Scientific Image (1980) revived anti-realism, Larry Laudan (1981) pressed the historical argument, and Kyle Stanford’s Exceeding Our Grasp (2006) added the problem of unconceived alternatives.","misread":"Anti-realists do not think science is arbitrary or unreliable; they are cautious only about what it says beyond observation.","variants":["Instrumentalism: theories are tools for prediction, not descriptions.","Constructive empiricism (van Fraassen): accept theories as empirically adequate, stay agnostic beyond.","Kuhnian relativism: theories are true only relative to frameworks."],"reading":[{"title":"Scientific Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/scientific-realism/"},{"title":"Constructive Empiricism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/constructive-empiricism/"}]},"k1b|instrument":{"summary":"Scientific theories are instruments for predicting and controlling observations; talk of unobservables is a calculating device, not a description.","idea":"Instrumentalism treats a theory like a well-made tool: you ask whether it works, not whether it is true. Statements about electrons or fields are shorthand for complex patterns linking observations, or bookkeeping devices for making predictions. On strict versions, such statements are not even candidates for truth or falsehood. The view denies that the aim of science is to describe hidden reality. It appeals because it avoids metaphysical risk and explains why outdated theories stay useful. A spreadsheet formula isn’t “true” or “false”; it either gives you the right totals or it doesn’t.","example":"Osiander’s preface: when Copernicus’s sun-centred astronomy was published in 1543, an unsigned preface by Andreas Osiander told readers that the hypotheses need not be true or even probable, only that they give calculations matching observations. It became a classic statement of the instrumentalist attitude.","history":"Osiander’s preface (1543) and George Berkeley’s De Motu (1721), which treated forces as mathematical hypotheses, are early examples. Ernst Mach and Pierre Duhem developed the view around 1900. John Dewey used “instrumentalism” for his broader pragmatism. Some logical empiricists treated theoretical terms instrumentally, and Karl Popper criticised the view in Conjectures and Refutations (1963).","misread":"Instrumentalists do not say science is mere guessing; they hold its tools are highly reliable for prediction and control.","variants":["Semantic instrumentalism: theoretical sentences lack truth-values; they are inference rules.","Epistemic instrumentalism: theories might be true, but their value lies only in prediction."],"reading":[{"title":"Scientific Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/scientific-realism/"}]},"k1b|empirical":{"summary":"Science aims at theories that are true about observable things; accepting a theory means believing only that, staying agnostic about unobservables.","idea":"Constructive empiricism distinguishes believing a theory from accepting it. To accept a theory is to believe it is empirically adequate, meaning true about everything observable, past, present and future, and to work within it. Whether it is also true about electrons is left open: not denied, just not believed. Constructive empiricists read theories literally, unlike instrumentalists, but deny that science needs belief beyond the observable. Think of hiring a guide: you trust her directions, which you can check, without having to believe her stories about the mountain spirits.","example":"Jupiter’s moons versus electrons: van Fraassen says Jupiter’s moons count as observable, because an astronaut could fly close and see them unaided. Electrons are not, because no possible human observer could see one. So belief in moons is warranted, while acceptance of electrons is enough.","history":"Bas van Fraassen introduced the view in The Scientific Image (1980) and extended it in Laws and Symmetry (1989) and The Empirical Stance (2002). Critics in Images of Science (1985), edited by Paul Churchland and Clifford Hooker, challenged his line between observable and unobservable. Van Fraassen’s later work develops a “structural empiricism.”","misread":"Constructive empiricism does not deny that electrons exist; it says belief in them is not required by science and not rationally compelled.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Constructive Empiricism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/constructive-empiricism/"}]},"k1b|pragmatic":{"summary":"Science succeeds by producing theories that keep working as inquiry continues; that ongoing success is all that “getting it right” means.","idea":"Pragmatism about science rejects the picture of theories as mirrors that might or might not match a hidden reality. Inquiry is a problem-solving activity, and a theory is right to the extent that it keeps resolving problems and surviving tests. This applies equally to talk of electrons and talk of tables, so there is no special worry about unobservables. It denies the realist and anti-realist framing alike. Compare a working cure: a doctor doesn’t ask whether the treatment matches a picture of health; she asks whether it keeps working across patients and trials.","example":"Peirce’s convergence: Peirce pointed out that investigators using quite different methods, such as different measurements of the speed of light, tend toward the same answer. He took this tendency of honest inquiry to converge as what scientific truth amounts to, with no need for a further mirror-like match.","history":"Charles Peirce (“How to Make Our Ideas Clear,” 1878), William James and John Dewey (Logic: The Theory of Inquiry, 1938) founded the tradition. Richard Rorty’s Philosophy and the Mirror of Nature (1979) attacked representation as the aim of knowledge. Hilary Putnam’s internal realism (1981) and the neo-pragmatism of Cheryl Misak and Huw Price continue it.","misread":"Pragmatists do not think science is just whatever is convenient; theories must keep surviving demanding tests from experience.","variants":["Peircean convergence: truth is what scientific inquiry would ultimately agree on.","Rortyan neo-pragmatism: drop the idea of knowledge as mirroring nature altogether."],"reading":[{"title":"Pragmatism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pragmatism/"},{"title":"John Dewey (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dewey/"}]},"k1b|framework":{"summary":"Scientific claims are judged within frameworks, or paradigms, and no neutral standpoint exists to say which framework matches reality.","idea":"Framework relativism holds that what counts as evidence, as a good explanation, and even what key words mean, is set by the shared framework of a scientific community. When frameworks change in a revolution, the old and new may be partly incommensurable: they lack a common measure. So asking which one truly describes reality has no framework-free answer. It denies that science simply accumulates truths toward a fixed picture. Compare languages that divide colours differently: neither carving is “the” correct one.","example":"The duck-rabbit: a famous drawing can be seen as a duck or a rabbit, but not both at once, though the lines never change. Kuhn compared scientific revolutions to such a gestalt switch: after Copernicus or Einstein, scientists saw the same data as a different world.","history":"Thomas Kuhn’s The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962) introduced paradigms and incommensurability. Paul Feyerabend’s Against Method (1975) pushed further. Rudolf Carnap’s linguistic frameworks (1950) offered a different, logical version. The sociology of scientific knowledge, such as the Edinburgh “Strong Programme” of David Bloor (1976), carried the idea into social science.","misread":"Kuhn did not say science makes no progress or is irrational; he held later paradigms solve more puzzles, while doubting they get closer to truth.","variants":["Kuhnian paradigms: normal science works within a paradigm until a revolution replaces it.","Feyerabend’s pluralism: no fixed method; competing frameworks should flourish."],"reading":[{"title":"Thomas Kuhn (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/thomas-kuhn/"},{"title":"The Incommensurability of Scientific Theories (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incommensurability/"}]},"k2|yes":{"summary":"Numbers and other mathematical objects really exist, outside space and time, independently of minds, and mathematicians discover their properties.","idea":"Mathematical Platonism says that mathematical statements are about real objects, which are abstract: not physical, not mental, and unchanging. “There are infinitely many primes” is true because there really are such numbers. Platonism denies that mathematics is invented or merely conventional. It attracts many mathematicians because it explains why maths feels like exploring a landscape, why results surprise their discoverers, and why everyone who proves a theorem gets the same answer. If you and a stranger across the world both work out the hundredth prime, you will agree, as if you had both visited the same place.","example":"Independent discovery: Isaac Newton and G. W. Leibniz developed calculus separately in the late seventeenth century, with different notations, and found the same results. Platonists see such convergence as two explorers reaching the same continent rather than two authors writing the same novel by chance.","history":"Plato’s theory of Forms (Republic, 4th century BCE) is the namesake. Gottlob Frege’s The Foundations of Arithmetic (1884) argued numbers are objects. Kurt Gödel defended Platonism in essays such as “What Is Cantor’s Continuum Problem?” (1947, revised 1964). Paul Benacerraf’s “Mathematical Truth” (1973) set the central challenge of how we could know abstract objects.","misread":"Platonists don’t think numbers float somewhere in space; “outside space and time” means they have no location at all.","variants":["Object Platonism (Frege, Gödel): numbers are abstract objects.","Full-blooded Platonism (Balaguer): every consistent mathematical theory describes some real abstract objects."],"reading":[{"title":"Platonism in the Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/platonism-mathematics/"}]},"k2|no":{"summary":"Mathematics does not require abstract objects existing apart from minds and matter; numbers are something else, or nothing at all.","idea":"Anti-Platonism groups every view that rejects free-standing abstract numbers. Some say maths studies physical patterns, some that it is mental construction, some that it is invented rules, a symbol game or a useful fiction. What unites them is the denial that “2 + 2 = 4” requires a realm of non-physical, non-mental objects. The main motivation is epistemic and economic: it is hard to see how creatures in space and time could know anything about objects that cannot affect us, and an extra realm is a large addition to reality. If you count chairs, you seem to need only chairs.","example":"Benacerraf’s puzzle: we know about things by causal contact: seeing, touching, measuring. Abstract numbers cause nothing. So how could our beliefs about them be reliable? Benacerraf pressed this as a dilemma: the best account of mathematical truth seems to make mathematical knowledge mysterious.","history":"Aristotle (Metaphysics, Books M and N) rejected Plato’s separate Forms. Nelson Goodman and W. V. O. Quine’s “Steps Toward a Constructive Nominalism” (1947) attempted maths without abstract objects. Paul Benacerraf’s “Mathematical Truth” (1973) sharpened the case, and Hartry Field’s Science Without Numbers (1980) tried to show physics can do without numbers.","misread":"Anti-Platonists usually accept that “2 + 2 = 4” is correct; they disagree about what makes it correct, not whether it is.","variants":["Nominalism: no abstract objects exist at all.","Psychologism and intuitionism: mathematical objects are mental constructions."],"reading":[{"title":"Nominalism in the Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nominalism-mathematics/"},{"title":"Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/philosophy-mathematics/"}]},"k2a|intuition":{"summary":"We know mathematical truths through a kind of intellectual perception, an insight that grasps abstract objects much as sight grasps colours.","idea":"This view answers the Platonist’s puzzle about access by positing a special faculty. When you understand a simple proof or axiom, you don’t just follow rules; you see that it must be so. This rational insight is treated as a source of evidence comparable to perception. It denies that our only access to reality is through the senses. The appeal is phenomenological: anyone who has suddenly “got” a proof knows the experience of seeing a truth. When the reason a trick works clicks, it feels less like being told and more like noticing.","example":"A picture proof: arrange dots in growing squares. Adding 1, then 3, then 5, then 7 dots, each new L-shaped layer completes a bigger square, so the sum of the first n odd numbers is n squared. Once you see it, it seems you grasp why it holds for every n.","history":"Plato’s Meno and Phaedo (4th century BCE) described knowledge of Forms as recollection. Descartes spoke of clear and distinct perception. Kurt Gödel argued in 1964 that axioms of set theory force themselves upon us, a perception-like insight. Recent rationalists such as George Bealer and Laurence BonJour treat rational intuition as basic evidence, while critics follow Benacerraf in asking how it could work.","misread":"Mathematical intuition here does not mean a gut feeling or hunch; it means a disciplined intellectual insight, the kind that understanding a proof produces.","variants":["Gödelian intuition: a perception-like faculty that grasps the axioms of set theory.","Rational intuition (Bealer, BonJour): intellectual seemings are basic evidence for necessary truths."],"reading":[{"title":"Platonism in the Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/platonism-mathematics/"},{"title":"Kurt Gödel (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/goedel/"}]},"k2a|logic":{"summary":"Mathematics, or at least arithmetic, is logic in disguise; mathematical truths follow from logic plus definitions, so reasoning alone reveals them.","idea":"Logicism claims that numbers can be defined in purely logical terms and that arithmetic’s truths can be proved from logical laws. If so, our knowledge of maths is no more mysterious than our knowledge of logic. In Frege’s version the numbers are still real abstract objects, which is why logicism appears here as a Platonist answer. It denies that maths rests on a special intuition or on experience. Matching is the core insight: you can know two groups are the same size by pairing them off, before you can count.","example":"Hume’s Principle: the number of Fs equals the number of Gs exactly when the Fs and Gs can be paired off one to one. A waiter who puts one saucer under each cup knows there are as many cups as saucers without counting. Frege and the neo-logicists build arithmetic from this principle.","history":"Gottlob Frege’s Foundations of Arithmetic (1884) and Basic Laws of Arithmetic (1893 and 1903) launched logicism. Bertrand Russell found a contradiction in Frege’s system in 1901 and wrote to him in 1902. Whitehead and Russell’s Principia Mathematica (1910 to 1913) tried again. Crispin Wright’s Frege’s Conception of Numbers as Objects (1983) and Bob Hale revived it as neo-logicism.","misread":"Logicism is not the claim that maths is trivial; deriving arithmetic from logic took enormous work and taught us much about both.","variants":["Fregean logicism: numbers are logical objects defined via classes.","Russellian logicism: maths reduces to logic within type theory.","Neo-logicism (Wright, Hale): arithmetic follows from Hume’s Principle plus logic."],"reading":[{"title":"Logicism and Neologicism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logicism/"}]},"k2a|indisp":{"summary":"We should believe in numbers because our best scientific theories require them, just as they require electrons.","idea":"The indispensability argument says: believe in whatever your best scientific theories need. Physics cannot be stated without numbers, functions and spaces. So the evidence that supports physics also supports the existence of mathematical objects. It denies that mathematics needs its own special source of knowledge, like intuition; maths is confirmed together with science. Its attraction is that it uses the same standards for everything. If you trust a weather model enough to cancel a picnic, you seem to trust the equations, and the numbers, it is built from.","example":"Cicadas and primes: some periodical cicadas emerge every 13 or 17 years. One explanation is that prime cycles minimise overlap with predators and related species. Alan Baker argued that here the mathematics of primes does the explaining, making numbers indispensable not only for calculating but for explaining.","history":"W. V. O. Quine’s writings on ontological commitment, from “On What There Is” (1948), and Hilary Putnam’s Philosophy of Logic (1971) gave the classic argument. Hartry Field’s Science Without Numbers (1980) tried to dispense with maths in physics. Mark Colyvan’s The Indispensability of Mathematics (2001) defended the argument, and Alan Baker (2005) developed the explanatory version.","misread":"The argument does not say “maths is useful, so it’s true”; it says we are committed to whatever our best confirmed theory says exists.","variants":["Quine and Putnam’s argument: confirmation of science extends to the maths it quantifies over.","Explanatory indispensability (Baker, Colyvan): maths genuinely explains physical facts."],"reading":[{"title":"Indispensability Arguments in the Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mathphil-indis/"}]},"k2b|empiric":{"summary":"Mathematics studies real properties and patterns of physical things, such as quantity, symmetry and ratio, not objects in a separate abstract realm.","idea":"Aristotelian realism says mathematical properties are real features of the physical world, like colour or mass. The number of eggs in a box, the symmetry of a snowflake and the ratio of a guitar string’s lengths are found in things, not apart from them. Mathematics studies these properties in general. It denies both Platonism, since nothing exists outside space and time, and nominalism, since quantity and structure really exist. The appeal is that it explains why maths applies so well to the world: it is about the world. Six eggs in a box are six whether or not anyone counts them.","example":"The bridges of Königsberg: in 1736 Leonhard Euler proved that no walk could cross each of the city’s seven bridges exactly once. This is a fact about how the physical bridges and landmasses are connected, which no amount of trying could change, and it is a mathematical fact.","history":"Aristotle’s Physics (Book II) and Metaphysics (Books M and N) held that mathematicians study physical things abstracted from matter. The view persisted in medieval Aristotelianism. Penelope Maddy’s Realism in Mathematics (1990) argued that we perceive some sets. James Franklin’s An Aristotelian Realist Philosophy of Mathematics (2014) is the main recent statement.","misread":"Aristotelians do not say maths is just generalised measurement; they think its properties are real and can be studied with full rigour.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Aristotle and Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-mathematics/"},{"title":"Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/philosophy-mathematics/"}]},"k2b|construct":{"summary":"Mathematical objects are mental constructions; a statement is true only when we can construct a proof of it, step by step.","idea":"Intuitionism says mathematics is an activity of the mind. Numbers exist because we can build them, starting from one and adding one; a mathematical claim is true when there is a construction proving it. This leads to a revised logic: you may not assert “either P or not P” for an infinite domain unless you can prove one side. It denies that maths describes a ready-made realm with every question already settled. Its appeal is honesty: it claims only what we can actually establish. A recipe you can follow is the model of a mathematical object.","example":"Goldbach’s conjecture: is every even number greater than 2 the sum of two primes? Nobody knows. A classical mathematician says it is either true or false. An intuitionist says that, until someone proves or refutes it, there is no ground for asserting either.","history":"L. E. J. Brouwer founded intuitionism in his 1907 dissertation, partly inspired by Kant’s view of time. Arend Heyting formalised intuitionistic logic in 1930. Hermann Weyl was a sympathiser for a time. Michael Dummett’s Elements of Intuitionism (1977) argued for it from a theory of meaning, and Errett Bishop’s Foundations of Constructive Analysis (1967) showed much analysis can be done constructively.","misread":"Intuitionism in mathematics has nothing to do with ethical intuitionism or gut feelings; “intuition” refers to Kant’s mental construction in time.","variants":["Brouwerian intuitionism: maths is free creation of the mind, prior to language and logic.","Dummett’s intuitionism: meaning is given by proof conditions, so classical logic is unjustified.","Bishop’s constructivism: do mathematics using only constructive methods."],"reading":[{"title":"Intuitionism in the Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/intuitionism/"},{"title":"Luitzen Egbertus Jan Brouwer (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/brouwer/"}]},"k2b|mixed":{"summary":"We invent mathematical rules and axioms, but their consequences are then fixed and must be discovered, like strategies in an invented game.","idea":"This view blends invention and discovery. The starting points of mathematics, its axioms, definitions and rules of proof, are human choices. Once chosen, though, what follows is not up to us, which is why mathematicians can be surprised and why proofs can be checked. It denies Platonism, since no abstract realm is needed, and it denies that maths is arbitrary, since consequences are objective. Many find this matches practice: people invented the rules of sudoku, but nobody chooses whether a given puzzle has a solution.","example":"Non-Euclidean geometry: for centuries mathematicians tried to prove Euclid’s parallel postulate. In the 1820s and 1830s Lobachevsky and Bolyai instead replaced it and discovered consistent geometries with strange theorems, such as triangles whose angles add to less than 180 degrees. A new choice of rules opened a new field of discoveries.","history":"Henri Poincaré’s Science and Hypothesis (1902) treated geometrical axioms as conventions. Rudolf Carnap’s Logical Syntax of Language (1934) and “Empiricism, Semantics, and Ontology” (1950) treated mathematical frameworks as chosen. Ludwig Wittgenstein’s Remarks on the Foundations of Mathematics (published 1956) compared maths to rule-following. Deductivism, or “if-thenism,” holds that maths states what follows from which axioms.","misread":"Calling axioms invented does not make theorems a matter of opinion; given the rules, the results are as fixed as checkmate.","variants":["Conventionalism (Poincaré, Carnap): axioms are conventions chosen for convenience.","Deductivism (if-thenism): maths asserts only that theorems follow from axioms.","Wittgensteinian view: mathematical propositions are rules of grammar we lay down."],"reading":[{"title":"Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/philosophy-mathematics/"},{"title":"Deductivism in the Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/deductivism-mathematics/"},{"title":"Wittgenstein’s Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wittgenstein-mathematics/"}]},"k2b|formal":{"summary":"Mathematics is the manipulation of meaningless symbols according to rules; there is nothing mathematical statements are about.","idea":"Formalism says mathematics consists of formal systems: strings of symbols, axioms, and rules for transforming strings. Proving a theorem means deriving a string by the rules, like reaching a position in a board game. Mathematical sentences don’t describe numbers, minds or the world. Formalism denies there is any truth behind the game beyond what the rules produce. Its attraction is that it removes metaphysical mysteries and matches the rigour of modern proof. A computer checking a proof manipulates symbols without understanding them, and still gets it right.","example":"The MIU puzzle: start with the string MI and use a few rules, such as doubling what follows M or replacing III with U. Can you produce MU? The puzzle has a definite answer, provably no, though the symbols mean nothing. Formalists see mathematics as this kind of activity, writ large.","history":"Eduard Heine and Johannes Thomae defended game formalism in the late nineteenth century, and Frege attacked it in Basic Laws of Arithmetic, volume 2 (1903). David Hilbert’s programme of the 1920s sought finite consistency proofs, but Kurt Gödel’s incompleteness theorems (1931) showed its limits. Haskell Curry’s Outlines of a Formalist Philosophy of Mathematics (1951) developed a later version. Douglas Hofstadter’s Gödel, Escher, Bach (1979) introduced the MIU puzzle.","misread":"Hilbert was not a pure game formalist; he treated finite arithmetic as meaningful and only the infinite parts as formal tools.","variants":["Game formalism (Heine, Thomae): maths is a game with meaningless marks.","Hilbert’s formalism: finitary maths is contentful; infinitary maths is justified by consistency proofs.","Curry’s formalism: maths is the science of formal systems."],"reading":[{"title":"Formalism in the Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/formalism-mathematics/"},{"title":"Hilbert’s Program (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hilbert-program/"}]},"k2b|fiction":{"summary":"Numbers don’t exist, so mathematical statements are literally false or empty, but they are true within the story of mathematics and useful.","idea":"Mathematical fictionalism takes maths at face value, as talking about numbers and sets, and then denies those objects exist. So “there are infinitely many primes” is, strictly, not true, just as “Holmes lives on Baker Street” isn’t. But it is true according to the story, and the story is very useful because it is consistent and lets us reason efficiently about physical things. Fictionalists deny that maths’ usefulness proves its objects exist. We say “the average family has 2.4 children” without believing in a family with 0.4 of a child.","example":"Field’s Newtonian gravity: Hartry Field rewrote Newton’s theory of gravitation using only points of space and relations among them, with no numbers. He argued numbers then serve as a convenient shortcut, like a calculator, whose results could in principle be reached without them.","history":"Hans Vaihinger’s The Philosophy of “As If” (1911) treated many scientific notions as useful fictions. Hartry Field’s Science Without Numbers (1980) launched modern mathematical fictionalism. Mark Balaguer, Stephen Yablo and Mary Leng (Mathematics and Reality, 2010) developed versions that do not require rewriting science.","misread":"Fictionalists don’t say mathematicians are liars or maths is worthless; maths is a rigorous, valuable story, just not a literal description.","variants":["Hard-road fictionalism (Field): rewrite science without numbers to show they are dispensable.","Easy-road fictionalism (Leng, Balaguer): numbers can be useful in science without being real.","Figuralism (Yablo): mathematical talk works like figurative speech."],"reading":[{"title":"Fictionalism in the Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fictionalism-mathematics/"},{"title":"Nominalism in the Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nominalism-mathematics/"}]},"k12|universal":{"summary":"Whenever there are some things, there is a further object composed of them, no matter how scattered or unrelated they are.","idea":"Mereological universalism answers the question “when do parts make a whole?” with “always.” Your nose and the Eiffel Tower compose an object; so do every penny in Paris. Most such objects are uninteresting and unnamed, but they exist. Universalists deny that composition depends on parts being touching, stuck together or alive. Its appeal is that any restricted answer seems to draw an arbitrary line: gradually moving bricks apart, there is no sharp moment when the wall stops existing. A deck of cards scattered across a room is still the deck.","example":"The trout-turkey: David Lewis’s example of an object made of the front half of a trout and the back half of a turkey. It sounds absurd, but Lewis argued it exists; we just have no use for the word, and so we ignore it in ordinary talk.","history":"Stanisław Leśniewski’s mereology (from 1916) and Henry Leonard and Nelson Goodman’s calculus of individuals (1940) treat composition as unrestricted. David Lewis’s On the Plurality of Worlds (1986) and Parts of Classes (1991) gave the argument from vagueness, developed by Theodore Sider in Four-Dimensionalism (2001). Peter van Inwagen’s Material Beings (1990) framed the special composition question it answers.","misread":"Universalists do not say odd objects matter or should be named; they only say such objects exist.","variants":["Classical extensional mereology: any things have exactly one fusion, fixed by its parts.","Universalism with four-dimensionalism (Sider): any parts at any times compose an object."],"reading":[{"title":"Mereology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mereology/"},{"title":"Material Composition (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mat-comp/"}]},"k12|ordinary":{"summary":"Some collections compose wholes and others don’t: ordinary things like chairs and cats exist, but arbitrary collections compose nothing.","idea":"Restricted composition matches common sense: there is a chair, a cat, a mountain, but no object made of your nose and the Eiffel Tower. The challenge is to say what the restriction is: touching, being fastened, forming a natural unit, or something else. Defenders deny that composition must be all or nothing. Some say composition happens when parts are unified by a form or function; some say it is a brute fact. Its appeal is plain: when you count objects on a desk, you list a laptop, a mug and a pen, not every combination of them.","example":"The handshake: if composition happened whenever things touched, you and a friend would form a new object while shaking hands, which would vanish when you let go. Peter van Inwagen used such cases to show how hard it is to find a principled restriction.","history":"Aristotle’s hylomorphism held that matter composes a substance when organised by a form. Peter van Inwagen’s Material Beings (1990) posed the special composition question and tested contact, fastening and cohesion. Ned Markosian’s “Brutal Composition” (1998) argued there may be no principled answer. Kathrin Koslicki’s The Structure of Objects (2008) defends a neo-Aristotelian view.","misread":"","variants":["Hylomorphism (Aristotle, Koslicki, Fine): parts compose a whole when unified by a form or structure.","Brutal composition (Markosian): there is no informative rule; composition is a brute fact."],"reading":[{"title":"Material Composition (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mat-comp/"},{"title":"Mereology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mereology/"}]},"k12|organic":{"summary":"Particles compose something only when their activity makes up a life; so organisms exist, but chairs, rocks and tables strictly do not.","idea":"Organicism says the only composite material objects are living things. The particles of a cat are caught up in a self-maintaining biological process, and that unity is what makes them one thing. A chair has no such unity; there are just particles arranged chair-wise. Organicists deny that artefacts and lumps exist in the strict sense, while allowing that ordinary talk of chairs is fine and true in its everyday meaning. The appeal is that it keeps us in existence, as thinking organisms, while avoiding the puzzles about when bits of furniture begin and end.","example":"The baseball and the window: a baseball shatters a window. But the atoms arranged baseball-wise, acting together, do all the shattering. If the baseball also caused it, the effect would be caused twice over. Trenton Merricks used this to deny baseballs, while arguing persons cause things their atoms don’t.","history":"Peter van Inwagen’s Material Beings (1990) argued that composition occurs only when the activity of the parts constitutes a life. Trenton Merricks’s Objects and Persons (2001) defended a related view, keeping only conscious beings, and gave the overdetermination argument. Both draw on Aristotle’s emphasis on organisms as the clearest substances.","misread":"Organicists don’t say “there are chairs” is false in ordinary speech; they say it is true when understood as “particles are arranged chair-wise.”","variants":["Van Inwagen’s organicism: composite objects are exactly the living organisms.","Merricks’s eliminativism: only persons, or conscious beings, and simples exist."],"reading":[{"title":"Material Composition (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mat-comp/"},{"title":"Material Constitution (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/material-constitution/"}]},"k12|nihil":{"summary":"Composition never happens: there are only simple particles arranged in various ways, and no composite objects like chairs, noses or cats.","idea":"Mereological nihilism says no things ever compose a further thing. What we call a chair is many simple particles arranged chair-wise. The view denies all composite objects, not just odd ones, while keeping everything science says about how particles behave. Its appeal is economy and the escape from puzzles: there is no question of when a growing pile of sand becomes a heap, or whether a repaired ship is the same ship, because heaps and ships were never extra things. A dot-matrix picture of a face is just dots; the “face” adds nothing.","example":"The sorites argument: take away one atom from a table. Surely still a table. Repeat. No single atom makes the difference, yet eventually no table remains. Peter Unger concluded that the word “table” never picked out anything real in the first place.","history":"Ancient atomists such as Democritus (5th century BCE) held that only atoms and the void are real. Buddhist Abhidharma texts and the Questions of King Milinda treated wholes as names for parts. Peter Unger’s “There Are No Ordinary Things” (1979) revived the view. Cian Dorr and Theodore Sider defended it in the 2000s and 2010s.","misread":"Nihilists don’t deny that you can sit; they deny that over and above the particles arranged chair-wise there is an additional object, the chair.","variants":["Atomistic nihilism: only partless simples exist.","Abhidharma reductionism: wholes are conventional designations for momentary basic elements.","Nihilism with exceptions: only simples and persons exist (Merricks, close to organicism)."],"reading":[{"title":"Mereology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mereology/"},{"title":"Material Composition (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mat-comp/"}]},"k12|monism":{"summary":"The universe as a whole is the one fundamental thing; ordinary objects are its regions or aspects, not separate building blocks.","idea":"Monism reverses the usual direction of explanation. Instead of small parts composing bigger things, the whole comes first, and the parts depend on it. Priority monism allows that parts exist, but makes them derivative. Existence monism says only one thing exists at all. It denies that reality is built bottom up from independent atoms. Its appeal includes physics, where entangled systems behave as wholes that cannot be fully described part by part. Think of waves on the ocean: real enough, but they are shapes of the ocean, not separate things floating on it.","example":"The rope and the snake: in Advaita Vedānta, a traveller at dusk mistakes a coiled rope for a snake. The snake was never there; only the rope was. Shankara used this image for how the many objects of the world are appearances of a single reality, Brahman.","history":"Parmenides’ poem (5th century BCE) argued that being is one and unchanging. Shankara (c. 8th century CE) systematised Advaita Vedānta. Baruch Spinoza’s Ethics (1677) held there is only one substance, God or Nature. F. H. Bradley’s Appearance and Reality (1893) defended an absolute whole. Jonathan Schaffer’s “Monism: The Priority of the Whole” (2010) revived priority monism.","misread":"Priority monism does not deny that your nose exists; it says your nose depends on the universe, rather than the universe being built from noses.","variants":["Priority monism (Schaffer): the cosmos is the one basic thing; its parts exist but are derivative.","Existence monism (Horgan and Potrč): only one concrete object, the cosmos, exists.","Advaita Vedānta (Shankara): Brahman alone is real; plurality is appearance."],"reading":[{"title":"Monism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/monism/"},{"title":"Baruch Spinoza (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spinoza/"},{"title":"Parmenides (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/parmenides/"}]},"k12|verbal":{"summary":"Whether scattered things compose “an object” is not a deep fact but depends on how we choose to use words like “object” and “exists.”","idea":"Ontological deflationism says disputes about composition are not about the world but about language. Once all the facts about particles and their arrangements are fixed, nothing further is left to discover about whether there is “also” a nose-tower. The universalist and the nihilist are using “there is” in different senses, and each speaks truly in their own language. It denies that metaphysics can settle such questions by deep investigation. Compare asking whether a hot dog is a sandwich: once you know everything about it, the remaining question is about the word.","example":"Putnam’s three individuals: imagine a world with just three particles, x1, x2 and x3. How many objects are there? Three, says one person. Seven, says a mereologist, counting every combination. Putnam argued there is no fact about which answer is right apart from a choice of conceptual scheme.","history":"Rudolf Carnap’s “Empiricism, Semantics, and Ontology” (1950) split existence questions into internal ones, answered within a framework, and external ones, which are practical choices. Hilary Putnam’s The Many Faces of Realism (1987) gave the three-individuals example. Eli Hirsch’s Quantifier Variance and Realism (2011) and Amie Thomasson’s Ontology Made Easy (2015) developed modern versions.","misread":"Deflationists don’t say nothing exists or that reality is invented; they say some disputes about existence are disputes about words.","variants":["Carnapian deflationism: external existence questions are choices of framework, not facts.","Quantifier variance (Hirsch): “exists” can have several equally good meanings.","Easy ontology (Thomasson): existence questions are answered by applying our concepts, trivially."],"reading":[{"title":"Rudolf Carnap (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/carnap/"},{"title":"Material Composition (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mat-comp/"}]},"k7|yes":{"summary":"Past, present and future things are all equally real; “now” is just where you are in time, as “here” is where you are in space.","idea":"Eternalism says reality is a four-dimensional block. Dinosaurs, you, and the first Mars colony all exist, at different times, just as Tokyo and London exist at different places. Time does not objectively flow; the sense of passage comes from how memory and expectation work. Eternalists deny that the present is metaphysically special. The view gains support from relativity, which has no single, universal present. Looking at a family photo album, you don’t think earlier pages stopped existing; eternalists see time the same way.","example":"The Andromeda paradox: two people pass each other on a street, one walking toward the Andromeda galaxy, the other away. Relativity implies that events on Andromeda which count as “now” for them can be days apart, so whether an Andromedan fleet has launched “now” depends on who is walking which way.","history":"Hermann Minkowski’s 1908 lecture recast Einstein’s 1905 relativity as a four-dimensional spacetime. C. W. Rietdijk (1966) and Hilary Putnam (“Time and Physical Geometry,” 1967) argued relativity supports eternalism, and Roger Penrose popularised the Andromeda case. J. J. C. Smart, David Lewis and Theodore Sider are leading philosophical defenders.","misread":"Eternalism is not fatalism: the future exists, but it is partly made the way it is by your choices, just as the past was.","variants":["B-theory: times are ordered only by earlier and later; tense is perspectival.","Perdurantism (Lewis, Sider): objects persist by having temporal parts at different times."],"reading":[{"title":"Time (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/time/"},{"title":"Eternalism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/eternalism/"},{"title":"Being and Becoming in Modern Physics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spacetime-bebecome/"}]},"k7|spotlight":{"summary":"All times exist, but one of them is objectively present, and the present moves along the series of times.","idea":"The moving spotlight theory tries to keep the best of both sides. Like eternalism, it says past and future things exist. Like presentism, it says there is an objective, privileged now, and that time genuinely passes as the now moves. This explains why time seems to flow without making the past or future unreal. It denies that passage is merely an illusion. Imagine a line of people in a dark hall, each waiting their turn, while a single spotlight moves steadily along them: everyone is there, but only one is lit.","example":"McTaggart’s two series: events can be ordered as past, present and future, which changes, or as earlier and later, which never changes. The spotlight theory accepts both: a fixed earlier-later order of existing times, and a changing fact about which of them is present.","history":"J. M. E. McTaggart’s “The Unreality of Time” (1908) distinguished the A-series and B-series. C. D. Broad named and criticised the spotlight picture in An Examination of McTaggart’s Philosophy (1938). Bradford Skow’s Objective Becoming (2015) and Ross Cameron’s The Moving Spotlight (2015) have revived it.","misread":"","variants":["Classical spotlight: all times exist and one has the property of being present.","Cameron’s version: only present things exist, but they carry temporal features that encode the past and future."],"reading":[{"title":"Time (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/time/"},{"title":"John M. E. McTaggart (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mctaggart/"}]},"k7|growing":{"summary":"The past and present exist, the future does not yet; as time passes, new slices of reality come into being.","idea":"The growing block theory says reality is a block of spacetime that grows at its leading edge. Past events are permanently real, which explains why claims about the past are true or false; future events are not yet real, which explains why the future feels open. It denies that the future already exists, and denies that the past has vanished. Its appeal is that it matches the asymmetry we feel between a fixed past and an open future. A diary is a good model: earlier pages are written for good, later pages are blank.","example":"Aristotle’s sea battle: will there be a sea battle tomorrow? Aristotle worried that if “there will be a sea battle tomorrow” were already true, tomorrow would be fixed. Growing block theorists say there is no future reality to make it true yet, so the future stays open.","history":"Aristotle’s On Interpretation (chapter 9) raised the sea battle problem. C. D. Broad’s Scientific Thought (1923) proposed the growing block. Michael Tooley’s Time, Tense, and Causation (1997) defended it with a causal account, and Peter Forrest, Fabrice Correia and Sven Rosenkranz developed later versions. Craig Bourne and David Braddon-Mitchell asked, in 2002 and 2004, how anyone could know they are at the edge.","misread":"","variants":["Broad’s growing block: the sum of existence is always increasing.","Tooley’s causal version: tensed facts are grounded in a growing causal order."],"reading":[{"title":"Time (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/time/"},{"title":"Eternalism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/eternalism/"}]},"k7|present":{"summary":"Only present things exist; past things no longer exist and future things do not exist yet.","idea":"Presentism says that to exist is to exist now. Dinosaurs existed, but they are not part of reality any more; your grandchildren may exist, but they aren’t real yet. Presentists deny that there is a four-dimensional block. Many find this the common-sense view: when something is destroyed, it really is gone. Presentists must explain what makes claims about the past true, and how presentism fits relativity. The everyday idea is simple: when you finish a sandwich, it does not continue existing at an earlier time; it is just gone.","example":"“Thank goodness that’s over”: after a painful dentist visit, you feel relief. Arthur Prior argued you are not relieved that the visit is earlier than this utterance, which was always true; you are relieved that it is past. Presentists say only a real present makes sense of this.","history":"Augustine’s Confessions (Book XI, c. 400 CE) argued that the past exists only in memory and the future in expectation. In Indian Buddhism, the Sautrāntika school held that only present phenomena exist, against the Sarvāstivāda claim that all three times exist. Arthur Prior developed tense logic and gave the “thank goodness” example (1959). Dean Zimmerman and Ned Markosian are recent defenders.","misread":"Presentists don’t deny that past events happened; they deny that past events still exist, somewhere else in time.","variants":["Ersatz presentism: abstract present-day “times” represent past and future states.","Truthmaker presentism: present traces or tensed properties make claims about the past true."],"reading":[{"title":"Time (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/time/"},{"title":"Eternalism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/eternalism/"}]},"k7|unreal":{"summary":"Time is not a feature of reality itself; the appearance of change and succession is an illusion or mind-dependent appearance.","idea":"Temporal anti-realism says the deep nature of reality is timeless. What we experience as one moment after another is a feature of appearance, of the mind’s way of representing, or of a less fundamental level of physics. It denies that time is fundamental or real in itself. Some reach this view by logical argument, some by idealist metaphysics, some by looking at physics where time may vanish at the fundamental level. A film is a helpful image: what seems like continuous motion is a series of still frames, and perhaps even the sequence is an artefact of the projector.","example":"McTaggart’s paradox: every event is past, present and future, which are incompatible. Saying it is each at different times only shifts the problem, since those times are themselves past, present and future, and so on forever. McTaggart concluded that time, which needs that series, cannot be real.","history":"Parmenides and Zeno (5th century BCE) denied change. Advaita Vedānta treats time as part of the world of appearance. Kant’s Critique of Pure Reason (1781) made time a form of our intuition. J. M. E. McTaggart’s “The Unreality of Time” (1908) gave the famous argument. Physicist Julian Barbour’s The End of Time (1999) argued for a timeless universe on physical grounds.","misread":"Denying time is real is not denying that clocks tick or that experience seems ordered; it denies that this order belongs to reality at its most basic.","variants":["McTaggart’s argument: the A-series is contradictory, so time is unreal.","Kantian ideality: time is a form of human experience, not of things in themselves.","Timeless physics (Barbour): time emerges from a fundamentally static universe."],"reading":[{"title":"John M. E. McTaggart (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mctaggart/"},{"title":"Time (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/time/"}]},"k7|verbal":{"summary":"The dispute between presentism and eternalism is merely verbal: once “exists” is made precise, all sides agree about the facts.","idea":"Deflationism about time says the debate over which times are real dissolves on inspection. If “Socrates exists” means “Socrates exists now,” presentism is obviously true and eternalists agree. If it means “Socrates exists at some time,” eternalism is obviously true and presentists agree. No third reading makes a substantial claim. So the apparent rival theories describe the same world in different words. It denies that there is a deep fact about which times are real. Compare arguing whether a glass is half full or half empty: the water level is not in dispute.","example":"Does Socrates exist? Ask a presentist: no, he did, but he doesn’t now. Ask an eternalist: yes, at an earlier time, though not now. Both agree he lived in Athens long ago and is not alive today. Deflationists ask what fact, beyond this, the two are disagreeing about.","history":"Ulrich Meyer’s “The Triviality of Presentism” (2005), Steven Savitt’s and Mauro Dorato’s papers of 2006, and earlier work by Lawrence Lombard argued the debate is largely verbal. Thomas Crisp and Theodore Sider replied that a substantive sense of “exists” remains. The worry parallels Carnap’s and Hirsch’s deflationism about composition.","misread":"Deflationists don’t say time is unreal or unimportant; they say one specific metaphysical dispute about it lacks real content.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"Being and Becoming in Modern Physics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spacetime-bebecome/"},{"title":"Eternalism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/eternalism/"}]},"k8|yes":{"summary":"Causation is a real relation in the world, a genuine producing or bringing about, not just one event regularly following another.","idea":"Causal realism says that when the switch causes the light, something real connects them beyond the fact that switch-flips are usually followed by light. Causes produce, bring about or necessitate their effects. Realists deny that causation reduces to patterns, counterfactuals or our expectations. The view is attractive because it explains why patterns hold and why we can manipulate things: if causation were only regularity, it would be a coincidence that the world keeps behaving lawfully. Pushing a door open seems to involve more than a push followed by a door moving.","example":"The barometer and the storm: a falling barometer reading is regularly followed by a storm, yet it does not cause the storm; falling air pressure causes both. Realists argue that only a real causal connection, not mere regular sequence, distinguishes the pressure’s role from the barometer’s.","history":"Aristotle’s theory of four causes (Physics, Book II) treats causation as real. Medieval Aristotelians and the Indian Nyāya school held robust views of causal production. G. E. M. Anscombe’s “Causality and Determination” (1971) attacked the Humean view, and Galen Strawson’s The Secret Connexion (1989) argued Hume himself believed in real causal powers. Modern realists include D. M. Armstrong and powers theorists.","misread":"Causal realism does not mean causation must be visible or simple; realists differ widely on what the connection is.","variants":["Powers view: causes have real dispositions to produce effects.","Process view: causes transmit a physical quantity to effects.","Necessitation: laws make effects necessary."],"reading":[{"title":"The Metaphysics of Causation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causation-metaphysics/"}]},"k8|no":{"summary":"There is no hidden necessary link between cause and effect; causation consists in patterns of events, dependence relations, or our way of seeing them.","idea":"This family of Humean views denies any extra ingredient, a power or necessary tie, binding causes to effects. All there is in the world is one thing happening, then another, in patterns. Causal claims are made true by those patterns, by what would have happened otherwise, or by our habits of expectation. Some go further and say causes don’t exist at all. Humeans deny we have any experience or idea of hidden necessity. The appeal is economy and fit with physics. When one domino tips another, you see one fall, then the next; you never see a “making.”","example":"Hume’s billiard balls: one ball strikes another, and the second moves. Hume observed that, however closely you watch, you see only motion followed by motion. Without experience, Adam, the first man, could not have predicted the second ball would move rather than stay still or bounce back.","history":"David Hume’s Treatise (1739 to 1740) and Enquiry (1748) argued we have no impression of necessary connection. Ernst Mach and Bertrand Russell’s “On the Notion of Cause” (1913) were skeptical of causation in physics. David Lewis’s doctrine of Humean supervenience, set out in the 1980s, holds that everything supervenes on a mosaic of local facts with no necessary connections.","misread":"Humeans don’t say causes are unreal or that anything could follow anything; most say causal claims are true, made true by patterns.","variants":["Regularity view: causation is constant conjunction.","Counterfactual view: causation is dependence.","Projectivism: causation is our expectation projected onto the world."],"reading":[{"title":"The Metaphysics of Causation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causation-metaphysics/"},{"title":"David Hume (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume/"}]},"k8a|powers":{"summary":"Things have real causal powers, dispositions to produce effects, and causation is the exercise of those powers in the right conditions.","idea":"The powers view says properties are not inert; they are abilities. Mass is the power to attract, charge the power to repel, solubility the power to dissolve. Causation happens when powers meet and manifest. Laws of nature then describe how powers behave, rather than ruling over passive matter. The view denies that properties are just categorical features with no built-in behaviour. Its appeal is that it explains why things behave as they do: the behaviour comes from what they are. Glass is fragile even sitting safely on a shelf, because of a real feature of its structure.","example":"The finkish wire: C. B. Martin imagined a live wire attached to a machine that makes it dead the instant anything touches it. The wire seems to have the power to shock, yet would never shock if touched. Martin used this to argue powers can’t be reduced to “if touched, it would shock.”","history":"Aristotle’s Metaphysics (Book Theta) developed the theory of potentiality. The view was eclipsed in early modern science and revived by C. B. Martin, Rom Harré and E. H. Madden (Causal Powers, 1975), and George Molnar’s Powers (2003). Brian Ellis’s Scientific Essentialism (2001), Alexander Bird’s Nature’s Metaphysics (2007) and Stephen Mumford and Rani Lill Anjum’s Getting Causes from Powers (2011) developed it.","misread":"Powers aren’t mysterious forces added to physics; powers theorists usually identify them with physical properties like charge and mass.","variants":["Dispositional essentialism (Ellis, Bird): properties have their causal roles essentially, making laws necessary.","Pandispositionalism (Mumford, Bird): all fundamental properties are powers.","Identity theory (Martin, Heil): properties are both powerful and qualitative at once."],"reading":[{"title":"Dispositions (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dispositions/"},{"title":"The Metaphysics of Causation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causation-metaphysics/"}]},"k8a|process":{"summary":"Causation is a physical process that transmits something conserved, such as energy or momentum, from cause to effect.","idea":"The process view looks to physics for what causation is. A causal process is a world-line of an object that carries a conserved quantity like momentum or charge; a causal interaction is where such quantities are exchanged. Causation is that physical transfer, not a regularity or a counterfactual. The view denies that causation is something added by our minds or by abstract laws. Its appeal is concreteness: a hammer drives a nail by delivering momentum to it. The challenge is causation by absence: not watering a plant kills it, though nothing is transferred.","example":"The searchlight’s spot: a rotating searchlight throws a spot that sweeps across a distant wall, potentially faster than light. But the spot transmits nothing; each spot is caused by the light, not by the previous spot. Wesley Salmon used this to separate pseudo-processes from genuine causal processes.","history":"Bertrand Russell’s Human Knowledge (1948) spoke of causal lines. Wesley Salmon’s Scientific Explanation and the Causal Structure of the World (1984) developed the mark-transmission account. Phil Dowe’s Physical Causation (2000) proposed the conserved quantity theory, which Salmon then adopted in modified form.","misread":"","variants":["Mark transmission (Salmon): causal processes can carry a mark or alteration forward.","Conserved quantity theory (Dowe): causal processes carry and exchange conserved quantities."],"reading":[{"title":"Causal Processes (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causation-process/"}]},"k8a|necessit":{"summary":"Laws of nature are real relations that make effects follow from causes; they govern events rather than merely summarise them.","idea":"This view says laws are more than descriptions of what always happens. A law is a real connection among properties, one that necessitates the pattern, so that the pattern holds because of the law. Causation is then the instancing of such laws. The view denies the Humean claim that laws are just regularities. Its appeal is explanatory: a law explains why all copper conducts electricity, while a mere summary cannot explain anything, since it just repeats what it summarises. Your speed limit doesn’t just describe how fast people drive; it is supposed to shape it.","example":"Gold and uranium spheres: there is no solid gold sphere over a mile wide, and no uranium sphere that big either. The first is an accident; there is just not that much gold gathered together. The second is a matter of law, since such a mass of uranium would explode. Necessitarians say something real separates them.","history":"Fred Dretske’s “Laws of Nature” (1977), Michael Tooley’s “The Nature of Laws” (1977) and D. M. Armstrong’s What Is a Law of Nature? (1983) independently proposed that laws are relations of necessitation among universals. Tim Maudlin’s The Metaphysics Within Physics (2007) defended laws as primitive governing facts.","misread":"Necessitarians usually mean physical necessity, not logical necessity: the laws could have been different, but given them, effects must follow.","variants":["Dretske, Tooley and Armstrong: laws are necessitation relations among universals.","Primitivism about laws (Maudlin): laws are basic and govern the evolution of states."],"reading":[{"title":"Laws of Nature (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/laws-of-nature/"}]},"k8a|primitive":{"summary":"Causation is a basic feature of reality that cannot be analysed in other terms; we grasp it directly, in experiences like pushing.","idea":"Causal primitivism says every attempt to define causation, through regularity, counterfactuals, processes or powers, fails, because causation is more basic than all of them. Instead, it is used to explain them. We have direct experience of causation, in pushing, pulling and being resisted, and our concept begins there. It denies that causal facts reduce to non-causal ones. The appeal is partly the record of failure in analysing causation, and partly phenomenology: when you lift a heavy box, you feel yourself making it rise.","example":"Anscombe’s verbs: we see a cat lap up milk, a knife cut bread, a wave knock someone over. Anscombe argued that ordinary verbs like “scrape,” “push,” “carry” and “eat” already report causings, so we observe causation all the time, contrary to Hume.","history":"Maine de Biran, around 1800, located our idea of cause in felt effort. G. E. M. Anscombe’s “Causality and Determination” (1971) argued causation is directly observed. Michael Tooley’s “Causation: Reductionism versus Realism” (1990) and John Carroll’s Laws of Nature (1994) defended non-reductive views.","misread":"Primitivists don’t say nothing can be said about causation; they say it can be described and related to other concepts, but not reduced to them.","variants":[],"reading":[{"title":"The Metaphysics of Causation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causation-metaphysics/"}]},"k8a|god":{"summary":"God is the only true cause; created things have no power of their own, and natural events are occasions on which God acts.","idea":"Occasionalism says that what we call natural causes are only regular occasions for divine action. When fire meets cotton, God creates the burning; when you decide to raise your arm, God moves it. Created things are real, but powerless. It denies that creatures have causal powers of their own. Its motivations are theological, giving God full sovereignty, and philosophical, including the difficulty of explaining how a mind could move a body. Natural order remains, because God acts by regular habit. Think of a puppet show where only the puppeteer moves anything, but always in reliable ways.","example":"Continuous re-creation: Ashʿarite theologians held that the world is made of atoms and properties that last only an instant, and that God re-creates everything in every moment, like frames of a film. Nothing can cause the next frame; only the one who projects each frame does.","history":"The Ashʿarite school of Islamic theology, founded by al-Ashʿarī (d. 935), developed occasionalism with atomism; al-Bāqillānī (d. 1013) refined it, and al-Ghazali made it famous. In seventeenth-century Europe, Cartesians such as Louis de La Forge, Géraud de Cordemoy and Arnold Geulincx adopted versions, with Nicolas Malebranche its leading defender.","misread":"Occasionalists do not think nature is chaotic or that science is pointless; God acts by regular custom, which science can study.","variants":["Ashʿarite occasionalism: atoms and accidents are recreated by God at every instant.","Cartesian occasionalism (Malebranche): bodies and minds are causally passive; God acts on the occasion of their states."],"reading":[{"title":"Occasionalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/occasionalism/"},{"title":"Causation in Arabic and Islamic Thought (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/arabic-islamic-causation/"},{"title":"al-Ghazali (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/al-ghazali/"}]},"k8b|regular":{"summary":"X causes Y when events like X are regularly followed by events like Y; laws are just the simplest summaries of such patterns.","idea":"The regularity view says causation is constant conjunction: lightning causes thunder because flashes of that kind are always followed by booms. Laws of nature, on the modern version, are the generalisations in the best system that balances simplicity and informativeness about everything that happens. The view denies any necessary connection or power beyond the pattern. Its attraction is that it adds nothing beyond what science observes. A recipe works because every time you combine these ingredients at this heat, you get bread.","example":"The short circuit: experts say a short circuit caused a house fire. It wasn’t sufficient alone, since oxygen and flammable material were needed, and other things could have started a fire. J. L. Mackie analysed it as an insufficient but necessary part of an unnecessary but sufficient condition: an INUS condition.","history":"David Hume’s Treatise (1739 to 1740) and Enquiry (1748) gave the regularity definition. John Stuart Mill’s A System of Logic (1843) developed methods for detecting causes. J. L. Mackie’s “Causes and Conditions” (1965) gave the INUS analysis. The best-system account of laws comes from Mill, Frank Ramsey (1928) and David Lewis (1973).","misread":"The regularity view doesn’t say every sequence is causal; correlations from a common cause or coincidence are excluded by refined versions.","variants":["INUS analysis (Mackie): a cause is an essential part of one of several sufficient conditions.","Best-system account of laws (Mill, Ramsey, Lewis): laws are axioms of the simplest, strongest summary."],"reading":[{"title":"Regularity and Inferential Theories of Causation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causation-regularity/"},{"title":"Laws of Nature (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/laws-of-nature/"}]},"k8b|counter":{"summary":"X caused Y when Y depended on X: had X not happened, Y would not have happened either.","idea":"The counterfactual view analyses causation as difference-making. Your flipping the switch caused the light because, had you not flipped it, the light would have stayed off. Counterfactuals are made true by similarities among possible situations, or, on the interventionist version, by what would happen under an idealised manipulation. The view denies that causation requires a hidden link or exceptionless regularity. Its appeal is that it fits how we test causes: medical trials ask what would happen to patients if they didn’t get the drug.","example":"Suzy and Billy: both throw rocks at a bottle. Suzy’s hits first and shatters it; Billy’s flies through the space where it stood. Suzy caused the shattering, yet had she not thrown, Billy’s rock would have shattered it anyway. This preemption case is a key test for counterfactual theories.","history":"Hume’s Enquiry (1748) offered a counterfactual formula alongside regularity. David Lewis’s “Causation” (1973) gave the modern analysis, refined in “Causation as Influence” (2000). Judea Pearl’s Causality (2000) and James Woodward’s Making Things Happen (2003) developed interventionism, now influential in science. The Buddhist formula of dependent arising has a similar structure.","misread":"","variants":["Lewis’s analysis: causation is a chain of counterfactual dependence.","Interventionism (Woodward, Pearl): X causes Y if intervening on X would change Y."],"reading":[{"title":"Counterfactual Theories of Causation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causation-counterfactual/"}]},"k8b|project":{"summary":"Necessary connection is not in the world; our minds, conditioned by repeated experience, project an expectation that we call “cause.”","idea":"Projectivism says causal necessity is like colour on a theory of secondary qualities: it seems to be out there, but it reflects how we respond. After seeing flame followed by heat many times, we feel a strong expectation, and we spread that feeling onto the events, calling it a cause. It denies that there is a necessity in nature to be perceived. Modern versions add that causal talk is still legitimate and can be true in a deflationary sense. Compare finding a joke funny: the funniness seems to be in the joke, but it depends on how audiences react.","example":"Hume’s impression of necessity: Hume searched for the original sense impression behind the idea of necessary connection and found none in the events themselves. What he found was a felt transition of the mind, from seeing one event to expecting another, which we mistake for a feature of the objects.","history":"David Hume’s Treatise (1739 to 1740), Book I, Part III, section 14, argued that the mind spreads itself on external objects. Simon Blackburn’s “Hume and Thick Connexions” (1990) developed quasi-realism about causation. Peter Menzies and Huw Price’s “Causation as a Secondary Quality” (1993) and Price’s later perspectival work tie causation to our standpoint as agents.","misread":"Projectivists don’t say causal claims are arbitrary or false; they explain causal talk as a disciplined expression of our expectations and agency.","variants":["Humean projectivism: causal necessity is a projected feeling of expectation.","Quasi-realism (Blackburn): causal claims can be called true while rooted in our attitudes.","Agency theory (Menzies and Price): causation is understood through our ability to manipulate."],"reading":[{"title":"David Hume (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume/"},{"title":"Regularity and Inferential Theories of Causation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causation-regularity/"}]},"k8b|none":{"summary":"Causation is not a real feature of the world; fundamental physics describes how states evolve, and “cause” belongs only to everyday talk.","idea":"Causal eliminativism says the notion of cause has no place in our deepest description of reality. Fundamental physics states laws as equations linking states of whole systems, often symmetric in time, without singling out causes and effects. Talk of causes is useful in daily life and special sciences, but it is a rough, local, human-centred approximation. It denies that the world contains causal relations at its fundamental level. Compare “sunrise”: a fine word for daily use, but the sun doesn’t literally rise.","example":"Russell’s planets: in Newtonian gravitation, the motions of planets are fixed by equations relating their masses, positions and accelerations at each moment. Nothing in the equations says which planet is cause and which effect. Russell argued that the old law of causality survives in philosophy but not in advanced physics.","history":"Ernst Mach doubted the scientific value of cause. Bertrand Russell’s “On the Notion of Cause” (1913) gave the classic argument. John Norton’s “Causation as Folk Science” (2003) and James Ladyman and Don Ross’s Every Thing Must Go (2007) developed neo-Russellian views. Critics such as Nancy Cartwright (1979) argued that we need causes to tell effective strategies from mere correlations.","misread":"Eliminativists don’t deny that switches turn on lights; they deny that the basic physics behind it involves a relation of causing.","variants":["Russellian eliminativism: physics has no use for cause.","Neo-Russellianism: causation is real but derivative, existing only in special sciences or everyday life."],"reading":[{"title":"Causation in Physics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causation-physics/"},{"title":"The Metaphysics of Causation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causation-metaphysics/"}]},"s7|yes":{"summary":"A law gives you a real moral reason to obey simply because it is the law, even when obeying achieves nothing else.","idea":"This view says the law’s command adds a reason of its own. Breaking a law is not wrong only when the act is harmful anyway: the fact that a legitimate authority has ruled counts by itself. The reason can be outweighed, so you may run the red light to rush a sick child to hospital, but it never simply vanishes. Defenders find it attractive because it explains a common feeling: a citizen who obeys only when she personally agrees is not really treating the law as law at all. It also explains why we feel a pang of guilt at that empty 3 a.m. crossing.","example":"Socrates in Plato’s Crito: condemned to death by an Athenian court he thought had judged wrongly, he turns down his friends’ plan to smuggle him out of prison. He imagines the Laws of Athens asking him whether he means to destroy them by disobeying, and he decides he must accept the verdict.","history":"Plato’s Crito (4th century BCE) is the first great statement. Hobbes, Leviathan (1651), and Locke, Second Treatise of Government (1689), grounded obedience in a social contract. H.L.A. Hart (1955) and John Rawls (1964, 1971) offered fair play and natural duty accounts, and Ronald Dworkin, Law’s Empire (1986), an associative one. Confucian thought also treats loyalty to rightful rulers as a real duty.","misread":"It does not mean you must obey every law no matter what; most defenders say the duty can be outweighed and that unjust laws may lose it.","variants":["Consent theory: the duty comes from our agreement","Fair play: the duty comes from sharing in a cooperative scheme","Natural duty: everyone must support just institutions"],"reading":[{"title":"Political Obligation","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/political-obligation/"},{"title":"Political Legitimacy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/legitimacy/"}]},"s7|no":{"summary":"Laws have no moral force of their own; you should do what laws demand only when it is right for independent reasons.","idea":"Philosophical anarchism says no actual state has genuine authority over its citizens. Murder is wrong, but because of what murder is, not because a statute forbids it. Where the law demands something pointless, such as waiting at an empty crossing, its say-so adds nothing. This is not a call to tear down governments or riot: philosophical anarchists usually obey almost every law, since most laws match what morality requires anyway. People find it attractive because it takes individual conscience seriously and because, on close inspection, every proposed source of a general duty to obey seems to have gaps.","example":"Robert Paul Wolff’s autonomous person: someone who takes full responsibility for her actions must decide for herself what to do. She may follow a command because she judges it wise, but if she obeys simply because she was commanded, she has handed her moral judgement to someone else.","history":"William Godwin, Enquiry Concerning Political Justice (1793), argued that reason, not government, should guide each person. Robert Paul Wolff, In Defense of Anarchism (1970), set out the modern argument from autonomy. A. John Simmons, Moral Principles and Political Obligations (1979), argued that every theory of political obligation fails for most citizens. Michael Huemer, The Problem of Political Authority (2013), extended the case.","misread":"It is not the same as wanting to abolish the state; many philosophical anarchists think states can be useful and even justified.","variants":["A priori anarchism: no state could ever have authority (Wolff)","A posteriori anarchism: no actual state has it, though one might (Simmons)"],"reading":[{"title":"Anarchism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/anarchism/"},{"title":"Political Obligation","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/political-obligation/"}]},"s7|nofact":{"summary":"There is no objective moral truth about whether we should obey the law, because there are no objective moral truths at all.","idea":"This answer applies moral skepticism to politics. It does not say obeying is wrong or right; it says neither claim is objectively true. Talk of a ‘duty to obey’ expresses feelings, social pressure or conventions, not facts in the world. Moral error theorists say such claims are all false; others say they are not even the kind of thing that can be true. You might still wait at the light out of habit or preference. People find this attractive because moral disagreement about authority runs deep and seems never to be settled, and because it is hard to say what a moral fact would be made of.","example":"Thrasymachus in Book I of Plato’s Republic argues that what rulers call ‘justice’ is simply whatever serves the stronger party. His challenge, that political morality may be a story told by the powerful, has been the starting point for skeptics ever since.","history":"Thrasymachus appears in Plato’s Republic (c. 375 BCE). Max Stirner, The Ego and Its Own (1844), treated duty, the state and morality as ‘spooks’ with no hold on the individual. J.L. Mackie, Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977), defended moral error theory: ordinary moral claims aim at objective truth but are all false. Richard Garner has argued that we would do better to abandon moral talk altogether.","misread":"Skeptics are not claiming everyone should break laws; they deny there is an objective ‘should’ in either direction.","variants":["Error theory: moral claims are all false (Mackie)","Non-cognitivism: moral claims express attitudes, not beliefs","Moral abolitionism: we should stop using moral language (Garner)"],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Skepticism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}]},"s7a|consent":{"summary":"You must obey the law because you have agreed to, either openly or by quietly accepting the benefits of living under it.","idea":"Consent theory says political authority, like any promise, rests on agreement. Free and equal people cannot be bound by others without their say, so the state’s right to rule must come from our consent. Since few people ever sign anything, defenders appeal to tacit consent: by choosing to stay, using public roads and accepting protection, you signal agreement. Naturalised citizens who swear an oath give express consent. The view is attractive because it respects each person’s freedom: no one is under authority who has not, in some sense, chosen it.","example":"John Locke’s highway: Locke argued that anyone who enjoys any part of a government’s territory, even someone merely travelling freely on its roads, gives tacit consent and is bound to obey its laws while there.","history":"Socrates’ argument in Plato’s Crito appeals to agreement. Hobbes, Leviathan (1651), and Locke, Second Treatise (1689), built social contract theory on consent, with Locke introducing tacit consent. Rousseau, The Social Contract (1762), sought a form of association in which each obeys only himself. David Hume, ‘Of the Original Contract’ (1748), raised the classic objection. Modern defenders include Harry Beran, The Consent Theory of Political Obligation (1987).","misread":"Consent theorists do not claim people literally signed a contract; most rely on tacit or implied consent shown by conduct.","variants":["Express consent: an explicit oath or agreement","Tacit consent: agreement shown by staying and accepting benefits (Locke)","Hypothetical consent: what reasonable people would agree to"],"reading":[{"title":"Political Obligation","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/political-obligation/"},{"title":"Hobbes’s Moral and Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hobbes-moral/"}]},"s7a|fair":{"summary":"Because you benefit from everyone else obeying the law, fairness requires you to do your share and obey too.","idea":"The principle of fair play says that when people cooperate under rules that produce benefits for all, each person who accepts those benefits owes the others compliance. Safe roads, public order and national defence exist because most people restrain themselves. If you take the benefits while ignoring the rules, you treat others’ sacrifices as your free lunch. The view is attractive because it needs no promise or oath: it rests on a simple and widely shared intuition against free-riding, the same one that makes skipping the queue feel wrong.","example":"A shared flat cleaning rota: everyone takes a turn scrubbing the kitchen, and everyone enjoys a clean kitchen. A flatmate who happily uses it but always skips his turn wrongs the others, even though he never signed anything.","history":"H.L.A. Hart introduced the principle in ‘Are There Any Natural Rights?’ (1955). John Rawls developed it in ‘Legal Obligation and the Duty of Fair Play’ (1964). Robert Nozick, Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), pressed a famous objection. George Klosko, The Principle of Fairness and Political Obligation (1992), revived the view by focusing on goods everyone needs, like security.","misread":"","variants":["Acceptance version: only benefits you actively accept bind you","Presumptive goods version: benefits everyone needs, like defence, bind you even unasked (Klosko)"],"reading":[{"title":"Political Obligation","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/political-obligation/"}]},"s7a|justice":{"summary":"Everyone has a basic moral duty to support just institutions where they live, whether or not they agreed or benefit.","idea":"The natural duty of justice says some duties bind us simply as moral persons, like the duty to help someone in danger. One of these is to support and comply with just institutions that apply to us. On this view, you obey reasonably just laws not because you promised or because you owe fellow citizens a return, but because justice itself needs institutions to exist, and those institutions need people to follow them. The view is attractive because it binds everyone, including those who never consented, while tying obedience to the justice of the system.","example":"The Samaritan case: you have a duty to pull a drowning stranger from a pond even though you never agreed to and owe him nothing. Christopher Wellman argues that obeying the law is similar: it helps rescue everyone from the dangers of a lawless world.","history":"John Rawls, A Theory of Justice (1971), argued that the natural duty of justice is the main basis for obeying just laws, moving away from his own earlier fair play view. Jeremy Waldron, ‘Special Ties and Natural Duties’ (1993), defended it against the objection that it cannot explain why you owe your own state more. Christopher Wellman developed a related ‘Samaritan’ account.","misread":"It does not require obeying any state at all: the duty applies only to institutions that are reasonably just.","variants":["Rawlsian natural duty: support just institutions that apply to you","Samaritanism: obey because the state rescues all from peril (Wellman)"],"reading":[{"title":"Political Obligation","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/political-obligation/"},{"title":"John Rawls","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rawls/"}]},"s7a|member":{"summary":"Being a member of a political community brings duties you never chose, just as belonging to a family does.","idea":"Associative theories say political obligation is like the obligations of family, friendship or neighbourhood. You did not choose your parents, yet you owe them things. In the same way, being part of a community with a shared history and common life makes its laws yours, and gives you duties to fellow members. The view denies that only chosen ties can bind. It is attractive because it matches how many people actually experience citizenship: as part of who they are, not as a contract they signed or a deal they struck.","example":"A sibling’s wedding: you feel bound to help out and show up, though you never agreed to be a sibling and gain nothing in return. Associative theorists say the bond of citizenship carries the same kind of unchosen duty.","history":"Ronald Dworkin, Law’s Empire (1986), argued that political obligation is a form of associative obligation in a community governed by principle. John Horton, Political Obligation (1992), defended membership as the ground of duty. Yael Tamir and David Miller connected it to national belonging. Confucian thought has long modelled the relation of ruler and people on family relations.","misread":"It does not mean any group can bind you; most defenders require the community to treat members with equal concern.","variants":["Dworkin’s community of principle","Identity-based accounts: duties flow from seeing oneself as a member (Horton)"],"reading":[{"title":"Political Obligation","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/political-obligation/"},{"title":"Patriotism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/patriotism/"}]},"s7a|mandate":{"summary":"Rulers hold authority because God or Heaven grants it, so obeying them is part of honouring a higher order.","idea":"This view grounds political authority in a source above any human agreement. In the Chinese tradition, Heaven (tian) grants a dynasty the right to rule so long as it governs for the people’s good. In the European tradition of divine right, God places kings on their thrones, and resisting them resists God’s order. Both deny that authority comes merely from popular choice. Defenders find it attractive because it makes rule a sacred trust with a moral purpose, and explains why authority feels weightier than any contract between equals.","example":"Mencius on King Wu: asked whether it was right for King Wu to kill the tyrant King Zhou of Shang, Mencius replied that a ruler who destroys benevolence and rightness is no longer a ruler but a mere ‘fellow’, so this was not the killing of a king.","history":"The Zhou dynasty justified overthrowing the Shang (c. 1046 BCE) by appeal to the Mandate of Heaven, and Mencius (4th century BCE) developed it. In Europe, St Paul’s Letter to the Romans (chapter 13) says the authorities are instituted by God. James VI and I, The True Law of Free Monarchies (1598), and Robert Filmer, Patriarcha (published 1680), defended divine right.","misread":"Not all versions demand blind obedience: the Mandate of Heaven can be lost by a ruler who governs badly.","variants":["Mandate of Heaven: authority lasts only while rule is benevolent","Divine right of kings: monarchs answer to God alone","Two swords doctrine: spiritual and temporal powers each from God"],"reading":[{"title":"Mencius","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mencius/"},{"title":"Mencius (Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mencius/"}]},"s7a|useful":{"summary":"Treating the law as binding is justified because everyone does far better when people follow it than when each decides alone.","idea":"Instrumental views say authority earns its place by results. People who follow the law usually act better than they would by relying on their own judgement each time: the law coordinates millions of strangers and pools knowledge no individual has. So you have reason to treat it as binding even when you think you know better in a particular case. The view denies that consent or membership is needed. It is attractive because it explains authority the way we explain trusting a doctor or a map: following it reliably gets you where you want to go.","example":"Which side of the road to drive on: it hardly matters whether it is left or right, but it matters enormously that everyone does the same. The law settles it, and that settlement gives everyone a reason to comply.","history":"David Hume, ‘Of the Original Contract’ (1748), argued that allegiance rests on the usefulness of government, not on any promise. Jeremy Bentham grounded obedience in utility. Joseph Raz, The Morality of Freedom (1986), gave the modern ‘service conception’: an authority is legitimate when following it helps people act better on reasons that already apply to them.","misread":"It does not say ‘obey only when useful in this case’; it says the policy of treating law as binding is what pays off.","variants":["Utilitarian justification: obedience maximises welfare (Bentham)","Service conception: authority helps us follow our own reasons better (Raz)"],"reading":[{"title":"Political Legitimacy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/legitimacy/"},{"title":"Authority","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/authority/"}]},"s7b|keep":{"summary":"A good state can be justified by the good it does, even though no state has a right to command your obedience.","idea":"This view separates two questions: whether a state is good to have, and whether it has authority over you. A state may protect rights, provide public goods and keep the peace, which makes its existence justified. But that does not mean its commands bind you merely because they are its commands. You might support it, pay taxes and obey most laws, all because doing so is right on independent grounds. The view is attractive to those who doubt political obligation but still think a world with decent states beats one without them.","example":"A helpful stranger who stops a fight: she is justified in stepping in because she prevents harm, but she has no right to order bystanders about. Simmons argues states can be like this: justified in what they do, without authority over each person.","history":"A. John Simmons drew the distinction in ‘Justification and Legitimacy’ (1999). Robert Paul Wolff, In Defense of Anarchism (1970), and Simmons, Moral Principles and Political Obligations (1979), denied general authority. Joseph Raz argued that authority, where it exists, is piecemeal: a state may have it for some people in some areas and not others. Michael Huemer reached similar conclusions in 2013.","misread":"Accepting a justified state does not mean secretly accepting authority; the claim is that justification and the right to command are different.","variants":["Weak philosophical anarchism: states may be justified though lacking authority","Piecemeal authority: states may have authority over some people in some matters (Raz)"],"reading":[{"title":"Political Legitimacy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/legitimacy/"},{"title":"Anarchism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/anarchism/"}]},"s7b|coop":{"summary":"The state should be replaced by voluntary cooperation, with communities and workplaces run democratically by their own members.","idea":"Social anarchism holds that both the state and private ownership of productive property create domination, and that people can organise their lives through free federations of communes, cooperatives and unions. Decisions are made from below by those affected, and goods are shared according to need or agreed rules. It denies that order requires rulers. Its defenders find it attractive because it unites freedom with equality and solidarity, and because it points to real moments when ordinary people ran farms, factories and towns themselves without bosses or police.","example":"The Spanish collectives of 1936: during the Spanish Civil War, workers and peasants in Catalonia and Aragon took over factories, transport and farms and ran many of them through assemblies, an episode social anarchists still study as a test of their ideas.","history":"Pierre-Joseph Proudhon, What Is Property? (1840), was the first to call himself an anarchist. Mikhail Bakunin led the anti-authoritarian wing of the First International. Peter Kropotkin, The Conquest of Bread (1892) and Mutual Aid (1902), argued cooperation drives survival. Emma Goldman spread the ideas in America. Leo Tolstoy, The Kingdom of God Is Within You (1894), gave a Christian, pacifist version.","misread":"Social anarchists do not want chaos or no rules; they want rules made and enforced by voluntary, egalitarian communities rather than the state.","variants":["Anarcho-communism: distribution according to need (Kropotkin)","Anarcho-syndicalism: society run through federated labour unions","Christian anarchism: obedience to God and nonviolence over the state (Tolstoy)"],"reading":[{"title":"Anarchism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/anarchism/"}]},"s7b|indiv":{"summary":"No one should rule anyone; free individuals should associate only by choice and leave whenever they wish.","idea":"Individualist anarchism puts the sovereign individual at the centre. It rejects the state because no person or group has a right to govern another without their agreement. People may form unions, trade, live in communities, but only by choice and only for as long as each member wants. Some individualists favour markets and private possession; others reject all fixed duties. It denies that society or the nation has claims above the individual. Defenders find it attractive because it takes personal freedom to its logical end: you belong to yourself.","example":"Stirner’s ‘union of egoists’: instead of a state or society that claims your loyalty, Stirner imagined free associations people join because it serves them, like friends who meet for a game and leave when it stops being worth it.","history":"Josiah Warren opened his ‘time store’ in 1827 and later founded experimental communities in the United States. Max Stirner, The Ego and Its Own (1844), attacked state, society and morality as ‘spooks’. Lysander Spooner, No Treason (1867 to 1870), denied the US Constitution bound anyone who had not signed it. Benjamin Tucker edited the journal Liberty (1881 to 1908) and combined individualism with opposition to monopoly.","misread":"Individualist anarchists are not all against cooperation; they oppose forced association, not voluntary groups.","variants":["Egoist anarchism: the individual owes nothing to any cause (Stirner)","American individualist anarchism: mutual exchange without monopoly (Tucker, Warren)"],"reading":[{"title":"Max Stirner","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/max-stirner/"},{"title":"Anarchism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/anarchism/"}]},"s7b|market":{"summary":"The state should be replaced by free markets, with protection and courts provided by competing private agencies people choose to pay.","idea":"Anarcho-capitalism holds that every service the state provides, including police, courts and defence, could be supplied by voluntary markets. People would subscribe to protection agencies and dispute resolution firms, which would compete on price and quality and settle conflicts by arbitration. It denies that anyone may be taxed or ruled without consent, treating the state as a monopoly kept in place by force. Defenders find it attractive because it applies one moral rule everywhere: no one, including governments, may initiate force against peaceful people, and competition improves services.","example":"Medieval Iceland: for roughly three centuries after about 930, Iceland had law courts and an assembly but no king or executive, and victims had to enforce judgements themselves or sell their claims to others. David Friedman studied it as a historical case of privately enforced law.","history":"Gustave de Molinari, ‘The Production of Security’ (1849), first proposed competing private defence firms. Murray Rothbard, For a New Liberty (1973), combined natural rights libertarianism with individualist anarchism. David Friedman, The Machinery of Freedom (1973), defended it on economic grounds. Michael Huemer, The Problem of Political Authority (2013), argued for it from philosophical anarchism.","misread":"It does not mean no law; anarcho-capitalists want law and courts, but supplied by competing voluntary providers.","variants":["Natural rights anarcho-capitalism: grounded in self-ownership (Rothbard)","Consequentialist anarcho-capitalism: markets simply work better (David Friedman)"],"reading":[{"title":"Libertarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/libertarianism/"},{"title":"Anarchism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/anarchism/"}]},"s7b|nofact":{"summary":"Whether states should exist has no true answer; states exist because people want them or are forced to accept them.","idea":"This is moral skepticism turned on the state itself. It does not argue that states are bad or good; it denies that any objective fact settles the matter. States arise from power, habit, interest and agreement, and our talk of their ‘legitimacy’ expresses preferences and pressures rather than truths. You can still prefer living with a state or without one. Defenders find this attractive because it explains why debates about legitimacy never close, and because it describes politics as it seems to work: as a contest of wants and forces.","example":"The Melian Dialogue in Thucydides’ History of the Peloponnesian War: Athenian envoys tell the people of Melos that questions of justice arise only between equals in power, and that the strong do what they can while the weak suffer what they must.","history":"Thucydides (5th century BCE) and Thrasymachus in Plato’s Republic voiced early versions. Max Stirner, The Ego and Its Own (1844), rejected the state as a ‘spook’ with no claim on anyone. J.L. Mackie, Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977), gave the general argument that there are no objective moral facts. Richard Garner has applied moral abolitionism to political debate.","misread":"Political skeptics need not oppose states; they deny that any moral verdict on states is objectively true.","variants":["Error theory about political morality","Political realism in the descriptive sense: politics is about power, not right"],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Skepticism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}]},"s8|yes":{"summary":"A distribution can be unjust even if every step was honest, because justice also concerns who ends up with what.","idea":"Patterned or end-state views say justice is not only about clean procedures. We must also look at the outcome: does everyone have enough, is the gap fair, do people get what they need or deserve? Honest exchanges can still leave a child hungry through no fault of her own, and the view says that result can be unjust and call for correction. It denies that fair steps automatically guarantee a fair result. Defenders find it attractive because outcomes are what people actually live with, and luck at birth shapes them so heavily.","example":"The natural lottery: two babies are born on the same day, one to wealthy parents in a safe city and one to poor parents in a neglected village. Neither did anything to earn their starting point, yet it shapes their whole lives. Patterned theorists ask whether that result can simply be left as it is.","history":"Aristotle, Nicomachean Ethics Book V, discussed distributive justice as giving shares in proportion to worth. John Rawls, A Theory of Justice (1971), argued that the basic structure of society must be judged by how it distributes primary goods. Robert Nozick, Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), coined the term ‘patterned’ for such views. Utilitarians, egalitarians, sufficientarians and socialists all hold versions.","misread":"It does not require equal outcomes; the ‘pattern’ might be enough for all, help for the worst-off, or reward for desert.","variants":["Egalitarian patterns: reduce unfair inequality","Sufficiency patterns: ensure everyone has enough","Welfarist patterns: maximise overall wellbeing"],"reading":[{"title":"Distributive Justice","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justice-distributive/"},{"title":"Egalitarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egalitarianism/"}]},"s8|no":{"summary":"If people acquired and transferred things justly, the resulting distribution is just, however unequal it turns out to be.","idea":"Robert Nozick’s entitlement theory says justice in holdings depends on history. If you acquired something without wronging anyone and others transferred their holdings to you freely, you are entitled to it. The result is just because each step was just, much as a conclusion validly drawn from true premises is true. It denies that justice requires any particular pattern, such as equality. Defenders find it attractive because it respects people’s choices and what they do with their own lives, and because maintaining any pattern would require constantly interfering with free exchanges.","example":"Nozick’s Wilt Chamberlain: start from whatever distribution you think is fair. A million fans each happily pay 25 cents to watch the basketball star play, and he ends up far richer than anyone else. Since everyone started fairly and chose freely, Nozick asks, how could the new result be unjust?","history":"John Locke, Second Treatise of Government (1689), argued that mixing one’s labour with unowned resources creates property, subject to leaving ‘enough and as good’ for others. Robert Nozick, Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), turned this into the entitlement theory, with principles of just acquisition, transfer and rectification. Murray Rothbard and Ayn Rand defended related property rights views.","misread":"It does not bless today’s holdings automatically; Nozick says past theft and fraud must be rectified, which may require large changes.","variants":["Lockean proviso: acquisition must not worsen others’ position","Principle of rectification: past injustices must be repaired"],"reading":[{"title":"Robert Nozick’s Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nozick-political/"},{"title":"Libertarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/libertarianism/"}]},"s8|rules":{"summary":"What counts as honestly owning something depends on rules society makes, so the real question is which rules are fair.","idea":"Conventionalism about property says ownership is not a natural fact that exists before the law. Contracts, money, inheritance, companies and taxes are all defined by legal rules, so there is no pre-tax income that belongs to you in some deeper sense. ‘Honestly earned’ already assumes a set of rules. The view denies that we can judge a distribution by checking whether each step respected property rights, since property rights are what is up for debate. Defenders find it attractive because it matches how property actually works and puts the focus on choosing good rules.","example":"Your salary: it is paid in state-backed money, under a contract the courts will enforce, by a company that exists as a legal person, with roads and police around it. Murphy and Nagel argue that without such rules your ‘pre-tax income’ would not exist, so taxes cannot simply be taking what was already yours.","history":"David Hume, A Treatise of Human Nature (1739 to 1740), treated property as a convention that arises because it serves everyone. Jeremy Bentham argued that property and law are born together. Jean-Jacques Rousseau, Discourse on Inequality (1755), linked property to social institutions. Liam Murphy and Thomas Nagel, The Myth of Ownership (2002), criticised ‘everyday libertarianism’ about pre-tax income.","misread":"It does not say property is unimportant or that anything goes; it says the rules defining property must themselves be justified.","variants":["Humean conventionalism: property rules emerge because they work","Legal positivism about property: rights exist only through law (Bentham)"],"reading":[{"title":"Property and Ownership","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/property/"},{"title":"Convention","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/convention/"}]},"s8|nofact":{"summary":"No distribution of wealth is objectively just or unjust, because there are no objective moral facts about justice.","idea":"This is moral skepticism applied to economic justice. Claims that inequality is unjust, or that it is perfectly fair, express values, interests or social conventions rather than truths about the world. The view does not favour rich or poor; it denies there is a fact for either side to be right about. You may still prefer one arrangement strongly. Defenders find it attractive because debates over fair shares seem endless and closely track people’s positions in society, which suggests to them that interests rather than discovery drive the answers.","example":"Two neighbours argue about whether a tax on inheritances is fair. One says it rewards the state for nothing; the other says it stops unearned advantage. The skeptic notes that both are certain, both appeal to ‘fairness’, and nothing they could observe would ever settle who is right.","history":"Thrasymachus in Plato’s Republic (c. 375 BCE) claimed justice serves the stronger. Karl Marx, in some readings, treated ideas of justice as reflections of economic relations rather than timeless truths. J.L. Mackie, Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977), defended moral error theory. Friedrich Hayek, in a different spirit, argued that ‘social justice’ is an empty idea when applied to market outcomes no one designed.","misread":"Skeptics are not defending the current distribution; they deny any distribution is objectively just, including the present one.","variants":["General moral error theory (Mackie)","Hayek’s view that ‘social justice’ is meaningless for unplanned market outcomes"],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Skepticism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral/"}]},"s8a|welfare":{"summary":"The just arrangement is the one that makes people’s lives go best overall, counting everyone’s gains equally.","idea":"Utilitarian justice judges rules and distributions by their total effect on wellbeing. Each person’s happiness counts the same, whoever they are. This view often favours some redistribution, because an extra pound means far more to someone poor than to a millionaire, but it also allows inequality when incentives make the whole pie bigger. It denies that equality or rights matter apart from their effects on welfare. Defenders find it attractive because it is impartial, focused on what really matters to people, and gives a clear test for every policy: does it make lives better?","example":"Diminishing marginal utility: £100 might feed a struggling family for a week, while the same £100 barely registers for a very rich person. A utilitarian asks which use does more good, and this simple fact often supports moving resources toward those who have less.","history":"Jeremy Bentham, An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789), proposed the greatest happiness principle. John Stuart Mill, Utilitarianism (1861), and Henry Sidgwick, The Methods of Ethics (1874), refined it. Economists like A.C. Pigou applied it to welfare. John Harsanyi argued in the 1950s that impartial choosers would pick average utilitarianism. The Chinese thinker Mozi (5th century BCE) judged policies by their benefit to all.","misread":"Utilitarians do not ignore inequality; because extra resources help the poor more, they often support substantial redistribution.","variants":["Total utilitarianism: maximise the sum of wellbeing","Average utilitarianism: maximise wellbeing per person (Harsanyi)","Rule utilitarianism: choose the rules that do most good"],"reading":[{"title":"The History of Utilitarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/utilitarianism-history/"},{"title":"Distributive Justice","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justice-distributive/"}]},"s8a|prior":{"summary":"Benefits matter more the worse off the person who receives them, so we should give priority to those who have least.","idea":"Prioritarianism says that improving someone’s life always counts, but it counts more when that person is badly off. It differs from strict equality: it does not care about gaps as such, only about how well each person is doing in absolute terms. It differs from utilitarianism: a unit of gain is not equally weighty for everyone. Defenders find it attractive because it captures the urgency of helping those in need without the odd implication that making the rich poorer, while helping no one, could be an improvement.","example":"The levelling down objection: imagine a disaster blinds everyone with sight, so now all are equally blind. A strict egalitarian must say something has improved in one respect. Prioritarians say nothing has improved at all, which is part of why Parfit preferred priority to equality.","history":"Derek Parfit set out the view in his Lindley Lecture ‘Equality or Priority?’ (1991, published 1995). Thomas Nagel’s essay ‘Equality’ (1979) anticipated the idea of urgency. Larry Temkin, Inequality (1993), discussed levelling down. Later defenders include Richard Arneson and Nils Holtug, and economists have developed weighted welfare functions with similar structure.","misread":"It is not egalitarianism: prioritarians care how badly off people are, not how they compare with others.","variants":["Absolute priority (leximin): always help the worst-off first","Weighted priority: gains to the worse-off count more but can be outweighed"],"reading":[{"title":"Prioritarianism as a Theory of Value","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/prioritarianism/"},{"title":"Egalitarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egalitarianism/"}]},"s8a|diff":{"summary":"Inequalities are just only if they make the worst-off group better off than they would be under any alternative.","idea":"John Rawls’s difference principle allows inequality, but only on condition. Higher rewards for doctors or entrepreneurs are fair if, through incentives, they produce more jobs, medicine and services that raise the position of the least advantaged. If an inequality does not help the worst-off, it should go. Rawls places this below equal basic liberties and fair equality of opportunity. Defenders find it attractive because it combines respect for markets and incentives with the conviction that a society should be judged by how its least fortunate members fare.","example":"The veil of ignorance: imagine choosing the rules of your society without knowing whether you will be born rich or poor, talented or not, healthy or sick. Rawls argued you would pick rules that make the worst position as good as possible, since it might be yours.","history":"John Rawls, A Theory of Justice (1971), introduced the difference principle and the original position. He revised it in Political Liberalism (1993) and Justice as Fairness: A Restatement (2001), where he favoured a ‘property-owning democracy’. Thomas Nagel, Thomas Scanlon and Samuel Freeman developed the approach. G.A. Cohen, Rescuing Justice and Equality (2008), criticised it from the left.","misread":"It does not require equality; it permits large inequalities if they truly raise the position of the worst-off.","variants":["Maximin reasoning: choose the option with the best worst outcome","Property-owning democracy: widely dispersed ownership of capital (later Rawls)"],"reading":[{"title":"John Rawls","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rawls/"},{"title":"Original Position","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/original-position/"}]},"s8a|luck":{"summary":"Inequalities are fair when they come from people’s own choices and unfair when they come from luck they could not control.","idea":"Luck egalitarianism says no one should be better or worse off because of unchosen circumstances like talent, health, family wealth or where they were born. But people should bear the results of their free choices, such as working more hours or taking a gamble. So it supports compensating someone born with a disability while letting a person who chose more leisure have less money. It denies that all inequality is unfair and that all market outcomes are fair. Defenders find it attractive because it combines equality with personal responsibility.","example":"Dworkin’s desert island auction: shipwrecked survivors each get an equal number of clamshells and bid for the island’s resources. If no one would trade their bundle for anyone else’s, the division is fair. Later differences from effort and chosen risks are fine; those from bad brute luck call for insurance.","history":"Ronald Dworkin, ‘What Is Equality?’ (1981) and Sovereign Virtue (2000), distinguished brute luck from option luck. Richard Arneson, ‘Equality and Equal Opportunity for Welfare’ (1989), and G.A. Cohen, ‘On the Currency of Egalitarian Justice’ (1989), developed rival versions. Elizabeth Anderson’s critique ‘What Is the Point of Equality?’ (1999) named the position and sparked wide debate.","misread":"Luck egalitarians do not want everyone equal regardless of effort; they want differences to track choices, not circumstances.","variants":["Equality of resources (Dworkin)","Equal opportunity for welfare (Arneson)","Equal access to advantage (Cohen)"],"reading":[{"title":"Justice and Bad Luck","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justice-bad-luck/"},{"title":"Egalitarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egalitarianism/"}]},"s8a|enough":{"summary":"What justice requires most is that everyone has enough for a decent life; gaps above that line matter much less.","idea":"Sufficientarianism says the central moral task is to bring everyone above a threshold of a good enough life, with adequate food, shelter, health, education and security. Once everyone is there, inequality matters far less or not at all. It denies that equality is valuable for its own sake. Defenders find it attractive because what seems urgent about poverty is not that some have more than others, but that some do not have enough. Worrying about the gap between a millionaire and a billionaire can distract from the person without a home.","example":"The millionaire and the billionaire: a society where everyone has a comfortable life but some are billionaires, versus one where all are equal but many go hungry. Sufficientarians, following Frankfurt, judge the first far better, since what troubles us about poverty is having too little, not having less.","history":"Harry Frankfurt, ‘Equality as a Moral Ideal’ (1987) and On Inequality (2015), launched the modern view. Roger Crisp (2003) defended a version based on compassion. Martha Nussbaum’s capabilities approach sets thresholds for central capabilities. Paula Casal, ‘Why Sufficiency Is Not Enough’ (2007), argued that sufficiency must be combined with other principles.","misread":"It is not stingy: sufficientarians often set a demanding threshold for a decent life, well above bare survival.","variants":["Strict sufficiency: nothing matters above the threshold (Frankfurt)","Mixed views: sufficiency first, then equality or priority above it","Capability thresholds: a minimum level of each central capability (Nussbaum)"],"reading":[{"title":"Egalitarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egalitarianism/"},{"title":"Distributive Justice","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justice-distributive/"}]},"s8a|status":{"summary":"Justice means people relate as equals: no one so poor they must bow to others, no one so powerful others must bow.","idea":"Relational egalitarianism says the point of equality is not equal shares of goods but a society where people stand as equals. What is wrong with caste, aristocracy, racial hierarchy or extreme poverty is that they make some people dependent on, subordinate to or excluded by others. Some material inequality is fine so long as it does not create domination or humiliation. Defenders find it attractive because it matches what equality movements have fought for in history: not identical bank balances, but the end of being looked down on, ordered around and shut out.","example":"The caste system: B.R. Ambedkar argued that caste was wrong not mainly because some had less wealth, but because it fixed people into ranks where some were ‘untouchable’, forbidden to share wells, temples and schools. The injustice lay in the relationships themselves.","history":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau, The Social Contract (1762), held that no citizen should be rich enough to buy another or poor enough to have to sell himself. B.R. Ambedkar, Annihilation of Caste (1936), attacked graded inequality. Elizabeth Anderson, ‘What Is the Point of Equality?’ (1999), named the modern view, developed by Samuel Scheffler and later in Anderson’s Private Government (2017).","misread":"It is not indifferent to money; large material gaps matter whenever they translate into power over others.","variants":["Democratic equality: equal standing as citizens (Anderson)","Social egalitarianism: no ranks of superior and inferior (Scheffler)"],"reading":[{"title":"Egalitarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egalitarianism/"},{"title":"Equality","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/equality/"}]},"s8a|desert":{"summary":"A just distribution gives each person what they deserve, based on their effort, contribution or merit.","idea":"Desert-based justice says fairness means proportion: those who contribute more or work harder deserve more, and those who freeload deserve less. It denies that equal shares or maximum welfare define justice. Many people across the political spectrum hold this view: some use it to defend market rewards, others to criticise inheritance and windfalls nobody earned. Defenders find it attractive because it matches deep everyday intuitions about fairness: we bristle when shirkers get the same as hard workers, or when the undeserving profit while those who did the work go unrewarded.","example":"A group project: four students share one grade, but one did nearly all the work while another did nothing. Most people feel the equal grade is unfair. Desert theorists say the same intuition applies to how a society rewards work and contribution.","history":"Aristotle, Nicomachean Ethics Book V, said justice gives equal shares to equals and unequal shares to unequals in proportion to merit. Joel Feinberg, ‘Justice and Personal Desert’ (1970), analysed desert claims. George Sher, Desert (1987), and David Miller, Principles of Social Justice (1999), defended desert as a central principle. Rawls, in contrast, denied that natural talents create deep desert.","misread":"Desert is not the same as market price; desert theorists often argue that markets reward luck as well as merit.","variants":["Contribution-based desert: reward for what you produce","Effort-based desert: reward for how hard you try","Merit-based desert: reward for virtue or excellence"],"reading":[{"title":"Desert","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/desert/"},{"title":"Distributive Justice","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justice-distributive/"}]},"s8a|common":{"summary":"Land and major industries should be owned in common, by workers or the public, rather than by private owners who profit.","idea":"Socialism holds that when a few privately own the factories, land and capital everyone needs to work, they can live off others’ labour, and workers have little say over their own working lives. Common ownership would let the wealth people produce together be shared and decisions be made democratically. It does not mean abolishing personal belongings like homes or clothes. Defenders find it attractive because it extends democracy into the economy and aims to replace competition for profit with cooperation for need.","example":"Cohen’s camping trip: friends on a camping trip share their gear, cook together and divide tasks. No one charges others for using their frying pan or bargains for the best pitch. G.A. Cohen asked why, if this feels right among friends, a whole society could not run more like it.","history":"Robert Owen ran a cooperative mill community at New Lanark in the early 1800s. Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels, The Communist Manifesto (1848), and Marx, Capital volume 1 (1867), gave the most influential critique of capitalism. Eduard Bernstein argued for democratic, gradual socialism. John Roemer developed market socialism, and G.A. Cohen wrote Why Not Socialism? (2009).","misread":"Socialism is not the abolition of personal property; it concerns ownership of the means of production, such as factories and land.","variants":["State socialism: public ownership run by government","Market socialism: worker or public firms competing in markets (Roemer)","Workers’ self-management: each firm run by its workers, as in cooperatives"],"reading":[{"title":"Socialism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/socialism/"},{"title":"Karl Marx","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marx/"}]},"s8a|settled":{"summary":"Rules are just when they are the long settled rules people have planned their lives around, applied fairly to all.","idea":"Conventional justice says the fairness of property and distribution lies in established rules, not in any ideal pattern or abstract theory. People build homes, businesses and families around stable expectations; overturning them, even for a seemingly better design, causes real harm and uncertainty. What matters most is that the rules are known, settled and applied impartially. Defenders find it attractive because it values the security and trust that long practice brings, and recognises that no single reformer can foresee all the effects of remaking the rules from scratch.","example":"Hume’s two rowers: two people row a boat together in time without ever making a promise. Each simply adjusts to the other’s stroke because it works. Hume thought property rules arise the same way, by gradual convention, and that their stability is what makes them valuable.","history":"David Hume, A Treatise of Human Nature, Book 3 (1739 to 1740), called justice an ‘artificial virtue’ grounded in convention. Edmund Burke, Reflections on the Revolution in France (1790), defended inherited institutions. Friedrich Hayek, Law, Legislation and Liberty (1973 to 1979), argued that evolved rules hold knowledge no planner has. Michael Oakeshott stressed the authority of tradition in practical life.","misread":"It does not say every old rule is just; it says stability and impartial application are central goods any change must respect.","variants":["Humean conventionalism: justice as a useful convention","Hayekian spontaneous order: evolved rules hold dispersed knowledge"],"reading":[{"title":"Hume’s Moral Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume-moral/"},{"title":"Convention","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/convention/"}]},"s8b|no":{"summary":"The state may not tax honestly earned money to help the poor; help should come from voluntary charity.","idea":"Strict libertarianism holds that people own themselves and the fruits of their honest labour. Taking someone’s earnings by force, even for a good cause, violates their rights. That does not mean ignoring the poor child: libertarians typically stress the duty to help, and the power of charities, churches, mutual aid societies and growing economies to do it. What they deny is that the state may compel it. They find the view attractive because it treats everyone, rich or poor, as an owner of their own life whom no majority may conscript.","example":"Nozick’s forced labour comparison: Nozick argued that taxing the earnings of someone’s work is like forcing them to work a number of hours for others’ purposes. If we would not force a person to spend each Friday working for strangers, how can we take Friday’s wages?","history":"Herbert Spencer, Social Statics (1851), defended the law of equal freedom. Ayn Rand, Atlas Shrugged (1957), celebrated self-reliance. Robert Nozick, Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), argued only a minimal state protecting against force, theft and fraud is justified. Murray Rothbard and Jan Narveson defended related views.","misread":"Libertarians do not oppose helping the poor; they oppose forcing others to pay for it.","variants":["Minarchism: a minimal state for police, courts and defence only (Nozick)","Objectivism: rational self-interest as a moral ideal (Rand)"],"reading":[{"title":"Libertarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/libertarianism/"},{"title":"Robert Nozick’s Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nozick-political/"}]},"s8b|floor":{"summary":"The state may guarantee everyone a basic minimum, but above that floor people should keep what they earn.","idea":"Classical liberalism favours free markets, private property and limited government, while accepting a modest safety net so no one falls into destitution. It treats this floor as insurance against misfortune or a condition of a free society, not as a way to equalise outcomes. Above it, free exchange should decide rewards. It denies both strict libertarianism’s ban on all redistribution and the egalitarian aim of narrowing gaps. Defenders find it attractive because it combines the prosperity and freedom of markets with basic decency toward those who fall behind.","example":"Friedman’s negative income tax: instead of many separate welfare programmes, Milton Friedman proposed that people earning below a certain line receive a payment from the tax system, topping them up, while still gaining from each extra pound earned. A simple floor, then the market.","history":"Adam Smith, The Wealth of Nations (1776), laid out the case for free markets. John Stuart Mill, Principles of Political Economy (1848), discussed public relief. Friedrich Hayek, The Road to Serfdom (1944) and The Constitution of Liberty (1960), endorsed a guaranteed minimum. Milton Friedman, Capitalism and Freedom (1962), proposed the negative income tax. John Tomasi, Free Market Fairness (2012), revived the tradition.","misread":"Classical liberals are not opposed to all government help; many explicitly endorse a guaranteed minimum.","variants":["Hayekian liberalism: a minimum outside the market","Negative income tax (Friedman)","Market democracy: free markets justified by benefits to the poor (Tomasi)"],"reading":[{"title":"Liberalism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/liberalism/"},{"title":"Libertarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/libertarianism/"}]},"s8b|commons":{"summary":"Natural resources belong to everyone, so those who own land and resources owe others a fair share of their value.","idea":"Left-libertarianism agrees with other libertarians that each person fully owns themselves and their labour, so taxing work is suspect. But it holds that no one made the earth, so land and natural resources start out belonging to all equally. Whoever takes more than an equal share must compensate the rest, for example through a land tax paid out as a dividend. It denies that first come is first served for the earth itself. Defenders find it attractive because it joins strong personal liberty with a real basis for sharing.","example":"The Alaska Permanent Fund: since 1982 Alaska has paid residents an annual dividend from its oil revenue fund. Left-libertarians often point to it as a working example of treating natural resource wealth as belonging to everyone.","history":"Thomas Paine, Agrarian Justice (1797), proposed a fund financed by landowners paying every citizen a sum at age 21, since land was the common property of humanity. Henry George, Progress and Poverty (1879), argued for a single tax on land value. Hillel Steiner, An Essay on Rights (1994), Peter Vallentyne and Michael Otsuka, Libertarianism without Inequality (2003), developed the modern view.","misread":"It is not socialism: left-libertarians defend full self-ownership and free markets, sharing only the value of natural resources.","variants":["Georgism: a single tax on land value (Henry George)","Equal share left-libertarianism: each is owed an equal share of natural resources (Steiner)"],"reading":[{"title":"Libertarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/libertarianism/"}]},"s10|neg":{"summary":"Freedom means no one interfering with your choices; lacking money or ability is a different problem from lacking freedom.","idea":"Negative liberty is freedom from interference by other people. You are free to the extent that nobody blocks, threatens or coerces you. A poor person who cannot afford a holiday is not unfree on this view, just poor, and calling it unfreedom blurs two different problems that may need different remedies. It denies that freedom means having resources or living by your ‘true self’. Defenders find it attractive because it is clear and measurable, and because it guards against rulers who claim to be making people ‘truly free’ while ordering them about.","example":"The locked door: if someone locks you in a room, you are unfree to leave. If the door is open but you are too tired to walk out, you are not unfree, just tired. Negative liberty theorists say the poor person is in the second situation, not the first.","history":"Thomas Hobbes, Leviathan (1651), defined liberty as the absence of external impediments to motion. Jeremy Bentham saw every law as a restriction of liberty. Isaiah Berlin, ‘Two Concepts of Liberty’ (1958), drew the famous distinction and favoured the negative concept. Friedrich Hayek, The Constitution of Liberty (1960), and Robert Nozick built on it. Ian Carter and Matthew Kramer developed precise modern versions.","misread":"Defenders need not think poverty is unimportant; they think it is a different problem from unfreedom.","variants":["Pure negative liberty: only physical prevention counts (Hobbes, Steiner)","Broader negative liberty: threats and coercion also count (Berlin)"],"reading":[{"title":"Positive and Negative Liberty","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/liberty-positive-negative/"}]},"s10|means":{"summary":"Freedom means actually being able to do things, which requires money, health and skills, not just being left alone.","idea":"Effective freedom says what matters is the real options a person has. A legal right to travel means little if you cannot afford a ticket; a right to free speech means little if you cannot read. So freedom expands when people gain resources, education and health, and shrinks when they lack them. It denies that the mere absence of interference makes someone free. Defenders find it attractive because it focuses on what people can actually do and be, and treats poverty and ignorance as real limits on liberty.","example":"Cohen’s train ticket: G.A. Cohen noted that if you board a train without a ticket, the conductor will put you off. So lacking money is not just lacking ability: it means others will stop you. Poverty, he argued, really does restrict freedom.","history":"T.H. Green, ‘Liberal Legislation and Freedom of Contract’ (1881), argued that freedom is a positive power of doing something worth doing. John Dewey and the New Liberals extended this. Amartya Sen, Development as Freedom (1999), defined development as expanding the real freedoms people enjoy, his ‘capabilities’. Martha Nussbaum, Women and Human Development (2000), listed central capabilities. G.A. Cohen wrote ‘Freedom and Money’ (2001).","misread":"It is not the same as self-mastery: effective freedom is about options and resources, not about which desires are truly yours.","variants":["Capability approach: freedom to achieve valuable functionings (Sen, Nussbaum)","New liberalism: freedom as positive power to act (Green)"],"reading":[{"title":"Positive and Negative Liberty","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/liberty-positive-negative/"},{"title":"The Capability Approach","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/capability-approach/"}]},"s10|nondom":{"summary":"Freedom means no one having arbitrary power over you, even if they never use it.","idea":"Freedom as non-domination, the republican idea of liberty, says you are unfree whenever someone could interfere with you at will, without having to answer for it. A slave with a kind master is unfree, because everything depends on the master’s mood. On the other hand, a law made through fair, accountable procedures may restrict you without dominating you. It denies both that all interference reduces freedom and that the mere absence of interference makes you free. Defenders find it attractive because it explains why dependence, not just coercion, is degrading.","example":"Nora in Ibsen’s A Doll’s House: her husband is affectionate and allows her many things, but she lives entirely at his pleasure, unable to act without his permission. Philip Pettit uses her as a picture of someone who suffers domination without interference.","history":"The idea goes back to Roman law, which defined a free person as one not under another’s power, and to Cicero and Livy. Niccolò Machiavelli, Discourses on Livy (c. 1517), and English republicans of the 1600s developed it, as did Mary Wollstonecraft, A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792). Philip Pettit, Republicanism (1997), and Quentin Skinner, Liberty before Liberalism (1998), revived it.","misread":"This ‘republican’ freedom has nothing to do with the US Republican Party; it comes from the Roman idea of the free citizen.","variants":["Neo-Roman liberty: freedom as not living in dependence (Skinner)","Pettit’s non-domination: freedom from uncontrolled power to interfere"],"reading":[{"title":"Republicanism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/republicanism/"},{"title":"Positive and Negative Liberty","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/liberty-positive-negative/"}]},"s10|self":{"summary":"Freedom means being ruled by your own reason and deepest values rather than by impulse, addiction or outside pressure.","idea":"Positive liberty as self-mastery says freedom is not just about others leaving you alone. Someone driven by compulsions they reject, or swept along by fashion and fear, is not fully free even if no one interferes. True freedom is living by values you have reflected on and endorse. It denies that simply doing whatever you want at the moment is freedom. Defenders find it attractive because it matches the experience of breaking a bad habit and feeling liberated, and because it connects freedom with the dignity of rational self-government.","example":"Plato’s charioteer, from the Phaedrus: the soul is like a charioteer driving two horses, one noble and one unruly. A person is free and well ordered when reason, the charioteer, guides the team, and enslaved when the unruly horse drags it wherever appetite pulls.","history":"Plato and the Stoics, especially Epictetus (c. 50 to 135 CE), held that the wise person is free even in chains. Spinoza, Ethics (1677), linked freedom to understanding. Rousseau, The Social Contract (1762), said obeying a law we give ourselves is liberty. Kant made autonomy central to morality. Buddhist traditions also see liberation as freedom from craving. Berlin (1958) named it and warned of its misuse.","misread":"Most defenders do not think the state should force people to be ‘truly free’; self-mastery is usually a personal ideal.","variants":["Stoic freedom: inner freedom no tyrant can take","Kantian autonomy: acting on laws one’s own reason gives","Rousseauian freedom: obeying laws we make together"],"reading":[{"title":"Positive and Negative Liberty","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/liberty-positive-negative/"},{"title":"Autonomy in Moral and Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/"}]},"s3|intrinsic":{"summary":"Democracy is right because giving everyone an equal say in shared laws is what respect between equals requires.","idea":"This view holds that democracy is valuable in itself, not only for its results. When people must live under common rules but disagree about them, the only fair way to decide is to give each an equal voice. Ruling over others without giving them a say treats them as less than equals, even if the rulers are wise. It denies that better outcomes alone could justify taking away people’s vote. Defenders find it attractive because it treats each citizen as an author of the laws, not merely someone subject to them.","example":"A shared house deciding on rules: even if one housemate is cleverer and would choose slightly better rules, the others would reasonably object to being governed by her alone. They live there too. Equal votes express that they are equal members of the household.","history":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau, The Social Contract (1762), argued that legitimate law expresses the general will of all citizens. John Stuart Mill also valued participation for the character it builds. Jeremy Waldron, Law and Disagreement (1999), defended majority rule as respect amid disagreement. Thomas Christiano, The Constitution of Equality (2008), argued democracy publicly realises equality.","misread":"It does not claim democracy always makes the best decisions; it claims fair process has value even when results are imperfect.","variants":["Equality-based view: democracy publicly expresses equal standing (Christiano)","Deliberative democracy: legitimacy through reasoned public discussion (Habermas)"],"reading":[{"title":"Democracy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/democracy/"}]},"s3|protect":{"summary":"Democracy is valuable because it lets people remove bad rulers peacefully and stops any group holding unchecked power.","idea":"The protective view says democracy’s chief virtue is defensive. No person or party can be trusted with power that cannot be taken away, so we need a system where rulers must face the people and can be voted out without violence. This keeps leaders attentive to citizens’ interests and limits abuse. It does not claim voters are wise or that voting is an expression of dignity. Defenders find it attractive because it rests on a modest, realistic view of human nature: power corrupts, so build in a way to take it back.","example":"Sen’s famine observation: Amartya Sen noted that no major famine has occurred in a functioning democracy with a free press. Rulers who must face elections and newspapers cannot ignore mass starvation the way unaccountable rulers can.","history":"James Madison, Federalist No. 51 (1788), argued that ambition must be made to counteract ambition. James Mill, ‘Essay on Government’ (1820), defended representation as protection against rulers’ self-interest. Karl Popper, The Open Society and Its Enemies (1945), recast the question of politics as how to remove bad rulers without bloodshed. Amartya Sen, Development as Freedom (1999), discussed famine prevention.","misread":"","variants":["Popperian democracy: a method for dismissing governments peacefully","Madisonian checks: dividing power so it limits itself"],"reading":[{"title":"Democracy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/democracy/"},{"title":"Karl Popper","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/popper/"}]},"s3|epistemic":{"summary":"Democracy is valuable because large, diverse groups of ordinary people tend to make better decisions than small expert groups.","idea":"Epistemic democracy holds that democracy is good at finding the right answers. Each voter has a partial view, and pooling many independent and varied views cancels out individual errors and brings in knowledge experts lack. Open debate also tests ideas. It denies that a small, well-educated elite would reliably govern better. Defenders find it attractive because it answers critics on their own ground: democracy is not just fair, it is smart, drawing on the scattered knowledge of millions rather than the blind spots of a few.","example":"Galton’s ox: in 1906 at a country fair in England, around 800 people guessed the weight of an ox. Francis Galton found the middle guess of the crowd was within about one per cent of the true weight, closer than almost any individual guess.","history":"Aristotle, Politics Book III, suggested the many together may judge better than the few, as a feast to which many contribute beats one provided by one person. The Marquis de Condorcet, Essay on the Application of Analysis to the Probability of Majority Decisions (1785), proved the jury theorem. David Estlund, Democratic Authority (2008), and Hélène Landemore, Democratic Reason (2013), developed modern versions.","misread":"It does not say any single voter is wise; it says groups can be wiser than their members.","variants":["Condorcet jury theorem: majorities of better than chance voters are very reliable","Diversity trumps ability: varied groups beat uniform experts (Landemore)"],"reading":[{"title":"Democracy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/democracy/"}]},"s3|no":{"summary":"Democracy has no special claim to be the right system; other forms of government could rule as well or better.","idea":"Democratic skepticism denies that democracy is required by justice or uniquely good at governing. Whether a system is good depends on its results, and voters are often uninformed, while other arrangements, such as rule by tested experts or wise officials, might perform better. It does not necessarily favour dictatorship or abolish rights; many skeptics keep strong protections for liberty. Defenders find it attractive because they see no reason to treat one decision procedure as sacred, and they take seriously evidence about how voters actually decide.","example":"Plato’s ship of state: in the Republic, Socrates pictures a ship whose sailors squabble for the helm and flatter the owner, while ignoring the one person who has studied navigation. Plato asks why we would choose rulers this way when we would never choose a ship’s pilot so.","history":"Plato, Republic (c. 375 BCE), criticised Athenian democracy. Confucian thinkers stressed rule by the worthy. Thomas Hobbes judged regimes by their ability to keep peace. Joseph Schumpeter, Capitalism, Socialism and Democracy (1942), gave a deflated view of democracy. Bryan Caplan, The Myth of the Rational Voter (2007), Jason Brennan, Against Democracy (2016), and Daniel Bell, The China Model (2015), revived skepticism.","misread":"Skeptics do not all oppose voting; many simply deny that it is the only legitimate method or that it has value in itself.","variants":["Instrumentalism: judge systems only by results","Epistocracy: more say for the knowledgeable","Political meritocracy: officials selected for ability and virtue"],"reading":[{"title":"Democracy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/democracy/"},{"title":"Plato’s Ethics and Politics in The Republic","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/plato-ethics-politics/"}]},"s3b|perform":{"summary":"Whoever delivers peace, order and good results has a claim to govern, however they came to power.","idea":"Results-based legitimacy says the test of a government is what it does for its people: security, prosperity, order and justice. How rulers were chosen matters far less. A competent government that keeps the peace earns obedience; one that fails loses it, whatever its pedigree or mandate. It denies that elections or bloodlines confer a special right to rule. Defenders find it attractive because it focuses on what ordinary people need most from a government, and because it holds rulers to a standard of ongoing performance rather than a single moment of selection.","example":"Hobbes’s state of nature: without a power able to keep everyone in awe, Hobbes argued, life would be a war of all against all, full of fear and poverty. Whoever actually provides protection from that condition has a claim on our obedience.","history":"Thomas Hobbes, Leviathan (1651), argued that obedience is owed to whoever can effectively protect subjects. The Confucian tradition judged rulers by the welfare of the people. Political scientists use the term ‘performance legitimacy’ for governments whose support rests on economic growth and order. Daniel Bell, The China Model (2015), discusses performance as one source of legitimacy.","misread":"It does not endorse any strongman; rulers lose their claim when they stop delivering peace and good results.","variants":["Hobbesian de facto authority: obedience in exchange for protection","Performance legitimacy: support earned through growth and order"],"reading":[{"title":"Political Legitimacy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/legitimacy/"},{"title":"Hobbes’s Moral and Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hobbes-moral/"}]},"s3b|epistoc":{"summary":"Everyone may take part, but those who know more about politics should have more say in decisions.","idea":"Epistocracy, rule by the knowledgeable, holds that political power should be weighted toward competence. Voting affects everyone, so ignorant or misinformed votes impose risks on others. Proposals include a basic civics test to vote, extra votes for the well informed, or expert bodies with veto power over uninformed choices. It denies that each citizen has a right to an equal share of power regardless of knowledge. Defenders find it attractive because we demand competence from doctors and pilots who make decisions affecting others, and see no reason politics should be different.","example":"Mill’s plural votes: John Stuart Mill proposed that everyone should have a vote, but that people with more education or more demanding occupations should have additional votes, so that knowledge carried more weight without anyone being shut out.","history":"Plato’s Republic argued for rule by those with knowledge. John Stuart Mill, Considerations on Representative Government (1861), proposed plural voting. Jason Brennan, Against Democracy (2016), argued that people have a right to competent government, which may favour epistocracy. Brennan and others have discussed variants such as restricted suffrage and an ‘enfranchisement lottery’.","misread":"Most epistocrats do not want rule by a tiny expert elite; many keep votes for everyone but weight them by knowledge.","variants":["Plural voting: extra votes for the educated (Mill)","Restricted suffrage: voting after passing a competence test","Epistocratic veto: expert bodies may overrule uninformed decisions"],"reading":[{"title":"Democracy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/democracy/"},{"title":"Mill’s Moral and Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mill-moral-political/"}]},"s3b|merit":{"summary":"Officials should be chosen for ability and good character through exams and proven records, and judged by how the people fare.","idea":"Political meritocracy holds that government should be in the hands of capable and virtuous people selected through rigorous tests and experience, not popularity contests or birth. Officials rise through lower posts, proving themselves, and are judged by how well the people actually live. It denies that equal votes are the only fair way to choose leaders. Defenders find it attractive because governing is hard and consequential, and a system that selects and trains for wisdom and public spirit seems more likely to serve everyone, including future generations who cannot vote.","example":"China’s imperial examinations: from the Sui dynasty (605) until 1905, candidates studied the Confucian classics and sat demanding written exams; those who passed became officials. In principle, a farmer’s son could rise to high office through study.","history":"Confucius and Mencius (6th to 4th centuries BCE) taught that rulers should employ the worthy and able. Plato’s Republic proposed rule by trained philosopher-guardians. The Chinese imperial examination system ran for about 1,300 years. Modern defenders include Daniel Bell, The China Model (2015), Joseph Chan, Confucian Perfectionism (2014), Tongdong Bai, Against Political Equality (2019), and Jiang Qing, A Confucian Constitutional Order (2012).","misread":"Political meritocrats need not reject all democracy; many propose hybrids combining elections with merit-selected bodies.","variants":["Vertical democratic meritocracy: democracy locally, meritocracy higher up (Bell)","Hybrid Confucian constitution: a chamber of merit beside an elected one (Bai, Jiang Qing)","Platonic guardianship: rule by philosophers"],"reading":[{"title":"Confucius","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confucius/"},{"title":"Plato’s Ethics and Politics in The Republic","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/plato-ethics-politics/"}]},"s3b|heir":{"summary":"A rightful ruler is one whose authority comes from birth, sacred calling or long tradition, such as a hereditary monarch.","idea":"Monarchism holds that a single ruler, usually hereditary and often advised by wise counsellors, is the best or most legitimate form of government. A monarch raised for the role stands above parties and factions, embodies the continuity of the nation and has a long-term stake in its future. Authority grounded in tradition or the sacred carries a dignity that elections cannot confer. It denies that legitimacy must come from popular choice. Defenders find it attractive because it offers stability, unity and a living link with history.","example":"Bagehot’s ‘dignified’ crown: Walter Bagehot argued that in Britain the monarch formed the dignified part of the constitution, commanding loyalty and reverence, while the cabinet did the efficient work of governing. A figure above politics, he thought, helped hold the nation together.","history":"Jean Bodin, Six Books of the Commonwealth (1576), theorised undivided sovereignty. Robert Filmer, Patriarcha (published 1680), modelled kingship on fatherhood. Thomas Hobbes, Leviathan (1651), argued monarchy best aligns rulers’ and subjects’ interests. Joseph de Maistre defended throne and altar after 1789. Walter Bagehot, The English Constitution (1867), analysed constitutional monarchy. Hans-Hermann Hoppe, Democracy: The God That Failed (2001), gave a libertarian case.","misread":"Many monarchists favour constitutional monarchy with elected parliaments, not absolute rule.","variants":["Absolute monarchy: the monarch holds all sovereign power","Constitutional monarchy: a hereditary head of state alongside elected government","Elective monarchy: monarchs chosen for life by an assembly"],"reading":[{"title":"Authority","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/authority/"},{"title":"Hobbes’s Moral and Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hobbes-moral/"}]},"s4|harm":{"summary":"The law may punish speech only when it directly harms particular people; bad ideas must be answered with more speech.","idea":"The harm principle for speech says the state may restrict expression only to prevent clear harm to others, such as threats, fraud, defamation or incitement to imminent violence. Offensive, wrong or hateful ideas must be met with argument, not punishment. It denies that the state may ban speech for being offensive or dangerous to public morals. Defenders find it attractive because they doubt anyone can be trusted to decide which ideas are too bad to hear, and because even false views force us to understand and sharpen the truth.","example":"Mill’s corn dealer: Mill said the opinion that corn dealers starve the poor may be freely printed in the press. But shouting the same opinion to an angry mob gathered outside a corn dealer’s house may justly be punished, because there it directly incites harm.","history":"John Milton, Areopagitica (1644), argued against licensing the press. John Stuart Mill, On Liberty (1859), gave the classic defence of free discussion. Justice Oliver Wendell Holmes’s dissent in Abrams v. United States (1919) introduced the ‘free trade in ideas’ image. Brandenburg v. Ohio (1969) set the US standard protecting speech unless it incites imminent lawless action.","misread":"It does not mean defenders approve of hateful speech; they hold that the law is the wrong tool against it.","variants":["Marketplace of ideas: truth emerges from open competition (Holmes)","Self-government argument: free speech is needed for democracy (Meiklejohn)"],"reading":[{"title":"Freedom of Speech","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freedom-speech/"},{"title":"Mill’s Moral and Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mill-moral-political/"}]},"s4|dignity":{"summary":"Besides direct harms, the law may punish attacks on people for who they are, because such speech undermines their equal standing.","idea":"The dignity principle says a just society must secure every member’s standing as an equal citizen. Speech that brands a racial, religious or other group as subhuman or dangerous does real damage: it tells members of that group they do not truly belong and erodes the public assurance that everyone will be treated as an equal. Such speech may be restricted even when no individual is directly threatened. Defenders find it attractive because it treats equal dignity as a public good that law protects, as most European democracies do.","example":"The Skokie case: in 1977 a neo-Nazi group planned to march through Skokie, Illinois, home to many Holocaust survivors. US courts upheld their right to march. Defenders of the dignity principle argue that in most European countries such a display could be prohibited, and should be.","history":"After World War II many democracies adopted laws against incitement to hatred, such as Germany’s Volksverhetzung provision in its criminal code. The International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights (1966), Article 20, requires states to prohibit advocacy of national, racial or religious hatred that incites discrimination, hostility or violence. Jeremy Waldron, The Harm in Hate Speech (2012), gave the leading philosophical defence.","misread":"It does not ban all offensive speech; it targets speech attacking people’s basic standing because of group membership.","variants":["Waldron’s assurance view: hate speech undermines the public good of inclusion","Critical race theory approaches: hate speech as a harm of subordination"],"reading":[{"title":"Hate Speech","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hate-speech/"},{"title":"Freedom of Speech","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freedom-speech/"}]},"s4|order":{"summary":"Besides direct harms, the law may punish speech that endangers public order or the shared values that hold society together.","idea":"The order principle says a society is more than a collection of individuals: it rests on shared values, peace between groups and respect for what its people hold sacred. Speech that undermines these, such as blasphemy, incitement of religious strife or attacks on shared moral foundations, may be restricted even if no particular person is harmed. It denies that only harm to identifiable individuals matters. Defenders find it attractive because social harmony and a common moral framework are fragile goods, and a community may protect them as it protects other public goods.","example":"The Hart and Devlin debate: Lord Devlin argued in 1959 that a society is held together by a shared morality and may use law to protect it, as it protects against treason. H.L.A. Hart replied that this gave majorities too much power. Their exchange remains the classic statement of the issue.","history":"Plato’s Republic and Laws proposed supervising poetry and teaching for the good of the city. Many legal systems have had blasphemy and sedition laws. Patrick Devlin, The Enforcement of Morals (1959 lecture, 1965 book), defended legal protection of shared morality. Singapore’s Maintenance of Religious Harmony Act (1990) is a modern example aimed at preserving harmony between faiths.","misread":"It is not only about protecting rulers; many defenders aim at peace between communities and protection of shared sacred values.","variants":["Legal moralism: law may enforce a society’s shared morality (Devlin)","Harmony-based approaches: protecting peace between religious and ethnic groups"],"reading":[{"title":"Freedom of Speech","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freedom-speech/"}]},"s4|skeptic":{"summary":"‘Free speech’ is not one clear principle; every society draws lines on speech for political reasons, and the real question is where.","idea":"Free speech skepticism says there is no coherent general right to free expression that can settle hard cases. Every society already restricts perjury, fraud, false advertising, copyright violation, state secrets and much else. The question is never whether to limit speech, but which speech, and that is answered by substantive judgements about values and interests. It denies that ‘free speech’ is a neutral principle above politics. Defenders find it attractive because it is honest about how speech law actually works and stops each side using ‘free speech’ as a slogan.","example":"The list of restrictions: perjury, fraud, insider tips, copyright infringement, threats, false advertising and leaking military secrets are all speech, and all are restricted in free societies. Skeptics argue that once we admit this, ‘free speech’ alone cannot tell us where to stop.","history":"Stanley Fish, There’s No Such Thing as Free Speech, and It’s a Good Thing, Too (1994), argued every speech regime reflects political choices. Larry Alexander, Is There a Right of Freedom of Expression? (2005), concluded that no coherent general right exists. Frederick Schauer’s work on speech exceptions has fed the debate. Critics on both left and right have argued that ‘neutral’ speech rules favour some groups.","misread":"Skeptics need not favour more censorship; they say decisions about speech should be argued on their merits, not by appeal to a slogan.","variants":["Fish’s view: speech rules always reflect substantive politics","Alexander’s view: no coherent right of expression exists"],"reading":[{"title":"Freedom of Speech","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freedom-speech/"}]},"s5|never":{"summary":"The state may never push you toward what is good for you; over your own life you are in charge, even if you choose badly.","idea":"Anti-paternalism holds that adults are sovereign over their own lives. The state may stop you harming others, but not force or steer you for your own good. Smoking, risky sports and odd life plans are your business. It denies that knowing better than someone gives you the right to overrule them. Defenders find it attractive because each person knows their own values best, because mistakes are part of how people learn and grow, and because a state that protects you from yourself treats you like a child rather than a free adult.","example":"Mill’s unsafe bridge: if an official sees someone about to cross a bridge he knows is unsafe and there is no time to warn him, he may grab him, since no one wants to fall into a river. But once warned, the person may decide to take the risk himself.","history":"John Stuart Mill, On Liberty (1859), argued that over himself, over his own body and mind, the individual is sovereign. Wilhelm von Humboldt, The Limits of State Action (written 1792), made a similar case. Joel Feinberg, Harm to Self (1986), defended ‘soft paternalism’, allowing intervention only to ensure choices are informed and voluntary. Robert Nozick and other libertarians reject paternalism outright.","misread":"Anti-paternalists may still allow stepping in when someone lacks information or is not choosing freely, as in Mill’s bridge.","variants":["Soft anti-paternalism: intervene only to ensure choices are informed and voluntary (Feinberg)","Hard anti-paternalism: never override competent adults’ self-regarding choices"],"reading":[{"title":"Paternalism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/paternalism/"},{"title":"Mill’s Moral and Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mill-moral-political/"}]},"s5|nudge":{"summary":"The state may steer you toward better choices, for example through helpful defaults, so long as you remain free to choose otherwise.","idea":"Libertarian paternalism says choices are always presented in some way, so the presentation might as well help people. Governments can set default options, order information or send reminders that guide people toward choices they would likely endorse, such as saving for retirement, while leaving them free to opt out at low cost. It denies both that the state must stay out entirely and that it must ban or require things. Defenders find it attractive because it respects freedom of choice while using what psychology shows about predictable human mistakes.","example":"Automatic pension enrolment: in the UK, from 2012, employers had to enrol workers in a pension scheme by default, with the right to opt out. Participation rose sharply, though nobody was forced to save.","history":"Richard Thaler and Cass Sunstein coined ‘libertarian paternalism’ in a 2003 article and popularised it in Nudge (2008). The research draws on the behavioural economics of Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky. The UK government set up its Behavioural Insights Team in 2010, and many governments followed. Critics and supporters, including Sunstein, Why Nudge? (2014), have debated its ethics since.","misread":"A nudge is not a mild ban; by definition the person must remain free to choose otherwise at little cost.","variants":["Default setting: making the beneficial option automatic","Boosts: building people’s own decision skills rather than steering them"],"reading":[{"title":"Paternalism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/paternalism/"}]},"s5|coerce":{"summary":"The state may require or ban things by law when people predictably harm themselves, as seatbelt laws do.","idea":"Coercive paternalism holds that when people reliably make choices that damage their own interests, through bias, weakness of will or misinformation, the state may use law to protect them. Seatbelt and helmet laws, prescription requirements and limits on dangerous products are examples. It denies that respecting freedom always means letting people choose badly. Defenders find it attractive because the costs to freedom are often small, the gains in lives and health large, and most people, on reflection, are glad to have such rules in place.","example":"Ulysses and the Sirens: knowing he would be unable to resist the Sirens’ song, Ulysses had his crew tie him to the mast and ignore his pleas. Gerald Dworkin used the story to show how people can rationally want limits on their own future choices.","history":"Gerald Dworkin, ‘Paternalism’ (1972), defended limited paternalism as something rational people would consent to. Plato and Confucian thinkers expected rulers to guide people toward their good. Sarah Conly, Against Autonomy (2013), argued from behavioural research that coercive paternalism is often justified. Public health ethics routinely supports such measures, such as seatbelt laws and tobacco controls.","misread":"It does not mean the state should control all personal choices; defenders target predictable, serious self-harms with small costs to liberty.","variants":["Hypothetical consent paternalism: rules rational people would choose (Dworkin)","Behavioural coercive paternalism: correcting predictable bias by law (Conly)"],"reading":[{"title":"Paternalism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/paternalism/"}]},"s5|nofact":{"summary":"There is no objective truth about whether the state should push people toward their own good.","idea":"This answer applies moral skepticism to paternalism. Whether governments should protect people from themselves is not a question with a correct answer, because there are no objective moral facts. Societies make choices about seatbelts, drugs and sugar taxes according to their preferences, cultures and interests. The view takes no side for or against paternalism. Defenders find it attractive because debates about paternalism keep returning to clashing intuitions about freedom and welfare, and the skeptic sees no deeper fact behind either intuition.","example":"Two countries, two rules: one country requires motorcyclists to wear helmets, while a neighbouring state lets adults ride without one. The skeptic argues that neither is objectively right; each reflects what its people happen to value more, safety or freedom.","history":"Moral skepticism has ancient roots in Greek thinkers like Thrasymachus and the Pyrrhonists. J.L. Mackie, Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977), argued there are no objective values. Non-cognitivists such as A.J. Ayer, Language, Truth and Logic (1936), treated moral claims as expressions of feeling. Applied to paternalism, such views make it a matter of social choice.","misread":"","variants":["Error theory (Mackie)","Emotivism: moral claims express feelings (Ayer)"],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Skepticism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral/"}]},"s13|neutral":{"summary":"The law should stay neutral between views of the good life, resting on reasons people of any faith or none can accept.","idea":"Liberal neutrality holds that in a society of deep and lasting disagreement about religion and the good life, the state should not take sides. Laws should be justified by shared political values such as rights, fairness and freedom, leaving each person to pursue their own idea of a good life. It denies that the state should promote one religion, philosophy or lifestyle as best. Defenders find it attractive because it lets believers and non-believers live together as equals, with no one forced to support a vision of life they reject.","example":"The wars of religion: after more than a century of conflict between Catholics and Protestants in Europe, thinkers like Locke concluded that the state cannot save souls and should tolerate different faiths. Liberal neutrality extends that settlement to all views of the good life.","history":"John Locke, A Letter Concerning Toleration (1689), argued that the magistrate’s power does not extend to saving souls. John Rawls, Political Liberalism (1993), developed public reason and the idea of an overlapping consensus. Ronald Dworkin, Charles Larmore, Bruce Ackerman and Gerald Gaus defended related forms of neutrality and public justification.","misread":"Neutrality is about the reasons for laws, not their effects; a neutral law may still affect some ways of life more than others.","variants":["Neutrality of justification: laws must not rest on a particular view of the good","Political liberalism: an overlapping consensus among reasonable doctrines (Rawls)","Public reason liberalism: laws must be justifiable to all (Gaus)"],"reading":[{"title":"Liberalism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/liberalism/"},{"title":"Perfectionism in Moral and Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/perfectionism-moral/"}]},"s13|perfect":{"summary":"The state may encourage what really makes lives go well, like learning, health and good character, and discourage what wastes them.","idea":"Perfectionism holds that some ways of living are genuinely better than others, and that government may help people flourish by promoting valuable pursuits and discouraging worthless or harmful ones. This might mean funding arts and education, taxing harmful habits or supporting character formation. Liberal perfectionists add that autonomy is itself part of a good life, so the state should usually encourage rather than force. It denies that the state must be neutral about the good. Defenders find it attractive because politics exists to help people live well, so it cannot be silent about what that means.","example":"Public museums and libraries: most states fund museums, libraries and orchestras, partly because art and learning enrich life. Perfectionists say this already takes a stand on the good life, and that this is perfectly proper.","history":"Aristotle, Politics (4th century BCE), held that the city exists for the sake of living well. Confucian thought made moral cultivation central to good government. T.H. Green and the British Idealists defended a perfectionist liberalism. Joseph Raz, The Morality of Freedom (1986), Thomas Hurka, Perfectionism (1993), Steven Wall and Joseph Chan, Confucian Perfectionism (2014), are modern defenders.","misread":"Perfectionism does not mean forcing people to be virtuous; many perfectionists prefer subsidies and education to coercion.","variants":["Liberal perfectionism: promote valuable options, including autonomy (Raz)","Aristotelian perfectionism: promote human flourishing","Confucian perfectionism: promote virtue through moderate state support (Chan)"],"reading":[{"title":"Perfectionism in Moral and Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/perfectionism-moral/"}]},"s13|commun":{"summary":"A community’s traditions, language and values are part of who its people are, and the law may protect them.","idea":"Communitarianism holds that people are not isolated choosers who pick values off a shelf. We are formed by families, communities, languages and traditions, and our identities depend on them. A shared way of life is therefore a real good, and the law may protect and support it, for example by preserving a language, honouring traditions or strengthening local institutions. It denies that the state must be neutral between all ways of life. Defenders find it attractive because it takes belonging seriously and resists the erosion of community by markets and bureaucracies.","example":"Quebec’s language law: in 1977 Quebec passed its Charter of the French Language to protect French in public life, signage and schooling. Charles Taylor argued that preserving a community’s language for future generations can be a legitimate public goal.","history":"G.W.F. Hegel, Philosophy of Right (1820), stressed ethical life embodied in family, society and state. In the 1980s, Michael Sandel, Liberalism and the Limits of Justice (1982), Alasdair MacIntyre, After Virtue (1981), Charles Taylor and Michael Walzer, Spheres of Justice (1983), criticised liberal individualism. Amitai Etzioni later led a ‘responsive communitarian’ movement. Confucian thought likewise emphasises roles and relationships.","misread":"Communitarians are not necessarily against rights; most want rights balanced with responsibilities and shared goods.","variants":["Philosophical communitarianism: critique of the ‘unencumbered self’ (Sandel)","Responsive communitarianism: balancing rights and community (Etzioni)","Politics of recognition: protecting cultural identities (Taylor)"],"reading":[{"title":"Communitarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/communitarianism/"}]},"s13|relig":{"summary":"Law should reflect the moral law given by God, and the state may honour and support religion.","idea":"Religious traditionalism holds that there is a moral order created by God, and human law is just insofar as it reflects it. Many in this tradition add that reason alone can discover much of this natural law, so it binds everyone. The state may therefore honour God, support religious institutions and uphold the moral teaching of the faith. It denies that law must be neutral between belief and unbelief. Defenders find it attractive because it gives law a firm foundation beyond human will, and sees public life as part of a larger sacred order.","example":"King’s Letter from Birmingham Jail (1963): Martin Luther King Jr., citing Augustine and Aquinas, argued that an unjust law is one out of harmony with the moral law of God, and so lacks the authority of true law. This shows how natural law reasoning can judge human laws.","history":"Augustine, City of God (early 5th century), contrasted earthly and heavenly cities. Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae (1265 to 1274), distinguished eternal, natural, human and divine law. Islamic jurisprudence developed sharia as a comprehensive law from the Qur’an and Sunna. Jewish halakha plays a similar role. Leo XIII’s encyclicals revived Thomism, and integralists and natural law theorists like John Finnis carry the tradition today.","misread":"Not all religious traditionalists want a theocracy; many defend natural law accessible to reason alongside religious freedom.","variants":["Natural law theory: moral law discoverable by reason (Aquinas, Finnis)","Catholic integralism: temporal power subordinate to spiritual authority","Established church: an official religion within a free society"],"reading":[{"title":"The Natural Law Tradition in Ethics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/natural-law-ethics/"},{"title":"Aquinas’ Moral, Political, and Legal Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aquinas-moral-political/"},{"title":"Religion and Political Theory","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-politics/"}]},"s12|nation":{"summary":"Governments owe much more to their own citizens, because a nation is a community with special bonds of loyalty, like a family.","idea":"Nationalism holds that nations, communities bound by shared history, language, culture and a sense of common destiny, are morally significant. Members owe each other special loyalty and concern, and a nation’s government should give priority to its own people’s interests and way of life. It denies that we must treat all humans everywhere exactly alike. Defenders find it attractive because shared national identity sustains trust, solidarity and willingness to sacrifice for others, which are what make welfare states and democratic self-government possible.","example":"The family analogy: we think a parent rightly puts their own child first, without thinking other children matter less in some cosmic sense. Nationalists argue that compatriots, too, may rightly come first, and that this partiality is a virtue, not a prejudice.","history":"Johann Gottfried Herder in the 1770s to 1790s praised each people’s distinct culture. Giuseppe Mazzini, The Duties of Man (1860), linked nationhood to freedom. Sun Yat-sen made nationalism one of his Three Principles of the People. Yael Tamir, Liberal Nationalism (1993), David Miller, On Nationality (1995), and Yoram Hazony, The Virtue of Nationalism (2018), are modern defenders.","misread":"Nationalism need not mean hostility to other nations; many nationalists hold that every nation deserves self-determination.","variants":["Liberal nationalism: national belonging within liberal rights (Tamir, Miller)","National conservatism: nations as the bearers of tradition (Hazony)","Civic nationalism: nationhood based on shared citizenship and values"],"reading":[{"title":"Nationalism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nationalism/"},{"title":"Patriotism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/patriotism/"}]},"s12|state":{"summary":"Fellow citizens owe each other special duties because they share laws and a common life, while outsiders are owed basic rights and emergency help.","idea":"Statism holds that duties of distributive justice arise among people who share a state: a coercive legal system they jointly maintain and are subject to. Such people owe each other fair shares. Toward people in other countries we have real but more limited duties: respecting human rights, helping in emergencies, not exploiting them. It denies both that nations are like families and that all humans have identical claims on every government. Defenders find it attractive because it explains special duties without relying on ethnicity or culture.","example":"Nagel’s shared rules: Thomas Nagel argued that citizens are both subject to and authors of their country’s laws, which are imposed on them in their name. This joint involvement, he said, gives rise to demands for fairness that do not extend to outsiders in the same way.","history":"John Rawls, The Law of Peoples (1999), limited his principles of distributive justice to domestic society, with only a ‘duty of assistance’ abroad. Michael Blake, ‘Distributive Justice, State Coercion, and Autonomy’ (2001), grounded special duties in state coercion. Thomas Nagel, ‘The Problem of Global Justice’ (2005), argued justice applies within sovereign states. Andrea Sangiovanni developed a reciprocity version.","misread":"Statists do not deny duties to foreigners; they hold that those duties are humanitarian rather than duties of equal distribution.","variants":["Coercion-based statism: shared coercion grounds special duties (Blake, Nagel)","Reciprocity-based statism: shared cooperation grounds them (Sangiovanni)"],"reading":[{"title":"Global Justice","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justice-global/"},{"title":"Nationalism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nationalism/"}]},"s12|cosmo":{"summary":"Borders do not change what we owe: every human life matters equally, wherever it is lived.","idea":"Cosmopolitanism holds that all human beings belong to a single moral community. A child dying of malaria abroad has the same claim on our concern as a child at home. Governments may organise their work locally for practical reasons, but they cannot treat foreigners’ lives as counting for less. It denies that nationality or citizenship changes the basic weight of a person’s interests. Defenders find it attractive because birthplace is a matter of luck, and it seems arbitrary that it should decide whose suffering counts and how much.","example":"Singer’s drowning child: if you walked past a shallow pond and saw a child drowning, you should wade in even if it ruins your clothes. Peter Singer argued that distance and nationality make no moral difference, so we should also help those dying of poverty far away.","history":"Diogenes the Cynic (4th century BCE) reportedly called himself a citizen of the world, and the Stoics developed the idea. The Chinese thinker Mozi taught impartial care for all. Immanuel Kant, Perpetual Peace (1795), proposed a cosmopolitan right. Peter Singer, ‘Famine, Affluence, and Morality’ (1972), Martha Nussbaum, ‘Patriotism and Cosmopolitanism’ (1994), and Thomas Pogge, World Poverty and Human Rights (2002), are modern landmarks.","misread":"Cosmopolitans need not want a world government; most focus on moral equality, not abolishing states.","variants":["Moral cosmopolitanism: equal moral concern for all humans","Institutional cosmopolitanism: global institutions to secure justice (Pogge)","Rooted cosmopolitanism: local attachments within universal concern (Appiah)"],"reading":[{"title":"Cosmopolitanism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/cosmopolitanism/"},{"title":"Global Justice","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justice-global/"}]},"s1|retrib":{"summary":"Criminals should be punished because they deserve it for what they did, in proportion to the seriousness of the crime.","idea":"Retributivism holds that wrongdoing itself makes punishment fitting. The guilty deserve to suffer a penalty proportionate to their crime, and justice is done when they receive it. Punishment is not mainly a tool for producing good effects. This view denies that we may punish the innocent to deter others, or punish anyone more than they deserve, however useful it might be. Defenders find it attractive because it treats offenders as responsible agents who chose their acts, and gives victims the assurance that the wrong done to them matters.","example":"Kant’s island: Kant imagined a society on an island about to dissolve and scatter. He argued it should still execute the last murderer in its prison first, so that each receives what his deeds deserve, even though punishment would now prevent nothing.","history":"The law of proportion appears in ancient codes like Hammurabi’s and the Hebrew Bible’s ‘eye for an eye’. Immanuel Kant, The Metaphysics of Morals (1797), and G.W.F. Hegel, Philosophy of Right (1820), gave classic philosophical defences. Herbert Morris, ‘Persons and Punishment’ (1968), and Michael Moore, Placing Blame (1997), revived it, and it shaped the ‘just deserts’ sentencing movement of the 1970s.","misread":"Retributivism is not revenge; it demands proportion, impartial judges and punishment only of the guilty.","variants":["Positive retributivism: desert requires punishment","Negative retributivism: desert permits punishment and limits it","Fair play retributivism: punishment removes the unfair advantage of crime (Morris)"],"reading":[{"title":"Retributive Justice","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justice-retributive/"},{"title":"Punishment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/punishment/"}]},"s1|forward":{"summary":"Punishment is justified only by its good effects: protecting others, deterring crime and helping offenders change.","idea":"Forward-looking or consequentialist views say punishment, which deliberately harms people, can only be justified by the good it produces. It may deter would-be offenders, keep dangerous people from harming others, or reform those who offended. If a punishment produces no such good, it is pointless cruelty. This denies that past wrongs alone call for suffering. Defenders find it attractive because it is humane and practical: it asks what will actually reduce harm and favours rehabilitation and prevention over vengeance.","example":"Norway’s Halden prison: opened in 2010, it focuses on education, work and preparing inmates for release. Norway’s low reoffending rate is often cited by those who believe punishment should aim at future safety rather than at matching past wrongs.","history":"Plato’s Protagoras says the reasonable punisher looks to the future, not the past. In China, the Legalist Han Feizi (3rd century BCE) defended punishment as deterrence. Cesare Beccaria, On Crimes and Punishments (1764), and Jeremy Bentham, An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789), developed the modern case. Twentieth century rehabilitation movements carried it into penal policy.","misread":"Forward-looking theorists do not all favour lenience; deterrence can support severe penalties if they work.","variants":["Deterrence: discourage crime by others and the offender","Incapacitation: prevent the dangerous from offending","Rehabilitation: reform offenders so they do not reoffend"],"reading":[{"title":"Punishment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/punishment/"},{"title":"Legal Punishment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/legal-punishment/"}]},"s1|mixed":{"summary":"Punishment aims to reduce crime, but only the guilty may be punished, and no more than they deserve.","idea":"Mixed theories combine forward-looking and backward-looking ideas. The general purpose of having punishment is to prevent crime and protect society. But desert limits how that purpose may be pursued: only those who are guilty may be punished, and never beyond what their crime deserves. It denies both pure retributivism, which ignores results, and pure consequentialism, which might allow punishing the innocent. Defenders find it attractive because it keeps the practical aim of protection while respecting the intuitions that the innocent must never be used and punishment must be proportionate.","example":"Rawls’s ‘telishment’: Rawls imagined an institution allowing officials to punish an innocent person whenever it would help society. He argued no one would accept it, which shows that the practice of punishment can be justified by its benefits while individual cases must still follow guilt.","history":"John Rawls, ‘Two Concepts of Rules’ (1955), distinguished justifying a practice from justifying actions within it. H.L.A. Hart, ‘Prolegomenon to the Principles of Punishment’ (1959), collected in Punishment and Responsibility (1968), argued that the aim is prevention while distribution is limited by guilt. Norval Morris developed ‘limiting retributivism’ in sentencing.","misread":"","variants":["Hart’s mixed theory: general aim prevention, distribution limited by desert","Limiting retributivism: desert sets a range, prevention chooses within it (Morris)"],"reading":[{"title":"Punishment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/punishment/"},{"title":"Legal Punishment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/legal-punishment/"}]},"s1|communic":{"summary":"Punishment is how a community publicly condemns a wrong and calls the offender to account.","idea":"Communicative and expressive theories say punishment is a kind of message. It declares, with authority, that what was done was seriously wrong, affirms the victim’s worth and invites the offender to recognise the wrong and repent. Words alone would not convey the same seriousness. It denies that punishment is mere deterrence or mere payback. Defenders find it attractive because it treats offenders as members of the moral community who can be reasoned with, and gives punishment a meaning that connects to repentance and reconciliation.","example":"Reintegrative shaming: John Braithwaite argued that communities reduce crime best when they firmly condemn the act while respecting and welcoming back the person, rather than branding offenders as outcasts. The message ‘what you did was wrong, but you are still one of us’ is central.","history":"Joel Feinberg, ‘The Expressive Function of Punishment’ (1965), argued punishment expresses resentment and condemnation. Jean Hampton, ‘The Moral Education Theory of Punishment’ (1984), saw it as teaching. John Braithwaite, Crime, Shame and Reintegration (1989), developed reintegrative shaming. R.A. Duff, Punishment, Communication, and Community (2001), gave the leading communicative theory.","misread":"","variants":["Expressive theory: punishment expresses condemnation (Feinberg)","Communicative theory: a two-way moral dialogue seeking repentance (Duff)","Moral education theory: punishment teaches (Hampton)"],"reading":[{"title":"Punishment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/punishment/"},{"title":"Legal Punishment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/legal-punishment/"}]},"s1|abolish":{"summary":"Nothing justifies punishment; we should repair harm instead, bringing victim and offender together to make things right.","idea":"Penal abolitionism holds that deliberately inflicting suffering on offenders is never justified, and that state punishment, especially prison, causes more harm than it prevents. Instead, conflicts should be handled by repairing harm: meetings between victim and offender, restitution, community support and addressing root causes. It denies that wrongdoing calls for pain. Defenders find it attractive because it centres the victim’s needs, treats offenders as people capable of change, and looks for ways to restore relationships rather than adding a second harm to the first.","example":"Family group conferences: in 1989 New Zealand made conferences central to its youth justice system, partly drawing on Māori practice. The young offender, the victim, families and community meet to discuss the harm and agree a plan to repair it, instead of a court imposing a sentence.","history":"Nils Christie, ‘Conflicts as Property’ (1977) and Limits to Pain (1981), argued the state ‘steals’ conflicts from those involved. Louk Hulsman in the Netherlands developed abolitionism in Europe. Howard Zehr, Changing Lenses (1990), shaped restorative justice. Angela Davis, Are Prisons Obsolete? (2003), and Ruth Wilson Gilmore advanced prison abolition in the United States.","misread":"Abolitionists do not want to ignore harm; they want to respond to it through repair rather than punishment.","variants":["Restorative justice: repairing harm through dialogue and restitution","Prison abolition: replacing prisons with community-based responses (Davis)"],"reading":[{"title":"Punishment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/punishment/"},{"title":"Legal Punishment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/legal-punishment/"}]},"s1|nofact":{"summary":"There is no objective truth about whether punishment is justified, because there are no objective moral facts.","idea":"This answer applies moral skepticism to punishment. Societies punish because of anger, fear, habit and interest; claims that punishment is ‘deserved’ or ‘justified’ express these attitudes rather than describe moral facts. The skeptic takes no side between harsh and lenient systems, or between punishment and its abolition. Defenders find it attractive because debates about desert and deterrence rest on intuitions that differ deeply across people and cultures, and because it is hard to see what fact could make someone objectively deserve to suffer.","example":"Nietzsche’s debtor and creditor: in On the Genealogy of Morals (1887), Nietzsche suggested that the idea of guilt grew out of debt, where a creditor could make a debtor suffer as repayment. Punishment, he proposed, has had many purposes over time, with no single true meaning.","history":"Friedrich Nietzsche, On the Genealogy of Morals (1887), offered a debunking history of guilt and punishment. J.L. Mackie, Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977), defended moral error theory, and Mackie also wrote on the paradox of retributivism. Free will skeptics like Derk Pereboom challenge desert from another direction, though usually without denying all moral facts.","misread":"","variants":["Error theory (Mackie)","Genealogical debunking (Nietzsche)"],"reading":[{"title":"Moral Skepticism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral/"},{"title":"Punishment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/punishment/"}]},"s6|piecemeal":{"summary":"Institutions should change step by step, testing each reform and correcting mistakes as we go.","idea":"Piecemeal reform holds that society is too complex for anyone to redesign as a whole. Better to identify the most pressing problems, try specific remedies, watch the results and adjust. Small changes make it possible to learn which policies work and undo those that fail. It denies both that existing institutions should be left alone and that society should be rebuilt according to a grand plan. Defenders find it attractive because it combines a drive for improvement with humility about what we know, much as science advances by trial and error.","example":"Pilot programmes: a government trials a new policy, such as a job training scheme or a special economic zone, in one region first. If it works, it spreads; if not, the damage is limited. China’s reforms from 1978, with experiments like the Shenzhen zone, are often described this way.","history":"Karl Popper, The Open Society and Its Enemies (1945) and The Poverty of Historicism (1957), defended ‘piecemeal social engineering’ against utopian blueprints. Charles Lindblom, ‘The Science of Muddling Through’ (1959), described incremental policy making. Eduard Bernstein’s evolutionary socialism and John Dewey’s experimental view of democracy share the spirit.","misread":"Piecemeal reform is not timid; reforms can accumulate into large changes, as long as each step is tested.","variants":["Popperian social engineering: fight specific evils, not build utopias","Incrementalism: policy made by successive limited comparisons (Lindblom)"],"reading":[{"title":"Karl Popper","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/popper/"}]},"s6|radical":{"summary":"The present system is unjust at its roots, so it must be transformed fundamentally rather than patched.","idea":"Radicalism holds that some injustices are built into the basic structure of society, its economy, its power relations or its laws, so small reforms cannot fix them and may even help them last longer by making them more bearable. Real justice needs fundamental transformation, peaceful or otherwise. It denies that gradual change is always wiser. Defenders find it attractive because history shows deep injustices, such as slavery, colonial rule and absolute monarchy, were ended by decisive breaks, not by tinkering.","example":"Abolitionism versus gradualism: in 1831 William Lloyd Garrison launched The Liberator demanding the immediate end of American slavery, rejecting plans for gradual emancipation. Radicals point to such cases where the evil itself, not its excesses, was the problem.","history":"The American (1776) and French (1789) revolutions broke with old orders, and Thomas Paine defended both. Karl Marx, ‘Theses on Feuerbach’ (1845) and The Communist Manifesto (1848), called for revolutionary change. Frantz Fanon, The Wretched of the Earth (1961), wrote on decolonisation. The Frankfurt School’s critical theory aimed at emancipation from domination.","misread":"Radical does not necessarily mean violent; it means going to the root, which can be pursued by peaceful mass movements.","variants":["Revolutionary socialism: overturning capitalism (Marx)","Anticolonial radicalism: breaking colonial rule (Fanon)","Critical theory: exposing and ending hidden domination (Frankfurt School)"],"reading":[{"title":"Karl Marx","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marx/"},{"title":"Critical Theory (Frankfurt School)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/critical-theory/"}]},"s6|conserv":{"summary":"Institutions should change carefully, building on what exists, because long-standing practices carry wisdom no one designed.","idea":"Conservatism holds that inherited institutions, customs and traditions embody the accumulated experience of many generations, including lessons no one can fully state. Societies are easier to break than to rebuild, so change should be cautious, building on what works rather than replacing it with theory. It does not reject all change: Burke said a state without the means of change lacks the means of its own preservation. Defenders find it attractive because it respects what people love and depend on, and guards against confident plans with unforeseen costs.","example":"The English common law: rather than being written by one lawgiver, it grew case by case over centuries as judges settled real disputes. Conservatives see it as a model of how institutions can hold more practical wisdom than any single designer could supply.","history":"Edmund Burke, Reflections on the Revolution in France (1790), is the founding text. David Hume stressed custom and convention. Michael Oakeshott, ‘On Being Conservative’ (1956) and Rationalism in Politics (1962), defended a disposition to prefer the familiar. Roger Scruton, The Meaning of Conservatism (1980), and others extended the tradition. Confucian thought shares a deep respect for inherited ritual.","misread":"Conservatism is not opposition to all change; it favours change that grows from and repairs existing institutions.","variants":["Burkean conservatism: reform to preserve","Oakeshottian conservatism: a disposition to enjoy the present and familiar","Fusionism: traditional values joined with market economics"],"reading":[{"title":"Conservatism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/conservatism/"}]},"s6|restore":{"summary":"We should recover good ways of life that were lost, because much modern change was a mistake.","idea":"Restorationism holds that the past contained forms of life, institutions or wisdom that were better than what replaced them, and that the right direction is backward, toward recovering them. Unlike conservatism, it does not mainly defend the present order: it may criticise the present sharply. It denies that history is a story of steady progress. Defenders find it attractive because they see what modernity lost, such as virtue, community, ritual or faith, and believe those goods can be renewed, as earlier generations renewed their own heritage.","example":"MacIntyre’s imagined catastrophe: Alasdair MacIntyre asked readers to imagine a world where science was destroyed and people later used scientific words without understanding them. He argued our moral language is in a similar state, fragments of a lost tradition that must be recovered.","history":"Confucius (551 to 479 BCE) sought to restore the rites of the early Zhou. Renaissance humanists aimed to revive classical learning, and Protestant reformers to return to the sources of faith. Islamic revival movements have sought a return to the practice of the early generations. Alasdair MacIntyre, After Virtue (1981), urged recovering the Aristotelian tradition of the virtues.","misread":"Restorationists rarely want to copy the past exactly; most seek to renew lost principles in present conditions.","variants":["Confucian restoration: renewing ancient rites and virtues","Aristotelian or Thomist revival: recovering the virtue tradition (MacIntyre)","Religious revival: return to the faith’s founding sources"],"reading":[{"title":"Confucius","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confucius/"},{"title":"Conservatism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/conservatism/"}]}},"patterns":{"aesthetics:Kantian about taste and art":{"summary":"Judgments of beauty rest on feeling yet rightly claim everyone’s agreement, and great art is great because of the rich, absorbing experience it gives to anyone who attends to it freely.","reading":[{"title":"Kant’s Aesthetics and Teleology","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Judgment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Experience","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-experience/"}],"idea":"This view joins a theory of taste to a theory of artistic value. On taste: when you call something beautiful, you are not applying a rule or measuring a property; you are reporting a pleasure. Yet it is a special pleasure, free of desire or personal interest, so you expect every attentive person to share it. On art: a work is great because of the experience it offers, a lively play of perception, imagination and thought that never settles into a single concept. The two parts fit, because the same free, disinterested experience both grounds the claim to agreement and makes art worth having. Picture leaving a concert and arguing with a friend about it. You cannot prove your verdict, but you also do not shrug “each to their own”. You point, describe and hope they will hear it too.","example":"Faced with a puzzling abstract painting, this person would not ask for a checklist of features. They would look slowly, set aside whether the painting is valuable or fashionable, and ask whether attending to it keeps their imagination and understanding in free, rewarding play. If it does, they say it is good, and they mean that you ought to find it so as well.","history":"Immanuel Kant set out both halves in the Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790): the judgment of taste is disinterested, claims “subjective universality”, and rests on the free play of imagination and understanding; fine art is the art of genius, which gives us “aesthetic ideas” that no concept can exhaust. Twentieth century writers on aesthetic experience, such as Monroe Beardsley, kept the experiential side while dropping much of Kant’s system.","challenge":"Why should a feeling, however disinterested, entitle anyone to demand agreement? Kant appeals to a shared “common sense” among human minds, but critics doubt that this exists. Real disagreement among careful, unbiased people is common, and it is hard to tell when someone has judged with genuine disinterest."},"aesthetics:Moralist about art":{"summary":"Art matters because it shapes character and community, so a work that corrupts its audience is bad art, not just bad behaviour that happens to look good.","reading":[{"title":"Ethical Criticism of Art","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ethical-criticism-of-art/"},{"title":"Plato’s Aesthetics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/plato-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Xunzi","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/xunzi/"}],"idea":"Moralists hold that art’s real job is to make people better. Music, stories and images train our feelings: they teach us what to admire, what to fear and what to laugh at. Because art works on us below the level of argument, it is powerful, and power can be used well or badly. So the moralist refuses to separate artistic quality from moral effect. A film that makes cruelty glamorous has failed at the very thing films are for, however skilful the camera work. The view is attractive because it takes art seriously. It treats songs and stories not as harmless decoration but as forces in a community’s life. Think of national anthems, hymns or protest songs: their value plainly lies partly in what they do to the people who sing them together.","example":"Plato’s Republic proposes that the guardians of an ideal city should hear only stories that show gods and heroes as good, and only musical modes that build courage and self-control. A modern moralist might reason the same way about a hit song that celebrates humiliating others: its catchiness makes it worse, not better, because it spreads the attitude more widely.","history":"Plato argued in the Republic (Books 2, 3 and 10) that poetry must be judged by its effect on the soul. Confucius, in the Analects, prized the ancient Shao music for its goodness and condemned the music of Zheng as licentious. Xunzi’s “Discourse on Music”, a reply to Mozi’s attack on music, defended ritual music as a tool for shaping character and harmonising society. Tolstoy revived a version in What Is Art? (1897).","challenge":"Much art that most people treasure, from Greek tragedy to dark modern novels, shows wrongdoing vividly and without clear condemnation. The moralist seems forced to rank such works low, and to hand someone the power to decide which art corrupts. Critics fear this leads to censorship and to bland, preachy art."},"aesthetics:Tolstoyan":{"summary":"Art is the sincere passing on of felt emotion from maker to audience, and the best art spreads good feelings that unite people; art that spreads bad feelings is bad art.","reading":[{"title":"Art and Emotion","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/art-and-emotion/"},{"title":"Ethical Criticism of Art","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ethical-criticism-of-art/"},{"title":"The Definition of Art","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-definition/"}],"idea":"For the Tolstoyan, art is a kind of contagion. An artist feels something, then shapes words, sounds or images so that others catch the same feeling. A work succeeds as art when the feeling comes through clearly and sincerely, and fails when it is faked, muddled or only clever. But Tolstoyans add a moral test on top. Since art spreads feelings through a whole society, the feelings it spreads matter. Art that passes on pride, lust or contempt does damage; art that passes on compassion and a sense of shared humanity does good. The view is attractive because it explains why art can move anyone, not only experts, and why we feel betrayed by art that manipulates. Compare a simple folk song that makes a whole room feel one sadness with an expensive advertisement that fakes emotion to sell a car.","example":"Tolstoy applied his test without mercy. In What Is Art? he dismissed much celebrated high art as insincere or understandable only by a small elite, criticised Beethoven’s Ninth Symphony as failing to unite all people, and classed nearly all of his own fiction as bad art, sparing only two short stories, “God Sees the Truth, but Waits” and “A Prisoner of the Caucasus”.","history":"Leo Tolstoy published What Is Art? in 1897 and 1898, after the religious conversion described in A Confession. He drew on an older idea that art expresses emotion, found in Romantic poetics, and joined it to his own Christian ethic of universal brotherhood. Later expression theorists, notably R. G. Collingwood, kept his focus on feeling but rejected his moral test and his idea of contagion.","challenge":"Sincerity and infectiousness seem neither necessary nor sufficient for great art. A sincere but clumsy poem may move its author’s friends, while a coolly crafted novel may endure for centuries. And judging feelings as good or bad threatens to put art under the control of one moral or religious outlook."},"aesthetics:Expression theorist":{"summary":"Something is art because it expresses its maker’s feeling, and it is great when it expresses that feeling truly and clearly; art is emotion given form.","reading":[{"title":"Collingwood’s Aesthetics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/collingwood-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Art and Emotion","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/art-and-emotion/"},{"title":"The Definition of Art","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-definition/"}],"idea":"Expression theorists answer two questions with one idea. What makes something art? It is the working out and showing of a feeling. What makes art great? Doing that truly and clearly. Expression here is not just venting. When a composer begins, they may feel only a vague unease; writing the piece is how they discover what the feeling is. Collingwood called this clarifying an emotion. So expression is a kind of discovery, and good art helps the audience recognise feelings they had not named. The view is attractive because it explains the intimacy of art: we sense another mind in a song or poem. It also separates art from craft and entertainment. A jingle built to make you buy, or a thriller built only to excite, aims at an effect planned in advance, while real art finds its feeling in the making.","example":"Imagine someone writing a poem after a friend moves away. The first draft says only “I am sad”. Later drafts find something more exact: relief mixed with guilt, and an odd loneliness at being free. For the expression theorist the poem becomes art as it gets this right, and it is great to the extent that readers recognise the exact feeling in themselves.","history":"Wordsworth’s Preface to Lyrical Ballads (1800) linked poetry to powerful feeling. Benedetto Croce’s Aesthetic (1902) identified art with expression and intuition. R. G. Collingwood’s The Principles of Art (1938) gave the classic philosophical version, distinguishing art proper from craft, amusement and magic. Tolstoy’s What Is Art? (1897) is another, but he stressed infecting the audience, which Collingwood thought less central.","challenge":"Many artworks do not seem to express anything their makers felt: a commissioned altarpiece, a cool abstract grid, a comic opera written in grief. Critics also ask how we could ever check what the artist felt. The theory may confuse expressive qualities in the work, like a sad melody, with the maker’s inner life."},"aesthetics:Aesthete":{"summary":"Only form makes art great: how its colours, sounds or words fit together. Morality is a separate matter, so a morally ugly work can still be artistically great.","reading":[{"title":"Aesthetic Formalism","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetic-formalism/"},{"title":"Ethical Criticism of Art","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ethical-criticism-of-art/"},{"title":"The Value of Art","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/value-of-art/"}],"idea":"The aesthete treats art as a world with its own laws. A painting is lines and colours, a symphony is sounds unfolding in time, a poem is words in patterns. What a work depicts, teaches or recommends adds nothing to its greatness as art. This leads straight to the second claim: since moral content is not part of artistic value, a moral flaw cannot lower it. A novel may glorify something terrible and still be a masterpiece of style. The view is attractive because it protects art from being judged by politics, piety or usefulness, and because it matches a familiar experience. Many listeners love instrumental music that has no message at all, and many readers admire the prose of writers whose opinions they reject. On this view, to ask whether art is good for us is to ask the wrong question.","example":"Clive Bell said that to appreciate a work of art we need bring nothing from life: no knowledge of its ideas or affairs, only a sense of form and colour. An aesthete looking at a Renaissance crucifixion would care about the balance of masses and the rhythm of the folds, not about the story or the doctrine it was painted to serve.","history":"Théophile Gautier’s preface to Mademoiselle de Maupin (1835) is an early manifesto of “art for art’s sake”. Eduard Hanslick’s On the Musically Beautiful (1854) argued that music’s content is “tonally moving forms”. Oscar Wilde’s preface to The Picture of Dorian Gray (1891) denied that books are moral or immoral. Clive Bell’s Art (1914) and Roger Fry defended formalism about painting.","challenge":"Most art is about something, and the meaning seems to matter to its value: a novel’s insight into grief, a portrait’s grasp of character. Critics also argue that we cannot always set moral reactions aside, because a work that asks us to admire cruelty may simply fail to win the response it seeks."},"aesthetics:Immoralist about art":{"summary":"Art’s value lies in insight, or in many kinds of value, and a moral flaw can sometimes make a work better, by letting us understand from inside what decent art cannot show.","reading":[{"title":"Ethical Criticism of Art","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ethical-criticism-of-art/"},{"title":"The Value of Art","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/value-of-art/"}],"idea":"Immoralists accept that art can be valuable as a way of understanding the world. That is why they think moral flaws can sometimes help. A novel that makes you sympathise with a murderer, or a series that makes you root for a mob boss, puts you in a position you would never take in life. Feeling the pull of that position shows you how people come to do terrible things, and how easily you might too. A morally spotless version could not teach this, because it would never get under your guard. So the flaw, the invitation to take up a wrong attitude, is part of what makes the work achieve its insight. The view does not say all immoral art is good, or that morality never matters. It says the link between moral and artistic value is not one way.","example":"Vladimir Nabokov’s Lolita is narrated by Humbert Humbert, a man who abuses a child, in prose so charming that readers catch themselves enjoying his voice. An immoralist argues that this is the novel’s achievement: by seducing us, it shows how self-justifying eloquence works, and how much moral attention it takes to see through it.","history":"Daniel Jacobson’s “In Praise of Immoral Art” (1997) and Matthew Kieran’s “Forbidden Knowledge: The Challenge of Immoralism” (2003) launched the modern debate, arguing against ethicists like Berys Gaut. A. W. Eaton’s “Robust Immoralism” (2012) discussed “rough heroes” such as Tony Soprano, whose appeal is an artistic achievement precisely because it is morally troubling. Earlier, readers argued similarly about Milton’s Satan.","challenge":"Critics say the value here comes from understanding, which is morally good, not from the flaw itself. If a work truly teaches us about evil, it is not really inviting a wrong attitude overall. Immoralists must show that the moral defect, not the insight it serves, is what raises the work."},"aesthetics:Moral critic":{"summary":"Great art reveals truths about life, so a work that invites a distorted or callous view of people partly fails as art, though other strengths can still outweigh the flaw.","reading":[{"title":"Ethical Criticism of Art","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ethical-criticism-of-art/"},{"title":"The Value of Art","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/value-of-art/"}],"idea":"The moral critic begins with the idea that art is a way of seeing the world truly. Novels, films and paintings can show us other people more clearly than we manage in daily life. If that is what art is for, moral flaws matter artistically. A work that asks us to see a group of people as less than human is not just offensive; it is getting the world wrong, and so failing at art’s task of truthful seeing. Moral critics differ on how far this goes. Moderates say a flaw counts only when it spoils the work on its own terms. Ethicists say every flaw in the attitude a work invites counts against it, though skill may outweigh it. The view is attractive because it explains why we feel a great novel has taught us something about people.","example":"A comedy builds a whole scene around mocking a disabled character. A moral critic would say the scene is worse as comedy, not only worse morally, because it asks for a laugh that attentive viewers cannot give. A work like George Eliot’s Middlemarch, by contrast, earns its greatness partly by seeing each character with patient, unselfish attention.","history":"Iris Murdoch, in The Sovereignty of Good (1970), argued that great art trains us to see reality justly and without selfish fantasy. Martha Nussbaum’s Love’s Knowledge (1990) treated novels, especially Henry James’s, as moral philosophy. Noël Carroll defended “moderate moralism” (1996), and Berys Gaut defended “ethicism” in Art, Emotion and Ethics (2007). Earlier, David Hume made a similar point in “Of the Standard of Taste” (1757).","challenge":"Aesthetes reply that this confuses two kinds of value: a work can be beautifully made and morally wrong. Immoralists reply that some moral flaws deepen insight. And it is often unclear whether a work endorses the attitude it shows or examines it, which makes moral verdicts on art risky."},"aesthetics:Art for life’s sake":{"summary":"Art is great for what it does for people, shaping character and binding communities, and moral flaws count against a work, though skill, form and invention can sometimes outweigh them.","reading":[{"title":"Dewey’s Aesthetics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dewey-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Ethical Criticism of Art","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ethical-criticism-of-art/"},{"title":"Confucius","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confucius/"}],"idea":"This view takes a middle road. Like the strict moralist, it judges art by its contribution to human life: art makes people better, holds communities together and moves them to act well. But it stops short of saying that any corrupting element makes art bad. A moral flaw is a real cost, sometimes or always, yet it can be outweighed by brilliance of form or invention. The result is a practical, balanced outlook. Art is not a separate world; it belongs to everyday life alongside work, worship and politics. The view is attractive to people who want art to matter socially without turning critics into censors. Think of a community choir or a mural painted by neighbours: its value lies largely in what it does for the people who make and see it, but that does not make technical quality irrelevant.","example":"Asked about a brilliant but cruel satire, this person would weigh both sides. The cruelty is a real defect, since it teaches contempt. But if the satire also exposes hypocrisy and is written with dazzling skill, the gains may outweigh the cost. The verdict comes from balancing, not from a single rule.","history":"John Dewey’s Art as Experience (1934) argued that art is continuous with everyday life and community. Confucian thought, from the Analects to Xunzi, valued music and ritual for shaping character. The Neo-Confucian Zhou Dunyi (11th century) wrote that writing should “convey the Way”. In the 19th century John Ruskin and William Morris tied art to the health of society. Noël Carroll and Berys Gaut defend modern ethical criticism.","challenge":"Balancing sounds sensible, but without a clear rule for weighing moral against artistic value, verdicts may simply track the critic’s politics. Aesthetes also object that judging art by its social effects confuses art with education or propaganda, while strict moralists say any real corruption should not be excused by skill."},"aesthetics:Artworld contextualist":{"summary":"Objects become art, and mean what they mean, through the history and theory of art; who made a work, when and why are part of what the work is.","reading":[{"title":"The Definition of Art","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-definition/"},{"title":"Conceptual Art","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/conceptual-art/"},{"title":"History of the Ontology of Art","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-ontology-history/"}],"idea":"The contextualist denies that an artwork is just what meets the eye. Two objects can look exactly alike and still be different works, or one art and the other not. What makes the difference is context: the history of art, the theories in the air, the problems artists were working on, and the identity of the maker. Andy Warhol’s Brillo Boxes look like supermarket cartons, but they are about something: they ask what art is. The cartons are about nothing. Likewise a perfect copy of a Vermeer painted in 1937 is not a Vermeer, because the same brushstrokes do different things at different moments. The view is attractive because it explains modern and conceptual art and the deep feeling that a forgery, however skilful, has lost something real. Compare a joke that is witty when first told and only borrowed when repeated.","example":"In Jorge Luis Borges’s story “Pierre Menard, Author of the Quixote” (1939), a 20th century writer produces pages word for word identical to Cervantes. The narrator insists Menard’s text is richer, because the same words mean something different coming from a modern Frenchman. Contextualists treat the story as a serious thought experiment: identical texts, different works.","history":"Arthur Danto’s essay “The Artworld” (1964), prompted by Warhol’s Brillo Boxes, argued that seeing something as art needs “an atmosphere of artistic theory”; his The Transfiguration of the Commonplace (1981) developed the idea of embodied meaning. Kendall Walton’s “Categories of Art” (1970) showed that what features a work has depends on its category. George Dickie’s institutional theory (1974) is a related view.","challenge":"If meaning depends on art history and theory, art seems to become a private club for experts, and ordinary viewers who simply look are shut out. Critics also ask why the history of an object, which we cannot see, should change its value, if two paintings give exactly the same experience."},"aesthetics:Contemplative aesthete":{"summary":"Great art is great for the experience it gives, a special state in which self and time fall away, offering rest from the ordinary restless striving of desire.","reading":[{"title":"Schopenhauer’s Aesthetics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/schopenhauer-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Experience","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-experience/"},{"title":"The Aesthetic Attitude","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetic-attitude/"}],"idea":"This view centres on a familiar experience: being so absorbed in music or a painting that you forget yourself and lose track of time. The contemplative aesthete thinks this state is not just pleasant but distinctive, unlike ordinary seeing or enjoying, and that it is the measure of art’s worth. The richer and more absorbing the experience, the greater the work. In daily life we look at things in terms of what we want: is it useful, threatening, mine? In aesthetic contemplation that stops, and we see the thing as it is. Schopenhauer thought this explains why art feels like relief. Wanting is a kind of suffering, and art briefly frees us from it. The view is attractive because it describes something many people recognise, from a sunset that stops you mid-sentence to a concert that leaves you blinking when the lights come up.","example":"Schopenhauer held that music is the highest art because it does not copy objects in the world but expresses the striving will itself. A listener lost in a slow movement, aware of nothing but the sound, is for a while free from their own wanting. That release, on this view, is the deepest thing art offers.","history":"Arthur Schopenhauer’s The World as Will and Representation (1818), Book 3, presented aesthetic contemplation as will-less, timeless perception of the Ideas. He drew on Kant’s idea of disinterested pleasure. In India, the theorist Abhinavagupta (around 1000 CE) described aesthetic savouring, rasa, as an impersonal state freed from ordinary personal concerns. Twentieth century writers on the “aesthetic attitude”, such as Edward Bullough, continued the theme.","challenge":"Is there really one special aesthetic state? George Dickie argued that the “aesthetic attitude” is just paying close attention. And many valuable works, such as political satire or challenging conceptual art, seem to aim at engagement, thought or discomfort, not self-forgetful rest."},"aesthetics:Goodmanian about forgery":{"summary":"Learning that a work is a forgery is a reason to look harder, and differences we missed start to show, because seeing is learned and art, whether through form or insight, is a kind of understanding.","reading":[{"title":"Goodman’s Aesthetics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/goodman-aesthetics/"},{"title":"History of the Ontology of Art","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-ontology-history/"}],"idea":"This view treats art as a way of knowing and perception as a skill. When experts were fooled by a forged Vermeer, it was not because the fake and the real were identical to the eye, but because they had not yet learned to see the differences. Once you know a painting is a fake, that knowledge changes how you train your eye, and in time the forgery’s flaws become visible. So knowing who made a work is not snobbery; it guides future perception. This fits the view that art’s value lies in its form or insight, both of which reward a trained eye. Art, on this picture, is a symbol system, like language or maps, and appreciating it is understanding. Think of wine tasters or bird watchers: at first two things seem the same, and with practice the difference becomes obvious.","example":"Han van Meegeren’s “Vermeers” fooled leading experts in the 1930s and 1940s. After his exposure in 1945, viewers began to notice what now seems obvious: the faces look like film stars of the 1930s. Goodman’s point is that the fake looked convincing only against what experts then knew, and learning it was fake taught them to see better.","history":"Nelson Goodman’s Languages of Art (1968), in the chapter “Art and Authenticity”, argued that knowing a picture is forged makes an aesthetic difference now, because it shapes how we learn to look. He also distinguished “autographic” arts like painting, which can be forged, from “allographic” arts like music, where any correct performance counts. Goodman treated aesthetics as part of the theory of knowledge.","challenge":"Suppose a forgery were truly indistinguishable, even to the most trained eye forever. Goodman’s argument would then give no reason to value the original more, yet many people still would. Contextualists say this shows that origin matters for its own sake, not just as a guide to perception."},"aesthetics:New Critic":{"summary":"A work’s meaning lies in its words and how language works, not in the author’s private intention, and works are judged by close attention to their form or insight.","reading":[{"title":"Art and Interpretation","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/art-and-interpretation/"},{"title":"Beardsley’s Aesthetics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beardsley-aesthetics/"}],"idea":"New Critics treat a poem or novel as a public object made of language. Once published, it belongs to readers, and its meaning depends on what the words mean in the language, not on what the author privately hoped. Diaries, letters and interviews may suggest interpretations, but they cannot settle what the text says. The critic’s job is close reading: attending to images, tensions, ironies and structure, and showing how they fit into a unified whole. This fits valuing works for their form or insight, both of which are found in the text itself. The view is attractive because it gives readers authority and a method, and keeps criticism from turning into gossip about authors’ lives. If you text a friend something ambiguous, you cannot later claim it clearly meant what you intended; the words said what they said.","example":"A poet writes about a closed door and an empty chair. Every reader takes it to be about lost love; her diary later shows she meant her grandmother. A New Critic says the poem is still about whatever its words and images support. If the text makes a lost love reading strong and coherent, the diary does not override it.","history":"W. K. Wimsatt and Monroe Beardsley’s “The Intentional Fallacy” (1946) argued that the author’s design is neither available nor desirable as a standard; their “The Affective Fallacy” (1949) rejected judging poems by readers’ reactions. I. A. Richards’s Practical Criticism (1929), T. S. Eliot’s essays and Cleanth Brooks’s The Well Wrought Urn (1947) shaped the movement, which dominated English teaching in the mid 20th century.","challenge":"Language alone often underdetermines meaning: irony, allusion and even word senses change over time and depend on who is speaking. Intentionalists such as E. D. Hirsch argue that without the author’s intention there is no fact about which reading is correct, only a contest of interpretations."},"aesthetics:Postmodern reader":{"summary":"Readers make meaning, so no single reading of a work is the correct one, and art cannot be defined, since artists keep remaking what art can be.","reading":[{"title":"Art and Interpretation","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/art-and-interpretation/"},{"title":"The Definition of Art","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-definition/"}],"idea":"This view rejects fixed essences twice. First, meaning: a text does not contain one meaning waiting to be found, whether the author’s or the words’. Readers bring their own histories, questions and communities, and each careful reading makes a meaning that is genuinely there. Second, art: no list of features fits everything artists make, because the point of much modern art is to break the rules of the last generation. Each new case, like a row of bricks on a gallery floor, needs a fresh decision. The two claims support each other, since both describe culture as open and always being remade. The view is attractive because it welcomes new readers and new art, and explains why classic works keep speaking to new generations. A song can mean one thing at a wedding and another at a funeral, and neither meaning is a mistake.","example":"When the Tate Gallery’s purchase of Carl Andre’s Equivalent VIII, a rectangle of 120 firebricks, became a newspaper scandal in 1976, the postmodern reader would say there is no fixed definition to settle whether it is art. The real question is what viewers, critics and institutions make of it, and that is an ongoing argument, not a fact to look up.","history":"Roland Barthes’s “The Death of the Author” (1967) argued that the birth of the reader comes at the cost of the author. Michel Foucault’s “What Is an Author?” (1969) and Stanley Fish’s Is There a Text in This Class? (1980), with its “interpretive communities”, extended the theme. Morris Weitz’s “The Role of Theory in Aesthetics” (1956) argued that “art” is an open concept that cannot be defined.","challenge":"If readers make meaning, it seems no reading could be wrong, yet we plainly can misread a contract, a joke or a poem. Critics also note that open concepts still need some criteria, or anything could be art. Defenders reply that “not fixed” does not mean “anything goes”."},"aesthetics:Defender of machine creativity":{"summary":"Art is defined by what a thing is and does, its beauty, form or cluster of typical features, not by who made it, so an AI that makes genuine creative choices can produce art.","reading":[{"title":"Creativity","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/creativity/"},{"title":"The Philosophy of Digital Art","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/digital-art/"},{"title":"The Definition of Art","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-definition/"}],"idea":"This view starts from a definition of art that says nothing about human makers. On one version, art is something made to be appreciated for its beauty or form. On another, art is a cluster concept: a thing with enough typical features, such as beauty, expression, skill and meaning, counts. Either way, the test is the object and its making, not the biology of the maker. So if an AI system makes choices no human made, selecting colours, compositions and details that the prompt did not specify, it is doing something creative, and the result can be art. The view is attractive because it avoids drawing a line based only on what kind of thing the maker is. We already accept that a photograph is art though a machine captured it, because a human chose; the defender asks why a machine’s own choices should count for nothing.","example":"Asked about a striking AI image made from a one-line prompt, this person would note that the prompt fixed almost nothing: the lighting, composition and mood came from the system. When Jason Allen’s AI-generated image won a prize at the Colorado State Fair in 2022, critics called it cheating. The defender would ask whether the image has the features that make things art, not who pressed the button.","history":"Margaret Boden’s The Creative Mind (1990) distinguished kinds of creativity and argued that computers could, in principle, be creative. Harold Cohen’s drawing program AARON, developed from the 1970s, raised the question early. Monroe Beardsley defended an aesthetic definition of art, and Berys Gaut’s “Art as a Cluster Concept” (2000) defended the cluster account, though neither was writing about AI.","challenge":"Critics argue that art requires intention, experience and something to express, and that current AI systems have none of these, only patterns learned from human work. On this objection the “choices” are statistical outputs, and calling them creative may be a metaphor. Who, if anyone, is the author also remains unclear."},"aesthetics:Critic of the culture industry":{"summary":"Art is valuable for challenging form or genuine insight, and music mass produced to sell to millions becomes standardised and soothing, working against exactly those values.","reading":[{"title":"Theodor W. Adorno","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/adorno/"},{"title":"The Aesthetics of Popular Music","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetics-of-popular-music/"}],"idea":"This view links a theory of art’s value to a critique of how popular culture is made. If great art challenges us through demanding form or shows us something true, then a system that produces songs and films like factory goods is a threat. To sell to millions, products must be predictable, easy and pleasant. Songs follow formulas; small surface differences make each seem fresh while the structure stays the same. The listener is soothed and distracted, not changed. On this view the issue is not that ordinary people have bad taste, but that the industry shapes taste to suit sales. The view is attractive because it explains a common feeling that much popular music sounds alike, and asks why. Think of how many chart songs share the same four chords and the same verse and chorus structure.","example":"Adorno argued that popular songs are built from interchangeable parts, so that a listener could swap a chorus from one hit into another without loss. He called the illusion of freshness “pseudo-individualisation”. A critic in this tradition might contrast a playlist designed by an algorithm to keep you listening with a difficult symphony that demands and rewards concentration.","history":"Theodor Adorno and Max Horkheimer coined “the culture industry” in Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944, revised 1947). Adorno’s “On Popular Music” (1941, with George Simpson) developed the ideas of standardisation and pseudo-individualisation; his Aesthetic Theory (1970) defended difficult modernist art. Clement Greenberg’s “Avant-Garde and Kitsch” (1939) and Dwight Macdonald’s “Masscult and Midcult” (1960) made related arguments.","challenge":"Critics say Adorno underestimated popular music, dismissing jazz with little understanding and ignoring how much variety and innovation commercial music contains. Listeners also use popular culture actively, not passively. And the view can look like elitism, treating the tastes of a highly educated minority as the standard of value."},"aesthetics:Sociologist of taste":{"summary":"No universal standard ranks tastes, and the ranking of high art above popular art reflects class and social power rather than real differences in quality.","reading":[{"title":"Aesthetic Taste","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetic-taste/"},{"title":"The Aesthetics of Popular Music","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetics-of-popular-music/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Judgment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/"}],"idea":"The sociologist of taste looks at who likes what and finds patterns. Preferences for opera, abstract painting or experimental fiction track education and family background; preferences for pop, romance novels or action films track other backgrounds. The ranking of the first group as “higher” is not a discovery about quality. It is a way for powerful groups to mark themselves as cultured and to turn their upbringing into an advantage. This fits the view that taste has no universal standard: either each tradition has its own standards, or no taste is better than another. Either way, the claim that a symphony is simply better than a great pop song is a social move dressed as an aesthetic one. The view is attractive because it is backed by evidence about class and culture, and because it is suspicious of snobbery. Notice how school syllabuses often decide what counts as serious art.","example":"Lawrence Levine’s Highbrow/Lowbrow (1988) showed that Shakespeare was popular entertainment for mixed audiences in 19th century America, performed alongside farces and acrobats, before he was moved into a separate sphere of “high” culture. A sociologist of taste takes this as evidence that the line between high and low is drawn by social history, not by the works themselves.","history":"Pierre Bourdieu’s Distinction (1979), based on surveys of French society, argued that taste expresses and reproduces class position through “cultural capital”. Herbert Gans’s Popular Culture and High Culture (1974) described parallel “taste cultures”, each with its own standards. Earlier, Thorstein Veblen’s The Theory of the Leisure Class (1899) linked refined consumption to status display.","challenge":"Showing that tastes correlate with class does not show that all tastes are equally good; the causes of a belief do not settle whether it is true. Critics also note that the view seems to undercut itself, since sociologists still judge some works richer than others, and that people from every background recognise great works."},"aesthetics:Anti-essentialist about art":{"summary":"Art has no definition, only overlapping family resemblances, and different kinds of art have their own standards, so a great pop song and a great symphony cannot be ranked against each other.","reading":[{"title":"Wittgenstein’s Aesthetics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wittgenstein-aesthetics/"},{"title":"The Definition of Art","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/art-definition/"},{"title":"The Definition of Art (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/definition-of-art/"}],"idea":"Anti-essentialists think the search for the essence of art is a mistake. Paintings, novels, dances, buildings and video games share no single feature that all and only artworks have. Instead they are linked like members of a family: some share a nose, others a way of walking, with no trait common to all. This fits naturally with the second claim. If art is a family of different practices, each has its own standards of excellence, and greatness is always greatness of a kind. Asking whether the best pop song beats the best symphony is like asking whether the best sprinter beats the best chess player. The view is attractive because it fits how varied art actually is, and it avoids both snobbery and the claim that anything goes. Each practice has real standards; they simply do not line up on one scale.","example":"Wittgenstein asked his readers to look at all the things we call “games”: board games, card games, ball games, a child throwing a ball at a wall. Do not say they must have something in common, he urged; look, and you will see a network of overlapping similarities. The anti-essentialist does the same with art, from cave paintings to hip hop.","history":"Ludwig Wittgenstein introduced family resemblance in Philosophical Investigations (1953). Morris Weitz’s “The Role of Theory in Aesthetics” (1956) applied it to art, calling “art” an open concept. Paul Ziff and William Kennick made similar arguments. Maurice Mandelbaum’s “Family Resemblances and Generalization concerning the Arts” (1965) replied that art might share a relational feature, such as how it is made or received, that is not visible.","challenge":"Mandelbaum’s objection still stands: family members really do share something, their ancestry. Art might likewise share a hidden feature, such as a place in art history or an institution. Critics of incommensurability add that we do compare across kinds, saying a novel is greater than a limerick, and seem justified in doing so."},"aesthetics:Humean critic":{"summary":"Some judges of taste really are better, those with practice, attention and freedom from bias, and a moral flaw counts against a work artistically when it stops decent readers from responding as the work asks.","reading":[{"title":"Hume’s Aesthetics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Ethical Criticism of Art","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ethical-criticism-of-art/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Taste","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetic-taste/"}],"idea":"This view comes from one essay that makes two linked claims. First, taste rests on feeling, but feelings can be refined. People with delicate perception, long practice, wide comparison and no prejudice judge better, and their joint verdict sets the standard. Second, a work that asks readers to admire vicious conduct loses its hold on them, and that is a real blemish. The two fit together because both come from attention to how responses actually work. A good judge does not simply suppress moral feeling while reading. If a poem expects us to cheer cruelty, a sensitive reader cannot, and the poem fails on its own terms. The view is attractive because it avoids both snobbery and relativism. A music teacher hears things a beginner misses, yet the teacher’s authority rests on skill anyone could acquire, not on rank.","example":"Hume borrowed a story from Don Quixote. Two of Sancho’s kinsmen taste wine from a barrel; one detects leather, the other iron. Everyone laughs until the barrel is emptied and an old key on a leather thong is found at the bottom. Fine taste, like theirs, picks out real qualities that most people miss.","history":"David Hume’s “Of the Standard of Taste” appeared in Four Dissertations (1757). It sets out five marks of the true judge (strong sense, delicacy, practice, comparison and freedom from prejudice) and argues that where vicious manners are described with approval, this must be allowed to deform a poem. Noël Carroll’s moderate moralism (1996) revived the second claim.","challenge":"Hume’s standard seems circular: we identify true judges by their good taste, and good taste by what true judges approve. And ideal judges may still disagree, which Hume himself allowed when he said differences in temperament and era can leave some disputes unsettled. Aesthetes also question his moral claim."},"aesthetics:Platonist about beauty":{"summary":"Beautiful things share in or reflect a perfect Beauty beyond the visible world, so beauty here is real but borrowed, and loving it can lead the mind upward to what is truly good.","reading":[{"title":"Plato’s Aesthetics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/plato-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Plotinus","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/plotinus/"},{"title":"Beauty","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauty/"}],"idea":"Platonists think the beauty of a face, a melody or a mathematical proof points beyond itself. Each beautiful thing is beautiful in part and for a while; it fades, or is beautiful in one respect but not another. Behind them all stands Beauty itself, perfect and unchanging, which the mind can grasp but the eye cannot see. Beautiful things are beautiful because they share in it. This is why beauty moves us so strongly: in loving a beautiful thing we half remember something higher. The view is attractive because it explains why beauty feels like a glimpse of something more important than pleasure, and why many traditions link beauty with goodness and truth. Many people feel that a starry sky or a great piece of music is not just pleasant but somehow significant, as if it showed them what really matters.","example":"In Plato’s Symposium, the priestess Diotima describes a “ladder of love”. One begins by loving a single beautiful body, then sees beauty in all bodies, then in souls, laws and kinds of knowledge, and finally catches sight of Beauty itself, pure and unmixed. Each step teaches the lover that the earlier beauties were partial reflections.","history":"Plato developed the view in the Symposium, Phaedrus and Republic (4th century BCE). Plotinus’s essay “On Beauty” (Enneads I.6, 3rd century CE) argued that beauty is not mere symmetry, since simple things like light and gold are beautiful, and that it comes from form and ultimately from the Good. Augustine, Pseudo-Dionysius and the Florentine Platonist Marsilio Ficino carried the idea into Christian thought.","challenge":"The view depends on a realm of perfect Forms that many philosophers find impossible to believe in or to know about. Critics also say it devalues the particular: loving a person or a painting as a mere step toward something higher may miss what makes that individual thing beautiful."},"aesthetics:Classical theorist of beauty":{"summary":"Beauty is harmony: order, proportion and unity among many parts, so that a beautiful thing’s parts fit together into one well formed whole.","reading":[{"title":"Beauty","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauty/"},{"title":"Ancient Aesthetics","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ancient-aesthetics/"}],"idea":"The classical theory says beauty is a matter of how parts relate to a whole. A beautiful face, temple or symphony has many elements, and they are arranged in fitting proportion, with nothing missing and nothing extra. The pleasure of beauty is the pleasure of seeing unity in variety. This makes beauty a real, partly measurable feature of things, not just a feeling. The theory is attractive because it explains a great deal: why we admire symmetry, why musical harmonies are pleasing, why a poorly balanced building looks wrong, and why mathematics and nature can be beautiful. It also gives makers guidance, since proportion can be studied and taught. Think of how a designer adjusts the spacing in a poster until everything “sits right”, or how one wrong note in a chord jars even untrained listeners.","example":"The Pythagoreans found that pleasing musical intervals correspond to simple ratios of string lengths: an octave is 2 to 1, a fifth is 3 to 2. To a classical theorist this shows beauty’s secret: order expressed in number. The Greek sculptor Polykleitos wrote a Canon setting out ideal proportions for the human figure on the same principle.","history":"The Pythagoreans (6th and 5th centuries BCE) linked beauty with number. Aristotle wrote in the Metaphysics that the chief forms of beauty are order, symmetry and definiteness. Vitruvius’s On Architecture applied proportion to buildings. Thomas Aquinas (13th century) named wholeness, proportion and clarity as conditions of beauty. Leon Battista Alberti and Francis Hutcheson, who spoke of “uniformity amidst variety” (1725), carried the idea forward.","challenge":"Plotinus objected that simple things like a single colour or a flash of lightning can be beautiful though they have no parts to proportion. And much admired beauty, from wild landscapes to rough modern art, seems to break harmony deliberately. Proportion may be one source of beauty, not its essence."},"aesthetics:Functional-beauty theorist":{"summary":"Things are beautiful when they look fit for what they are or do, so beauty and usefulness are allies, not opposites.","reading":[{"title":"Environmental Aesthetics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/environmental-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Beauty","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauty/"}],"idea":"This view holds that much beauty is the visible expression of function. A racing yacht, a cheetah, a well balanced chef’s knife and a slender suspension bridge please us because their forms show that they are well made for their purposes. To see this kind of beauty you need to know what a thing is for: a leg that looks perfect on a racehorse would look wrong on a draft horse. The view is attractive because it joins beauty to understanding and resists the idea that the aesthetic must be useless. It also fits a lot of design and engineering, where getting the function right often produces elegance, and it helps explain why a healthy animal or a thriving plant looks beautiful to people who know what flourishing looks like for that kind of thing.","example":"In Xenophon’s Memorabilia, Socrates claims that a dung basket can be beautiful and a golden shield ugly, if the basket is well made for its work and the shield badly made for its. A functional-beauty theorist would say the same of a cheap but perfectly balanced garden spade compared with an ornate one that blisters your hands.","history":"Xenophon’s Memorabilia (Book III, chapter 8) presents Socrates linking beauty and fitness. Kant distinguished “free” beauty from “dependent” beauty that presupposes a concept of what a thing should be (1790). The architect Louis Sullivan’s slogan “form follows function” (1896) shaped modern design. Glenn Parsons and Allen Carlson’s Functional Beauty (2008) is the main recent defence, applying it to nature, artefacts and architecture.","challenge":"Many beautiful things have no function, such as a sunset or an abstract painting, and some highly functional things are ugly. Critics also ask whether looking fit is really beauty or only a different kind of approval. Defenders usually reply that functional beauty is one important kind of beauty, not the whole story."},"aesthetics:Lover of wabi-sabi":{"summary":"Beauty lives in what is imperfect, worn, incomplete or passing, like a cracked tea bowl or falling blossoms, and it is bound up with time and simple honesty.","reading":[{"title":"Japanese Aesthetics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/japanese-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Beauty","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauty/"}],"idea":"Where the classical theory finds beauty in perfect proportion, this view finds it in imperfection. An uneven handmade bowl, a weathered wooden gate, moss on a stone, cherry blossoms that fall within a week: these move us precisely because they show the marks of time and will not last. The feeling is gentle and a little sad, an awareness that everything passes, but it is also calm and accepting. Simplicity matters too: the beauty of a bare tea room comes from what is left out. The view is attractive because it honours ordinary, aging things and fits a world where nothing is perfect or permanent. Think of how an old pair of shoes, a scratched family table or a mended sweater can seem more beautiful than a new one, because their wear tells a story.","example":"In kintsugi, broken pottery is repaired with lacquer mixed with gold, so the cracks are highlighted rather than hidden. A lover of wabi-sabi sees the mended bowl as more beautiful than it was before it broke. Yoshida Kenkō asked, in his Essays in Idleness, whether we should look at cherry blossoms only in full bloom; their scattering, he suggested, is moving too.","history":"Yoshida Kenkō’s Essays in Idleness (around 1330) praised the incomplete and the fleeting. The tea master Sen no Rikyū (1522 to 1591) made rustic, imperfect tea wares central to the tea ceremony. The poet Matsuo Bashō (1644 to 1694) gave the mood its classic poetry, and Motoori Norinaga (1730 to 1801) theorised mono no aware, the pathos of things. Tanizaki’s In Praise of Shadows (1933) extended the tradition.","challenge":"Critics ask whether this is a theory of beauty or a particular cultural taste, and whether any imperfection could count. A chipped mass-produced mug is worn too. Defenders reply that wabi-sabi needs a sensibility trained to see quiet, honest imperfection, but that makes the standard hard to state."},"aesthetics:Humean about taste":{"summary":"Some people’s taste really is better: those with practice, attention, wide comparison and no prejudice, whose shared verdict, confirmed by works that keep pleasing over centuries, sets the standard.","reading":[{"title":"Hume’s Aesthetics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Taste","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetic-taste/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Judgment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/"}],"idea":"Humeans begin with a puzzle. Taste seems to rest on feeling, and feelings are personal, so how can one person’s taste be better? Yet we all think someone who prefers a dull poem to Homer is simply wrong. Hume’s answer is that feelings respond to real features of works, but only reliably in people whose sensibility is in good order. Some are better placed: they perceive finely, have practised and compared many works, and set aside personal and cultural bias. Their joint verdict is the standard, and history tests it, since works that keep pleasing such judges across ages and nations have proved their worth. The view is attractive because it keeps both feeling and standards. A trained sommelier and a beginner both taste the wine; the sommelier is simply better at noticing what is there.","example":"Hume notes that the same Homer who pleased Athens and Rome two thousand years ago is still admired in Paris and London. Fashionable works come and go, but a work that survives changes of language, politics and custom has passed a real test. A Humean might say the same of a pop song still loved decades after release.","history":"David Hume’s “Of the Standard of Taste” (1757) is the source, with its five marks of the true judge and its appeal to the test of time. It answered both strict subjectivism and rule-based neoclassical criticism. Eighteenth century writers such as Joseph Addison and Francis Hutcheson had discussed taste as a refined sense. Jerrold Levinson and others continue to debate Hume’s solution.","challenge":"The standard risks circularity: true judges are those with good taste, and good taste is what true judges like. And the test of time may reflect which works powerful institutions chose to preserve rather than lasting quality. Ideal judges might still disagree, leaving the standard silent where we most want it."},"aesthetics:Kantian about beauty":{"summary":"A judgment of beauty rests on a disinterested feeling, not on rules, yet it rightly demands everyone’s agreement, because it rests on capacities all human minds share.","reading":[{"title":"Kant’s Aesthetics and Teleology","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-aesthetics/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Judgment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/"},{"title":"Beauty","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauty/"}],"idea":"Kantians hold that beauty is neither a measurable property like size nor a private liking like a taste for coffee. When you call a rose beautiful, you report a pleasure, so no rule or proof can force the verdict. But the pleasure is special. It is disinterested: you do not want to own, use or eat the rose, and you are not judging whether it is good for anything. It comes from the free play of imagination and understanding as you take in its form. Because everyone has these powers, you speak as if with a universal voice and expect agreement. The view is attractive because it explains why we argue about beauty at all. Nobody argues about whether you like mint ice cream, yet people argue passionately about whether a building or a film is beautiful.","example":"Kant distinguished the merely agreeable from the beautiful. If you say “canary wine is pleasant”, you add “to me”, and no one argues. If you say a sunset is beautiful, you do not add “to me”; you expect others to agree, and you think they are missing something if they do not. That expectation, he argued, needs explaining.","history":"Immanuel Kant set out the theory in the Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790), especially the “Analytic of the Beautiful” with its four moments: disinterestedness, universality without concepts, purposiveness without purpose, and necessity. It shaped Friedrich Schiller’s Letters on the Aesthetic Education of Man (1795) and much later aesthetics, including theories of the aesthetic attitude and formalism.","challenge":"Can a feeling really carry a demand on everyone? Kant’s answer appeals to a shared “common sense”, which critics find too optimistic given real disagreement. It is also hard to know when one’s pleasure is truly disinterested, and later thinkers doubted that beauty is as free from interest and culture as Kant claimed."},"aesthetics:Aesthetic relativist":{"summary":"Standards of taste are real but local: within a tradition, judgments can be better or worse, but no standard stands above all traditions to rank them.","reading":[{"title":"Aesthetic Judgment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Taste","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetic-taste/"}],"idea":"The aesthetic relativist takes a middle road between “anything goes” and “one true standard”. Within a tradition, such as Indian classical music, Japanese tea ceremony or European opera, there are real standards, and experts know a fine performance from a poor one. A singer who goes off pitch in a raga has made a mistake, judged by the raga’s rules. But there is no higher standpoint from which to judge whether the raga tradition is better or worse than opera. Values are tied to the communities, histories and purposes that give them life. The view is attractive because it respects expertise and also respects cultural difference. It avoids both the snob who ranks one culture’s art above all others and the subjectivist who says no one can be wrong. Think of judging a gymnastics routine by football rules: a category mistake, not a verdict.","example":"A relativist hearing a Javanese gamelan for the first time would not judge it by European harmony and declare it out of tune. Its tuning systems differ deliberately. The right question is how the piece stands by gamelan standards, which experienced listeners in that tradition can answer, and which outsiders must learn before judging.","history":"Barbara Herrnstein Smith’s Contingencies of Value (1988) argued that all value, including literary value, depends on changing contexts and communities. Joseph Margolis defended a “robust relativism” about interpretation. Hume himself allowed that differences in era and temperament can leave some disputes without a single right answer. Debates about cultural difference in anthropology and comparative aesthetics have kept the question alive.","challenge":"Traditions are not sealed boxes: they borrow, mix and argue, and people judge across them all the time, as when a Western listener comes to love Indian music. Critics ask what counts as a tradition, and whether relativism can explain why some works win admiration across many cultures."},"aesthetics:Subjectivist about taste":{"summary":"No one’s taste is better than anyone else’s: calling something beautiful reports one’s own liking, so in disputes about taste there is no winner.","reading":[{"title":"Aesthetic Taste","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/aesthetic-taste/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Judgment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/"},{"title":"Hume’s Aesthetics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume-aesthetics/"}],"idea":"The subjectivist takes the familiar saying at face value: there is no disputing about taste. When you call a song beautiful, you are telling us how it strikes you, and on that you are the final authority. Someone who hates the song is not making a mistake; they are reporting a different reaction. The view is attractive for several reasons. It explains the huge variety of tastes across people and cultures. It removes the snobbery of experts who tell others they are wrong to enjoy what they enjoy. And it seems honest about what is going on when we argue about films or music: often we are simply trading reactions. Think of friends debating whether a film was good. If one loved it and one was bored, the subjectivist says both are right about their own experience, and nothing more needs settling.","example":"Spinoza argued in the Ethics that beauty is not a quality of the object but an effect in the person who looks, so that what one person finds beautiful another finds ugly. A subjectivist faced with a critic who scorns pop music would say the critic is reporting a preference, not discovering a fact, and has no special authority over anyone else’s pleasure.","history":"The Latin proverb “de gustibus non est disputandum” states the idea. Spinoza’s Ethics (1677) treated beauty as an effect on the perceiver. Hume described this as the common view in “Of the Standard of Taste” (1757), before arguing against it. George Santayana’s The Sense of Beauty (1896) defined beauty as pleasure regarded as a quality of a thing.","challenge":"We do seem to think some judgments are wrong: someone who prefers a forgettable jingle to Bach is not just different. Hume pressed this point. Subjectivism also struggles to explain why we argue, give reasons and change our minds about art, as if there were something to get right."},"aesthetics:Aesthetic quasi-realist":{"summary":"Aesthetic judgments express attitudes rather than state facts, yet some responses are better than others, being more informed, more attentive and easier to defend.","reading":[{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Judgment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/"}],"idea":"The quasi-realist tries to have it both ways. At bottom, saying a painting is beautiful expresses an attitude, not a description of a property in the world. But attitudes can be better or worse. A response formed after long attention, with knowledge of the work’s context, free of distraction and bias, is better than a snap reaction. And a person can come to see that their earlier response was poor, which is what we mean by realising we were wrong. So the quasi-realist keeps the ordinary language of mistakes, improvement and expertise, while denying that beauty is a mysterious feature built into things. The view is attractive because it explains why taste is personal yet open to argument. When you return to a book you dismissed at fourteen and find it wonderful, you feel you have grown, not just changed. Quasi-realism makes sense of that feeling.","example":"A student dismisses a slow film as boring. After learning how its long takes build tension, and watching attentively, she finds it moving. The quasi-realist says her new response is better, not because it now matches a fact, but because it is more informed and attentive, and she can defend it with reasons that her first reaction could not survive.","history":"Simon Blackburn developed quasi-realism for ethics in Spreading the Word (1984) and Ruling Passions (1998), building on Hume’s idea that the mind “spreads” its feelings onto the world. Allan Gibbard’s norm-expressivism is a close relative. Applications to aesthetics follow the same pattern, and Hume’s own account of refined taste is an ancestor.","challenge":"If no response states a fact, what makes an informed response better rather than just different? Realists suspect the view borrows the benefits of objectivity without paying for them. Subjectivists suspect it smuggles standards back in. Quasi-realists must explain “better” without saying “closer to the truth”."},"aesthetics:Expressivist about beauty":{"summary":"“Beautiful” describes nothing; it expresses delight, like “Wow!”, so no response to beauty is better than another, and disputes about art are clashes of attitude, not facts.","reading":[{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"},{"title":"Aesthetic Judgment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/"},{"title":"The Concept of the Aesthetic","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-concept/"}],"idea":"Expressivists think aesthetic language is misleading on its surface. “That sunset is beautiful” looks like “that sunset is red”, a statement that could be true or false. But really it works like a cry of delight. It expresses your feeling; it does not describe the sunset. On the stronger version held here, that means no aesthetic response is better than another. Preferring Mozart to a jingle is like preferring tea to coffee. Aesthetic criticism can still point out features of a work, such as its structure or history, and those are facts. But the final verdict is an attitude. The view is attractive because it is economical: it needs no special property of beauty and no special faculty to detect it. It also explains why aesthetic arguments often end with each side saying, in effect, “I just love it” and “I just don’t”.","example":"A. J. Ayer argued that aesthetic words, like ethical ones, are used to express feelings and evoke a response, not to state facts. On his view, when a critic praises a poem, the factual part of the review is the description of its imagery and meter; the praise itself is a kind of applause, and there is nothing in it to be true or false.","history":"A. J. Ayer set out emotivism about ethics and aesthetics in Language, Truth and Logic (1936), chapter 6. Charles Stevenson’s Ethics and Language (1944) developed the theory, stressing how evaluative words also try to influence others. Both drew on the logical positivist view that only statements testable by experience are factual, and on Hume’s sentimentalism.","challenge":"We reason about beauty in ways that make little sense for exclamations: “if the second act is weak, the play is not great.” This is the Frege-Geach problem. And the claim that no response is better clashes with the strong sense that taste can be educated. Quasi-realism arose partly to answer these worries."},"aesthetics:Aesthetic realist":{"summary":"Beauty is in the thing: whether a sunset is beautiful is true or false of the sunset itself, whatever anyone feels, and taste is a kind of perception that can miss what is there.","reading":[{"title":"Aesthetic Judgment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/"},{"title":"Beauty","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauty/"},{"title":"The Concept of the Aesthetic","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-concept/"}],"idea":"Aesthetic realists take beauty to be a real feature of objects, as real as shape or colour, though harder to describe. The sunset is beautiful whether or not anyone is watching; a person who feels nothing is missing something, the way a colour-blind person misses a shade of red. Our feelings are how we detect beauty, not what beauty is. This explains why we treat aesthetic disputes as disputes, why we think tastes can improve, and why experts can teach us to notice what we overlooked. Realists usually say beauty depends on other features, such as colour, shape and arrangement, so that two things alike in those respects must be alike in beauty. The view is attractive because it matches how beauty seems: when you hear a great song, it feels as if you are discovering something, not inventing a feeling.","example":"G. E. Moore asked readers to imagine two worlds no one will ever see: one exceedingly beautiful, the other a heap of filth. Is it better that the beautiful one exist? Moore thought it plainly is, which suggests that beauty is good in itself, not just good for us. A realist would add that the beautiful world has its beauty with no one there to feel it.","history":"Thomas Reid’s Essays on the Intellectual Powers of Man (1785), Essay VIII, argued against Hume that beauty is a real excellence in objects that taste perceives. G. E. Moore’s Principia Ethica (1903) treated beauty as intrinsically valuable. Frank Sibley’s “Aesthetic Concepts” (1959) compared taste to perception, and Nick Zangwill’s The Metaphysics of Beauty (2001) is a modern defence.","challenge":"Deep, lasting disagreement among careful people, across cultures and centuries, is hard to explain if beauty is simply there to be perceived. Critics also ask what kind of property beauty could be, and how we detect it, since no instrument can measure it and our responses vary so much."},"ethics:Constrained consequentialist":{"summary":"You think good outcomes are the main point of morality, but some acts, such as torturing or killing an innocent person, stay off-limits even when crossing the line would turn out better.","reading":[{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"},{"title":"Rights (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rights/"},{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"}],"idea":"This view mixes two ideas that usually fight. Most of the time you reason like a consequentialist: ask which option makes things go best, and do that. But you also accept a few hard limits, often called side constraints, that you will not cross whatever the payoff. Outcomes set your goals; constraints fence in the means. People find this attractive because it matches how most of us actually think. We want to do as much good as possible, yet we recoil at framing an innocent person to calm a riot, even if the riot would kill more people. Everyday example: a manager who works hard to maximise the team's success but refuses to lie on a safety report, even when the lie would save the project and everyone's jobs.","example":"Take the classic transplant case. A surgeon could kill one healthy patient and use the organs to save five. A pure consequentialist seems pushed toward doing it. You say no: the five deaths are a terrible outcome, and you should work hard to prevent such outcomes in other ways, but killing an innocent person is simply not an available means.","history":"Robert Nozick's Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974) described rights as side constraints on pursuing goals, though Nozick himself was not a consequentialist. Amartya Sen's paper \"Rights and Agency\" (1982) proposed a \"goal rights system\" that builds respect for rights into the outcomes being promoted. Frances Kamm and other moderate deontologists have also explored theories that care about good results but forbid certain acts, such as harming as a means.","challenge":"Critics call this the paradox of deontology. If killing the innocent is so bad, why not minimise the total number of such killings? Refusing to kill one to prevent five other killings looks like caring more about your own clean hands than about the victims."},"ethics:Theistic utilitarian":{"summary":"You judge actions by their results for everyone, and ground moral facts in God: God wills the happiness of all, so promoting general wellbeing is how we follow God's will.","reading":[{"title":"The History of Utilitarianism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/utilitarianism-history/"},{"title":"George Berkeley (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/berkeley/"},{"title":"Theological Voluntarism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/voluntarism-theological/"}],"idea":"Theistic utilitarianism joins two things many people assume are rivals: religious ethics and calculating results. The thought runs like this. God is good and loves every creature. So God wants all creatures to be happy. Morality is what God wants. Therefore the right act is the one that does the most good for everyone. God supplies the authority, the reason you must care; the happiness of all supplies the content, what you must aim at. The view also offers a neat answer to why we should be moral when it costs us: a just God will reward and punish. Everyday example: a believer who gives to the charity that saves the most lives, reasoning that this is what a God who loves everyone equally would want.","example":"Berkeley asked why we should obey moral rules such as \"do not rebel against the government\" even when breaking one seems helpful. His answer: God wills the general good, and general good is best served by everyone keeping fixed rules, so we must keep them even in hard cases. This makes him an early rule utilitarian as well as a theist.","history":"George Berkeley's Passive Obedience (1712) and John Gay's \"Preliminary Dissertation\" (1731) argued that God wills human happiness. William Paley's The Principles of Moral and Political Philosophy (1785) made theological utilitarianism a standard Cambridge textbook for decades. John Austin's The Province of Jurisprudence Determined (1832) also grounded utility in divine command. Bentham and Mill later removed God from the theory.","challenge":"Critics ask what God is doing in the theory. If general happiness is what makes acts right, belief in God seems to add nothing; if God's will makes acts right, God could in principle have willed something else. The view also inherits all the ordinary objections to utilitarianism."},"ethics:Prioritarian":{"summary":"You judge actions by their results for everyone, but give extra weight to benefits for the worse off: the less someone has, the more a gain to them matters.","reading":[{"title":"Prioritarianism as a Theory of Value (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/prioritarianism/"},{"title":"Egalitarianism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egalitarianism/"},{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"}],"idea":"Prioritarians keep the consequentialist idea that we should make lives go better, but they deny that every unit of benefit counts the same. Helping someone who is badly off matters more than giving the same help to someone who is already doing well. This is not because equality is good in itself. It is because of how badly off the person is, full stop. That difference sounds small but matters: a prioritarian never thinks it is any improvement to make the rich poorer if no one else gains. Everyday example: a school with money for one extra tutor gives it to the struggling students rather than the top class, even if the top class would gain slightly more marks overall.","example":"Parfit's levelling down objection shows why prioritarians reject pure equality. Suppose everyone with sight could be blinded so that the sighted and the blind are equal. A strict egalitarian has to say something is better about that world. The prioritarian says it is better in no way at all, because no one is helped.","history":"Derek Parfit named and defended \"the priority view\" in his 1991 Lindley Lecture, \"Equality or Priority?\". Earlier, John Rawls's difference principle in A Theory of Justice (1971) gave the worst off a special place, though in a stricter form. Thomas Nagel's \"Equality\" (1979) discussed similar ideas. Later work by Larry Temkin, Nils Holtug and others debated how priority differs from egalitarianism.","challenge":"Why should the weight of a benefit depend on a person's absolute level rather than on comparison with others? Critics also note puzzles under risk: prioritarianism can recommend choices for someone that differ from what that same person, thinking carefully about their own uncertain future, would choose."},"ethics:Consequentialist with a personal prerogative":{"summary":"You think the best outcome usually makes an act right, but you may sometimes put your own life, projects and loved ones first, even when that is not best overall.","reading":[{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"},{"title":"Impartiality (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/impartiality/"}],"idea":"Pure consequentialism seems to say that whenever you could do more good, you must. That would mean giving away almost everything, since your money and time could always help someone worse off. This view keeps the goal of doing good but adds a permission, or prerogative: you may give your own life extra weight. You are allowed to do the best thing, and admired for it, but not always required to. Unlike constraints, the prerogative never forbids doing good; it only releases you from always maximising. Many find this attractive because it keeps morality serious without making it swallow your whole life. Everyday example: spending a weekend on your music instead of volunteering, while still giving a real share of your income to effective charities.","example":"Peter Singer's drowning child case makes the pressure clear. If you would ruin your shoes to save a child in a pond, why not give the price of the shoes to save a distant child? The prerogative view agrees you must do a great deal, but says you may stop short of reducing yourself to near poverty.","history":"Samuel Scheffler's The Rejection of Consequentialism (1982) introduced the \"agent-centred prerogative\" as a reply to the demandingness objection. That objection had been pressed by Bernard Williams, whose essay in Utilitarianism: For and Against (1973) argued that impartial theories threaten a person's integrity. Shelly Kagan's The Limits of Morality (1989) then criticised both prerogatives and constraints.","challenge":"How much extra weight may you give yourself, and why that amount? Kagan argued that once you allow a prerogative to avoid doing good, it is hard to explain why you may not also harm others for personal gain, unless you add constraints as well."},"ethics:Ideal utilitarian":{"summary":"You judge acts by their results for everyone, but think good results include more than wellbeing: beauty, knowledge and love can be good in themselves even if no one is better off.","reading":[{"title":"Moore's Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moore-moral/"},{"title":"Well-Being (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/well-being/"},{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"}],"idea":"Classical utilitarians said only happiness is good. Ideal utilitarians widen the list. Some things, such as beauty, knowledge, friendship and the appreciation of art, are valuable in themselves, not only because they make people feel good. You still judge acts by their results: the right act produces the most good overall. But the good being counted is plural, so a world full of pleasure and empty of understanding would not be the best world. This appeals to people who feel that the experience machine misses something, yet still want ethics to be about making the world better. Everyday example: funding a museum to preserve a masterpiece, partly because beauty and knowledge matter in their own right, not merely because visitors will enjoy it.","example":"Moore asked us to imagine two worlds that no one will ever see. One is exceedingly beautiful; the other is a heap of filth. Is the beautiful world better, even with no one to enjoy it? Moore thought yes, and so beauty must be good in itself. Ideal utilitarians count such goods when choosing acts.","history":"G. E. Moore's Principia Ethica (1903) argued that the greatest goods are personal affection and the enjoyment of beautiful things, and that right acts produce the most good. Hastings Rashdall's The Theory of Good and Evil (1907) gave the view its name, \"ideal utilitarianism\". Moore's ideas strongly shaped the Bloomsbury Group, including John Maynard Keynes.","challenge":"Once goods are plural, how do you compare them? There is no obvious common scale for trading an amount of knowledge against an amount of suffering relieved. And claims that beauty matters with no one to see it rely on intuitions many people do not share."},"ethics:Classical utilitarian":{"summary":"You hold that the right act produces the greatest total happiness, counting everyone equally; that happiness is pleasure and the absence of pain; and that each act is judged by its own results.","reading":[{"title":"The History of Utilitarianism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/utilitarianism-history/"},{"title":"Hedonism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hedonism/"},{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"}],"idea":"Classical utilitarianism has three parts. First, results are all that matter morally. Second, the only thing good in itself is pleasure, and the only thing bad in itself is pain; this is hedonism. Third, everyone's pleasure and pain counts equally, whoever they are, and each act is judged directly by what it produces. The theory is simple, impartial and practical: it gives one test for every question, from personal choices to law and prison reform. Its founders used it to argue against cruel punishments and for wider rights. Everyday example: deciding whether a new road is worth building by adding up the time saved and stress avoided for commuters and subtracting the noise and disruption for residents.","example":"Robert Nozick's experience machine (1974) is the test case. Would you plug into a machine that gives you any experiences you want for life? Classical utilitarians who answer yes, as this view does, accept that a life that feels just as good is just as good, and hold firm against the intuition that we also want real contact with reality.","history":"Jeremy Bentham's An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (printed 1780, published 1789) set out the principle of utility and a method for measuring pleasures. John Stuart Mill's Utilitarianism (1861) defended the theory and distinguished higher pleasures from lower ones. Henry Sidgwick's The Methods of Ethics (1874) gave it its most careful nineteenth century form.","challenge":"The theory seems to permit terrible acts if they produce more total pleasure, such as punishing an innocent person to calm a mob. And many people judge, against hedonism, that a life in the experience machine is missing something important even if it feels wonderful."},"ethics:Two-level utilitarian":{"summary":"You think the right act is the one that does the most good for everyone, but that in practice you hit it more often by following trusted rules and habits, calculating only in rare or conflicting cases.","reading":[{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"},{"title":"Henry Sidgwick (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sidgwick/"},{"title":"The History of Utilitarianism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/utilitarianism-history/"}],"idea":"Two-level utilitarians separate two questions: what makes an act right, and how should I decide what to do? The first answer is purely about results. The second is practical. Humans are biased, rushed and short of information, so trying to calculate every choice tends to go wrong. Better to live by well-tested rules such as keep promises, do not lie, help those in need, and to have the character that goes with them. Only when rules clash, or a case is strange, do you step back and reason about outcomes directly. This keeps utilitarianism's goal while explaining why good utilitarians look like ordinary decent people. Everyday example: you keep your word to meet a friend without recalculating, because reliable promise keeping does the most good over time.","example":"Hare imagined two characters. The \"archangel\" has perfect knowledge and no bias, and can always calculate the best act. The \"prole\" has none of these powers and must rely entirely on rules. Real people, Hare said, are mixtures: we should think like proles most of the time and like archangels only when we have the time and calm to do so.","history":"Henry Sidgwick's The Methods of Ethics (1874) argued that common sense rules are largely justified by their good results. R. M. Hare's Moral Thinking (1981) set out the intuitive and critical levels explicitly. Peter Railton's \"Alienation, Consequentialism, and the Demands of Morality\" (1984) defended a related \"sophisticated consequentialism\" in which people need not think in consequentialist terms at all.","challenge":"If you know the rule is only a rule of thumb, why follow it in a case where you can see that breaking it does more good? Bernard Williams also criticised the view as \"Government House utilitarianism\", because it can recommend that the theory itself be kept hidden from ordinary people."},"ethics:Rule utilitarian":{"summary":"You count everyone's wellbeing equally, but judge acts by rules: the right act follows the rules whose general acceptance would produce the most good, even when breaking one would do better this time.","reading":[{"title":"Rule Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism-rule/"},{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"}],"idea":"Rule utilitarians move the test of results from single acts to rules. Ask: which set of rules would make things go best if nearly everyone accepted them? Then do what those rules require. A world where people keep promises, tell the truth and never kill the innocent goes far better than one where everyone breaks such rules whenever they think it helps. So even in a case where breaking a rule would do slightly more good, you keep it. People like this because it grounds morality in wellbeing while still explaining why justice and rights feel binding. Everyday example: you wait at a red light on an empty road at night, because a rule everyone follows saves more lives than a rule of \"cross when it seems safe\".","example":"The transplant surgeon could kill one patient to save five. An act utilitarian may have to approve. A rule utilitarian asks what would happen if doctors generally accepted a rule allowing this. People would avoid hospitals, trust would collapse and far more would die. So the best rules forbid it, and the surgeon must not.","history":"Some readers find rule utilitarian ideas in Berkeley's Passive Obedience (1712) and in parts of Mill's Utilitarianism (1861). J. O. Urmson's 1953 paper interpreting Mill and Richard Brandt's work in the 1950s to 1970s developed the view explicitly. Brad Hooker's Ideal Code, Real World (2000) is the leading modern defence.","challenge":"J. J. C. Smart called rule utilitarianism \"rule worship\": if the point is good outcomes, sticking to a rule when you know breaking it is better seems irrational. If you add exceptions to fix this, the rules may collapse back into act utilitarianism."},"ethics:Act utilitarian":{"summary":"You hold that the right act is the one that produces the most wellbeing for everyone, counted equally, judged act by act. Rules are only rough guides.","reading":[{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"},{"title":"The History of Utilitarianism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/utilitarianism-history/"}],"idea":"Act utilitarianism is the most direct version of utilitarianism. For each choice, the right option is whichever produces the greatest total wellbeing, counting everyone affected equally. Rules such as \"do not lie\" are useful summaries of experience, but they have no authority of their own: if breaking one would do more good this time, you should break it. Supporters like the theory's honesty and simplicity. It refuses to put anything, even tradition or a feeling of disgust, above the real welfare of real beings, and it has driven arguments for animal welfare and global aid. Everyday example: you skip an expensive birthday gift for a friend who already has plenty, and give the money to a charity that buys malaria nets.","example":"Peter Singer's drowning child (1972) is the classic case. You pass a shallow pond where a child is drowning. Saving her ruins your clothes. Of course you should wade in. But then, Singer argues, you should also give money that could save children far away, since distance does not change the value of a life.","history":"Jeremy Bentham's An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789) is the founding text. J. J. C. Smart's essay in Utilitarianism: For and Against (1973), written with Bernard Williams, is the classic modern statement. Peter Singer's \"Famine, Affluence, and Morality\" (1972) and Practical Ethics (1979) apply the approach to poverty, animals and medicine, and inspired the effective altruism movement.","challenge":"Act utilitarianism seems to allow terrible acts whenever the numbers favour them, such as killing one patient to save five or punishing an innocent to stop a riot. It may also demand that you give up almost everything you care about."},"ethics:Utilitarian":{"summary":"You think an act is right because of its results, and that results should count everyone's wellbeing equally: a stranger's happiness matters as much as yours.","reading":[{"title":"The History of Utilitarianism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/utilitarianism-history/"},{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"},{"title":"Well-Being (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/well-being/"}],"idea":"Utilitarianism has two core commitments. First, consequentialism: what makes an act right or wrong is how it affects the world. Second, impartial welfarism: what matters in those effects is how well lives go, with everyone's wellbeing counting the same. Beyond these, utilitarians disagree about what wellbeing is (pleasure, getting what you want, or a list of goods) and whether to judge single acts or general rules. The view's attraction is its clear, humane focus: morality exists to reduce suffering and make lives better, and no one's life counts for more because of who they are. Everyday example: choosing a career partly by asking where your skills could help the most people, rather than only where you would earn the most.","example":"A government health service has money for one programme: an expensive treatment that helps a few people a great deal, or cheap vaccines that protect thousands from a moderate illness. A utilitarian asks which choice produces more total wellbeing, often using measures such as quality adjusted life years, and funds that option.","history":"In ancient China, some scholars read Mozi (5th century BCE) as an early consequentialist who judged practices by their benefit to all. Jeremy Bentham (1789) and John Stuart Mill's Utilitarianism (1861) founded the modern theory, and Henry Sidgwick refined it in 1874. Peter Singer is its best known living defender.","challenge":"Utilitarianism counts everyone equally, which many find too impartial: it seems to leave no special room for your own family, and to allow sacrificing one person whenever it benefits a larger number."},"ethics:Sidgwickian dualist":{"summary":"You think reason supports two principles: do what is best for everyone, and do what is best for yourself. Usually they agree, but when they clash you see no rational way to decide.","reading":[{"title":"Henry Sidgwick (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sidgwick/"},{"title":"Egoism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egoism/"}],"idea":"Most theories pick a side: either morality wins, or self-interest does. The Sidgwickian dualist refuses to pick. It seems self-evident that it is reasonable to promote the good of all, impartially. It also seems self-evident that it is reasonable to look after your own good, since your life is the one you have to live. In ordinary life these rarely conflict: being honest and kind usually serves you too. But in rare cases, such as risking your life for strangers, reason seems to speak with two voices. The view is attractive because it is honest about a real tension instead of explaining it away. Everyday example: deciding whether to donate a kidney to a stranger, and feeling that both yes and no are fully reasonable.","example":"Imagine a soldier who can save his whole unit by throwing himself on a grenade. Impartial reason says do it: many lives outweigh one. Self-interested reason says this is the end of everything you have. Sidgwick thought philosophy could not show either verdict to be the uniquely rational one.","history":"Henry Sidgwick, at the end of The Methods of Ethics (1874), called this \"the dualism of the practical reason\" and treated it as the deepest unsolved problem in ethics. He suggested only something like a moral order in the universe, perhaps God, could guarantee the two principles agree. Derek Parfit returned to the problem in Reasons and Persons (1984) and On What Matters (2011).","challenge":"Leaving reason divided against itself makes ethics incoherent at its core. Critics such as Thomas Nagel, in The Possibility of Altruism (1970), argued that reasons are not private to the person who has them, so self-interest should not count as a rival principle of equal standing."},"ethics:Self-referential altruist":{"summary":"You judge acts by their results and count everyone's good, but give extra weight to those closer to you: yourself, family, friends and community, with concern weaker as it spreads outward.","reading":[{"title":"Impartiality (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/impartiality/"},{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"}],"idea":"Self-referential altruism describes concern that spreads out from you in rings. Everyone's welfare counts for something, including distant strangers. But your own family counts for more than acquaintances, your community more than people far away, and so on. This sits between strict impartial utilitarianism, where all count equally, and egoism, where only you count. Many find it the most honest picture of how decent people already live: we care about the whole world, yet we would be strange parents if we treated our children like anyone else's. It also explains duties to help strangers in emergencies, since they still count. Everyday example: you spend most of your savings on your children's education, but still give part of your income to famine relief.","example":"The Stoic Hierocles described each person as surrounded by circles: self, family, relatives, fellow citizens, all humanity. Self-referential altruists accept this picture. Asked whether to help a neighbour move house or volunteer the same afternoon for strangers, they weigh both, but give the neighbour a reasonable edge.","history":"C. D. Broad coined the term \"self-referential altruism\" in the twentieth century to describe the structure of common sense morality. The Stoic Hierocles (2nd century CE) described expanding circles of concern and urged drawing outer circles inward. Confucian thinkers such as Mencius defended graded love, strongest toward kin, against Mozi's impartial care.","challenge":"How much extra weight, and for whom? The view gives no clear rate of discount, and the same pattern could seem to justify favouring your own nation, ethnic group or class over others, which many think needs much more justification."},"ethics:Objectivist":{"summary":"You hold that each person should pursue their own interest, understood as flourishing as a rational, independent person who lives by their own judgment and productive work.","reading":[{"title":"Ayn Rand (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ayn-rand/"},{"title":"Egoism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egoism/"}],"idea":"Objectivism is a form of rational egoism. Your own life is your highest value, and the proper aim of action is your long-term flourishing. But flourishing does not mean doing whatever you feel like. It means living by reason, being productive, honest with yourself and others, independent in judgment and proud of your achievements. Rand saw these as virtues because they are what a human life requires. She rejected altruism, the idea that self-sacrifice is the essence of morality, but also rejected exploiting others: people should deal with each other as traders, exchanging value for value. Supporters like its celebration of achievement and personal responsibility. Everyday example: an entrepreneur who builds a business she loves and earns customers through quality rather than lobbying or deception.","example":"In The Fountainhead, the architect Howard Roark refuses to let his designs be altered to suit popular taste, even when that costs him commissions and leaves him working in a quarry. Objectivists see him as an ideal: a person whose integrity is part of his own interest, not a sacrifice of it.","history":"Ayn Rand set out her ethics in The Fountainhead (1943), Atlas Shrugged (1957) and the essay collection The Virtue of Selfishness (1964). Her idea of value draws on Aristotle's ethics of flourishing. Later writers such as Leonard Peikoff, Tara Smith and David Kelley developed Objectivism in more academic forms, and the Ayn Rand Institute, founded in 1985, promotes her ideas.","challenge":"Critics, including Robert Nozick in \"On the Randian Argument\" (1971), doubt that Rand's step from \"life is the standard of value\" to \"each should pursue only their own life\" is valid. Others question whether it gives enough reason to help people in serious need."},"ethics:Epicurean egoist":{"summary":"You hold that each person should pursue their own good, and that this good is lasting pleasure and freedom from pain, wisely managed through a simple, tranquil life with friends.","reading":[{"title":"Epicurus (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epicurus/"},{"title":"Hedonism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hedonism/"},{"title":"Egoism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egoism/"}],"idea":"Epicureans are egoists and hedonists, but not party animals. Your goal is your own pleasure, but the best pleasure is calm: no pain in the body and no anxiety in the mind. To get there you must manage desires. Some are natural and necessary, like food and shelter; some natural but unnecessary, like fancy food; some empty, like fame and luxury. Satisfy the first, enjoy the second occasionally, drop the third. Friendship is one of life's greatest goods, and justice is a useful agreement not to harm one another. Fear of death, Epicurus argued, is a mistake: when death is, we are not. Everyday example: turning down a stressful high salary job for a modest one that leaves time for friends and peace of mind.","example":"Epicurus' symmetry argument, later developed by Lucretius, asks: you were not troubled by the eternity before you were born, so why be troubled by the eternity after you die? Removing this fear, Epicureans hold, removes one of the greatest sources of anxiety and frees you to enjoy a tranquil life.","history":"Epicurus (341 to 270 BCE) founded the Garden in Athens and set out his ethics in the Letter to Menoeceus and the Principal Doctrines. The Roman poet Lucretius' On the Nature of Things (1st century BCE) spread his ideas. In ancient India, the Cārvāka school is also reported, mainly by its critics, to have made pleasure the aim of life.","challenge":"Can an egoist really value friends for their own sake, as Epicurus wanted? If friendship is only a means to your own tranquility, it seems less than real friendship. Critics also say a life aimed at the absence of pain is too cautious and misses great goods that involve risk."},"ethics:Ethical egoist":{"summary":"You hold that the right act is whatever is best for you in the long run. Helping others is right when, and because, it serves your interest, which it often does.","reading":[{"title":"Egoism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egoism/"},{"title":"Hobbes's Moral and Political Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hobbes-moral/"}],"idea":"Ethical egoism says you ought to do what best serves your own long-term interest. This is a claim about what you should do, not just a claim that people are in fact selfish. Egoists usually point out that cooperation, honesty and kindness serve you most of the time: people trust you, return favours and want you around. So an egoist need not be a monster. But the reason to be good is always, at bottom, your own benefit. Supporters say this is clear and honest: it does not ask people to give up their lives for abstract duties, and it treats each person as the one in charge of their own life. Everyday example: you return a lost wallet because being trustworthy pays off over a lifetime.","example":"The test case is Plato's Ring of Gyges, from Republic Book II. A shepherd finds a ring that makes him invisible, so he can do anything without being caught. If being good is only in your interest because of reputation and punishment, an egoist with the ring seems to have no reason to stay just. Egoists reply that character and peace of mind still matter.","history":"Yang Zhu (4th century BCE) was criticised by Mencius for refusing to sacrifice even a hair to benefit the world. Thomas Hobbes's Leviathan (1651) built morality from self-interest and is often read as egoist. Henry Sidgwick's The Methods of Ethics (1874) treated egoism as one of three main methods. Ayn Rand defended egoism as a virtue in the 20th century.","challenge":"James Rachels argued that egoism is arbitrary: it treats one person, yourself, as special without any relevant difference to justify it. Others note egoism gives no fair way to settle conflicts, since it tells each party to win."},"ethics:Kantian absolutist":{"summary":"You hold that moral duties come from reason itself, and that some can never be outweighed, whatever the consequences: lying, or killing an innocent person, is always wrong.","reading":[{"title":"Kant's Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-moral/"},{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"}],"idea":"Kantian absolutists combine two claims. First, duties come from reason: any rational person thinking clearly would see that some acts are wrong, such as treating a person merely as a tool. Second, some of these duties are exceptionless. No amount of good results can make it right to kill an innocent person or, on Kant's own strict view, to lie. Rightness is not a sum to be maximised; it is respect for persons, and respect cannot be traded away. People find this attractive because it gives moral life a firm backbone: there are things you will not do, and no clever calculation can talk you into them. Everyday example: refusing to sign a false statement against a colleague, even when told it will save the company.","example":"The murderer at the door. A friend hides in your house; a killer knocks and asks where he is. Kant, replying to Benjamin Constant, argued you may not lie even here. Many Kantians disagree with Kant's verdict, but absolutists hold that some lines, if not this one, can never be crossed.","history":"Immanuel Kant’s Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785) introduced the categorical imperative, including the formula that we must treat humanity never merely as a means. His 1797 essay “On a Supposed Right to Lie from Philanthropy” defended the strict duty of truthfulness, even toward a murderer at the door. Later Kantians such as Christine Korsgaard, in “The Right to Lie” (1986), kept Kant’s respect for persons but argued that his verdict on the murderer was mistaken.","challenge":"Catastrophe cases are the hardest. If lying or killing one innocent person would prevent a nuclear war, refusing seems to put your own clean record above millions of lives. Absolute duties may also conflict, leaving no permissible option at all."},"ethics:Deontologist":{"summary":"You hold that certain acts are required or forbidden by morality itself, knowable by reason, even when breaking the rule would turn out better.","reading":[{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"},{"title":"Kant's Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-moral/"},{"title":"William David Ross (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/william-david-ross/"}],"idea":"Deontologists hold that rightness is not only about outcomes. Some acts are wrong in themselves: lying, breaking promises, harming the innocent. Others are duties: keeping your word, repaying debts, being fair. These bind you even when breaking them would make the world slightly better. Deontologists also distinguish doing from allowing: actively killing someone is worse than failing to save them. Some, like Kant, ground duties in a single principle of reason; others, like Ross, list several basic duties. The attraction is that this fits deep convictions about rights and justice: a person is not just a container of wellbeing that can be traded for more. Everyday example: keeping a promise to help a friend move, even though you could do a bit more good volunteering instead.","example":"Compare two trolley cases. In one, you can divert a runaway trolley so it kills one person instead of five. In the other, you can push a large man off a footbridge to stop it. Many deontologists say diverting is permitted but pushing is not, because pushing uses the man as a means. Philippa Foot (1967) and Judith Jarvis Thomson (1985) made these cases famous.","history":"Immanuel Kant's Groundwork (1785) grounded duty in the categorical imperative: act only on principles you could will everyone to follow, and treat humanity never merely as a means. W. D. Ross's The Right and the Good (1930) set out several prima facie duties. Modern deontologists include Thomas Nagel, Frances Kamm and Christine Korsgaard.","challenge":"The paradox of deontology: if violating rights is so bad, why not violate one right to prevent five equal violations? Deontologists must explain why the agent's own act matters so much more than the outcome, without seeming self-absorbed."},"ethics:Contractarian":{"summary":"You hold that moral rules come from an agreement that people accept because the rules benefit each of them: morality is a cooperative bargain among self-interested people.","reading":[{"title":"Contractarianism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/contractarianism/"},{"title":"Hobbes's Moral and Political Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hobbes-moral/"}],"idea":"Contractarians explain morality without appealing to God or mysterious moral facts. Imagine people without any rules. Each pursues their own interest, so life is insecure: anyone might steal or attack. Everyone would do better by agreeing to limits, such as do not kill, keep your agreements, respect property, as long as others agree too. Morality just is the set of rules that rational, self-interested people would accept for mutual benefit. This makes moral rules feel less like commands and more like a fair deal. It also explains why morality binds: breaking the rules is, in the long run, against your own interest. Everyday example: housemates who agree on a cleaning rota because each one is better off than in a filthy free-for-all.","example":"The prisoner's dilemma is the classic model. Two suspects each do better by betraying the other, yet both end up worse than if they had cooperated. Contractarians see morality as the solution: rules and dispositions that let rational people reach the cooperative result.","history":"In Plato's Republic, Book II, Glaucon presents justice as a compact among people who want to avoid being wronged. Thomas Hobbes's Leviathan (1651) described escaping a war of all against all by agreeing to a sovereign. David Gauthier's Morals by Agreement (1986) gave a game theoretic version, and Jan Narveson defended related views.","challenge":"Those with nothing to offer in a bargain, such as animals, infants, future generations or severely disabled people, seem to fall outside morality. Most people think we owe them a great deal, so a theory built on mutual advantage seems to leave out the most vulnerable."},"ethics:Contractualist":{"summary":"You hold that an act is wrong if it is ruled out by principles that no one affected could reasonably reject. Morality is about being able to justify yourself to each person as an equal.","reading":[{"title":"Contractualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/contractualism/"},{"title":"Kant's Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-moral/"}],"idea":"Contractualists also use the idea of agreement, but the parties are not bargaining for advantage. The question is: could I justify this act to each person it affects? A principle is acceptable only if no one could reasonably reject it. This means the complaint of each individual matters on its own; you cannot simply add up small benefits to many people to outweigh a serious loss to one. The view appeals to people who think morality is about mutual respect: treating others as equals whose point of view must be answered. It inherits Kant's idea that we should act on principles everyone could accept. Everyday example: before deciding a school rule, asking whether any student could reasonably object to how it treats them, not just whether most would like it.","example":"Scanlon's Transmitter Room case: Jones has suffered an accident in a TV station's transmitter room and is receiving painful shocks. Rescuing him means interrupting a World Cup broadcast enjoyed by millions. Contractualism says rescue him now, because no single viewer's complaint is as strong as Jones's, however many viewers there are.","history":"T. M. Scanlon's \"Contractualism and Utilitarianism\" (1982) and What We Owe to Each Other (1998) set out the theory. John Rawls's A Theory of Justice (1971) used a related device, the original position behind a veil of ignorance, to choose principles of justice. Both draw on Kant's idea of principles all could will.","challenge":"The redundancy objection: a principle seems reasonable to reject because the act it allows is already wrong, so the contract may do no real work. Critics also worry about refusing to aggregate: should a million mild headaches never outweigh one broken arm?"},"ethics:Natural law theorist":{"summary":"You hold that morality comes from God, but God commands acts because they are good. God built purposes into human nature, and reason can discover what fulfils them.","reading":[{"title":"The Natural Law Tradition in Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/natural-law-ethics/"},{"title":"Aquinas' Moral, Political, and Legal Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aquinas-moral-political/"}],"idea":"Natural law theory holds that right and wrong are rooted in human nature, which God designed. Humans naturally seek certain basic goods: life, knowledge, friendship, family, social order and knowing God. Acting well means pursuing these goods reasonably and never directly attacking any of them. Because these goods are built into what we are, any person can come to know the basic moral law by reason, whatever their religion. God commands what is right because it really is good for creatures like us, not by arbitrary decree. This makes the view attractive to people who want morality that is both religious and rational, open to argument with anyone. Everyday example: a doctor who refuses to falsify results, because truth and human health are basic goods.","example":"Aquinas used the principle of double effect to explain killing in self-defence. One act can have two effects: saving your life and the attacker's death. You may act if you intend only the good effect and the harm is proportionate. The principle is still used in medical ethics and the ethics of war.","history":"Thomas Aquinas’s Summa Theologiae (1265 to 1274) is the classic source, building on Aristotle, the Stoics and Cicero. Martin Luther King Jr.’s “Letter from Birmingham Jail” (1963) quoted Augustine’s saying that “an unjust law is no law at all”, and Aquinas’s account of an unjust law as a human law not rooted in eternal law and natural law. Germain Grisez developed the “new natural law” theory, and John Finnis set it out in Natural Law and Natural Rights (1980).","challenge":"Critics ask how facts about human nature produce moral obligations, and who decides what our nature's true purposes are. Natural law arguments about sex and marriage are especially contested. Some also ask whether God is needed if reason alone can discover the goods."},"ethics:Modified divine command theorist":{"summary":"You hold that moral obligations come from God's commands, but those commands flow from God's perfectly good and loving nature, so they cannot be arbitrary.","reading":[{"title":"Divine Command Theory (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/divine-command-theory/"},{"title":"Theological Voluntarism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/voluntarism-theological/"},{"title":"Religion and Morality (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-morality/"}],"idea":"This view tries to keep what is strong in divine command theory while avoiding its classic problem. It separates goodness from obligation. Goodness is grounded in God's nature: God is the standard of what is excellent and loving. Obligation, what you are required to do, comes from God's commands, much as duties in a family or friendship come from real requests. Because God's commands express a perfectly good character, God would never command cruelty for its own sake. So morality is neither independent of God nor arbitrary. People find this attractive because it explains why moral demands feel like they come from someone, with authority, while still matching what good people already recognise. Everyday example: a believer who forgives an enemy because God commands love, and sees that love is good.","example":"Plato's Euthyphro asks: is something good because the gods love it, or do the gods love it because it is good? The modified theory answers: obligations exist because God commands them, but God's commands are not random, since they flow from God's nature, which is goodness itself. Neither horn applies cleanly.","history":"Robert Merrihew Adams's paper \"A Modified Divine Command Theory of Ethical Wrongness\" (1973) introduced the view, and his Finite and Infinite Goods (1999) developed it fully. C. Stephen Evans's God and Moral Obligation (2013) and William Lane Craig have defended related versions. The view also draws on medieval debates between thinkers such as Aquinas, who grounded morality in God's reason, and Ockham, who stressed God's will.","challenge":"Critics argue the arbitrariness problem just moves back a step. Why is God's nature the standard of good, rather than good being the standard by which we call God good? There is also the practical problem of knowing which commands really come from God."},"ethics:Divine command theorist":{"summary":"You hold that moral duties come from God: an act is right because God commands it, and God's will is the ultimate source of moral obligation.","reading":[{"title":"Divine Command Theory (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/divine-command-theory/"},{"title":"Theological Voluntarism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/voluntarism-theological/"},{"title":"al-Ghazali (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/al-ghazali/"}],"idea":"Divine command theory says what makes an act right or wrong is God's will. Murder is wrong because God forbids it; generosity is required because God commands it. This gives morality a clear foundation and serious authority: moral rules are not just human preferences or social conventions, but come from the creator of everything. It also explains why morality applies to everyone, everywhere, and why it can demand more than self-interest. Many religious believers find it natural to understand ethics as obedience to God, made known through scripture, prophets or conscience. Everyday example: a person who keeps the Sabbath and gives to the poor because God commanded these things, and who sees obedience to God as the heart of being good.","example":"The Euthyphro dilemma from Plato is the central test. If acts are right just because God commands them, could God have made cruelty right by commanding it? Strong voluntarists such as Ockham bit the bullet and said God's power to command is unlimited. Others respond that God's goodness guarantees God would never command such things.","history":"William of Ockham (14th century) held that God could have commanded otherwise and that moral rightness depends on God's free will. In Islamic thought, Ash‘arite theologians, including al-Ghazali (1058 to 1111), held that good and bad are fixed by God's command, not by reason alone. Philip Quinn defended a modern version in the 20th century.","challenge":"The arbitrariness horn: if God's command alone makes things right, morality seems to rest on a will that could have chosen anything. Critics also ask how we are to know God's commands, given that religions and interpreters disagree."},"ethics:Care ethicist":{"summary":"You hold that ethics begins with relationships: morality is about caring well for particular people, noticing needs and sustaining connection. Abstract rules for everyone come later, if at all.","reading":[{"title":"Care Ethics (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/care-ethics/"},{"title":"Feminist Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/feminism-ethics/"}],"idea":"Care ethics starts from the fact that every human life depends on care: as infants, when ill, when old. Morality, on this view, is not mainly about impartial rules or adding up benefits. It is about attentive, responsive care for the particular people in front of you. Good moral agents notice what others need, listen, respond and keep relationships healthy. Care ethicists argue that traditional theories, written largely by men who rarely did care work, ignored this side of moral life and treated people as isolated individuals. Care starts with those close to you but can be extended to strangers and to political questions, such as how a society supports carers. Everyday example: a nurse who adapts how she explains a diagnosis to each patient's fears, rather than reading from a script.","example":"Carol Gilligan studied children's answers to the Heinz dilemma: should a man steal a drug he cannot afford to save his dying wife? An eleven-year-old boy, Jake, treated it like a math problem of rights. A girl, Amy, asked whether Heinz could talk to the druggist and what would happen to the relationship. Gilligan argued Amy's answer reflected a different, equally serious moral voice.","history":"Carol Gilligan's In a Different Voice (1982) and Nel Noddings's Caring (1984) founded care ethics. Joan Tronto's Moral Boundaries (1993) and Virginia Held's The Ethics of Care (2006) extended it to politics and society. Eva Feder Kittay's Love's Labor (1999) applied it to disability and dependency. Some scholars see parallels with Confucian ethics and with African ubuntu thinking.","challenge":"If ethics starts with caring for those close to us, what do we owe distant strangers we will never meet? Critics also worry that praising care could reinforce the expectation that women do unpaid care work."},"ethics:Partialist":{"summary":"You hold that relationships such as family and friendship are valuable in themselves and create special duties you do not owe to strangers. Impartial morality cannot capture this.","reading":[{"title":"Impartiality (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/impartiality/"},{"title":"Care Ethics (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/care-ethics/"}],"idea":"Partialists hold that we have special duties to particular people because of our relationships with them. A parent owes things to their own child that they do not owe to every child; a friend owes loyalty that strangers cannot claim. These duties are not just useful habits or shortcuts to the general good. They come from the value of the relationships themselves. Partialists argue that a fully impartial morality would make love and friendship impossible, since being someone's friend partly means treating them as special. People find this attractive because it fits the moral experience of almost everyone: we do not think a mother is biased for feeding her own child first. Everyday example: visiting your friend in hospital instead of spending the evening helping strangers.","example":"Bernard Williams imagined a man who can save only one of two drowning people, one of whom is his wife. If he first checks whether impartial morality permits saving her, he has, in Williams's phrase, \"one thought too many\". The motivating thought should simply be that it is his wife.","history":"Bernard Williams's \"Persons, Character and Morality\" (1976), reprinted in Moral Luck (1981), argued that impartial theories leave no room for personal attachments. Susan Wolf's \"Moral Saints\" (1982) questioned whether a perfectly moral life would be good. Samuel Scheffler, in Boundaries and Allegiances (2001), and others have since defended associative duties to family and community. Confucian ethics, with its stress on filial duties, offers a much older form of partialism.","challenge":"Which relationships generate special duties? Few deny duties to family, but similar reasoning is used to justify favouring compatriots, co-religionists or one's ethnic group. Partialists need a principled way to tell valuable relationships from unjust favouritism."},"ethics:Ethicist of relationships and care":{"summary":"You hold that right action is about doing right by the people in your life and the care these real relationships call for. Ethics starts from particular attachments, not impartial rules or calculations.","reading":[{"title":"Care Ethics (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/care-ethics/"},{"title":"Impartiality (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/impartiality/"},{"title":"Feminist Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/feminism-ethics/"}],"idea":"This is the broad view behind care ethics and related traditions. Moral life happens in relationships: between parents and children, friends, neighbours, colleagues. What you owe depends on who someone is to you and what they need. Being good means being attentive, reliable and responsive within these bonds, not applying one rule to everyone or adding up benefits. The view leaves open why relationships matter so much: because care is the root of morality, because relationships have value in themselves, or because caring for one's own is how goodness spreads. People find it attractive because it describes how most moral effort is actually spent. Everyday example: changing your plans to sit with a friend who has just had bad news.","example":"A student must choose between attending a volunteering event that would help many strangers a little, and helping her younger brother prepare for an important exam. An ethicist of relationships sees a real duty to the brother, grounded in the relationship and his specific need, rather than treating him as just one beneficiary among many.","history":"Care ethics was founded by Carol Gilligan's In a Different Voice (1982) and Nel Noddings's Caring (1984), and extended by Virginia Held's The Ethics of Care (2006). Confucian ethics has long placed relationships at the centre. African ubuntu thought, summed up in the idea that a person is a person through other persons, has been developed by philosophers such as Thaddeus Metz.","challenge":"If morality grows from relationships, what grounds duties to strangers with whom we have no relationship, such as people suffering far away or future generations? Critics fear the view may excuse indifference to anyone outside our circle."},"ethics:Stoic":{"summary":"You hold that what matters is character, that virtue is what lets a human life flourish, and that virtue is enough: poverty, pain or prison cannot take away what truly matters.","reading":[{"title":"Stoicism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/stoicism/"},{"title":"Virtue Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-virtue/"}],"idea":"Stoics hold that the only true good is virtue: wisdom, justice, courage and self-control. Health, wealth and reputation are \"indifferents\": some are naturally preferred, but none is good or bad in itself, because they can be used well or badly. What is fully in your control is your own judgments and choices, so happiness, which depends only on virtue, is always within reach. This is not about suppressing feelings, but about correcting the false judgments that cause destructive passions such as rage and panic. People find Stoicism attractive because it offers freedom from fortune: nothing outside you can ruin your life. Everyday example: after failing an exam despite real effort, you focus on what you can do next instead of resenting the result.","example":"James Stockdale, a US pilot held as a prisoner of war in Vietnam for over seven years, later wrote that Epictetus's teaching about what is and is not in our control helped him endure. His captors could hurt his body, he reasoned, but his choices and character remained his own.","history":"Zeno of Citium founded Stoicism in Athens around 300 BCE, teaching in the Painted Porch (Stoa Poikile). Chrysippus systematised it. In Rome, Seneca wrote his Letters to Lucilius, Epictetus taught the ideas recorded in the Discourses and the Handbook, and the emperor Marcus Aurelius (121 to 180 CE) wrote the Meditations. Modern cognitive behavioural therapy was partly inspired by Stoic ideas about judgments and emotions.","challenge":"Aristotle and many since think it is simply not true that a virtuous person being tortured is happy. Calling health and the lives of loved ones mere \"indifferents\" seems to deny how much they matter to a good life."},"ethics:Virtue consequentialist":{"summary":"You hold that ethics centres on character, but a trait is a virtue because it reliably produces good outcomes for others and the world. Kindness and honesty earn their status by what they do.","reading":[{"title":"Virtue Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-virtue/"},{"title":"Consequentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consequentialism/"}],"idea":"Virtue consequentialists agree with virtue ethicists that character is central to ethics: what matters most is what kind of person you are. But they explain what makes a trait a virtue in terms of results. Kindness, honesty, courage and justice are virtues because people who have them systematically make the world better. Cruelty is a vice because it reliably does harm. This explains why virtues vary somewhat with circumstances, and lets you assess new traits, such as being good with technology, by their effects. People find it attractive because it combines the realism of virtue ethics about how good people actually think with a clear standard. Everyday example: valuing patience in a teacher because patient teachers help students learn more and feel safe asking questions.","example":"Julia Driver discussed modesty. On her account, a modest person tends to underestimate their own worth to some degree, which is a kind of ignorance. Yet modesty is still a virtue, because modest people tend to get along better and cause less envy and conflict. What matters is the trait's good effects, not the accuracy of the person's beliefs.","history":"Julia Driver's Uneasy Virtue (2001) is the main statement of the view. David Hume's account of virtues as traits useful to their owner or others is often seen as an ancestor. Thomas Hurka's Virtue, Vice, and Value (2001) offered another account in which virtues are defined in relation to goods and evils.","challenge":"On this view, whether a trait is a virtue depends on its effects in the world, which can depend on luck. Critics argue this misses what virtue ethicists care about: the inner quality of a person's motives and practical wisdom, not just their reliable output."},"ethics:Neo-Aristotelian":{"summary":"You hold that right action is what a virtuous person would do, and that virtues are traits that help their owner live a flourishing human life, grounded in human nature.","reading":[{"title":"Virtue Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-virtue/"},{"title":"Aristotle's Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-ethics/"}],"idea":"Neo-Aristotelians start from a question: what is a good human life? Humans, like other living things, have a nature, and a member of a species can be a good or defective example of its kind. A good oak has deep roots; a good wolf hunts with the pack. A good human is one who lives well as a rational, social animal, and the traits that make this possible are the virtues: courage, justice, temperance, honesty and practical wisdom. Right action is what a person with these traits would do. The view is attractive because it connects ethics to facts about what helps people thrive, and puts character and judgment, not rules, at the centre. Everyday example: becoming honest by practice, telling the truth in small things until it becomes second nature.","example":"Aristotle's doctrine of the mean says each virtue lies between two vices. Courage sits between cowardice and recklessness; generosity between stinginess and wastefulness. A neo-Aristotelian facing a tough situation, such as standing up to a bully, asks what the courageous response is: neither running away nor starting a fight, but finding the right action with good judgment.","history":"Aristotle's Nicomachean Ethics (4th century BCE) is the founding text. Elizabeth Anscombe's \"Modern Moral Philosophy\" (1958) called for a return to virtue. Alasdair MacIntyre's After Virtue (1981), Rosalind Hursthouse's On Virtue Ethics (1999) and Philippa Foot's Natural Goodness (2001) developed the modern view. Martha Nussbaum's capabilities approach, which lists what humans need to flourish, applies similar Aristotelian ideas to politics and development.","challenge":"Can facts about human nature really settle what is good? Evolution shaped us for survival and reproduction, not virtue. Critics also say \"do what the virtuous person would do\" gives little guidance when you are unsure what that is."},"ethics:Humean sentimentalist":{"summary":"You hold that ethics centres on character, and that virtues are the traits we naturally approve of when we view a person sympathetically: traits useful or agreeable to their owner or others.","reading":[{"title":"Hume's Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume-moral/"},{"title":"Moral Sentimentalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-sentimentalism/"},{"title":"Virtue Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-virtue/"}],"idea":"Humean sentimentalists think moral judgment rests on feeling. Reason can tell us facts, such as that an act will cause pain, but approval and disapproval come from our sentiments. When we consider a person's character from a general, impartial point of view, we feel approval for some traits and disapproval for others. The virtues are the traits that win this approval: those useful or agreeable to the person or to others, such as benevolence, justice, wit and good humour. Sympathy, our natural tendency to share others' feelings, is what makes this approval possible. The view appeals to people who think ethics is rooted in human nature rather than pure reason. Everyday example: admiring a classmate who quietly helps others, simply because that kindness moves you.","example":"Hume argued that if you examine a wilful murder, you will find passions, motives and thoughts, but no fact called \"vice\" until you turn to your own feeling of disapproval. The vice, he said, lies in the observer's sentiment. Sentimentalists still hold that we correct our feelings by taking a general point of view, not just our personal one.","history":"Francis Hutcheson's An Inquiry into the Original of Our Ideas of Beauty and Virtue (1725) argued for a moral sense. David Hume's A Treatise of Human Nature (1739 to 1740) and An Enquiry Concerning the Principles of Morals (1751) developed the view. Adam Smith's The Theory of Moral Sentiments (1759) introduced the impartial spectator. Michael Slote has defended a modern version.","challenge":"If virtues are whatever we naturally approve of, what if people approve of different traits, or of bad ones? Critics ask whether sentimentalism can explain how our approvals themselves could be mistaken."},"ethics:Confucian virtue ethicist":{"summary":"You hold that ethics centres on character, and that virtues are traits that fit your roles and relationships: child, parent, friend, ruler, guided by ritual propriety and humaneness.","reading":[{"title":"Confucius (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confucius/"},{"title":"Chinese Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-chinese/"},{"title":"Virtue Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-virtue/"}],"idea":"Confucian virtue ethics holds that we are not isolated individuals but people defined by relationships: son or daughter, sibling, friend, student, citizen. Being good means fulfilling these roles well. The key virtues are ren, humaneness or care for others; li, ritual propriety, the forms of respect and courtesy that shape behaviour; yi, rightness; and xiao, filial piety. Rituals, from greeting elders to mourning the dead, are not empty: they train feelings and make relationships harmonious. A person becomes good by long practice and learning, cultivating the self so that right action becomes natural. Many find this attractive because it treats ethics as a way of life lived among others. Everyday example: a student who shows respect to teachers through attentive listening and courtesy.","example":"Confucius taught the \"rectification of names\" (Analects 13.3): when asked what he would do first in government, he said he would make sure names were correct. A ruler should act as a ruler, a father as a father. When people live up to the meaning of their roles, society becomes orderly without needing harsh punishment.","history":"Confucius (551 to 479 BCE) taught these ideas, recorded by his followers in the Analects. Mencius (4th century BCE) and Xunzi (3rd century BCE) developed them in different directions. Neo-Confucians such as Zhu Xi (1130 to 1200) systematised them. Roger Ames and Henry Rosemont have described the tradition as \"Confucian role ethics\".","challenge":"Roles can be unjust. Traditional Confucian roles included rigid hierarchies, such as wives' subordination to husbands. Critics ask whether role ethics can criticise unfair roles, or whether it tends to support whatever social order exists."},"ethics:Virtue ethicist":{"summary":"You hold that right action is about character: what a kind, honest, brave and wise person would do. Ethics asks what kind of person to be, not only which rules to follow.","reading":[{"title":"Virtue Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-virtue/"},{"title":"Aristotle's Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-ethics/"}],"idea":"Virtue ethicists think the central moral question is not \"what rule applies?\" or \"what will produce the best results?\" but \"what kind of person should I be?\". Virtues are stable traits of character, such as honesty, courage, kindness and justice, combined with practical wisdom, the ability to see what a situation calls for. A virtuous person does the right thing for the right reasons and with the right feelings, not grudgingly. Right action is what such a person would characteristically do. Virtue ethicists disagree about what makes a trait a virtue, but agree that character comes first. Many find this attractive because ethics becomes about growth and living well, not just avoiding forbidden acts. Everyday example: telling a friend a hard truth kindly, at the right moment.","example":"Consider two people who give the same amount to charity. One does so gladly, moved by compassion. The other gives grudgingly, only to look good. Rule and outcome theories may rate them the same, since the act and result are identical. Virtue ethicists say the first acted better, because her action expresses genuine generosity.","history":"Aristotle's Nicomachean Ethics (4th century BCE) founded the Western tradition, and Confucius and Mencius developed a rich virtue ethics in China. Elizabeth Anscombe's \"Modern Moral Philosophy\" (1958), Alasdair MacIntyre's After Virtue (1981), Philippa Foot and Rosalind Hursthouse led its modern revival. Today virtue ethics is one of the three main approaches in normative ethics, alongside consequentialism and deontology, and is widely used in professional and medical ethics.","challenge":"The action guidance objection: \"do what a virtuous person would do\" seems unhelpful when you are not yet virtuous and do not know what such a person would do in a hard case. Virtues can also seem to conflict, as honesty and kindness sometimes do."},"ethics:Moral particularist":{"summary":"You hold that several things matter morally, but there are no reliable general rules for weighing them: a feature that counts in favour of an act in one case can count against it in another.","reading":[{"title":"Moral Particularism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-particularism/"},{"title":"Intuitionism in Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/intuitionism-ethics/"}],"idea":"Particularists think moral reasons depend on context all the way down. Usually, that an act causes pleasure counts in its favour. But if the pleasure is a torturer's delight, it counts against. Usually, a promise gives a reason to act, but a promise extracted by force may give none. So no rule of the form \"X is always a reason\" holds. Moral wisdom is therefore not about learning principles, but about developing the sensitivity to see what matters in each situation. This appeals to people who distrust neat theories and think real cases are too varied for any formula. Everyday example: deciding whether to tell a friend the truth about a mistake, by looking at the specific situation rather than applying a rule about honesty.","example":"Dancy compares moral reasons to reasons in ordinary perception. That a thing looks red normally gives a reason to think it is red, but not if you know you have taken a drug that makes blue things look red. In the same way, moral features can switch direction depending on the other features present. Good judgment means seeing the whole case.","history":"Jonathan Dancy's Moral Reasons (1993) and Ethics Without Principles (2004) are the leading defences of particularism. John McDowell's \"Virtue and Reason\" (1979) argued that moral knowledge is a matter of trained perception, not rules. Aristotle's remark that ethics admits only as much precision as its subject is often cited as a precursor.","challenge":"Without principles, how do we teach ethics to children, check our own consistency or explain our choices to others? Critics also argue that some reasons, such as causing gratuitous suffering, seem to count against an act in every possible case."},"ethics:Ranked pluralist":{"summary":"You hold that several basic moral considerations matter, such as justice, wellbeing and not harming, but that some always outrank others, for example justice before wellbeing.","reading":[{"title":"William David Ross (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/william-david-ross/"},{"title":"Rights (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rights/"},{"title":"Intuitionism in Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/intuitionism-ethics/"}],"idea":"Ranked pluralists agree that morality has several basic sources: not harming, justice, honesty, doing good. But unlike Rossian pluralists, who weigh these case by case, ranked pluralists think some always take priority. For example, not harming might always outweigh helping, so you may not harm one person to benefit others. Or justice might always come before welfare, so a gain in prosperity can never justify an injustice. This structure gives the view some of the firmness of absolutism while still recognising many values. People find it attractive because it explains why we treat some moral considerations as almost sacred while others can be traded off freely. Everyday example: a referee who will not bend the rules to help the more popular team, however many fans would be pleased.","example":"Rawls ranked his principles of justice so that equal basic liberties come first. Suppose restricting free speech would raise national income a great deal. On a ranked view, this is ruled out: liberty has priority, and no amount of economic gain can outweigh it. Only once liberties are secured do economic considerations come into play.","history":"John Rawls's A Theory of Justice (1971) introduced the idea of \"lexical priority\", borrowed from the ordering of words in a dictionary, to rank liberty above other social goods. Many deontologists treat negative duties not to harm as stronger than positive duties to help. Ranked structures also appear in some views of human rights as trumps, a phrase used by Ronald Dworkin.","challenge":"Strict ranking means even a tiny gain in a higher value outweighs a huge loss in a lower one. Few accept that the smallest increase in liberty should outweigh mass starvation, so ranked pluralists may need to soften the ranking."},"ethics:Rossian pluralist":{"summary":"You hold that several basic moral factors, such as keeping promises, doing good, not harming, justice and gratitude, all count and none always wins. In each case you weigh them using judgment.","reading":[{"title":"William David Ross (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/william-david-ross/"},{"title":"Intuitionism in Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/intuitionism-ethics/"}],"idea":"Rossian pluralists think morality cannot be reduced to one master principle. Instead there are several basic duties, each binding unless outweighed. Ross called them prima facie duties: duties \"at first glance\", which hold in every case but can be overridden by a stronger duty in a particular situation. His list included fidelity (keeping promises), reparation, gratitude, justice, beneficence, self-improvement and not harming others. When duties conflict, there is no formula; you weigh them using practical wisdom. People find this attractive because it fits ordinary moral experience: we feel the pull of several obligations, and hard cases are hard because more than one is in play. Everyday example: deciding whether to keep a promise to meet a friend or to stop and help a stranger who has fallen.","example":"Ross gave the case of promising to meet a friend for a trivial purpose. On the way, you come across an accident and can help the injured. The duty to keep your promise is real, but here the duty to relieve distress is stronger. Breaking the promise is right, though you still owe your friend an explanation.","history":"W. D. Ross's The Right and the Good (1930) set out the list of prima facie duties, building on H. A. Prichard. Robert Audi's The Good in the Right (2004) updated Ross's view. Tom Beauchamp and James Childress's Principles of Biomedical Ethics (first edition 1979) applied a similar pluralist approach with four principles.","challenge":"Without any method for weighing duties, critics say pluralism leaves hard cases to unexplained intuition. Two careful people can weigh the same duties differently, and the theory gives no way to show which of them is right."},"ethics:Beauvoirian existentialist":{"summary":"You hold that no values are handed to us in advance, so we create them through choice, and that a choice is better when it frees others too. To will your own freedom, you must will theirs.","reading":[{"title":"Simone de Beauvoir (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauvoir/"},{"title":"Existentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/existentialism/"}],"idea":"Beauvoir agreed with other existentialists that human beings are free and that values are not given by God or nature. We create meaning through our projects. But she rejected the idea that this leaves ethics arbitrary. Our freedom is ambiguous: we are both free subjects and dependent beings, needing others to give our projects meaning and continuation. So you cannot truly will your own freedom while denying it to others. Oppression is the central evil, because it blocks people from shaping their own lives. Good choices open up possibilities for others. People find this attractive because it keeps existentialism's emphasis on freedom and responsibility while grounding solidarity. Everyday example: a teacher who helps students find their own interests, rather than pushing them into the path she prefers.","example":"Beauvoir described the \"serious man\", who hides his freedom by treating some cause or value as absolute and beyond question. Such a person will sacrifice others to the cause without noticing his own responsibility. The authentic person, by contrast, commits to projects while remembering that they are freely chosen and must respect others' freedom.","history":"Simone de Beauvoir's Pyrrhus and Cineas (1944) and The Ethics of Ambiguity (1947) set out her existentialist ethics. The Second Sex (1949) applied these ideas to women's oppression, arguing that women had been made into \"the Other\". Her work developed alongside Jean-Paul Sartre's but differed on the role of others and situation.","challenge":"Why exactly does willing your own freedom commit you to willing everyone's? Critics argue that an existentialist who denies given values cannot show this without slipping in a universal moral principle of the kind existentialism set out to reject."},"ethics:Nietzschean":{"summary":"You hold that no moral rules are handed down from God or reason, so values must be created, and that some created values are higher than others: braver, more creative, more life-affirming.","reading":[{"title":"Nietzsche's Moral and Political Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nietzsche-moral-political/"},{"title":"Existentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/existentialism/"}],"idea":"Nietzscheans think traditional morality, especially Christian morality, is not a timeless truth but a human creation with a history. Nietzsche argued that much of it grew from resentment: the weak redefined humility, pity and meekness as good, and strength and pride as evil. This \"slave morality\" may suppress the highest human possibilities. Since no values are given, we must create them, but not all creations are equal. Higher values affirm life, including its suffering; they express strength, creativity and self-overcoming. Nietzsche admired figures who shape their own character into something like a work of art. People find this attractive because it treats ethics as a creative task and challenges complacent conformity. Everyday example: an artist who pursues a demanding personal vision rather than what is popular.","example":"Nietzsche's thought experiment of eternal recurrence (The Gay Science, 1882): suppose a demon told you that you must live your life again, exactly the same, infinitely many times. Would you curse him, or welcome the news? A life-affirming person would welcome it. Nietzscheans use this as a test of whether they truly affirm the way they live.","history":"Friedrich Nietzsche developed these ideas in The Gay Science (1882), Thus Spoke Zarathustra (1883 to 1885), Beyond Good and Evil (1886) and On the Genealogy of Morality (1887). After his collapse, his sister Elisabeth edited his work and linked it with German nationalism, a distortion later scholars such as Walter Kaufmann worked to correct.","challenge":"If no values are objectively given, what makes some created values \"higher\"? Critics argue Nietzscheans need an objective standard they officially deny. Others worry that his praise of strength and contempt for pity can be used to excuse cruelty."},"ethics:Existentialist":{"summary":"You hold that no rules are handed to you in advance: you make yourself through your choices and are fully responsible for them. Authenticity means owning your choices instead of hiding behind excuses.","reading":[{"title":"Existentialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/existentialism/"},{"title":"Jean-Paul Sartre (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sartre/"}],"idea":"Existentialists hold that humans have no fixed nature or given purpose: \"existence precedes essence\". A knife is made for cutting, but no one designed you for anything. You become who you are through what you choose. This means total responsibility: you cannot blame your upbringing, your society or human nature for your choices, though they shape your situation. Hiding from this freedom by pretending you had no choice is what Sartre called \"bad faith\". Authenticity means facing your freedom and owning your choices. Existentialists disagree about whether this leads to any shared values. People find the view bracing because it treats each person as the author of their own life. Everyday example: choosing a career you believe in, rather than following a path because \"that's what people expect\".","example":"Sartre told of a student during the Second World War who had to choose between joining the Free French to fight the occupation or staying with his mother, who lived only for him. Sartre said no ethical theory could decide for him. His advice was: \"You are free, therefore choose.\" Whatever the student chose, he would be responsible.","history":"Søren Kierkegaard (1813 to 1855) is often seen as a forerunner. Jean-Paul Sartre's Being and Nothingness (1943) and his lecture \"Existentialism Is a Humanism\" (1945) gave the movement its best known form. Simone de Beauvoir's The Ethics of Ambiguity (1947) and Albert Camus, who rejected the label, explored related themes.","challenge":"If no values are given, why is authenticity better than bad faith? And if every choice creates its own value, critics argue, the view cannot condemn someone who freely and sincerely chooses cruelty, which seems far too permissive."},"ethics:Moral fictionalist":{"summary":"You doubt that anything is objectively right or wrong, but think we should keep using moral language as a useful pretence, like talk of fictional characters, because it helps us cooperate and resist temptation.","reading":[{"title":"Fictionalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fictionalism/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}],"idea":"Moral fictionalists start where error theorists end: moral claims, taken literally, are false, because there are no objective moral facts. But they think abandoning moral talk would be a mistake. Moral language is useful: it helps people make commitments, coordinate and resist the pull of short-term temptations. So we should keep using it, in something like the way we talk about Sherlock Holmes: we can say \"Holmes lives on Baker Street\" without believing it. Revolutionary fictionalists say we should adopt this attitude; hermeneutic fictionalists argue we already speak this way. The view appeals to people who are persuaded there are no moral facts, but who value moral practice. Everyday example: saying \"cheating is wrong\" with full conviction in conversation, while privately thinking there is no objective fact behind it.","example":"Joyce compares moral thinking to a person trying to quit smoking who tells themselves \"cigarettes are absolutely forbidden\". The rule is self-imposed, yet treating it as absolute helps resist temptation better than weighing pros and cons each time. Moral fictionalists think moral language can play a similar role for all of us.","history":"Richard Joyce's The Myth of Morality (2001) defended revolutionary moral fictionalism as the best response to error theory. Mark Kalderon's Moral Fictionalism (2005) defended a hermeneutic version, claiming ordinary moral talk is already non-literal. Some scholars see Nietzsche's attitude to useful fictions as a precursor, as well as Hans Vaihinger's The Philosophy of \"As If\" (1911), which argued that many useful ideas are fictions we knowingly accept.","challenge":"Can a pretence really guide us if we know it is a pretence? Critics doubt that moral thoughts keep their motivating power once people see them as fiction. Others ask how fictionalists justify keeping morality without appealing to moral values themselves."},"ethics:Moral skeptic":{"summary":"You hold that no action is really right or wrong: there are no objective moral facts, so moral claims either fail to be true or cannot be known.","reading":[{"title":"Moral Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}],"idea":"Moral skeptics doubt that morality rests on anything objective. Some argue there are no moral facts at all, so moral claims are false or empty; others argue that even if there were moral facts, we could never know them. Skeptics point to deep moral disagreement across cultures and history, to the way evolution and upbringing explain our moral feelings without needing any moral truth, and to the strangeness of facts that would bind everyone regardless of their desires. This does not mean skeptics must behave badly. Many live decent lives, guided by sympathy, habit or self-interest, without believing those things make actions truly right. Everyday example: a person who keeps promises out of care for friends, while thinking \"promise breaking is wrong\" states no objective fact.","example":"Mackie's argument from queerness: if objective values existed, they would be entities unlike anything else in the universe, with the strange power to tell everyone what to do whatever they want. We would also need a strange faculty to detect them. Since the world shows no sign of such things, Mackie concluded it is simpler to deny they exist.","history":"Ancient Pyrrhonian skeptics, recorded by Sextus Empiricus (2nd century CE), suspended judgment about whether anything is good or bad by nature. J. L. Mackie's Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977) launched modern moral error theory. Walter Sinnott-Armstrong's Moral Skepticisms (2006) surveyed the epistemological versions. Richard Joyce's The Myth of Morality (2001) added an evolutionary argument that our moral sense would exist even if no moral facts did.","challenge":"Companions in guilt: the arguments against moral facts seem to apply just as well to facts about what we ought to believe, yet few accept that no belief is more reasonable than another. And many people are more certain that torturing children for fun is wrong than of any skeptical premise."},"meta:theistic moral realist":{"summary":"You think moral facts are objective, true whatever anyone believes, and that they are grounded in God's nature or will, which gives them their authority.","reading":[{"title":"Moral Arguments for the Existence of God (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-arguments-god/"},{"title":"Religion and Morality (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-morality/"},{"title":"Theological Voluntarism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/voluntarism-theological/"}],"idea":"Theistic moral realists agree with other realists that morality is not invented: cruelty is wrong whatever any person or culture thinks. But they ask what could make such facts true and binding on everyone. Their answer is God. Moral facts are grounded in the nature of a perfectly good, necessary being, or in God's commands that express that nature. This explains three puzzling features of morality at once: its objectivity, since it does not depend on us; its authority, since it comes from the source of all being; and our ability to know it, since God made us able to recognise the good. People find the view attractive because it answers the question \"why be moral?\" with more than human preference. Everyday example: a believer who sees honesty as reflecting the character of God.","example":"The moral argument for God runs in the other direction. If objective moral obligations exist, and the best explanation of them is a good God, then their existence is evidence that God exists. Versions appear in Kant, who treated God as a postulate of practical reason, in C. S. Lewis's Mere Christianity (1952) and in recent work by William Lane Craig.","history":"Thomas Aquinas grounded the moral law in God's eternal law. In Islamic thought, Ash‘arite theologians grounded good and bad in God's command. Robert Merrihew Adams's Finite and Infinite Goods (1999) argued that God is the standard of the good. Mark Murphy's God and Moral Law (2011) and Linda Zagzebski have offered other theistic accounts.","challenge":"The Euthyphro dilemma asks whether things are good because God wills them, which seems to make morality arbitrary, or God wills them because they are good, which seems to make God unnecessary. Critics also note that many atheists know morality perfectly well."},"meta:naturalist moral realist":{"summary":"You think moral facts are objective and part of the natural world, like facts about health or wellbeing, discovered by observation and theory rather than by anything supernatural.","reading":[{"title":"Moral Naturalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/naturalism-moral/"},{"title":"Moral Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-realism/"}],"idea":"Naturalist moral realists want morality to be real without being mysterious. Moral facts, they say, are natural facts, perhaps facts about what promotes human flourishing, or what an informed and rational person would want. Compare health: whether a diet is healthy is an objective fact, though complex and sometimes disputed, and science can study it. Moral facts are similar. Wrongness might be a cluster of natural properties, such as causing needless suffering and undermining cooperation, that tend to go together. This lets morality fit within a scientific picture of the world, and explains how moral knowledge grows through experience. People find the view attractive because it combines realism with scientific respectability. Everyday example: concluding that bullying is wrong partly from evidence of the lasting harm it causes.","example":"Nicholas Sturgeon argued that moral facts can explain events. Why did Hitler start a world war and order the Holocaust? Part of the best explanation, Sturgeon said, is that he was morally depraved. If moral facts play a role in good explanations, as facts in science do, we have reason to believe they are real natural facts.","history":"Aristotle's grounding of virtue in human nature is an ancestor. In the 1980s, the \"Cornell realists\", including Nicholas Sturgeon (\"Moral Explanations\", 1985), Richard Boyd (\"How to Be a Moral Realist\", 1988) and David Brink (Moral Realism and the Foundations of Ethics, 1989), developed modern versions. Peter Railton's \"Moral Realism\" (1986) offered a reductive naturalist account.","challenge":"G. E. Moore's open question argument: for any natural property, it still seems an open question whether something with that property is good. Horgan and Timmons's \"moral twin earth\" thought experiment pressed a similar worry against the Cornell realists."},"meta:non-naturalist moral realist":{"summary":"You think moral facts are objective but are not physical facts science could measure; they are a distinct kind of truth, known by reason, as truths of logic or mathematics are.","reading":[{"title":"Moral Non-Naturalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-non-naturalism/"},{"title":"Intuitionism in Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/intuitionism-ethics/"},{"title":"Moral Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-realism/"}],"idea":"Non-naturalist moral realists think moral facts are real, objective and irreducible. That pain is bad, or that we have reason to help others, is not a fact about atoms, brains or evolution, though it may depend on such facts. It is a normative fact, a fact about what matters and what we ought to do, and such facts form their own category. We know them not through the senses but through reflection and intuition, much as we know that 2 + 2 = 4 or that contradictions cannot be true. People find this attractive because it takes morality at face value: moral claims seem to be about reasons and values, not about anything science measures. Everyday example: being sure that betraying a trusting friend for a small gain is wrong, without any experiment.","example":"Moore's open question argument: take any natural definition of \"good\", such as \"what we desire to desire\". Ask, \"Is what we desire to desire really good?\" The question still seems open and meaningful, unlike \"Is a bachelor unmarried?\". Moore concluded that goodness cannot be identical to any natural property.","history":"G. E. Moore's Principia Ethica (1903) founded the modern view, and W. D. Ross's The Right and the Good (1930) developed intuitionism. Recent defenders include Russ Shafer-Landau, Moral Realism: A Defence (2003), Michael Huemer, Ethical Intuitionism (2005), Derek Parfit, On What Matters (2011), and David Enoch, Taking Morality Seriously (2011).","challenge":"How do we know non-natural facts, and how can they matter to physical creatures like us? Sharon Street's \"Darwinian dilemma\" (2006) argues evolution shaped our moral beliefs without tracking such facts, so it would be a coincidence if they were true."},"meta:moral realist":{"summary":"You think moral claims state objective facts: torturing a child for fun is wrong whatever anyone thinks or feels. Morality is discovered, not invented, and moral progress is real.","reading":[{"title":"Moral Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-realism/"},{"title":"Metaethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaethics/"}],"idea":"Moral realists hold that some moral claims are true, and true independently of what any person or culture believes or feels. If a whole society approved of slavery, it would still be wrong; the society would simply be mistaken. This explains why moral disagreement feels like a dispute about something real, why we can criticise other cultures and our own past, and why reforms such as abolishing slavery look like genuine progress rather than changes in fashion. Realists disagree about what moral facts are: natural facts, non-natural facts or facts grounded in God. But they share the conviction that morality is something to discover. People find this attractive because it matches how most people speak and argue about right and wrong. Everyday example: telling someone they are mistaken, not merely different, when they defend cruelty to animals.","example":"Think about the abolition of slavery. Most people want to say the abolitionists were right and the defenders of slavery were wrong, and that society learned something. A realist can say this straightforwardly: there was a moral truth, and people came to see it. Many anti-realists must explain this in other terms.","history":"Plato's theory of the Form of the Good is an early realist view. G. E. Moore defended realism in Principia Ethica (1903), as did Thomas Aquinas in a theistic form. Contemporary realists include Derek Parfit, Russ Shafer-Landau and David Enoch. In the 2020 PhilPapers survey, a majority of philosophers accepted or leaned toward moral realism.","challenge":"Persistent moral disagreement across cultures and history is harder to explain than disagreement in science. Mackie also argued that objective, action-guiding facts would be metaphysically strange, and evolutionary debunking arguments suggest our moral beliefs were shaped by survival, not truth."},"meta:Kantian constructivist":{"summary":"You think moral truths are produced by correct practical reasoning, not found in the world, and that every rational agent, just by reasoning well, is committed to the same basic principles.","reading":[{"title":"Constructivism in Metaethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/constructivism-metaethics/"},{"title":"Kant's Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-moral/"}],"idea":"Kantian constructivists say moral truths are not facts waiting out there to be discovered, but neither are they mere feelings. They are what correct practical reasoning produces. When any rational agent thinks clearly about what to do, they are committed to certain principles: for example, that they must value their own humanity as a source of reasons, and so, by consistency, the humanity of others. Morality is built from the structure of rational agency itself. This gives morality universal authority, since anyone who can reason is bound by it, without needing mysterious moral facts. People find the view attractive because it combines the objectivity realists want with a modest metaphysics. Everyday example: realising you cannot consistently demand that others keep promises to you while planning to break your own.","example":"Kant's universalisability test is the model. Suppose you plan to borrow money with a false promise to repay. Ask whether everyone could act on that plan. If they did, no one would believe promises, and your plan would defeat itself. Constructivists see such tests as procedures that construct moral truths from reason.","history":"Immanuel Kant's Groundwork (1785) is the inspiration. John Rawls's Dewey Lectures, \"Kantian Constructivism in Moral Theory\" (1980), gave the view its name. Onora O'Neill's Constructions of Reason (1989) and Christine Korsgaard's The Sources of Normativity (1996) developed it further. Korsgaard argues that our practical identity as reflective agents commits us to valuing humanity in ourselves and others. Her Self-Constitution (2009) extends this argument.","challenge":"Can pure reasoning really yield substantive moral content? Since Hegel, critics have charged that Kantian tests are empty formalism. Others doubt that a clever, consistent person who simply does not value others is making any mistake of reasoning."},"meta:Humean constructivist":{"summary":"You think moral truths are produced by reasoning from the values people already hold, corrected for consistency and information. Most people share enough values for common morality, but reason alone cannot compel a consistent monster.","reading":[{"title":"Constructivism in Metaethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/constructivism-metaethics/"},{"title":"Hume's Moral Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume-moral/"}],"idea":"Humean constructivists agree that moral truths are constructed by reasoning, not discovered. But they deny that reasoning alone commits everyone to the same principles. What you have reason to do depends on what you already care about, once your values are made consistent and informed. Since nearly all humans share deep concerns, for their own survival, for loved ones, for fairness, they can reason their way to largely shared morality. But someone with utterly different starting values, who reasons perfectly, may have no reason to be kind. This makes morality depend on us without being arbitrary. People find this attractive because it avoids both mysterious facts and pure subjectivism. Everyday example: persuading a friend to stop mocking others by appealing to things she already cares about, such as her own sense of fairness.","example":"Sharon Street discusses an \"ideally coherent Caligula\", an example from Allan Gibbard: a person who values torturing others for fun, and whose values are perfectly consistent and fully informed. Humean constructivists bite the bullet: such a being would have no reason to stop, though we can still oppose and restrain him.","history":"David Hume's view that reason is the servant of the passions is an ancestor. Bernard Williams's \"Internal and External Reasons\" (1979), reprinted in Moral Luck (1981), argued that reasons depend on an agent's motivations. Sharon Street's \"A Darwinian Dilemma for Realist Theories of Value\" (2006) and later papers are the leading modern defence.","challenge":"Most people think the ideally coherent Caligula is not just different but wrong, and wrong because of something about torture, not about our values. Critics say Humean constructivism makes morality too contingent on what people happen to care about."},"meta:constructivist":{"summary":"You think moral claims can be true or false, but their truth is what careful, idealised reasoning about how to act produces, not a matter of facts out in the world.","reading":[{"title":"Constructivism in Metaethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/constructivism-metaethics/"},{"title":"Metaethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaethics/"}],"idea":"Constructivists offer a middle path between realism and anti-realism. Moral claims can be correct or incorrect, but what makes them correct is not some fact independent of all reasoning. Instead, moral truths are the outputs of an idealised procedure: for example, what rules could be willed by everyone, what principles would be chosen behind a veil of ignorance, or what everyone affected could accept. Just as the rules of a fair game are made, yet some calls are clearly right within it, morality is built yet binding. Constructivists differ about whether the procedure yields the same results for every rational agent (Kantian) or depends on agents' values (Humean). Many find this attractive because it promises objectivity without strange metaphysics. Everyday example: settling a family dispute by asking what rule everyone could fairly accept.","example":"Rawls's original position is the most famous constructivist procedure. Imagine choosing principles of justice without knowing whether you will be rich or poor, male or female, talented or not. Whatever principles would be chosen under that veil of ignorance count as the principles of justice. The procedure does not discover justice; it constructs it.","history":"Immanuel Kant's categorical imperative is the model for most constructivists. John Rawls's A Theory of Justice (1971) and \"Kantian Constructivism in Moral Theory\" (1980) made the approach central to modern ethics. Christine Korsgaard, Onora O'Neill, T. M. Scanlon in some readings, and Sharon Street have developed versions. Thomas Hobbes's account of morality as the outcome of rational agreement is sometimes counted as an early relative of constructivism.","challenge":"A dilemma: if the procedure gives the right answers because of some prior moral facts, such as fairness, constructivism collapses into realism. If it does not, why should its outputs bind us at all? Constructivists must say what makes their procedure the right one."},"meta:relativist":{"summary":"You think moral claims are not true or false absolutely, but only relative to the standards of a culture or person.","reading":[{"title":"Moral Relativism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-relativism/"},{"title":"Moral Relativism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/moral-re/"}],"idea":"Moral relativists hold that whether an act is right or wrong depends on the moral framework of a society or individual. \"Eating meat is wrong\" might be true relative to one culture's standards and false relative to another's, and there is no neutral standpoint from which to say which framework is correct. Relativists point to the huge variety of moral codes across history and cultures, and argue it is arrogant to assume that our own code is uniquely right. Many relativists hope the view encourages tolerance and humility. Philosophical relativists, such as Harman and Wong, also argue that it best explains persistent disagreement among informed, reasonable people. Everyday example: accepting that customs about how to treat elderly parents differ between cultures, without judging one as simply correct.","example":"Herodotus told how the Persian king Darius asked some Greeks what it would take for them to eat their dead fathers; they said no sum could persuade them. He then asked the Callatians, who did eat their dead, what it would take for them to burn their fathers; they cried out in horror. Herodotus concluded that custom is king of all.","history":"Herodotus's Histories (5th century BCE) gave the classic illustration. The anthropologist Ruth Benedict's \"Anthropology and the Abnormal\" (1934) defended cultural relativism. Gilbert Harman's \"Moral Relativism Defended\" (1975) and David Wong's Moral Relativity (1984) and Natural Moralities (2006) are leading philosophical defences. Wong argues for a \"pluralistic relativism\": several moralities can be equally true, but not every morality is acceptable, since all must serve human needs.","challenge":"Relativism seems to make moral reformers wrong by definition: Martin Luther King Jr. opposed his society's standards. It also seems to leave no ground for condemning practices such as slavery in other cultures, and it is unclear which group's standards count."},"meta:moral abolitionist":{"summary":"You think there are no objective moral facts, so moral claims are false, and that we would do better to stop using moral talk altogether and speak openly of our desires and interests.","reading":[{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"},{"title":"Moral Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral/"}],"idea":"Moral abolitionists accept error theory: moral claims try to state objective facts, but there are none, so the claims are false. Unlike fictionalists, they do not think we should keep up the pretence. Moral language, they argue, does more harm than good: it lets people dress up their own preferences as universal commands, fuels self-righteousness and makes conflicts harder to resolve, since each side believes it has the moral truth. Without moral talk, people can still care about others, cooperate and resist cruelty. They would simply say \"I don't want this\" or \"this causes suffering\" rather than \"this is evil\". People find this attractive because it promises honesty and less hostility. Everyday example: telling a roommate \"I'd really like you to stop\" instead of \"you're being selfish\".","example":"Imagine two groups arguing about a divisive issue. Each condemns the other as wicked. An abolitionist suggests both stop using moral words and state their interests and fears directly. On their view, the conflict often becomes easier to negotiate once no one claims the backing of objective moral truth.","history":"Ian Hinckfuss's The Moral Society: Its Structure and Effects (1987) argued that morality causes more harm than good. Richard Garner's Beyond Morality (1994) defended \"moral abolitionism\". Joel Marks, after rejecting his earlier moral views, defended a similar position in Ethics without Morals (2013). Some see Friedrich Nietzsche's attack on morality as an earlier source of the abolitionist attitude.","challenge":"Moral talk may play an important role in coordinating behaviour and motivating sacrifice; dropping it could make people behave worse. Critics also point out that abolitionists' claim that we \"should\" drop moral talk itself sounds like a normative claim, though abolitionists reply it is only prudential."},"meta:Mackie-style moral inventor":{"summary":"You think there are no objective moral facts, so moral claims taken literally are false, yet you still hold a substantive moral theory: ethics is something humans must invent and build, not discover.","reading":[{"title":"Moral Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}],"idea":"This view combines a sceptical metaethics with a working ethics. At the level of theory, you agree with error theorists: morality as people ordinarily understand it claims an objective authority that nothing in the world has. But at the practical level, you do not give up on moral thinking. Humans still need ways to live together, limit harm and cooperate, so they must create moral rules, much as they create laws. You can then argue about which invented morality is best, using facts about human needs and interests, and hold a view about results, duties or virtues. People find this attractive because it is honest about the foundations without being paralysed. Everyday example: supporting fair rules for a sports league, knowing they were invented but recognising good reasons for them.","example":"Mackie's book is the model. Its first chapter argues that there are no objective values. The rest of the book then discusses what morality we should adopt, drawing on Hobbes, Hume and the idea of morality as a device to counteract limited sympathies and make cooperation possible. The subtitle states the project: inventing right and wrong.","history":"J. L. Mackie's Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977) argued for error theory in its first part and proposed a practical, broadly rule-based morality in the rest. Protagoras and Hume, who saw justice as a human artifice, are earlier influences. Richard Joyce's later work also explores what we should do after error theory.","challenge":"If all moral claims are false, how can you sincerely assert that one invented morality is better than another without contradicting yourself? Critics argue the practical theory either secretly assumes moral facts or reduces to claims about what people happen to want."},"meta:error theorist":{"summary":"You think moral claims aim to state objective facts, but there are no such facts, so all positive moral claims are false, like claims about witches.","reading":[{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"},{"title":"Moral Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral/"},{"title":"Metaethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaethics/"}],"idea":"Error theorists take moral language at face value. When people say \"torture is wrong\", they mean to state an objective fact that binds everyone, whatever their desires. That is exactly why, error theorists argue, moral claims fail: nothing in the world has that kind of built-in authority. So all claims that something is morally right, wrong or obligatory are false, just as claims about witches casting spells are false even though people once sincerely believed them. This is a claim about the truth of morality, not a recommendation to behave badly. Error theorists disagree about what to do next: keep moral talk, treat it as fiction or abolish it. Many find the view attractive because it is clear-eyed and fits a scientific worldview. Everyday example: noting that people's moral convictions can be explained by evolution and upbringing alone.","example":"Compare morality to witchcraft. In the seventeenth century many people sincerely said \"she is a witch\" and meant it literally. We now think all such claims were false, because no one has magical powers. Error theorists think the same about \"that act is wrong\": the claim presupposes objective prescriptivity, and nothing has it.","history":"J. L. Mackie's Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977) gave the classic arguments from relativity and queerness. Richard Joyce's The Myth of Morality (2001) and Jonas Olson's Moral Error Theory: History, Critique, Defence (2014) are leading modern defences. Olson traces precursors in Hume and the Swedish philosopher Axel Hägerström, who argued in the early twentieth century that value judgments describe nothing real.","challenge":"Companions in guilt: epistemic claims, such as \"you ought to believe what the evidence supports\", seem to have the same kind of authority. If error theory applies to them too, it would undermine its own arguments. Many also find \"torturing for fun is wrong\" more certain than any premise against it."},"meta:quasi-realist":{"summary":"You think moral claims express attitudes rather than describe facts, yet some attitudes are better informed and more defensible than others, so you can say torture is really wrong without positing moral facts.","reading":[{"title":"Ethical Expressivism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/eth-expr/"},{"title":"Moral Cognitivism vs. Non-Cognitivism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-cognitivism/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}],"idea":"Quasi-realists start as expressivists: moral judgments express attitudes, such as disapproval or plans for how to live, rather than describing facts. But they argue this does not make morality second-rate. The full range of moral talk, including \"true\", \"fact\" and \"wrong whatever anyone thinks\", can be earned within expressivism. To say \"torture would be wrong even if everyone approved of it\" is itself to express an attitude: disapproval of torture that does not depend on approval. Attitudes can be criticised for being inconsistent, ignorant or poorly reasoned. People find this attractive because it keeps a naturalistic picture, with no strange moral facts, while letting us talk and argue like realists. Everyday example: insisting that racism is wrong and that those who approve of it are mistaken.","example":"A quasi-realist is asked: \"If you just express attitudes, does that mean kicking dogs would be fine if you approved of it?\" She answers no. Her disapproval of kicking dogs is aimed at the cruelty, not at her own feelings, so it does not change with approval. Saying kicking dogs would still be wrong expresses exactly that attitude.","history":"Simon Blackburn coined “quasi-realism” in Spreading the Word (1984) and developed it in Essays in Quasi-Realism (1993) and Ruling Passions (1998). Allan Gibbard’s Wise Choices, Apt Feelings (1990) and Thinking How to Live (2003) defended a related norm-expressivism. Both build on Hume’s sentimentalism. Blackburn’s popular book Being Good (2001) presents these ideas for general readers, and Mark Schroeder’s Being For (2008) examined whether the expressivist programme can succeed.","challenge":"The Frege-Geach problem: moral words keep the same meaning inside sentences such as \"if lying is wrong, then...\", where no attitude is being expressed. Explaining this is hard. Critics also argue that if quasi-realists can say everything realists say, the views may become indistinguishable."},"meta:expressivist":{"summary":"You think moral claims are not statements of fact but express attitudes such as approval, disapproval or acceptance of norms. This explains why moral judgments move us to act.","reading":[{"title":"Ethical Expressivism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/eth-expr/"},{"title":"Moral Cognitivism vs. Non-Cognitivism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-cognitivism/"},{"title":"Metaethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaethics/"}],"idea":"Expressivists say that when you call something wrong, you are not describing a property of the act, the way you would describe its colour. You are expressing an attitude toward it: disapproval, or a plan not to do it, or acceptance of a norm against it. This explains several features of morality. Moral judgments motivate: sincerely thinking something is wrong tends to make you avoid it. No scientific instrument detects wrongness, yet morality is not a mistake. And moral disagreement is disagreement in attitude, like people who want different things. People find this attractive because it fits a scientific worldview while taking morality's practical role seriously. Everyday example: saying \"cheating is wrong\" to express your firm rejection of cheating and to encourage others to share it.","example":"Ayer's own example: if I say \"you acted wrongly in stealing that money\", I state no more than if I had said \"you stole that money\". Adding \"wrongly\" expresses my disapproval, as if I said \"you stole that money!\" in a horrified tone. On early versions this is sometimes called the \"boo-hurrah\" theory.","history":"David Hume's sentimentalism is an ancestor. A. J. Ayer's Language, Truth and Logic (1936) and Charles Stevenson's Ethics and Language (1944) set out emotivism. R. M. Hare's The Language of Morals (1952) developed prescriptivism. Simon Blackburn and Allan Gibbard developed more sophisticated modern versions. The view became a major position in metaethics, debated alongside realism and error theory.","challenge":"The Frege-Geach problem, raised by Peter Geach in \"Assertion\" (1965): in \"if lying is wrong, then getting your brother to lie is wrong\", nothing is being disapproved, yet the argument is valid. Expressivists must explain how moral words keep their meaning in such contexts."},"god:Religious pluralist":{"summary":"You hold that the great religions are different human responses to one ultimate reality. Each sees it through its own culture, so no single tradition has the whole truth, and none is simply wrong.","reading":[{"title":"Religious Diversity (Pluralism) (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-pluralism/"},{"title":"John Hick (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/hick/"},{"title":"Religious Pluralism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/rel-plur/"}],"idea":"Religious pluralists start from a striking fact: Christians, Muslims, Hindus, Buddhists and others all seem to produce people who are wise, kind and transformed, yet their teachings contradict each other. Rather than declare one right and the rest mistaken, the pluralist says they are all partial views of the same ultimate reality, which no human idea can fully capture. Some call it God, some Brahman, some the Dao, some nirvana. The differences come from history, language and culture, like different maps of one mountain drawn from different sides. People find the view attractive because it takes the depth of every tradition seriously and removes the uncomfortable thought that billions of sincere people are lost simply because of where they were born. In everyday life, it looks like a person who prays in a church but feels equally at home meditating in a Buddhist temple.","example":"The classic image is the Indian parable of the blind men and the elephant. One feels the trunk and says it is a snake, another the leg and says it is a pillar, a third the side and says it is a wall. Each is partly right. The pluralist reads the religions this way, while admitting no one has the sighted person’s view.","history":"A Rig Veda verse (1.164.46) says the one truth is called by many names. The Hindu teacher Ramakrishna (1836 to 1886) taught that many religious paths lead to the same goal, and his student Vivekananda spread the idea at the 1893 Parliament of the World’s Religions in Chicago. In philosophy the leading defender is John Hick, who called for a “Copernican revolution” in theology in God and the Universe of Faiths (1973) and gave the full case in An Interpretation of Religion (1989).","challenge":"Critics say pluralism quietly replaces every real religion with a new one. Most believers think their teachings are literally true: either God is personal or not, either there is rebirth or not. Saying all are equally valid responses seems to mean none is true as stated, which few believers would accept."},"god:Advaita Vedantin":{"summary":"You hold that one reality, Brahman, alone is ultimately real, and your deepest self is identical with it. The Upanishads, rightly understood, are the main guide to this truth.","reading":[{"title":"Śaṅkara (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/shankara/"},{"title":"Advaita Vedanta (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/advaita-vedanta/"}],"idea":"Advaita means “not two.” Advaita Vedanta teaches that behind everything there is one reality, Brahman: pure being, consciousness and bliss, with no parts and no personality at the highest level. Your true self, ātman, is not a separate soul but Brahman itself. The world of many separate things is not simply false, but it is like an appearance on top of the one reality, produced by ignorance. Shankara’s famous image is a rope seen in dim light as a snake: the fear is real, but the snake never was. Liberation, moksha, comes from knowing this truth directly, not from earning a reward. Because ordinary reasoning works inside the world of appearances, the Upanishads are treated as the reliable source, with reason and meditation helping to understand them. The appeal is a vision of deep unity, in which nothing, including you, is ever truly separate.","example":"In the Chandogya Upanishad, the father Uddalaka asks his son Shvetaketu to dissolve salt in water. The salt is gone from sight, yet every sip tastes of it. So too, the father says, the finest essence pervades everything, and then the famous line: “you are that” (tat tvam asi). Advaitins read this as a statement that the self is Brahman.","history":"Gauḍapāda (around the 6th century CE) laid early groundwork. Shankara (traditionally 8th century) made Advaita a full system in commentaries on the Upanishads, the Bhagavad Gita and the Brahma Sutras, and in his Upadeśasāhasrī. Later thinkers such as Vācaspati Miśra developed it further. In modern times Swami Vivekananda (1863 to 1902) and Ramana Maharshi (1879 to 1950) made it widely known, in India and in the West.","challenge":"Rival Vedantins like Ramanuja pressed a hard question: whose ignorance creates the illusion? If only Brahman exists, Brahman would have to be ignorant, which contradicts its perfection. If the ignorance belongs to individuals, then individuals seem real after all. Others ask why scripture should be trusted on such a claim."},"god:Non-dualist":{"summary":"You hold that behind all things is one reality without parts or personality, and your deepest self is not separate from it. Separate things and persons are real in everyday life, but not ultimately.","reading":[{"title":"Śaṅkara (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/shankara/"},{"title":"Plotinus (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/plotinus/"},{"title":"God and Other Ultimates (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/god-ultimates/"}],"idea":"Non-dualists think the deepest truth is that reality is one. The many separate things we see, including our own separate selves, are real enough for daily life, but they are not the final word. Beneath them is a single source or ground that has no features we could list and no personality, because any feature would make it one thing among others. Everything flows from it or simply is it, and so are you at your core. The view is found in very different cultures, which its holders take as a sign that it tracks something real. It appeals to people who want a spiritual picture without a God who chooses and judges, and who find in meditation a sense that the boundary between self and world can dissolve. A simple everyday image: waves are many, but each is only the ocean in a certain shape.","example":"Plotinus compares the One to the sun: light pours out of it without the sun losing anything or choosing to shine. Everything else, mind, soul and the physical world, flows from it in stages. The goal of life is to turn back inward and rejoin that source. Plotinus’s student Porphyry reports that his teacher reached this union a few times in his life.","history":"The Upanishads (from about the 8th century BCE) and the Advaita Vedanta of Shankara (traditionally 8th century CE) give the classic Indian form. In the West, Plotinus (c. 204 to 270) described the One in the Enneads, which his student Porphyry edited. The Christian mystic Meister Eckhart (c. 1260 to 1328) spoke of a Godhead beyond the God of ordinary worship. Some forms of Buddhism and Daoism are also read as non-dual, though their own teachers often describe it differently.","challenge":"If the One has no features at all, it is hard to say anything about it, including that it exists or that it is one. Critics also ask why a perfect, unchanging reality would give rise to an imperfect world of many things, and why the appearance of separateness exists at all."},"god:Daoist":{"summary":"You hold that the ultimate is the Dao, the nameless source and pattern all things follow, with no plan or will. Wisdom is living in harmony with it, acting without forcing.","reading":[{"title":"Daoism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/daoism/"},{"title":"Laozi (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/laozi/"},{"title":"Zhuangzi (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/zhuangzi/"}],"idea":"For the Daoist, the deepest reality is the Dao, usually translated “the Way.” It is not a god who thinks, plans or judges. It is the nameless source from which things arise and the natural pattern they follow, like the way seasons turn or water finds the lowest ground. Because it has no shape, words always fall short of it. The practical heart of Daoism is wu wei, often translated “non-forcing” or “effortless action”: acting in tune with how things naturally go rather than struggling against them. Daoists praise softness, simplicity and humility, and are suspicious of rigid rules and showy ambition. People find this attractive because it offers a calm, flexible way of living that does not depend on belief in a creator. An everyday example is an experienced swimmer who moves with the current instead of fighting it.","example":"In the Zhuangzi, Cook Ding carves an ox so smoothly that his blade stays sharp for nineteen years. He explains that he no longer sees the whole ox, but follows its natural joints and spaces, never hacking at bone. The story shows wu wei: skill that works with the grain of things. Zhuangzi’s dream of being a butterfly makes a related point about how loosely we should hold our usual categories.","history":"The Daodejing, traditionally credited to Laozi, took shape around the 4th century BCE. The Zhuangzi gathers writings linked to Zhuang Zhou (4th century BCE). Later commentators such as Wang Bi (226 to 249) and Guo Xiang (died 312) shaped how both were read. From the 2nd century CE, religious Daoist movements such as the Celestial Masters developed temples, rituals and deities. Many of them honour Laozi as divine, so religious Daoism is more theistic than this philosophical reading.","challenge":"If the Dao has no will or purpose, why should we follow it? Critics ask how “going with the natural flow” can guide action when cruelty and disease are natural too. Confucian critics also argued that dismissing rules and effort would leave society without the structures that make good lives possible."},"god:Buddhist non-theist":{"summary":"You hold that there is no creator God and no lasting self, yet freedom from suffering, nirvana, is real and reachable through ethics, meditation and insight rather than divine grace.","reading":[{"title":"Buddha (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/buddha/"},{"title":"God and Other Ultimates (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/god-ultimates/"},{"title":"Dharmakīrti (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dharmakiirti/"}],"idea":"Buddhism, in this classical reading, is a religion without a creator. The Buddha taught that life is marked by suffering or unsatisfactoriness, that suffering comes from craving, that it can end, and that there is a path to ending it: the Four Noble Truths. A key teaching is anattā, “no self”: there is no permanent soul, only a changing stream of body, feelings, perceptions and mind. Gods may exist, but they are mortal beings caught in rebirth like everyone else, not makers of the world. Nirvana is not a heaven given by a god but the blowing out of greed, hatred and delusion. It is reached by one’s own practice: ethical conduct, meditation and wisdom. The appeal is a hopeful path built on practice and testing, not belief in an unseen being. Everyday example: noticing that an anxious craving passes if you watch it rather than feed it.","example":"In the Parable of the Poisoned Arrow (Majjhima Nikāya 63), a monk demands answers to metaphysical questions before he will practise. The Buddha replies that he is like a man shot with an arrow who refuses treatment until he learns who shot it and what the bow was made of. He will die first. The point: focus on ending suffering, not on speculation.","history":"The Buddha, Siddhattha Gotama, lived in northern India around the 5th century BCE. In the Pali canon’s Brahmajāla Sutta, the god Brahmā wrongly believes he created the world. Nāgārjuna (around the 2nd century CE) and Vasubandhu (around the 4th to 5th century) developed Buddhist philosophy, and Dharmakīrti (7th century) and Śāntideva (8th century) argued directly against a creator God. Some later Mahāyāna and Pure Land forms, devoted to cosmic Buddhas like Amitābha, look more theistic.","challenge":"Without a lasting self, who is reborn, and who reaches nirvana? Buddhists answer with a causal stream, like a flame passed from candle to candle, but critics find this hard to square with moral responsibility. Naturalists add that rebirth and karma face the same evidential doubts as God."},"god:Jain non-theist":{"summary":"You hold that the universe is uncreated and every soul is eternal. Souls free themselves from karma and rebirth through non-violence and self-discipline. Liberated souls are honoured, but no one made the world.","reading":[{"title":"Jaina Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/jaina-philosophy/"},{"title":"Jain Philosophy (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/jain/"},{"title":"God and Other Ultimates (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/god-ultimates/"}],"idea":"Jainism teaches that the universe has always existed and was never created. It contains countless souls, jīvas, each eternal and by nature all-knowing and blissful. But souls are weighed down by karma, which Jains picture as a fine kind of matter that sticks to the soul when it acts with passion or harm. Liberation, moksha, comes from stopping new karma and shedding the old, through right faith, right knowledge and right conduct, above all ahiṃsā, non-violence toward every living being. Liberated souls, including the twenty-four great teachers called tīrthaṅkaras, are honoured and worshipped as perfect examples, but they do not answer prayers or run the world. The appeal is a fully moral universe where each soul is responsible for its own freedom. Everyday example: Jain monks sweep the path ahead of them so as not to crush insects.","example":"Jain thinkers are famous for anekāntavāda, “many-sidedness”: reality is complex, and any single statement captures only one angle. The parable of the blind men and the elephant appears in Jain teaching to make this point. It shapes how Jains approach other religions, as partial truths, while still holding that the claim of a world creator is mistaken.","history":"Mahāvīra (traditionally 599 to 527 BCE; many scholars place him in the 5th century BCE) is the twenty-fourth tīrthaṅkara; Pārśvanātha came before him. Umāsvāti’s Tattvārtha Sūtra (early centuries CE) is accepted by both main Jain branches. Haribhadra (around the 8th century) wrote on comparing doctrines, and the Digambara monk Jinasena (9th century) gave a well-known argument against a creator in his Mahāpurāṇa.","challenge":"Critics question karma as a kind of matter: how does a non-physical soul get stuck to particles? Others find total non-violence impossible, since breathing and walking harm tiny beings. And if the universe needs no creator, some ask what explains its moral order, in which karma reliably brings fitting results."},"god:Mystic":{"summary":"You hold that something sacred is real, but no words, concepts or single tradition fully capture it. It is known by direct experience or by letting go of inadequate ideas.","reading":[{"title":"Mysticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mysticism/"},{"title":"Religious Experience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-experience/"},{"title":"Plotinus (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/plotinus/"}],"idea":"Mystics believe the sacred is real but lies beyond what language can hold. Every name for it, God, Brahman, the One, the Dao, is at best a finger pointing at the moon. So knowledge of it comes in two main ways: direct experience, often after long prayer or meditation, and the “way of negation,” which removes wrong ideas one by one: the sacred is not a body, not limited, not a thing among things. Mystics often belong to a tradition and love its practices, but hold its words loosely. Many notice that mystics from very different religions describe strikingly similar experiences of unity, peace and timelessness. The view appeals to people who feel something real in moments of awe but distrust confident doctrines. Everyday example: standing under a vast night sky and feeling something important that no sentence quite captures.","example":"William James, in The Varieties of Religious Experience (1902), listed four marks of mystical states: they cannot be fully described (ineffability), they feel like knowledge (noetic quality), they are brief (transiency), and they feel received rather than made (passivity). A mystic would say these marks fit their experience, and that the felt knowledge, not the words, is what counts.","history":"Plotinus (c. 204 to 270) described union with the One. Pseudo-Dionysius (around 500) wrote The Mystical Theology, the classic Christian text of negation, and the anonymous English Cloud of Unknowing (14th century) followed it. Meister Eckhart, Teresa of Ávila and John of the Cross are Christian examples; Rumi (1207 to 1273) and Ibn ʿArabī (1165 to 1240) are Sufi Muslim ones; the Kabbalah’s Zohar is a Jewish one. W. T. Stace’s Mysticism and Philosophy (1960) defended a shared core.","challenge":"Steven Katz (1978) argued that there is no pure, unshaped experience: a Christian mystic meets Christ, a Buddhist meets emptiness, because their traditions shape what they experience. If so, mystical experience cannot confirm a single sacred reality beyond traditions. Skeptics add that brain states can explain the experiences."},"god:Religious non-theist":{"summary":"You hold that something sacred and ultimate is real, but it is not a personal God who thinks and chooses. It may be a reality, like Brahman or the Dao, or a liberation, like nirvana.","reading":[{"title":"God and Other Ultimates (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/god-ultimates/"},{"title":"Buddha (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/buddha/"},{"title":"Daoism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/daoism/"}],"idea":"Many people assume religion means belief in God. Religious non-theists show otherwise. They hold that there is something sacred and ultimate, worth orienting a whole life around, but it is not a being with a mind and a will. For some it is a reality underlying everything, such as Brahman in Advaita Vedanta or the Dao in Daoism. For others it is a state of freedom that can be reached, such as nirvana in Buddhism or moksha in Jainism. What unites them is the rejection of a creator who plans, commands and answers prayer, combined with the conviction that life has a spiritual depth and goal. The appeal is that it keeps practice, reverence and transformation while avoiding hard problems that face a personal God, like why a loving creator would allow suffering. Everyday example: a Buddhist who meditates daily but prays to no one.","example":"Asked whether the universe is eternal or whether the enlightened exist after death, the Buddha set the questions aside. Laozi says the Dao that can be spoken of is not the constant Dao. A religious non-theist sees a shared lesson: the ultimate is not a being to argue about but a truth to realise in how you live.","history":"The view’s main classical figures are the Buddha (around the 5th century BCE), Mahāvīra, Laozi and Zhuangzi, and Shankara (traditionally 8th century CE). The Mīmāṃsā school of Hindu philosophy also rejected a creator while upholding the Vedas. In 20th century Christian theology, Paul Tillich argued in his Systematic Theology (1951) that God is being-itself rather than a being, a view that comes close to this one.","challenge":"Theists ask how something impersonal can be the ultimate source of persons, love and moral value. Atheists ask the opposite: if the ultimate is not a being and does nothing, what makes it more than nature described in reverent words? The view must explain why its ultimate is real and sacred."},"god:Process theist":{"summary":"You hold that the world is part of God but God is more than the world, and that God’s power works by persuasion, not force. God cannot simply stop evil but suffers with creatures and draws good from it.","reading":[{"title":"Process Theism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/process-theism/"},{"title":"Panentheism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/panentheism/"},{"title":"The Problem of Evil (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/evil/"}],"idea":"Process theists picture reality as a web of events always in change, not a set of fixed things. God is part of this process: God offers every event its best possibilities and lures it toward beauty and goodness, but every event also has some freedom to respond. So God’s power is like that of a wise teacher, not a puppeteer. This combines panentheism (the world is within God) with a limited view of divine power, and the combination has a payoff: it answers the problem of evil. God does not allow suffering when God could stop it; God cannot override creatures’ freedom, but feels every pain and works to bring good out of it. God also grows, enriched by the world’s experiences. God becomes a loving companion rather than a distant ruler. Everyday example: a parent who cannot control a teenager but keeps encouraging them toward good.","example":"Faced with a child’s death from cancer, a classical theist may search for a hidden reason God allowed it. The process theist says God did not want it and could not prevent it by force, since cells and molecules have their own power. God suffers this loss and works within the situation, offering comfort and new possibilities. Whitehead called God “the fellow-sufferer who understands.”","history":"Alfred North Whitehead set out the metaphysics in Process and Reality (1929). Charles Hartshorne developed the theology, for example in The Divine Relativity (1948). John Cobb and David Ray Griffin wrote Process Theology: An Introductory Exposition (1976), and Griffin’s God, Power, and Evil (1976) applied it to suffering. The idea of a God of persuasion also echoes the craftsman god of Plato’s Timaeus, who shapes a world he did not make from nothing.","challenge":"Critics say a God who cannot guarantee that good wins is not worth worship or trust. If God cannot stop a child’s cancer, can God ensure any future hope, such as life after death? Classical theists also object that a changing, growing God is not perfect."},"god:Panentheist":{"summary":"You hold that the world exists within God, roughly as a body within a self, yet God is greater than the whole world. God is present in everything and affected by it, but not reduced to it.","reading":[{"title":"Panentheism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/panentheism/"},{"title":"Rāmānuja (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ramanuja/"},{"title":"Process Theism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/process-theism/"}],"idea":"Panentheism means “all in God.” It sits between two older views. Classical theism keeps God and the world quite separate: God made the world but is not changed by it. Pantheism makes them identical: God simply is the universe. Panentheism says the world is inside God, part of God’s life, yet God is more than the world, just as you are more than your body even though your body is part of you. God is therefore intimately present in every atom and every life, and God really feels what happens in the world. People find this attractive because it combines closeness and greatness: God is not far away, but also not just another name for nature. It also fits a scientific picture of an interconnected universe. Everyday example: a person who feels God’s presence in a forest but would never say the forest itself is God.","example":"Panentheists often quote Paul’s speech in Athens in the book of Acts (17:28): in God “we live, and move, and have our being.” They take this literally rather than as a figure of speech. Rāmānuja makes the body comparison central: the world and souls are God’s body, controlled by God and existing for God, while God remains their inner self.","history":"Rāmānuja (c. 1017 to 1137) taught in his Viśiṣṭādvaita Vedānta that the world and souls are the body of Brahman, a personal God. The German philosopher Karl Krause (1781 to 1832) coined the word panentheism. Hegel and Schelling are often read as panentheists, though this is debated. In the 20th century Charles Hartshorne defended it explicitly, and many recent theologians, Christian, Jewish and Hindu, describe their views this way.","challenge":"Critics say the body analogy cuts both ways: if the world is God’s body, then the world’s evils seem to be part of God. Others find the view unclear about what “in God” means: it must be more than God being everywhere, but less than God being identical with the world."},"god:Reformed epistemologist":{"summary":"You believe in a personal God and hold that this belief can be rational without argument, as a basic belief arising from experience, like trusting your memory or that other people have minds.","reading":[{"title":"Reformed Epistemology (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/ref-epis/"},{"title":"The Epistemology of Religion (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-epistemology/"}],"idea":"Most of what we believe we never prove. You cannot prove that the past was real, that other people have minds, or that your senses are reliable, yet it is perfectly rational to believe these things. They are “properly basic”: they arise naturally from experience and are reasonable unless something defeats them. Reformed epistemologists say belief in God can work the same way. Looking at a starry sky, feeling guilt, or reading scripture, a person may simply find themselves believing in God, without inferring it from premises. If this belief comes from a faculty designed by God to produce it, it can count as knowledge. Arguments still have a role: answering objections such as the problem of evil. The appeal is that ordinary believers who have never studied philosophy are not irrational. Everyday example: you trust that a friend loves you without ever constructing a proof.","example":"Alvin Plantinga raised the “Great Pumpkin objection” against his own view: if belief in God can be basic, why not belief that the Great Pumpkin returns every Halloween? His reply is that being basic does not mean arbitrary. Some beliefs are formed by reliable faculties in the right conditions, and the Great Pumpkin belief, he argues, is not.","history":"The view draws on John Calvin’s idea of a sensus divinitatis, a sense of the divine (Institutes of the Christian Religion, 1536 onward), and on Thomas Reid’s 18th century common sense philosophy. Alvin Plantinga’s “Is Belief in God Properly Basic?” (1981) and Faith and Rationality (1983, edited with Nicholas Wolterstorff) launched it. Plantinga’s Warranted Christian Belief (2000) and William Alston’s Perceiving God (1991) are major later works.","challenge":"Religious diversity is the hardest problem. A devout Hindu or Muslim can claim their own beliefs are properly basic in exactly the same way. If so, basicality seems to support every tradition equally, and knowing that others disagree may itself be a defeater that requires argument after all."},"god:Natural theologian":{"summary":"You believe in a personal God and hold that careful reasoning, without relying on scripture, can show that God exists: for example, that the universe needs a cause or that its order points to a mind.","reading":[{"title":"Cosmological Argument (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/cosmological-argument/"},{"title":"Teleological Arguments for God’s Existence (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/teleological-arguments/"},{"title":"Natural Theology (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/theo-nat/"}],"idea":"Natural theologians think the existence of God can be shown by the same kind of reasoning used in science and philosophy, open to anyone, believer or not. They point to features of the world and argue that God is the best or only explanation. Cosmological arguments ask why anything exists at all, or why the universe began, and conclude that there must be a necessary first cause. Design arguments point to order, laws of nature, or the apparent fine-tuning of physical constants for life. Some, following Anselm, argue from the very idea of a perfect being. Natural theology does not replace faith for most of its defenders, but it shows that faith is reasonable. The appeal is that it treats God as a question of truth that can be publicly examined. Everyday example: if you found a watch on a beach, you would infer a watchmaker.","example":"Thomas Aquinas’s Five Ways in the Summa Theologiae include the argument from contingency. Everything around us might not have existed. If everything were like that, at some time nothing would have existed, and then nothing could have come to be. So something must exist necessarily, not borrowing its existence from anything else. This, Aquinas says, all call God.","history":"Aristotle argued for an unmoved mover. Ibn Sīnā (Avicenna, 980 to 1037) gave an influential proof of a Necessary Existent, and al-Kindī and al-Ghazālī defended the kalām argument from the universe’s beginning. In India, the Nyāya thinker Udayana (around the 10th to 11th century) defended proofs of God. Aquinas, Leibniz and William Paley’s Natural Theology (1802) followed. Modern defenders include Richard Swinburne, The Existence of God (1979), and William Lane Craig.","challenge":"Hume’s Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion (1779) and Kant pressed the classic objection: even if the arguments show a first cause or designer, they do not show it is one, all-good, all-powerful, or personal. Others say evolution and modern cosmology explain what design arguments appealed to."},"god:Open theist":{"summary":"You believe in a personal, loving God who does not know in advance what free creatures will choose. God takes real risks, responds to prayer and enters into genuine give and take with the world.","reading":[{"title":"Open Theism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/o-theism/"},{"title":"Omniscience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/omniscience/"}],"idea":"Classical theism says God knows the whole future, including every free choice you will make. Open theists find this hard to square with real freedom: if God already knows you will lie tomorrow, how can you do otherwise? Their answer is that the future is partly “open”: free choices that have not yet been made are not yet there to be known. God still knows everything that can be known, and is wise enough to handle any outcome, but God learns as we choose. This makes God responsive: prayers can change what God does, and God can be disappointed or delighted. Open theists argue this fits the many biblical stories in which God changes plans. The appeal is a relationship with God that is real, two-way and risky, like human love. Everyday example: a good parent who prepares for many possibilities but cannot know what their child will decide.","example":"In the book of Jonah, God announces that the city of Nineveh will be destroyed in forty days. The people repent, and God relents. A classical theist reads this as God speaking in human terms. The open theist reads it plainly: God responded to a real change, which shows that God’s plans depend partly on human choices.","history":"The medieval Jewish philosopher Gersonides (1288 to 1344) held that God knows future free choices only as possibilities. The 16th and 17th century Socinians held similar views. The modern movement began with Richard Rice’s The Openness of God (1980), followed by a 1994 book of the same title by Clark Pinnock, Rice, John Sanders, William Hasker and David Basinger, and Gregory Boyd’s God of the Possible (2000).","challenge":"Critics argue the Bible contains detailed prophecies of free human acts, which an open God could not make with certainty. Others ask whether a God who takes risks can guarantee that good finally wins. Classical theists say God’s timeless knowledge of the future does not cause our choices, so openness is unnecessary."},"god:Pantheist":{"summary":"You hold that God and the universe are one and the same: nature as a whole is divine, with nothing beyond it. Reverence for God is reverence for the order and unity of reality.","reading":[{"title":"Pantheism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pantheism/"},{"title":"Baruch Spinoza (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spinoza/"}],"idea":"Pantheism means “all is God.” Pantheists deny that God is a separate person who stands outside the world, makes plans and judges. Instead, the whole of reality, with its laws, its unity and its endless variety, is itself divine. God does not create the universe at a moment; God is the universe, considered as a single, self-existing whole. Pantheists often feel a deep awe toward nature, and they treat understanding the world, through science and philosophy, as a kind of worship. Their ethics often stress our place as parts of a larger whole, which can support a strong sense of ecological belonging. The appeal is a religious attitude that fits fully with science: nothing supernatural, but nothing merely ordinary either. Everyday example: someone who calls the night sky the closest thing they have to a church.","example":"Spinoza’s Ethics argues that there can be only one substance, which he calls “God or Nature.” Everything else, including you, is a mode, a way that one substance exists. God has no purposes and does not answer prayer. When Einstein was asked in 1929 whether he believed in God, he replied that he believed in Spinoza’s God, revealed in the lawful harmony of what exists.","history":"The ancient Stoics held that a divine reason (logos) pervades the cosmos. Baruch Spinoza gave the classic modern system in his Ethics, published after his death in 1677. The Irish writer John Toland coined the word “pantheist” in 1705. Some read Laozi’s Dao or certain Upanishads in a similar way, though those traditions have their own terms. Albert Einstein expressed admiration for Spinoza’s God.","challenge":"Critics ask why the universe deserves the name “God.” If God has no mind, plans or love, pantheism seems to be atheism with reverent vocabulary, a point Schopenhauer made sharply. Theists add that it cannot explain why the universe exists, and leaves no room for divine goodness."},"god:Deist":{"summary":"You believe a God created the world and its laws but does not intervene: no miracles, special revelations or answered prayers. Reason, not scripture, gives knowledge of God, through the order of nature.","reading":[{"title":"Enlightenment (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/enlightenment/"},{"title":"Thomas Paine (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/paine/"}],"idea":"Deists accept a creator but reject most of what organised religions add. The world’s order, they think, is strong evidence of an intelligent maker. But once the world was set up with its laws, the maker leaves it to run. There are no miracles, because a perfect designer would not need to patch the machine. There is no special revelation given to one people, because a fair God would make the truth available to everyone through reason. Many deists also believed in a moral law and in some life after death, but rejected doctrines like the Trinity. The appeal, especially in the Enlightenment, was a religion of reason that fit Newton’s new science and avoided the wars of religion fought over scripture. Everyday example: a person who believes there must be a creator, but does not pray and sees no God in daily events.","example":"The image often linked with deism is a clockmaker who builds and winds a perfect clock and then lets it run. In The Age of Reason, Thomas Paine argued that the true word of God is the creation we can see, which no human can forge or alter, unlike written texts passed down by people.","history":"Lord Herbert of Cherbury’s De Veritate (1624) is often taken as the start of English deism. John Toland’s Christianity Not Mysterious (1696) and Matthew Tindal’s Christianity as Old as the Creation (1730) followed. Voltaire championed a deist God in France, and Thomas Paine’s The Age of Reason (1794 to 1807) made the case for a wide audience. Several American founders, including Thomas Jefferson, held deist leanings.","challenge":"Critics from both sides object. Theists ask why a God who cared enough to create would never respond to creatures. Atheists say the design evidence deists relied on was weakened by Darwin, and that without revelation we have no reason to think the creator is good, or still exists."},"god:Polytheist":{"summary":"You believe in many gods, each with their own character and domain, rather than one supreme God. They may be honoured through offerings, festivals and shrines tied to places, families and seasons.","reading":[{"title":"Monotheism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/monotheism/"},{"title":"God and Other Ultimates (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/god-ultimates/"},{"title":"David Hume: Religion (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/hume-rel/"}],"idea":"Polytheists believe the divine is plural. There are many gods, goddesses and spirits, each with a personality, a sphere of power and a relationship with particular people or places: a god of the sea, a goddess of the hearth, the kami of a mountain. Religion is less about believing the right doctrines and more about relationships kept up through ritual: offerings, festivals, prayers and respect. Polytheists often see the world’s variety and conflict as better reflected by many divine powers than by one. Some traditions also hold that the many gods are expressions of a deeper unity, so the line between polytheism and monotheism is not always sharp. The appeal is a religion that feels close to nature, community and the ancestors, and that can honour other peoples’ gods too. Everyday example: a Shinto family visiting a local shrine at New Year.","example":"In Hume’s Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion (1779), the skeptic Philo points out that the design argument cannot show there is only one designer. A ship is built by many workers, so why not a world by many gods? A polytheist can take this seriously: the evidence of order fits several divine powers as well as one.","history":"Ancient Egyptian, Mesopotamian, Greek, Roman and Norse religions were polytheist; Hesiod’s Theogony (around 700 BCE) tells the Greek gods’ origins. The Neoplatonist Proclus (412 to 485) and the emperor Julian (reigned 361 to 363) defended traditional gods against Christianity. Japan’s Kojiki (712) records the kami of Shinto. Yoruba religion honours many orishas. Modern revivals include Heathenry, officially recognised in Iceland in 1973, and Hellenic polytheism.","challenge":"The classic objection is that many gods cannot be the ultimate explanation of everything: where did the gods themselves come from, and what unifies the laws of nature they all obey? Monotheist philosophers also argue that a perfect being must be unique, since two perfect beings would limit each other."},"god:Limited theist":{"summary":"You believe in one personal God but hold that suffering counts against God being all-powerful. So God is good but limited in power, struggling against evil rather than permitting it.","reading":[{"title":"The Problem of Evil (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/evil/"},{"title":"Process Theism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/process-theism/"}],"idea":"Limited theists take the problem of evil seriously from inside belief. If God could prevent a child’s painful death but chose not to, it is hard to call God perfectly good. So they give up one of God’s traditional features: God is fully good and personal, but not all-powerful. God may face obstacles, such as stubborn matter, natural laws God did not make, or the freedom of creatures, and works against evil rather than allowing it for some hidden purpose. This keeps what many believers care about most, a loving God who is on our side, while avoiding answers to suffering that many find cruel. Unlike process theism, it does not require the world to be part of God. Everyday example: a believer who prays for strength to face an illness, but does not think God sent the illness.","example":"Rabbi Harold Kushner’s book When Bad Things Happen to Good People (1981) was written after his son died of a rare ageing disease. He concluded that God does not cause such tragedies and cannot always prevent them, but gives people strength and compassion to face them. He reads the book of Job as hinting at the same conclusion.","history":"Plato’s Timaeus describes a divine craftsman limited by the material he works with. John Stuart Mill argued in Three Essays on Religion (1874, published after his death) that the evidence best supports a good but limited God. William James defended a finite God in A Pluralistic Universe (1909), and Edgar Brightman developed the idea in A Philosophy of Religion (1940).","challenge":"Critics ask whether a limited God is still worthy of worship, and whether such a God can guarantee that good will win in the end. The view also seems to need another explanation for the world: if God did not have full control, what made the forces God struggles against?"},"god:Fideist":{"summary":"You believe in a personal God on the basis of faith, which neither needs nor seeks reason’s backing. Faith is a commitment of the whole person, not a conclusion drawn from evidence.","reading":[{"title":"Fideism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fideism/"},{"title":"Søren Kierkegaard (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kierkegaard/"},{"title":"Faith (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/faith/"}],"idea":"Fideists think that trying to prove God misunderstands what faith is. Faith is not a hypothesis you accept because the evidence reaches 51 percent. It is a passionate commitment, a trust that involves your whole life, more like falling in love than solving a puzzle. Reason has its uses, but it cannot reach God, and some fideists think it should not even try, since a God proved by argument would be just another fact. Others hold a milder view: reason cannot settle the question either way, so faith must decide. The appeal is that it frees belief from depending on philosophical debates most believers never follow, and it takes seriously the way religious commitment actually feels from the inside. Everyday example: someone who says “I don’t believe because of arguments; I believe because I trust God,” and is unmoved when an argument fails.","example":"Kierkegaard’s Fear and Trembling (1843) retells the story of Abraham, commanded to sacrifice his son Isaac. By ordinary ethics, Abraham’s willingness is horrifying, and no reasoning can make it acceptable. Yet Abraham is called the father of faith. For Kierkegaard, this shows that faith goes beyond what reason and ethics can justify: it is a leap.","history":"Blaise Pascal wrote in his Pensées (1670) that the heart has its reasons which reason does not know. Søren Kierkegaard developed fideism most fully, in Fear and Trembling and Concluding Unscientific Postscript (1846). The early Christian writer Tertullian is often called a forerunner, though the famous saying “I believe because it is absurd” is a misquotation. Some read Wittgenstein’s remarks on religion in a fideist way.","challenge":"If faith needs no reasons, it seems no better than any other leap. A believer in a different God, or in something harmful, could say exactly the same. Critics say fideism makes faith immune to criticism, which removes any way to tell good faith from bad."},"god:Moral theist":{"summary":"You believe in a personal God mainly because morality seems to require it: real right and wrong, binding obligations, and the hope that goodness will finally prevail. God is the source and guarantee of the moral order.","reading":[{"title":"Moral Arguments for the Existence of God (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-arguments-god/"},{"title":"Western Concepts of God (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/god-west/"}],"idea":"Moral theists start from something almost everyone accepts: some things really are wrong, like torturing a child for fun, and not just because a culture says so. But what could make moral facts real and binding? A universe of atoms and forces seems to contain no obligations. Moral theists argue that the best explanation is a perfectly good God, whose nature sets the standard of goodness and whose commands create duties. Some versions, following Kant, argue differently: morality demands that we work for a world where goodness and happiness match, and that only makes sense if God can bring it about. The appeal is that it ties religion to something we already feel certain of. Everyday example: someone who reads about a terrible injustice and thinks this is not merely unpleasant, it is truly wrong, and asks where that “truly” comes from.","example":"A moral theist meets the Euthyphro dilemma from Plato’s dialogue: is something good because God commands it, or does God command it because it is good? Robert Adams answers that obligations come from God’s commands, but God’s commands flow from God’s loving nature, so they are neither arbitrary nor based on a standard outside God.","history":"Immanuel Kant argued in the Critique of Practical Reason (1788) that we must postulate God and immortality to make sense of the highest good. John Henry Newman argued from conscience. C. S. Lewis made a popular moral argument in Mere Christianity (1952). Robert Merrihew Adams’s Finite and Infinite Goods (1999) is a major modern defence, and Islamic Ashʿarite theologians long held that God’s commands define right and wrong.","challenge":"The Euthyphro dilemma remains the central challenge. Secular moral philosophers also argue that morality can be grounded without God, in human wellbeing, reason or non-natural moral facts. And many atheists live deeply moral lives, which suggests moral knowledge, at least, does not depend on belief."},"god:Theist of experience":{"summary":"You believe in a personal God mainly because you, or people you trust, have experienced God directly. Such experiences count as evidence, the way ordinary perception does, unless there is good reason for doubt.","reading":[{"title":"Religious Experience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-experience/"},{"title":"The Epistemology of Religion (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-epistemology/"}],"idea":"We normally trust our experiences. If you seem to see a tree, it is reasonable to believe there is a tree unless you have a reason for doubt. Theists of experience apply the same principle to religious experience. Millions of people, across cultures and centuries, report experiences of God’s presence: in prayer, in worship, in crisis, or quietly in daily life. These are not usually visions or voices, but a strong sense of being known, loved or addressed. If such experiences are treated like other perceptions, they give a reason to believe, at least to the person who has them, and some reason to others who trust that person. The appeal is that it matches how many believers actually come to faith: not from arguments, but from encounter. Everyday example: someone who, at the worst moment of their life, felt an overwhelming presence of peace and never doubted afterward.","example":"Richard Swinburne’s “principle of credulity” says that if it seems to you that something is present, it probably is, unless there are special reasons to doubt it. William Alston compared Christian experience to sense perception: both are established practices that we cannot check without circularity, and both are reasonable to trust if they hold up over time.","history":"Friedrich Schleiermacher, in On Religion (1799), grounded religion in feeling rather than proof. William James’s The Varieties of Religious Experience (1902) treated such experiences seriously. Richard Swinburne’s The Existence of God (1979) introduced the principle of credulity, and William Alston’s Perceiving God (1991) gave the fullest defence. Bhakti poets such as Mīrābāī and Sufi writers ground faith in a similar way.","challenge":"Religious experiences conflict across traditions: Christians experience the Trinity, Hindus Krishna, Buddhists emptiness. If all are equally trustworthy, they cannot all be accurate. Skeptics also note that psychology and neuroscience can trigger or explain such experiences, which gives a reason for doubt that ordinary perception lacks."},"god:Theist of revelation":{"summary":"You believe in a personal God mainly because of revelation: scripture, or a tradition you have good reason to trust as coming from God. Reason helps understand faith, but revelation comes first.","reading":[{"title":"Faith (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/faith/"},{"title":"al-Ghazali (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/al-ghazali/"},{"title":"Faith: Historical Perspectives (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/faith-re/"}],"idea":"For theists of revelation, the main way we know God is that God has spoken. The Torah, the Bible, the Qur’an, the Guru Granth Sahib and other scriptures are, for their followers, not human guesses about God but God’s own self-disclosure. Reason has an important role: it can show that trusting a source is reasonable, for example by weighing its history, its fulfilled promises or the lives it changes, and it can help interpret what is revealed. But the content of faith comes from revelation, not from human reasoning alone, since a finite mind could never work out God’s purposes on its own. The appeal is that it explains how ordinary people can know God without becoming philosophers. Everyday example: a person who trusts a doctor’s diagnosis without having studied medicine, because the doctor is a reliable source.","example":"Augustine summed up the method in the phrase “believe in order to understand,” drawn from his reading of Isaiah 7:9. One first accepts what God has revealed, and then reason works to understand it more deeply. Anselm later called this “faith seeking understanding.” The believer does not wait for proof before trusting, but faith is not the end of thinking.","history":"Augustine of Hippo (354 to 430) set the pattern for Western Christianity in works like the Confessions and On the Trinity. In Islam, al-Ghazālī (1058 to 1111) argued in The Incoherence of the Philosophers (1095) that philosophers had overstepped reason, and that revelation must guide. Anselm, Karl Barth and many Jewish and Sikh thinkers also give revelation priority. The Mīmāṃsā school treated the Vedas as authoritative and authorless.","challenge":"The obvious problem is choice: different scriptures claim to be God’s word and contradict each other. To pick one, it seems you must use reason or experience, so revelation cannot be the true foundation. Critics also say trusting a scripture because it claims divine authority is circular."},"god:Theist":{"summary":"You believe there is a God, or gods: a being or beings worthy of worship who made or govern the world. This is the core claim shared by most of the world’s religious believers.","reading":[{"title":"Western Concepts of God (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/god-west/"},{"title":"Monotheism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/monotheism/"},{"title":"The Epistemology of Religion (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-epistemology/"}],"idea":"Theism is the belief that reality depends on a God who knows, wills and cares, or on several such gods. Most theists believe in one God who is all-powerful, all-knowing and perfectly good, creator of everything else. This is the view of Judaism, Christianity, Islam, Sikhism and much of Hinduism, held by billions of people. Theists hold that the world exists for a purpose, that moral values are rooted in God, and that human beings can relate to God through prayer, worship and moral life. Theists disagree among themselves about how God is known (argument, experience, revelation, faith) and about God’s nature. The appeal is that theism offers answers to the biggest questions, why anything exists, why the universe is orderly, why morality matters, and whether life has meaning, in a single picture. Everyday example: saying grace before a meal.","example":"A theist faced with the question “why is there something rather than nothing?” has an answer: the universe exists because God, who exists necessarily, freely chose to create it. Leibniz posed this question directly and argued that only a being containing its own reason for existing could finally answer it.","history":"Philosophical defences of God run from Plato and Aristotle through Augustine (354 to 430), Ibn Sīnā (980 to 1037), Maimonides (1138 to 1204), Thomas Aquinas (1225 to 1274) and Rāmānuja (c. 1017 to 1137), to Descartes and Leibniz. In the 20th century, Alvin Plantinga, Richard Swinburne and others led a revival of theism in academic philosophy.","challenge":"The problem of evil is the most pressing objection: why would an all-good, all-powerful God allow so much suffering? Others include divine hiddenness, the diversity of religions, and the claim that science can explain the world without God."},"god:Logical atheist":{"summary":"You hold that there is no God because evil logically rules out an all-good, all-powerful God: if such a God existed, there would be no evil. The two cannot both be real.","reading":[{"title":"Logical Problem of Evil (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/evil-log/"},{"title":"The Problem of Evil (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/evil/"}],"idea":"The logical problem of evil claims a contradiction at the heart of traditional theism. Three statements seem to clash: God is all-powerful, God is perfectly good, and evil exists. A good being prevents evil as far as it can. An all-powerful being can prevent any evil. So if God existed, there would be no evil. But evil plainly exists, so God does not. The logical atheist thinks this is not just strong evidence but a proof, in the way that a square circle is impossible. Theists have many replies, but the logical atheist argues that none succeeds: an all-powerful God could have made free creatures who always choose rightly, for instance. The appeal is clarity: it uses only the theist’s own definition of God. Everyday example: thinking that no good parent with unlimited power would let their child be tortured.","example":"J. L. Mackie’s “Evil and Omnipotence” (1955) argued that theological beliefs are positively irrational, since parts of them contradict each other. He asked why God could not have made people who always freely choose the good. If it is possible for one person to freely do right on one occasion, he argued, it must be possible for everyone to do so always.","history":"An early version is attributed to Epicurus (341 to 270 BCE) by the Christian writer Lactantius. David Hume put it into the mouth of Philo in Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion (1779), echoing Epicurus’s old questions. The classic modern version is J. L. Mackie’s “Evil and Omnipotence” in the journal Mind (1955), developed in his book The Miracle of Theism (1982). H. J. McCloskey defended similar arguments.","challenge":"Alvin Plantinga’s free will defence, in God, Freedom, and Evil (1974), argued that it is possible that God could not create a world of free creatures who never do wrong. If that is even possible, there is no contradiction. Many philosophers, including many atheists, think this defeats the logical version."},"god:Atheist from incoherence":{"summary":"You hold that there is no God because the classical idea of God does not hang together: some features God is supposed to have rule each other out, so such a being cannot exist.","reading":[{"title":"Omniscience (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/omniscience/"},{"title":"Immutability (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/immutability/"},{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"}],"idea":"Most arguments about God weigh evidence. This one claims no evidence is needed, because the concept itself is broken, like a married bachelor. The classical God is said to be all-powerful, all-knowing, perfectly good, unchanging, timeless and free, among other things. The atheist from incoherence argues that some of these clash. An unchanging God could not know what time it is now, since what is true “now” keeps changing. A perfectly good God could not do evil, so it seems not all-powerful. A timeless being could not act at particular times. If even one pair truly conflicts, then the God described cannot exist. The appeal is that it argues on the theist’s own terms and needs no scientific premises. Everyday example: a puzzled student asking whether God can create a stone so heavy God cannot lift it.","example":"Norman Kretzmann’s paper “Omniscience and Immutability” (1966) argued that a being who knows everything must know what time it is. But what time it is changes constantly, so a being who always knows it must change constantly too. Therefore no being can be both all-knowing and unchanging, as the classical God is said to be.","history":"Norman Kretzmann’s “Omniscience and Immutability” (1966) is a landmark. Anthony Kenny’s The God of the Philosophers (1979) argued that the classical attributes cannot be held together as usually stated. Michael Martin and Ricki Monnier collected arguments of this type in The Impossibility of God (2003). Buddhist arguments against an unchanging creator raise related concerns.","challenge":"Theists reply that the attributes are misunderstood. Omnipotence means power to do anything logically possible, not to make contradictions true. Some theists give up strict unchangeability or timelessness. The atheist from incoherence must show that every reasonable version of the concept fails, not just one."},"god:Atheist (Buddhist argument)":{"summary":"You hold that there is no creator God because an unchanging, eternal creator could not cause a world in which things arise one after another. Either everything would happen at once, or other causes do the real work.","reading":[{"title":"Dharmakīrti (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dharmakiirti/"},{"title":"Vasubandhu (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/vasubandhu/"},{"title":"God and Other Ultimates (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/god-ultimates/"}],"idea":"This argument comes from classical Indian Buddhist philosophy. Hindu theists of the Nyāya school argued that the world, like a pot, must have an intelligent maker, which they called Īśvara, the Lord. Buddhists replied with a puzzle about causes. A cause produces its effect when it is complete. If God is eternal and unchanging, God is always complete, so God should produce all effects at once, forever. But things arise in a sequence. If God needs other conditions to act, like seeds needing rain, then those conditions are doing the real causal work, and God adds nothing. Buddhists held that everything real is momentary and changing, so an unchanging creator could not be real. It attacks the core idea of a first cause. Everyday example: a light switch that never moves cannot explain why the light comes on at 8 and off at 11.","example":"Śāntideva’s Bodhicaryāvatāra (chapter 9) asks a theist: if God is eternal and needs nothing else, why does he not create everything at once? If he depends on other conditions, then those conditions, not God, are really the cause. The argument targets God’s changelessness, not religion as such.","history":"Vasubandhu (around the 4th to 5th century CE) argued against a creator in his Abhidharmakośabhāṣya. Dharmakīrti (7th century) developed the argument in the Pramāṇavārttika, and Śāntideva (8th century) put it into verse. Ratnakīrti (11th century) wrote a full refutation of proofs of God. Nyāya thinkers such as Udayana (around the 10th to 11th century) wrote detailed replies. Jain and Mīmāṃsā thinkers also rejected a creator.","challenge":"Theists reply that an unchanging God can will from eternity that different things happen at different times, just as a person can decide today what will happen next week. The argument also targets only an unchanging God; a God who changes, as open and process theists believe, escapes it."},"god:Hiddenness atheist":{"summary":"You hold that there is no God mainly because a perfectly loving God would make sure sincere seekers could find God, yet many seek and never find any sign. Divine silence counts against God.","reading":[{"title":"Hiddenness of God (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/divine-hiddenness/"},{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"}],"idea":"Love seeks relationship. A perfectly loving parent would not stay hidden from a child who longs to know them. The hiddenness atheist applies this to God. If a perfectly loving God existed, God would always be open to a personal relationship with every creature able to have one, so no one who sincerely sought God would lack belief in God, unless they had resisted. Yet there are many sincere, open seekers who never find God, and many people in history who never had the chance. So, the argument concludes, there is probably no perfectly loving God. This is different from the problem of evil: it is about God’s silence, not about suffering. The appeal is that it takes God’s love, which believers emphasise, as its starting point. Everyday example: a person who prayed for years, honestly and humbly, for any sign, and received none.","example":"J. L. Schellenberg imagines a mother who could be present to her child but hides, leaving the child confused and alone, even when the child cries out for her. We would not call her perfectly loving. If God is perfectly loving, Schellenberg argues, the existence of nonresistant nonbelievers shows God does not exist.","history":"The theme is old: Isaiah 45:15 speaks of a God who hides himself, and Pascal wrote of the hidden God in his Pensées. J. L. Schellenberg’s Divine Hiddenness and Human Reason (1993) turned it into a formal argument for atheism. Daniel Howard-Snyder and Paul Moser edited Divine Hiddenness: New Essays (2002), and Schellenberg revisited the argument in The Hiddenness Argument (2015).","challenge":"Theists reply that God may stay partly hidden for good reasons: so that people can freely choose to love God rather than being overwhelmed, or so that faith and moral courage can develop. Others question whether any seeker is truly nonresistant, though critics find that claim uncharitable."},"god:Evidential atheist":{"summary":"You hold that there is no God mainly because the amount of seemingly pointless suffering makes an all-good, all-powerful God very unlikely, even if not strictly impossible.","reading":[{"title":"The Evidential Problem of Evil (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/evil-evi/"},{"title":"The Problem of Evil (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/evil/"},{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"}],"idea":"The evidential atheist accepts that God and evil might be logically compatible. Perhaps there could be reasons for allowing some suffering, such as free will or growth of character. But the world contains a staggering amount of suffering that seems to serve no purpose at all: animals dying in agony over millions of years, children with painful diseases, natural disasters. If an all-good, all-powerful God existed, we would expect far less of this. So the suffering is strong evidence against God, in the way a detective weighs clues, even if it is not a proof. The appeal is that it is modest and realistic: it does not claim certainty, only that the evidence points one way. Everyday example: watching news of an earthquake and thinking that if there were a loving God in control, this would not happen.","example":"William Rowe’s famous example: in a distant forest, lightning starts a fire. A fawn is trapped, badly burned, and suffers for several days before it dies. No human knows. Rowe argued that we can see no good this serves, and so it is reasonable to believe there is no good that could justify an all-powerful God in allowing it.","history":"David Hume’s Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion (1779, Part XI) argued that the world’s mixture of good and evil fits an indifferent cause better than a good one. William Rowe’s “The Problem of Evil and Some Varieties of Atheism” (1979) gave the classic modern form. Paul Draper’s “Pain and Pleasure” (1989) argued that suffering fits indifference better than theism.","challenge":"Skeptical theists reply that we should not expect to see God’s reasons. Stephen Wykstra (1984) argued that a mind as limited as ours seeing no purpose is weak evidence that there is none, as a toddler cannot see the point of a vaccination. Other theists say evidence for God outweighs the suffering."},"god:Debunking atheist":{"summary":"You hold that there is no God mainly because belief in God is better explained by human psychology, upbringing and culture than by God being real. Since belief would arise either way, it gives no evidence.","reading":[{"title":"Evolutionary Approaches to Religion (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-evolutionary/"},{"title":"David Hume: Religion (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/hume-rel/"},{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"}],"idea":"Debunking atheists ask not “is there a God?” but “why do people believe in God?” Their answer is that belief is best explained by natural causes. Humans are primed to see agents everywhere, like mistaking a shadow for an intruder, which helped our ancestors survive. We tend to see purpose in nature, fear death, and want protection. Children absorb the religion of their parents, and societies spread beliefs that bind groups together. If these causes would produce belief in God whether or not God exists, then the existence of belief is no evidence for God. Combined with no other good evidence, the reasonable conclusion is atheism. The appeal is that it explains a huge and puzzling fact, religion’s universality, without supposing anything supernatural. Everyday example: noticing that almost everyone holds the religion they were raised in.","example":"The ancient Greek poet Xenophanes observed that Ethiopians picture their gods as dark-skinned and flat-nosed, Thracians as blue-eyed and red-haired, and that if horses could draw, they would draw gods shaped like horses. The debunker sees in this an early insight: gods look like their believers because believers make them.","history":"Xenophanes (6th century BCE) made the first known argument of this kind. David Hume’s The Natural History of Religion (1757) traced religion to fear and hope. Ludwig Feuerbach’s The Essence of Christianity (1841) called God a projection of human ideals, and Sigmund Freud’s The Future of an Illusion (1927) called belief wish fulfilment. Today the cognitive science of religion studies these mechanisms, though some of its leading researchers, like Justin Barrett, are believers.","challenge":"The main objection is the genetic fallacy: explaining how a belief arose does not show it is false. We believe in other minds through evolved mechanisms too. Theists add that God could work through such mechanisms, and that a similar debunking could target atheism as a product of culture."},"god:Atheist from religious diversity":{"summary":"You hold that there is no God mainly because the world’s religions contradict each other and none has a better claim than the rest. Belief seems to follow birthplace rather than truth.","reading":[{"title":"Religious Diversity (Pluralism) (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-pluralism/"},{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"},{"title":"David Hume: Religion (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/hume-rel/"}],"idea":"There are thousands of religions, and they make incompatible claims: one God or many, a personal God or none, rebirth or a single life, salvation through Christ or through the Qur’an or through insight. Each tradition has sincere, intelligent, morally serious followers, and each has its scriptures, miracles and experiences. Yet most people simply adopt the religion of their family and region. This suggests that religious belief tracks culture, not truth. If no tradition has stronger evidence than the others, then even if one were true, no one could reasonably believe it was theirs. The atheist from diversity goes further: the pattern is just what we would expect if all are human creations. The appeal is fairness: it asks believers to judge their own tradition by the standard they apply to others. Everyday example: a Christian noticing they reject Zeus and Vishnu for the same reasons others reject Jesus.","example":"John Stuart Mill wrote in On Liberty (1859) that the same causes which make a person a churchman in London would have made them a Buddhist or Confucian in Peking. The atheist from diversity asks: if you had been born elsewhere, you would believe something else with equal confidence. What, then, makes your confidence reliable?","history":"Xenophanes noticed that different peoples picture different gods. David Hume argued in “Of Miracles” (1748) that the miracles of rival religions cancel each other out as evidence. Mill’s On Liberty (1859) made the birthplace point. Recent epistemologists ask whether such deep disagreement among equally informed people should lower confidence in any one religion.","challenge":"Critics note that disagreement exists everywhere, in ethics, politics and philosophy, including about atheism, yet we do not conclude no view is true. Diversity may support agnosticism more than atheism. And religions also agree on much, which pluralists take as evidence for, not against, a sacred reality."},"god:Scientific naturalist":{"summary":"You hold that there is no God because no one has shown God exists and science explains the world without God. Science is our best guide to reality, and natural causes are all there are.","reading":[{"title":"Naturalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/naturalism/"},{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"},{"title":"Bertrand Russell (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/russell/"}],"idea":"Scientific naturalists combine two ideas. First, the burden of proof: claims need evidence, and the arguments for God fail. Second, a positive picture: science has repeatedly found natural explanations for things once credited to gods, lightning, disease, the variety of life, and has never needed a supernatural cause. So the best working assumption is that nature is all there is, and that our best scientific theories describe it roughly as it is. This is not just a lack of belief but a full worldview, in which humans are part of nature, morality and meaning arise from human life, and knowledge comes from careful inquiry. The appeal is consistency: the same methods that give us medicine and spaceflight should guide our biggest beliefs. Everyday example: someone who hears of a “miracle” cure and wants the medical records first.","example":"Laplace, presenting his account of the solar system, is reported to have told Napoleon he had no need of the hypothesis of God. The story may be embellished, but it captures the naturalist idea: a good scientific explanation contains no gap where God is required, and each filled gap makes such a need less likely.","history":"The Indian Cārvāka (Lokāyata) school rejected gods and an afterlife; the Roman poet Lucretius’s On the Nature of Things (1st century BCE) explained the world through atoms. Baron d’Holbach’s The System of Nature (1770) was an early open defence. Bertrand Russell’s Why I Am Not a Christian (1927), W. V. O. Quine’s naturalized epistemology and Daniel Dennett’s Breaking the Spell (2006) are modern landmarks.","challenge":"Critics ask whether science can explain why there are laws of nature at all, or why anything exists. Others point to consciousness and moral values. Alvin Plantinga argued that if our minds are purely products of unguided evolution, we cannot trust them, including their belief in naturalism."},"god:Atheist by default":{"summary":"You hold that there is no God because no one has shown that God exists, and without good reason the right default is not to believe. The burden of proof lies with the person making the claim.","reading":[{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"},{"title":"Bertrand Russell (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/russell/"}],"idea":"Atheists by default argue that disbelief is the natural starting point. We do not believe in fairies, unicorns or invisible dragons just because no one has disproved them. Anyone claiming such things exist must provide evidence. The same applies to God: theists make the claim, so they carry the burden of proof. Having examined the arguments, cosmological, design, moral and experiential, the atheist by default finds them inadequate, and so does not believe. Some describe this as merely lacking belief; others go further and say that when good evidence would be expected and is missing, we may reasonably believe something does not exist. The appeal is that it applies a standard we use everywhere else, without special treatment for religion. Everyday example: someone who would want strong proof before believing their neighbour had seen a ghost, and asks the same for God.","example":"Bertrand Russell imagined a china teapot orbiting the Sun between Earth and Mars, too small for any telescope to see. No one could disprove it, but it would be absurd to demand that others believe it, and more absurd to treat doubt as a fault. Russell argued that the same applies to claims about God.","history":"W. K. Clifford’s “The Ethics of Belief” (1877) argued that it is wrong to believe anything on insufficient evidence. Russell’s teapot comes from his article “Is There a God?” (written 1952). Antony Flew’s “The Presumption of Atheism” (1972) argued that the burden lies with the theist. Notably, Flew later changed his mind, announcing in 2004 that he had come to believe in a deist God.","challenge":"Critics say the burden of proof applies to anyone who makes a claim, including “there is no God.” Reformed epistemologists add that belief in God may be rational without argument, like belief in other minds. And whether theistic arguments are “good enough” is itself what is in dispute."},"god:Atheist":{"summary":"You hold that there is no God or gods: the world is not made or ruled by any divine being. You may base this on suffering, divine silence, weak arguments for God or natural explanations of belief.","reading":[{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"},{"title":"The Problem of Evil (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/evil/"},{"title":"Naturalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/naturalism/"}],"idea":"Atheists believe there is no God. Most do not claim certainty, any more than they are certain there are no fairies, but they think the balance of reasons points clearly against God. Their reasons vary. Some point to the problem of evil, the vast suffering an all-good God would prevent. Some point to divine hiddenness, or to the success of science in explaining the world naturally, or to the contradictions between religions. Many hold that the arguments for God simply fail, so disbelief is the reasonable default. Atheism by itself says nothing about values or meaning; atheists find these in human relationships, reason, compassion and the natural world. The appeal is honesty: following the evidence even when the conclusion is uncomfortable. Everyday example: someone who faces a loved one’s death without belief in an afterlife but finds meaning in the life they shared.","example":"Asked what he would say if he met God after death, Bertrand Russell reportedly said he would complain that God had not given enough evidence. Whatever its exact wording, the story captures a common atheist position: disbelief is not defiance, but the response a reasonable person gives to the evidence available.","history":"Atheist and materialist views appear in ancient India in the Cārvāka school, and in Greece with thinkers like Diagoras of Melos and the Epicurean tradition, which denied the gods any role in the world. Baron d’Holbach (1723 to 1789) published one of the first openly atheist books. Modern defenders include Bertrand Russell, J. L. Mackie in The Miracle of Theism (1982), and many contemporary philosophers.","challenge":"Theists argue that atheism struggles to explain why the universe exists, why it is orderly and fine-tuned for life, why there is consciousness, and what makes moral values objective. Atheists offer answers, but critics say these leave important questions unexplained rather than answered."},"god:Pascalian wagerer":{"summary":"You think reason cannot settle whether God exists, but hold it is rational to bet on God anyway, since the possible gain is infinite and the cost of being wrong is small.","reading":[{"title":"Pascal’s Wager (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pascal-wager/"}],"idea":"Pascal’s wager starts from agnosticism: reason cannot prove or disprove God. But you cannot avoid choosing, because how you live is itself a bet. So treat it like a decision. If you bet on God and God exists, you gain infinite happiness. If you bet on God and God does not exist, you lose little: perhaps some pleasures. If you bet against God and God exists, you lose everything. If you bet against God and God does not exist, you gain little. Since an infinite gain outweighs any finite loss, the rational choice is to bet on God. What about the fact that belief cannot be chosen at will? Pascal suggested acting as believers do, going to services and practising, until belief follows. The appeal is that it turns an unanswerable question into a practical one. Everyday example: buying insurance against a disaster you think unlikely but catastrophic.","example":"Imagine a friend who says, “I honestly can’t tell if God exists.” The wagerer says: then compare outcomes. Living as a believer costs you a few hours a week and some habits. Being wrong in the other direction could cost an infinite good. Pascal would add that living a faithful life also makes you honest, grateful and generous, so you lose less than you think.","history":"Blaise Pascal (1623 to 1662) wrote the wager in notes found after his death and published as the Pensées (1670). Earlier, the Christian writer Arnobius (around 300 CE) made a somewhat similar point about the prudence of belief. The wager is now seen as a founding example of decision theory, discussed by philosophers such as Ian Hacking and Nicholas Rescher.","challenge":"The “many gods” objection: if you bet on the wrong God, you may lose too, and infinitely many gods could be imagined. Denis Diderot noted that a Muslim leader could reason the same way. Others ask whether a God would reward belief adopted for profit."},"god:Agnostic open to faith":{"summary":"You think the evidence cannot settle whether God exists, but hold that when evidence is indecisive on a question that matters for life, it is reasonable to let your hopes and commitments decide.","reading":[{"title":"William James (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/james/"},{"title":"Faith (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/faith/"}],"idea":"This view starts from the same place as Pascal’s wager: reason alone cannot settle whether God exists. But it rejects treating faith as a cold bet for a reward. Instead, it says that some questions are too important to leave open, and refusing to decide is itself a decision. When a choice is real to you, unavoidable and momentous, and the evidence cannot settle it, it is legitimate to let your passions, hopes and sense of what makes life worth living decide. This leaves room for faith without proof, but it does not require faith either: the person may decide to believe, or keep the door open while trying out a religious life. The appeal is that it respects both honesty about evidence and the reality that people must live by some answer. Everyday example: deciding to trust a new friend before you have proof they are trustworthy.","example":"William James pointed out that in some cases belief helps create its own evidence. If you never trust anyone until they have proven themselves, you may never form the friendships that would have shown them trustworthy. Likewise, he suggested, if there is a God who responds to openness, waiting for proof might be exactly what keeps you from finding it.","history":"William James gave the classic statement in “The Will to Believe” (1896), a lecture to philosophy clubs at Yale and Brown. He was answering W. K. Clifford’s claim, in “The Ethics of Belief” (1877), that it is always wrong to believe on insufficient evidence. James developed related ideas in The Varieties of Religious Experience (1902) and Pragmatism (1907).","challenge":"Critics say this licenses wishful thinking: if hopes can decide religious questions, why not any question we care about? Clifford’s worry is that beliefs formed this way spread and harm others. Also, many people feel James’s method could equally justify believing in many different gods."},"god:Strong agnostic":{"summary":"You hold that whether God exists is beyond what human minds and evidence can ever reach. Neither believer nor atheist can know, because the question lies outside the limits of human knowledge.","reading":[{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"}],"idea":"Ordinary agnostics say the evidence is currently balanced or unclear. Strong agnostics make a bolder claim: the question cannot be settled by humans at all, now or ever. Our knowledge comes through our senses and reasoning, which evolved to deal with the world around us. A God, if one exists, would be beyond space, time and the ordinary causes we can study. So arguments for and against God reach beyond where our concepts can safely go. This is not indifference: strong agnostics often care deeply about the question, but think intellectual honesty requires admitting its limits. The appeal is humility. It avoids the overconfidence that critics see on both sides of the debate. Everyday example: someone who, asked whether God exists, answers seriously, “No one can know that, including me.”","example":"The Nāsadīya Sūkta, the hymn of creation in the Rig Veda (10.129), asks how the world began and ends with doubt: who really knows, and perhaps even the one who oversees it from the highest heaven does not know. A strong agnostic hears in it an ancient recognition that the deepest question may be beyond any knower.","history":"Protagoras (5th century BCE) wrote that he could not know whether the gods exist, because of the obscurity of the subject and the brevity of human life. Immanuel Kant’s Critique of Pure Reason (1781) argued that speculative reason can neither prove nor disprove God, though he then defended moral faith. Thomas Huxley coined the word “agnostic” in 1869, explaining it in his essay “Agnosticism” (1889).","challenge":"Critics say strong agnosticism claims to know something big: that the question can never be answered. How can we know the limits of our knowledge so precisely? Some also argue the word “God” covers many claims, some of which may be testable, so a blanket verdict is too broad."},"god:Religious non-realist":{"summary":"You think religions are ways of living rather than rival theories about the world. Religious language expresses commitments, values and pictures that guide life, not factual claims about a supernatural being.","reading":[{"title":"Religious Language (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-language/"},{"title":"Ludwig Wittgenstein (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wittgenstein/"}],"idea":"Religious non-realists say we misunderstand religion when we treat it as a set of hypotheses, like scientific theories about hidden entities. Religious words work differently. When a believer prays, confesses sins or speaks of God’s love, they are not mainly reporting facts but taking part in a form of life: expressing gratitude, commitment, hope and moral seriousness. On this view, asking “does God exist?” the way we ask whether a planet exists misses the point. That is why the non-realist does not lean toward believing or denying God as a fact. Religions do not compete as rival theories, but as different ways of shaping a life. The appeal is that it explains why religious arguments rarely change minds, and lets one take part in religious life without accepting supernatural claims. Everyday example: someone who sings hymns and prays sincerely but would not call God an object in the universe.","example":"In his Lectures on Religious Belief, Wittgenstein discusses someone who believes in a Last Judgment. He says he would not simply contradict that person, because the belief does not work like a forecast based on evidence. It shapes how the person lives and sees everything. The disagreement is less about a fact than about a whole way of life.","history":"Wittgenstein’s Lectures on Religious Belief, from notes taken around 1938, were published in 1966. R. B. Braithwaite’s “An Empiricist’s View of the Nature of Religious Belief” (1955) treated religious claims as expressing moral commitments. D. Z. Phillips developed a Wittgensteinian approach in The Concept of Prayer (1965) and later works. Don Cupitt’s Taking Leave of God (1980) inspired the Sea of Faith movement.","challenge":"The main objection is that most believers do mean their claims literally. When they say God exists or Jesus rose, they think they are stating facts. Critics say non-realism therefore redescribes religion into something its practitioners would not recognise, and is closer to atheism than it admits."},"god:Agnostic":{"summary":"You neither believe nor disbelieve in God, because the reasons seem balanced, too weak on both sides, or beyond what we can know. This is a considered judgment, not mere indecision.","reading":[{"title":"Atheism and Agnosticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/atheism-agnosticism/"},{"title":"Pascal’s Wager (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pascal-wager/"}],"idea":"Agnostics suspend judgment on whether God exists. They have weighed the arguments, cosmological arguments, the problem of evil, religious experience, scientific explanation, and found that they do not settle the matter. Some agnostics think the evidence on both sides is strong and roughly cancels out. Others think all the arguments are weak. Others think the question is beyond human knowledge altogether. What unites them is the conviction that confident belief and confident disbelief both go beyond the evidence. Agnosticism is not the same as not caring: many agnostics find the question deeply important and keep investigating. The appeal is intellectual honesty and humility, refusing to claim more than one knows on a question where brilliant people disagree. Everyday example: a person who attends a friend’s religious service respectfully and also enjoys an atheist’s arguments, finding neither fully convincing.","example":"Charles Darwin wrote in an 1879 letter that “agnostic” would be the more correct description of his state of mind, adding that he had never been an atheist in the sense of denying God’s existence. A modern agnostic, asked to choose sides, might answer similarly: I do not know, and I think honesty requires saying so.","history":"Protagoras (5th century BCE) professed not knowing whether the gods exist. Thomas Huxley coined “agnostic” in 1869. Robert Ingersoll (1833 to 1899) was nicknamed “the Great Agnostic.” Charles Darwin described himself as agnostic. Anthony Kenny, in The Unknown God (2004) and What I Believe (2006), defended agnosticism as the most reasonable position.","challenge":"Critics from both sides say agnosticism is unstable. Theists argue that some evidence favours God, and Pascal said you must choose by how you live. Atheists argue that lack of evidence for God is itself reason to disbelieve, as with any unsupported claim, so suspending judgment is too generous."},"god:Theological noncognitivist":{"summary":"You hold that talk of God fails to make a genuine factual claim: nothing anyone could experience would count for or against it, or the word “God” has no clear meaning. So the question cannot be answered.","reading":[{"title":"Religious Language (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religious-language/"},{"title":"Logical Empiricism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logical-empiricism/"},{"title":"Alfred Jules Ayer (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ayer/"}],"idea":"Most people treat “Does God exist?” as a question with a true answer: yes or no. Theological noncognitivists disagree. They argue that a sentence only states a fact if some possible experience could show it true or false. “There is a cat on the mat” passes; you can look. But “God exists,” as usually meant, describes a being beyond space, time and observation, compatible with any possible experience. If nothing could ever count for or against it, it says nothing factual at all. So the theist and the atheist are not really disagreeing about a fact. Some noncognitivists simply say the word “God” has never been clearly defined. The appeal is that it seems to explain why the debate never ends. Everyday example: a student who, asked whether God exists, first asks, “What exactly do you mean by God?”","example":"Antony Flew’s parable of the invisible gardener (1950): two explorers find a clearing with flowers and weeds. One says a gardener tends it. They watch, set up electric fences, bring dogs; no gardener appears. The believer says he is invisible, intangible, silent. The skeptic asks how such a gardener differs from no gardener at all.","history":"Rudolf Carnap’s “The Elimination of Metaphysics Through Logical Analysis of Language” (1932) and A. J. Ayer’s Language, Truth and Logic (1936) argued that statements about God are literally meaningless. Antony Flew’s “Theology and Falsification” (1950) adapted a parable from John Wisdom’s paper “Gods” (1944). The related position is sometimes called “ignosticism,” a term associated with the rabbi Sherwin Wine.","challenge":"The verification principle seems to fail its own test: no experience could verify the statement that only verifiable statements are meaningful. John Hick replied with “eschatological verification”: God’s existence could be confirmed after death. Most philosophers now reject strict verificationism, which weakens the view."},"life:Schopenhauerian pessimist":{"summary":"Life has no real point and holds more suffering than good, so starting a new life is a bad bet for the child; the fitting response is compassion and quieting desire, not despair.","reading":[{"title":"Arthur Schopenhauer","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/schopenhauer/"},{"title":"Anti-Natalism","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/anti-natalism/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"}],"idea":"This view joins three claims. Nothing gives human life a real point. Life as a whole contains more striving and pain than satisfaction. So bringing a new person into existence is usually, or always, a harm to that person. The pessimist’s reasoning is that desire is endless: we want, get, and want again, and satisfaction is brief while lack is constant. The practical conclusion is not cruelty or despair but gentleness. If all living beings suffer, we owe one another compassion. Schopenhauer also found real relief in art, in calm contemplation and in loosening one’s grip on desire. The view is attractive to people struck by how much suffering the world holds. Think of how quickly the joy of a new phone fades, while a toothache holds your whole attention. The question is a philosophical one about how to weigh lives.","example":"Schopenhauer suggested a test: compare the pleasure of an animal eating another with the pain of the animal being eaten. The two do not balance. A Schopenhauerian pessimist applies the same weighing to human lives and concludes that starting a life is a gamble with poor odds for the one who must live it. He insisted that the answer lies in compassion, not in ending a life.","history":"Arthur Schopenhauer’s The World as Will and Representation (1818, expanded 1844) and Parerga and Paralipomena (1851) set out the view. He argued that ending one’s life affirms the will rather than escaping it, and grounded ethics in compassion. Giacomo Leopardi, Eduard von Hartmann and Peter Wessel Zapffe’s “The Last Messiah” (1933) developed pessimism. David Benatar’s Better Never to Have Been (2006) revived the argument against procreation.","challenge":"Most people, asked about their own lives, judge them worth having. Pessimists reply that such judgments are biased by optimism, but this risks dismissing all contrary evidence. Critics also ask how pleasure and pain can be weighed on one scale, and whether meaning, love and achievement are left out of the tally."},"life:Anti-natalist":{"summary":"Coming into existence is always a harm, yet death is also bad for those who exist; a life not worth starting can still be well worth continuing.","reading":[{"title":"Anti-Natalism","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/anti-natalism/"},{"title":"Parenthood and Procreation","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/parenthood/"},{"title":"Death","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"}],"idea":"Anti-natalists argue that being born is always a harm, however good the life. The key is an asymmetry. If a person exists, their pains are bad and their pleasures good. If they never exist, the absence of pain is still good, even though no one enjoys it, but the absence of pleasure is not bad, since no one is deprived. So never existing comes out ahead. Crucially, this view also holds that death is bad for those who already exist, and that death is probably the end. These fit together through a distinction: a life not worth starting can still be well worth continuing. Once someone exists, they have interests, plans and relationships, and losing them is a real harm. The claim concerns whether to create new people, not how existing people should regard their own lives.","example":"Benatar sets out four boxes. If someone exists: (1) pain, which is bad; (2) pleasure, which is good. If they never exist: (3) no pain, which is good; (4) no pleasure, which is not bad. Comparing the columns, he concludes that coming into existence is always a harm. He then argues separately that people already alive have strong interests in continuing to live.","history":"David Benatar’s Better Never to Have Been (2006) gave the asymmetry argument its modern form, and his The Human Predicament (2017) argued that death is also bad for us, so that existence is a predicament. A chorus in Sophocles’ Oedipus at Colonus voiced the old thought that not to be born is best. Schopenhauer and Zapffe were earlier pessimists about procreation.","challenge":"Critics doubt the asymmetry: why is absent pain “good” when there is no one for whom it is good, while absent pleasure is “not bad”? The view also implies that humanity should gradually end by not having children, which most find a reason to reject a premise. Defenders reply that intuitions here are biased."},"life:Buddhist about death":{"summary":"Death is not the loss of a lasting self, since there never was one, only a changing stream of experiences; the fitting response is to accept impermanence without clinging.","reading":[{"title":"Buddha","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/buddha/"},{"title":"Mind in Indian Buddhist Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mind-indian-buddhism/"},{"title":"Japanese Zen Buddhist Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/japanese-zen/"}],"idea":"Buddhists teach that everything conditioned is impermanent, and that the self is no exception. What we call “I” is a stream of bodily processes, feelings, perceptions, intentions and awareness, changing moment by moment, with no fixed core underneath. Fear of death assumes there is a solid self that death will destroy. Seeing clearly that there is no such self loosens that fear. This does not mean nothing matters; suffering and its causes are real, and compassion is central. But clinging, to possessions, to people, to one’s own continuation, is the root of suffering, and letting go brings peace. The view is attractive because it offers a way to face death that does not depend on believing in an immortal soul. You are already not the same person you were at five years old, yet that change did not feel like dying.","example":"In a traditional story, Kisa Gotami, grieving her dead child, begs the Buddha for medicine. He asks her to bring a mustard seed from a house where no one has died. She searches the village and finds no such house. Her grief does not vanish, but she understands that death touches everyone, and she lets go of the demand that her loss be undone.","history":"The Buddha (5th century BCE, by most estimates) taught impermanence (anicca), no-self (anattā) and suffering (dukkha). The Questions of King Milinda illustrates no-self with a chariot that is nothing over and above its parts. The Japanese Zen master Dōgen (1200 to 1253) wrote on birth and death in his Shōbōgenzō. Derek Parfit’s Reasons and Persons (1984) defended a similar view of personal identity.","challenge":"If there is no self, critics ask, who suffers, who is reborn and why should anyone care about their future? Buddhist traditions offer detailed answers about continuity without a self. Others object that losing a future of good experiences still seems a loss, even if the stream has no fixed owner."},"life:Daoist about death":{"summary":"Death is part of nature’s constant transformation, like autumn following summer, and so is no evil; the wise accept change rather than cling to one form of existence.","reading":[{"title":"Zhuangzi","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/zhuangzi/"},{"title":"Death","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"}],"idea":"Daoists see the world as a ceaseless process of change, the Dao, in which things arise, transform and pass into other things. Life and death are phases of this process, as natural as the seasons. To cling to life and dread death is to treat one stage of the process as if it were the whole, and to fight against the way things are. The wise person moves with change, accepting whatever form comes next. This acceptance is not resignation but a kind of freedom: one stops struggling against what cannot be otherwise. The view is attractive because it offers calm without needing an afterlife or a fixed self. It also has a playful spirit. Daoist stories treat death with wonder and humour rather than fear. Consider a leaf falling in autumn: we do not usually call it a tragedy, but part of the tree’s cycle.","example":"When Zhuangzi’s wife died, a friend found him drumming on a tub and singing. Zhuangzi explained that he had grieved at first, then reflected that she had once had no life, no form and no breath; these came through change, and now change had carried her on, like the passing of the seasons. To keep wailing, he said, would show he did not understand fate.","history":"The Zhuangzi (4th to 3rd centuries BCE), chapters 6 and 18, contains the classic stories, including the dying Master Lai, who wonders cheerfully whether the Creator will make him into a rat’s liver or an insect’s arm next. The Daodejing, attributed to Laozi, frames life and death within the Dao. Later Daoist and Chan Buddhist writers drew on these ideas.","challenge":"Many things are natural yet seem bad, such as disease or a child’s early death, so “natural” may not mean “not an evil”. Critics also worry that serene acceptance can seem cold toward the grief of losing someone. Zhuangzi’s own initial grief suggests the view aims at transforming sorrow, not denying it."},"life:Stoic about death":{"summary":"Death is a natural event in a rational cosmos and so no evil, and keeping it in mind daily helps us live with focus and accept it calmly when it comes.","reading":[{"title":"Stoicism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/stoicism/"},{"title":"Death","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"}],"idea":"Stoics teach that the only true good is virtue and the only true evil is vice. Everything else, including health, wealth and length of life, is “indifferent”: some indifferents are preferable, but none can make a person good or bad. Death, as a natural event in an orderly universe, is therefore no evil. What makes it seem terrible is our judgment about it. Stoics practised remembering death regularly. This was not meant to be gloomy. Keeping mortality in view cuts away trivial worries, makes us value the present and readies us to accept loss without being shattered. The view is attractive because it offers a practical training for fear, not just an argument. Think of how a near miss in traffic can suddenly make petty quarrels seem pointless. The Stoic tries to keep that clarity without needing the scare.","example":"Epictetus taught that people are disturbed not by things but by their judgments about things: death is not terrible, but the judgment that it is terrible is. He advised keeping death before one’s eyes each day, so that one would never think anything low or desire anything excessively. Marcus Aurelius urged himself, in the Meditations, to do each act as if it were his last.","history":"Seneca’s Letters to Lucilius and On the Shortness of Life (1st century CE), Epictetus’s Discourses and Handbook (early 2nd century), and Marcus Aurelius’s Meditations (written around 170 to 180) are the main sources. They developed ideas from the earlier Greek Stoics Zeno and Chrysippus, who held that nature is rational and that virtue is sufficient for happiness.","challenge":"Critics doubt that losing one’s life, or a loved one, is truly “indifferent”; the claim can seem to deny real loss. And “natural” is not obviously “not bad”. Defenders reply that Stoics allowed grief’s first pangs and aimed at accepting loss, not at feeling nothing."},"life:Lucretian":{"summary":"Death is not bad because no one is left to be harmed, and the endless time after death mirrors the endless time before birth, which never troubled anyone.","reading":[{"title":"Lucretius","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lucretius/"},{"title":"Death","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"},{"title":"Epicurus","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epicurus/"}],"idea":"Lucretians combine two arguments. First, from Epicurus: to be harmed, you must exist. Once dead, you do not exist, so nothing can be bad for you. Death is not an experience you will have; it is the end of all experience. Second, the symmetry argument: think about the billions of years before you were born. You were not there, and that was not bad for you. Death is simply the mirror image, another stretch of time without you. If the first does not trouble you, consistency says the second should not either. The view is attractive because it uses plain reasoning to address a deep fear. It does not ask you to believe anything about souls or an afterlife. It asks you to notice that you already accept one long absence calmly, and to treat the other the same way.","example":"In On the Nature of Things, Lucretius imagines Nature herself speaking to a person who complains about dying. If your life has been good, she asks, why not leave like a guest who has had enough at a banquet? And if it has been bad, why want more of it? Then he points to the time before birth as a mirror that shows us what death will be.","history":"Lucretius (1st century BCE) set out these arguments in Book III of On the Nature of Things (De Rerum Natura), a long Latin poem defending Epicurus’s philosophy. Epicurus (341 to 270 BCE) had argued that death is nothing to us. The symmetry argument was revived and debated in the 20th century by Thomas Nagel, Frederik Kaufman and others.","challenge":"Thomas Nagel replied that the two periods are not alike. You could not have been born much earlier and still be you, but you could die later and have more of your own life. So death takes something from you that prenatal nonexistence never could. Lucretians argue the asymmetry reflects bias, not reason."},"life:Epicurean":{"summary":"Only experience, pleasure and the absence of pain, makes a life go well, so death, which ends all experience, cannot harm the one who dies, and neither can betrayals never discovered.","reading":[{"title":"Epicurus","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epicurus/"},{"title":"Hedonism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hedonism/"},{"title":"Well-Being","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/well-being/"}],"idea":"Epicureans hold a simple theory of well-being: a life goes well to the extent that it contains pleasure and freedom from pain, especially calm of mind. Two striking conclusions follow. First, things that never affect your experience cannot affect how your life goes. A man deceived all his life by a cheating wife and mocking friends, who never finds out, had as good a life as one where the love was real, because every moment felt the same. Second, death cannot harm you, since being dead involves no experience at all. These fit tightly: if only experience matters, both secret betrayal and death are nothing to the person concerned. The view is attractive because it is clear and consistent, and because it offers relief from fear. Epicurus thought the good life is easy to get: friendship, simple food and freedom from anxiety.","example":"Thomas Nagel used exactly the betrayed husband case to argue against Epicureans: most people think the deceived man has been wronged and that his life went worse, even though he never knew. An Epicurean would bite the bullet and say that his life, for him, went just as well. Our sense that it went badly comes from imagining how we would feel if we found out.","history":"Epicurus (341 to 270 BCE) set out the view in his Letter to Menoeceus and Principal Doctrines; Lucretius developed it in On the Nature of Things (1st century BCE). Later hedonists such as Jeremy Bentham shared the experience-based theory of well-being. Thomas Nagel’s “Death” (1970) and Robert Nozick’s experience machine in Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974) are the classic challenges.","challenge":"Nozick asked whether you would plug into a machine giving you any experiences you like, forever. Most people say no, because they want to really do things and really be loved, not just feel as if they are. If so, more than experience matters for how a life goes, and death may harm us after all."},"life:Epicurean about death":{"summary":"Death cannot harm the dead, since they no longer exist, and once we understand this the fear of death goes away, leaving us free to enjoy the life we have.","reading":[{"title":"Epicurus","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epicurus/"},{"title":"Death","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"}],"idea":"This view treats the fear of death as a problem that philosophy can cure. The argument is short. Good and bad lie in sensation. Death is the end of sensation. So death is nothing to us: while we exist, death is not here, and when death is here, we do not exist. The point is not merely theoretical. Epicurus thought fear of death poisons life, driving people to greed, ambition and superstition. Once you see that being dead cannot hurt you, the fear loosens, and you can enjoy simple pleasures and friendships in peace. The view is attractive because it promises calm through understanding rather than through hope in an afterlife. It is like realising that a monster you feared in the dark was only a coat on a hook: the fear goes once you see what is really there.","example":"On his last day, according to Diogenes Laertius, Epicurus wrote to a friend that it was a blessed day, even though he was in great pain from illness, because the memory of their philosophical conversations outweighed it. Later Epicureans summed up the teaching in a “fourfold remedy”: do not fear the gods, do not worry about death, the good is easy to get, and the terrible is easy to endure.","history":"Epicurus (341 to 270 BCE) gave the argument in his Letter to Menoeceus and in Principal Doctrines 2. Lucretius developed it in On the Nature of Things (1st century BCE). The “fourfold remedy” is preserved in the writings of Philodemus (1st century BCE). The argument remains central to the modern philosophy of death.","challenge":"Critics distinguish fearing being dead from regretting what death takes away. Even if being dead is nothing, death may deprive us of future goods we would otherwise have had. Thomas Nagel argued that a harm need not be felt to be real, so the argument may calm one fear while missing the real loss."},"life:Life-plan theorist about death":{"summary":"Death is bad when it cuts off a life before its central plans and hopes are fulfilled, and living forever would eventually become empty as those deep desires ran out.","reading":[{"title":"Bernard Williams","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/williams-bernard/"},{"title":"Death","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"}],"idea":"This view ties the badness of death to what gives us reasons to go on living: our deep projects, loves and hopes. Bernard Williams called these “categorical desires”, desires that are not conditional on staying alive but give us a reason to stay alive. When death comes before such projects are completed, it robs us of something we care about deeply, and that is when it is bad. But the same idea suggests that endless life would not be good. Over infinite time, a person with a fixed character would eventually do everything that mattered to them, and life would become boring and empty. So death is bad when too early, but mortality is not a curse. The view is attractive because it links death to the shape of a whole life, the way a story can end too soon but would not be improved by never ending.","example":"In Karel Čapek’s play The Makropulos Affair (1922), Elina Makropulos has taken an elixir that has kept her alive for 342 years. She has become cold and bored, with nothing left that matters to her, and finally refuses to take the elixir again. Williams used her case to argue that immortality, for creatures like us, would end in tedium.","history":"Bernard Williams’s essay “The Makropulos Case: Reflections on the Tedium of Immortality” (1973), reprinted in Problems of the Self, is the classic source. It drew on Čapek’s play and Leoš Janáček’s opera based on it (1926). Simone de Beauvoir’s novel All Men Are Mortal (1946) explored the same theme. Samuel Scheffler’s Death and the Afterlife (2013) continues the debate.","challenge":"John Martin Fischer argued that many goods, like friendship, music or a good meal, can be enjoyed again and again without growing stale, so immortality need not be boring. Others ask why death is not bad even after one’s projects are complete, if more good life remained."},"life:Deprivationist about death":{"summary":"Death is bad because it takes away good life one would otherwise have had, at any age; this differs from not having been born earlier, and endless healthy life would be good.","reading":[{"title":"Death","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"},{"title":"Well-Being","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/well-being/"}],"idea":"Deprivationists explain the badness of death simply: death is bad for you because it deprives you of the good things your life would have contained if you had lived on. It need not be felt to be bad. You can be harmed by losses you never experience, just as you can be harmed by betrayal you never discover. This applies at any age: dying at eighty is a loss if more good years were possible. The view also answers the Lucretian symmetry argument. Prenatal nonexistence is not a loss, because you could not have been born much earlier and still be you, whereas you could have lived longer. And if more good life is always better, then never-ending healthy life would be desirable, since there would always be more to learn, love and do. The view is attractive because it matches the ordinary sense that dying young is tragic.","example":"Nagel compared death to the case of an intelligent man who suffers a brain injury and is left with the mind of a contented infant. He is not unhappy, yet most people think something terrible has happened to him. Death, Nagel argued, is a similar loss of possibilities, bad for the person even though they never experience its badness.","history":"Thomas Nagel’s essay “Death” (1970), reprinted in Mortal Questions (1979), is the classic statement. Fred Feldman’s Confrontations with the Reaper (1992) and Ben Bradley’s Well-Being and Death (2009) developed the deprivation account in detail. John Martin Fischer defended the desirability of immortality against Bernard Williams’s claim that endless life would become tedious. It is widely treated as the leading reply to the Epicurean argument.","challenge":"Epicureans ask when death is bad for you: not while you are alive, since it has not happened, and not after, since you are gone. Deprivationists must explain the timing. Williams’s worry about the tedium of immortality also challenges the claim that more life is always better."},"life:Extreme supernaturalist":{"summary":"Meaning comes from God, and without God and eternity no life could have any meaning, since death would erase everything we do.","reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"Augustine of Hippo","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/augustine/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}],"idea":"Extreme supernaturalists hold that a meaningful life requires God, and usually also life after death. Two reasons are common. First, purpose: a life has meaning by fulfilling a purpose, and only a creator can give human life a purpose that is not arbitrary. Second, permanence: if everything ends in death and the universe eventually goes cold, all our achievements and loves are wiped out, and nothing we do makes a lasting difference. Only eternity can rescue them. Without God, then, nothing would truly matter. The view is attractive because it takes the question of meaning with full seriousness and refuses easy comfort. Many people, at moments of loss or crisis, feel exactly this pull: that if nothing lasts, nothing counts. Think of building a sandcastle as the tide comes in. The view says that, without God, all of human history is that castle.","example":"In A Confession, Tolstoy retells an Eastern fable. A traveller fleeing a beast falls into a well with a dragon at the bottom. He clings to a branch while two mice, one white and one black, gnaw at it, and he licks honey from its leaves. Tolstoy saw his own life this way, the honey of family and fame unable to hide the coming end, until he found meaning in faith.","history":"Augustine’s Confessions (around 397 to 400) begins by saying that our heart is restless until it rests in God. Blaise Pascal’s Pensées (published 1670) explored human misery without God. Leo Tolstoy’s A Confession (1882) describes a crisis of meaning resolved by faith. William Lane Craig’s essay “The Absurdity of Life without God” is a modern defence.","challenge":"Critics ask why endless existence would supply meaning if finite existence does not: if a short life is pointless, an infinitely long one might be infinitely pointless. Kurt Baier also argued that a purpose assigned by someone else could make us like tools. And many atheists report finding real meaning in love and work."},"life:Moderate supernaturalist":{"summary":"God is the deepest source of meaning, but love, work and goodness would still give lives real meaning even without God; faith adds a further, deeper significance.","reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}],"idea":"Moderate supernaturalists hold a middle position. They believe God exists and that a relationship with God, or a purpose God gives, offers the greatest meaning a life can have. But they deny that without God everything would be pointless. Loving a child, helping a stranger, discovering a truth or making something beautiful matter on their own. God and eternity add a further layer: they connect these goods to a larger story, guarantee that they are not lost, and raise their significance. The view is attractive because it lets believers take their faith seriously while recognising the real meaning in the lives of people who do not share it. It also avoids the claim that a loving, generous atheist lives a meaningless life. Compare a song that is beautiful on its own, but gains depth when you learn the story behind it.","example":"Imagine a nurse who spends her life caring for the dying. A moderate supernaturalist would say her life is plainly meaningful even if there is no God: the comfort she gives is real. But if God exists, her work also takes part in a divine purpose and its effects are not lost at death, which makes it more meaningful still.","history":"The label comes from Thaddeus Metz’s survey of theories of life’s meaning, where moderate supernaturalism is contrasted with the extreme view. Richard Swinburne, in works such as The Existence of God (1979), defends a theism in which human goodness has its own value within a created world. John Cottingham’s On the Meaning of Life (2003) argues that a religious framework gives meaning its fullest grounding.","challenge":"Extreme supernaturalists ask how love and work can be meaningful without God if death erases them. Naturalists ask what God adds if these goods already matter. The moderate must explain exactly what the extra layer of meaning consists in, without making it either essential or idle."},"life:Theist about life and death":{"summary":"Life’s meaning comes from God, and the person survives death, either as an immortal soul or through the resurrection of body and soul at the end of time.","reading":[{"title":"Thomas Aquinas","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aquinas/"},{"title":"Heaven and Hell in Christian Thought","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/heaven-hell/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"}],"idea":"This view joins a claim about meaning to a claim about death. Meaning comes from God: from a purpose God gives, or from a relationship with God that is the highest good a person can know. And death is not the end. Some believers hold that the soul continues without the body; others, following the main teaching of Judaism, Christianity and Islam, expect the resurrection of the whole person, body and soul, at the end of time. Many hold both, with the soul awaiting reunion with a renewed body. The two claims fit, because a relationship with an eternal God makes most sense if it can last beyond death. The view is attractive because it answers the fear of death and the hope for justice: wrongs left unrighted in this life can be set right. For billions of people it frames how they understand both grief and hope.","example":"Thomas Aquinas argued that the human soul is the form of the body, so a soul without its body is incomplete. After death it survives, but human fulfilment awaits the resurrection, when soul and body are reunited and the person enjoys the direct vision of God. A believer at a funeral might draw on exactly this hope.","history":"Plato’s Phaedo argued for the soul’s survival without the body. Thomas Aquinas, in the Summa Theologiae (1265 to 1274), held that happiness lies in the vision of God and that the soul awaits the body’s resurrection. Islamic thinkers such as al-Ghazali defended bodily resurrection. Richard Swinburne’s The Evolution of the Soul (1986) and Peter van Inwagen’s “The Possibility of Resurrection” (1978) are modern discussions.","challenge":"Neuroscience shows that memory, personality and thought depend closely on the brain, which makes survival without it hard to explain. Resurrection faces a puzzle of identity: what makes the resurrected person you rather than a perfect copy? Believers offer answers, such as a continuing soul, but critics find them unconvincing."},"life:Absurdist":{"summary":"The universe offers no meaning to match our hunger for it, and the right response is revolt: living fully and lucidly, without false hope and without giving up.","reading":[{"title":"Albert Camus","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/camus/"},{"title":"Albert Camus (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/camus/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"}],"idea":"Absurdists start from a clash. Humans long for meaning, order and clarity; the universe is silent and indifferent. The absurd is neither in us nor in the world, but in their confrontation. Camus considered three responses. One could escape into religious hope, which he called “philosophical suicide” because it leaps beyond what we know. One could give up on life. Or one could revolt: keep the clash in view and live as fully and passionately as possible anyway. Camus rejected the first two and argued firmly for the third, saying that life can be lived all the better for having no given meaning. Revolt brings freedom, since no rules are handed down, and passion, since every experience counts. The view is attractive because it is honest without being bleak. Think of an athlete who plays wholeheartedly although the game has no point beyond itself.","example":"Camus retold the myth of Sisyphus, condemned by the gods to push a rock up a hill forever, only to watch it roll down. Sisyphus knows his task is futile, yet he walks back down with a clear mind, owning his fate. Camus concludes that the struggle itself is enough to fill a heart, and that we must imagine Sisyphus happy.","history":"Albert Camus set out the view in The Myth of Sisyphus (1942), alongside his novel The Stranger (1942). The Plague (1947), where Dr Rieux keeps fighting an epidemic without hope of final victory, and The Rebel (1951) developed revolt into solidarity with others. Camus drew on Dostoevsky, Kierkegaard and Nietzsche, though he rejected Kierkegaard’s leap of faith.","challenge":"If nothing has meaning, why is revolt better than any other response? Camus seems to smuggle in values such as honesty, freedom and passion. Thomas Nagel argued that heroic defiance takes our situation too seriously, calling Camus’s response romantic and slightly self-pitying, and suggested irony instead."},"life:Ironic absurdist":{"summary":"Nothing gives life an ultimate point, and the fitting response is irony: keep caring about your life while smiling at how seriously you take yourself.","reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"},{"title":"Death","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/"}],"idea":"Ironic absurdists locate the absurd inside us. We live with great seriousness: we worry about careers, appearances and plans. But we can also step back and see ourselves from outside, as one creature among billions on a small planet, and from there all that seriousness looks arbitrary. We cannot stop caring, and we cannot stop stepping back. The absurd is the collision between the two views. The ironic absurdist does not fight this like a hero or sink into despair. They keep living and caring, while seeing the comedy in it, much as you can be absorbed in a board game while knowing it is only a game. The view is attractive because it is calm and humane. It takes absurdity as a sign of something good about us, our capacity for reflection, rather than as a tragedy.","example":"Nagel noted that people often say nothing matters because in a million years nothing we do now will matter. But, he replied, if that were so, then nothing in a million years matters now either, including the fact that nothing now will matter then. The real source of the absurd is not size or time but our ability to step back, which would remain even in an endless, cosmic life.","history":"Thomas Nagel’s essay “The Absurd” (1971), reprinted in Mortal Questions (1979), argued that absurdity comes from the clash between the seriousness of our lives and the detached view we can take of them. He recommended irony over Camus’s defiance. His later book The View from Nowhere (1986) explored the tension between subjective and objective standpoints more fully.","challenge":"Some worry that irony can slide into detachment, so that one stops caring properly about people and causes that deserve full seriousness. Others question whether the detached view is privileged at all: perhaps seeing ourselves from nowhere is not seeing more truly, just seeing less, so it need not unsettle us."},"life:Pessimist":{"summary":"Life has no ultimate meaning and suffering outweighs satisfaction, so the wise response is to expect little, want less, find rest in art and show compassion to fellow sufferers.","reading":[{"title":"Arthur Schopenhauer","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/schopenhauer/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"Nihilism","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/nihilism/"}],"idea":"Pessimists make two claims. Nothing gives life an ultimate point, and life as lived contains more pain and frustration than good. Desire never rests: we want, get, and quickly want again, while suffering is vivid and lasting. Unlike some who share these premises, the pessimist draws a gentle practical lesson. If striving brings suffering, strive less. Lower expectations, loosen your hold on desire, and find quiet in art, nature and contemplation. Above all, treat others with compassion, since they suffer as you do. Schopenhauer explicitly rejected ending one’s life as a response, arguing it does not escape the problem he described. The view is attractive to people who find cheerful philosophies unconvincing, and it can be oddly consoling: if suffering is the common lot, it is not your personal failure. The everyday version is simply not expecting the world to be fair.","example":"Schopenhauer compared human life to a pendulum swinging between pain and boredom. When we lack what we want, we suffer; when we get it, we soon grow bored, and new wants arise. A pessimist facing a disappointment would not be surprised. They would treat it as part of the human condition, and look for calm and kindness rather than more striving.","history":"Arthur Schopenhauer’s The World as Will and Representation (1818) and Parerga and Paralipomena (1851) gave pessimism its classic form, with an ethics grounded in compassion. Giacomo Leopardi’s Zibaldone and Operette morali voiced a literary pessimism in early 19th century Italy. Eduard von Hartmann developed it further, and Joshua Foa Dienstag’s Pessimism (2006) defends it as a political philosophy.","challenge":"Many people judge their own lives to be good on balance, and the pessimist must explain this away as illusion. Critics also ask how pain and pleasure can be weighed on one scale, and why compassion matters if nothing has a real point. Pessimists answer that suffering itself is real and bad."},"life:Thoroughgoing nihilist":{"summary":"Nothing is really valuable, so no life has meaning and no way of living is better than any other; this view recommends nothing at all, despair included.","reading":[{"title":"Nihilism","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/nihilism/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"}],"idea":"The thoroughgoing nihilist follows value nihilism all the way. Value is not part of the world. We project it onto things, the way we see faces in clouds. If so, nothing could make a life worthwhile, and there is no fact about how one should live. Camus recommends revolt and Nagel recommends irony, but the thoroughgoing nihilist says both smuggle values back in. Without real value, revolt is no better than surrender, and no attitude is required. This is a thesis, not a mood. It does not say life is terrible, which would itself be a value judgment, and it gives no reason for despair any more than for joy. In practice, nihilists usually go on caring about friends and projects out of habit and feeling. The view is attractive to those who want consistency above comfort and distrust any claim that the universe hands us values.","example":"Hume described the mind as “gilding or staining” natural objects with colours borrowed from inner sentiment. The thoroughgoing nihilist takes this projection picture and drops Hume’s confidence in shared human feeling. Asked how to live, they would say the question has no answer; whatever you do, including continuing to care for others, is not ruled out, because nothing is ruled in or out.","history":"The word “nihilist” was popularised by Ivan Turgenev’s novel Fathers and Sons (1862). Friedrich Nietzsche diagnosed nihilism as a crisis of modern culture, though he hoped to overcome it. J. L. Mackie’s Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977) defended an error theory of morality, arguing that moral claims are all false. Thoroughgoing nihilism extends such error theory to every kind of value.","challenge":"If there are no reasons of any kind, there seem to be no reasons to believe nihilism either, so the view may undermine itself. It is also very hard to live: nihilists still choose, prefer and argue as if some things matter, which critics take as evidence that value is harder to deny than to assert."},"life:Maker of meaning":{"summary":"No meaning is handed to us; we make it through what we choose and commit to, personal fulfilment is enough, and death is the end of this one life we have.","reading":[{"title":"Existentialism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/existentialism/"},{"title":"Jean-Paul Sartre","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sartre/"},{"title":"Simone de Beauvoir","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauvoir/"}],"idea":"This view, at the heart of existentialism, holds that human beings have no fixed nature or given purpose. We first exist, then define ourselves through our choices. Meaning is not found but made: whatever you care about and commit to gives your life its meaning, and if a pursuit fulfils you, that is enough. There is no God to assign a role, and death is simply the end. Rather than making life pointless, this makes freedom total and responsibility heavy. You cannot blame your nature, upbringing or society for what you become. The view is attractive because it puts each person in charge of their own life and rejects the idea that some external authority decides what counts. Think of someone who leaves a secure career to become a musician, not because anyone told them to, but because that is the life they choose to stand for.","example":"Sartre told of a student during the German occupation who had to choose between joining the Free French forces and staying to care for his mother. No moral theory could decide it for him. Sartre’s advice was that he was free and must choose, that is, invent. By choosing, the student would make one path meaningful for himself.","history":"Jean-Paul Sartre’s Being and Nothingness (1943) and the lecture Existentialism Is a Humanism (1945, published 1946), with its slogan “existence precedes essence”, are the classic sources. Simone de Beauvoir’s The Ethics of Ambiguity (1947) developed an existentialist ethics of freedom, and her novel All Men Are Mortal (1946) explored why an immortal life might lose its meaning.","challenge":"If anything fulfilling counts, then a life devoted to a trivial or harmful pursuit is as meaningful as one devoted to love or justice. Many find that hard to accept. Beauvoir herself argued that willing one’s own freedom means willing others’ freedom too, which suggests some choices are better than others."},"life:Subjectivist about meaning":{"summary":"A life’s meaning comes from what its owner cares about, so even an unusual passion, like counting blades of grass, can make a life meaningful if it truly fulfils them.","reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}],"idea":"Subjectivists hold that meaning depends on the person whose life it is. Whatever you care about, choose or commit to gives your life meaning; there is no outside standard that ranks pursuits. So if a man spends his life counting blades of grass and finds it deeply fulfilling, his life is meaningful. This may sound strange, but subjectivists point out that any standard of “worthwhile” activity would have to come from somewhere, and they doubt the universe supplies one. Meaning, like love, is something that happens between a person and what they care about. The view is attractive because it respects people’s own judgments about their lives and avoids elitism about which hobbies, careers or passions count. A person devoted to model trains or birdwatching need not justify that devotion to anyone else.","example":"Richard Taylor retold the myth of Sisyphus with a twist: suppose the gods implanted in Sisyphus a deep desire to roll stones. Now his endless task is exactly what he wants. Taylor argued that his life would then have meaning from the inside. A subjectivist says the same of the grass counter: his passion supplies all the meaning a life can have.","history":"The grass counter comes from John Rawls’s A Theory of Justice (1971), where it illustrates a strange but rational plan of life. Richard Taylor’s Good and Evil (1970) contains his reworking of Sisyphus. Harry Frankfurt’s The Reasons of Love (2004) grounds what matters in what we care about, and A. J. Ayer denied that life has any purpose beyond those people give it.","challenge":"Susan Wolf and others object that the view makes meaning too cheap. A life spent on a pointless task seems lacking, however satisfied the person feels. And if fulfilment is all that matters, a drug that induced intense satisfaction in anything at all would make any life meaningful, which many find hard to accept."},"life:Fitting-fulfilment theorist":{"summary":"Meaning needs both caring and worth: a life is meaningful when someone actively loves what truly deserves to be loved. Fulfilment in a pointless task falls short, and so does valuable work done without engagement.","reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}],"idea":"This hybrid view takes something from each side of the debate. Subjectivists are right that meaning requires the person to care: a life of valuable work that bores you completely is missing something. Objectivists are right that not just anything counts: a life devoted to a pointless task, however satisfying, is missing something too. Meaning arises when real love meets something worthy of love, such as relationships, helping others, discovering truth or creating beauty. This explains both cases at once. The grass counter is fulfilled but his object is not worth the devotion; the bored doctor does valuable work but is not engaged. The view is attractive because it matches many people’s intuitions about both. It also explains why meaning differs from happiness and from morality: a meaningful life may be stressful, and it need not be devoted to helping strangers. Think of a passionate teacher.","example":"Susan Wolf contrasts several lives: the Blob, who drifts through life drinking beer and watching sitcoms; someone devoted to a pet goldfish; and Sisyphus, endlessly pushing his rock. Each lacks meaning, though in different ways. Compare them with a person who devotes herself, with real love, to her family, a craft or a cause worth caring about.","history":"Susan Wolf developed this view in essays from the 1990s and in Meaning in Life and Why It Matters (2010), based on her Tanner Lectures, where she says meaning arises when subjective attraction meets objective attractiveness. Commentators including Robert M. Adams, Nomy Arpaly and Jonathan Haidt replied in the same volume. Thaddeus Metz’s survey classes such views as hybrid theories.","challenge":"Who decides what is objectively worth loving? Critics say the list can look like the values of educated professionals, and Wolf admits the risk of elitism. Subjectivists also ask what is really lacking in the grass counter’s life if he never feels any lack."},"life:Objectivist about meaning":{"summary":"A life is meaningful through what it accomplishes or engages with, such as helping others, discovering truth or creating beauty, whatever the person feels about it.","reading":[{"title":"The Meaning of Life","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}],"idea":"Objectivists hold that meaning depends on what a life actually contains and achieves, not on how the person feels. Some activities are worthwhile in themselves: loving and being loved, relieving suffering, discovering truths, creating beauty. A life that engages with these is meaningful, and a life devoted to trivial pursuits is not, however satisfying. On the strongest version held here, even a doctor who saves thousands of lives while feeling bored by her work lives a meaningful life, because what she does matters. Many objectivists nonetheless hold that a hybrid view, adding the person’s caring, gives the full picture. The view is attractive because it captures the sense that meaning is about making a real difference. We can admire a life as meaningful even if the person who lived it never thought so, like an unsung figure whose quiet work later changed history.","example":"Peter Singer suggests that one way to find meaning is to work for a cause larger than oneself, especially reducing suffering. An objectivist would say the bored doctor’s life is meaningful because of the lives she saves, and that a cheerful life spent on nothing worthwhile lacks meaning, however content its owner feels.","history":"Peter Singer’s Practical Ethics (1979), in its final chapter, and How Are We to Live? (1993) link meaning to ethical commitment to something larger than oneself. Thaddeus Metz’s Meaning in Life (2013) defends an objective theory in which meaning comes from engaging with fundamental conditions of human existence. Robert Nozick, in Philosophical Explanations (1981), linked meaning to transcending one’s limits.","challenge":"The bored doctor seems to show a gap: we may think her life is valuable but feel she herself is missing something. Susan Wolf argues meaning also needs engagement. Objectivists must also explain what makes some activities worthwhile in themselves without simply reflecting their own preferences."},"life:Nihilist about meaning":{"summary":"No human life has a real point, and nothing we make could give it one; nihilists differ on why and on how to respond, but agree the search for meaning comes up empty.","reading":[{"title":"Nihilism","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/nihilism/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}],"idea":"Nihilists about meaning hold that human lives lack any real point. They disagree about the reason. Some say meaning would require God, and there is no God. Some say we are too small and brief in a vast, indifferent universe for anything we do to matter. Some say nothing is truly valuable at all. They also disagree about how to live: Camus urged revolt, Nagel irony, Schopenhauer compassion and resignation, and others nothing in particular. What unites them is the verdict that the search for meaning comes up empty. Many hold it calmly, as a thesis about value, not as a mood, and go on living full lives. The view is attractive because it refuses to pretend, and because it frees people from the pressure to justify their lives by a cosmic standard. A person can enjoy a walk without needing it to matter forever.","example":"Tolstoy, at the height of his fame, described asking himself what would come of everything he did, and finding no answer: his works would be forgotten, and he himself would be gone. A nihilist about meaning thinks Tolstoy saw correctly. Unlike Tolstoy, who found an answer in faith, the nihilist accepts the question has no answer and asks how to live with that.","history":"The biblical book of Ecclesiastes voices the thought that all is “vanity”, though it ends in faith. Arthur Schopenhauer, Albert Camus in The Myth of Sisyphus (1942) and Thomas Nagel in “The Absurd” (1971) all denied that life has an ultimate meaning, while recommending very different responses. Friedrich Nietzsche analysed nihilism as a crisis of modern culture.","challenge":"Critics argue that nihilists set the bar too high, demanding cosmic or eternal significance when meaning could be more modest: loving one’s family or doing good work. If meaning only requires engaging with things worth caring about, as Susan Wolf argues, then many lives plainly have it."},"life:Skeptic about the question":{"summary":"Asking for “the meaning of life” rests on a confusion, since a life is not a word or a tool, so the question does not get answered so much as stop arising.","reading":[{"title":"Ludwig Wittgenstein","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wittgenstein/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/life-meaning/"},{"title":"The Meaning of Life: Contemporary Analytic Perspectives","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mean-ana/"}],"idea":"This view questions the question. Words have meanings, and tools have purposes, so we ask what a word means or what a tool is for. But a life is neither a sentence nor a hammer. Asking for its “meaning” stretches the word beyond the place where it makes sense. On this view there is nothing to find and nothing to miss. The feeling that something is lacking comes from the form of the question, not from life itself. This is not the same as nihilism, which says life lacks a meaning it could have had. The skeptic says the demand was confused from the start. The view is attractive because it can relieve a vague, nagging worry, and because people who seem to live well rarely ask the question at all. Asking what a life means is a bit like asking what colour the number seven is.","example":"Wittgenstein wrote near the end of the Tractatus that the solution of the problem of life is seen in the vanishing of the problem, and that people who have found life’s sense after long doubt often cannot say what it consists in. A skeptic might point to someone absorbed in raising children or tending a garden: they are not answering the question; they have simply stopped asking it.","history":"Ludwig Wittgenstein’s Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus (1921), propositions 6.52 to 6.521, and his “Lecture on Ethics” (1929) treat the question as pointing beyond what language can say. Logical positivists such as Rudolf Carnap regarded many such questions as meaningless. Terry Eagleton’s The Meaning of Life (2007) discusses whether the question even makes sense.","challenge":"Critics reply that the question can be restated clearly: what makes a life worth living, significant or admirable? Philosophers such as Thaddeus Metz and Susan Wolf answer exactly that. If so, dissolving the old wording does not dissolve the real question; it only changes the subject."},"mind:Buddhist reductionist":{"summary":"You hold that “I” is a convenient name for a changing stream of body and mind, and that once all the facts about that stream are known, there is no further fact about whether a person survives.","reading":[{"title":"Mind in Indian Buddhist Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mind-indian-buddhism/"},{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"},{"title":"Personal Identity and Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-ethics/"}],"idea":"This view joins two claims. First, there is no self over and above the body, feelings, perceptions, impulses and awareness that make up a life; “I” is a label for them, the way “car” labels wheels, engine and frame. Second, in puzzle cases such as teleportation, once you know every physical and mental fact, asking “But is it really me?” adds nothing. There is no hidden answer left to discover. The two fit neatly: if the self were a separate thing, there would be a deep fact about where it went. Many find this freeing. Think of a band that replaces every member over forty years. Arguing over whether it is “still the same band” changes nothing about the music. Holders hope that seeing the self this way loosens anxiety and selfishness.","example":"In the Milindapañha, the monk Nāgasena asks King Milinda what a chariot is. Not the axle, not the wheels, not the frame, and nothing besides them. “Chariot” is a name used for the parts in working order. So too with “Nāgasena”. Faced with Parfit’s teleporter, the Buddhist reductionist says: we know exactly what happened, so the question “Is it me?” is a choice about words.","history":"The Buddha taught anattā, not-self, in early discourses such as the Anattalakkhaṇa Sutta. The chariot simile appears in the Milindapañha, and Vasubandhu (around the 4th to 5th century CE) defended a reductionist reading in his Abhidharmakośa. Derek Parfit, in Reasons and Persons (1984), argued that personal identity is not a further fact and added an appendix comparing his view with the Buddha’s. Mark Siderits coined the label “Buddhist Reductionism” in Personal Identity and Buddhist Philosophy (2003).","challenge":"If there is no self, who suffers, who is reborn, and who is liberated? Critics also argue that the reasons we care about our own future seem to need a real subject: being told “there is no further fact” about whether I will feel tomorrow’s pain does not obviously make it matter less."},"mind:Advaita Vedāntin":{"summary":"You hold that your true self is pure, unchanging awareness, and that this awareness is identical with the one reality behind everything; the world of separate things is appearance, not independent stuff.","reading":[{"title":"Advaita Vedanta (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/adv-veda/"},{"title":"Idealism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/idealism/"}],"idea":"Advaita means “not two”. The view starts from the self. Your body changes, your thoughts come and go, your personality shifts, yet something is present through all of it: the awareness in which they appear. That witnessing awareness, ātman, is your real self. Advaita then claims that ātman is identical with brahman, the single reality underlying the whole world. So reality is, at bottom, one consciousness, and the many separate objects we see are how it appears to us, not things existing on their own. Liberation (mokṣa) is not gaining something new but recognising what was always so. The appeal is a deep unity: the same awareness looking out through every pair of eyes. Compare a dream: the people and places seem separate, yet all of them are made of one dreaming mind.","example":"The classic image is the rope and the snake. At dusk you see a snake on the path and feel fear. A lamp shows it was a rope all along. Nothing new was created; an error was removed. For Advaita, the world of separate selves and objects is like the snake, and brahman is the rope. The Chāndogya Upaniṣad’s phrase tat tvam asi, “you are that”, names the insight.","history":"The roots are the Upaniṣads, especially the Chāndogya and Bṛhadāraṇyaka. Gauḍapāda’s verses on the Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad are an early Advaita text. Śaṅkara (traditionally dated to the 8th century CE) systematised the school in his commentaries on the Brahmasūtras, the Upaniṣads and the Bhagavad Gītā. Rāmānuja (11th to 12th century) became its best known critic within Vedānta. In modern times Swami Vivekananda and Ramana Maharshi spread Advaita ideas widely.","challenge":"If brahman alone is real and is pure knowing, where does the ignorance that produces the illusion come from, and whose ignorance is it? Rāmānuja pressed this hard. Others ask how an awareness that never acts or changes could be the self that seeks liberation."},"mind:Cartesian dualist":{"summary":"You hold that you are a simple, non-physical soul, distinct from your body, and that soul and body genuinely act on each other: your choices move your limbs, and bodily events produce your sensations.","reading":[{"title":"Dualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/"},{"title":"René Descartes (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/descartes/"},{"title":"Elisabeth, Princess of Bohemia (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/elisabeth-bohemia/"}],"idea":"This is the classic picture of mind and body. You are a thinking thing, a soul with no size, shape or parts, while your body is an extended physical machine. The two are distinct: the soul could in principle exist without the body. Yet they interact in both directions. When you decide to wave, your soul moves your arm; when you burn your finger, the damage produces pain in your soul, which is part of why you pull away. The soul’s simplicity matters too: since it has no parts, it cannot be split or decay as bodies do. Many find this view natural because it matches how life feels: your thoughts seem private and unified, unlike the world of matter, yet clearly connected to your body.","example":"Descartes argued that he can clearly conceive of his mind existing without his body, and whatever God can make separately is really distinct. He also noted that the body can be divided, but the mind cannot: you cannot have half a thought or half a self. Asked how they connect, he pointed to the pineal gland in the brain as the main seat of interaction.","history":"René Descartes set out the view in the Meditations on First Philosophy (1641), and described interaction in The Passions of the Soul (1649). Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia, in letters from 1643, asked how an unextended soul could move a body. Earlier, Plato’s Phaedo argued that the soul is simple and so cannot decay. Today Richard Swinburne defends a substance dualism with interaction, in The Evolution of the Soul (1986).","challenge":"The interaction problem: how can something with no size, location or mass push matter, and how does matter affect it? A further worry is that physics seems causally complete, with no gaps for a soul to act. Brain damage changing memory and character also suggests the mind depends heavily on the brain."},"mind:Ash‘arite":{"summary":"You hold that the soul is distinct from the body, that created things, mind and body included, do not truly cause one another, since God creates every event, and yet that your choices are still genuinely yours.","reading":[{"title":"al-Ghazali (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/al-ghazali/"},{"title":"Causation in Arabic and Islamic Thought (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/arabic-islamic-causation/"},{"title":"Occasionalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/occasionalism/"}],"idea":"This view combines three claims. The soul is not the body. Mind and body run in step but do not push each other. And your choices can be free even if they are fully settled in advance, because what matters is that they come from you. The Ash‘arite school of Islamic theology joins these through two ideas. First, God creates every event directly at each moment, so the regular patterns we call causes are God’s habits. Second, when you choose, God creates the act, and you “acquire” it (kasb) through your choosing, which makes it yours and grounds praise and blame. It appeals to believers who want to keep God’s complete power while holding people responsible. Everyday analogy: two clocks that always agree, not because one drives the other but because the same maker keeps both.","example":"Al-Ghazali’s most famous case is cotton and fire. When fire touches cotton, the cotton burns. But, he argued, all we observe is that burning happens together with contact; we never see fire making the cotton burn. God creates the burning when the fire touches, and could do otherwise. Likewise, when you choose to raise your hand, God creates the raising, and you acquire the act.","history":"Abu al-Hasan al-Ash‘ari (died around 935 CE) founded the school and developed the doctrine of acquisition. Abu Hamid al-Ghazali (1058 to 1111) defended its view of causation in The Incoherence of the Philosophers, its seventeenth discussion containing the cotton and fire example. Ibn Rushd (Averroes) replied in The Incoherence of the Incoherence. Scholars still debate whether acquisition amounts to genuine compatibilism or a form of divine determinism.","challenge":"Critics, from Mu‘tazilite theologians to modern philosophers, ask whether “acquisition” is more than a label: if God creates both the act and the choosing, it is hard to see what the human contributes, or why the person rather than God bears responsibility. Denying real causes in nature also seems to undercut science."},"mind:Parallelist or occasionalist":{"summary":"You hold that the mind is a non-physical soul, yet mind and body never act on each other; their events match because God arranged them, once at creation or freshly at every moment.","reading":[{"title":"Occasionalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/occasionalism/"},{"title":"Leibniz’s Philosophy of Mind (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/leibniz-mind/"},{"title":"Mental Causation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mental-causation/"}],"idea":"The puzzle behind this view is old: if the soul has no size or location, how could it push matter? Parallelists and occasionalists answer that it doesn’t. Mind and body run in step without either causing the other. Pain arrives exactly when your finger burns, and your arm rises exactly when you decide, but there is no push between them. Leibniz said God set mind and body in pre-established harmony at creation, like two perfectly made clocks wound to keep the same time. Occasionalists said God produces each matching event at every moment: your decision is merely the occasion on which God moves your arm. Both views keep the soul fully non-physical while sidestepping the interaction problem. Think of a film with a perfectly synced soundtrack: picture and sound match, yet neither causes the other.","example":"Leibniz’s image is two clocks that always agree. There are three ways this could happen: one clock moves the other, a clockmaker keeps adjusting both, or they were built so perfectly that they never drift. These match interaction, occasionalism and pre-established harmony. Leibniz chose the third as most worthy of a perfect God.","history":"Al-Ghazali (1058 to 1111) argued that God, not created things, is the only true cause. In Europe, Nicolas Malebranche developed occasionalism in The Search after Truth (1674 to 1675), with Arnold Geulincx and Louis de La Forge as fellow Cartesians. Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz proposed pre-established harmony in his New System of Nature (1695) and the Monadology (1714).","challenge":"Most critics find these views extravagant: they need God to explain every ordinary correlation, such as pain following injury, which looks like a miracle on demand. They also seem to make bodily causes idle, and if God produces every event, it becomes harder to say why human choices are our own."},"mind:Spinozist":{"summary":"You hold that mind and matter are two sides of one underlying reality, and that everything, including every choice, follows necessarily from prior causes, so no one has the free will that grounds deep blame.","reading":[{"title":"Baruch Spinoza (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spinoza/"},{"title":"Neutral Monism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/neutral-monism/"},{"title":"Causal Determinism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/determinism-causal/"}],"idea":"Spinoza thought there is only one substance, which he called God or Nature. It has infinitely many aspects, and we know two: thought and extension (physical space and matter). Your mind and your body are the same thing seen under these two aspects. So there is no interaction problem: a decision and its brain process are one event described two ways. Everything in Nature follows necessarily from its causes, like theorems from axioms, so no choice could have gone otherwise and no one deserves blame in the deep sense. Yet Spinoza found freedom here: we become free to the degree that we understand the causes acting on us rather than being pushed around by passions we misunderstand. Like seeing why a friend snapped at you, understanding dissolves anger.","example":"Spinoza compared our sense of freedom to a stone that, thrown through the air, becomes conscious and thinks it keeps moving because it wants to. We feel free because we know our desires but not their causes. Faced with someone who wronged them, a Spinozist tries to understand the causes behind the act, which replaces hatred with understanding.","history":"Baruch Spinoza (1632 to 1677) set out the view in the Ethics, published after his death in 1677 and written in geometric form, with definitions, axioms and proofs. Part Two states that the order and connection of ideas is the same as the order and connection of things. The flying stone comparison comes from a 1674 letter. Albert Einstein said he believed in Spinoza’s God, and the neuroscientist Antonio Damasio wrote Looking for Spinoza (2003).","challenge":"The parallel between thought and extension is hard to pin down: does every physical thing, even a rock, have a mind of some kind? And critics ask whether calling understanding “freedom” changes the subject, since nothing could ever have gone otherwise and praise and blame seem to lose their point."},"mind:Humean":{"summary":"You hold that there is no single self behind your experiences, only a bundle of perceptions linked by resemblance and causation, and that free will fits with determinism: a choice is free when it flows from your own will, unforced.","reading":[{"title":"David Hume (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume/"},{"title":"Compatibilism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/compatibilism/"},{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"}],"idea":"Hume looked inward for his self and found only particular perceptions: a feeling of warmth, a thought, a memory, a pang of hunger. He never caught a self apart from them. So he concluded that a mind is a bundle of perceptions, linked by resemblance and causation, which we mistake for a single unchanging thing. On free will, Hume argued that the debate was mostly verbal. Liberty, properly understood, is acting according to your own will, as opposed to being chained or forced. That kind of freedom is compatible with your will having causes, and indeed needs them, since actions with no cause in your character would not be yours. The two ideas share a down-to-earth spirit: no hidden metaphysical self and no uncaused choices, just people acting from their desires.","example":"Try Hume’s experiment. Close your eyes and look for the “you” that is having your experiences. You find sounds, a feeling of your feet, some thoughts. Can you find the experiencer itself, apart from those? For free will, Hume compares a prisoner in chains with someone walking out the door because they want to. Both acts have causes, but only the second is free.","history":"David Hume (1711 to 1776) argued for the bundle view in A Treatise of Human Nature (1739 to 1740), Book One, Part Four, Section Six, “Of Personal Identity”, though in the Treatise’s Appendix he confessed dissatisfaction with it. His compatibilism appears in An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748), Section Eight, “Of Liberty and Necessity”. Thomas Hobbes had defended compatibilism earlier in Leviathan (1651). Derek Parfit later developed Hume’s view of persons.","challenge":"If the self is just a bundle, what ties the bundle together, and who mistakes it for one thing? Hume himself could not explain this. On free will, incompatibilists object that if your will was itself fixed by causes before your birth, acting on it hardly seems enough for real responsibility."},"mind:Hylomorphist":{"summary":"You hold that the soul is not a separate thing inside the body but its form: the organisation that makes a body a living, thinking human, so a person is one unified being of matter and form.","reading":[{"title":"Aristotle’s Psychology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-psychology/"},{"title":"Thomas Aquinas (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aquinas/"},{"title":"Dualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/"}],"idea":"Hylomorphism comes from the Greek hylē (matter) and morphē (form). Everything natural is matter organised in a certain way. A bronze statue is bronze with a shape; a living thing is matter with the form of life. For Aristotle, the soul is that form: what makes a body able to grow, sense and think. Plants and animals have souls too, just simpler ones. So asking whether soul and body are one is, he said, like asking whether wax and its imprint are one. This view avoids both a ghost in a machine and a pile of chemicals: you are one thing, a living human being. It appeals to many because it treats life and mind as natural, yet doesn’t reduce a person to their parts. A dead body is no longer a human in the full sense, because the form that organised it is gone.","example":"Aristotle’s own example: if an eye were an animal, sight would be its soul. The eyeball is the matter; seeing is what makes it an eye. An eye that cannot see, like a painted eye, is an eye in name only. In the same way, a body without its soul is a human only in name.","history":"Aristotle (384 to 322 BCE) set out the view in On the Soul (De Anima), defining the soul as the first actuality of a natural body that has life. Thomas Aquinas (1225 to 1274) adapted it in the Summa Theologiae, holding that the human soul is the body’s form yet, because thinking does not use a bodily organ, can survive death. Today philosophers such as David Oderberg and Kathrin Koslicki defend versions of hylomorphism.","challenge":"Critics ask whether “form” is a real ingredient or just another word for physical structure, which would make hylomorphism a version of physicalism. For Aquinas’s version, the hardest question is how a form, which organises a body, could exist without any body at all."},"mind:Eliminativist":{"summary":"You hold that the mind is physical and that everyday talk of beliefs and desires is a rough folk theory that mature neuroscience will replace, much as chemistry replaced phlogiston.","reading":[{"title":"Eliminative Materialism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/materialism-eliminative/"},{"title":"Physicalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/physicalism/"}],"idea":"Most physicalists expect science to find the brain states that are our beliefs, desires and feelings. Eliminativists make a bolder bet. They say our everyday way of explaining behaviour (“she went to the fridge because she wanted milk and believed there was some”) is a theory, folk psychology, and a theory can be wrong. If it is wrong enough, its categories will not be matched by anything in the brain; they will just be dropped. History offers examples: chemists once explained burning with “phlogiston”, a substance released by fire, and astronomers once spoke of crystal spheres. Nobody found what those words referred to; the words disappeared. Eliminativists think folk psychology, which has changed little in thousands of years and says nothing about sleep, memory or mental illness, may go the same way.","example":"Paul Churchland compared folk psychology to ancient theories that explained wind and storms by the moods of gods. Those theories were not reduced to meteorology; they were abandoned. An eliminativist predicts that a future neuroscientist may describe your trip to the fridge in terms of activation patterns across neural populations, with no item in the brain corresponding to “the belief that there is milk”.","history":"Paul Feyerabend (1963) and Richard Rorty, in “Mind-Body Identity, Privacy, and Categories” (1965), gave early versions. Paul Churchland’s “Eliminative Materialism and the Propositional Attitudes” (1981) is the classic statement. Patricia Churchland’s Neurophilosophy (1986) argued that neuroscience should guide the philosophy of mind. Stephen Stich’s From Folk Psychology to Cognitive Science (1983) also took the possibility seriously.","challenge":"Critics say the view undermines itself: claiming that eliminativism is true, or arguing for it, seems to express a belief and aim to change other people’s beliefs. Lynne Rudder Baker pressed this in Saving Belief (1987). Eliminativists reply that this assumes the very folk theory under question."},"mind:Illusionist about consciousness":{"summary":"You hold that the mind is physical and that the sense of a private inner glow on top of brain processing is an illusion created by the brain’s simplified model of itself.","reading":[{"title":"Consciousness (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consciousness/"},{"title":"Qualia (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/qualia/"},{"title":"The Hard Problem of Consciousness (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/hard-problem-of-conciousness/"}],"idea":"Most people think that besides everything the brain does, there is something extra: the private felt quality of an experience, what philosophers call qualia. Illusionists say that extra is not real. The brain does represent itself as having such inner qualities, and that representation is very convincing, but it misdescribes what is going on. What is really there is complex processing, including processing that makes us judge and insist that we have ineffable inner feelings. This does not mean pain doesn’t hurt or that you are not conscious. It means hurting is a matter of what your brain does, not an extra glow. The appeal is that it turns the “hard problem” into a tractable one: explain why brains generate the conviction that there is something magical inside.","example":"Visual illusions offer a model. In the Müller-Lyer illusion two lines look different lengths even after you measure them. Knowing the truth doesn’t make the appearance go away. The illusionist suggests our sense of inner qualities is like that: a persistent appearance produced by how the brain models itself, which science can explain without adding anything non-physical.","history":"Daniel Dennett attacked qualia in “Quining Qualia” (1988) and Consciousness Explained (1991). Keith Frankish named and defended illusionism in “Illusionism as a Theory of Consciousness” (Journal of Consciousness Studies, 2016), with replies from many philosophers. Michael Graziano’s attention schema theory offers a neuroscientific cousin. David Chalmers’ “The Meta-Problem of Consciousness” (2018) treats explaining our belief in consciousness as a key problem for every theory.","challenge":"The most common objection is that an illusion is itself something experienced: for it to seem that you have inner feelings, there must already be a seeming, which is just what consciousness is. Galen Strawson called denying experience the silliest claim ever made. Illusionists reply that “seeming” can be understood as representation, not felt glow."},"mind:Panpsychist":{"summary":"You hold that experience cannot arise from wholly unfeeling matter, so some simple form of experience is a basic feature of matter itself, and brains combine it into minds like ours.","reading":[{"title":"Panpsychism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/panpsychism/"},{"title":"Bertrand Russell (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/russell/"},{"title":"Consciousness (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consciousness/"}],"idea":"Panpsychism starts from a puzzle. Physics tells us what matter does: how particles move, attract and repel. It is silent on what matter is in itself. Meanwhile we know one thing about matter from the inside: in our brains, it feels. Panpsychists propose that experience is part of what matter is everywhere, in an extremely simple form. Electrons do not think or worry; at most they have some flicker of experience. Brains organise vast numbers of these into rich minds. The attraction is that it avoids two leaps: from no feeling at all to feeling, which seems like magic, and from matter to a separate soul, which leads to interaction problems. Compare how magnetism was once thought special to lodestones but turned out to be a feature of matter generally.","example":"Bertrand Russell observed that physics describes matter only in terms of its structure and behaviour, leaving open its intrinsic nature. Panpsychists add Thomas Nagel’s point that experience cannot emerge from the purely non-experiential. So they ask: when you taste chocolate, what you directly know is the intrinsic nature of some of your brain’s matter. Why assume other matter has no inner nature at all?","history":"Versions of the idea appear in Spinoza and Leibniz. Bertrand Russell’s The Analysis of Matter (1927) supplied the structure argument. Thomas Nagel’s essay “Panpsychism” (1979) set out an argument for it. Galen Strawson’s “Realistic Monism” (2006) argued that physicalism leads to panpsychism. Philip Goff’s Galileo’s Error (2019) brought it to a wide audience, and David Chalmers has explored it sympathetically.","challenge":"The combination problem: how do billions of tiny experiences add up to one unified mind with a single point of view, rather than staying a crowd? William James raised this in The Principles of Psychology (1890). Critics also say there is no evidence that particles have any experience at all."},"mind:Idealist":{"summary":"You hold that reality is fundamentally mental: tables and stars exist as ideas or experiences in minds rather than as independent material stuff, though the world remains stable and shared.","reading":[{"title":"Idealism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/idealism/"},{"title":"George Berkeley (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/berkeley/"}],"idea":"Idealism reverses the usual picture. Most of us think matter is basic and minds arose late within it. The idealist says mind is basic, and what we call matter is made of experience. A table just is what is seen, felt and could be perceived; there is no extra “material substance” hiding behind those experiences. This does not mean the world is a private dream. It is orderly and shared, and science still works, because its patterns are patterns in experience. Berkeley held that God perceives everything constantly, which keeps the world in being when no human is looking. The view appeals because the only reality we ever directly meet is experience, and because it makes the mind-body problem disappear: there is no unfeeling matter to explain mind from.","example":"Berkeley asked: can you even conceive of a tree existing unperceived? When you try, you imagine the tree, which means you are perceiving it in thought. A popular limerick about a tree in the Quad captures the reply: the tree continues to be, since it is observed by God. Samuel Johnson famously kicked a stone, saying “I refute it thus,” though Berkeley never denied stones feel solid.","history":"George Berkeley (1685 to 1753) argued for idealism in A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge (1710) and Three Dialogues between Hylas and Philonous (1713), summed up as esse est percipi, to be is to be perceived. Earlier, the Yogācāra school of Buddhism, notably Vasubandhu’s Twenty Verses, argued that only consciousness exists. Later absolute idealists, such as Hegel and F. H. Bradley, saw reality as one cosmic mind.","challenge":"Why is experience so orderly and shared if nothing stands behind it? Berkeley needed God; without God, idealists must explain why different people perceive the same world. Critics also note that science describes a long history before any minds existed, which seems hard to recast in terms of experience."},"mind:Neutral monist":{"summary":"You hold that mind and matter are both built from one underlying kind of stuff that is itself neither mental nor physical; the same events count as mental grouped one way and physical grouped another.","reading":[{"title":"Neutral Monism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/neutral-monism/"},{"title":"Bertrand Russell (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/russell/"}],"idea":"Neutral monism tries to dissolve the mind-body problem by denying that either side is basic. Imagine a set of events, such as the flashes of colour and shape in your visual field. Grouped by their connections to your memories and other experiences, they form part of your mind. Grouped by their connections to light, objects and nerves, they form part of the physical world. Nothing about the events themselves is mental or physical; those are two ways of organising them. So there is only one kind of reality, but it isn’t matter in the physicist’s sense or mind in the idealist’s sense. The view appeals because it avoids both a soul and a world of dead matter that somehow sparks experience. Compare a population census: the same people can be sorted by street or by family.","example":"William James used the example of a room you are sitting in. The very same experience of the room belongs to two histories: the history of your mind (you walked in, you feel tired, you notice the window) and the history of the house (built in a certain year, painted, later sold). It is counted twice, once in each series, without being two things.","history":"Ernst Mach argued in The Analysis of Sensations (1886) that sensations are the neutral elements of both physics and psychology. William James made the case in “Does ‘Consciousness’ Exist?” (1904) and his essays on radical empiricism. Bertrand Russell adopted the view in The Analysis of Mind (1921). Spinoza is often seen as a forerunner, and Russellian monism today develops related ideas.","challenge":"What exactly is the neutral stuff? Critics say that when neutral monists describe it, it tends to sound like experience, which collapses the view into idealism or panpsychism, or else like physical structure, which collapses it into physicalism. It is hard to say what neutral means positively."},"mind:Epiphenomenalist":{"summary":"You hold that the mind is not fully physical, but that experiences cause nothing physical: the brain does all the work, and feelings ride along as by-products.","reading":[{"title":"Epiphenomenalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epiphenomenalism/"},{"title":"Mental Causation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mental-causation/"},{"title":"Qualia (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/qualia/"}],"idea":"Epiphenomenalism accepts two things that seem to clash. First, experiences such as the taste of coffee or the hurt of pain are not just physical processes; something about them goes beyond physics. Second, the physical world is causally complete: every physical event, including every movement of your body, has a full physical cause. The way to keep both is to say that experiences are caused by brain processes but cause nothing themselves. When you pull your hand from a hot stove, nerve signals do all the pulling; the pain is real but is a side effect. The appeal is that it respects both the reality of experience and the success of neuroscience, which keeps finding physical causes for behaviour. Compare the noise of a car engine: produced by its working, but not what moves the car.","example":"Thomas Huxley compared consciousness to the steam whistle of a locomotive, which sounds as the engine works but plays no part in driving it. Frank Jackson’s Mary is another classic case: a scientist who knows every physical fact about colour vision but has never seen red learns something new on seeing it. Jackson concluded that qualia are non-physical, and epiphenomenal.","history":"Thomas Henry Huxley defended the view in “On the Hypothesis that Animals are Automata, and Its History” (1874). Shadworth Hodgson held a similar view around the same time. Frank Jackson’s “Epiphenomenal Qualia” (1982) gave the most famous modern argument, though he later abandoned it for physicalism. William Robinson and others continue to defend versions of it today.","challenge":"If experiences cause nothing, they cannot cause us to talk or write about them, so our conviction that we have experiences would be the same even without them. Critics find it strange that we can know about, or evolve to have, things that make no difference to what we do."},"mind:Illusionist about free will":{"summary":"You hold that no one has the free will needed to truly deserve blame, yet you think society benefits if most people go on believing they do, so the belief should be handled with care.","reading":[{"title":"Skepticism About Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral-responsibility/"},{"title":"Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freewill/"},{"title":"Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-responsibility/"}],"idea":"This view is honest about two things that pull apart. On the philosophical question, it agrees with free will skeptics: no one has the kind of control that would make them deserve praise or blame in the deepest sense. But on the practical question, it worries about what would happen if everyone believed this. The belief in free will supports self-respect, a sense of achievement, respect for others as responsible agents, and a sense of justice that protects the innocent from being punished merely because it is useful. So the illusionist thinks the belief, though false, plays a valuable role, and that philosophers should not rush to tear it down. Compare a family story about a grandparent that is not quite true but helps everyone treat each other well.","example":"Smilansky asks us to imagine a society where everyone accepts that nobody deserves anything. Why not punish an innocent person if that deters crime efficiently, since the guilty do not really deserve punishment either? The illusionist thinks a widespread belief in desert helps block such reasoning, even if, at the deepest level, the belief is not strictly true.","history":"Saul Smilansky defended this view in Free Will and Illusion (Oxford University Press, 2000), arguing for illusionism alongside a partial compatibilism. Psychology experiments, such as those by Kathleen Vohs and Jonathan Schooler (2008), reported that reading anti-free-will passages increased cheating, though later attempts to replicate this had mixed results. The idea that some beliefs are socially valuable despite being false has a long history in political philosophy, going back to Plato’s “noble lie” in the Republic.","challenge":"Critics say the view is paternalistic and unstable: a small group knowing the truth and hoping the rest do not is uncomfortable in a democracy, and illusions tend not to survive once named. Free will skeptics such as Pereboom argue that life without belief in desert would actually be better, with less anger and harsh punishment."},"mind:Free will impossibilist":{"summary":"You hold that free will of the kind needed for true desert is impossible in principle, because it would require being the cause of yourself, which nothing can be.","reading":[{"title":"Skepticism About Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral-responsibility/"},{"title":"Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freewill/"},{"title":"Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-responsibility/"}],"idea":"Most debates about free will ask whether determinism is true. The impossibilist argues that the answer does not matter. You do what you do because of the way you are: your desires, character and reasoning. To be truly responsible for what you do, you would have to be responsible for the way you are. But you could only be responsible for the way you are if you had chosen to become that way, using some earlier way you were, which you would also have to be responsible for, and so on forever. Since that regress can never end, ultimate responsibility is impossible, whether the universe is deterministic or random. The appeal is its simplicity: the argument fits in a few lines and seems to rule out deep desert for any possible agent, even God-like ones.","example":"Consider someone who works hard and becomes a doctor. We praise their effort. But their capacity for effort came from genes, upbringing and luck they never chose. Even if they decided to become hard-working at age twelve, that decision came from the twelve-year-old they already were. Galen Strawson concludes that heaven and hell, as eternal reward and punishment, could never be just.","history":"Galen Strawson set out the Basic Argument in “The Impossibility of Moral Responsibility” (Philosophical Studies, 1994) and earlier in Freedom and Belief (1986). Friedrich Nietzsche made a similar point in Beyond Good and Evil (1886), mocking the desire to be causa sui, the cause of oneself. Neil Levy’s Hard Luck (2011) and Robert Sapolsky’s Determined (2023) develop related arguments about luck and biology.","challenge":"Compatibilists reply that responsibility never required self-creation: it is enough that you act from reasons you endorse and could respond to. On their view, the Basic Argument sets an impossible standard and then complains that no one meets it. Libertarians add that self-forming choices can make you partly responsible for who you are."},"mind:Hard incompatibilist":{"summary":"You hold that free will is ruled out whether or not determinism is true: determined choices come from causes beyond your control, and undetermined ones come down to chance, which is not control either.","reading":[{"title":"Skepticism About Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral-responsibility/"},{"title":"Arguments for Incompatibilism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-arguments/"},{"title":"Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freewill/"}],"idea":"Traditional hard determinists said: determinism is true, so there is no free will. Hard incompatibilists say that is too narrow. Suppose determinism is true: then your choices were settled by events before you were born, so they were not up to you. Now suppose it is false, and some choices are not settled until the moment you make them. Then, given everything about you up to that moment, which way you go is a matter of chance, and chance is not control either. Either way, you lack the kind of control needed to deserve blame or praise in the basic, backward-looking sense. Hard incompatibilists still think we can hold people to account in forward-looking ways: protecting others, encouraging reform, and repairing relationships. They argue this would make life kinder, not bleaker.","example":"Pereboom’s four-case argument starts with Plum, whom neuroscientists directly manipulate to kill White. Plum reasons normally, yet seems not responsible. In later cases Plum is programmed at birth, then shaped by upbringing, and finally produced by ordinary deterministic causes. Since there seems to be no relevant difference between the cases, if Plum is not responsible in the first, he is not responsible in the last.","history":"Derk Pereboom named and developed hard incompatibilism in Living Without Free Will (2001) and Free Will, Agency, and Meaning in Life (2014). The four-case manipulation argument comes from that work. Earlier thinkers such as Spinoza and Baron d’Holbach argued against free will from determinism, and the dilemma about chance goes back at least to debates in the 18th century. Gregg Caruso and Neil Levy defend related positions today.","challenge":"Critics argue that the four-case argument cuts both ways: compatibilists can say Plum is responsible in all four cases, or point to real differences between manipulation and ordinary causes. Libertarians claim that agent causation or effort-driven indeterminism gives control rather than mere chance."},"mind:Public-health skeptic":{"summary":"You hold that no one deserves punishment in the deepest sense, but society may still protect itself from dangerous people, as it quarantines carriers of disease, using the least harm needed.","reading":[{"title":"Skepticism About Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral-responsibility/"},{"title":"Punishment (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/punishment/"}],"idea":"If no one deserves blame, what should we do about crime? Retributivists punish because wrongdoers deserve to suffer. Public-health skeptics reject that. Instead they look to how we treat infectious disease. A person carrying a dangerous disease is not to blame for it, yet we may isolate them to protect others, as briefly and humanely as possible, with care and treatment. Likewise, they argue, we may restrain dangerous offenders only as much as safety requires, aiming to rehabilitate and release them. Because the model is public health, prevention comes first: reduce the poverty, abuse and lack of education that make crime likely. The appeal is that it keeps society safe while dropping the urge to make people suffer for its own sake. Many point to rehabilitative prison systems as partial real examples.","example":"Imagine two people who each pose a danger to others: one carries a deadly contagious virus, the other has committed violent assaults. The quarantine model says both may be separated from the public, neither should be made to suffer more than needed, and both should receive treatment aimed at returning them safely. As with disease, the focus shifts to prevention.","history":"Derk Pereboom proposed the quarantine model in Living Without Free Will (2001). Gregg Caruso developed its public health side in Rejecting Retributivism: Free Will, Punishment, and Criminal Justice (2021), stressing the social causes of crime. Caruso’s debate book with Daniel Dennett, Just Deserts (2021), sets it against compatibilist views. Earlier, Clarence Darrow argued in courtrooms in the 1920s that criminals are products of their circumstances.","challenge":"Critics such as Saul Smilansky argue that the model could justify restraining people who have done nothing wrong but are predicted to be dangerous, and that without desert, nothing principled stops harsh treatment if it worked. Defenders reply that the same limits that restrain quarantine for disease apply here."},"mind:Hard determinist":{"summary":"You hold that every event, including every choice, is fixed by earlier causes and the laws of nature, and that this rules out free will, since your choices were never ultimately up to you.","reading":[{"title":"Causal Determinism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/determinism-causal/"},{"title":"Arguments for Incompatibilism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-arguments/"},{"title":"Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freewill/"}],"idea":"Hard determinism combines two claims. Determinism: given the past and the laws of nature, only one future is possible. Incompatibilism: if determinism is true, no one has free will. Together they mean free will is an illusion. Your choices come from your desires and beliefs, which come from your genes and experiences, which come from events reaching back before you were born. At no point did you step in as an uncaused cause. The appeal is that it takes science seriously: the more we learn about brains, the more behaviour looks like the result of causes. Many hard determinists also think the view should make us more forgiving. A person who commits a crime is like a domino that fell because the previous one hit it, so hatred makes little sense.","example":"Pierre-Simon Laplace imagined an intellect that knew the position of every particle and every force at one moment. Such a being, he wrote, could calculate the entire future and past. A hard determinist says that this intellect could have predicted your breakfast choice before you were born, and so the choice was never genuinely open.","history":"Baruch Spinoza argued in the Ethics (1677) that everything follows necessarily from the nature of God or Nature. Baron d’Holbach gave a forceful materialist version in The System of Nature (1770). Pierre-Simon Laplace described his all-knowing intellect in 1814. William James coined the term “hard determinism” in “The Dilemma of Determinism” (1884), contrasting it with “soft” determinism, his name for compatibilism.","challenge":"Modern physics is not clearly deterministic: quantum mechanics, on many interpretations, involves genuine chance. Compatibilists also challenge the incompatibilist step, arguing that freedom means acting from your own reasons without coercion, which determinism does not threaten."},"mind:Free will skeptic":{"summary":"You hold that no one has the kind of free will that would make them truly deserve praise or blame, though people still deliberate and choose, and that harsh retributive punishment is therefore unjustified.","reading":[{"title":"Skepticism About Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-moral-responsibility/"},{"title":"Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freewill/"},{"title":"Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-responsibility/"}],"idea":"Free will skeptics agree on a conclusion rather than a single argument. People make choices, and those choices matter. But no one has the kind of control that would make them deserve, in the basic backward-looking sense, to be praised or blamed, rewarded or punished. Some reach this through determinism, some through the problem of chance, and some through the impossibility of making yourself. Skeptics typically argue that this conclusion is not bleak. We can still hold people to account, protect society, admire good qualities and pursue meaningful lives. What changes is the anger and moral resentment that drive revenge, and policies built on making wrongdoers suffer. Many find this view attractive because it fits a scientific picture of human beings and encourages compassion.","example":"Imagine a friend who lets you down. A free will skeptic need not pretend it didn’t matter or stop telling them. They can explain the hurt, ask for change and protect themselves. What they drop is the thought that the friend deserves to suffer for it. Skeptics argue that this makes relationships more forgiving, not less honest.","history":"Baruch Spinoza in the Ethics (1677) and Baron d’Holbach in The System of Nature (1770) rejected free will. Galen Strawson’s Basic Argument (1994) and Derk Pereboom’s Living Without Free Will (2001) are central modern works. Gregg Caruso’s Rejecting Retributivism (2021) applies skepticism to criminal justice. Robert Sapolsky’s Determined: A Science of Life Without Free Will (2023) argues from biology.","challenge":"Critics argue that our practices of resentment, gratitude and moral responsibility are too deeply woven into human life to be given up, a point P. F. Strawson made in “Freedom and Resentment” (1962). Others worry that without desert, there is no principled limit on punishing people for the sake of social benefit."},"mind:Agent-causal libertarian":{"summary":"You hold that free will requires undetermined choices, and that what settles a free choice is you, the agent, acting as a basic cause, rather than any earlier event in your brain.","reading":[{"title":"Incompatibilist (Nondeterministic) Theories of Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-theories/"},{"title":"Thomas Reid (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reid/"},{"title":"Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freewill/"}],"idea":"Libertarians think free will is real and incompatible with determinism. Agent-causal libertarians add an account of what settles a free choice. Usually we explain events by earlier events: the window broke because the ball hit it. But when you freely decide, they say, the cause is not some brain event; it is you. The agent, a persisting being with reasons, starts a new chain of causes without being made to by anything earlier. This aims to capture what “I did it” really means. Without it, they worry, an undetermined choice is just a random event in the brain, which you no more control than a coin flip. Agent causation is meant to give you control without determinism. Compare raising your hand in class: you seem to be the one who does it, not merely the place where it happens.","example":"Roderick Chisholm used the example of a person moving a staff, which moves a stone. The stone was moved by the staff, the staff by the hand, the hand by certain muscles, and so on. But eventually, he argued, we reach the agent, who moved the hand without being moved by anything else. In this respect, he wrote, each of us is a prime mover unmoved.","history":"Thomas Reid defended agent causation in Essays on the Active Powers of Man (1788). Roderick Chisholm revived it in his lecture “Human Freedom and the Self” (1964). Timothy O’Connor’s Persons and Causes (2000) and Randolph Clarke’s Libertarian Accounts of Free Will (2003) give detailed modern versions. Richard Swinburne defends a related view in Mind, Brain, and Free Will (2013).","challenge":"Critics find agent causation mysterious: what is it for a person, rather than an event, to cause something, and when does it happen? It is also unclear how it fits with neuroscience and physics, and whether it really escapes the luck problem, since nothing explains why the agent caused one choice rather than another."},"mind:Leeway libertarian":{"summary":"You hold that free will requires undetermined choices and that responsibility requires genuine alternatives: being able to do otherwise. So cases meant to show responsibility without alternatives either fail or reduce responsibility.","reading":[{"title":"Moral Responsibility and the Principle of Alternative Possibilities (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/alternative-possibilities/"},{"title":"Incompatibilist (Nondeterministic) Theories of Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-theories/"},{"title":"Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freewill/"}],"idea":"For most people, “I could have done otherwise” sounds like part of what it means to be free. Leeway libertarians defend this. Being responsible for an act, they say, requires that you had some real alternative, and that requires that the past and laws did not fix your choice. In 1969 Harry Frankfurt challenged the first claim with cases in which a hidden device ensures you choose one way but never has to act, because you choose that way on your own. Leeway libertarians respond in two ways. Some say the device does reduce your responsibility, since you had no real alternative. Others use the dilemma defence: if your choice was undetermined, the device could not know in advance which way you were heading, so you did have an alternative after all.","example":"In the Frankfurt case, a scientist secretly wires your brain to switch you to Ben if you were about to vote for Ana, but you choose Ben on your own. The dilemma defence asks: what tells the device you were about to vote for Ana? If a prior sign settles your choice, determinism is assumed. If it doesn’t, the device can’t act in time, and you could have voted otherwise.","history":"Harry Frankfurt’s “Alternate Possibilities and Moral Responsibility” (1969) introduced the challenge. Peter van Inwagen’s An Essay on Free Will (1983) presented the consequence argument for incompatibilism and defended the importance of alternatives. Robert Kane’s The Significance of Free Will (1996) and David Widerker’s 1995 article “Libertarianism and Frankfurt’s Attack on the Principle of Alternative Possibilities” developed the dilemma defence. Carl Ginet also pressed related objections.","challenge":"Defenders of Frankfurt reply with new cases that do not rely on a prior sign, such as “blockage” cases in which certain brain pathways are blocked, leaving only one option open. Critics also argue that tiny, irrelevant alternatives, such as flinching, are too thin to ground responsibility."},"mind:Event-causal libertarian":{"summary":"You hold that free will requires undetermined choices, and that in free choices your own reasons and efforts cause the outcome without fixing it in advance; no special agent causation is needed.","reading":[{"title":"Incompatibilist (Nondeterministic) Theories of Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-theories/"},{"title":"Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freewill/"},{"title":"Arguments for Incompatibilism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-arguments/"}],"idea":"Event-causal libertarians want libertarian free will without anything mysterious. They accept that choices are caused by events in your mind and brain, such as your beliefs, desires and efforts. But they say these causes can be indeterministic: they make an outcome likely without guaranteeing it. In hard choices, when you are torn between two options, some indeterminacy in brain processes keeps the outcome open until you decide. Whichever way you go, the choice is caused by your own reasons and effort, so it is yours. Robert Kane calls such choices “self-forming actions”, because through them you shape the character from which later choices flow. The appeal is that it fits a broadly scientific picture of the mind and allows that, given the same past, you might have chosen differently.","example":"Kane’s example is a businesswoman on her way to an important meeting who sees an assault in an alley. She is torn between stopping to help and pressing on with her career. Her effort to resist temptation is indeterministic. If she helps, it is because of her moral reasons and effort; if she goes on, it is because of her ambition. Either way, she made it happen.","history":"Robert Kane developed the view in Free Will and Values (1985) and The Significance of Free Will (1996). Carl Ginet and Laura Ekstrom have defended related positions, and Mark Balaguer’s work on torn decisions belongs nearby. Epicurus, as reported by Lucretius in On the Nature of Things, appealed to a random swerve of atoms to make room for freedom, an ancient forerunner of the idea.","challenge":"The luck objection: if two people with exactly the same past, reasons and effort could choose differently, then the difference between them seems to come down to chance. Peter van Inwagen’s “rollback” argument and Alfred Mele’s work press this point hard against event-causal views."},"mind:Libertarian about free will":{"summary":"You hold that we have free will and that it requires our choices not to be fixed by prior causes: with the past exactly as it was, you could have chosen differently.","reading":[{"title":"Incompatibilist (Nondeterministic) Theories of Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-theories/"},{"title":"Arguments for Incompatibilism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-arguments/"},{"title":"Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freewill/"}],"idea":"Libertarianism about free will (unrelated to political libertarianism) holds two things. First, free will is incompatible with determinism: if your choice was fixed before you were born, it was not truly up to you. Second, we do have free will, so determinism must be false, at least for human choices. Libertarians take our experience of deliberation seriously: when you weigh options, it seems that more than one is genuinely open. They also argue that responsibility, praise and blame depend on that openness. Libertarians differ on what settles an undetermined choice: some say the agent, as a basic cause; others say the person’s reasons and efforts, acting indeterministically. Many find the view attractive because it takes both moral responsibility and our first-person sense of choosing at face value.","example":"The key test is the replay. Rewind time to just before you chose tea over coffee, with every atom and thought as it was. The libertarian says that if you chose freely, the replay could come out differently: you might choose coffee. Peter van Inwagen’s consequence argument backs this: if the past and laws fix your act, and you can’t change them, you can’t change your act.","history":"Epicurus, as reported by Lucretius, posited a swerve of atoms to allow freedom. Thomas Reid’s Essays on the Active Powers of Man (1788) defended a libertarian view. In the 20th century, C. A. Campbell and Roderick Chisholm defended it. Peter van Inwagen’s An Essay on Free Will (1983), Robert Kane’s The Significance of Free Will (1996) and Timothy O’Connor’s Persons and Causes (2000) are leading modern works.","challenge":"The two main worries are luck and science. If nothing prior settles a choice, critics ask what makes it controlled rather than random. And it is unclear whether brains contain the right kind of indeterminism at the right place, since quantum effects may be too tiny to influence decisions."},"mind:Buddhist about the self":{"summary":"You hold that there is no lasting self, only a changing stream of body, feelings, perceptions, mental formations and consciousness, and that clinging to “I” as something solid fuels suffering.","reading":[{"title":"Mind in Indian Buddhist Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mind-indian-buddhism/"},{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"}],"idea":"The Buddha taught that everything in experience falls into five groups, or aggregates: physical form, feelings, perceptions, mental formations (such as habits and intentions) and consciousness. Look at each, he said, and you find something changing, conditioned and not under your full control. None of them is a self, and there is no self behind them. “I” is a useful label for the stream, like “river” for flowing water. The point is not just theory. Treating the label as a solid, permanent thing leads to craving and fear: we cling to things as “mine” and dread losing them. Seeing through the self is part of the path to liberation. The view appeals to many because it links a claim about reality with a practical way to reduce suffering. Think of how anger fades when you stop taking an insult as an attack on a fixed “me”.","example":"In the Anattalakkhaṇa Sutta, the Buddha asks his followers whether physical form is permanent or impermanent. Impermanent, they reply. And is what is impermanent satisfactory or unsatisfactory? Unsatisfactory. Then is it fitting to regard it as “this is mine, this I am, this is my self”? No. The same questioning is applied to each of the five aggregates.","history":"The Buddha (traditionally 5th century BCE) taught anattā in early discourses such as the Anattalakkhaṇa Sutta, said to be his second sermon. The Milindapañha (around the turn of the Common Era) gives Nāgasena’s chariot simile. Vasubandhu’s Abhidharmakośa (around the 4th to 5th century CE) refuted the idea of a real person. The Pudgalavāda school dissented, arguing for a person neither identical to nor distinct from the aggregates.","challenge":"If there is no self, who acts, remembers, suffers and is reborn, and how can karma connect one life to the next? Buddhist schools gave detailed answers, but critics, including the Nyāya school in India, argued that memory and recognition require a single, lasting subject."},"mind:Witness-self theorist":{"summary":"You hold that the real you is not your body, thoughts or personality, but the silent, unchanging awareness in which they all appear; this witness never thinks or chooses, it only watches.","reading":[{"title":"Advaita Vedanta (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/adv-veda/"},{"title":"Self-Consciousness (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/self-consciousness/"}],"idea":"This view starts from a simple observation. You can notice your body, your moods, even your thoughts. Whatever you can observe, it says, is not the observer. So the real self is not anything that appears in experience; it is the awareness to which everything appears. This witness (sākṣin in Sanskrit) is always present, never changes and takes no part in the action. Thinking, choosing and feeling belong to the mind, which is part of nature; the witness simply illuminates them. Traditions differ on what follows. Advaita Vedānta says there is one witness, identical with brahman. Sāṃkhya and Yoga say each being has its own witness, called puruṣa. The appeal is a sense of calm: whatever happens to body and mind, the awareness watching it is untouched, like a sky behind passing clouds.","example":"Sāṃkhya texts compare the witness to a spectator at a dance. Nature, prakṛti, performs, and once the spectator has seen the performance and understood that it is not the dancer, the dance has done its work. In meditation practice, the view suggests simply watching thoughts arise and pass, noticing that the watcher is not one of them.","history":"The idea appears in the Upaniṣads, for example the Bṛhadāraṇyaka’s description of the self as the unseen seer. Īśvarakṛṣṇa’s Sāṃkhyakārikā (roughly 4th to 5th century CE) gives the Sāṃkhya version, and Patañjali’s Yoga Sūtras describe the seer resting in its own nature. Śaṅkara (traditionally 8th century CE) made the witness central to Advaita Vedānta. Ramana Maharshi taught self-inquiry, asking “Who am I?”, in the 20th century.","challenge":"If the witness never thinks, acts or changes, how can it be the self that seeks liberation, or know that it is witnessing? Buddhist critics argued that no such unchanging observer is ever found in experience, only changing moments of awareness."},"mind:No-self theorist":{"summary":"You hold that there is no self beyond the brain’s own picture of one: the brain builds a model of the organism as a self, and because we can’t see it as a model, we feel like someone inside.","reading":[{"title":"Self-Consciousness (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/self-consciousness/"},{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"},{"title":"Consciousness (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consciousness/"}],"idea":"This is a scientific version of the no-self view. Your brain constantly builds models: of the space around you, of other people, and of your own body and mind. One of these is a self-model, a representation of the organism as a single, unified someone who has experiences and makes decisions. The model is transparent: you look through it rather than at it, so you do not experience it as a model. You simply experience being a self. On this view there are organisms, brains and self-models, but no further thing that is the self. The appeal is that it explains why the self feels so real while fitting neuroscience, which has found no single place in the brain where “you” live. Compare a video game avatar so smoothly controlled that you forget it is a picture.","example":"The rubber hand illusion is a favourite example. Hide someone’s real hand, place a rubber hand in front of them, and stroke both at the same time. Within minutes many people feel the rubber hand as their own. Metzinger takes this as evidence that the sense of what belongs to you is built by the brain’s model, which can be fooled.","history":"Thomas Metzinger developed the self-model theory of subjectivity in Being No One (2003) and presented it for general readers in The Ego Tunnel (2009). The rubber hand illusion was reported by Matthew Botvinick and Jonathan Cohen in Nature (1998). Related ideas appear in Daniel Dennett’s description of the self as a “center of narrative gravity” and, much earlier, in the Buddha’s teaching of not-self.","challenge":"Who or what is fooled by the self-model? Critics argue that experiencing a model requires someone to experience it, which reintroduces a subject. Others suggest the view may be compatible with a modest self: if the organism is real and has a unified point of view, perhaps that is all a self ever was."},"mind:Substance dualist":{"summary":"You hold that the mind is a non-physical thing, distinct from the body, which thinks and feels and could in principle exist without it: you are, or have, a soul not made of matter.","reading":[{"title":"Dualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/"},{"title":"René Descartes (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/descartes/"},{"title":"Mental Causation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mental-causation/"}],"idea":"Substance dualism says reality contains two basic kinds of thing: physical things, which have size, shape and location, and minds, or souls, which think and feel. Your body and brain are physical; you, the subject of your experiences, are not. The soul is closely connected to the body during life but is a separate substance that could, in principle, exist without it. Many find this view natural. Your thoughts and feelings seem private and unified in a way matter doesn’t, and it is hard to see how chemistry alone could add up to someone who feels. It also fits many religious traditions’ beliefs about life after death, though some dualists defend it on purely philosophical grounds. Picture a pilot and a plane: closely linked, but distinct.","example":"Ibn Sīnā’s flying man asks you to imagine being created suddenly in mid-air, blindfolded, limbs spread so they touch nothing, with no sensation at all. Would you still know that you exist? He argued yes, even though you would be aware of no body, so the self must be something other than the body.","history":"Plato’s Phaedo argued that the soul is distinct from the body and survives death. Ibn Sīnā (Avicenna, 980 to 1037) presented the flying man in his Book of the Soul. René Descartes gave the classic modern version in the Meditations (1641). Today Richard Swinburne, in The Evolution of the Soul (1986), and others such as J. P. Moreland and William Hasker defend versions of dualism.","challenge":"The interaction problem: how does a non-physical soul move a physical brain, and how do brain events affect it? Critics add that brain damage, drugs and sleep alter thought and personality, suggesting the mind depends on the brain more deeply than dualism expects."},"mind:Property dualist":{"summary":"You hold that there is no soul, but brains have properties that are not physical: how pain feels or red looks from the inside, which arise from the brain yet cannot be derived from physics alone.","reading":[{"title":"Dualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/"},{"title":"The Hard Problem of Consciousness (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/hard-problem-of-conciousness/"},{"title":"Consciousness (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consciousness/"}],"idea":"Property dualists accept that there is only one kind of thing: physical stuff, including brains. But they think brains have two kinds of property. Some are physical, such as electrical activity and chemistry. Others are experiential: what it is like, from the inside, to feel pain or see red. These felt qualities arise from brain processes, perhaps by basic laws of nature linking the physical and the mental. But no amount of physical description, however complete, would logically imply them. So there is no ghost in the machine, yet physics does not tell the whole story. The appeal is that it takes the hard problem of consciousness seriously while avoiding a separate soul. It is like learning everything about a piano’s strings and hammers, yet still not knowing what the music sounds like.","example":"Chalmers’ zombie argument asks you to imagine a being physically identical to you, atom for atom, but with no experience at all: all is dark inside. If such a zombie is even conceivable, then experience is not entailed by physical facts. Frank Jackson’s Mary, who knows all about colour science but has never seen red, makes a similar point.","history":"Thomas Nagel’s “What Is It Like to Be a Bat?” (1974) and Frank Jackson’s “Epiphenomenal Qualia” (1982) helped motivate the view. Saul Kripke’s Naming and Necessity (1980) argued against identifying pain with brain states. David Chalmers’ The Conscious Mind (1996) is the leading statement, calling the view “naturalistic dualism” and coining the label “the hard problem of consciousness” in 1995.","challenge":"If experiences are non-physical, do they cause anything? Physics seems to give every bodily movement a complete physical cause, so non-physical properties appear to be idle, which threatens to make property dualism a form of epiphenomenalism. Jaegwon Kim pressed this causal exclusion problem."},"mind:Biological naturalist":{"summary":"You hold that minds are natural biological phenomena caused by the specific workings of brains, so a machine would need the brain’s causal powers, not just its program, to genuinely understand or feel.","reading":[{"title":"The Chinese Room Argument (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/chinese-room/"},{"title":"Physicalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/physicalism/"}],"idea":"Biological naturalism has two parts. First, consciousness and understanding are perfectly natural, caused by brain processes and realised in the brain, like digestion in the stomach or photosynthesis in leaves. There is no soul and no mystery about whether minds belong to nature. Second, what matters is the biology, not the computation. A computer simulation of digestion digests nothing, and a simulation of a rainstorm leaves no one wet. Likewise, running a program that simulates a brain would not produce a mind unless the machine had causal powers equivalent to the brain’s. So programs alone cannot think. The view appeals to people who are both physicalists and sceptical that chatbots, however fluent, understand anything. It neither puts the mind outside nature nor reduces it to software.","example":"Searle’s Chinese room asks you to imagine yourself in a room following a rulebook for matching Chinese symbols. People outside slip in questions in Chinese, and your answers are so good they think a native speaker is inside. Yet you understand no Chinese; you only shuffle symbols. Since a computer running a program does the same, Searle argued, running the program is not enough for understanding.","history":"John Searle introduced the Chinese room in “Minds, Brains, and Programs” (Behavioral and Brain Sciences, 1980), coining the term “strong AI” for the view he attacked. He developed biological naturalism in Intentionality (1983), The Rediscovery of the Mind (1992) and The Mystery of Consciousness (1997). Ned Block and Hubert Dreyfus also raised early doubts about computational theories of mind.","challenge":"The systems reply: perhaps the person in the room doesn’t understand Chinese, but the whole system, person plus rulebook, does. Critics also ask which causal powers of brains matter, and why silicon couldn’t have them. Searle never specified them, which makes the view hard to test."},"mind:Patternist":{"summary":"You hold that you are the pattern of your mind, not the stuff it runs on, and that minds are information processing that could run on neurons or chips, so a perfect upload or teleport copy would be you.","reading":[{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"},{"title":"The Computational Theory of Mind (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/computational-mind/"},{"title":"Functionalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/functionalism/"}],"idea":"This view joins two ideas. On personal identity: what makes you you is your psychology, your memories, personality and ways of thinking, not the particular atoms in your body, which are constantly replaced anyway. On the nature of mind: a mind is a way of processing information, so it could run on different hardware, just as the same software runs on different computers. Put together, these suggest that if your mental pattern were perfectly copied to a new body or a computer, you would continue there. The teleporter that scans and rebuilds you on Mars is just very fast travel. The view appeals to many interested in technology because it promises that minds might one day be backed up or extended. Compare a song: it is the same song on vinyl, CD or streaming.","example":"In the teleporter case, a machine scans you, destroys the original and builds a perfect copy on Mars with all your memories. The patternist says you simply arrive on Mars. Turing’s imitation game supports the second idea: if a machine’s conversation cannot be told apart from a person’s, the patternist sees no principled reason to deny it a mind.","history":"John Locke’s Essay Concerning Human Understanding, in its 1694 edition, tied personal identity to consciousness and memory. Alan Turing’s “Computing Machinery and Intelligence” (1950) asked whether machines can think. Hilary Putnam developed functionalism in the 1960s. Hans Moravec’s Mind Children (1988) described uploading a mind to a computer, and Ray Kurzweil calls himself a patternist in The Singularity Is Near (2005).","challenge":"The duplication problem: if the machine made two copies, or left the original alive, both could not be you, yet each has an equal claim. This suggests copying is not survival. Critics such as Searle also deny that running the right program is enough for a mind at all."},"mind:Mysterian":{"summary":"You hold that the mind is physical and the brain produces experience somehow, but human minds may be built in a way that keeps us from ever understanding how.","reading":[{"title":"Consciousness (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consciousness/"},{"title":"The Hard Problem of Consciousness (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/hard-problem-of-conciousness/"}],"idea":"Mysterians agree with physicalists that consciousness arises naturally from the brain; there is no soul and no magic. But they doubt we will ever understand how. Every mind has limits set by how it is built. A dog cannot grasp algebra, not for lack of effort but because its brain lacks the right concepts. Humans may be in the same position with consciousness: the property of the brain that explains experience might lie outside the concepts we can form. We know experience from the inside and brains from the outside, and we may have no way to join the two. The appeal is honesty: after centuries of effort, the problem still seems as hard as ever, and the view explains why without giving up on naturalism. It is a claim about us, not about the world.","example":"Leibniz imagined a machine that thinks, enlarged so you could walk inside it like a mill. You would see parts pushing parts, he said, but nothing to explain perception. McGinn adds that our concepts come from either inner experience or outer observation, and neither alone can reveal the link between them, which leaves it beyond our grasp.","history":"Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz’s mill passage appears in the Monadology (1714). Thomas Nagel’s “What Is It Like to Be a Bat?” (1974) argued that we lack concepts to bridge subjective and objective. Colin McGinn’s “Can We Solve the Mind-Body Problem?” (Mind, 1989) introduced “cognitive closure”. Owen Flanagan coined the label “new mysterians” in 1991, and Noam Chomsky distinguished problems we can solve from mysteries we cannot.","challenge":"Critics call the view defeatist and untestable: how could we know a problem is beyond us, rather than just unsolved so far? Many past puzzles, such as how living things can arise from chemistry, looked hopeless before being solved. Others note that if we can’t understand the link, we also can’t be sure it is physical."},"mind:Functionalist":{"summary":"You hold that the mind is physical and that a mental state is defined by its role, what causes it, what it causes, and how it links to other states, so pain is whatever plays pain’s part.","reading":[{"title":"Functionalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/functionalism/"},{"title":"The Computational Theory of Mind (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/computational-mind/"},{"title":"Qualia (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/qualia/"}],"idea":"Functionalism asks what makes something a pain. Not, it says, the specific stuff it is made of, but the job it does. Pain is the state caused by bodily damage that makes you wince, want it to stop, avoid the cause and think about it. In humans this job is done by certain neurons. In an octopus, with a very different nervous system, other cells might do it. In principle, silicon chips could too. Mental states are like a goalkeeper: being a goalkeeper is defined by a role in the team, not by height or hair colour. Functionalism became the leading view in philosophy of mind and cognitive science because it explains how many creatures could share a mental state and allows that minds might be built from different materials, including perhaps computers.","example":"Ned Block’s “China brain” tests functionalism. Imagine the population of China, each person with a two-way radio, simulating the connections between neurons in a brain for an hour. The system plays all the right functional roles. Would it feel pain? Many say no, which suggests function alone leaves out experience. Functionalists often bite the bullet and say yes.","history":"Hilary Putnam proposed functionalism in “Psychological Predicates” (1967), arguing that mental states are multiply realisable. Jerry Fodor applied it in The Language of Thought (1975). David Lewis and D. M. Armstrong developed related role-based versions. Sydney Shoemaker defended functionalism about qualia. Ned Block’s “Troubles with Functionalism” (1978) offered the China brain objection.","challenge":"Functionalism seems to leave out how experiences feel. Two beings could, it seems, share every functional role while one sees red where the other sees green, the inverted spectrum, or while one has no experience at all. If so, function does not capture everything about the mind."},"mind:Mind-brain identity theorist":{"summary":"You hold that the mind is physical and that each kind of mental state simply is a kind of brain process, as lightning simply is electrical discharge: science discovers what pain is.","reading":[{"title":"The Mind/Brain Identity Theory (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mind-identity/"},{"title":"Physicalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/physicalism/"}],"idea":"The identity theory says that mental states are brain states, not in the sense of being caused by them or linked to them, but in the sense of being the very same thing. Pain is not something extra that accompanies certain nerve firings; it is those firings. This is an identity discovered by science, like the discoveries that water is H2O and lightning is electrical discharge. Before chemistry, people knew water but not its composition; before neuroscience, people knew pain but not what it is. The appeal is simplicity: there is just one kind of thing, so there is no puzzle about how minds and brains interact. Philosophers often use “C-fibre firing” as a stand-in for pain’s neural basis, though real pain processing is far more complex.","example":"J. J. C. Smart compared the identity to that between lightning and electrical discharge. When you see lightning, you are seeing an electrical discharge, even if you don’t know it. Smart argued that when you feel pain, you are undergoing a brain process, even if you don’t know it. That the words “pain” and “brain process” have different meanings does not show they refer to different things.","history":"U. T. Place’s “Is Consciousness a Brain Process?” (1956) and J. J. C. Smart’s “Sensations and Brain Processes” (1959) founded the view, together with Herbert Feigl. D. M. Armstrong’s A Materialist Theory of the Mind (1968) and David Lewis’s “An Argument for the Identity Theory” (1966) developed it. Its main critics were Hilary Putnam and Saul Kripke, writing in 1967 and 1980.","challenge":"Multiple realisability: if pain just is a particular human brain process, then octopuses, aliens or robots with different physical make-ups could not feel pain, which seems wrong. Kripke also argued that pain can be conceived without any brain state, unlike genuine scientific identities, which threatens the analogy with water and lightning."},"mind:Reasons-responsiveness compatibilist":{"summary":"You hold that free will fits with determinism, and that a choice is truly yours when it comes from your own reasoning, which would respond to good reasons to act differently.","reading":[{"title":"Compatibilism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/compatibilism/"},{"title":"Moral Responsibility (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-responsibility/"},{"title":"Moral Responsibility and the Principle of Alternative Possibilities (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/alternative-possibilities/"}],"idea":"Compatibilists think free will and determinism can both be true. This version explains freedom in terms of how your choices relate to reasons. Your choice is free and you are responsible for it when it comes from a mechanism in you that responds to reasons: it recognises good reasons and would, in at least some situations, lead you to act differently if those reasons were present. This captures why we excuse young children, people with severe delusions, and those acting under compulsion. Their behaviour does not track reasons as an adult’s normally does. Determinism doesn’t threaten this, because a reasons-responsive mechanism can work deterministically. The view also requires that you take ownership of the mechanism. Everyday example: a friend who drinks too much but would stop if told they had to drive is responsible; someone addicted might not be.","example":"Fischer uses Frankfurt-style cases. A device in your brain would switch you to vote for Ben if you were about to vote for Ana, but you choose Ben on your own. You could not have done otherwise, yet you seem responsible. Fischer explains this: the mechanism that actually produced your choice, your own reasoning, was responsive to reasons, even if the device made alternatives unavailable.","history":"John Martin Fischer and Mark Ravizza presented the view in Responsibility and Control: A Theory of Moral Responsibility (1998), distinguishing “guidance control” from “regulative control”. Fischer’s The Metaphysics of Free Will (1994) laid the groundwork. Susan Wolf’s Freedom Within Reason (1990) and Daniel Dennett’s Elbow Room (1984) offer related reason-based compatibilisms. Harry Frankfurt’s 1969 cases shaped the whole approach.","challenge":"Manipulation arguments, such as Pereboom’s four cases, describe agents whose reasons-responsive mechanisms were secretly engineered by others. Such agents seem not responsible, yet they meet the conditions. Critics ask what difference there is between being manipulated and being shaped by deterministic causes."},"mind:Parfitian reductionist":{"summary":"You hold that your identity follows your memories and personality, but in a branching case there is no fact about which copy is you, and that doesn’t matter: what you care about survives in both.","reading":[{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"},{"title":"Personal Identity and Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-ethics/"},{"title":"Personal Identity (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/person-i/"}],"idea":"Most of us think personal identity is what matters: when we look forward to the future, we care that it will be us. Parfit argued that this is a mistake. What matters is psychological connection and continuity: memories, plans, character and loves carrying on. Usually that goes with identity, but not always. Suppose the teleporter makes two copies. Each is equally continuous with you, and both can’t be you, since they are two different people. There is simply no fact about which one is you. Yet nothing you care about is lost: your memories, projects and relationships continue in both. So identity is not what matters. Parfit found this liberating: it made him care less about his own death and more about other people. Compare a club that splits in two, each keeping its traditions.","example":"Parfit’s branch-line case: the teleporter works, but the original on Earth is left alive and will die in a few days, while your replica lives on Mars. Many react with horror. Parfit argued that, on reflection, the relation between you and your replica contains everything that ordinarily matters in survival. Being destroyed and replicated is about as good as ordinary survival.","history":"Derek Parfit set out the view in Part Three of Reasons and Persons (1984), drawing on fission cases from David Wiggins’ Identity and Spatio-Temporal Continuity (1967) and his own paper “Personal Identity” (1971). He compared his view to the Buddha’s and wrote that it made his life feel less like a glass tunnel. Bernard Williams’ “The Self and the Future” (1970) gave cases pulling the other way.","challenge":"Many people find it hard to accept that they should care no more about their own future than about a duplicate’s. Critics such as Mark Johnston argue that the special concern we have for our own future is reasonable. Others note that Parfit’s view may unsettle ideas of desert, promises and prudence."},"mind:Reductionist about personal identity":{"summary":"You hold that a person’s existence just consists in physical and psychological facts, so once all those facts are known, “Is it still me?” has no further answer: it is a question about words.","reading":[{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"},{"title":"Personal Identity (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/person-i/"},{"title":"Personal Identity and Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-ethics/"}],"idea":"Reductionists say that a person is not a separate entity, like a soul, over and above their body, brain and mental life. Your existing over time just consists in certain physical and psychological continuities. Most of the time, this gives clear answers: you are the person who went to bed last night. But in puzzle cases, such as teleportation or gradual replacement of brain cells, the facts may leave the question open. Once we know exactly what happened to every cell and memory, there is no hidden extra fact about whether it is really you. The question becomes how to use the word “you”. Compare a club that changes members and venue over decades. Whether it is still the same club depends on how we choose to describe it, not on some extra fact.","example":"In the teleporter case, a machine scans your body, destroys it and builds a perfect copy on Mars with all your memories. The reductionist says: we know exactly what happened. There is physical discontinuity and full psychological continuity. Whether to call the Martian “you” is a decision about words, not a mystery to be solved. What matters is whether the relation is good enough.","history":"David Hume’s A Treatise of Human Nature (1739 to 1740) argued that the idea of a lasting self is a fiction built from linked perceptions. John Locke’s 1694 discussion of memory and identity is another root. Derek Parfit’s Reasons and Persons (1984) is the classic statement of reductionism. Sydney Shoemaker’s work and the Buddhist tradition of not-self are often linked to the view.","challenge":"Many feel there must be a fact about whether they will exist tomorrow, especially in cases involving pain or death: if someone tells you a future person will be tortured, “It’s up to us whether to call them you” seems no comfort. Non-reductionists say this suggests a deep further fact."},"mind:Animalist":{"summary":"You hold that you are a human animal, a living organism, so you persist as long as that organism lives, even through total memory loss, and a teleported copy would be someone else.","reading":[{"title":"Animalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/animalism/"},{"title":"Personal Identity (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/identity-personal/"}],"idea":"Animalism gives a straightforward answer to “What are you?” You are a human animal, a member of the species Homo sapiens. So you persist as long as that animal lives. Memory, personality and self-awareness are things you have, not what you are. You were once a fetus with no memories, and you might one day lose all your memories to illness, yet still be the same organism. A copy built on Mars from new materials, however exact, would be a different animal and so a different person. The appeal is common sense: when you look in the mirror, you see a human animal, and it seems strange to say that the thing thinking your thoughts is anything other than that animal. Compare a tree that loses its leaves each autumn: it is the same tree.","example":"Eric Olson’s thinking animal argument: there is a human animal sitting in your chair. It has a functioning brain, so it seems to think your thoughts. If you were something other than that animal, there would be two thinkers in your chair, you and the animal. Since that is absurd, you must be the animal.","history":"Aristotle treated human beings as rational animals. Modern animalism was developed by Peter van Inwagen in Material Beings (1990), Paul Snowdon in his 1990 paper “Persons, Animals, and Ourselves” and later book Persons, Animals, Ourselves (2014), and Eric Olson in The Human Animal: Personal Identity Without Psychology (1997). Olson’s What Are We? (2007) compares animalism with its rivals.","challenge":"The transplant intuition: if your brain were moved into another body, most people think you would go with it, not stay behind in the brainless body. Animalists must say either that you stay behind or that you become a mere part of an organism. Critics also note that psychology seems central to what we care about."},"mind:Free will agnostic":{"summary":"You hold that free will would require our choices to be undetermined, but that whether they are is an open empirical question about the brain, so you neither affirm nor deny that we have free will.","reading":[{"title":"Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freewill/"},{"title":"Incompatibilist (Nondeterministic) Theories of Free Will (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/incompatibilism-theories/"}],"idea":"Free will agnostics accept the libertarian claim that free will requires indeterminism: if the replay of a choice must come out the same, it was not free. But they think whether the brain is indeterministic in the right way is a question for science, not philosophy, and science has not settled it. Physics leaves room for genuine chance, but no one knows whether chance plays a role in the brain processes behind decisions. So the honest answer to “Do we have free will?” is “We don’t yet know.” The appeal is modesty: it refuses to claim more than the evidence supports in either direction. It also shifts attention from armchair arguments to research. Compare asking whether there is life on other planets: the question is meaningful, but only evidence can answer it.","example":"Balaguer focuses on torn decisions, where you have reasons on both sides and feel you could go either way, such as choosing between two universities you love equally. If the neural events behind such a decision are undetermined, he argues, then the choice is free in the libertarian sense. Whether they are is something neuroscience might eventually discover.","history":"Mark Balaguer defended this stance in Free Will as an Open Scientific Problem (MIT Press, 2010) and the shorter Free Will (MIT Press Essential Knowledge, 2014). He argues that the philosophical case against libertarian freedom fails, leaving the question to science. Benjamin Libet’s experiments on readiness potentials (1983) sparked wide debate about whether neuroscience can settle free will, though their interpretation remains disputed.","challenge":"Compatibilists deny that free will requires indeterminism, so for them the question is already answered. Skeptics argue that even undetermined choices would be a matter of luck, so agnosticism about the brain doesn’t leave free will open. Others doubt any experiment could detect the right kind of indeterminism."},"mind:Quietist about the mind-body problem":{"summary":"You hold that the question whether the mind is physical has no definite answer, because “physical” can’t be defined well, so the debate is less a factual dispute than a confusion.","reading":[{"title":"Physicalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/physicalism/"}],"idea":"Physicalism says everything is physical. But what does “physical” mean? This view, inspired by Hempel’s dilemma, argues that every answer goes wrong. If it means whatever today’s physics describes, physicalism is probably false, since physics is incomplete and will change. If it means whatever an ideal future physics would describe, the claim is close to empty, since that physics might include anything, even consciousness as a basic feature. Noam Chomsky adds that since Newton introduced action at a distance, there has been no clear concept of body or matter that the mind could be contrasted with. So asking whether the mind is physical is less like asking whether whales are fish and more like a question that hasn’t been made precise. The appeal is to avoid fighting over a word.","example":"Chomsky points out that when Newton described gravity acting at a distance, many, including Newton himself, found it unintelligible on the mechanical picture of matter. Scientists accepted it anyway, and the idea of “body” expanded. Similarly, he suggests, if the study of the mind finds strange properties, they will just be added to our picture of nature; there is no fixed concept of the physical to violate.","history":"Carl Hempel posed the dilemma in writings of 1969 and 1980 about physicalism and physics. Noam Chomsky developed his doubts about the concept of body in New Horizons in the Study of Language and Mind (2000). Bas van Fraassen’s The Empirical Stance (2002) also argued that physicalism is not a factual thesis. Barbara Montero and others have discussed the problem of defining the physical.","challenge":"Many philosophers answer that “physical” can be defined well enough: for example, as whatever involves no fundamental mentality, so physicalism says that mind is not basic. On that definition the question is meaningful and open. Critics also say that dissolving the debate seems to sidestep the hard problem rather than solve it."},"mind:Physicalist":{"summary":"You hold that the mind is entirely the working of the brain and body: thoughts and feelings are physical processes, and nothing non-physical is needed to explain them.","reading":[{"title":"Physicalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/physicalism/"},{"title":"The Mind/Brain Identity Theory (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mind-identity/"},{"title":"Consciousness (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consciousness/"}],"idea":"Physicalism says everything that exists is physical or depends entirely on the physical. Applied to the mind, it holds that thinking, feeling and choosing are things your brain and body do, just as digestion is something your stomach does. There is no soul and no extra non-physical property. Physicalists point to strong evidence: damage to particular brain areas affects particular abilities; drugs alter mood and thought; brain scans can detect what someone is attending to. They also note that physics seems causally closed: every physical event has a full physical cause, leaving no room for a non-physical mind to act. Physicalists differ among themselves: some identify mental states with brain states, some define them by their roles, some think parts of our mental self-image are illusions. Physicalism is the most popular view among philosophers today.","example":"A common physicalist argument from causation runs: your decision to raise your arm causes the arm to rise. That rise has a complete physical cause in nerve signals and muscle contractions. Unless the arm rise is caused twice over, the decision must be one of those physical causes. So mental events are physical events.","history":"Ancient atomists such as Democritus, and the Cārvāka school in India, held materialist views. Thomas Hobbes defended materialism in Leviathan (1651). In the 20th century, J. J. C. Smart, D. M. Armstrong, David Lewis and Daniel Dennett gave influential versions. In the 2020 PhilPapers Survey of professional philosophers, about half accepted or leaned towards physicalism about the mind, more than any rival view.","challenge":"The hard problem of consciousness: even a complete account of the brain’s physical processes seems to leave unexplained why there is anything it is like to have experiences. Arguments such as Jackson’s Mary and Chalmers’ zombies aim to show that physical facts do not settle all the facts about experience."},"mind:Non-physicalist":{"summary":"You hold that at least some features of the mind, above all how experiences feel, cannot be fully explained by physical processes, so even a complete brain science would leave something out.","reading":[{"title":"Dualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/"},{"title":"The Hard Problem of Consciousness (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/hard-problem-of-conciousness/"},{"title":"Consciousness (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consciousness/"}],"idea":"Non-physicalists agree on what physicalism misses, even if they disagree about what fills the gap. The key feature is the felt quality of experience: what it is like to taste coffee, hear music or feel pain. Neuroscience can track which brain areas activate, but, they argue, describing neural activity seems to leave untouched why it feels like anything at all. Non-physicalists then divide. Substance dualists posit a soul. Property dualists say brains have non-physical properties. Panpsychists say experience is part of matter everywhere. Idealists say reality is mental at bottom. Neutral monists say both mind and matter come from something more basic. The appeal is that it takes seriously what we seem to know most directly, our own experience. Explaining what a song does to the eardrum doesn’t seem to explain how it sounds.","example":"Thomas Nagel asked what it is like to be a bat, which navigates by echolocation. We could learn everything about a bat’s brain and still not know what echolocation feels like from the inside. Nagel concluded that physical science, which describes things from an objective standpoint, may be unable to capture the subjective character of experience.","history":"Thomas Nagel’s “What Is It Like to Be a Bat?” (1974), Frank Jackson’s “Epiphenomenal Qualia” (1982), Saul Kripke’s Naming and Necessity (1980) and David Chalmers’ The Conscious Mind (1996) are central works. Earlier, Leibniz’s mill argument in the Monadology (1714) and Descartes’ Meditations (1641) argued that mind cannot be explained mechanically. Philip Goff and Galen Strawson defend non-physicalist positions today.","challenge":"If non-physical features exist, how do they relate to the brain? Either they cause physical events, which seems to conflict with physics being causally closed, or they don’t, which makes them idle and leaves it puzzling how we can know or talk about them."},"reality:Occasionalist":{"summary":"Occasionalists hold that God is the only true cause: fire never burns cotton by its own power, since God creates the burning each time. Natural regularities are God’s habits, not powers inside things.","reading":[{"title":"Occasionalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/occasionalism/"},{"title":"al-Ghazali (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/al-ghazali/"}],"idea":"Occasionalists agree that the world is full of reliable patterns: fire is followed by burning, food by fullness, a push by motion. What they deny is that the things themselves do the work. Created things have no power of their own. Each event is created directly by God, and the “cause” we see is only the occasion on which God acts. Why believe this? Some find it the best way to honour God’s total power, so that nothing happens apart from God. Others note, as Hume later did, that we never actually observe a cause making its effect happen; we see only one thing after another. Occasionalism fills that gap with God. Everyday example: when you flip a switch and the light comes on, the switch did not produce the light. God reliably creates light whenever the switch is flipped.","example":"Al-Ghazali’s classic case is cotton touching fire. We see contact, then burning, and assume the fire did it. But observation shows only that burning happens together with contact, not because of it. God could, if He willed, let the cotton touch the fire without burning, as in the story of Abraham thrown into the fire unharmed. Miracles are then no harder for God than ordinary events.","history":"The Ash’arite school of Islamic theology developed occasionalism from the tenth century. Al-Ghazali (died 1111) gave its most famous defence in The Incoherence of the Philosophers, attacking philosophers who held that causes act by necessity. Ibn Rushd (Averroes) replied in The Incoherence of the Incoherence. In seventeenth century Europe, Louis de La Forge and Géraud de Cordemoy held related views, and Nicolas Malebranche defended occasionalism fully in The Search after Truth (1674 to 1675).","challenge":"If God does everything, created things seem idle, and science’s talk of what fire, magnets or genes do becomes strictly false. Leibniz complained that occasionalism turns nature into a perpetual miracle. It also makes God the direct cause of every harmful event and, some argue, of every human act."},"reality:Logical positivist":{"summary":"Logical positivists hold that a factual claim has meaning only if experience could check it. Untestable worries, like an undetectable dream, and disputes about whether scattered things form an object, are about words or frameworks, not the world.","reading":[{"title":"Vienna Circle (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/vienna-circle/"},{"title":"Logical Empiricism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logical-empiricism/"},{"title":"Rudolf Carnap (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/carnap/"}],"idea":"Logical positivists wanted philosophy to be as clear as science. Their test: a sentence is either true by meaning (like “all bachelors are unmarried”) or it says something about the world that observation could confirm or disconfirm. Anything else is not false but empty. This test does two jobs in this view. It dissolves the dream worry, since a perfect dream no test could ever expose makes no checkable difference. And it dissolves puzzles about composition, since once all the facts about particles are settled, asking whether they “really” form one more thing is a choice about language, not a discovery. The appeal: endless debates turn out to be confusions. Everyday example: two friends argue whether a hot dog is “really” a sandwich. Once they agree on every fact about the bread and sausage, nothing is left to discover; they are only deciding how to use a word.","example":"Rudolf Carnap imagined two geographers, one a realist and one an idealist, sent to check whether a certain mountain exists. They would climb it, measure it and agree on every observable fact. Their disagreement about whether the mountain is “really” mind-independent changes no possible observation. So, Carnap concluded, it is a pseudo-question, not a deep one.","history":"The movement began with the Vienna Circle in the 1920s, led by Moritz Schlick, with Rudolf Carnap, Otto Neurath and others, influenced by Wittgenstein’s Tractatus (1921). Carnap’s Pseudoproblems in Philosophy (1928) applied the test to realism versus idealism, and his “Empiricism, Semantics, and Ontology” (1950) treated questions about what exists as choices of linguistic framework. A. J. Ayer brought the view to English readers in Language, Truth and Logic (1936).","challenge":"The verification principle seems to fail its own test: it is neither true by definition nor checkable by observation. Attempts to state it precisely either let in nonsense or ruled out real science. Quine’s “Two Dogmas of Empiricism” (1951) also attacked the sharp line between truths of meaning and truths of fact."},"reality:Quasi-realist":{"summary":"Quasi-realists think moral claims express attitudes, not descriptions of moral facts, yet they happily call moral claims true and some attitudes better than others. A deflationary view of truth lets them talk just like realists.","reading":[{"title":"Moral Cognitivism vs. Non-Cognitivism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-cognitivism/"},{"title":"Deflationism About Truth (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/truth-deflationary/"}],"idea":"Quasi-realism starts from expressivism: when you say “cruelty is wrong,” you are voicing disapproval, not reporting a fact about the universe. But plain expressivism seems to clash with how we talk. We say moral claims are true, argue about them, and think people can be mistaken. Quasi-realists try to earn the right to all of this. Two parts fit together. First, a deflationary view of truth: “it’s true that cruelty is wrong” says no more than “cruelty is wrong,” so calling it true needs no moral facts out in the world. Second, attitudes can be ranked: some are better informed, more consistent and more stable under reflection. The appeal is getting the scientific modesty of anti-realism without giving up ordinary moral talk. Everyday example: you can say “slavery was wrong even when people approved of it”, as a firm attitude about all times.","example":"Ask a quasi-realist: “Would kicking dogs be wrong if everyone approved of it?” A naive expressivist might seem forced to say no. The quasi-realist says yes. The question asks for a moral verdict, and their verdict, an attitude about the act, not about anyone’s feelings, is that kicking dogs for fun is wrong whatever people think.","history":"Simon Blackburn coined the term and developed the project in Spreading the Word (1984), Essays in Quasi-Realism (1993) and Ruling Passions (1998). He took inspiration from David Hume’s idea that the mind “spreads itself” on the world, projecting feelings onto things. Allan Gibbard developed a related expressivism in Wise Choices, Apt Feelings (1990) and Thinking How to Live (2003). Deflationary views of truth came from Frank Ramsey and later Paul Horwich.","challenge":"The Frege-Geach problem, pressed by Peter Geach, asks how “lying is wrong” keeps the same meaning inside “if lying is wrong, then getting your brother to lie is wrong,” where no attitude is expressed. A second worry: if quasi-realists can say everything realists say, it is unclear what the difference between the views still is."},"reality:Buddhist reductionist":{"summary":"Buddhist reductionists hold that wholes, including persons, are convenient names for collections of parts: a chariot is its parts arranged, a self a stream of bodily and mental events. Clinging to a lasting self causes suffering.","reading":[{"title":"Buddha (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/buddha/"},{"title":"Mind in Indian Buddhist Philosophy (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mind-indian-buddhism/"},{"title":"Mereology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mereology/"}],"idea":"This view combines two claims. First, about objects: strictly, there are no chariots, houses or noses, only basic elements arranged in certain ways. Words for wholes are useful labels, “conventionally true” but not ultimately true. Second, about persons: the self is treated the same way. There is no unchanging owner behind your experiences, only a flow of body, feelings, perceptions, mental habits and consciousness, the five “aggregates.” The two claims support each other: if no whole is more than its parts, the self cannot be either. Its purpose is practical. Seeing through the self is meant to loosen craving and fear, which Buddhists see as the roots of suffering. Everyday example: a football club keeps its name for a century though every player has been replaced. The name tracks a continuing series, not some hidden extra thing. Buddhist reductionists say you are like that.","example":"In the Questions of King Milinda, the monk Nāgasena asks the king whether his chariot is the axle, the wheels, the frame or the reins. It is none of them alone, and nothing beyond them all. “Chariot” is a name used for the parts in a certain arrangement. In the same way, Nāgasena says, his own name is only a label for a collection of changing parts.","history":"The teaching of non-self (anātman) goes back to the Buddha (fifth century BCE, dates uncertain). The Abhidharma schools systematised it, listing the momentary elements (dharmas) that are ultimately real; Vasubandhu’s Abhidharmakośa (around the fourth or fifth century CE) is a major summary. The chariot dialogue is from the Milindapañha. The label “Buddhist Reductionism” comes from Mark Siderits, Personal Identity and Buddhist Philosophy (2003), and Derek Parfit compared his own view to the Buddha’s in Reasons and Persons (1984).","challenge":"If there is no self, who suffers, who acts, and who reaches liberation? Buddhists reply that a stream of events can do all of this, but critics doubt that experience makes sense without a subject. Within Buddhism, Madhyamaka thinkers like Nāgārjuna argued the Abhidharma elements are themselves empty, so the reduction never reaches a final level."},"reality:Idealist":{"summary":"Idealists think reality is made of minds and their experiences. There is no hidden world of matter behind appearances, so the skeptic’s gap between appearance and reality never opens: your hands are what you see and feel.","reading":[{"title":"Idealism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/idealism/"},{"title":"George Berkeley (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/berkeley/"},{"title":"Vasubandhu (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/vasubandhu/"}],"idea":"Most people picture two layers: experiences in our heads, and a material world outside causing them. The skeptic then asks how we know the outside layer matches the inside one. Idealists remove the outside layer. A table just is something seen, touched and expected to be seen and touched again. To be, for ordinary objects, is to be perceived. So there is nothing behind experience for a dream to fake. Idealists still distinguish dreams from waking life: waking experience is vivid, orderly and shared, while dreams are patchy. The appeal is twofold. It answers skepticism at the root, and it avoids the puzzle of how lifeless matter could ever produce experience. Everyday example: an apple, for you, is redness, crispness, sweetness and smell. Take away all of these. The idealist says nothing does, so the apple is that bundle of perceptions, not something hidden behind them.","example":"Berkeley’s Three Dialogues pits Philonous (the idealist) against Hylas (the materialist). Philonous asks Hylas to conceive of a tree existing unperceived; but in conceiving it, Hylas is already perceiving it in thought. What then keeps the tree in the quad when no one looks? For Berkeley, God perceives everything continuously, so objects do not flicker in and out of existence.","history":"George Berkeley set out the view in A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge (1710) and Three Dialogues between Hylas and Philonous (1713), with the slogan “to be is to be perceived.” In India, the Yogācāra Buddhist Vasubandhu argued in his Twenty Verses (around the fourth or fifth century CE) that we experience only consciousness, using the dream as an example of experience without outer objects. Later idealisms, like Hegel’s, took different forms.","challenge":"Science describes a world that existed long before any minds, with dinosaurs and stars no one perceived. Idealists must explain this, often by appealing to God or to possible perceptions, which critics find costly. Samuel Johnson famously kicked a stone in reply, though Berkeley could answer that the kick and the hardness are experiences too."},"reality:Pyrrhonian skeptic":{"summary":"Pyrrhonian skeptics find that every argument about hidden reality can be matched by an equally strong one against it. So they suspend judgment, live by how things appear, custom and need, and find calm as a result.","reading":[{"title":"Ancient Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-ancient/"},{"title":"Sextus Empiricus (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sextus-empiricus/"},{"title":"Pyrrho (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/pyrrho/"}],"idea":"Pyrrhonism is not the claim that knowledge is impossible. That would be another bold belief. Instead, it is a practice. Pick any question about how things really are, like whether honey is truly sweet or the world truly orderly, and you will find arguments on both sides that seem equally strong. Faced with this balance, the skeptic suspends judgment. Life goes on: they eat when hungry, follow local customs, practise a trade, and say “honey seems sweet to me.” What they drop is the extra claim about how things are beyond appearance. The dream worry is handled the same way. The surprising promise is peace of mind (ataraxia). People are troubled, Pyrrhonists said, by believing things are really good or bad. Everyday example: someone stops agonising over whether a career choice is objectively the right one and simply acts on how things seem, finding they are calmer.","example":"Sextus Empiricus tells of the painter Apelles, who could not paint a horse’s foam and in frustration threw his sponge at the picture. The mark it left looked exactly like foam. Skeptics, Sextus says, are similar: they sought calm by trying to settle every question, failed, gave up and suspended judgment, and then found calm followed, as if by chance.","history":"Pyrrho of Elis (around 360 to 270 BCE), who travelled with Alexander the Great to India, wrote nothing; his student Timon reported his views. Aenesidemus revived the tradition in the first century BCE and listed Ten Modes for producing suspension. Sextus Empiricus’s Outlines of Pyrrhonism (around the second century CE) is the main surviving source. Its rediscovery shaped the Renaissance, above all Michel de Montaigne’s “Apology for Raymond Sebond” in his Essays (1580).","challenge":"The oldest objection is that no one can live without beliefs: to walk away from a cliff edge, you must believe it is dangerous. Pyrrhonists reply that acting on appearances is enough. Critics also ask whether suspending judgment is psychologically possible, and whether it really brings calm rather than anxiety."},"reality:Humean skeptic":{"summary":"Humean skeptics accept that reason cannot defeat skepticism about the external world, but think human nature makes belief unavoidable. Doubts that seem unanswerable in the study fade in ordinary life, leaving a modest, mitigated skepticism.","reading":[{"title":"David Hume (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume/"},{"title":"Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism/"}],"idea":"This view takes the skeptic’s arguments seriously. You cannot prove you are not dreaming, and no chain of reasoning shows that the future will resemble the past. But Humean skeptics draw a different lesson from the Pyrrhonists. Suspending belief is not really an option. Nature has built us to believe in bodies, causes and other minds. So the proper response is a mixture: humility about what argument can establish, and trust in instinct and habit for practical life. Philosophy shows the limits of reason; nature carries us past them. The attraction is honesty. It neither pretends to refute the skeptic nor pretends to live as one. Everyday example: after a late night reading about simulation theory, you may feel shaken. Next morning, you make breakfast, catch the bus and argue with friends, and the doubt has no grip at all, even though you never answered it.","example":"At the end of Book 1 of his Treatise, Hume describes falling into deep philosophical gloom, unable to answer his own skeptical arguments. Reason cannot cure it. What does is ordinary life: he dines, plays backgammon and talks with friends, and after a few hours the arguments seem cold and strained. He returns to philosophy later, but more modestly.","history":"David Hume set out this position in A Treatise of Human Nature (1739 to 1740), especially the conclusion of Book 1, and in An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748), whose final section recommends “mitigated” skepticism over the extreme Pyrrhonian kind. In the twentieth century, P. F. Strawson’s Skepticism and Naturalism (1985) developed the idea that some beliefs are too deeply rooted in human nature to need, or allow, justification.","challenge":"Critics say the view gives up too much. If belief in the external world rests only on instinct, not reasons, then we do not really know there is one, and the skeptic has won after all. Others ask why nature’s compulsion should count as any kind of answer rather than a confession."},"reality:Academic skeptic":{"summary":"Academic skeptics deny that we have certain knowledge, but think some impressions are more persuasive than others. They hold beliefs as more or less probable, test them harder when more is at stake, and act on the best available.","reading":[{"title":"Carneades (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/carneades/"},{"title":"Ancient Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-ancient/"}],"idea":"Academic skeptics agree that you cannot rule out dreaming, so you do not strictly know you have hands. But they reject the Pyrrhonian conclusion that you should stop believing. Some impressions are clearer, more coherent with others, and better tested than others. These are “persuasive,” and it is rational to go with them, while always admitting you might be wrong. So belief comes in degrees, and knowledge, in the strict sense of certainty, is a label never earned. The appeal is that this looks like how careful people already reason, and how science works: no certainty, but well tested best guesses that you are ready to revise. Everyday example: a doctor cannot be certain a patient has a certain illness, but after examining symptoms and running tests, she treats the most probable diagnosis while staying open to new evidence. The Academic skeptic does that with everything.","example":"Carneades used the case of a coiled rope in a dark room. At first glance it looks like a snake, and you might jump back. If there is time, you test the impression: poke it, see it does not move, look more closely. Each check makes “it is a rope” more persuasive. You still lack certainty, but you have enough to act sensibly.","history":"Arcesilaus (around 316 to 241 BCE), head of Plato’s Academy, turned it toward skepticism, arguing against the Stoic claim that some impressions guarantee their truth. Carneades (214 to 129 BCE) developed the idea of the persuasive impression and, on an embassy to Rome in 155 BCE, argued for and against justice on successive days. Cicero’s Academica (45 BCE) preserves the debate, and Augustine attacked the view in Against the Academics (386).","challenge":"If nothing is known, how can we tell what is more probable? Probability seems to need some known baseline, like a knowledge of how often similar impressions turned out true. Pyrrhonists added that ranking impressions as persuasive is already a hidden claim about reality, so Academics are less skeptical than they claim."},"reality:Internal realist":{"summary":"Internal realists think science really is right about electrons and black holes, but understand truth as what would be rationally accepted under ideal inquiry. Facts are real, yet only within a conceptual scheme, not seen from nowhere.","reading":[{"title":"The Pragmatic Theory of Truth (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/truth-pragmatic/"},{"title":"Scientific Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/scientific-realism/"}],"idea":"This view tries to keep what is right in both scientific realism and pragmatism. With realists, it holds that electrons and black holes exist much as our best theories say. With pragmatists, it denies that truth is a mysterious match between our words and a world described from no point of view at all. Instead, a statement is true if it would be accepted by inquirers under ideal conditions, with all relevant evidence in. Truth is still objective: believing does not make it so. But what counts as an object or a fact depends partly on the concepts we bring. It avoids both “anything goes” relativism and an impossible God’s-eye view. Everyday example: asking how many things are on your desk has no answer until you decide whether to count pens, pen caps, molecules or the desk set as a whole. Fix the scheme, and the answer is factual.","example":"Putnam imagined a world with three individuals, x1, x2 and x3. How many objects are there? Using ordinary counting, three. But a logician who counts every combination of individuals as an object says seven. Putnam argued neither answer is wrong. Once a way of counting is chosen, the answer is objective; asking which way the world itself prefers makes no sense.","history":"Hilary Putnam introduced internal realism in Reason, Truth and History (1981), which also contains his brain-in-a-vat argument, and developed it in The Many Faces of Realism (1987). He defined truth as idealised rational acceptability, drawing on Peirce and on Kant. In the 1990s, especially his Dewey Lectures (1994), Putnam moved away from this definition toward what he called a “natural” or commonsense realism, while keeping his conceptual relativity.","challenge":"Defining truth as ideal acceptability seems to make truth depend on what we could find out, yet some facts, like the exact number of hairs on Caesar’s head the day he died, may be lost forever. Critics also argue the idea of “ideal conditions” can only be explained by using the very notion of truth it was meant to replace."},"reality:Constructive empiricist":{"summary":"Constructive empiricists think science aims to get the observable world right. Accepting a theory means believing it fits all observations while staying agnostic about unobservables like electrons, though theories are still read literally.","reading":[{"title":"Constructive Empiricism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/constructive-empiricism/"},{"title":"Scientific Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/scientific-realism/"}],"idea":"When a physicist talks about electrons, is she describing hidden things that really exist? The constructive empiricist says: maybe, but science does not need us to believe it. Theories should be taken at face value, as genuinely claiming that electrons exist. But the aim of science is only that theories be “empirically adequate”: true about everything observable, past, present and future. To accept a theory is to believe that much, while suspending belief about the unobservable parts. Why limit belief this way? Because history is full of successful theories whose hidden entities later vanished, and because observation is our only check. Belief beyond it is risk without payoff. Everyday example: you can trust a GPS app because it reliably gets you to the right places, without having any view about how its servers actually work. The constructive empiricist treats electrons a bit like the servers.","example":"Where does “observable” end? For van Fraassen, the moons of Jupiter count as observable, since astronauts could fly close and see them unaided, even if we now use telescopes. Particles in a cloud chamber do not: we see only a trail, and no human could ever see the particle itself. So we may believe in Jupiter’s moons, but stay agnostic about the electron.","history":"Bas van Fraassen introduced and named the view in The Scientific Image (1980) as an alternative to scientific realism, and developed it in Laws and Symmetry (1989) and The Empirical Stance (2002). It has older roots in thinkers who doubted unobservables, such as Pierre Duhem, who saw physical theory as a way to organise phenomena, and Ernst Mach, who long doubted the reality of atoms.","challenge":"Grover Maxwell argued that there is no sharp line between observing with eyes, glasses, microscopes and particle detectors, so the line between observable and unobservable seems arbitrary. Realists also press the “no miracles” argument: if theories about unobservables were not roughly true, their predictive success would be an astonishing coincidence."},"reality:Pragmatist about science":{"summary":"Pragmatists about science reject the choice between theories as mirrors of hidden reality and theories as mere tools. Inquiry is problem-solving, and “getting it right” means producing theories that keep working as testing goes on.","reading":[{"title":"Pragmatism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pragmatism/"},{"title":"John Dewey (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dewey/"},{"title":"Scientific Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/scientific-realism/"}],"idea":"The realism debate pictures a gap between our theories and the world, then asks whether theories cross it. Pragmatists question the picture. Science, for them, is a form of inquiry that starts with real problems, proposes answers and tests them in practice. A good theory is one that keeps working: it predicts, explains, guides experiments and survives further testing. That is what “getting it right” has always meant. So pragmatists see no special problem about electrons as opposed to tables. Both are things our best working theories talk about, and talk of them earns its place the same way. The appeal is that it ties philosophical words like “truth” and “reality” to what scientists actually do. Everyday example: a mechanic does not ask whether her model of the engine mirrors reality from some absolute viewpoint. She asks whether it lets her diagnose faults and fix cars.","example":"Peirce’s pragmatic maxim says that to make an idea clear, ask what practical effects it would have. Apply it to “this is hard”: it means scratching it would fail with many substances. Apply it to “electrons exist”: it means certain experiments, devices and predictions will work. On this view, there is no further difference between “electrons are a useful posit” and “electrons are real.”","history":"Charles Sanders Peirce founded pragmatism in papers like “The Fixation of Belief” (1877) and “How to Make Our Ideas Clear” (1878). William James popularised it in Pragmatism (1907), and John Dewey developed a full theory of inquiry in Logic: The Theory of Inquiry (1938). Richard Rorty revived the tradition with Philosophy and the Mirror of Nature (1979), rejecting the idea of knowledge as an accurate mirror of reality.","challenge":"Realists ask why some theories keep working while others fail. The natural answer is that successful theories are roughly true of a world that exists independently of us. If pragmatists accept this, they seem to become realists; if they refuse, they seem unable to explain success. Critics also worry that “what works” is too vague."},"reality:Quinean naturalist":{"summary":"Quinean naturalists take science as the best guide to what exists. Our best theories are roughly true and cannot be stated without numbers, so we should believe in numbers just as we believe in electrons.","reading":[{"title":"Indispensability Arguments in the Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mathphil-indis/"},{"title":"Willard Van Orman Quine (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/quine/"},{"title":"Naturalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/naturalism/"}],"idea":"Quinean naturalists start with a refusal: there is no philosophy standing above science, judging it from outside. Philosophy is part of the same web of knowledge. So, to find out what exists, look at what our best scientific theories need to say exists. Those theories mention electrons, so we should believe in electrons. But the same theories also mention numbers, functions and sets, and could not work without them. The evidence for physics is therefore evidence for the mathematics inside it. That is why this view combines scientific realism with belief in abstract mathematical objects. The appeal is consistency and modesty: no special faculty of pure reason, no armchair metaphysics, just taking science at its word. Everyday example: if you trust a weather forecast built on equations involving real numbers, you are already relying on those numbers doing real work, not just on the clouds.","example":"Take Newton’s law of gravitation: the force between two bodies equals a constant times their masses divided by the square of their distance. It quantifies over real numbers. You cannot believe what it says about planets without believing what it says about numbers. Quine and Putnam concluded that if the law is confirmed, so are the numbers it relies on.","history":"W. V. O. Quine set out the outlook in “On What There Is” (1948), “Two Dogmas of Empiricism” (1951) and “Epistemology Naturalized” (1969), holding that to be is to be the value of a bound variable in our best theory. He accepted sets only reluctantly, for this reason. Hilary Putnam gave the indispensability argument its classic statement in Philosophy of Logic (1971), and Mark Colyvan defended it in The Indispensability of Mathematics (2001).","challenge":"Scientists use idealisations, like frictionless planes or infinitely deep oceans, without believing in them, so being needed by a theory may not mean being believed in, as Penelope Maddy argued. Hartry Field also tried to show that physics could be done without numbers. And unused parts of mathematics seem just as true as the parts physics uses."},"reality:Rationalist Platonist":{"summary":"Rationalist Platonists think pure reason can give knowledge of reality, and that mathematics is the clearest case: numbers exist independently of minds and matter, and mathematicians discover truths about them by thinking alone.","reading":[{"title":"Platonism in the Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/platonism-mathematics/"},{"title":"Rationalism vs. Empiricism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rationalism-empiricism/"},{"title":"Kurt Gödel (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/goedel/"}],"idea":"Two ideas support each other here. The Platonist idea: numbers and other mathematical objects are real, existing outside space and time, unchanged whether anyone counts. The rationalist idea: reason alone can reach important truths without observation. Put together, mathematics becomes the model of knowledge: certain, necessary, and about something real, yet discovered with eyes closed. Rationalist Platonists point out that mathematicians talk of discovering, not inventing, and that results often surprise their discoverers. Mathematics written for its own beauty later turns out to describe the physical world, which is easy to explain if both answer to a real, rational structure. Everyday example: no one has ever checked all whole numbers, yet everyone who follows the proof knows there is no largest prime. Experience could never deliver that certainty. Reason does, and, the rationalist Platonist adds, what it reveals was true before any human thought of it.","example":"In Plato’s Meno, Socrates draws a square and asks an uneducated slave boy how to double its area. The boy first guesses wrongly, then, guided only by questions, sees that the square built on the diagonal is the answer. Socrates claims the boy was not taught but recalled: the truth was already accessible to his reason.","history":"Plato (around 427 to 347 BCE) held that eternal Forms, including mathematical ones, are known by reason, in the Meno, Phaedo and Republic. René Descartes, in the Fifth Meditation (1641), argued that a triangle has a true nature not invented by him. Kurt Gödel, in “What is Cantor’s Continuum Problem?” (revised 1964), wrote that we have something like a perception of mathematical objects through intuition.","challenge":"Paul Benacerraf’s “Mathematical Truth” (1973) asked how we could know about objects outside space and time that cannot affect our brains in any way. Normally knowledge needs some connection with what is known. Rationalist Platonists must explain this mysterious access, without just giving it a name like “intuition.”"},"reality:Humean naturalist":{"summary":"Humean naturalists see the world as a vast four-dimensional mosaic of events with no necessary connections among them. All times are equally real, and causes and laws are patterns in the mosaic, not forces behind it.","reading":[{"title":"David Lewis (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/david-lewis/"},{"title":"Laws of Nature (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/laws-of-nature/"},{"title":"Time (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/time/"}],"idea":"Picture the whole history of the universe laid out at once, like a giant mosaic spread across space and time. Each tile is a local fact: this much mass here, this charge there, at this moment. The past and future tiles are as real as the present ones; “now” is just your location. Nothing glues the tiles together. Causation and laws of nature are not hidden forces making events happen. They are the patterns in the mosaic, the simplest and most informative summaries of how the tiles are arranged. If all times exist together, nothing needs to “push” the future into being. The appeal is economy and fit with physics: no mysterious connections, no special present, just events and their patterns. Everyday example: laws are like the description of a wallpaper design. The design does not make each flower appear; it summarises where the flowers are.","example":"Why does dropping a glass cause it to break? For a Humean naturalist, it is because glass droppings like this one are, across the whole mosaic, followed by breakings, and this regularity follows from the best system of laws, the simplest description of the world that captures the most information. Nothing over and above the pattern makes this glass break.","history":"David Lewis named the core doctrine “Humean supervenience” in the introduction to his Philosophical Papers, Volume II (1986), describing the world as a vast mosaic of local matters of particular fact. His best-system view of laws built on ideas from John Stuart Mill and Frank Ramsey. J. J. C. Smart defended the tenseless, all-times-real view in Philosophy and Scientific Realism (1963), drawing on relativity, and both claimed inspiration from Hume.","challenge":"If laws are only summaries of events, can they explain those events? It seems circular to say the glass broke because of a pattern partly made up of such breakings. Critics like David Armstrong argued laws must be something more. Others say the view cannot capture our experience of time passing."},"reality:Humean empiricist":{"summary":"Humean empiricists hold that all knowledge of the world comes from experience, and experience never reveals a necessary connection between cause and effect, only one event regularly following another, plus our habit of expecting it.","reading":[{"title":"David Hume (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume/"},{"title":"Regularity and Inferential Theories of Causation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causation-regularity/"}],"idea":"Start with empiricism: every idea and every fact about the world traces back to experience. Now look for the idea of causal power, the “must” in “the ball must move when struck.” Watch any causal event closely and you find only a sequence: one thing, then another, then the same again on other occasions. You never see the necessity itself. So, the Humean empiricist concludes, causation in the world is regular succession, and the felt “must” comes from us, from the habit of expecting the usual result. The two parts fit tightly: empiricism is what rules out any hidden connection, because nothing in experience shows one. The appeal is intellectual honesty about what we observe. Everyday example: a child touches a hot stove once and avoids it afterwards. She has seen heat followed by pain, not a necessity linking them, yet habit makes her expectation completely firm.","example":"Hume’s famous case is two billiard balls. One rolls into another, and the second moves. Someone seeing this for the first time, Hume argues, could not predict the result by reason alone; the first ball might just as well stop or bounce back. Only after seeing many collisions do we expect the movement, and that expectation is custom, not insight.","history":"David Hume combined these views in A Treatise of Human Nature (1739 to 1740) and An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748), especially the section “Of the Idea of Necessary Connexion.” The fourteenth century thinker Nicholas of Autrecourt had argued that one cannot infer one thing’s existence from another with certainty. In India, the Cārvāka school accepted perception as the only reliable source of knowledge. Hume’s views shaped Mill and the logical positivists.","challenge":"If there are no necessary connections, why expect the future to resemble the past? This is Hume’s own problem of induction, and the view seems to leave it unsolvable. Thomas Reid also noted that night regularly follows day without day causing night, so regular succession cannot be all there is to causation."},"reality:Verificationist":{"summary":"Verificationists hold that a statement has factual meaning only if some possible experience could count for or against it. A dream or deception no test could ever detect makes no real claim, so the skeptic’s question dissolves.","reading":[{"title":"Vienna Circle (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/vienna-circle/"},{"title":"Logical Empiricism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logical-empiricism/"}],"idea":"Verificationism is a test for meaning. To know what a factual sentence says, ask what observations would show it true or false. If none could, even in principle, the sentence may sound meaningful but says nothing about the world. Applied to skepticism, the result is striking. The skeptic imagines a dream so perfect that no experience could ever reveal it. But then “I am dreaming” and “I am awake” predict exactly the same experiences forever. By the test, there is no real difference between them, and so nothing to worry about. Verificationists do not answer the skeptic; they claim the question was never a real one. The appeal is that it links meaning to evidence and cuts off endless, unsettleable debates. Everyday example: if someone says an invisible, silent, weightless dragon lives in their garage, which no instrument could detect, the verificationist says the claim is not false but empty.","example":"Suppose someone claims that last night everything in the universe doubled in size, including every ruler, every atom and your own body, so that all measurements came out the same. Is this true or false? A verificationist says that since no possible observation could tell the difference, the claim has no factual content. The same goes for the undetectable dream.","history":"Moritz Schlick, Rudolf Carnap and the Vienna Circle made verification the centre of logical positivism in the 1920s and 1930s, drawing on Wittgenstein’s Tractatus (1921). Schlick’s “Meaning and Verification” (1936) defended it against critics. A. J. Ayer’s Language, Truth and Logic (1936) used it to declare metaphysics and theology meaningless, choosing a sentence from F. H. Bradley about the Absolute as an example. Peirce’s pragmatic maxim was an earlier relative.","challenge":"The verification principle seems unable to pass its own test: no observation could verify it, and it is not true by definition. Precise versions also failed. Strong versions ruled out general scientific laws, while weak versions let almost any sentence count as meaningful. Most philosophers abandoned strict verificationism by the 1960s."},"reality:Simulation realist":{"summary":"Simulation realists accept that we might live in a computer simulation, but deny this would make our world fake. Hands, tables and cities would still be real, made of computation instead of fundamental particles.","reading":[{"title":"The Brain in a Vat Argument (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/brainvat/"},{"title":"Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism/"}],"idea":"The usual worry is: if I am in a simulation, nothing around me is real. Simulation realists answer that this confuses what things are with what they are made of. Your hand is the thing you see, use and grip things with, which behaves as hands do. Physics has already told us that hands are made of atoms, then of particles and fields we barely understand. If the next layer down turned out to be digital processes on a computer, that would be a surprising discovery about what hands are built from, not a proof that there are no hands. So the simulation hypothesis is like a new theory of physics, not a skeptical nightmare. Most of what we believe stays true either way. Everyday example: a song stored as digital data on your phone is still a real song, made of bits instead of grooves on vinyl.","example":"Think of the film The Matrix. Neo learns that his world is a computer simulation. Did he spend his life believing falsehoods? Chalmers argues no: when Neo believed “there is a chair here,” he was right. There was a chair, made of digital processes. What he was wrong about was deeper matters, like what the universe is ultimately made of.","history":"David Chalmers defended the view in “The Matrix as Metaphysics” (2003) and at length in Reality+: Virtual Worlds and the Problems of Philosophy (2022), where he argues virtual objects are real digital objects. Nick Bostrom’s paper “Are You Living in a Computer Simulation?” (2003) made the simulation possibility widely discussed. Hilary Putnam’s brain-in-a-vat argument in Reason, Truth and History (1981) earlier suggested that the words of a vatted brain refer to things inside its world.","challenge":"Critics argue that something made of code, run by outside programmers who can rewrite it at will, is not what we mean by a hand or a chair, so “real” is being stretched. Simulation realists also admit that some beliefs would be false, for example if a simulation began recently, so skepticism is reduced, not eliminated."},"reality:Hinge epistemologist":{"summary":"Hinge epistemologists think certainties like “I have hands” are not conclusions supported by evidence but the background that makes evidence and doubt possible. Demanding proof of them misunderstands how reasoning works.","reading":[{"title":"Certainty (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/certainty/"},{"title":"Ludwig Wittgenstein (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wittgenstein/"},{"title":"George Edward Moore (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moore/"}],"idea":"When you check a fact, you rely on many things you never check: that your eyes work, that the world did not begin five minutes ago, that you have hands. These are not things you discovered. They are the fixed points around which inquiry turns, like hinges on which a door moves. Hinge epistemologists say such certainties are not known in the usual way, because nothing is more certain that could serve as evidence for them. But that does not make them doubtful. Doubt itself needs reasons and a background of things not in doubt. Doubting your hands for no reason is not careful thinking but leaving the game of reasons. The appeal is that it explains why skeptical doubts feel both irrefutable and unreal. Everyday example: a referee checks whether a goal was offside, but never checks whether the pitch exists. Checking presupposes it.","example":"Moore had argued: “Here is one hand, here is another, so the external world exists.” Wittgenstein, in On Certainty, thought Moore was right not to doubt his hands but wrong to say he “knew” them as a piece of evidence. He noted a king raised to believe the world began with him could not be argued out of it easily: hinges are bedrock, not conclusions.","history":"G. E. Moore’s “A Defence of Common Sense” (1925) and “Proof of an External World” (1939) prompted Ludwig Wittgenstein’s notes written in the last eighteen months of his life (1949 to 1951), published as On Certainty (1969). The image of “hinges” comes from these notes. Recent developments include Crispin Wright’s idea of rational “entitlement” (2004), Danièle Moyal-Sharrock’s Understanding Wittgenstein’s On Certainty (2004) and Duncan Pritchard’s Epistemic Angst (2015).","challenge":"If hinges are not known and not supported by evidence, the skeptic may say that is exactly the point: our beliefs rest on something unjustified. Critics also worry about relativism. Different communities might hold different hinges, and the view seems to give no way to say which hinges are correct."},"reality:Monist":{"summary":"Monists hold that reality is fundamentally one. Ordinary objects like noses and towers are regions or aspects of a single whole rather than independent building blocks; some say parts depend on the whole, others that only the whole exists.","reading":[{"title":"Monism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/monism/"},{"title":"Baruch Spinoza (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spinoza/"},{"title":"Śaṅkara (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/shankara/"}],"idea":"Most people picture reality built bottom up: particles make atoms, atoms make molecules, and so on up to towers and galaxies. Monists reverse the picture. The most basic thing is the whole cosmos, and everything else is a part, region, or way of being of that one thing. Some monists (priority monists) say the parts exist but depend on the whole, as the parts of a circle depend on the circle. Others (existence monists) say strictly only the one exists, and the many are appearance. So your boot and the Eiffel Tower do not compose some further object; both are already aspects of one universe. Support comes from reasoning about being, mystical experience and, for some, quantum physics, where whole systems have properties their parts lack. Everyday example: a wave is not a separate thing sitting on the ocean. It is the ocean, moving in a certain way.","example":"Spinoza argued that a substance is something that exists in itself and needs nothing else to exist. He concluded there can be only one: God, or Nature. Your body and mind, the sun, and the sea are not separate substances but “modes,” ways in which this one substance exists. A person is to Nature roughly as a ripple is to a pond.","history":"Parmenides (fifth century BCE) argued in his poem that what is must be one and unchanging. Baruch Spinoza’s Ethics (1677) identified one infinite substance, God or Nature. Śaṅkara (around the eighth century CE), the great teacher of Advaita Vedānta, held that Brahman alone is fully real and the world of many things is māyā. F. H. Bradley’s Appearance and Reality (1893) defended a version, and Jonathan Schaffer revived priority monism in 2010.","challenge":"The world seems full of many distinct, changing things. Existence monists must explain this appearance of plurality without making the appearances themselves new things. Priority monists must explain why the whole should be more basic than its parts, when most science explains wholes by their parts."},"reality:Mereological nihilist":{"summary":"Mereological nihilists hold that no things ever make up a further thing: there are only simple particles arranged chair-wise, nose-wise, cat-wise. This removes puzzles about wholes while leaving physics untouched.","reading":[{"title":"Mereology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mereology/"},{"title":"Material Composition (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mat-comp/"}],"idea":"“Mereology” is the study of parts and wholes. Nihilists about it give the most radical answer to the question of when several things make up one more thing: never. Strictly, there are no chairs, houses or noses. There are only the smallest parts, “simples,” arranged in certain ways. Ordinary talk is fine as a shortcut: saying “there is a chair” is a handy way of saying “there are simples arranged chair-wise.” Why accept this? It dissolves many puzzles. When does a pile of sand become a heap? When does a ship with all its planks replaced stop being the same ship? If there are no heaps or ships strictly speaking, these questions lose their bite. Also, everything a chair does, its particles already do between them. Everyday example: a flock of birds turns in the sky. Strictly, there is no flock over and above birds flying in formation.","example":"Take the Ship of Theseus. Plank by plank, its parts are replaced, and the old planks are used to build a second ship. Which ship is the real Ship of Theseus? The nihilist says the puzzle assumes there were ships at all. There were only planks (or really, particles) arranged ship-wise, first in one place and then in two. Every fact about particles is settled.","history":"Peter Unger defended a radical version in “There Are No Ordinary Things” (1979), using vagueness arguments, and even argued in “I Do Not Exist” (1979) that he himself does not exist. Cian Dorr and Gideon Rosen defended treating composition as a fiction (2002), and Theodore Sider argued for nihilism in “Against Parthood” (2013). The Buddhist Abhidharma tradition held a related view that only basic elements are ultimately real.","challenge":"What if there are no simples at all, and matter is divisible forever (“gunky”)? Then nihilism implies nothing material exists. And what about you? If there are no composite objects, it is hard to explain who is thinking these thoughts, unless persons are simples, which seems unlikely, or do not exist, which seems absurd."},"reality:Organicist":{"summary":"Organicists think the only composite material objects are living things. A cat exists because its particles share a single life; a chair, strictly, does not, since its particles are merely arranged chair-wise.","reading":[{"title":"Material Composition (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mat-comp/"},{"title":"Mereology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mereology/"}],"idea":"Organicism is a middle path between nihilism (no wholes at all) and universalism (any things make a whole). When do particles make up a further object? Answer: when their activity constitutes a life. A cat is a real object because its particles are caught up in one self-maintaining, self-organising process: metabolism, growth, repair. A chair is not, because its particles do nothing together; they just happen to stay put. So strictly there are no chairs, tables or mountains, only particles arranged that way. Nihilism seems to deny that you exist. But you clearly think, and thinking cannot be done by particles that merely sit near each other. Organicism keeps you, as a living animal, while gaining most of the nihilist’s freedom from puzzles. Everyday example: a cut flower and a plastic flower look alike. The organicist says only the living one, while it lives, is truly one thing.","example":"On Peter van Inwagen’s view, think about eating an apple. Its particles do not become part of you when you swallow it, but they do once they are caught up in your metabolism. Particles constantly enter and leave the life that is you. The plastic chair you sit on, by contrast, never has a life to catch particles up in, so it is not a real object.","history":"Peter van Inwagen defended this answer to what he called the Special Composition Question in Material Beings (1990). Trenton Merricks defended a related view in Objects and Persons (2001), allowing only objects with conscious mental powers, such as people, alongside simples. The idea that living things are the clearest examples of true unities goes back to Aristotle, who treated organisms as paradigm substances.","challenge":"Life comes in borderline cases: viruses, a dying animal, the first cells. If being an object depends on life, then whether something exists may be vague, which many find unintelligible. Critics also find it bizarre that chairs, mountains and planets do not exist, while bacteria do."},"reality:Powers metaphysician":{"summary":"Powers metaphysicians think properties are real abilities: charge is the power to repel, fragility the power to break. Causation is powers being exercised, and laws of nature describe how powers behave.","reading":[{"title":"Dispositions (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dispositions/"},{"title":"The Metaphysics of Causation (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causation-metaphysics/"}],"idea":"Ask what it is for salt to be soluble. The answer is about what salt can do: dissolve in water. Powers metaphysicians think all basic properties are like this. Mass is a power to resist acceleration and attract other masses; charge is a power to repel like charges. A thing is what it can do. Causation is then no mystery: an effect happens when powers meet the right partners and are exercised, as when salt and water together produce dissolving. Laws of nature are not rules imposed on passive matter from outside; they describe the powers things already have. This restores real “oomph” to nature, unlike the Humean picture. The appeal is that it matches how scientists and ordinary people talk about magnets pulling and acids corroding. Everyday example: a match can light even if it never does. It has the power all along.","example":"Salt dissolving in water is a classic case. Neither salt nor water does it alone. The salt’s power to dissolve and the water’s power to dissolve things are “manifestation partners.” When they meet, the dissolving happens. The cause is not one event before another, but powers working together, and often several causes add up or cancel, as when one push partly offsets another.","history":"Aristotle distinguished potentiality (dunamis) from actuality (energeia), especially in Metaphysics Book Theta. Modern defenders include C. B. Martin, George Molnar in Powers (2003), Stephen Mumford in Dispositions (1998) and Laws in Nature (2004), Mumford and Rani Lill Anjum in Getting Causes from Powers (2011), and Alexander Bird in Nature’s Metaphysics (2007).","challenge":"Molière mocked a doctor who said opium causes sleep because of its “dormitive power,” and critics say powers explain nothing in the same way. Powers also seem to point at things that may never happen, like a dissolving that never occurs, which some find mysterious. Defenders must show powers do real explanatory work."},"reality:Knowledge-first epistemologist":{"summary":"Knowledge-first epistemologists hold that knowledge cannot be built from belief, truth and justification. Instead, knowledge is a basic state of mind used to explain the others: belief aims at knowledge, and your evidence is what you know.","reading":[{"title":"The Analysis of Knowledge (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/knowledge-analysis/"},{"title":"Knowledge-First Theories of Justification (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/knowledge-first-theories-of-justification/"}],"idea":"For decades philosophers tried to define knowledge by recipe: knowledge equals belief plus truth plus something else. Every recipe met counterexamples, like the stopped clock that happens to show the right time. Knowledge-first epistemologists suggest the recipe approach is backwards. Knowledge is not a mixture of simpler parts; it is a basic mental state, like seeing that something is so. Rather than define knowledge, we should use it to explain everything else. To believe is to treat something as if you knew it. You should only assert what you know. Your evidence is exactly what you know. In the clock case, you never had knowledge, only a belief that luckily matched the facts. The appeal: ending a failed project and explaining other ideas simply. Everyday example: when someone asks “how do you know?”, they want to hear how you came to see it, not a checklist of ingredients.","example":"Edmund Gettier’s 1963 cases show someone with a justified, true belief who still does not know. Analysts kept adding a fourth condition, and new cases kept defeating it. The knowledge-first response: Gettier showed knowledge is not justified true belief plus anything. We understand the cases fine using knowledge itself: the person does not know, because their belief is only luckily true.","history":"Timothy Williamson launched the programme in Knowledge and Its Limits (2000), arguing that knowledge is the most general factive mental state and that one’s evidence is one’s knowledge (E = K). Earlier, Linda Zagzebski argued in “The Inescapability of Gettier Problems” (1994) that every recipe for knowledge would fail. Many epistemologists have since built “knowledge-first” theories of justification, assertion and evidence.","challenge":"Take a person who is deceived, perhaps a brain in a vat, with experiences exactly like yours. Knowledge-first theory says they have less evidence than you, since they know less. Many find it unfair: they seem just as reasonable. Critics also say refusing to analyse knowledge leaves it unexplained."},"reality:Common-sense philosopher":{"summary":"Common-sense philosophers hold that basic certainties, like having hands or the world being old, are more secure than any clever argument against them. When a skeptical argument conflicts with them, one of its premises must be false.","reading":[{"title":"George Edward Moore (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moore/"},{"title":"Thomas Reid (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reid/"},{"title":"Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism/"}],"idea":"Skeptical arguments work by starting from premises that sound plausible and reaching a shocking conclusion: you do not know you have hands. Common-sense philosophers notice a simple point of logic. If an argument’s conclusion is less believable than the denial of its premises, you should reject a premise, not accept the conclusion. And nothing is more believable than that you have hands. So, even if you cannot say which premise of the skeptic’s argument fails, you can be rationally sure one does. This does not mean ignoring philosophy. It means letting deep, shared certainties count as evidence in philosophy, as observations count in science. Everyday example: if a magician seems to saw a person in half, you are more confident that people do not survive being sawn in half than you are of any theory about how the trick works.","example":"In “Proof of an External World” (1939), G. E. Moore raised his hands and said, in effect, here is one hand and here is another, so external objects exist. Many found it too simple. Moore’s point was that the premise, that he had hands, was more certain than anything a skeptic could offer against it, so the proof was perfectly good.","history":"Thomas Reid founded the Scottish common sense school in An Inquiry into the Human Mind on the Principles of Common Sense (1764) and Essays on the Intellectual Powers of Man (1785), responding to Hume. G. E. Moore revived the approach in “A Defence of Common Sense” (1925) and “Proof of an External World” (1939). Roderick Chisholm’s The Problem of the Criterion (1973) defended a related “particularist” method.","challenge":"Critics say Moore’s proof begs the question: the skeptic’s whole point is that you might not know you have hands. Common sense has also often been wrong, for example about the sun moving round the Earth, so its authority in philosophy is disputed."},"reality:Explanationist":{"summary":"Explanationists defend knowledge of the external world the way scientists and detectives reason: a real, stable world explains the order of our experience far better than a dream or deception, so belief in it is justified.","reading":[{"title":"Abduction (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/abduction/"},{"title":"Skepticism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism/"}],"idea":"You cannot prove that you are not dreaming. But scientists cannot prove their theories either; they accept the best explanation of the evidence. Explanationists say our belief in the external world works the same way. Our experiences have a striking pattern. Objects stay where we left them. Other people report seeing what we see. Dreams, by contrast, are patchy and shifting. The simplest, most complete explanation of this order is that there really is a stable world causing our experiences. The skeptic’s alternatives, a perfect dream or a deceiving demon, would have to copy the world’s whole structure in detail, making them more complicated, not less. The appeal is that it answers the skeptic with ordinary, respectable reasoning. Everyday example: if a cake vanishes from the kitchen and your brother has crumbs on his shirt, you need no confession to know what happened.","example":"Bertrand Russell asks us to imagine a cat that appears at one moment in one part of the room, then later in another. If the cat were only a series of experiences, it would be puzzling that it gets hungry between meals while unseen. If it is a real cat that moved and grew hungry when unobserved, everything makes sense. So the real-world hypothesis wins.","history":"Bertrand Russell gave this argument in The Problems of Philosophy (1912), chapter 2, defending belief in matter as the simplest hypothesis. The pattern of reasoning, “abduction,” was named by Charles Peirce and called “inference to the best explanation” by Gilbert Harman (1965). Jonathan Vogel refined the anti-skeptical version in “Cartesian Skepticism and Inference to the Best Explanation” (1990), as did Laurence BonJour.","challenge":"Why think the simplest explanation is more likely true? Simplicity may reflect our convenience, not the world. Skeptics can also design alternatives that copy the real world’s structure exactly, such as a computer simulation, so it is unclear that the real-world hypothesis is truly simpler."},"reality:Mathematical fictionalist":{"summary":"Mathematical fictionalists read mathematics at face value, as talk about numbers, and then deny that numbers exist. “2 + 2 = 4” is true within the story of mathematics, a very useful story, but strictly false.","reading":[{"title":"Fictionalism in the Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fictionalism-mathematics/"},{"title":"Nominalism in the Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nominalism-mathematics/"}],"idea":"Fictionalists agree with Platonists about what mathematical sentences mean: “there is a prime number between 10 and 12” really does claim that a number exists. But they deny that any numbers, sets or functions exist outside the story. So the sentence is literally false, though true according to mathematics, as “Sherlock Holmes lives on Baker Street” is true according to the stories. Why would anyone choose this? It avoids the mystery of how we could know about abstract objects outside space and time. And it does not change mathematical practice at all: mathematicians still prove theorems, which tell us what follows within the story. Mathematics is useful because it is a consistent tool for reasoning about physical things, not because it describes a hidden realm. Everyday example: map makers draw lines of latitude. No such lines are painted on the earth, but the fiction helps sailors find real places.","example":"Hartry Field tried to show that Newtonian gravitational theory could be rewritten without numbers, using only points of space and their relations. If physics can in principle do without mathematics, then mathematics is just a shortcut. It helps us reach true conclusions about physical things faster, but it never leads from true physical premises to a false physical conclusion. So numbers need not exist.","history":"Hartry Field defended the view in Science Without Numbers (1980), rewriting Newtonian gravitational theory without numbers, and in Realism, Mathematics and Modality (1989). Mark Balaguer defended fictionalism in Platonism and Anti-Platonism in Mathematics (1998), and Stephen Yablo developed related “figurative” readings. Hans Vaihinger’s The Philosophy of “As If” (1911) had earlier treated many concepts, including mathematical ones, as useful fictions.","challenge":"Stories can be told any way we like, but mathematics seems objective: everyone who checks agrees that there are infinitely many primes. Fictionalists must explain why. Also, Field’s rewriting has not been done for quantum mechanics, so whether science truly can dispense with numbers remains open."},"reality:Intuitionist":{"summary":"Intuitionists hold that mathematical objects are mental constructions, and a mathematical statement is true only when there is a proof of it. This means giving up some classical reasoning about infinite collections.","reading":[{"title":"Intuitionism in the Philosophy of Mathematics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/intuitionism/"},{"title":"Luitzen Egbertus Jan Brouwer (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/brouwer/"}],"idea":"For intuitionists, mathematics is something minds do. A number exists only once it could be built step by step in thought, starting from the simple sense of one moment following another. Mathematical truth is then not about a ready-made realm: a statement is true when we have a construction or proof of it. This has a striking consequence. In ordinary logic, every statement is either true or false (“the law of excluded middle”). Intuitionists accept this for finite cases, but for infinite collections they will not assert “P or not P” unless they can prove one side. The appeal is honesty about infinity: no claims to know what no mind could ever survey. Everyday example: you cannot say a library has a copy of some book until someone can actually find it. A promise that it must be on some shelf, without a way to locate it, is not enough.","example":"Ask: does the decimal expansion of π contain a run of a billion consecutive 7s somewhere? A classical mathematician says it either does or does not, even if we never find out. The intuitionist refuses to assert that. Until we have a proof one way or the other, the statement is neither shown true nor shown false, and pretending otherwise claims knowledge of an infinite whole.","history":"L. E. J. Brouwer founded intuitionism in his 1907 dissertation and later papers, partly inspired by Kant’s view of time. He clashed in the 1920s with David Hilbert, who compared banning excluded middle to taking the telescope from the astronomer. Arend Heyting formalised intuitionistic logic (1930), and Michael Dummett defended it on grounds of meaning in Elements of Intuitionism (1977).","challenge":"Intuitionism forces mathematicians to give up many accepted proofs, including some that rely on proof by contradiction to show that something exists. Most mathematicians find this too costly. Critics also ask whose mind does the constructing, and whether a truth can really depend on what has been proved so far."},"reality:Contextualist":{"summary":"Contextualists think the standards for “knowing” shift with the conversation. In ordinary talk you know you have hands; when someone raises the dream possibility, the bar rises and you no longer count as knowing.","reading":[{"title":"Epistemic Contextualism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/contextualism-epistemology/"},{"title":"Contextualism in Epistemology (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/contextu/"}],"idea":"Words like “tall,” “flat” or “empty” change their standards with context. A fridge is “empty” if there is no food, even if it is full of air. Contextualists think “know” works like this too. In everyday conversation, the standard is moderate: you know your car is in the car park if you parked it there an hour ago. But when someone seriously raises a possibility like theft or dreaming, the standard rises, and in that conversation it becomes false to say you know. Both claims can be true, each in its own context. So the skeptic is right in the philosophy seminar, and you are right at the dinner table. The appeal is that it explains why skeptical arguments feel compelling without making everyday knowledge claims false. Everyday example: you “know” you will be at school tomorrow, but if asked “are you sure you won’t be ill?”, you hesitate.","example":"Keith DeRose’s bank case: on a Friday, with no urgency, you say “I know the bank is open on Saturday, I was there two weeks ago.” Now suppose a large cheque must be deposited before Monday, and your partner mentions banks sometimes change hours. Here it seems wrong to say “I know.” Your evidence is the same; the standard has changed.","history":"Stewart Cohen’s “How to Be a Fallibilist” (1988) and Keith DeRose’s “Contextualism and Knowledge Attributions” (1992), which introduced the bank case, launched the modern view. DeRose’s “Solving the Skeptical Problem” (1995) applied it to skepticism, and David Lewis’s “Elusive Knowledge” (1996) gave the rules by which possibilities raised in conversation become relevant.","challenge":"If “know” really shifted meaning, speakers should notice, as they do with “tall.” But people argue about knowledge as if they disagree, not as if they talk past each other. Critics call this “semantic blindness.” Others say contextualism concedes too much: whenever skepticism is discussed, the skeptic wins."},"reality:Universalist":{"summary":"Universalists hold that any things whatever make up a further object: your nose plus the Eiffel Tower, or every coin in Paris. Most such objects are unnamed and uninteresting, but drawing a line elsewhere would be arbitrary.","reading":[{"title":"Mereology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mereology/"},{"title":"Material Composition (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mat-comp/"}],"idea":"When do several things make up one more thing? Universalists say: always. Take any objects at all, however scattered, and there is an object made of exactly them, their “sum.” Most are odd, useless and unnamed. But they are as real as cats and cars. Why believe something so strange? Because every alternative seems to draw an arbitrary line. Must parts touch? Then a solar system or a bikini would not be one thing. Must they move together? Then a loose pile of bricks is not one thing until mortared. Any such rule, universalists argue, will be either arbitrary or vague, and existence cannot be vague. The oddness is only in our interests: we name objects we care about. Everyday example: a football team is scattered across a pitch, and we happily treat it as one thing. Universalists say the same holds for any collection.","example":"David Lewis’s favourite oddity is the “trout-turkey,” made of the front half of a trout and the back half of a turkey. Does it exist? Lewis says yes. It is a strange object, and nobody needs to mention it, but denying it existence would force us to say where exactly the line falls between wholes that exist and wholes that do not.","history":"Formal theories of unrestricted composition go back to Stanisław Leśniewski (from 1916) and to Henry Leonard and Nelson Goodman’s “calculus of individuals” (1940). W. V. O. Quine accepted any portion of space-time content as a physical object. David Lewis defended universalism in On the Plurality of Worlds (1986) and Parts of Classes (1991), and Theodore Sider, in Four-Dimensionalism (2001), argued that any restriction would make existence vague.","challenge":"Universalism fills reality with an enormous number of bizarre objects nobody believes in. Critics say this is too high a price for avoiding arbitrariness. Organicists and nihilists also argue that a principled line can be drawn, for example at life, so universalism is not the only non-arbitrary option."},"reality:Ontological deflationist":{"summary":"Ontological deflationists think many disputes about what exists are verbal. Once all the facts about particles are fixed, asking whether they also compose “an object” is about how to use words, not about the world.","reading":[{"title":"Rudolf Carnap (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/carnap/"},{"title":"Mereology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mereology/"}],"idea":"Imagine two philosophers looking at the same particles. One says there are three things; the other says there are seven, because combinations count too. Ontological deflationists say they do not really disagree. They agree on every physical fact; they just use “thing” and “exists” in slightly different ways. Each is speaking truly in their own language. So the debate between nihilists, organicists and universalists is not a rivalry of theories but a choice between languages, and the choice should rest on convenience, not on which reflects “reality’s true joints.” Ordinary questions, like whether a forest has tigers, stay real. What is empty is the extra question whether the world itself prefers one way of counting. This ends debates with no possible test. Everyday example: is a pizza one thing, or eight slices? It depends on how you want to count, not on anything about the pizza.","example":"Rudolf Carnap distinguished internal from external questions. Inside the framework of number talk, “is there a prime between 10 and 12?” has an easy answer: yes. But asking from outside every framework, “do numbers really exist?”, is not a factual question. It is really a practical question: is the number framework useful? The same goes for frameworks of composite objects.","history":"Rudolf Carnap started the view in “Empiricism, Semantics, and Ontology” (1950). Hilary Putnam’s “conceptual relativity,” in The Many Faces of Realism (1987), made a similar point about counting objects. Eli Hirsch developed it as “quantifier variance” in Quantifier Variance and Realism (2011), and Amie Thomasson’s Ontology Made Easy (2015) defended a related “easy” approach to existence questions.","challenge":"Theodore Sider, in Writing the Book of the World (2011), argued that some ways of describing the world carve it at its natural joints better than others, so disputes about what exists can be real after all. Quine also attacked the line between questions settled by language and questions settled by fact."},"reality:Truth pluralist":{"summary":"Truth pluralists think truth is not one property everywhere. For claims about physical things, it may be matching the facts; for mathematics, provability; for morals, something like coherence or ideal agreement.","reading":[{"title":"Pluralist Theories of Truth (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/truth-pluralist/"},{"title":"Truth (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/truth/"}],"idea":"Philosophers usually propose one theory of truth for everything: correspondence (matching the facts), coherence (fitting together), pragmatism (what works), or deflationism (no deep property at all). Each works well in some areas and badly in others. Correspondence seems right for “there is milk in the fridge,” but what fact out there would “7 is prime” or “cruelty is wrong” match? Truth pluralists suggest that truth can be realised in different ways in different areas of talk. What unites these is the role truth plays: true claims are what assertion aims at, what good reasoning preserves, and what inquiry seeks. Different properties can fill that role in physics, mathematics, morality or comedy. The appeal is that it lets each area be taken seriously on its own terms. Everyday example: “winning” means different things in chess, football and an election, yet in each case it is the point of the game.","example":"Consider three true claims: “Snow is white,” “2 + 2 = 4,” and “torturing for fun is wrong.” A pluralist might say the first is true because it corresponds to a physical state of affairs, the second because it is provable from accepted axioms, and the third because it would survive the most careful, informed moral reflection. All three are “true,” in a shared functional sense.","history":"Crispin Wright’s Truth and Objectivity (1992) argued that a thin truth predicate can be realised by different properties, such as “superassertibility,” in different areas. Michael Lynch developed the view in Truth in Context (1998) and Truth as One and Many (2009). Precursors include William James, who allowed truth to work differently in different domains.","challenge":"Mixed sentences cause trouble: “snow is white and cruelty is wrong.” If its parts are true in different ways, which kind of truth does the whole have? Christine Tappolet (1997) pressed a similar problem for mixed arguments. Pluralists answer with a general truth above the local ones, but critics doubt this is stable."},"reality:Pragmatist":{"summary":"Pragmatists understand truth through inquiry and practice: true beliefs are the ones that work and would survive any amount of further testing. Truth is tied to experience and action, not a copy of hidden reality.","reading":[{"title":"The Pragmatic Theory of Truth (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/truth-pragmatic/"},{"title":"Pragmatism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pragmatism/"},{"title":"Charles Sanders Peirce (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/peirce/"}],"idea":"Pragmatists start by asking what difference truth makes in practice. A belief is a habit of action; if it is true, acting on it succeeds, and further evidence keeps confirming it. So, pragmatists say, the true belief is the one that would keep working however long we investigated, the belief that inquiry, honestly pursued, would converge on. This is not the claim that whatever is convenient is true. A comforting false belief stops working when reality pushes back. Rather, truth is the end point of inquiry, not a static match between sentence and fact that we could never check. This gives “truth” a usable meaning, linked to evidence and testing. Everyday example: you believe a bridge will hold you. You cross it, others cross it, engineers test it for years, and it never fails. For a pragmatist, that open-ended success is what its being true amounts to.","example":"Peirce imagined many investigators starting from different places and methods, studying, say, the speed of light. Each gets different results at first, but as inquiry goes on, their results move closer together toward one value. That destined meeting point, Peirce said, is what we mean by truth, and the object represented in it is what we mean by the real.","history":"Charles Sanders Peirce gave the first pragmatic account of truth in “How to Make Our Ideas Clear” (1878), linking truth to the opinion that inquiry is destined to reach. William James developed a more practical version in Pragmatism (1907) and The Meaning of Truth (1909). John Dewey spoke of “warranted assertibility” in Logic: The Theory of Inquiry (1938). Later pragmatists include Hilary Putnam and Cheryl Misak.","challenge":"Bertrand Russell and G. E. Moore objected that a belief can be useful without being true, and true without being useful. Critics also point to “buried secrets”: facts, like what Julius Caesar ate on his tenth birthday, that no inquiry will ever settle but surely have a true answer."},"reality:Deflationist about truth":{"summary":"Deflationists think “true” names no deep property. Saying “it is true that snow is white” says no more than “snow is white”; the word is useful for agreeing and generalising, and there is no hidden nature of truth.","reading":[{"title":"Deflationism About Truth (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/truth-deflationary/"},{"title":"Truth (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/truth/"}],"idea":"Most theories of truth try to say what all true sentences have in common: matching the facts, fitting together, working in practice. Deflationists say there is nothing to find. Everything we need to know about truth is in simple equivalences: “snow is white” is true just in case snow is white; “grass is green” is true just in case grass is green; and so on. Why then have the word? Because it is a handy device. It lets us agree without repeating (“that’s true”) and make general claims we could not otherwise state, like “everything the witness said was true,” when we do not know what she said. Truth is a logical tool, not a property to investigate. Long debates about truth’s nature simply disappear. Everyday example: saying “it’s true that the shop closes at six” adds nothing to “the shop closes at six,” except perhaps emphasis.","example":"Think of the sentence “Everything the Pope said today is true.” You might not know what the Pope said. Without the word “true,” you would have to list every claim: if he said it is raining, it is raining; if he said Rome is old, Rome is old; and so on forever. “True” bundles this into one sentence. That, deflationists say, is its whole job.","history":"Frank Ramsey proposed a “redundancy” view in “Facts and Propositions” (1927). Alfred Tarski’s work on truth (1933) supplied the schema “S is true if and only if p,” which deflationists adopted. W. V. O. Quine called truth “disquotation” in Philosophy of Logic (1970). Paul Horwich defended “minimalism” in Truth (1990), and Hartry Field defended a disquotational view in the 1990s.","challenge":"Many think truth does explanatory work: we succeed in action because our beliefs are true, and science aims at truth. If truth is only a logical device, critics ask, how can it explain anything? Deflationists must show these explanations can be rewritten without a deep property of truth."},"reality:Truth-tracker":{"summary":"Truth-trackers think knowledge requires a belief connected to the truth, not just good reasons that happen to land on it. The test is whether you would still believe the claim if it were false; if so, you do not know.","reading":[{"title":"The Analysis of Knowledge (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/knowledge-analysis/"},{"title":"Reliabilist Epistemology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reliabilism/"}],"idea":"In the stopped clock case, you have a true belief and decent reasons, yet something is missing. Truth-trackers say what is missing is a link between your belief and the facts. Your belief should “track” the truth: if it were false, you would not believe it; and if it were true in slightly different circumstances, you would still believe it. The stopped clock fails the first test: if it had been 2:05, you would still have believed it was 2:00. Real knowledge, like reading a working clock, varies with the facts. Knowing is not just being right, but being right in a way that would follow the facts if they changed. This simply explains why lucky guesses are not knowledge. Everyday example: a smoke alarm that beeps all the time is right during fires, but tells you nothing. A good alarm beeps only when there is smoke.","example":"Nozick’s view has a famous consequence. You know you have hands: if you did not, you would notice. But do you know you are not a handless brain in a vat? If you were one, everything would seem the same, so you would still believe you are not. So you know you have hands, yet do not know you are not a handless brain in a vat. Nozick accepted this.","history":"Robert Nozick proposed the tracking account in Philosophical Explanations (1981). Fred Dretske had earlier argued in “Epistemic Operators” (1970) and “Conclusive Reasons” (1971) that knowledge need not carry over to everything one knows follows from it. Ernest Sosa later proposed a “safety” condition (1999) as an alternative: a belief is safe if it could not easily have been false.","challenge":"Keith DeRose called Nozick’s consequence an “abominable conjunction”: “I know I have hands, but I don’t know I’m not a handless brain in a vat.” Giving up the principle that knowledge carries over to known consequences strikes many as too costly. Saul Kripke also offered counterexamples, such as a red barn in a field of fake barns."},"reality:Rationalist":{"summary":"Rationalists hold that reason alone can reveal important truths about the world, without observation. Mathematics, logic and perhaps metaphysics are known by thinking, and some ideas may be innate.","reading":[{"title":"Rationalism vs. Empiricism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rationalism-empiricism/"},{"title":"Descartes’ Epistemology (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/descartes-epistemology/"}],"idea":"Some things you know without looking. You do not need to check every triangle to know its angles add up to two right angles, or survey the world to know that nothing can be red and green all over at once. Rationalists take such cases seriously. They argue that reason, not just the senses, is a source of knowledge about reality. Many rationalists add that some ideas, like those of number, cause or God, are innate: we are born ready to recognise them, and experience only triggers them. Experience matters too, but cannot explain the necessity of mathematics or logic. The appeal is that it explains how we can know things that must be true, everywhere and always. Everyday example: you know that if Anna is taller than Ben, and Ben is taller than Cal, then Anna is taller than Cal, without meeting any of them.","example":"Descartes, doubting everything, found one thing he could not doubt: that he was thinking, and so existed. No observation was needed. From this starting point, by reason alone, he tried to rebuild knowledge. In his wax example, he argued that knowing a piece of wax stays the same as it melts comes from the mind’s judgment, not the senses.","history":"Plato’s Meno and Phaedo argued that reason recalls truths known before birth. René Descartes’s Meditations on First Philosophy (1641) used reason to establish his own existence and God’s. Baruch Spinoza built his Ethics (1677) in geometric form. G. W. Leibniz defended innate ideas in his New Essays on Human Understanding (written around 1704, published 1765), answering Locke. Laurence BonJour defended rationalism in In Defense of Pure Reason (1998).","challenge":"Empiricists argue that knowledge by pure reason tells us only about meanings, not about the world. Euclidean geometry once seemed the perfect example of rational knowledge of space, yet non-Euclidean geometries and Einstein’s relativity showed physical space need not be Euclidean. Self-evidence can mislead."},"reality:Growing block theorist":{"summary":"Growing block theorists think the past and present are real, but the future does not yet exist. As time passes, reality grows, adding new slices of events, which explains why the past is fixed and the future open.","reading":[{"title":"Time (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/time/"},{"title":"Being and Becoming in Modern Physics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spacetime-bebecome/"}],"idea":"Picture all of history as a stack of slices, one for each moment. Growing block theorists say the stack is real up to now, and nothing lies beyond it. Your tenth birthday is still part of reality, though no longer present; your thirtieth is not anywhere yet. As time passes, new slices are added to the top. This combines two attractive ideas. Like the block universe view, it treats the past as fully real, so “Napoleon lost at Waterloo” is made true by something that exists. Like presentism, it gives a real, moving present and an open future: tomorrow’s events are genuinely undecided because they do not yet exist. This matches common sense: the past is settled, the future still to be made. Everyday example: a diary grows one page a day. Earlier pages remain, but there are no blank pages already waiting with tomorrow’s entry.","example":"Consider Aristotle’s sea battle: will there be a sea battle tomorrow? The growing block theorist can say that “there was a battle yesterday” is already true, since yesterday is part of the block. But “there will be a battle tomorrow” is not yet true or false, because tomorrow does not exist. When it arrives, the block grows and settles the question.","history":"C. D. Broad proposed the view in Scientific Thought (1923), describing reality as a growing sum of existence. Michael Tooley developed it in Time, Tense, and Causation (1997), connecting it to causation. Peter Forrest and others have since defended versions, and the view is often discussed alongside presentism and eternalism, as in Ned Markosian’s and Craig Bourne’s work on time.","challenge":"If past moments are as real as the present, people in 1800 are really there, thinking “this is now.” How do you know you are at the growing edge, not in the past? David Braddon-Mitchell pressed this in 2004. Relativity also denies a single universal “now” where the edge could be."},"reality:Block universe theorist":{"summary":"Block universe theorists hold that past, present and future are equally real parts of four-dimensional spacetime. Time does not objectively flow; “now” is just where you are in time, as “here” is where you are in space.","reading":[{"title":"Time (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/time/"},{"title":"Being and Becoming in Modern Physics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spacetime-bebecome/"},{"title":"Eternalism (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/eternalism/"}],"idea":"Think of a map of a country. Every town is equally real; “here” just picks out where you stand. Block universe theorists (also called eternalists) see time the same way. All events, from the Big Bang to the far future, exist together in a four-dimensional block of spacetime. Dinosaurs and your future grandchildren are as real as you, just located at other times. The feeling that time flows, that the present is special, is a feature of how creatures like us experience the world, not of the world itself. The main motivation is physics. Einstein’s relativity shows that observers moving differently disagree about which events are simultaneous, so there is no single, universal “now” dividing the real present from the unreal future. The block view fits this perfectly. Everyday example: a film reel contains every frame at once, though you watch it one frame at a time.","example":"The Rietdijk-Putnam argument: imagine two observers passing each other at high speed. According to relativity, events simultaneous for one are not simultaneous for the other. If each observer’s present is real, and reality is the same for both, then events in one observer’s future are already real for the other. Repeat this reasoning and every event comes out real: past, present and future.","history":"Hermann Minkowski’s 1908 lecture “Space and Time” unified space and time into spacetime. J. M. E. McTaggart’s “The Unreality of Time” (1908) distinguished the B-series, ordering by earlier and later, which block theorists treat as basic. J. J. C. Smart, Hilary Putnam in “Time and Physical Geometry” (1967), and Theodore Sider in Four-Dimensionalism (2001) defended the view. Einstein expressed sympathy with it in later letters.","challenge":"The block view seems to deny what we experience most vividly: time passing and the future being open. If the future already exists, critics ask whether our choices make any difference, though eternalists argue they do. It must also explain why we remember the past but not the future."},"reality:Empiricist":{"summary":"Empiricists hold that all knowledge of the world comes from experience. The mind starts with no built-in knowledge of reality, and truths that seem knowable by thought alone are about meanings or were learned after all.","reading":[{"title":"Rationalism vs. Empiricism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rationalism-empiricism/"},{"title":"John Locke (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/locke/"}],"idea":"Where do our ideas come from? Empiricists answer: from experience, from the senses and from noticing our own thoughts and feelings. A newborn mind contains no knowledge of the world, only the ability to receive and combine impressions. Even ideas that seem very abstract, like infinity or substance, are built from simpler ideas that experience supplied. What about mathematics and logic, which seem known without looking? Empiricists say these either tell us only about how our concepts relate (“all bachelors are unmarried”), or are very general facts learned from experience after all. The appeal is a kind of accountability: every claim about the world should be traceable to evidence, which guards against prejudice dressed up as self-evident truth. Everyday example: no one knows what a mango tastes like until they try one. No description and no amount of thinking could give you that knowledge.","example":"William Molyneux asked Locke: suppose a man born blind learned to tell a cube from a sphere by touch, then suddenly gained sight. Could he tell which was which just by looking? Locke answered no. The connection between how things feel and how they look must be learned from experience, not worked out by reasoning.","history":"John Locke’s An Essay Concerning Human Understanding (1689) attacked innate ideas and compared the mind at birth to white paper. George Berkeley and David Hume developed empiricism further, Hume reaching skeptical conclusions about causation and the self. John Stuart Mill’s A System of Logic (1843) treated even mathematics as based on experience. In ancient India, the Cārvāka school accepted perception as the only reliable source of knowledge.","challenge":"Mathematics and logic seem necessary and certain, which experience alone cannot show: experience tells you what is, not what must be. Noam Chomsky also argued that children learn language too quickly, from too little evidence, to start with no built-in structure."},"reality:Presentist":{"summary":"Presentists hold that only the present exists: the past is gone and the future is not yet, so dinosaurs existed but are not part of reality now. This fits the common feeling that time truly passes.","reading":[{"title":"Presentism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/presentism/"},{"title":"Time (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/time/"}],"idea":"Presentism is perhaps the most common-sense view of time. What exists is what exists now. The past did exist, but it has gone; the future will exist, but it is not here yet. Time passing is not a feature of how we experience things; it is reality itself changing, with things coming into and going out of existence. On this view, talking about the past is not talking about a distant place that still exists somewhere. It is like using a tense: “there were dinosaurs” is true, but there are no dinosaurs anywhere in reality now. The appeal is that presentism takes seriously our experience of change, of things genuinely ending, and of the future as open. Everyday example: when a candle burns out, presentists say the flame does not survive somewhere back in time. It simply no longer exists.","example":"Augustine asked: what is time? The past no longer is, the future is not yet, and the present, if it always stayed present, would be eternity rather than time. He concluded that past and future exist only in the mind, as memory and expectation, while attention holds the present. This puzzle, set out in Book XI of the Confessions, is a classic starting point.","history":"Augustine explored the view in Book XI of his Confessions (around 397 to 400 CE). Arthur Prior developed it with his tense logic in Past, Present, and Future (1967) and “Changes in Events and Changes in Things” (1962). Later defenders include Ned Markosian in “A Defense of Presentism” (2004), Dean Zimmerman, and Craig Bourne in A Future for Presentism (2006).","challenge":"If the past does not exist, what makes “dinosaurs existed” true now? This “truthmaker” problem is hard to solve. Relativity also seems to deny a single universal present, which presentism needs. And relations across time, like admiring Abraham Lincoln, seem to require that Lincoln exist in some sense."},"reality:Kantian":{"summary":"Kantians hold that the mind supplies forms, space, time and cause and effect, that structure all experience, while the senses supply the content. So some truths are knowable in advance, but only about the world as it appears to us.","reading":[{"title":"Kant’s Transcendental Idealism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-transcendental-idealism/"},{"title":"Rationalism vs. Empiricism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rationalism-empiricism/"}],"idea":"Kantians take a middle path between rationalism and empiricism. With empiricists, they say all knowledge begins with experience. With rationalists, they say not all of it comes from experience. The mind is not a blank page. It has built-in ways of organising whatever it receives: it places everything in space and time, and connects events by cause and effect. Because these forms come from us, we can know in advance that every experience will obey them. That explains how geometry can be both informative and certain. The price is a limit. We know things only as they appear through our forms, not as they are in themselves. This explains scientific certainty without claiming access beyond experience. Everyday example: if you always wore blue glasses, you could know in advance that everything you see will look bluish, without knowing what colour things really are.","example":"Kant compared his idea to Copernicus. Copernicus explained the stars’ motion by supposing the observer moves, not the stars. Kant proposed that, rather than our knowledge conforming to objects, objects as we experience them conform to our knowledge. That is why we can know, before meeting any particular event, that it will occur in time and have a cause.","history":"Immanuel Kant set out the view in the Critique of Pure Reason (1781, revised 1787) and the shorter Prolegomena to Any Future Metaphysics (1783), where he credited Hume with waking him from his “dogmatic slumber.” Arthur Schopenhauer adapted it in The World as Will and Representation (1818). Later neo-Kantians, such as Hermann Cohen and Ernst Cassirer, revised Kant’s fixed forms in light of new science.","challenge":"If cause and effect apply only to appearances, how can things in themselves cause our sensations? Friedrich Heinrich Jacobi raised this in 1787. Also, non-Euclidean geometry and relativity showed that space need not be Euclidean, though Kant thought its Euclidean form was known with certainty in advance."},"society:Anarcho-capitalist":{"summary":"You think no state has rightful authority, and that everything a state does, including police, courts and defence, could be done better by competing firms that people choose freely to pay.","reading":[{"title":"Anarchism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/anarchism/"},{"title":"Libertarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/libertarianism/"},{"title":"Authority","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/authority/"}],"idea":"Anarcho-capitalists start from a simple thought: if it is wrong for you or me to take money from neighbours by threat, it is wrong for a government too. Taxation, on this view, is taking without consent, and a legal monopoly on protection is a monopoly like any other. So they ask markets to supply what states now supply. Insurance-like agencies would offer protection, and private courts or arbitrators would settle disputes, all competing for customers. Because honest trade produces just holdings, nobody needs a central power to fix the results. The view attracts people who prize voluntary cooperation and distrust concentrated power. Everyday example: many businesses already settle contract disputes through private arbitration they agreed to in advance, and gated communities often hire private security. Anarcho-capitalists imagine extending that model to everything.","example":"Suppose two neighbours argue over a fence. Each subscribes to a protection agency. Rather than fight, which is costly, the two agencies have a standing contract to send such cases to an independent arbitrator they both trust. The arbitrator rules, the losing agency pays or makes its client comply, and no tax-funded court was needed. David Friedman’s The Machinery of Freedom works through cases like this.","history":"The Belgian economist Gustave de Molinari argued in 1849 that security could be produced by competing firms. Murray Rothbard built a full system on natural rights in For a New Liberty (1973) and The Ethics of Liberty (1982). David Friedman defended a similar order on economic grounds in The Machinery of Freedom (1973). Michael Huemer’s The Problem of Political Authority (2013) argued from everyday moral intuitions that states lack authority. Historians sometimes point to medieval Iceland’s decentralised legal system as a partial precedent.","challenge":"Critics ask what stops protection agencies from fighting each other, or from the strongest one swallowing the rest and becoming a state in all but name. Robert Nozick argued in Anarchy, State, and Utopia that a dominant agency would emerge naturally. Others worry that people too poor to buy protection would be left exposed."},"society:Social anarchist":{"summary":"You think the state lacks real authority and should give way to communities and workplaces run by their members, sharing and helping one another, because freedom and equality go together.","reading":[{"title":"Anarchism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/anarchism/"},{"title":"Socialism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/socialism/"}],"idea":"Social anarchists believe people can organise their lives well without rulers, and that the state mostly protects the privileges of the powerful. Unlike anarcho-capitalists, they also care about how goods end up: a society where some own everything and others must sell their labour to survive is not free, even if every trade was honest. So they hope to replace both the state and large private ownership with voluntary federations of communes, cooperatives and councils, where decisions are made by those affected. The attraction is a picture of freedom and fairness reinforcing each other, rooted in ordinary human kindness. Everyday example: a neighbourhood that runs its own food bank, tool library or volunteer fire brigade, with no boss and no profit, shows the mutual aid social anarchists think could run much more of life.","example":"Kropotkin’s Mutual Aid (1902) answered people who read Darwin as proving life is a brutal competition. He gathered examples from animals, villages and medieval guilds to show that cooperation also helps species and communities survive. A social anarchist facing a local problem, such as a factory closing, would favour the workers taking it over as a cooperative and linking with other cooperatives, rather than waiting for the state or a new owner.","history":"Pierre-Joseph Proudhon asked What Is Property? in 1840 and called himself an anarchist. Mikhail Bakunin and Peter Kropotkin developed collectivist and communist versions; Kropotkin’s The Conquest of Bread (1892) and Mutual Aid (1902) are classics. Leo Tolstoy, in The Kingdom of God Is Within You (1894), rejected the state on Christian, nonviolent grounds. In 1936, during the Spanish Civil War, anarchist unions ran many farms and factories in Catalonia and Aragon. Emma Goldman spread the ideas in the United States.","challenge":"The hardest questions are about scale and protection. How can millions of people coordinate trade, roads and hospitals without some body that can make binding decisions? And what stops a well-armed group from taking over communities that have given up the state? Critics also doubt that mutual aid alone can handle people who refuse to contribute."},"society:Individualist anarchist":{"summary":"You think no one has a right to rule anyone, and that free individuals should join or leave groups only by choice. Honest acquisition yields just holdings, so a taxing, commanding state has no basis.","reading":[{"title":"Anarchism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/anarchism/"},{"title":"Max Stirner","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/max-stirner/"}],"idea":"Individualist anarchists put the single person at the centre. Each of us is sovereign over our own life, and every group, from a club to a nation, is legitimate only as long as its members choose to stay. Since honest work and trade give people a rightful claim to what they have, nothing justifies a state that takes by tax and commands by law. Some individualists build this on natural rights; others, following Max Stirner, reject moral talk altogether and simply refuse to be ruled. What they share is suspicion of any collective, including the nation, the party or even “society”, that claims to stand above real people. The appeal is a radical, consistent respect for individual choice. Everyday example: a group of friends who share a flat, set their own rules, and let anyone move out freely is the model of association they favour.","example":"Faced with a new law, an individualist anarchist asks one question: did I agree to this? Benjamin Tucker answered state demands by arguing that in a truly free market, without government-granted monopolies over money, land and patents, interest and rent would shrink and workers would keep the full value of their labour. Freedom, not redistribution, would make society fair.","history":"Josiah Warren ran experiments in “equitable commerce” in the United States from the 1820s. Max Stirner’s The Ego and Its Own (1844) rejected every authority above the individual, including morality itself. Lysander Spooner’s No Treason (1867 to 1870) argued that the US Constitution bound no one who had not signed it. Benjamin Tucker edited the journal Liberty from 1881 to 1908 and called his view “anarchistic socialism” because he thought free markets would end exploitation.","challenge":"Without any shared authority, who enforces contracts and settles disputes between people with clashing claims? Critics also note a tension: if Stirner is right that there are no moral rights, the claim that taxation wrongs anyone loses its force, yet without such rights the view struggles to explain why anyone should respect your choices."},"society:Free-market conservative":{"summary":"You hold that honestly acquired holdings are just and the state should redistribute little, while valuing inherited institutions and cautious change. Markets, property, family and tradition form one order that grew over time.","reading":[{"title":"Conservatism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/conservatism/"},{"title":"Edmund Burke","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/burke/"},{"title":"Friedrich Hayek","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/friedrich-hayek/"}],"idea":"Free-market conservatives see two kinds of hard-won wisdom working together. Markets gather knowledge spread across millions of people that no planner could hold. Traditions, families, churches and local customs gather knowledge across generations. Both grew without a master plan, and both are easier to damage than to rebuild. So this view defends private property and low redistribution, perhaps with a basic safety net, and also defends the moral habits, such as thrift, honesty and family duty, that make a market society decent. It is attractive to people who want both economic freedom and social stability. Everyday example: a family business that has run for three generations, with long ties to its town, its workers and its church, captures the hoped-for blend of enterprise and rootedness.","example":"Asked whether the government should fix inequality by heavy taxation, a free-market conservative would say no: honest gains belong to those who made them, and charities, families and churches help neighbours in ways a distant bureaucracy cannot. Burke made a related point in Thoughts and Details on Scarcity (1795), warning that government attempts to control food prices would harm the poor they meant to help.","history":"Edmund Burke defended inherited institutions in Reflections on the Revolution in France (1790) and admired Adam Smith’s economics. In the twentieth century, Frank Meyer argued for joining traditional values with free markets in In Defense of Freedom (1962); critics such as L. Brent Bozell labelled the blend “fusionism”, and it shaped much of postwar American conservatism. Margaret Thatcher and Ronald Reagan joined market reform with appeals to family and nation. Friedrich Hayek shared much of this outlook but insisted he was not a conservative.","challenge":"Markets constantly change things: new technologies and business models remake towns, jobs and families. Critics, including Hayek in Why I Am Not a Conservative (1960), ask whether a philosophy of preserving traditions can sit comfortably with an economy built on creative disruption. Others ask why a safety net is acceptable but further redistribution is not."},"society:Left-libertarian":{"summary":"You combine strong self-ownership with the belief that the earth belongs to everyone. People may keep what their own work earns, but those who claim land and natural resources owe others a fair share of their value.","reading":[{"title":"Libertarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/libertarianism/"},{"title":"Distributive Justice","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justice-distributive/"}],"idea":"Left-libertarians agree with other libertarians that you own yourself: your body, your talents and the fruits of your labour are yours, and the state may not take them simply because others need them. But no one made the land, oil, minerals or radio spectrum. When someone claims a piece of the earth, they shut everyone else out of it, so they owe the rest of us compensation. The natural result is a tax on land and resource value, paid out as a dividend to all or used for shared purposes, while wages and honest profits stay untaxed. The view attracts people who want individual freedom and a fair start for everyone. Everyday example: Alaska pays every resident a yearly dividend from a fund built on its oil revenue, treating the oil as belonging to all Alaskans.","example":"Thomas Paine’s Agrarian Justice (1797) argued that private ownership of land had taken away everyone’s natural share of the earth. He proposed a national fund, paid for by a charge on inherited land, that would give every person a lump sum on reaching adulthood and a yearly pension in old age. Payment would be a right, not charity.","history":"John Locke wrote that people may appropriate land only if “enough and as good” is left for others, an idea left-libertarians build on. Thomas Paine’s Agrarian Justice (1797) proposed a citizens’ fund. Henry George’s Progress and Poverty (1879) argued for a single tax on land value. In recent decades, Hillel Steiner, Peter Vallentyne and Michael Otsuka, in Libertarianism without Inequality (2003), developed the view philosophically.","challenge":"It is hard to say how much of an asset’s value comes from nature and how much from human work, and how to measure the “fair share” owed. Right-libertarians reply that unowned land wrongs no one when first claimed. Egalitarians reply that self-ownership still lets unequal talents produce large, unearned inequalities."},"society:Libertarian":{"summary":"You think honestly acquired holdings are rightfully owned, however unequal, and that the state may not take them by force even to help the poor. The state should protect people from force, theft and fraud.","reading":[{"title":"Libertarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/libertarianism/"},{"title":"Robert Nozick’s Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nozick-political/"}],"idea":"Libertarians start from the idea that each person owns themselves and has rights that others, including governments, must not cross. If you acquired something fairly, by work, trade or gift, it is yours, and no pattern of equality gives anyone a claim to take it. That does not mean ignoring the poor: libertarians often stress charity, mutual aid societies and the way free markets have lifted billions out of poverty. But forced redistribution treats some people as tools for others. So the right state is a minimal one: police, courts and defence. The attraction is a clear, principled respect for individual choice and a deep suspicion of power. Everyday example: if you save for years to buy a bike, most people think it would be wrong for a neighbour to take it, even to give it to someone who needs it more. Libertarians think the same holds for governments.","example":"Nozick’s Wilt Chamberlain example: start with any distribution you think is just. Now a million fans each freely pay 25 cents to watch a basketball star play. He ends up far richer than everyone else. Each step was voluntary, so what could be unjust about the result? Nozick concludes that keeping any fixed pattern would require constantly interfering with free choices.","history":"John Locke’s Second Treatise of Government (1689) grounded property in labour and limited government in consent. Robert Nozick’s Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974) gave the classic philosophical statement, defending a minimal state and an “entitlement theory” of justice. Ayn Rand, Milton Friedman and Friedrich Hayek defended related views on moral and economic grounds, though Friedman and Hayek accepted some welfare provision.","challenge":"Almost every present holding traces back, somewhere, to conquest, theft or fraud, so the history the theory relies on is rarely clean. Nozick admitted that correcting past injustice might require large transfers. Critics also ask whether self-ownership really implies full ownership of whatever the market rewards."},"society:Classical liberal":{"summary":"You think honest exchange produces just holdings and that markets, property and limited government underpin a free, prosperous society, while a modest safety net keeps anyone from destitution.","reading":[{"title":"Liberalism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/liberalism/"},{"title":"Adam Smith’s Moral and Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/smith-moral-political/"},{"title":"Friedrich Hayek","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/friedrich-hayek/"}],"idea":"Classical liberals believe ordinary people, trading and cooperating freely under clear and equal laws, create far more prosperity than any ruler could plan. Prices pass along knowledge spread among millions; property gives people a stake in the future; limited government keeps power from being abused. So the state’s main jobs are to keep the peace, enforce contracts and provide a few public goods. Many classical liberals add a guaranteed floor, so no one falls into destitution, because a free society should not leave its members starving and a minimum interferes less with markets than detailed control does. Above the floor, people keep what they earn. The attraction is a balance of freedom, prosperity and basic decency. Everyday example: a pencil is made from wood, graphite and rubber from different continents, yet no one person knows how to make one; markets coordinate it all.","example":"Adam Smith’s pin factory: one worker alone might make barely one pin a day, but ten workers dividing the job into steps made tens of thousands. Classical liberals use the example to show how freedom to trade and specialise multiplies wealth. Asked about poverty, a classical liberal like Milton Friedman might propose a negative income tax: a simple cash floor, without micromanaging how people live.","history":"Adam Smith’s The Wealth of Nations (1776) founded the tradition. John Locke, David Hume and later John Stuart Mill are often counted in it. Friedrich Hayek’s The Road to Serfdom (1944) warned against central planning, while accepting a guaranteed minimum income, as did The Constitution of Liberty (1960). Milton Friedman’s Capitalism and Freedom (1962) proposed a negative income tax.","challenge":"Libertarians ask why taxing people for a safety net is allowed if taking honest earnings is otherwise wrong. Egalitarians ask why the line stops at a bare minimum, when luck of birth shapes so much of what people earn. Finding a principle that fixes the floor’s height is hard."},"society:Revolutionary socialist":{"summary":"You believe land and major industries should be owned in common, and that the current system is unjust at its roots, so it must be transformed fundamentally rather than patched.","reading":[{"title":"Karl Marx","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marx/"},{"title":"Revolution","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/revolution/"},{"title":"Socialism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/socialism/"}],"idea":"Revolutionary socialists argue that when a few people own the factories, land and banks, everyone else must work for them to live, and the owners keep part of what workers produce. This is exploitation, built into the system rather than the result of bad individuals. Because the owning class also shapes politics, media and law, reforms tend to be limited or reversed. So real justice needs a decisive break: working people taking collective control of the economy and running it for need rather than profit. The attraction is a clear diagnosis of why poverty persists amid plenty, and a hope for a society without classes. Everyday example: a warehouse worker who helps earn huge profits for a company but cannot afford rent nearby sees, on this view, the system working exactly as designed.","example":"The Communist Manifesto (1848) argued that all history is a history of class struggle, and that capitalism, which had transformed the world, was creating the working class that would replace it. A revolutionary socialist asked about a minimum wage rise would welcome it, but add that as long as owners control production, gains will be pressured and true freedom for workers will not come.","history":"Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels wrote The Communist Manifesto (1848); Marx’s Capital, volume 1, followed in 1867. Rosa Luxemburg defended revolution against gradual reform in Reform or Revolution (1900). Lenin led the Russian Revolution of 1917. In the anticolonial world, many independence movements adopted socialist goals. Angela Davis, a Communist Party candidate for US vice president in 1980 and 1984, linked class with race and gender in Women, Race and Class (1981).","challenge":"Twentieth century revolutions in the name of socialism, in the Soviet Union, China and elsewhere, often produced one-party rule, repression and famine. Defenders reply that these betrayed socialism or faced impossible conditions. Critics also ask how a planned or common economy can match markets at using information and creating incentives."},"society:Socialist":{"summary":"You believe land and major industries should be owned in common, by workers or the public, so that wealth produced together serves everyone and economic life is run democratically.","reading":[{"title":"Socialism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/socialism/"},{"title":"Karl Marx","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marx/"},{"title":"Exploitation","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/exploitation/"}],"idea":"Socialists believe that wealth is created together: by workers, engineers, teachers who trained them, and public roads that carry goods. So the big means of production, such as land, factories and major firms, should belong to everyone or to those who work in them, not to private owners who profit from others’ labour. Personal belongings stay private: socialism is about who owns the factory, not your phone. Socialists disagree about the form: state ownership, worker cooperatives, local community ownership or market socialism with competing cooperatives. They also disagree about whether to get there by reform or revolution. The attraction is a vision of community and fairness at work, where people have a voice in the decisions that shape their days. Everyday example: Spain’s Mondragon cooperatives, owned by their worker-members, show one version on a large scale.","example":"G.A. Cohen’s camping trip: imagine friends on a camping trip sharing equipment and taking turns cooking, fishing and washing up. Almost no one would want to run the trip as a market, where each charges the others for fish. Cohen argued that the spirit of the trip, equality and community, is attractive, and the main question is whether it can be extended to a whole society.","history":"Early socialists such as Robert Owen and Henri de Saint-Simon wrote in the early 1800s. Karl Marx gave socialism its most influential theory in Capital (1867). Eduard Bernstein and others sought it through elections. In the twentieth century, Yugoslavia experimented with worker self-management, and market socialists like Oskar Lange answered critics. G.A. Cohen defended the ideal in Why Not Socialism? (2009).","challenge":"Ludwig von Mises and Friedrich Hayek argued that without private ownership and market prices, planners cannot know what to produce or how efficiently. Critics also worry that common ownership concentrates power in the state. Socialists reply with market socialism and cooperatives, but debate continues on whether these work at national scale."},"society:Monarchist":{"summary":"You doubt democracy has a special claim and favour a ruler whose right comes from birth, sacred calling or long tradition, standing above parties and embodying the nation’s continuity, ideally guided by wise counsel.","reading":[{"title":"Political Legitimacy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/legitimacy/"},{"title":"Sovereignty","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sovereignty/"},{"title":"Democracy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/democracy/"}],"idea":"Monarchists value something elections cannot easily provide: continuity, unity and a ruler who belongs to no faction. A hereditary monarch is raised from childhood for the role, does not need to win votes every few years, and has a lifelong stake, passed on to their children, in the country’s long-term good. The crown can symbolise the whole nation, its history and, for many, its link to the sacred. Wise advisers and old customs guide and limit the ruler. The attraction is a politics of stability and loyalty rather than endless campaigning. Everyday example: many people in constitutional monarchies such as Japan, Norway or Britain say their monarch unites the country in a way no party politician could, even when the monarch holds little power.","example":"Hans-Hermann Hoppe’s argument in Democracy: The God That Failed (2001): an elected leader is like a temporary caretaker who gains by using up resources quickly, while a hereditary monarch is like an owner who wants the property to keep its value for his heirs. A monarchist uses this to explain why kings might plan for the long term.","history":"Robert Filmer’s Patriarcha, published in 1680, argued that kings inherit authority as fathers of their peoples; John Locke wrote his First Treatise against it. Jean Bodin had defended strong royal sovereignty in 1576. After the French Revolution, Joseph de Maistre defended monarchy as divinely rooted in Considerations on France (1797). In China, the idea of the Mandate of Heaven tied a dynasty’s right to rule to sacred calling, conditional on ruling well.","challenge":"Birth does not guarantee good character or ability, and history has many weak or cruel heirs. Without elections, there may be no peaceful way to remove a bad ruler. Critics also ask why being born into a family should give anyone a right to rule others who are, morally, their equals."},"society:Religious restorationist":{"summary":"You hold that law should reflect God’s moral order and that society has drifted from it, so the right path is recovering a lost religious foundation rather than defending present arrangements.","reading":[{"title":"Religion and Political Theory","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-politics/"},{"title":"The Natural Law Tradition in Ethics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/natural-law-ethics/"}],"idea":"Religious restorationists believe that a society’s laws and customs should honour the moral order God has given, and that modern societies have lost their way by treating faith as a private hobby. The task is not small adjustment but return: rebuilding public life on the sacred foundations it once had. This combination is distinctive. It shares the traditionalist’s reverence for religion, but sees the present as a falling away rather than something to conserve, and so can call for deep change. Its attraction is a sense that meaning, morality and community hang together, and that a society which forgets God loses its moral compass. Everyday example: someone who feels that public holidays, laws on family life and school curricula have drifted from the faith that once shaped them, and wants them renewed, shares this outlook.","example":"Joseph de Maistre, writing after the French Revolution, argued that the attempt to rebuild society by abstract reason had produced terror, and that lasting constitutions rest on Providence and tradition, not paper designs. A restorationist judging a new law would ask whether it brings public life closer to the divine order or further from it.","history":"Joseph de Maistre’s Considerations on France (1797) and Louis de Bonald called for restoring throne and altar after 1789. In the Islamic world, Sayyid Qutb’s Milestones (1964) argued that Muslim societies had fallen away from God’s sovereignty and needed to return to it. Movements for religious revival in many faiths, from Hindu, Jewish and Christian to Buddhist, contain restorationist strands, though they differ greatly in aims and methods.","challenge":"In societies with many faiths and none, whose religion should shape the law, and what happens to dissenters? Critics also ask whether the restored past is remembered accurately, and warn that attempts to impose a religious order by force have often led to violence. Restorationists reply that a secular order is itself not neutral."},"society:Religious traditionalist":{"summary":"You hold that law should reflect a moral order given by God, much of it knowable by reason, and that change should come carefully, building on inherited institutions.","reading":[{"title":"Aquinas’ Moral, Political, and Legal Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aquinas-moral-political/"},{"title":"The Natural Law Tradition in Ethics","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/natural-law-ethics/"},{"title":"Religion and Political Theory","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/religion-politics/"}],"idea":"Religious traditionalists believe there is a moral law built into the world by God, which human reason can partly grasp and revelation completes. Human laws are good when they apply that moral law wisely to a particular people and time. Because institutions such as family, church and common law carry generations of moral learning, they should be improved carefully, not torn down. The state may honour religion and support the common good, while respecting conscience. Faith, reason and tradition work together rather than competing. The attraction is a stable, coherent picture: morality is real, knowable and humane, and society is a living inheritance to be tended. Everyday example: a community that keeps a weekly day of rest, rooted in religious tradition but valued by believers and others alike for family and health, fits this outlook.","example":"Aquinas asked whether human law should forbid every vice. His answer was no: law is made for a whole people, most of whom are not perfect, so it should forbid chiefly the graver wrongs that harm others and threaten society, like murder and theft, and lead people toward virtue gradually. This gentle realism is typical of the traditionalist approach.","history":"Thomas Aquinas, in the Summa Theologiae (1265 to 1274), explained how human law flows from natural law and eternal law. Pope Leo XIII applied this tradition to modern labour questions in Rerum Novarum (1891). John Finnis revived natural law theory in Natural Law and Natural Rights (1980). Comparable pairings of divine law, reason and inherited custom appear in Jewish, Islamic and Confucian traditions.","challenge":"In a pluralistic society, citizens disagree about God and about what natural law requires. Critics ask whether laws grounded in one faith’s moral vision can be fair to those who do not share it. Traditionalists reply that natural law is meant to be knowable by reason alone, but whether it is remains disputed."},"society:Political meritocrat":{"summary":"You doubt equal votes are the only legitimate way to choose rulers, and favour officials selected for ability and good character through exams and proven records, judged by how well the people fare.","reading":[{"title":"Confucius","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confucius/"},{"title":"Mencius","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mencius/"},{"title":"Plato’s Ethics and Politics in The Republic","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/plato-ethics-politics/"}],"idea":"Political meritocrats think governing is a demanding skill and a moral responsibility, so the people who do it should be chosen carefully, the way we choose surgeons or pilots. Officials should rise by passing hard exams, showing good character and proving themselves in lower posts before reaching higher ones. Their success is judged not by winning votes but by results: are the people healthy, prosperous, safe and well educated? Many meritocrats keep democracy at the local level and use it to give feedback. The attraction is good government by capable, virtuous people who can plan beyond the next election. Everyday example: most countries already choose judges, central bankers and senior civil servants largely by expertise rather than election, because these jobs need knowledge and independence.","example":"Imperial China’s civil service exams, held for over a thousand years until 1905, let men of humble birth rise to high office through learning. Daniel Bell’s The China Model (2015) proposes a modern version: democracy at the bottom, experimentation in the middle, and meritocratic promotion at the top, with officials tested at each level.","history":"Confucius taught that rulers should govern by virtue and employ the worthy. Mencius held that the people matter most and that rulers who harm them lose their right to rule. In Plato’s Republic, philosopher-kings trained for decades govern. China selected officials by examination from the Sui dynasty until 1905. Daniel A. Bell defended political meritocracy in The China Model (2015).","challenge":"Who chooses the selectors, and what keeps a meritocratic elite from serving itself? Without elections, citizens may have no peaceful way to remove officials who fail them. Critics also doubt that exams can measure character, and worry that the selection process will favour the already privileged."},"society:Epistocrat":{"summary":"You doubt democracy has special value and think political power should lean toward knowledge: everyone may take part, but those who understand the issues should have more say.","reading":[{"title":"Democracy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/democracy/"},{"title":"Political Legitimacy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/legitimacy/"}],"idea":"Epistocrats point out that voting is not just self-expression: your vote helps decide policies imposed on everyone else. Since bad policies can cause wars, recessions and suffering, voters arguably owe others competence, as a doctor owes patients. Surveys show many voters know little about basic political facts. So epistocrats propose structures that weight decisions toward the informed: extra votes for those who pass a test of basic political knowledge, a voter qualification exam, or a council of experts with a veto over clearly uninformed laws. Most keep universal rights and participation. The attraction is a sober concern for outcomes: what matters is that laws actually make life better. Everyday example: we require tests before people can drive, because driving badly endangers others. Epistocrats ask why voting, which also affects others, should be different.","example":"Brennan, in Against Democracy (2016), sorts citizens into “hobbits” (uninterested), “hooligans” (fierce partisans who ignore contrary evidence) and “vulcans” (careful, impartial thinkers). He argues democracy gives hooligans great power, and suggests experiments such as a “government by simulated oracle”, which estimates what an informed public would want.","history":"Plato argued in the Republic that ruling requires knowledge few possess. John Stuart Mill, in Considerations on Representative Government (1861), proposed giving extra votes to the better educated while keeping universal suffrage. Jason Brennan’s Against Democracy (2016) is the leading modern defence. David Estlund named the view “epistocracy” and criticised it in Democratic Authority (2008).","challenge":"Estlund’s main objection: reasonable people can always dispute who counts as knowledgeable, and any test may reflect the biases of those who design it. Knowledge tests might also reduce the voice of poorer or marginalised groups whose interests then get ignored. Democrats add that equal votes express equal respect."},"society:Hobbesian":{"summary":"You think authority comes from agreement and that a government is legitimate when it delivers peace and order, however it came to power, because without a strong sovereign life would collapse into conflict.","reading":[{"title":"Hobbes’s Moral and Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hobbes-moral/"},{"title":"Contractarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/contractarianism/"},{"title":"Political Obligation","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/political-obligation/"}],"idea":"Hobbesians begin with a frightening thought experiment: imagine no government at all. People who fear each other would strike first, no one could trust a deal, and no one could invest in farms or knowledge. To escape, everyone has reason to agree to obey a sovereign strong enough to keep the peace. What matters is not how the ruler got power, by election, inheritance or even conquest, but whether they provide security. By living under the law’s protection, we accept its terms. The attraction is hard-headed realism: order is the precondition of every other good, and it is fragile. Everyday example: when a country’s government collapses, as in civil wars, even people who disliked the old regime often find that the chaos that follows is worse.","example":"Hobbes’s state of nature, in Leviathan (1651): without a common power to keep everyone in awe, there would be no industry, no arts and no society, and the life of man would be “solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short.” A Hobbesian judging a revolution asks first whether it is likely to end in a stable peace, or in years of war.","history":"Thomas Hobbes wrote Leviathan (1651) during and after the English Civil War, arguing that people covenant to obey a sovereign who protects them, and that sovereignty can arise even by conquest. In ancient China, the Legalist Han Feizi also argued that order depends on a strong ruler and clear laws, though not on a contract. Modern contractarians such as David Gauthier have revived Hobbesian reasoning in ethics.","challenge":"If the sovereign has nearly unlimited power, what protects people from the sovereign? John Locke joked that this is like guarding against polecats and foxes by being devoured by lions. Critics also ask whether consent given under fear, or just by living somewhere, is real consent at all."},"society:Conservative":{"summary":"You believe institutions should change carefully, building on what exists, because inherited practices hold wisdom no one designed and are easier to break than to rebuild. Shared traditions are part of who people are.","reading":[{"title":"Conservatism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/conservatism/"},{"title":"Edmund Burke","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/burke/"},{"title":"Michael Oakeshott","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/oakeshott/"}],"idea":"Conservatives are humble about how much any one generation, or any clever plan, can know. Institutions like the family, common law, local communities and religious practices were shaped by countless people solving real problems over centuries. Their reasons are often unstated, so tearing them down for an abstract design risks losing things we did not know we needed. That does not mean never changing: it means reforming the way a gardener prunes, not the way a bulldozer clears. Conservatives also see traditions, language and shared values as part of a community’s identity, worth protecting. The attraction is gratitude for what we have inherited and caution about what we might lose. Everyday example: a fence that seems pointless may be there for a reason; G.K. Chesterton advised finding out why it was built before taking it down.","example":"Faced with a proposal to replace a long-standing institution, a conservative asks: what problems does it currently solve, perhaps quietly? Can we fix the specific fault while keeping the rest? Burke, in Reflections on the Revolution in France (1790), argued that the revolutionaries’ attempt to rebuild France on abstract principles would lead to disorder, a prediction many felt the Terror bore out.","history":"Edmund Burke’s Reflections on the Revolution in France (1790) is the founding text. Michael Oakeshott’s essay “On Being Conservative” (1956) described conservatism as a disposition to prefer the familiar to the unknown. Confucius, centuries earlier, described himself as a transmitter who loved antiquity. Russell Kirk, Roger Scruton and many others carried the tradition into the twentieth century.","challenge":"Inherited institutions have sometimes contained serious injustices, such as slavery or the exclusion of women, and conservatism can seem to protect them. Critics ask how to tell real wisdom from mere habit or privilege. Conservatives reply that reform is welcome when it grows from a tradition’s own best values."},"society:National conservative":{"summary":"You favour careful change built on inherited institutions, and see the nation as a community of special loyalty whose government owes most to its own people. Shared history sustains trust and self-government.","reading":[{"title":"Conservatism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/conservatism/"},{"title":"Nationalism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nationalism/"},{"title":"Patriotism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/patriotism/"}],"idea":"National conservatives believe the nation is more than a legal arrangement. It is a home: a community bound by shared language, history, landscape and customs, whose members owe each other the special loyalty family members do. That loyalty is what makes people willing to pay taxes for strangers, accept election results they dislike and sacrifice for the common good. So a government’s first duty is to its own citizens, and national institutions and culture deserve protection. Change should be gradual and in keeping with the nation’s character. The attraction is a sense of belonging and a defence of self-government against distant, unaccountable powers. Everyday example: the pride people feel singing their anthem at a sporting event, or caring for a local war memorial, reflects the attachment national conservatives think holds a democracy together.","example":"Scruton coined the word oikophilia, love of home, for the attachment that makes people care for their place. Asked about a trade or migration policy, a national conservative would ask first what it means for the nation’s workers, communities and shared culture, believing that a government which serves everyone in the world equally serves no one well.","history":"Edmund Burke wrote of the “little platoon” we belong to as the first link in public affection. Roger Scruton developed national conservatism in England: An Elegy (2000), The Need for Nations (2004) and Green Philosophy (2012). Yoram Hazony’s The Virtue of Nationalism (2018) defended a world of independent nations against empires and global governance.","challenge":"Critics ask who counts as a member of the nation, and worry that emphasis on shared culture can sideline minorities and newcomers. History shows that national loyalty has sometimes turned aggressive. Cosmopolitans also question why being born on one side of a border should change how much a person matters."},"society:Humean conservative":{"summary":"You think rules of property and justice are just when they are settled conventions people have long lived by and planned around, and that change should respect them, because stability lets people plan their lives.","reading":[{"title":"Hume’s Moral Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume-moral/"},{"title":"Michael Oakeshott","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/oakeshott/"},{"title":"Conservatism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/conservatism/"}],"idea":"Humean conservatives think that justice is not handed down from heaven or worked out by pure reason. It is a set of human conventions, about property, promises and government, that grew because they let people cooperate. Once established, these rules matter enormously: people build their homes, businesses and plans around them. Constantly rewriting them for some ideal pattern would destroy the trust that makes cooperation possible. So the settled rules, applied fairly and predictably, are what justice largely consists in, and change should be careful and gradual. The attraction is practical wisdom without mystery: justice serves real human needs. Everyday example: driving on the left or right is arbitrary, but once a country chooses, everyone gains from sticking to it. Many rules of property work similarly.","example":"Hume’s two rowers: two people rowing a boat fall into a shared rhythm without ever making a promise, simply because each sees the benefit. Hume said justice and property arise the same way. A Humean conservative facing a proposal to redistribute land would worry about unsettling expectations everyone relies on, even if the original division was somewhat arbitrary.","history":"David Hume, in A Treatise of Human Nature (Book 3, 1740), called justice an “artificial virtue” grounded in convention, and in “Of the Original Contract” (1748) rejected social contract theories. In ancient China, Xunzi argued that sages created ritual and rules to curb conflict over scarce goods. Friedrich Hayek drew on Hume, and Michael Oakeshott defended rule-governed practice over rationalist planning.","challenge":"If justice is whatever conventions are settled, how can we criticise settled conventions that are cruel, such as caste or slavery? Critics also note that those who benefit from existing rules often decide which ones count as settled. Humeans reply that conventions are judged by how well they serve everyone."},"society:Restorationist":{"summary":"You believe much modern change was a mistake and that we should recover good ways of life that were lost, with the state supporting a shared vision of the good or a community’s traditions.","reading":[{"title":"Confucius","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confucius/"},{"title":"Alasdair Chalmers MacIntyre (IEP)","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/mac-over/"},{"title":"Communitarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/communitarianism/"}],"idea":"Restorationists deny that history always moves forward. Some changes, they think, cost us things of real value: strong communities, shared moral language, practices that formed good character, respect for elders and craftsmanship. The task is to recover them, not by copying the past exactly, but by renewing what was wise in it. Because these goods are shared rather than private, the community and state may support them, through education, public ritual and law. The attraction is a serious answer to modern loneliness and moral confusion, and the hope that wisdom from the past can be relearned. Everyday example: revivals of traditional crafts, family meals, communal festivals or classical education often come from people who feel modern life lost something worth getting back.","example":"MacIntyre’s After Virtue (1981) opens with a thought experiment: imagine that science was destroyed and later rebuilt from fragments, so people used scientific words without understanding them. He argued that modern moral talk is in this state. The book ends hoping for new communities that can sustain the virtues through a dark age, as monasteries once did.","history":"Confucius sought to restore the rites of the early Zhou dynasty, which he saw as a lost model of harmonious order. Alasdair MacIntyre’s After Virtue (1981) argued for recovering an Aristotelian tradition of the virtues. Many movements, from Renaissance humanism, which looked back to antiquity, to modern revivals of religious and local traditions, have taken a restorationist shape.","challenge":"The past also contained deep injustices, and critics ask whether the lost golden age is remembered accurately. Even if old ways were better, conditions have changed, and it may be impossible to recreate them. Restorationists reply that they seek to renew principles, not reproduce every detail of the past."},"society:Philosophical anarchist":{"summary":"You deny that any state’s commands bind you merely because they are commands, yet think a good state can be justified by what it does for people. You may obey most laws because they demand what is right anyway.","reading":[{"title":"Political Obligation","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/political-obligation/"},{"title":"Authority","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/authority/"},{"title":"Anarchism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/anarchism/"}],"idea":"Philosophical anarchists separate two questions. Is it good that a state exists? Possibly yes, if it keeps peace and provides public goods. Does that give it the right to command you, so that you must obey just because it says so? No. None of the standard arguments, such as consent, fair play or gratitude, seem to create a general duty to obey every law. So you obey laws against murder because murder is wrong, not because they are laws, and you are free to judge harmless rule-breaking on its merits. This is not a call to overthrow the state. The attraction is intellectual honesty: it keeps what is good about the state while refusing to treat its commands as sacred. Everyday example: crossing an empty road at 3 a.m. on a red light seems harmless, and many people feel no real duty to wait.","example":"Robert Paul Wolff argued that a responsible adult must always decide for themselves what to do, so accepting any command just because it is a command gives up moral autonomy. A philosophical anarchist asked to pay taxes would usually do so, because the taxes fund schools and hospitals, while denying that the mere fact of the law settles the question.","history":"Henry David Thoreau’s “Civil Disobedience” (1849) argued that conscience comes before law. Robert Paul Wolff’s In Defense of Anarchism (1970) argued that state authority conflicts with autonomy. A. John Simmons’s Moral Principles and Political Obligations (1979) examined and rejected each major argument for a duty to obey. Joseph Raz, in The Authority of Law (1979), denied that there is a general obligation to obey the law, even in a just society.","challenge":"If everyone obeys only laws they personally judge right, the coordination that laws provide may weaken. Defenders of political obligation, such as H.L.A. Hart and John Rawls, argued that people who benefit from a fair cooperative scheme owe it their part. The view must also explain why a state may enforce laws that citizens need not obey."},"society:Penal abolitionist":{"summary":"You think nothing justifies deliberately inflicting punishment, and that society should answer wrongdoing by repairing harm, bringing victim and offender together to make things right.","reading":[{"title":"Legal Punishment","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/legal-punishment/"}],"idea":"Penal abolitionists question whether deliberately making someone suffer is ever the right response to wrongdoing. Prisons, they note, often damage people, break up families and return offenders more likely to reoffend, while victims get little say. Abolitionists propose shifting from punishment to repair: the offender faces those harmed, acknowledges what happened and makes amends, and the community helps address causes such as addiction or poverty. Some keep secure confinement for the truly dangerous, framed as protection rather than punishment. The attraction is a humane focus on healing victims and restoring relationships rather than adding a second harm to the first. Everyday example: when a student vandalises a classroom, many schools now have them meet the teacher, apologise and help repair the damage, rather than simply suspending them.","example":"Nils Christie’s essay “Conflicts as Property” (1977) argued that the state “steals” conflicts from the people involved: lawyers and judges take over, and the victim becomes a bystander. In a restorative conference, by contrast, victim, offender and their families meet with a facilitator, talk about the harm, and agree together on how to put things right.","history":"Nils Christie wrote “Conflicts as Property” (1977) and Limits to Pain (1981). Howard Zehr’s Changing Lenses (1990) helped launch the restorative justice movement. New Zealand’s 1989 youth justice law made family group conferences, drawing on Māori practices, central to handling young offenders. Angela Davis asked Are Prisons Obsolete? (2003). Many Indigenous traditions emphasise repair and reintegration over punishment.","challenge":"Restorative processes depend on offenders willing to take part and victims willing to meet them, and many are not. Critics also ask how society can protect people from dangerous offenders, and deter crime, without some threat of punishment. Defenders of retribution add that serious wrongs may deserve a response beyond repair."},"society:Communitarian":{"summary":"You believe a community’s traditions, language and values are part of who its people are, and that law may protect them. Justice involves desert, sufficiency or equal standing within a shared way of life.","reading":[{"title":"Communitarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/communitarianism/"},{"title":"Hegel’s Social and Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hegel-social-political/"}],"idea":"Communitarians reject the picture of people as isolated choosers who pick their values like items in a shop. We are formed by families, languages, religions and communities before we can choose anything, and these attachments are part of who we are. A good society therefore cares for the shared life that sustains its members, not only their separate rights. Justice, too, must make sense within that shared life: what people deserve, what they need for a decent life, and how they stand as equals depend on the community’s understanding of goods like healthcare, education and honour. The attraction is a richer picture of human beings, and a defence of belonging against loneliness and pure market logic. Everyday example: a town that protects its local language in schools, or keeps its central square for markets and festivals, acts on communitarian ideas.","example":"Sandel, in What Money Can’t Buy (2012), asks whether people should be allowed to pay others to stand in line for them, or whether schools should pay children to read. He argues that putting a price on some goods changes their meaning and crowds out the shared values that should govern them, which is a question markets alone cannot answer.","history":"G.W.F. Hegel’s Philosophy of Right (1820) described ethical life, the customs and institutions in which freedom becomes real. In the 1980s, Michael Sandel’s Liberalism and the Limits of Justice (1982), Michael Walzer’s Spheres of Justice (1983), Alasdair MacIntyre and Charles Taylor criticised individualist liberalism. Confucian thought, with its stress on roles and relationships, shares much with communitarianism.","challenge":"Communities can be oppressive, especially to minorities, women or dissenters within them. Critics ask who decides what a community’s values are, and what happens to people who want to leave or change them. Liberals reply that rights exist precisely to protect individuals from the majority."},"society:Political skeptic":{"summary":"You doubt there are objective moral facts about political authority or justice. States exist because people want them or are made to accept them, and legitimacy claims express interests and attitudes.","reading":[{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"},{"title":"Max Stirner","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/max-stirner/"}],"idea":"Political skeptics apply a general doubt about objective morality to politics. When people say a government is legitimate, a law must be obeyed or a distribution is just, skeptics think they are expressing preferences, loyalties or interests, not reporting facts like the boiling point of water. States exist because enough people support them, or are made to go along. This does not mean skeptics have no political views. They may strongly prefer peace, freedom or equality, and argue for them. They just deny that the universe takes sides. The attraction is clear-eyed honesty: it strips away grand claims and lets people see politics as a negotiation among real human desires. Everyday example: two people arguing whether a tax is “fair” may each be voicing what they want, dressed up in language that sounds objective.","example":"In Plato’s Republic, Book I, Thrasymachus argues that justice is simply the advantage of the stronger: rulers make laws that suit themselves and call them just. A political skeptic need not be so cynical, but would read a debate about whether a state is legitimate as a clash of interests and values, and look for workable compromises rather than the one right answer.","history":"Thrasymachus in Plato’s Republic gave an early version. Max Stirner’s The Ego and Its Own (1844) treated morality, the state and rights as “spooks” that rule the mind. J.L. Mackie’s Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977) defended moral error theory. Richard Garner’s Beyond Morality (1994) argued we could abandon moral language and still live well, drawing in part on Daoist and Buddhist ideas.","challenge":"If no political arrangement is truly better than another, can the skeptic say that slavery or genocide was wrong, rather than just disliked? Critics also note that skeptics still argue, protest and prefer, which seems to assume some views are better supported. Skeptics reply that preferences can be strong and shared without being objective facts."},"society:Cosmopolitan":{"summary":"You believe borders do not change what we owe: every human life counts equally. Justice also attends to outcomes, so we have serious duties toward the world’s poor.","reading":[{"title":"Cosmopolitanism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/cosmopolitanism/"},{"title":"International Distributive Justice","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/international-justice/"}],"idea":"Cosmopolitans believe that every human being has equal moral worth, whatever their nationality. Being born in one country rather than another is luck, and it should not decide whether someone has clean water, schooling or safety. Combined with a concern that inequalities benefit the worst off, or that everyone has enough for a decent life, this means wealthy people and countries have real duties to the global poor: through aid, fairer trade rules and reform of international institutions. Cosmopolitans can still love their own countries; they just deny that love cancels obligations to outsiders. The attraction is moral consistency and a vision of humanity as one community. Everyday example: when people give to fight malaria in countries they will never visit, they act on the cosmopolitan idea that a distant child’s life matters as much as a nearby one.","example":"Peter Singer’s drowning child (1972): if you saw a child drowning in a shallow pond, you would wade in even if it ruined your shoes. Singer argued that distance does not change the duty, so we should give to save children dying of preventable causes far away. Thomas Pogge adds that rich countries also shape the global rules that keep many poor.","history":"Diogenes the Cynic reportedly called himself a citizen of the world, and the Stoics developed the idea. In ancient China, Mozi taught impartial care for all. Immanuel Kant’s Perpetual Peace (1795) proposed a cosmopolitan right. Martha Nussbaum’s “Patriotism and Cosmopolitanism” (1994) and Thomas Pogge’s World Poverty and Human Rights (2002) are leading modern statements.","challenge":"Many people feel they owe more to family, neighbours and fellow citizens, and critics argue these special ties are valuable, not prejudice. Others ask whether global justice can work without a global government, which might be dangerous. Thomas Nagel argued that strong duties of justice exist only within states."},"society:Radical egalitarian":{"summary":"You think the present system is unjust at its roots and must be transformed, not patched, and that justice means equal standing and fair shares rather than ranks of superior and inferior.","reading":[{"title":"Frantz Fanon","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/frantz-fanon/"},{"title":"Egalitarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egalitarianism/"},{"title":"Equality","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/equality/"}],"idea":"Radical egalitarians believe that hierarchies of race, class, caste, gender and empire are not accidents in an otherwise fair system. They are built into how wealth, power and respect are distributed, so small reforms leave the structure standing. Justice requires transforming that structure until people meet as equals: no one dominating, no one forced to bow, and shares of wealth and opportunity that do not depend on the luck of birth. The attraction is moral seriousness about historic wrongs, and a refusal to accept that deep inequality is natural or permanent. Everyday example: someone who notes that families who were barred from owning homes or land generations ago still have far less wealth today, and concludes that patching the system will not fix the gap, is thinking like a radical egalitarian.","example":"Du Bois, in The Souls of Black Folk (1903), described “double consciousness”, the experience of seeing oneself through the eyes of a society that looks on with contempt. A radical egalitarian would ask of any reform whether it actually ends that unequal standing, or only softens it while leaving the underlying hierarchy in place.","history":"W.E.B. Du Bois wrote The Souls of Black Folk (1903) and Black Reconstruction in America (1935), and later embraced socialism. Frantz Fanon’s Black Skin, White Masks (1952) and The Wretched of the Earth (1961) analysed colonialism and called for its complete overthrow. B.R. Ambedkar’s Annihilation of Caste (1936) demanded the end, not reform, of caste. Many anticolonial and civil rights thinkers share this outlook.","challenge":"Critics ask whether sweeping transformation can avoid creating new hierarchies or harms, as some revolutions did. Reformers point out that gradual change, such as civil rights laws, has achieved real gains. There is also debate over which equality matters most: of standing, of resources or of opportunity."},"society:Republican (freedom as non-domination)":{"summary":"You believe freedom means no one holding arbitrary power over you, and that justice requires equal standing or enough for all, so no one depends on another’s goodwill. Accountable laws protect freedom.","reading":[{"title":"Republicanism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/republicanism/"},{"title":"Mary Wollstonecraft","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wollstonecraft/"},{"title":"Niccolò Machiavelli","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/machiavelli/"}],"idea":"Republicans in this philosophical sense (not the US party) define freedom as not being dominated. A slave with a kind master may never be punished, but is still unfree, because everything depends on the master’s mood. Likewise, a worker who can be fired on a whim, or a wife with no legal rights, lives at someone else’s mercy. So real freedom requires protections: laws made through accountable, contestable processes; enough resources that no one must beg; and equal civic standing. Good laws do not reduce freedom; they create it by limiting arbitrary power. The attraction is a picture of citizens who can look each other in the eye. Everyday example: employees who have legal protection against unfair dismissal can speak up at work without fear, even if their boss is pleasant; republicans say they are freer.","example":"Philip Pettit’s eyeball test: people are free when they can look others in the eye without reason for fear or deference that a power of interference might inspire. Applied to a policy, a republican asks whether it leaves anyone subject to another’s unchecked will, whether that is an employer, a spouse, an official or a powerful company.","history":"Roman writers such as Cicero contrasted the free citizen with the slave. Niccolò Machiavelli’s Discourses on Livy (written around 1517) praised mixed government that checks the powerful. Mary Wollstonecraft’s A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792) argued that women’s dependence on men made them unfree. Philip Pettit’s Republicanism (1997) and Quentin Skinner’s Liberty before Liberalism (1998) revived the tradition.","challenge":"Critics ask what exactly counts as “arbitrary” power, and whether a good law is any less an interference than a bad one. Some liberals argue that freedom as non-interference is clearer. Others worry that eliminating all domination would require extensive state power, which could itself dominate."},"society:Social democrat":{"summary":"You believe freedom means real options, not just absence of interference, and that justice requires inequalities to serve the worst off or everyone to have enough, reached step by step through democratic reform.","reading":[{"title":"Dewey’s Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dewey-political/"},{"title":"Positive and Negative Liberty","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/liberty-positive-negative/"},{"title":"Socialism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/socialism/"}],"idea":"Social democrats think freedom is about what you can actually do. A person with no money, poor health and little education is not very free, even if nobody blocks them. So a just society gives everyone real capabilities through public schools, healthcare, social insurance and fair wages, while keeping markets, which produce wealth and choice, under democratic rules. Change comes through elections, unions and tested reforms, keeping what works and fixing what fails, rather than revolution. The attraction is a practical balance: the dynamism of markets with the security of a strong welfare state, built by consent. Everyday example: in many European countries, students attend university for low or no fees and everyone has public health coverage, while private businesses compete freely, which is the social democratic model at work.","example":"Faced with high child poverty, a social democrat would not seize industries or simply leave it to charity. Instead they would test policies, such as a child benefit, free school meals or subsidised childcare, measure the results, and expand what works. John Dewey called this approach intelligent social action: treating policy as an ongoing democratic experiment.","history":"Eduard Bernstein’s The Preconditions of Socialism (1899), known in English as Evolutionary Socialism, argued for reaching socialist goals by gradual reform. John Dewey’s Liberalism and Social Action (1935) defended active democratic government. The Nordic countries built extensive welfare states from the 1930s on. T.H. Green earlier, and Amartya Sen’s capability approach later, gave positive freedom philosophical depth.","challenge":"Critics on the right worry that high taxes and a large state reduce incentives, strain budgets and crowd out families and civil society. Critics on the left argue that social democracy manages capitalism without ending its inequalities, and that gains can be reversed when political winds shift."},"society:Millian liberal":{"summary":"You think adults should be sovereign over their own lives, speech may be limited only to prevent direct harm, and just arrangements make lives go best, with perhaps extra weight for the worse off.","reading":[{"title":"Mill’s Moral and Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mill-moral-political/"},{"title":"Paternalism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/paternalism/"},{"title":"Freedom of Speech","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/freedom-speech/"}],"idea":"Millian liberals combine two commitments that might seem to clash: individual liberty and making people’s lives go well. Mill’s bridge is that liberty is good for people. Adults generally know their own interests best; people learn and grow by making their own choices; and societies discover truth and new ways of living only when ideas and experiments are free. So the state may restrain you only to prevent harm to others, never just for your own good, and offensive ideas must be answered with argument, not censorship. At the same time, social arrangements should be judged by how well they make lives go. The attraction is a hopeful, open picture of progress through freedom. Everyday example: a friend takes up a risky hobby like rock climbing. You can warn them, but on Mill’s view no one may stop them.","example":"Mill’s corn dealer example in On Liberty (1859): it is fine to publish the opinion that corn dealers starve the poor, but saying the same thing to an angry mob outside a corn dealer’s house may rightly be punished, because there it directly incites harm. Mill also argued that even a false opinion is worth hearing, since challenging it keeps truth alive.","history":"John Stuart Mill’s On Liberty (1859) set out the harm principle, and Utilitarianism (1861) defended the greatest happiness principle. His The Subjection of Women (1869), written with input from Harriet Taylor Mill, applied liberty to women’s equality. Wilhelm von Humboldt’s The Limits of State Action influenced Mill. Joel Feinberg’s The Moral Limits of the Criminal Law (1984 to 1988) developed the harm principle further.","challenge":"If what matters is that lives go well, why not restrict choices that clearly harm the chooser, like smoking or not wearing a seatbelt? Critics argue utility and liberty can come apart. It is also hard to define “harm” precisely: does offence count, or harm to someone’s reputation or community?"},"society:Perfectionist":{"summary":"You believe the state may encourage what makes lives go well, such as learning, health and good character, and that politics exists to help people flourish rather than staying neutral between ways of life.","reading":[{"title":"Perfectionism in Moral and Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/perfectionism-moral/"},{"title":"Aristotle’s Political Theory","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-politics/"}],"idea":"Perfectionists think some ways of living really are better than others: lives with knowledge, friendship, skill, health and good character go better than lives wasted in idleness or addiction. Since government shapes so much of life anyway, it may as well help people flourish: funding museums, libraries and the arts, encouraging education and exercise, and discouraging what wastes lives. Liberal perfectionists stress that the state should mostly encourage rather than force, and that autonomy, choosing your own path among good options, is itself part of flourishing. Justice, on this view, rewards contribution and ensures everyone has enough to live well. The attraction is honesty about values and a positive vision of public life. Everyday example: when a city funds free public libraries, parks and sports leagues rather than treating all pastimes as equal, it is acting on perfectionist reasoning.","example":"Aristotle argued in the Politics that a city exists not merely for life but for the good life, and that a lawgiver should aim to make citizens virtuous. A modern perfectionist asked whether to tax sugary drinks and subsidise sports clubs would say yes, since healthy, active lives are genuinely better, while leaving people free to choose.","history":"Aristotle’s Nicomachean Ethics and Politics held that politics aims at human flourishing. Confucian thinkers saw government as cultivating virtue, and Joseph Chan’s Confucian Perfectionism (2014) revives this view. Joseph Raz’s The Morality of Freedom (1986) defended liberal perfectionism built on autonomy. Thomas Hurka’s Perfectionism (1993) gave a systematic philosophical account of the idea.","challenge":"Who decides what a good life is? People disagree deeply, and critics argue that state support for some ways of life disrespects those who choose others. Liberals worry that even gentle encouragement can slide into paternalism, using public money and power to favour the tastes of those in charge."},"society:Liberal nationalist":{"summary":"You believe a nation is a community of special loyalty whose government owes most to its own members, and that national solidarity makes social justice, through desert, sufficiency or equal standing, possible.","reading":[{"title":"Nationalism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nationalism/"},{"title":"Patriotism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/patriotism/"}],"idea":"Liberal nationalists join two ideas: national belonging and liberal justice. People who share a language, history and public culture trust each other more and feel responsible for each other, which is why they will pay taxes for strangers’ healthcare and accept democratic decisions they voted against. So nations are not obstacles to justice; they make it possible. Within a nation, citizens owe each other fair rewards for contribution, a decent minimum and equal standing. Nations can be open and diverse, as long as citizens share a common public identity, and their governments rightly put their own citizens first while respecting human rights everywhere. The attraction is a grounded, realistic route to a fair society. Everyday example: people often accept higher taxes for a national health service partly because they see it as “ours”, a shared national project.","example":"David Miller compares nations to families: we rightly give more to our own children while respecting other families’ rights. Asked whether to spend on domestic welfare or foreign aid, a liberal nationalist would give priority to fellow citizens, while still accepting duties to prevent severe suffering abroad and to respect human rights everywhere.","history":"John Stuart Mill argued in Considerations on Representative Government (1861) that free institutions work best within a shared nationality. Giuseppe Mazzini linked national self-determination with liberty. Yael Tamir’s Liberal Nationalism (1993) and David Miller’s On Nationality (1995) are the main modern statements; Will Kymlicka explored minority nations and multicultural citizenship.","challenge":"Critics ask whether giving priority to fellow nationals is fair to people born in poorer countries through no fault of their own. Others worry that a shared national identity can exclude minorities and immigrants. Cosmopolitans argue that justice should not stop at borders."},"society:Rawlsian liberal":{"summary":"You believe inequalities are fair only when they make the worst off better off than they would otherwise be, the principle people would choose behind a veil of ignorance.","reading":[{"title":"John Rawls","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rawls/"},{"title":"Original Position","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/original-position/"},{"title":"Distributive Justice","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justice-distributive/"}],"idea":"Rawlsian liberals ask a clever question: what rules would you pick for society if you did not know who you would be: rich or poor, talented or not, which religion or race? Behind this “veil of ignorance”, Rawls argued, you would first secure equal basic liberties and fair equality of opportunity for all. Then you would allow economic inequalities only if they improve the position of the worst off, for instance, higher pay for doctors that leads to more and better medical care for everyone. This keeps markets and incentives, but harnesses them for the least advantaged. The attraction is a fair procedure that respects freedom and equality together. Everyday example: when one person cuts a cake and another chooses first, the cutter has every reason to cut fairly. The veil of ignorance works similarly.","example":"Rawls’s original position, from A Theory of Justice (1971): imagine designing society behind a veil of ignorance. Asked whether to cut taxes on the rich, a Rawlsian would ask whether the cut would, through investment and jobs, end up raising the prospects of the poorest. If so, it may be just; if not, it is not.","history":"John Rawls’s A Theory of Justice (1971) revived political philosophy and set out the difference principle. His Political Liberalism (1993) addressed how people with different worldviews can share a just society. Thomas Nagel’s Equality and Partiality (1991) and “The Problem of Global Justice” (2005) developed related views. Rawls’s work drew on the social contract tradition of Locke, Rousseau and Kant.","challenge":"Robert Nozick objected that the difference principle ignores how holdings came about and treats talents as a common asset. Economist John Harsanyi argued that rational people behind the veil would maximise average welfare, not insure against the worst case. G.A. Cohen argued from the left that incentives for the talented are themselves unjust."},"society:Luck egalitarian":{"summary":"You believe inequalities are fair when they flow from people’s own choices and unfair when they flow from luck, such as talent, health or family. Justice compensates bad luck while holding people responsible for choices.","reading":[{"title":"Egalitarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egalitarianism/"},{"title":"Distributive Justice","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justice-distributive/"}],"idea":"Luck egalitarians try to combine two widely shared intuitions. First, it is unfair when someone is worse off through no fault of their own: born with a disability, into poverty or without marketable talents. Second, people should take responsibility for their choices: someone who works long hours deserves more than someone who chooses leisure. So justice should neutralise brute luck while respecting choice. The state compensates for unchosen disadvantages, through health care, education and insurance, but does not erase gains or losses that come from genuine choices. The attraction is a theory that is both compassionate and fair to the hardworking. Everyday example: most people think a child born with a serious illness deserves help paying for treatment, but feel differently about someone who gambles away their savings at a casino.","example":"Dworkin’s desert island auction: shipwrecked survivors each get equal clamshells to bid on the island’s resources, so no one envies another’s bundle. They also buy insurance against bad luck, such as illness. Dworkin used this to model a fair society: equal starting resources, insurance against brute luck, and freedom to make choices with their consequences.","history":"Ronald Dworkin’s “What is Equality? Part 2: Equality of Resources” (1981) and Sovereign Virtue (2000) launched the view. Richard Arneson and G.A. Cohen developed versions; Cohen’s “On the Currency of Egalitarian Justice” (1989) argued from a socialist perspective. Elizabeth Anderson gave the view its name, critically, in “What Is the Point of Equality?” (1999).","challenge":"Anderson’s objection: strict luck egalitarianism seems to abandon people who make bad choices, such as an uninsured driver injured through their own carelessness. It also requires the state to judge which outcomes came from choice and which from luck, which is intrusive and very hard, since choices are shaped by upbringing."},"society:Relational egalitarian":{"summary":"You believe justice means people relating as equals: no one so poor they must bow, no one so powerful others must bow to them. What matters is ending domination and exclusion, not equal shares.","reading":[{"title":"Egalitarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egalitarianism/"},{"title":"Equality","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/equality/"}],"idea":"Relational egalitarians say the point of equality is not that everyone has the same amount of stuff. It is that people live together as equals, without masters and servants, superiors and inferiors. What is wrong with caste, racism, sexism or extreme poverty is that they create relationships of domination, humiliation and exclusion. So justice requires that everyone has what they need to stand as an equal citizen: enough income, education and health, equal political voice and freedom from contempt. Some inequality of wealth is acceptable, but not so much that the rich can control the poor. The attraction is that it focuses on what people in egalitarian movements actually fought for: dignity and respect. Everyday example: a workplace where everyone, from cleaner to CEO, eats in the same canteen and is addressed with the same courtesy reflects relational equality.","example":"Ambedkar’s Annihilation of Caste (1936), a speech so radical its organisers cancelled the event, argued that caste is not just a division of labour but a division of labourers into graded ranks of honour and contempt. A relational egalitarian would ask of any policy not only who gets what, but whether it ends such ranks.","history":"Elizabeth Anderson’s “What Is the Point of Equality?” (1999) set out democratic equality. Samuel Scheffler and others developed the view. B.R. Ambedkar wrote Annihilation of Caste (1936) and chaired the drafting committee of India’s Constitution, which abolished untouchability. Mary Wollstonecraft and Frederick Douglass are earlier figures who opposed relations of domination.","challenge":"Critics ask what exactly “relating as equals” requires, and whether the view gives clear answers about distribution. Luck egalitarians argue that some inequalities, like one person’s untreated illness, are unjust even if they create no relation of domination."},"society:Prioritarian":{"summary":"You believe benefits count more the worse off the person receiving them, so policy should give priority to those who have least. Gaps do not matter in themselves, and not all gains weigh the same.","reading":[{"title":"Egalitarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egalitarianism/"},{"title":"Distributive Justice","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justice-distributive/"}],"idea":"Prioritarians think that helping someone who is badly off matters more than giving the same help to someone already doing well. This is not because inequality itself is bad, but because the worse off a person is, the more weight a gain to them carries. Prioritarianism sits between two views. Unlike strict egalitarians, prioritarians do not care about gaps for their own sake: they would never make the rich poorer if no one gained. Unlike plain utilitarians, they do not count every unit of wellbeing the same: a benefit to the poorest counts extra. The attraction is a principle that captures concern for the disadvantaged without valuing equality for its own sake. Everyday example: most people think an extra hour of medical care matters more for a seriously ill patient than for someone with a minor complaint.","example":"Parfit’s levelling down objection: imagine making everyone equal by blinding the sighted, so no one can see. Strict egalitarianism seems to say this is in one way better, since it removes an inequality. Parfit thought that was absurd, and proposed priority instead: helping the worst off matters most, but harming the better off to equalise gains nothing.","history":"Derek Parfit gave the classic statement in his Lindley Lecture “Equality or Priority?” (1991, published 1995). Earlier, Thomas Nagel’s “Equality” (1979) defended an urgency-based priority to the worse off. Larry Temkin, Richard Arneson and Matthew Adler have since developed and debated the view, including in debates about health and climate policy.","challenge":"Exactly how much extra weight should the worst off get? A tiny bonus makes the view close to utilitarianism; a huge one makes it close to Rawls’s difference principle. Egalitarians reply that inequality itself, such as when the rich control politics, matters in ways priority misses."},"society:Sufficientarian":{"summary":"You believe justice requires most of all that everyone has enough for a decent life, and that gaps above that line matter much less, or not at all.","reading":[{"title":"Egalitarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/egalitarianism/"},{"title":"Distributive Justice","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justice-distributive/"}],"idea":"Sufficientarians think the real moral problem is not that some have more than others, but that some have too little. What matters is that everyone has enough: food, shelter, health care, education and the security to live a decent life. Once everyone is above that threshold, differences in wealth matter much less, or not at all. This focuses attention on lifting people out of poverty rather than on comparing incomes. It can appeal across the political spectrum: to those who care deeply about poverty, and to those who think envy of the rich is no basis for policy. The attraction is a clear priority: deal with urgent need first. Everyday example: a society where everyone has a home, health care and good schools, but some people have yachts, seems far less troubling to many people than one where some go hungry.","example":"Frankfurt, in “Equality as a Moral Ideal” (1987), argued that preoccupation with equality distracts people from thinking about what they themselves need. A sufficientarian asked about a policy that would reduce the top incomes but leave the poor no better off would see little to gain, while strongly supporting any policy that lifted people above the line.","history":"Harry Frankfurt gave the classic statement in “Equality as a Moral Ideal” (1987), later expanded as On Inequality (2015). Roger Crisp and Yitzhak Benbaji developed versions. Amartya Sen’s and Martha Nussbaum’s capabilities approach, which lists basic capabilities everyone should have, is often read as a form of sufficientarianism in practice.","challenge":"Where exactly is the threshold, and what if resources cannot lift everyone above it? Critics also argue that large inequalities above the line still matter, for instance when great wealth buys political influence or shapes others’ opportunities."},"society:Utilitarian":{"summary":"You believe the just arrangement is the one that makes lives go best overall, with everyone’s gains counting equally. This often supports redistribution, while allowing incentives that grow the whole.","reading":[{"title":"The History of Utilitarianism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/utilitarianism-history/"},{"title":"Distributive Justice","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justice-distributive/"}],"idea":"Utilitarians judge laws and institutions by one test: how much do they improve people’s lives, counting everyone equally? This sounds simple, but has striking implications. Because an extra dollar means far more to a poor person than to a millionaire, shifting resources from rich to poor often increases total wellbeing, so utilitarians frequently support redistribution. But they also care about growth: if incentives make everyone richer, some inequality may be worth it. Utilitarians follow the evidence on what works. The attraction is impartiality and practicality: everyone counts, and policies are judged by results. Everyday example: a city deciding whether to spend on a new stadium or on clinics in poor neighbourhoods might ask which option improves more lives by more, which is the utilitarian question.","example":"Bentham asked of every law whether it adds to or subtracts from happiness, and used that test to call for prison reform and the decriminalisation of homosexuality, ideas well ahead of his time. Singer applies it today, asking where a donation or policy can do the most good for the most people, wherever they live.","history":"Jeremy Bentham’s An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789) founded modern utilitarianism. John Stuart Mill’s Utilitarianism (1861) and Henry Sidgwick’s The Methods of Ethics (1874) refined it. In ancient China, Mozi judged practices by whether they benefited the people. Peter Singer, in Practical Ethics (1979) and elsewhere, is the best-known modern defender.","challenge":"If total wellbeing is all that matters, could it ever justify sacrificing a minority for the majority’s benefit? Rawls objected that utilitarianism ignores the separateness of persons. Critics also note that measuring and comparing wellbeing across people is very hard."},"society:Desert theorist":{"summary":"You believe a just distribution gives each person what they deserve for their effort and contribution. The hardworking and contributing should not get the same as those who shirk.","reading":[{"title":"Desert","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/desert/"},{"title":"Distributive Justice","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justice-distributive/"}],"idea":"Desert theorists hold that fairness is about proportion: people should get rewards that match what they put in. Someone who works harder, takes on harder tasks or contributes more to others deserves more than someone who does less. This is one of the most widely shared ideas of fairness, found in children dividing chores, in workplaces and across cultures. It differs from pure equality, which gives all the same, and from pure market outcomes, which reward luck as well as effort. Desert theorists may support policies that make rewards track contribution more closely, for example by rewarding care work or by reducing unearned windfalls. The attraction is that it takes responsibility and effort seriously. Everyday example: in a group project, most students think it unfair if one person who did nothing gets the same grade as those who did the work.","example":"Aristotle, in Book V of the Nicomachean Ethics, said justice in distribution is a kind of proportion: if two people are unequal in merit, they should receive unequal shares in the same ratio. A desert theorist asked about executive pay would ask whether it matches actual contribution, or reflects luck and bargaining power instead.","history":"Aristotle’s Nicomachean Ethics, Book V, gave the classic account of proportional justice. Joel Feinberg’s “Justice and Personal Desert” (1970) analysed desert philosophically. David Miller’s Principles of Social Justice (1999) made desert one of three central principles, alongside need and equality. Confucian thought also links reward and office to virtue and effort.","challenge":"Rawls argued that even effort and talent are shaped by luck: by genes, family and upbringing, so no one deeply deserves them. It is also very hard to measure contribution: how do you compare a nurse’s work with a banker’s or a parent’s?"},"society:Radical":{"summary":"You believe present institutions are unjust at their roots, so patching them only prolongs the problem; real justice requires fundamental transformation.","reading":[{"title":"Revolution","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/revolution/"},{"title":"Karl Marx","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marx/"},{"title":"Frantz Fanon","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/frantz-fanon/"}],"idea":"Radicals think some problems are not glitches in a good system but products of the system itself. If an economy creates poverty as it creates wealth, or an empire enriches one people by ruling another, small reforms leave the cause in place, and may even make it look acceptable. So radicals look for the roots (radix in Latin) and aim to change them. History gives them examples: slavery, colonial rule and absolute monarchy were ended by decisive breaks, not polite adjustments. Radicals differ about what the root is, whether economic, racial, colonial or something else, and about the means. The attraction is moral urgency and clarity: refusing to accept injustice as normal. Everyday example: someone who thinks a broken school system needs to be rebuilt from scratch, not given another small grant, reasons like a radical.","example":"Fanon, in The Wretched of the Earth (1961), argued that colonialism could not be reformed into fairness because it rested on the rule of one people over another; it had to end entirely, and the newly free nation had to build something new rather than copy the old masters. A radical judging a reform asks: does this change the structure, or only manage it?","history":"Karl Marx argued in the Theses on Feuerbach (1845) that philosophy should aim to change the world, and with Engels called for revolution in The Communist Manifesto (1848). Frantz Fanon’s The Wretched of the Earth (1961) analysed the anticolonial struggle. Abolitionists who demanded the immediate end of slavery, rather than gradual emancipation, are also part of the radical tradition.","challenge":"Fundamental transformations are unpredictable, and some revolutions replaced one injustice with another. Reformers argue that step-by-step change has delivered real gains, such as voting rights and welfare states, with less risk. Radicals reply that such gains were often won by radical pressure."},"society:Liberal":{"summary":"You believe law should stay neutral between ways of life, resting on reasons people of any faith or none can accept, such as rights and fairness, and leaving the good life to each person.","reading":[{"title":"Liberalism","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/liberalism/"},{"title":"Toleration","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/toleration/"},{"title":"Locke’s Political Philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/locke-political/"}],"idea":"Liberals start from the fact that people deeply disagree about religion and the meaning of life, and that these disagreements will not go away. Rather than have the state pick a winner, liberals say laws should rest on reasons all citizens can share: equal rights, fair treatment and basic liberties. Each person is then free to pursue their own vision of a good life, whether religious or secular, traditional or unusual, as long as they respect others’ equal freedom. Toleration lets people who disagree live together in peace as free and equal citizens. The attraction is a fair framework for diversity: no one is forced to live by someone else’s beliefs. Everyday example: a public school that teaches about many religions without promoting any one of them, and lets students of all faiths attend as equals, acts on liberal neutrality.","example":"Locke’s A Letter Concerning Toleration (1689) argued that the state’s job is to protect life, liberty and property, not to save souls, and that force cannot produce genuine belief anyway. A liberal judging a law banning a religious practice would ask whether there is a public reason, like preventing harm, that people of all faiths could accept.","history":"John Locke’s A Letter Concerning Toleration (1689) and Two Treatises of Government (1689) laid foundations. Immanuel Kant and John Stuart Mill developed liberal ideas of autonomy and liberty. John Rawls’s Political Liberalism (1993) argued that laws must be justifiable by public reason to citizens with different worldviews. Ashoka’s edicts in ancient India, urging respect for all sects, offer an early non-Western parallel.","challenge":"Critics argue that neutrality is impossible: every law favours some ways of life over others, and liberalism itself is a particular vision of the good. Perfectionists and communitarians say the state cannot avoid shaping character, so it should do so wisely. Religious critics say public reason excludes their deepest convictions."},"ethics:Moral absolutist":{"summary":"You hold that some acts, such as killing an innocent person or torture, are always wrong, whatever good might come of them. No amount of benefit can make them right.","idea":"Moral absolutists think morality contains a few exceptionless prohibitions. Most moral rules can bend, but some acts are ruled out completely, even to prevent a disaster. Absolutists come from different traditions. Kant grounded his absolutes in respect for persons as rational beings. Many in the Catholic natural law tradition, and the philosopher Elizabeth Anscombe, ground them in what the act is: deliberately aiming at the death of an innocent person. These thinkers often use the doctrine of double effect: you may sometimes cause a harm as a side effect you foresee, but you may never make it your goal or your means. The attraction is a firm moral backbone: some things you simply will not do, and no clever calculation can talk you into them. Everyday example: a doctor who gives a dying patient enough painkiller to ease the pain, even if it may shorten life, but will never give a dose meant to kill.","example":"Bernard Williams’s case of Jim and the Indians (1973): Jim, a visitor to a small town, is told by an army captain that if he shoots one of twenty captives, the other nineteen will go free. If he refuses, all twenty will be shot. An absolutist says Jim may not kill the innocent man; if the captain then kills twenty, those deaths are the captain’s doing, not Jim’s. Williams himself thought Jim should probably shoot, but argued that the case shows what a purely results-based theory leaves out.","history":"Thomas Aquinas (13th century) gave the classic statement of double effect, in his discussion of killing in self-defence. Immanuel Kant’s 1797 essay “On a Supposed Right to Lie from Philanthropy” held that even lying to a murderer is wrong. Elizabeth Anscombe protested Oxford’s honorary degree for President Truman in “Mr Truman’s Degree” (1956), calling the bombing of Hiroshima and Nagasaki murder, and her “Modern Moral Philosophy” (1958) argued that some acts must be excluded whatever the outcome. John Finnis’s Moral Absolutes (1991) and the papal encyclical Veritatis Splendor (1993) defend exceptionless norms.","challenge":"Catastrophe cases are the hardest. If killing one innocent person would prevent a nuclear war, refusing can look like keeping your own hands clean at the cost of millions. Critics also ask whether the line between intending a harm and merely foreseeing it can bear so much weight.","reading":[{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"},{"title":"Doctrine of Double Effect (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/double-effect/"}]},"ethics:Threshold deontologist":{"summary":"You hold that duties bind even when breaking them would turn out better, but that a big enough catastrophe can override them: don’t torture one person to save five, but perhaps to save a city.","idea":"Threshold deontologists take a middle path between absolute rules and adding up results. Below a certain threshold, duties win: you may not kill an innocent person to save two or three others, even though more lives would be saved. Rights are not just one benefit to be weighed against others. But duties are not infinitely strong. When the stakes become enormous, such as thousands or millions of lives, results take over and the duty gives way. The attraction is that it keeps what seems right about duties in ordinary cases while avoiding the conclusion that you must let a whole city die rather than break a rule. Everyday example: you would never break a promise just because breaking it would be slightly more useful, but you would break it to get someone to hospital.","example":"A terrorist has hidden a bomb that will kill a million people, and torturing him is the only way to find it in time. An absolutist says torture is still forbidden. A threshold deontologist may say that here, and only at stakes like this, the prohibition gives way, though torturing him to save one or two people would still be wrong.","history":"Robert Nozick, in Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), defended rights as side constraints but left open in a footnote whether they may be broken to avoid “catastrophic moral horror”. The legal philosopher Michael S. Moore defended threshold deontology in “Torture and the Balance of Evils” (1989). Larry Alexander examined its difficulties in “Deontology at the Threshold” (2000).","challenge":"Where is the threshold, and why there? If a million lives override the duty, why not 999,999, and so on down? Critics argue that any line is arbitrary, and that once results can win at some point, it is hard to explain why they don’t count all along.","reading":[{"title":"Deontological Ethics (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-deontological/"}]},"meta:moral fictionalist":{"summary":"You think there are no moral facts, so moral claims are strictly false, but that we should keep using moral language as a useful pretence, the way we talk about Sherlock Holmes.","idea":"Moral fictionalists accept the error theorist’s verdict: when people say “cruelty is wrong”, they mean to state an objective fact, and there is no such fact. But fictionalists think dropping moral talk would be a mistake. Moral language helps people make firm commitments, cooperate and resist short-term temptation. So we should keep it, while knowing it is not literally true, much as we can say “Holmes lives on Baker Street” without believing there was a real Holmes. Revolutionary fictionalists say we should switch to this attitude; hermeneutic fictionalists say ordinary moral talk already works this way. The view appeals to people persuaded there are no moral facts who still value moral practice. Everyday example: telling your children firmly that lying is wrong, while privately thinking there is no objective fact behind the rule.","example":"Compare a strict personal rule such as “I never eat sweets on weekdays”. Nothing in the world makes the rule binding, yet treating it as fixed helps more than weighing every biscuit on its merits. Fictionalists think moral rules can work like that for everyone: a story we knowingly keep because living by it goes better.","history":"Richard Joyce’s The Myth of Morality (2001) defended revolutionary moral fictionalism as the best response to J. L. Mackie’s error theory. Mark Kalderon’s Moral Fictionalism (2005) defended the hermeneutic version. Hans Vaihinger’s The Philosophy of “As If” (1911) had earlier argued that many useful ideas are fictions we knowingly accept.","challenge":"Can a pretence still guide us once we know it is a pretence? Critics doubt that moral thoughts keep their force when people see them as fiction. Others ask how fictionalists can say keeping morality is better without relying on the values they deny.","reading":[{"title":"Fictionalism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fictionalism/"},{"title":"Moral Anti-Realism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/"}]},"ethics:Graded-care ethicist":{"summary":"You hold that ethics starts with the people you are bound to, and that caring well for your own family is how goodness spreads outward to everyone.","reading":[{"title":"Mencius (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mencius/"},{"title":"Confucius (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confucius/"},{"title":"Mohism (SEP)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mohism/"}],"idea":"Confucian partialists see the family as the school of virtue. You first learn love, respect and responsibility by being a good child, sibling and parent. These feelings are natural and strongest toward kin. A good person then extends them: treating others' elders as you treat your own, and others' children as your own. So love is graded, not equal, but it is not narrow; the goal is a humane society built outward from well-run families. Impartial love for all, on this view, is unnatural and would weaken the very bonds that teach people to care. Everyday example: a man who looks after his ageing parents at home, and who, because he knows what that takes, supports policies that help other families care for theirs.","example":"In Analects 13.18, a duke boasts that in his land a son would testify against his father for stealing a sheep. Confucius replies that in his land fathers cover for sons and sons for fathers, and uprightness lies in this. Partialists read this as saying that family loyalty is part of moral goodness, not a corruption of it.","history":"Confucius (551 to 479 BCE) placed filial piety at the root of humaneness in the Analects. Mencius (4th century BCE) defended graded love against the Mohists, who taught impartial care for all, and the egoist Yang Zhu. Later Confucians such as Zhu Xi (1130 to 1200) kept graded love central. The Confucian ideal of extending care outward from the family also appears in the Great Learning, a classic text on ordering the family, the state and the world.","challenge":"Graded love can slide into nepotism and favouritism, as in officials appointing relatives or covering up crimes. Mozi's challenge remains: if partiality is the root of goodness, why not also of the rivalries and injustices that come from people favouring their own?"}}};
